is guided by Putin. It was using the sport for soft political power,\u201d a former IBA board member told ProPublica. \u201cIt\u2019s geopolitics. That it\u2019s boxing is just happenstance.\u201d Kremlev, 43, is also heavily involved in an organization called Healthy Fatherland, run by his 23-year-old wife\u2019s twin sister. The group \u2014 which promotes healthy eating and youth sports \u2014 is under Ukrainiansanctionsfor its alleged role in a program of abducting Ukrainian children from occupied territories and relocating them under the guise of \u201crehabilitation.\u201d (Healthy Fatherland did not respond to a request for comment.)\nKremlev\u2019s cozy relationship with the government has made him rich. Putin used the levers of the state to make Kremlev a dominant player in the Russian sports betting industry, according to Proekt, and one of his companies was chosen to operate the national lottery. After Putin nationalized Russia\u2019s largestcar dealershipcompany in 2023, Kremlev became the owner of that too.\nHe now flaunts that wealth through his boisterous public persona. In one video Kremlev posted on social media, he surprises a young mother by giving her a free car. In another, hecriticizeshis young female aide\u2019s outfit as not feminine enough and makes her change. Heshadowboxes with ostrichesand brings in celebrities like Rick Ross and Naomi Campbell for IBA events; hepraises Stalinand rides private jets.\nFollowing the Russian invasion of Ukraine in 2022, the U.S. began to indict Russian oligarchs and seize yachts and other assets. Kremlev repeatedly told associates he was worried about being targeted by a U.S. government probe, according to a person close to IBA leadership. \u201cHe was very afraid of American sanctions,\u201d the person added. (Kremlev does not appear on public U.S. sanction lists.)\nWith the business environment in Europe growing more hostile to Russia, Kremlev moved much of the IBA\u2019s operations from Switzerland to the United Arab Emirates. A new Dubai entity, IB Challenger, would later pay wedding expenses for Trump Jr. The current sources of the IBA\u2019s funds are murky, and, in recent years, top executives have givenconflicting accountsof the status of its relationship with Gazprom. (Kremlev\u2019s press office told ProPublica the IBA\u2019s sponsorship contract with Gazprom \u201cexpired long ago.\u201d) People close to the organization said they understood that its money still comes from Russia.\nIn 2023, the International Olympic Committee stripped Kremlev\u2019s IBA of its role organizing Olympic boxing. It has cited a host of governance issues and the group\u2019s refusal to \u201ctransparently explain the sources of its financing or to explain its full financial dependency, at the time, on a single state-owned company.\u201d\nIt was a major setback that deprived the IBA of a key source of its international influence. Kremlev has raged against the Olympic committee ever since and has said that his IBA predecessor, who he blames for the problems, \u201cmust be shot.\u201d\nKremlev saw a potential ally in President Trump. After Trump\u2019s second inauguration, in January 2025, Kremlev sent an open letter to the president asking him to look into the Olympic committee\u2019s actions in advance of the 2028 Los Angeles Games. \u201cWe look forward with great optimism to the possibility of working together to make the Olympic movement great again,\u201d he wrote.\nIn September 2025, the IBA brought in Trump Jr. for apanel discussionin Istanbul about boxing. With his then-girlfriend, Anderson, sitting offstage, he criticized transgender women competing in women\u2019s sports and reminisced about watching Saturday night fights as a child. Trump Jr. shared the stage with Kremlev, the boxer Manny Pacquiao and Muhammad Ali\u2019s daughter Rasheda. (Trump Jr.\u2019s spokesperson said he was not paid for the appearance and was already in Turkey for an unrelated event.)\nThe president\u2019s son had reason to be attuned to the sensitivity of cozying up with a Putin associate, especially while his father navigates the Russia-Ukraine war. Trump Jr.\u2019s June 2016 meeting with a Russian attorney in Trump Tower became a major focus of the Robert Mueller investigation.\nThe Trump Tower meeting was arranged after an email offering Trump Jr. damaging information about Hillary Clinton as \u201cpart of Russia and its government\u2019s support for Mr. Trump.\u201d Trump Jr. famously responded, \u201cIf it\u2019s what you say I love it especially later in the summer.\u201d Trump Jr. later dismissed the matter as a \u201cwitch hunt,\u201d and the Mueller report concluded that there was not enough evidence to convict him of a crime.\nRecently, Kremlev has been working to expand into the U.S. market. In July, his group hosted abare-knuckle boxingevent in Miami, a chance to showcase a particularly bloody form of the sport in which contenders fight without gloves. But the debut was overshadowed when manosphere influencer Andrew Tate, who the IBA had brought in to host,was arrestedby U.S. Marshals outside the arena. (Tate is facing rape and sex trafficking charges in the United Kingdom, which he has denied.)\nMuch about Kremlev\u2019s relationship with Trump Jr., and where it is headed, remains unknown. In a press release after the Istanbul panel, the IBA hinted there was more to come.\n\u201cPresident Kremlev and Donald Trump Jr made it clear \u2013 this alliance will not remain symbolic,\u201d the press release read. \u201cMore joint initiatives will follow.\u201d\nThe story you just read was made possible by our readers. We hope it inspires you to support ProPublica, so we can continue producing investigations that shine a light on power, uncover the truth and drive real change.\nProPublica is a nonprofit newsroom dedicated to nonpartisan, evidence-based journalism that holds power accountable. Founded in 2008 to address the decline in investigative reporting, we\u2019ve spent over 15 years uncovering injustice, corruption and abuse of power \u2014 work that is slow, expensive and more critical than ever to our democracy. With nine Pulitzer Prizes and reforms sparked in state and local governments, businesses, institutions and more, our reporting ensures that the public interest comes first.\nToday, the stakes are higher than ever. From ethics in our government offices, to reproductive health care, to the climate crisis and beyond, ProPublica remains on the front lines of the stories that matter most. Your gift helps us keep the powerful accountable and the truth accessible.\nJoin over 90,000 supporters nationwidein standing up for investigative journalism that informs, inspires and creates lasting impact. Thank you for making this work possible.\nSupportIndependentJournalism:\nAs a nonprofit newsroom, we rely on donations from individuals like you to fuel our work.Your support today will directly fund investigations that shine a light on urgent issues:corporations prioritizing profits over public safety, health insurers denying coverage to patients, unintended consequences of new laws and more.\nInvestigative Journalism in the Public Interest\nSign up for ProPublica's Big Story newsletter and get our latest stories delivered straight to your inbox.", "date": "Mon, 14 Sep 2026 09:00:00 +0000"}, {"title": "Vouchers Promise Students With Disabilities a Path to Private School. Parents Say That\u2019s Not the Reality.", "url": "https://www.propublica.org/article/school-vouchers-disabilities", "body": "Investigative Journalism in the Public Interest\nProPublica is a nonprofit, investigative newsroom that exposes\n            corruption. We report in all 50 states andpartner with local newsrooms. Our work spursreal-world impactand has received numerous awards, including nine Pulitzer Prizes.\nHow Abortion Bans Lead to Preventable Deaths\nHow Recent Arrivals at the Border Have Changed the Country and Its Attitudes\nSCOTUS Justices\u2019 Beneficial Relationships With Billionaire Donors\nPrivate Schools, Public Money\nProPublica is a nonprofit newsroom that investigates abuses of power. Sign up to receiveour biggest storiesas soon as they\u2019re published.\nThese highlights were written by the reporters and editors who worked on this story.\nIt\u2019s after 6 p.m. by the time Angela Johnson hurries home from work. Her 12-year-old daughter, Amelia, is at their house outside of Pensacola, Florida, with a nurse trained to care for medically fragile children. Amelia, who was born with a rare genetic anomaly, has endured many surgeries and requires a brain shunt and a feeding tube.\nThe nurse starts caring for Amelia as soon as a bus from the public school drops her off. Johnson loves much about the school. But as a physical therapist, she also recognizes its limitations. The district\u2019s physical and occupational therapists have to travel from school to school. The classroom teacher can become overburdened.\nFor all of Amelia\u2019s life, Johnson has had to fight for the education and the school resources her daughter is entitled to receive under federal law. It has exhausted her.\nTwo years ago, she thought she\u2019d found a better option for Amelia when she learned Florida has a state-funded voucher program for children with disabilities. She jumped at the promise of \u201cschool choice.\u201d The program, the largest of its kind in the country, channels taxpayer dollars into accounts that parents can use to pay for private school tuition and other education expenses.\nJohnson envisioned a private school with smaller classes and more one-on-one time with trained staff, a place where she didn\u2019t have to battle for every minute of therapy her child required. She imagined Amelia learning to cut a straight line and to write her name.\nIn 2024, Amelia was awarded about $10,500 from the program. Thrilled, Johnson embarked on a weekslong hunt for a private school.\nShe began scouring school websites, most of which said nothing about services for children with disabilities. She started calling schools, first an upscale Christian one nearby with a strong reputation. It had no nurse trained to care for medically fragile children, so she reached out to several other schools. At first, some said they could help \u2014 until Johnson explained that her daughter had a feeding tube, that she couldn\u2019t yet hold a pencil correctly and needed assistance toileting. Then they all said no.\nJohnson couldn\u2019t find a single one that provided nurses equipped to care for her daughter\u2019s medical needs. Or teachers trained to educate nonverbal children. Or even a bus ride home.\nTherein lies the catch in \u201cschool choice\u201d for children like Amelia. Unlike public schools, private ones generally aren\u2019t required to educate students with disabilities. And they often don\u2019t.\nThe choice wasn\u2019t hers, Johnson realized. It was the schools\u2019. The vouchers \u201care basically designed to make you go to private school,\u201d Johnson said, \u201cbut my kid can\u2019t access private school.\u201d\nJohnson returned the money to the state, along with a very stern email.\nVoucher programs and the private schools they increasingly fund have proliferated across the country. Yet those schools often reject children with disabilities \u2014 the very kids who were used to help sell early voucher programs to lawmakers and the public \u2014 for having needs that are too costly, complex or disruptive. That leaves those children with few options beyond the public schools. And as public schools lose enrollment as a result of more and more voucher use, they lose resources that would benefit children who remain, including those with disabilities.\nAbout 3 million school-age children with disabilities live in states with universal voucher eligibility. Florida, one of those states, illustrates how the promise of school choice can be empty for these children.\nWhen children are approved for education savings accounts, a voucher-style program like the one Florida uses, states often put public money into accounts parents can use to pay expenses like tuition. If families can\u2019t find a private school and don\u2019t use the money, it may roll over to another year or, depending on the state, get returned.\nAbout 8% of the $1.7 billion Florida distributed last year through its voucher program for disabled children was returned because recipients \u2014 kids like Amelia \u2014 were enrolled in public school instead, according to data from the nonprofit that disburses most of the funds. By comparison, just 1% of money in the state\u2019s program open to all students was returned for the same reason.\nFlorida is one of three states where ProPublica obtained data allowing us to make such comparisons.\nLouisiana, which launched an education savings account program in the 2025-26 school year, delivered $43.1 million to parent-controlled accounts. Overall, 6% was returned because the families didn\u2019t spend the money, state data shows. But among students with disabilities, that rate was far higher at 30%.\nIn Texas, data is still coming in for the first year of its $1 billion voucher program. But so far, students with disabilities are opting out of using the money at slightly higher rates than all other students.\nHave you had trouble finding a school or using a voucher-style program? Do you have concerns about schools \u2014 public or private \u2014 in your area? Help us understand how families across the country are navigating their school options.\nThough families return the money for multiple reasons, many have indicated they did so because they couldn\u2019t find suitable private schools that would enroll their students.\n\u201cFamilies are at the mercy of the schools \u2014 and schools choosing their kid \u2014 versus the other way around,\u201d said Chris Roe, director of state policy at the Council of Parent Attorneys and Advocates, a national association working on behalf of children with disabilities.\nToday, at least 18 states have voucher-style programs with universal eligibility or are phasing them in. Florida eclipses them all. More than a half-million Florida schoolchildren \u2014 roughly 1 in 6 \u2014 use a voucher today. When lawmakers opened the state\u2019s voucher program to all children in 2023, two-thirds of the newly enrolled students were already attending private schools.\nYet private schooling often remains out of reach for children like Amelia, the children lawmakers once used to gain a toehold for future voucher expansions. They shared disabled children\u2019s compelling stories, named legislation in their honor and created some of the nation\u2019s first statewide voucher programs specifically for these students.\nThey started in Florida.\nIn 1999, an unassuming man wearing glasses and a lavender tie stood before his colleagues in the Florida Senate to champion a bill that would change the trajectory of American education. John McKay, a Republican, wanted to give parents of children with disabilities a chunk of taxpayer money to pay for private school tuition.\nMcKay pointed to his own family\u2019s experience. Public schools had failed to meet the needs of his daughter, who had significant learning disabilities. He and his wife could afford to send their daughter to a private boarding school, but not all parents had that option.\n\u201cWe\u2019re alienating parents,\u201d McKay argued. \u201cI\u2019ve been alienated by the public system when I was told I didn\u2019t have any choices with regard to my children.\u201d\nThe McKay Scholarships began as a pilot program \u2014 the nation\u2019s first voucher system specifically for students with disabilities. Lawmakers soon expanded it, creating the nation\u2019s first statewide voucher program for this group of children. Ohio followed Florida\u2019s lead, then Utah and Arizona and Georgia and, later, Louisiana and Oklahoma and others.\nAcross the statehouses, politicians stressed one sentiment: choice. Parents would no longer be tethered to their local public schools and could choose where to send their children.\nGeorgia\u2019s sponsor said he sought \u201cto give any disabled student whose parents are dissatisfied with their assigned public school the ability to attend the public or private school that best fits their needs.\u201d\n\u201cThey can just take the scholarship and go to private school,\u201d an Oklahoma lawmaker pledged.\nThe programs have since helped thousands of children with disabilities move to private schools, including those designed to meet their unique needs. But for many children, especially those with more severe disabilities or who live in areas where few if any private schools operate, the promises of choice were never realistic.\nSome legislators backing those early programs said they recognized this problem but argued that any children helped by the new options made the effort worthwhile. And they hoped that competition fueled by money flowing into private schools would create a more robust school marketplace in the long run, one that would accommodate students with special needs.\nIn 2021, West Virginia lawmakers adopted their first and only foray into vouchers, an education savings program that they have since opened to all students. To get the bill passed, some legislators made familiar promises to disabled children \u2014 even though the state has only a handful of schools that specifically say they will serve even the least-intensive of children\u2019s needs.\n\u201cOur kids can\u2019t go to a private school,\u201d said Christy Black, an advocate for Disability Rights of West Virginia who has a daughter with Down syndrome. \u201cI called every private school from Huntington to Charleston because that\u2019s as far as we could drive her and work. But no one would accept her because she has a cognitive disability.\u201d\nMore than half of Utah\u2019s counties have no private schools that take its vouchers for students with disabilities. In Georgia, 71% of counties have none. And in Louisiana last year, among about 350 private schools,only 14 reportedenrolling a single child with a disability. Eight of those admitted five or fewer such students.\nEven in Florida, with its urban centers and roughly $5 billion voucher programs, ProPublica found that more than a dozen counties have no private schools that serve kids with disabilities or have only one school with limited offerings. Our analysis of state data also shows that more than half of Florida\u2019s private schools say they do not serve students with any type of disability.\n\u201cIt\u2019s touted that this program is an equalizer, and then people have choice,\u201d said Jinny Kim, managing attorney at the Disability Rights Education and Defense Fund, a national civil rights and policy nonprofit that advocates for people with disabilities. \u201cBut then if you really look at the details, it\u2019s not actually how it happens.\u201d\nAnxious about her daughter\u2019s upcoming transition to middle school, Atlanta-area mother Marguerite Lane decided to explore private schools that take the state\u2019s voucher. Her daughter, who has autism, does well academically but can be very sensitive and struggle in bigger classrooms without enough behavioral support.\nLane applied to and visited several private schools and found a small, arts-infused one that said it would accept some children with learning challenges. She thought it would serve her daughter well, but then she opened an email from the enrollment director.\n\u201cWe don\u2019t believe we can support her needs and help her thrive in this class,\u201d it read, \u201cand we also don\u2019t want to compromise the educational experience of the other students.\u201d The last part hurt most. Lane thought, \u201cGod forbid the other children learn empathy.\u201d\nAs politicians debated those first voucher programs for children with disabilities, a common refrain echoed across statehouse chambers: The plans were just small programs for a specific group of students.\n\u201cIt\u2019s a very limited scholarship,\u201d a Utah representative assured colleagues in 2004. \u201cThis really is about special needs.\u201d\n\u201cIt\u2019s not, as I heard some of you say to me, the beginning of vouchers. No, ladies and gentlemen, it\u2019s a program to help a group of students that need our help,\u201d a Georgia House member told colleagues in 2007.\nBehind the political scenes, a different message spread.\nAs Arizona adopted its first program for children with disabilities in 2006, Clint Bolickwrote an essayentitled \u201cToe-Hold Strategies.\u201d Bolick, who was then president of the advocacy group Alliance for School Choice, is now a justice on the Arizona Supreme Court.\nVoucher advocates, he wrote, were \u201cacting smarter.\u201d Rather than take on powerful teachers unions directly, they were pushing smaller programs geared toward specific students whose needs were harder for politicians to oppose. \u201cThe strategy makes sense from a moral perspective, for it focuses assistance on the neediest schoolchildren,\u201d he added. \u201cIt also works politically, because choice begets choice: Once the Rubicon is crossed and legislators vote to adopt a school choice program \u2014 no matter how small or targeted \u2014 it becomes easier to support a new one, or expand the old one, the next time around.\u201d\nBy 2011, a half dozen states had launched voucher programs specifically for students with disabilities. Ata \u201cschool choice\u201d conferencethat year, Howie Beigelman sat on a panel that discussed the strategy. At the time, he was deputy director of public policy for the advocacy arm of the Orthodox Union, a prominent Orthodox Jewish umbrella organization. He offered guidance to those who might want to get into vouchers.\n\u201cSpecial needs is a very easy way to get into this business,\u201d Beigelman said. \u201cAnyone, no matter how partisan they are, it\u2019s very hard to say special needs is not where people need \u2018special\u2019 education.\u201d (He recently told ProPublica via email that this was his opinion and he had \u201cnever seen anything \u2018intentional\u2019 about using special needs scholarships for anything more than what they are plainly for: helping educate children with special needs.\u201d)\nShortly after vouchers for disabled children spread to Arkansas in 2016, the University of Arkansasposted on its websitean essay by Sarah McKenzie, executive director of the college\u2019s Office for Education Policy. \u201cSpecial education private school choice programs are often seen as a \u2018foot in the door\u2019 for school choice laws,\u201d she wrote. \u201cOnce some success has been shown to the public, more laws can be passed to expand these programs.\u201d\nIndeed, following those wins, lawmakers across the country opened the floodgates, often to all or most comers, expanding voucher-style programs intoa $10.6 billiontide of public money.\nToday,two-thirds of stateshave some kind of voucher-style program on the books. These programs mostly serve a very different population of students than the early ones did. Most current voucher recipients don\u2019t have disabilities, nor do they need to hunt for a private school that will enroll them. They werealready in private schoolswhen they tapped this new windfall.\nNorth Carolina state Sen. Lisa Grafstein, a Democrat and a civil rights lawyer who advocates for people with disabilities, opposes her state\u2019s voucher system, which is now open to all students. \u201cThe thing that genuinely infuriates me,\u201d she said, \u201cis when people with disabilities are used as pawns.\u201d\nTwo decades after states began adopting vouchers for children with disabilities, lawmakers have done little to improve access to private schools for them \u2014 even as they have created programs open to all comers. Most legislatures haven\u2019t required private schools that take this public money to accept even a few kids with such needs. Nor have they created effective incentives to help operators open more specialized private schools, which can be costly to run.\nThat\u2019s largely because one of the driving forces behind vouchers is the goal of keeping rules to a minimum so private schools will take the public money.\n\u201cGetting the government involved in private schools isn\u2019t the answer,\u201d said former West Virginia Delegate Amy Summers, a Republican who played a central role in creating her state\u2019s universal voucher-like program. She thinks competition for students will prompt private and public schools to better serve children with disabilities.\nAnother key advocate in West Virginia was Republican state Sen. Patricia Puertas Rucker. She homeschooled her two children with disabilities, including a now-adult daughter who she said came home from public school with bite marks and bruises.\nRucker hopes that the state\u2019s voucher program will give more families a way to foot tuition bills or band together to create small schools called microschools. But she regrets that her colleagues approved the payments without additional money for students with disabilities, as some states offer. That extra money could help families afford the higher tuition often charged by specialized schools \u2014 and make it more viable for operators to open them.\n\u201cIt is very expensive to have all this special equipment and to even build the buildings for children that have very specialized special needs,\u201d Rucker said.\nBut beyond raising those payments, she doesn\u2019t think the state should do anything to cajole or require private schools to enroll children with disabilities. \u201cThe minute you start doing that, you are basically disincentivizing private schools from participating, and then you\u2019re denying parents. I do not want to do that.\u201d\nIt\u2019s not that private schools haven\u2019t opened to take advantage of the voucher windfall. They in facthave been proliferatingin recent years. And some of these new schools specifically welcome children with disabilities. But most of them do not.\nParents described private school rejections as \u201cinsulting\u201d and \u201cheartbreaking,\u201d especially when school personnel have met the children. But families don\u2019t always need to apply or even call the schools to get a denial. Some websites or handbooks make it clear.\nOne small Baptist academy in rural Georgia invites applicants to \u201cjoin our family\u201d but declares in its handbook that it isn\u2019t equipped to educate children with learning disabilities or attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder. \u201cIf such students are accepted in our school, it is up to the parents to provide extra services other than what the classroom teachers would do in the confines of the classroom.\u201d\nIn 2023, Taylor Cordes, a former special education teacher, embarked on what she calls her \u201cpersonal passion project\u201d to see how many private schools in her home state of North Carolina post such clear rejections. She dug into every handbook she could find for 660 schools and found that only about a third of them indicated they would accept students with even limited disabilities. More than 1 in 10 declared they would not serve such students, she said.\nHave you had trouble finding a school or using a voucher-style program? Do you have concerns about schools \u2014 public or private \u2014 in your area? Help us understand how families across the country are navigating their school options.\nMany families endure deeply hurtful personal rejections in the search for education. Private schools typically decide who to enroll after meeting parents and their children, as Diavonni Jordan, an Atlanta-area mother, learned. Her now-8-year-old son, Jamir Souders, has ADHD. When he was suspended multiple times from his public school and started to get into fights, she searched for an option with smaller class sizes where she thought he would focus and learn better.\nA single mother of two, Jordan applied to a local Christian school, then took time off work to visit it. She filled with hope as she toured the well-kept campus and spoke to its friendly staff. But within a week, she opened an email from the school. \u201cThe support that we could give in our 1st grade would not be enough for him this year,\u201d it read. The school\u2019s program was small, ill-equipped to provide for his needs, it added.\nJordan expanded her search to other traditional private schools, even when they would mean longer drives for her. She applied to five and toured two. But the verdicts never changed.\n\u201cUnfortunately \u2014 it doesn\u2019t look like we would be a good fit for your family,\u201d another Christian school wrote. Jordan wondered what child constituted a \u201cgood fit\u201d for the school and felt the heartbreak of knowing it wasn\u2019t her son. Perhaps, she thought, she will homeschool Jamir in the future. But for now, with a new school year upon them, he remains in public school.\nMollie Simoncontributed research.Mario ArizaandAsia Fieldscontributed reporting.\nI write about the South and am interested in exposing all kinds of harm, especially related to families and children.\nI am especially interested in stories about the health and welfare of children and families.\nI pursue stories about abuses of power \u2014 often focusing on schools and education \u2014 and stories about private businesses throughout the Midwest.\nTips about government and business, particularly in the Midwest, are welcome.  I\u2019m also eager to hear from educators and government officials. I want to connect with parents and students experiencing the administration\u2019s policy changes.\nCreative Commons License (CC BY-NC-ND 3.0)\nThank you for your interest in republishing this story. You are free to republish it so long as you do the following:\nIt\u2019s after 6 p.m. by the time Angela Johnson hurries home from work. Her 12-year-old daughter, Amelia, is at their house outside of Pensacola, Florida, with a nurse trained to care for medically fragile children. Amelia, who was born with a rare genetic anomaly, has endured many surgeries and requires a brain shunt and a feeding tube.\nThe nurse starts caring for Amelia as soon as a bus from the public school drops her off. Johnson loves much about the school. But as a physical therapist, she also recognizes its limitations. The district\u2019s physical and occupational therapists have to travel from school to school. The classroom teacher can become overburdened.\nFor all of Amelia\u2019s life, Johnson has had to fight for the education and the school resources her daughter is entitled to receive under federal law. It has exhausted her.\nTwo years ago, she thought she\u2019d found a better option for Amelia when she learned Florida has a state-funded voucher program for children with disabilities. She jumped at the promise of \u201cschool choice.\u201d The program, the largest of its kind in the country, channels taxpayer dollars into accounts that parents can use to pay for private school tuition and other education expenses.\nJohnson envisioned a private school with smaller classes and more one-on-one time with trained staff, a place where she didn\u2019t have to battle for every minute of therapy her child required. She imagined Amelia learning to cut a straight line and to write her name.\nIn 2024, Amelia was awarded about $10,500 from the program. Thrilled, Johnson embarked on a weekslong hunt for a private school.\nShe began scouring school websites, most of which said nothing about services for children with disabilities. She started calling schools, first an upscale Christian one nearby with a strong reputation. It had no nurse trained to care for medically fragile children, so she reached out to several other schools. At first, some said they could help \u2014 until Johnson explained that her daughter had a feeding tube, that she couldn\u2019t yet hold a pencil correctly and needed assistance toileting. Then they all said no.\nJohnson couldn\u2019t find a single one that provided nurses equipped to care for her daughter\u2019s medical needs. Or teachers trained to educate nonverbal children. Or even a bus ride home.\nTherein lies the catch in \u201cschool choice\u201d for children like Amelia. Unlike public schools, private ones generally aren\u2019t required to educate students with disabilities. And they often don\u2019t.\nThe choice wasn\u2019t hers, Johnson realized. It was the schools\u2019. The vouchers \u201care basically designed to make you go to private school,\u201d Johnson said, \u201cbut my kid can\u2019t access private school.\u201d\nJohnson returned the money to the state, along with a very stern email.\nVoucher programs and the private schools they increasingly fund have proliferated across the country. Yet those schools often reject children with disabilities \u2014 the very kids who were used to help sell early voucher programs to lawmakers and the public \u2014 for having needs that are too costly, complex or disruptive. That leaves those children with few options beyond the public schools. And as public schools lose enrollment as a result of more and more voucher use, they lose resources that would benefit children who remain, including those with disabilities.\nAbout 3 million school-age children with disabilities live in states with universal voucher eligibility. Florida, one of those states, illustrates how the promise of school choice can be empty for these children.\nWhen children are approved for education savings accounts, a voucher-style program like the one Florida uses, states often put public money into accounts parents can use to pay expenses like tuition. If families can\u2019t find a private school and don\u2019t use the money, it may roll over to another year or, depending on the state, get returned.\nAbout 8% of the $1.7 billion Florida distributed last year through its voucher program for disabled children was returned because recipients \u2014 kids like Amelia \u2014 were enrolled in public school instead, according to data from the nonprofit that disburses most of the funds. By comparison, just 1% of money in the state\u2019s program open to all students was returned for the same reason.\nFlorida is one of three states where ProPublica obtained data allowing us to make such comparisons.\nLouisiana, which launched an education savings account program in the 2025-26 school year, delivered $43.1 million to parent-controlled accounts. Overall, 6% was returned because the families didn\u2019t spend the money, state data shows. But among students with disabilities, that rate was far higher at 30%.\nIn Texas, data is still coming in for the first year of its $1 billion voucher program. But so far, students with disabilities are opting out of using the money at slightly higher rates than all other students.\nThough families return the money for multiple reasons, many have indicated they did so because they couldn\u2019t find suitable private schools that would enroll their students.\n\u201cFamilies are at the mercy of the schools \u2014 and schools choosing their kid \u2014 versus the other way around,\u201d said Chris Roe, director of state policy at the Council of Parent Attorneys and Advocates, a national association working on behalf of children with disabilities.\nToday, at least 18 states have voucher-style programs with universal eligibility or are phasing them in. Florida eclipses them all. More than a half-million Florida schoolchildren \u2014 roughly 1 in 6 \u2014 use a voucher today. When lawmakers opened the state\u2019s voucher program to all children in 2023, two-thirds of the newly enrolled students were already attending private schools.\nYet private schooling often remains out of reach for children like Amelia, the children lawmakers once used to gain a toehold for future voucher expansions. They shared disabled children\u2019s compelling stories, named legislation in their honor and created some of the nation\u2019s first statewide voucher programs specifically for these students.\nThey started in Florida.\nIn 1999, an unassuming man wearing glasses and a lavender tie stood before his colleagues in the Florida Senate to champion a bill that would change the trajectory of American education. John McKay, a Republican, wanted to give parents of children with disabilities a chunk of taxpayer money to pay for private school tuition.\nMcKay pointed to his own family\u2019s experience. Public schools had failed to meet the needs of his daughter, who had significant learning disabilities. He and his wife could afford to send their daughter to a private boarding school, but not all parents had that option.\n\u201cWe\u2019re alienating parents,\u201d McKay argued. \u201cI\u2019ve been alienated by the public system when I was told I didn\u2019t have any choices with regard to my children.\u201d\nThe McKay Scholarships began as a pilot program \u2014 the nation\u2019s first voucher system specifically for students with disabilities. Lawmakers soon expanded it, creating the nation\u2019s first statewide voucher program for this group of children. Ohio followed Florida\u2019s lead, then Utah and Arizona and Georgia and, later, Louisiana and Oklahoma and others.\nAcross the statehouses, politicians stressed one sentiment: choice. Parents would no longer be tethered to their local public schools and could choose where to send their children.\nGeorgia\u2019s sponsor said he sought \u201cto give any disabled student whose parents are dissatisfied with their assigned public school the ability to attend the public or private school that best fits their needs.\u201d\n\u201cThey can just take the scholarship and go to private school,\u201d an Oklahoma lawmaker pledged.\nThe programs have since helped thousands of children with disabilities move to private schools, including those designed to meet their unique needs. But for many children, especially those with more severe disabilities or who live in areas where few if any private schools operate, the promises of choice were never realistic.\nSome legislators backing those early programs said they recognized this problem but argued that any children helped by the new options made the effort worthwhile. And they hoped that competition fueled by money flowing into private schools would create a more robust school marketplace in the long run, one that would accommodate students with special needs.\nIn 2021, West Virginia lawmakers adopted their first and only foray into vouchers, an education savings program that they have since opened to all students. To get the bill passed, some legislators made familiar promises to disabled children \u2014 even though the state has only a handful of schools that specifically say they will serve even the least-intensive of children\u2019s needs.\n\u201cOur kids can\u2019t go to a private school,\u201d said Christy Black, an advocate for Disability Rights of West Virginia who has a daughter with Down syndrome. \u201cI called every private school from Huntington to Charleston because that\u2019s as far as we could drive her and work. But no one would accept her because she has a cognitive disability.\u201d\nMore than half of Utah\u2019s counties have no private schools that take its vouchers for students with disabilities. In Georgia, 71% of counties have none. And in Louisiana last year, among about 350 private schools,only 14 reportedenrolling a single child with a disability. Eight of those admitted five or fewer such students.\nEven in Florida, with its urban centers and roughly $5 billion voucher programs, ProPublica found that more than a dozen counties have no private schools that serve kids with disabilities or have only one school with limited offerings. Our analysis of state data also shows that more than half of Florida\u2019s private schools say they do not serve students with any type of disability.\n\u201cIt\u2019s touted that this program is an equalizer, and then people have choice,\u201d said Jinny Kim, managing attorney at the Disability Rights Education and Defense Fund, a national civil rights and policy nonprofit that advocates for people with disabilities. \u201cBut then if you really look at the details, it\u2019s not actually how it happens.\u201d\nAnxious about her daughter\u2019s upcoming transition to middle school, Atlanta-area mother Marguerite Lane decided to explore private schools that take the state\u2019s voucher. Her daughter, who has autism, does well academically but can be very sensitive and struggle in bigger classrooms without enough behavioral support.\nLane applied to and visited several private schools and found a small, arts-infused one that said it would accept some children with learning challenges. She thought it would serve her daughter well, but then she opened an email from the enrollment director.\n\u201cWe don\u2019t believe we can support her needs and help her thrive in this class,\u201d it read, \u201cand we also don\u2019t want to compromise the educational experience of the other students.\u201d The last part hurt most. Lane thought, \u201cGod forbid the other children learn empathy.\u201d\nAs politicians debated those first voucher programs for children with disabilities, a common refrain echoed across statehouse chambers: The plans were just small programs for a specific group of students.\n\u201cIt\u2019s a very limited scholarship,\u201d a Utah representative assured colleagues in 2004. \u201cThis really is about special needs.\u201d\n\u201cIt\u2019s not, as I heard some of you say to me, the beginning of vouchers. No, ladies and gentlemen, it\u2019s a program to help a group of students that need our help,\u201d a Georgia House member told colleagues in 2007.\nBehind the political scenes, a different message spread.\nAs Arizona adopted its first program for children with disabilities in 2006, Clint Bolickwrote an essayentitled \u201cToe-Hold Strategies.\u201d Bolick, who was then president of the advocacy group Alliance for School Choice, is now a justice on the Arizona Supreme Court.\nVoucher advocates, he wrote, were \u201cacting smarter.\u201d Rather than take on powerful teachers unions directly, they were pushing smaller programs geared toward specific students whose needs were harder for politicians to oppose. \u201cThe strategy makes sense from a moral perspective, for it focuses assistance on the neediest schoolchildren,\u201d he added. \u201cIt also works politically, because choice begets choice: Once the Rubicon is crossed and legislators vote to adopt a school choice program \u2014 no matter how small or targeted \u2014 it becomes easier to support a new one, or expand the old one, the next time around.\u201d\nBy 2011, a half dozen states had launched voucher programs specifically for students with disabilities. Ata \u201cschool choice\u201d conferencethat year, Howie Beigelman sat on a panel that discussed the strategy. At the time, he was deputy director of public policy for the advocacy arm of the Orthodox Union, a prominent Orthodox Jewish umbrella organization. He offered guidance to those who might want to get into vouchers.\n\u201cSpecial needs is a very easy way to get into this business,\u201d Beigelman said. \u201cAnyone, no matter how partisan they are, it\u2019s very hard to say special needs is not where people need \u2018special\u2019 education.\u201d (He recently told ProPublica via email that this was his opinion and he had \u201cnever seen anything \u2018intentional\u2019 about using special needs scholarships for anything more than what they are plainly for: helping educate children with special needs.\u201d)\nShortly after vouchers for disabled children spread to Arkansas in 2016, the University of Arkansasposted on its websitean essay by Sarah McKenzie, executive director of the college\u2019s Office for Education Policy. \u201cSpecial education private school choice programs are often seen as a \u2018foot in the door\u2019 for school choice laws,\u201d she wrote. \u201cOnce some success has been shown to the public, more laws can be passed to expand these programs.\u201d\nIndeed, following those wins, lawmakers across the country opened the floodgates, often to all or most comers, expanding voucher-style programs intoa $10.6 billiontide of public money.\nToday,two-thirds of stateshave some kind of voucher-style program on the books. These programs mostly serve a very different population of students than the early ones did. Most current voucher recipients don\u2019t have disabilities, nor do they need to hunt for a private school that will enroll them. They werealready in private schoolswhen they tapped this new windfall.\nNorth Carolina state Sen. Lisa Grafstein, a Democrat and a civil rights lawyer who advocates for people with disabilities, opposes her state\u2019s voucher system, which is now open to all students. \u201cThe thing that genuinely infuriates me,\u201d she said, \u201cis when people with disabilities are used as pawns.\u201d\nTwo decades after states began adopting vouchers for children with disabilities, lawmakers have done little to improve access to private schools for them \u2014 even as they have created programs open to all comers. Most legislatures haven\u2019t required private schools that take this public money to accept even a few kids with such needs. Nor have they created effective incentives to help operators open more specialized private schools, which can be costly to run.\nThat\u2019s largely because one of the driving forces behind vouchers is the goal of keeping rules to a minimum so private schools will take the public money.\n\u201cGetting the government involved in private schools isn\u2019t the answer,\u201d said former West Virginia Delegate Amy Summers, a Republican who played a central role in creating her state\u2019s universal voucher-like program. She thinks competition for students will prompt private and public schools to better serve children with disabilities.\nAnother key advocate in West Virginia was Republican state Sen. Patricia Puertas Rucker. She homeschooled her two children with disabilities, including a now-adult daughter who she said came home from public school with bite marks and bruises.\nRucker hopes that the state\u2019s voucher program will give more families a way to foot tuition bills or band together to create small schools called microschools. But she regrets that her colleagues approved the payments without additional money for students with disabilities, as some states offer. That extra money could help families afford the higher tuition often charged by specialized schools \u2014 and make it more viable for operators to open them.\n\u201cIt is very expensive to have all this special equipment and to even build the buildings for children that have very specialized special needs,\u201d Rucker said.\nBut beyond raising those payments, she doesn\u2019t think the state should do anything to cajole or require private schools to enroll children with disabilities. \u201cThe minute you start doing that, you are basically disincentivizing private schools from participating, and then you\u2019re denying parents. I do not want to do that.\u201d\nIt\u2019s not that private schools haven\u2019t opened to take advantage of the voucher windfall. They in facthave been proliferatingin recent years. And some of these new schools specifically welcome children with disabilities. But most of them do not.\nParents described private school rejections as \u201cinsulting\u201d and \u201cheartbreaking,\u201d especially when school personnel have met the children. But families don\u2019t always need to apply or even call the schools to get a denial. Some websites or handbooks make it clear.\nOne small Baptist academy in rural Georgia invites applicants to \u201cjoin our family\u201d but declares in its handbook that it isn\u2019t equipped to educate children with learning disabilities or attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder. \u201cIf such students are accepted in our school, it is up to the parents to provide extra services other than what the classroom teachers would do in the confines of the classroom.\u201d\nIn 2023, Taylor Cordes, a former special education teacher, embarked on what she calls her \u201cpersonal passion project\u201d to see how many private schools in her home state of North Carolina post such clear rejections. She dug into every handbook she could find for 660 schools and found that only about a third of them indicated they would accept students with even limited disabilities. More than 1 in 10 declared they would not serve such students, she said.\nMany families endure deeply hurtful personal rejections in the search for education. Private schools typically decide who to enroll after meeting parents and their children, as Diavonni Jordan, an Atlanta-area mother, learned. Her now-8-year-old son, Jamir Souders, has ADHD. When he was suspended multiple times from his public school and started to get into fights, she searched for an option with smaller class sizes where she thought he would focus and learn better.\nA single mother of two, Jordan applied to a local Christian school, then took time off work to visit it. She filled with hope as she toured the well-kept campus and spoke to its friendly staff. But within a week, she opened an email from the school. \u201cThe support that we could give in our 1st grade would not be enough for him this year,\u201d it read. The school\u2019s program was small, ill-equipped to provide for his needs, it added.\nJordan expanded her search to other traditional private schools, even when they would mean longer drives for her. She applied to five and toured two. But the verdicts never changed.\n\u201cUnfortunately \u2014 it doesn\u2019t look like we would be a good fit for your family,\u201d another Christian school wrote. Jordan wondered what child constituted a \u201cgood fit\u201d for the school and felt the heartbreak of knowing it wasn\u2019t her son. Perhaps, she thought, she will homeschool Jamir in the future. But for now, with a new school year upon them, he remains in public school.\nThe story you just read was made possible by our readers. We hope it inspires you to support ProPublica, so we can continue producing investigations that shine a light on power, uncover the truth and drive real change.\nProPublica is a nonprofit newsroom dedicated to nonpartisan, evidence-based journalism that holds power accountable. Founded in 2008 to address the decline in investigative reporting, we\u2019ve spent over 15 years uncovering injustice, corruption and abuse of power \u2014 work that is slow, expensive and more critical than ever to our democracy. With nine Pulitzer Prizes and reforms sparked in state and local governments, businesses, institutions and more, our reporting ensures that the public interest comes first.\nToday, the stakes are higher than ever. From ethics in our government offices, to reproductive health care, to the climate crisis and beyond, ProPublica remains on the front lines of the stories that matter most. Your gift helps us keep the powerful accountable and the truth accessible.\nJoin over 90,000 supporters nationwidein standing up for investigative journalism that informs, inspires and creates lasting impact. Thank you for making this work possible.\nSupportIndependentJournalism:\nAs a nonprofit newsroom, we rely on donations from individuals like you to fuel our work.Your support today will directly fund investigations that shine a light on urgent issues:corporations prioritizing profits over public safety, health insurers denying coverage to patients, unintended consequences of new laws and more.\nInvestigative Journalism in the Public Interest\nSign up for ProPublica's Big Story newsletter and get our latest stories delivered straight to your inbox.", "date": "Fri, 11 Sep 2026 10:00:00 +0000"}, {"title": "EPA to Loosen Methane Rules, Boosting Pollution From Oil and Gas Wells", "url": "https://www.propublica.org/article/methane-gas-epa-proposal-stripper-wells", "body": "Investigative Journalism in the Public Interest\nInvestigative Journalism in the Public Interest\nInvestigative Journalism in the Public Interest\nProPublica is a nonprofit, investigative newsroom that exposes\n            corruption. We report in all 50 states andpartner with local newsrooms. Our work spursreal-world impactand has received numerous awards, including nine Pulitzer Prizes.\nHow Abortion Bans Lead to Preventable Deaths\nHow Recent Arrivals at the Border Have Changed the Country and Its Attitudes\nSCOTUS Justices\u2019 Beneficial Relationships With Billionaire Donors\nProPublica is a nonprofit newsroom that investigates abuses of power. Sign up to receiveour biggest storiesas soon as they\u2019re published.\nThe Trump administration is poised to loosen environmental restrictions on oil and gas wells that produce very little energy but release vast amounts of methane, a highly potent greenhouse gas.\nThe Environmental Protection Agency is proposing to drastically weaken requirements for leak inspections and equipment upgrades at more than 700,000 low-producing \u201cstripper wells,\u201d according to a draft rule being reviewed by the White House and seen by ProPublica. These wells \u2014 which tend to be old, poorly maintained and thus prone to leaking \u2014 produce just 6% of the country\u2019s oil and natural gas but are responsible for roughly half the sector\u2019s methane pollution, studies show.\nThe EPA acknowledged similar figures in the draft. But it said that the cost of complying with existing regulations would force the lowest-producing wells to shut down and that this was \u201cunreasonable\u201d \u2014 even though it would eliminate just 0.4% of U.S. oil and gas production, according to an industry estimate cited in the proposed rule.\nThe proposal, which would also weaken methane controls in the wider oil industry, is expected to save companies $42 billion through 2050. An attached memo says the rollback will help to \u201cunleash\u201d American energy, one of President Donald Trump\u2019s favorite slogans.\nEnvironmental advocates said deregulating stripper wells will do little to boost energy output while significantly increasing climate pollution.\n\u201cThis is not about energy dominance,\u201d said Darin Schroeder of the Clean Air Task Force, a climate advocacy group. \u201cIt\u2019s about padding the pockets of oil and gas operators and saddling society with the costs.\u201d\nThe proposed changes are the culmination of a campaign by a previously low-profile faction of the oil industry that has gained unprecedented influence during the second Trump administration. In the draft, the EPA said it is deregulating stripper wells \u2014 defined as those that produce up to 15 barrels a day \u2014 in response to petitions from the Independent Petroleum Association of America and the National Stripper Well Association, among other groups.\nAs ProPublicareported in June, the IPAA has long enjoyed the support of a little-known oil billionaire named Jeffery Hildebrand. He is the founder and owner of Hilcorp, a privately held company known for buying up old, poorly maintained stripper wells \u2014 a business that was threatened when the Biden administration imposed aggressive restrictions on methane pollution in 2024.\nIn response, Hildebrand became one of the oil industry\u2019s biggest Trump donors. Trump, back in office, promptly appointed a former Hilcorp lobbyist named Aaron Szabo to a top post at the EPA, putting him in charge of the effort to unravel the new methane rules.\nSzabo had previously helped todraft a letteron behalf of the American Exploration and Production Council \u2014 which has Hilcorp\u2019s CEO on its board \u2014 opposing those rules. He also gave advice on climate regulations for Project 2025, the deregulatory roadmap for the current administration.\nAmong Project 2025\u2019s recommendations: eliminate an EPA program that would track \u201csuper-emitter\u201d events \u2014 enormous methane releases that have long plagued the oil industry \u2014 and would oblige companies to respond to them. The AXPC and the IPAA have also called for an end to the program.\nAnd the current proposal from Trump\u2019s EPA aims to do just that.\nAn IPAA spokesperson declined to comment on the group\u2019s influence in the Trump administration but said in an emailed statement that its lobbying \u201chas focused on ensuring regulations are workable for low-production and marginal wells.\u201d\nThe NSWA and AXPC didn\u2019t respond to emailed requests for comment. NSWA representatives previously told ProPublica that they had asked the EPA to soften restrictions on stripper wells because many of their members couldn\u2019t afford the compliance costs. AXPC CEO Anne Bradbury previously told ProPublica that the group\u2019s members were \u201ccommitted to building on a legacy of world-leading methane emission reductions.\u201d\nHilcorp spokesperson Nick Piatek didn\u2019t provide a comment either but previously told ProPublica that the company was \u201cproud\u201d of recent efforts to reduce its emissions.\nWhile Szabo didn\u2019t respond to emails from ProPublica, the EPA\u2019s press office said in a statement that he \u201chad not done any work for AXPC for well over a year before he started working for the federal government\u201d and that he had reviewed federal ethics rules with the agency\u2019s ethics staff upon joining. The agency declined to comment on the substance of the methane rule revisions except to confirm that they were being reviewed by the White House Office of Management and Budget. The OMB\u2019s press office didn\u2019t respond to an emailed request for comment.\nThe rules now being rolled back were a key component of former President Joe Biden\u2019s ambitious climate agenda. They would have cut methane pollution from the oil industry by 80%,the EPA saidat the time. Because methane breaks down relatively quickly \u2014 in about a dozen years \u2014 cutting these emissions is one of the few known ways to reduce global warming in our lifetimes. Methane accounts for one-third of the rise in temperatures since the Industrial Revolution, according to the United Nations Environment Programme.\nSince methane is the main component of natural gas, the rules would also have prevented useful energy from being lost to the atmosphere in the form of leaks and other releases. All told, Biden\u2019s EPA had valued the rules\u2019 climate, health and energy benefits at more than $7 billion a year, even after accounting for increased compliance costs.\nBreaking with precedent, Trump\u2019s EPA didn\u2019t include calculations of the environmental and health impacts of the new proposal. But if it goes into effect, much of the public benefit will evaporate while oil and gas producers see increased profits.\n\nI report on climate change and how people and institutions are responding to it.\nI welcome tips or documents about Trump administration climate policy or actions by private companies or institutions that may impact the climate.\nCreative Commons License (CC BY-NC-ND 3.0)\nThank you for your interest in republishing this story. You are free to republish it so long as you do the following:\nThe Trump administration is poised to loosen environmental restrictions on oil and gas wells that produce very little energy but release vast amounts of methane, a highly potent greenhouse gas.\nThe Environmental Protection Agency is proposing to drastically weaken requirements for leak inspections and equipment upgrades at more than 700,000 low-producing \u201cstripper wells,\u201d according to a draft rule being reviewed by the White House and seen by ProPublica. These wells \u2014 which tend to be old, poorly maintained and thus prone to leaking \u2014 produce just 6% of the country\u2019s oil and natural gas but are responsible for roughly half the sector\u2019s methane pollution, studies show.\nThe EPA acknowledged similar figures in the draft. But it said that the cost of complying with existing regulations would force the lowest-producing wells to shut down and that this was \u201cunreasonable\u201d \u2014 even though it would eliminate just 0.4% of U.S. oil and gas production, according to an industry estimate cited in the proposed rule.\nThe proposal, which would also weaken methane controls in the wider oil industry, is expected to save companies $42 billion through 2050. An attached memo says the rollback will help to \u201cunleash\u201d American energy, one of President Donald Trump\u2019s favorite slogans.\nEnvironmental advocates said deregulating stripper wells will do little to boost energy output while significantly increasing climate pollution.\n\u201cThis is not about energy dominance,\u201d said Darin Schroeder of the Clean Air Task Force, a climate advocacy group. \u201cIt\u2019s about padding the pockets of oil and gas operators and saddling society with the costs.\u201d\nThe proposed changes are the culmination of a campaign by a previously low-profile faction of the oil industry that has gained unprecedented influence during the second Trump administration. In the draft, the EPA said it is deregulating stripper wells \u2014 defined as those that produce up to 15 barrels a day \u2014 in response to petitions from the Independent Petroleum Association of America and the National Stripper Well Association, among other groups.\nAs ProPublicareported in June, the IPAA has long enjoyed the support of a little-known oil billionaire named Jeffery Hildebrand. He is the founder and owner of Hilcorp, a privately held company known for buying up old, poorly maintained stripper wells \u2014 a business that was threatened when the Biden administration imposed aggressive restrictions on methane pollution in 2024.\nIn response, Hildebrand became one of the oil industry\u2019s biggest Trump donors. Trump, back in office, promptly appointed a former Hilcorp lobbyist named Aaron Szabo to a top post at the EPA, putting him in charge of the effort to unravel the new methane rules.\nSzabo had previously helped todraft a letteron behalf of the American Exploration and Production Council \u2014 which has Hilcorp\u2019s CEO on its board \u2014 opposing those rules. He also gave advice on climate regulations for Project 2025, the deregulatory roadmap for the current administration.\nAmong Project 2025\u2019s recommendations: eliminate an EPA program that would track \u201csuper-emitter\u201d events \u2014 enormous methane releases that have long plagued the oil industry \u2014 and would oblige companies to respond to them. The AXPC and the IPAA have also called for an end to the program.\nAnd the current proposal from Trump\u2019s EPA aims to do just that.\nAn IPAA spokesperson declined to comment on the group\u2019s influence in the Trump administration but said in an emailed statement that its lobbying \u201chas focused on ensuring regulations are workable for low-production and marginal wells.\u201d\nThe NSWA and AXPC didn\u2019t respond to emailed requests for comment. NSWA representatives previously told ProPublica that they had asked the EPA to soften restrictions on stripper wells because many of their members couldn\u2019t afford the compliance costs. AXPC CEO Anne Bradbury previously told ProPublica that the group\u2019s members were \u201ccommitted to building on a legacy of world-leading methane emission reductions.\u201d\nHilcorp spokesperson Nick Piatek didn\u2019t provide a comment either but previously told ProPublica that the company was \u201cproud\u201d of recent efforts to reduce its emissions.\nWhile Szabo didn\u2019t respond to emails from ProPublica, the EPA\u2019s press office said in a statement that he \u201chad not done any work for AXPC for well over a year before he started working for the federal government\u201d and that he had reviewed federal ethics rules with the agency\u2019s ethics staff upon joining. The agency declined to comment on the substance of the methane rule revisions except to confirm that they were being reviewed by the White House Office of Management and Budget. The OMB\u2019s press office didn\u2019t respond to an emailed request for comment.\nThe rules now being rolled back were a key component of former President Joe Biden\u2019s ambitious climate agenda. They would have cut methane pollution from the oil industry by 80%,the EPA saidat the time. Because methane breaks down relatively quickly \u2014 in about a dozen years \u2014 cutting these emissions is one of the few known ways to reduce global warming in our lifetimes. Methane accounts for one-third of the rise in temperatures since the Industrial Revolution, according to the United Nations Environment Programme.\nSince methane is the main component of natural gas, the rules would also have prevented useful energy from being lost to the atmosphere in the form of leaks and other releases. All told, Biden\u2019s EPA had valued the rules\u2019 climate, health and energy benefits at more than $7 billion a year, even after accounting for increased compliance costs.\nBreaking with precedent, Trump\u2019s EPA didn\u2019t include calculations of the environmental and health impacts of the new proposal. But if it goes into effect, much of the public benefit will evaporate while oil and gas producers see increased profits.\nThe story you just read was made possible by our readers. We hope it inspires you to support ProPublica, so we can continue producing investigations that shine a light on power, uncover the truth and drive real change.\nProPublica is a nonprofit newsroom dedicated to nonpartisan, evidence-based journalism that holds power accountable. Founded in 2008 to address the decline in investigative reporting, we\u2019ve spent over 15 years uncovering injustice, corruption and abuse of power \u2014 work that is slow, expensive and more critical than ever to our democracy. With nine Pulitzer Prizes and reforms sparked in state and local governments, businesses, institutions and more, our reporting ensures that the public interest comes first.\nToday, the stakes are higher than ever. From ethics in our government offices, to reproductive health care, to the climate crisis and beyond, ProPublica remains on the front lines of the stories that matter most. Your gift helps us keep the powerful accountable and the truth accessible.\nJoin over 90,000 supporters nationwidein standing up for investigative journalism that informs, inspires and creates lasting impact. Thank you for making this work possible.\nSupportIndependentJournalism:\nAs a nonprofit newsroom, we rely on donations from individuals like you to fuel our work.Your support today will directly fund investigations that shine a light on urgent issues:corporations prioritizing profits over public safety, health insurers denying coverage to patients, unintended consequences of new laws and more.\nInvestigative Journalism in the Public Interest\nSign up for ProPublica's Big Story newsletter and get our latest stories delivered straight to your inbox.", "date": "Fri, 11 Sep 2026 09:00:00 +0000"}, {"title": "Empty Seat: U.S. Absent as Western Powers Meet on Wartime Protections for Civilians", "url": "https://www.propublica.org/article/trump-hegseth-dod-nato-civilian-harm-reduction-absent", "body": "Investigative Journalism in the Public Interest\nInvestigative Journalism in the Public Interest\nInvestigative Journalism in the Public Interest\nProPublica is a nonprofit, investigative newsroom that exposes\n            corruption. We report in all 50 states andpartner with local newsrooms. Our work spursreal-world impactand has received numerous awards, including nine Pulitzer Prizes.\nHow Abortion Bans Lead to Preventable Deaths\nHow Recent Arrivals at the Border Have Changed the Country and Its Attitudes\nSCOTUS Justices\u2019 Beneficial Relationships With Billionaire Donors\nProPublica is a nonprofit newsroom that investigates abuses of power. Sign up to receiveour biggest storiesas soon as they\u2019re published.\nThree years ago, the Defense Department led an ambitious international effort to better protect civilians during combat, a movement built on the bloody lessons of Afghanistan and Iraq.\nThe Pentagon created a network with European partners, driving the creation of shared standards. Civilian harm expertsdescribed the momentas a \u201cunique opportunity\u201d for American leadership by example.\nToday, those efforts continue \u2014 without the United States, after Defense Secretary Pete Hegsethdismantled the civilian protectionmission.\nAnd Thursday, no U.S. delegation will be in the room when the international forum the U.S. cofounded meets at NATO headquarters in Belgium.\nThe country\u2019s absence from the International Contact Group on Civilian Harm Mitigation and Response summit reinforces the abandonment of a mission the Pentagon had encouraged partners to embrace, said current and former officials. Most spoke to ProPublica on condition of anonymity for fear of retaliation from President Donald Trump\u2019s administration.\nThey called the step back \u201chumiliating\u201d and \u201cdangerous,\u201d pointing to reports of rising civilian casualties in Iran following strikes on schools and homes.\n\u201cThere will be a day when people who care about mitigating civilian harm can come back and say, \u2018OK, are we ready to solve this problem?\u2019\u201d said Jenny McAvoy, a former Defense Department official who helped shape the U.S. civilian protection program. \u201cBut there are already enormous consequences of abandoning this work.\u201d\nThe Pentagon declined to comment about the lack of U.S. participation in the Brussels talks. A spokesperson previously told ProPublica that the Defense Department factors civilian protections into all phases of operational planning and \u201cremains committed to fulfilling its civilian harm mitigation and response responsibilities.\u201d\nThe Pentagon adopted the civilian harm mitigation and response mission in 2022 after years of bloodshed in the post-9/11 \u201cforever wars.\u201d Known as CHMR and pronounced \u201cchimmer,\u201d the framework called for a specialized center to collect strike data and for advisers embedded with regional commands to help mitigate the risk to noncombatants.\nNot long after, the U.S. teamed up with the Netherlands to coordinate efforts internationally. The two countries had been frequent partners in recent military campaigns, including the fight against the Islamic State group, and were both coming to terms with high-profile civilian casualty incidents. For the United States, years of harm in Iraq and Afghanistan had drawn international outrage and pledges from commanders to better collect data and learn from deadly mistakes.\nThe Dutch were still addressing the fallout from a 2015 operation in Iraq in which air strikes targeting a car-bomb factory ignited a nearby munitions depot, setting off an enormous explosion that killed at least 70 civilians and wounded hundreds. Dutch responsibility wasn\u2019t revealed until 2019, causing a national scandal and accusations of a cover-up.\nIn 2023, the U.S. and the Netherlands formed the loose coalition on CHMR. Participants described a small, informal group that has met half a dozen times since 2023 to share best practices and hear briefings on civilian casualty trends.\nAdvocacy groups said it had taken years to get the U.S. military to think of civilian casualties as more than \u201ccollateral damage,\u201d showing commanders how the deaths of innocents were anathema to their morals and strategically harmful to their missions. The eventual adoption of CHMR guidelines by American defense officials helped their counterparts abroad make the case for programs in their own countries.\n\u201cBy the U.S. putting itself out there and saying, \u2018We learned, we can do better,\u2019 it gives permission and makes it OK for other governments to similarly acknowledge, \u2018Yeah, we can do better too,\u2019\u201d said McAvoy.\nAs participants gather this week in Brussels to discuss how to continue strengthening CHMR, however, the early momentum from the U.S. side has evaporated.\nHegseth, who derides rules of engagement as \u201cwoke,\u201dgutted the CHMR program over the objections of the nation\u2019s top commanders, ProPublica reported last month. Staff plunged from nearly 200 to about two dozen.\nDays before the latest summit in Brussels, the United States wasn\u2019t listed as a participant and there was no sign anyone from the Defense Department would attend. Current and former officials predicted either a no-show or, at most, a single delegate from one of the commands showing up.\nTo experts, the message was clear: The era of U.S. leadership on civilian protection was over.\n\u201cThe U.S. stopped participating in this group last year and people assumed that meant the group would die,\u201d said McAvoy. \u201cBut the group continued to exist and to meet \u2014 without the U.S. \u2014 under the leadership of the U.K. and the Netherlands.\u201d\nThe two-day summit opens with a broad discussion of CHMR, such as how it applies in coalitions or in operations at sea, that includes advocacy groups and academics, a draft agenda reviewed by ProPublica shows. Day 2 is a closed-door session for more intimate talks among senior representatives of member states. The meeting wraps with a \u201clooking ahead\u201d panel.\nPast attendees included Austria, Australia, Belgium, Denmark, Finland, Germany and Norway. The nations represent a wide variation in approaches and needs when it comes to CHMR, participants said.\n\nThe U.K., for example, doesn\u2019t have a formal framework for civilian harm mitigation and response, and advocates have spent years pushing the British Ministry of Defense to adopt policies similar to those of the United States. Though humanitarian groups generally regarded the American CHMR enterprise as nascent and imperfect, the overall framework provided a worthy standard, said Mae Thompson of the Ceasefire Centre for Civilian Rights, a London-based advocacy group.\nAdvocates have framed the Pentagon\u2019s rollback as an opportunity for the U.K. to step into a leadership role, Thompson said, and they\u2019ve been encouraged by a recentinternal report by the defense ministrythat calls for formalization of a program in Britain, including systems to track civilian harm, investigate, and make amends and offer post-harm response.\nNATO boasts a civilian protection policy that predates the American model, but the organization is now incorporating elements of the U.S. approach, said Andrew Hyde, who studies U.S.-European relations at the Stimson Center, a nonpartisan foreign policy think tank.\nHyde said international talks are important for sorting out how CHMR applies in joint operations when the United States, NATO and European nations all have different interpretations of harm mitigation. The diminished U.S. role, he said, means NATO and other partners must step up on coordination.\nThe shift in leadership could end up shielding civilian protection work from the whims of whichever U.S. administration is in power and leading to more enduring reforms, Hyde said.\n\u201cNATO has continued to push forward without U.S. support or participation, keeping up the momentum and ready for U.S. re-engagement,\u201d Hyde said.\nFrom Washington, I cover national security issues, with a focus on militant movements and counterterrorism efforts.\nI\u2019m interested in tips about counterterrorism, court cases involving surveillance or civil liberties, national security personnel changes, threat assessments and the proximity of extremist movements to federal power.\nI cover national security, the U.S. military and how the armed forces are deployed here and abroad.\nTell me about changes being made to how the military operates and where, who is in charge, and how decisions are determined. I\u2019m interested in how norms and safeguards are transforming on paper, through personnel and in the field.\nThe story you just read was made possible by our readers. We hope it inspires you to support ProPublica, so we can continue producing investigations that shine a light on power, uncover the truth and drive real change.\nProPublica is a nonprofit newsroom dedicated to nonpartisan, evidence-based journalism that holds power accountable. Founded in 2008 to address the decline in investigative reporting, we\u2019ve spent over 15 years uncovering injustice, corruption and abuse of power \u2014 work that is slow, expensive and more critical than ever to our democracy. With nine Pulitzer Prizes and reforms sparked in state and local governments, businesses, institutions and more, our reporting ensures that the public interest comes first.\nToday, the stakes are higher than ever. From ethics in our government offices, to reproductive health care, to the climate crisis and beyond, ProPublica remains on the front lines of the stories that matter most. Your gift helps us keep the powerful accountable and the truth accessible.\nJoin over 90,000 supporters nationwidein standing up for investigative journalism that informs, inspires and creates lasting impact. Thank you for making this work possible.\nSupportIndependentJournalism:\nAs a nonprofit newsroom, we rely on donations from individuals like you to fuel our work.Your support today will directly fund investigations that shine a light on urgent issues:corporations prioritizing profits over public safety, health insurers denying coverage to patients, unintended consequences of new laws and more.\nInvestigative Journalism in the Public Interest\nSign up for ProPublica's Big Story newsletter and get our latest stories delivered straight to your inbox.", "date": "Thu, 10 Sep 2026 11:00:00 +0000"}, {"title": "25 Years After 9/11, Questions About the FBI\u2019s Pursuit of Saudi Suspects in the Case Have Only Grown", "url": "https://www.propublica.org/article/fbi-911-saudi-bayoumi-investigation", "body": "Investigative Journalism in the Public Interest\nInvestigative Journalism in the Public Interest\nInvestigative Journalism in the Public Interest\nProPublica is a nonprofit, investigative newsroom that exposes\n            corruption. We report in all 50 states andpartner with local newsrooms. Our work spursreal-world impactand has received numerous awards, including nine Pulitzer Prizes.\nHow Abortion Bans Lead to Preventable Deaths\nHow Recent Arrivals at the Border Have Changed the Country and Its Attitudes\nSCOTUS Justices\u2019 Beneficial Relationships With Billionaire Donors\nProPublica is a nonprofit newsroom that investigates abuses of power. Sign up to receiveour biggest storiesas soon as they\u2019re published.\nThese highlights were written by the reporters and editors who worked on this story.\nWithin a few days of the terror attacks that killed nearly 3,000 people on Sept. 11, 2001, FBI agents identified an important possible suspect: a middle-aged Saudi graduate student who provided crucial help to two of the al-Qaida hijackers as they settled in San Diego early the year before.\nThe Saudi, Omar al-Bayoumi, had since moved to England, and he was arrested there at the FBI\u2019s request. A police search of his home and office yielded a trove of evidence that quickly deepened investigators\u2019 suspicions about Bayoumi and his dealings with the terrorists. But what happened to much of that evidence after it was delivered to the FBI remains a mystery.\nBayoumi was questioned and released by British police and later returned to Saudi Arabia. For years thereafter, the FBI would insist he wasn\u2019t really a suspect at all \u2014 that Bayoumi had met the hijackers by chance, had helped them unwittingly and seemed nothing like a militant Islamist.\nAs the 25th anniversary of the 9/11 attacks is marked Friday, questions about how the FBI handled Bayoumi\u2019s case have only grown, driven in part by evidence that was seized from him in 2001 but not made public for years or even shared with field agents who sought to investigate his role.\nThat evidence and other FBI information, now filed in a federal lawsuit against the Saudi government by victims of the attacks, has undercut both Bayoumi\u2019s claims of innocence and the FBI\u2019s profile of him as a genial, pro-American civil servant. While he was living publicly as a student, it shows, Bayoumi worked closely with Saudi religious officials, operated as a Saudi intelligence asset and collaborated with several clerics who would later emerge as militant Islamists tied to al-Qaida.\nThis week, representatives of the 9/11 families are again demanding an end to what they have called the Justice Department\u2019s silence about what happened to the Bayoumi evidence and why he and other Saudis were not more aggressively pursued. The families have also questioned whether the Trump administration \u2014 which has strengthened U.S. diplomatic and business ties to the Saudi royal family \u2014 is committed to pursuing the truth about the possible role of Saudi officials.\n\u201cWe still have not been given the full picture of what happened,\u201d said Kathleen Zapata, whose father, Joseph Coppo, was killed in the World Trade Center. \u201cWhy are we having to fight against our own government simply to get answers about the most horrible attack ever on American soil?\u201d\nA White House spokesperson said, \u201cPresident Trump is leading the most transparent administration in history, and his team is working diligently on identifying records that are responsive to these families\u2019 request.\u201d A Justice Department spokesperson declined to comment, noting that many of the officials involved in the early 9/11 investigation no longer work for the government.\nWhile former Presidents Bill Clinton, George W. Bush, Barack Obama and Joe Biden are expected to attend a ceremony at the 9/11 memorial in New York, President Donald Trump has said he will commemorate the anniversary separately at the Pentagon.\nThe Saudi government has long denied any role in the attacks, often citing FBI and CIA statements that the royal family was an enemy of al-Qaida and its Saudi-born leader, Osama bin Laden. A spokesperson for the Saudi Embassy in Washington did not return messages asking for comment, including a request to speak to Bayoumi.\nA ProPublica examination of how the FBI handled the Bayoumi evidence shows that questions surrounding the Saudi connections to the 9/11 plot date to Jan. 15, 2000, when the first two hijackers landed in Los Angeles on a flight from Bangkok.\nThe CIA had followed the two men, Nawaf al-Hazmi and Khalid al-Mihdhar, as they met with other Qaida operatives in Malaysia days before. But the agency said it lost track of them when they flew to Thailand and then entered the United States using their real names and Saudi passports. (The CIA did not inform the FBI of their presence in the United States until Aug. 24, 2001, according to the report of the 9/11 Commission.)\nUnlike several Qaida operatives who led the hijacking teams, Mihdhar and Hazmi spoke no English and knew almost nothing of life in a Western society. The mastermind of the plot, Khalid Sheikh Mohammed, told CIA interrogators he advised them to seek help from Muslim communities in California, according to the 9/11 Commission. But the commission and many U.S. terrorism experts expressed skepticism that he would deploy such ill-equipped operatives without providing them with any contacts in the United States.\nBayoumi claimed his meeting with the hijackers was happenstance. After driving from San Diego on Feb. 1, 2000, he said, he had a meeting at the Saudi Consulate in Los Angeles and then went to a nearby halal cafe. There, he later told the FBI, he overheard Hazmi and Mihdhar speaking Gulf-accented Arabic and introduced himself. Seeking to be hospitable, he said, he told them they might want to try San Diego.\nShortly after the men stepped off a bus there three days later, Bayoumi said, he happened to run into them again at a local mosque. The next day, he arranged for them to rent an apartment in the building where he lived with his family, co-signed their lease and set up a bank account for them, briefly loaning them about $1,500 for the deposit. He went on to introduce them to more than two dozen local men who helped them in various ways.\nThe FBI had already looked closely at Bayoumi, launching a preliminary investigation in 1998 after neighbors reported what they said were suspicious gatherings of young Arab men at his apartment. That inquiry determined that Bayoumi, who had worked for the Saudi civil aviation ministry, was still receiving a generous government stipend through an aviation company for which he did no work. The investigation found no criminal activity, however, and it was closed after six months, officials said.\nBarely a week after the 9/11 attacks, the FBI asked the British authorities to arrest Bayoumi in Birmingham, England, where he had begun graduate business studies at Aston University.\nDavid Campbell, then a young terrorism investigator, was dispatched from London by the Metropolitan Police Service, which is responsible for counterterrorism efforts across Britain. He recalled flying back down the highway in a special police vehicle with his handcuffed prisoner in the back seat, the sirens blaring. \u201cThey\u2019d been told they had one of the 9/11 terrorists,\u201d he said in an interview.\nThe FBI sent three agents from the United States to assist with Bayoumi\u2019s interrogation at the Paddington Green police station in Central London. They were joined by a senior FBI attache, Joseph Hummell, who worked closely with the police service\u2019s\u00a0 Anti-Terrorist Branch, known as SO13.\nBut despite those close contacts and the FBI\u2019s considerable information about Bayoumi, very little of it was shared with Campbell and the Birmingham detective who joined him in questioning the suspect.\n\u201cWe were really in the dark,\u201d Campbell recalled. \u201cThey never even mentioned that he had been under investigation by the FBI in San Diego in 1998.\u201d\nNor, Campbell said, were the interrogators briefed about the materials that had just been taken from Bayoumi\u2019s Birmingham home and office, which included boxes of papers, correspondence, photographs, computer disks, videotapes and an address book. Yet some of those items had been immediately flagged as important, records show.\nAmong them was a diagram in Bayoumi\u2019s handwriting that appeared to calculate the trajectory of an airplane to a point on the horizon. Another was a video of Bayoumi touring and describing the U.S. Capitol, a building that had been identified by U.S. investigators as a prospective al-Qaida target.\nAfter questioning Bayoumi for almost a week, Campbell said, he and the Birmingham detective joined two senior SO13 officers on a conference call with Justice Department attorneys in the United States. The two interrogators were given a brief opportunity to express their view that Bayoumi\u2019s account seemed deeply suspicious and full of falsehoods, Campbell said.\nIt didn\u2019t seem to matter. The U.S. officials, who included lawyers in Washington and federal prosecutors in New York, concluded quickly that they did not have sufficient evidence to seek Bayoumi\u2019s extradition.\n\u201cWhen the call ended, we just looked at each other in complete disbelief,\u201d Campbell recalled.\nFBI documents declassified in response to a 2021 executive order by Biden give a partial answer to what happened to the Bayoumi evidence.\nOne memorandum on Oct. 11, 2001, notes that New Scotland Yard, as the Metropolitan Police Service was often called, had provided the FBI attache\u2019s office with \u201ccopies of all recovered exhibits, including mirror images of all computers.\u201d A complete copy of the trove was also sent via Federal Express to the FBI\u2019s New York Field Office, the memo noted.\nBy then, however, the agency\u2019s new director, Robert Mueller, had taken the unusual step of ordering that the 9/11 case be run out of its Washington headquarters. The Penttbomteam, as it became known in the FBI\u2019s clunky acronym for Pentagon/Twin Towers Bombing, mobilized thousands of FBI agents and analysts around the world.\nOne group focused on the hijackers of American Airlines Flight 77, the plane that crashed into the Pentagon, tracing the path of Hazmi and Mihdhar from Southern California to Dulles International Airport in Virginia, where they abandoned a used Toyota Corolla registered to one of their former addresses in San Diego.\nHow the headquarters team translated and analyzed the materials obtained from Bayoumi in Britain is unclear. But the team did not appear to share the evidence widely. In another memo, dated May 15, 2002, the San Diego FBI office noted that it was asked to review only five of the more than 80 VHS tapes seized from Bayoumi in Birmingham. Two of those were duplicates and included footage of a paintball fight and a family trip to Sea World. \u201cNothing of significant investigative/evidential value has been discovered,\u201d the memo stated.\nAlthough Bayoumi\u2019s friends and associates helped the hijackers with everything from part-time jobs to flying lessons (they flunked out because their English was so poor), FBI leaders dismissed the idea that any of the people who helped them knew they were Qaida operatives sent on a still-evolving terrorist mission.\n\u201cAs far as we know, they contacted no known terrorist sympathizers in the United States,\u201dMueller told a joint panelof the Senate and House intelligence committees in September 2002. \u201cTo this day, we have found no one in the United States except the actual hijackers who knew of the plot.\u201d\nThe FBI\u2019s more conclusive account of its Penttbom findings came before the bipartisan 9/11 Commission. The young agent leading the Flight 77 investigation team, Jacqueline Maguire, echoed Mueller\u2019s earlier testimony, saying in 2004 that Bayoumi\u2019s meeting with the California hijackers had been \u201ca random encounter\u201d and that he had helped them without knowing their true aim. Maguire also said the FBI had no indication the hijackers had initially sought out another figure who raised suspicions, Fahad al-Thumairy, the imam of the Saudi-built King Fahd Mosque in Culver City, California.\nMichael Jacobson, a former State Department counterterrorism official who was a key commission investigator, said he knew some FBI agents in California were convinced that Bayoumi and Thumairy had helped the hijackers deliberately. But Maguire and her boss, Mary Galligan, who oversaw the Penttbom team, \u201cwere both consistently pushing back hard on any narrative of a support network,\u201d Jacobson said in an interview.\nMaguire and Galligan declined to comment.\nWhen investigators from the 9/11 Commission interviewed Bayoumi and Thumairy in Saudi Arabia in 2003, Maguire \u2014 who had already interviewed the two Saudis for the FBI months earlier \u2014 joined them as an official escort. The interviews, which were managed by Saudi intelligence officials, were notable for Bayoumi\u2019s efforts to ingratiate himself and Thumairy\u2019s seemingly brazen lies, former officials said. Thumairy said he had never met the hijackers and did not even know Bayoumi, despite telephone records showing numerous calls between them. The Saudi Embassy did not respond to a request to speak to Thumairy.\nIn its final report, the commission said it could not confirm that Thumairy helped the two terrorists but found it was \u201cfairly certain that Hazmi and Mihdhar spent time at the King Fahd mosque and made some acquaintances there.\u201d\nEchoing the FBI\u2019s assessment, the commission described Bayoumi as \u201ca devout Muslim, obliging and gregarious,\u201d and found no evidence he \u201cbelieved in violent extremism or knowingly aided extremist groups.\u201d It would take years before those conclusions were forcefully challenged.\nDaniel Gonzalez, a San Diego FBI agent who had been looking at Hazmi and Mihdhar\u2019s circle since the day after the attacks, became the case agent for Operation Encore, a \u201csubfile\u201d or follow-on inquiry to the original 9/11 case. But while he and other agents made significant breakthroughs, former officials said, they faced constant skepticism from FBI headquarters and were sometimes blocked outright in their efforts to pursue the case.\nIn 2007, in an interview with a key source in Jordan, Gonzalez learned of two other important witnesses in Los Angeles, young Muslim immigrants who had also helped the hijackers. The two later confirmed the hijackers had gone to the King Fahd Mosque after arriving in Los Angeles and met repeatedly with Thumairy. One of the men said Thumairy also arranged for the hijackers to be driven to their first meeting with Bayoumi at the halal cafe \u2014 undercutting Bayoumi\u2019s claim that he met them by chance.\nFor years, Encore agents said, they continued to press for more of the materials seized from Bayoumi in Britain. At one point, Gonzalez got a phone call from Washington asking if he wanted some old Penttbom evidence that was about to be destroyed. The materials \u2014 copies of some of Bayoumi\u2019s long-sought papers, including the suspicious flight diagram \u2014 stunned the Encore team, Gonzalez said.\nIn June 2012, a small group of Encore investigators and analysts flew to London to try to review the Bayoumi search evidence more thoroughly. According to three former officials familiar with the trip, they viewed dozens of hours of videotapes and took new copies of much of the Metropolitan Police material.\nFor reasons that remain unclear, however, the team either did not see or did not focus on a few videotapes that would later become evidence in the 9/11 families\u2019 lawsuit, including the tape of Bayoumi at the U.S. Capitol. While some terrorism experts have interpreted the recording as Bayoumi\u2019s effort to \u201ccase\u201d the site for a possible attack, lawyers for the Saudi government described it as a tourist video.\nThe Bayoumi videos also included extensive footage of two Saudi religious officials who not long before had commanded attention from both the FBI and CIA. The two men, Adel al-Sadhan and Mutaeb al-Sudairy, were emissaries of the Saudi Ministry of Islamic Affairs who had traced what intelligence officials saw as a strikingly suspicious path around the United States. The Saudi Embassy did not respond to a request to speak to the men.\nIn addition to traveling to Washington, D.C., with Bayoumi, the two clerics visited Los Angeles and San Diego a year before the hijackers and stayed at one of the homes where Hazmi and Mihdhar later lived. Sudairy also spent several months living in Columbia, Missouri, with an American al-Qaida member, Ziyad Khaleel, who provided Osama bin Laden with the satellite phone that FBI officials said he used to orchestrate the 1998 bombings of U.S. embassies in Kenya and Tanzania.\nJust before and after the hijackers arrived in San Diego, telephone records show, Bayoumi also made a series of calls to Sudairy in Washington, Thumairy in Los Angeles and Anwar al-Aulaqi, a Yemeni American cleric. Aulaqi, who ran a San Diego mosque that Bayoumi had funded through Saudi donations, was described by FBI witnesses as a spiritual adviser to Hazmi and Mihdhar. Aulaqi later emerged as an important al-Qaida leader before he was killed in a U.S. drone strike in 2011.\nIn 2014 and 2015, a panel of security experts was assembled to review the FBI\u2019s implementation of reforms called for by the 9/11 Commission. The 9/11 Review Commission, as the second panel was called, was also tasked with looking again at questions about the 9/11 plot that remained unanswered.\nMaguire, the FBI\u2019s primary liaison to the review panel, again disputed suspicions about Bayoumi. \u201cHe came here for school and everything seems accidental with Bayoumi,\u201d the commission was told in a 2014 briefing led by Maguire.\nThe review panel concluded there was not enough new information to revise the 9/11 Commission\u2019s finding that there was no \u201cwitting assistance\u201d to the hijackers. But Tim Roemer \u2014 a Democratic former congressman from Indiana who served on the 2002 joint intelligence inquiry, the 9/11 Commission and the Review Commission \u2014 made a point of pressing for further investigation into the Saudi question. He was struck that Maguire and other FBI officials were still pushing back.\n\u201cThere was consistent feedback from some levels of the FBI that simply wanted to close the case and claim that what Bayoumi did was purely coincidence and somehow accidental,\u201d Roemer said.\nRoemer insisted that the Review Commission formally recommend that the FBI continue to examine the Saudi issue. But shortly after it did so, officials at FBI headquarters renewed their pressure to shut down Operation Encore. In 2016, the head of the agency\u2019s Joint Terrorism Task Forces finally relented, reassigning the case and disbanding the team that had been investigating it for years.\nThe next year, a pair of memorandums sent by the FBI\u2019s Washington field office confirmed something that the Encore agents had long suspected: Between the late 1990s and Sept. 11, 2001, Bayoumi was paid a monthly stipend as a co-optee, or part-time agent, of the Saudi intelligence services.\nBy then, Gonzalez had retired. The Encore investigators who remained were working new jobs. Along with other documents from the FBI investigation, the memos were initially withheld from the 9/11 families by the Justice Department, which classified them as state secrets during Trump\u2019s first term. The Encore agents saw the memos for the first time when they were finally made public in 2022 under Biden\u2019s executive order.\n\u201cSomebody needs to explain to the American people why we didn\u2019t see this information before,\u201d Gonzalez said in an interview.\nTim Golden is a reporter at ProPublica, concentrating on national security, foreign policy and criminal justice.\nCreative Commons License (CC BY-NC-ND 3.0)\nThank you for your interest in republishing this story. You are free to republish it so long as you do the following:\nWithin a few days of the terror attacks that killed nearly 3,000 people on Sept. 11, 2001, FBI agents identified an important possible suspect: a middle-aged Saudi graduate student who provided crucial help to two of the al-Qaida hijackers as they settled in San Diego early the year before.\nThe Saudi, Omar al-Bayoumi, had since moved to England, and he was arrested there at the FBI\u2019s request. A police search of his home and office yielded a trove of evidence that quickly deepened investigators\u2019 suspicions about Bayoumi and his dealings with the terrorists. But what happened to much of that evidence after it was delivered to the FBI remains a mystery.\nBayoumi was questioned and released by British police and later returned to Saudi Arabia. For years thereafter, the FBI would insist he wasn\u2019t really a suspect at all \u2014 that Bayoumi had met the hijackers by chance, had helped them unwittingly and seemed nothing like a militant Islamist.\nAs the 25th anniversary of the 9/11 attacks is marked Friday, questions about how the FBI handled Bayoumi\u2019s case have only grown, driven in part by evidence that was seized from him in 2001 but not made public for years or even shared with field agents who sought to investigate his role.\nThat evidence and other FBI information, now filed in a federal lawsuit against the Saudi government by victims of the attacks, has undercut both Bayoumi\u2019s claims of innocence and the FBI\u2019s profile of him as a genial, pro-American civil servant. While he was living publicly as a student, it shows, Bayoumi worked closely with Saudi religious officials, operated as a Saudi intelligence asset and collaborated with several clerics who would later emerge as militant Islamists tied to al-Qaida.\nThis week, representatives of the 9/11 families are again demanding an end to what they have called the Justice Department\u2019s silence about what happened to the Bayoumi evidence and why he and other Saudis were not more aggressively pursued. The families have also questioned whether the Trump administration \u2014 which has strengthened U.S. diplomatic and business ties to the Saudi royal family \u2014 is committed to pursuing the truth about the possible role of Saudi officials.\n\u201cWe still have not been given the full picture of what happened,\u201d said Kathleen Zapata, whose father, Joseph Coppo, was killed in the World Trade Center. \u201cWhy are we having to fight against our own government simply to get answers about the most horrible attack ever on American soil?\u201d\nA White House spokesperson said, \u201cPresident Trump is leading the most transparent administration in history, and his team is working diligently on identifying records that are responsive to these families\u2019 request.\u201d A Justice Department spokesperson declined to comment, noting that many of the officials involved in the early 9/11 investigation no longer work for the government.\nWhile former Presidents Bill Clinton, George W. Bush, Barack Obama and Joe Biden are expected to attend a ceremony at the 9/11 memorial in New York, President Donald Trump has said he will commemorate the anniversary separately at the Pentagon.\nThe Saudi government has long denied any role in the attacks, often citing FBI and CIA statements that the royal family was an enemy of al-Qaida and its Saudi-born leader, Osama bin Laden. A spokesperson for the Saudi Embassy in Washington did not return messages asking for comment, including a request to speak to Bayoumi.\nA ProPublica examination of how the FBI handled the Bayoumi evidence shows that questions surrounding the Saudi connections to the 9/11 plot date to Jan. 15, 2000, when the first two hijackers landed in Los Angeles on a flight from Bangkok.\nThe CIA had followed the two men, Nawaf al-Hazmi and Khalid al-Mihdhar, as they met with other Qaida operatives in Malaysia days before. But the agency said it lost track of them when they flew to Thailand and then entered the United States using their real names and Saudi passports. (The CIA did not inform the FBI of their presence in the United States until Aug. 24, 2001, according to the report of the 9/11 Commission.)\nUnlike several Qaida operatives who led the hijacking teams, Mihdhar and Hazmi spoke no English and knew almost nothing of life in a Western society. The mastermind of the plot, Khalid Sheikh Mohammed, told CIA interrogators he advised them to seek help from Muslim communities in California, according to the 9/11 Commission. But the commission and many U.S. terrorism experts expressed skepticism that he would deploy such ill-equipped operatives without providing them with any contacts in the United States.\nBayoumi claimed his meeting with the hijackers was happenstance. After driving from San Diego on Feb. 1, 2000, he said, he had a meeting at the Saudi Consulate in Los Angeles and then went to a nearby halal cafe. There, he later told the FBI, he overheard Hazmi and Mihdhar speaking Gulf-accented Arabic and introduced himself. Seeking to be hospitable, he said, he told them they might want to try San Diego.\nShortly after the men stepped off a bus there three days later, Bayoumi said, he happened to run into them again at a local mosque. The next day, he arranged for them to rent an apartment in the building where he lived with his family, co-signed their lease and set up a bank account for them, briefly loaning them about $1,500 for the deposit. He went on to introduce them to more than two dozen local men who helped them in various ways.\nThe FBI had already looked closely at Bayoumi, launching a preliminary investigation in 1998 after neighbors reported what they said were suspicious gatherings of young Arab men at his apartment. That inquiry determined that Bayoumi, who had worked for the Saudi civil aviation ministry, was still receiving a generous government stipend through an aviation company for which he did no work. The investigation found no criminal activity, however, and it was closed after six months, officials said.\nBarely a week after the 9/11 attacks, the FBI asked the British authorities to arrest Bayoumi in Birmingham, England, where he had begun graduate business studies at Aston University.\nDavid Campbell, then a young terrorism investigator, was dispatched from London by the Metropolitan Police Service, which is responsible for counterterrorism efforts across Britain. He recalled flying back down the highway in a special police vehicle with his handcuffed prisoner in the back seat, the sirens blaring. \u201cThey\u2019d been told they had one of the 9/11 terrorists,\u201d he said in an interview.\nThe FBI sent three agents from the United States to assist with Bayoumi\u2019s interrogation at the Paddington Green police station in Central London. They were joined by a senior FBI attache, Joseph Hummell, who worked closely with the police service\u2019s\u00a0 Anti-Terrorist Branch, known as SO13.\nBut despite those close contacts and the FBI\u2019s considerable information about Bayoumi, very little of it was shared with Campbell and the Birmingham detective who joined him in questioning the suspect.\n\u201cWe were really in the dark,\u201d Campbell recalled. \u201cThey never even mentioned that he had been under investigation by the FBI in San Diego in 1998.\u201d\nNor, Campbell said, were the interrogators briefed about the materials that had just been taken from Bayoumi\u2019s Birmingham home and office, which included boxes of papers, correspondence, photographs, computer disks, videotapes and an address book. Yet some of those items had been immediately flagged as important, records show.\nAmong them was a diagram in Bayoumi\u2019s handwriting that appeared to calculate the trajectory of an airplane to a point on the horizon. Another was a video of Bayoumi touring and describing the U.S. Capitol, a building that had been identified by U.S. investigators as a prospective al-Qaida target.\nAfter questioning Bayoumi for almost a week, Campbell said, he and the Birmingham detective joined two senior SO13 officers on a conference call with Justice Department attorneys in the United States. The two interrogators were given a brief opportunity to express their view that Bayoumi\u2019s account seemed deeply suspicious and full of falsehoods, Campbell said.\nIt didn\u2019t seem to matter. The U.S. officials, who included lawyers in Washington and federal prosecutors in New York, concluded quickly that they did not have sufficient evidence to seek Bayoumi\u2019s extradition.\n\u201cWhen the call ended, we just looked at each other in complete disbelief,\u201d Campbell recalled.\nFBI documents declassified in response to a 2021 executive order by Biden give a partial answer to what happened to the Bayoumi evidence.\nOne memorandum on Oct. 11, 2001, notes that New Scotland Yard, as the Metropolitan Police Service was often called, had provided the FBI attache\u2019s office with \u201ccopies of all recovered exhibits, including mirror images of all computers.\u201d A complete copy of the trove was also sent via Federal Express to the FBI\u2019s New York Field Office, the memo noted.\nBy then, however, the agency\u2019s new director, Robert Mueller, had taken the unusual step of ordering that the 9/11 case be run out of its Washington headquarters. The Penttbomteam, as it became known in the FBI\u2019s clunky acronym for Pentagon/Twin Towers Bombing, mobilized thousands of FBI agents and analysts around the world.\nOne group focused on the hijackers of American Airlines Flight 77, the plane that crashed into the Pentagon, tracing the path of Hazmi and Mihdhar from Southern California to Dulles International Airport in Virginia, where they abandoned a used Toyota Corolla registered to one of their former addresses in San Diego.\nHow the headquarters team translated and analyzed the materials obtained from Bayoumi in Britain is unclear. But the team did not appear to share the evidence widely. In another memo, dated May 15, 2002, the San Diego FBI office noted that it was asked to review only five of the more than 80 VHS tapes seized from Bayoumi in Birmingham. Two of those were duplicates and included footage of a paintball fight and a family trip to Sea World. \u201cNothing of significant investigative/evidential value has been discovered,\u201d the memo stated.\nAlthough Bayoumi\u2019s friends and associates helped the hijackers with everything from part-time jobs to flying lessons (they flunked out because their English was so poor), FBI leaders dismissed the idea that any of the people who helped them knew they were Qaida operatives sent on a still-evolving terrorist mission.\n\u201cAs far as we know, they contacted no known terrorist sympathizers in the United States,\u201dMueller told a joint panelof the Senate and House intelligence committees in September 2002. \u201cTo this day, we have found no one in the United States except the actual hijackers who knew of the plot.\u201d\nThe FBI\u2019s more conclusive account of its Penttbom findings came before the bipartisan 9/11 Commission. The young agent leading the Flight 77 investigation team, Jacqueline Maguire, echoed Mueller\u2019s earlier testimony, saying in 2004 that Bayoumi\u2019s meeting with the California hijackers had been \u201ca random encounter\u201d and that he had helped them without knowing their true aim. Maguire also said the FBI had no indication the hijackers had initially sought out another figure who raised suspicions, Fahad al-Thumairy, the imam of the Saudi-built King Fahd Mosque in Culver City, California.\nMichael Jacobson, a former State Department counterterrorism official who was a key commission investigator, said he knew some FBI agents in California were convinced that Bayoumi and Thumairy had helped the hijackers deliberately. But Maguire and her boss, Mary Galligan, who oversaw the Penttbom team, \u201cwere both consistently pushing back hard on any narrative of a support network,\u201d Jacobson said in an interview.\nMaguire and Galligan declined to comment.\nWhen investigators from the 9/11 Commission interviewed Bayoumi and Thumairy in Saudi Arabia in 2003, Maguire \u2014 who had already interviewed the two Saudis for the FBI months earlier \u2014 joined them as an official escort. The interviews, which were managed by Saudi intelligence officials, were notable for Bayoumi\u2019s efforts to ingratiate himself and Thumairy\u2019s seemingly brazen lies, former officials said. Thumairy said he had never met the hijackers and did not even know Bayoumi, despite telephone records showing numerous calls between them. The Saudi Embassy did not respond to a request to speak to Thumairy.\nIn its final report, the commission said it could not confirm that Thumairy helped the two terrorists but found it was \u201cfairly certain that Hazmi and Mihdhar spent time at the King Fahd mosque and made some acquaintances there.\u201d\nEchoing the FBI\u2019s assessment, the commission described Bayoumi as \u201ca devout Muslim, obliging and gregarious,\u201d and found no evidence he \u201cbelieved in violent extremism or knowingly aided extremist groups.\u201d It would take years before those conclusions were forcefully challenged.\nDaniel Gonzalez, a San Diego FBI agent who had been looking at Hazmi and Mihdhar\u2019s circle since the day after the attacks, became the case agent for Operation Encore, a \u201csubfile\u201d or follow-on inquiry to the original 9/11 case. But while he and other agents made significant breakthroughs, former officials said, they faced constant skepticism from FBI headquarters and were sometimes blocked outright in their efforts to pursue the case.\nIn 2007, in an interview with a key source in Jordan, Gonzalez learned of two other important witnesses in Los Angeles, young Muslim immigrants who had also helped the hijackers. The two later confirmed the hijackers had gone to the King Fahd Mosque after arriving in Los Angeles and met repeatedly with Thumairy. One of the men said Thumairy also arranged for the hijackers to be driven to their first meeting with Bayoumi at the halal cafe \u2014 undercutting Bayoumi\u2019s claim that he met them by chance.\nFor years, Encore agents said, they continued to press for more of the materials seized from Bayoumi in Britain. At one point, Gonzalez got a phone call from Washington asking if he wanted some old Penttbom evidence that was about to be destroyed. The materials \u2014 copies of some of Bayoumi\u2019s long-sought papers, including the suspicious flight diagram \u2014 stunned the Encore team, Gonzalez said.\nIn June 2012, a small group of Encore investigators and analysts flew to London to try to review the Bayoumi search evidence more thoroughly. According to three former officials familiar with the trip, they viewed dozens of hours of videotapes and took new copies of much of the Metropolitan Police material.\nFor reasons that remain unclear, however, the team either did not see or did not focus on a few videotapes that would later become evidence in the 9/11 families\u2019 lawsuit, including the tape of Bayoumi at the U.S. Capitol. While some terrorism experts have interpreted the recording as Bayoumi\u2019s effort to \u201ccase\u201d the site for a possible attack, lawyers for the Saudi government described it as a tourist video.\nThe Bayoumi videos also included extensive footage of two Saudi religious officials who not long before had commanded attention from both the FBI and CIA. The two men, Adel al-Sadhan and Mutaeb al-Sudairy, were emissaries of the Saudi Ministry of Islamic Affairs who had traced what intelligence officials saw as a strikingly suspicious path around the United States. The Saudi Embassy did not respond to a request to speak to the men.\nIn addition to traveling to Washington, D.C., with Bayoumi, the two clerics visited Los Angeles and San Diego a year before the hijackers and stayed at one of the homes where Hazmi and Mihdhar later lived. Sudairy also spent several months living in Columbia, Missouri, with an American al-Qaida member, Ziyad Khaleel, who provided Osama bin Laden with the satellite phone that FBI officials said he used to orchestrate the 1998 bombings of U.S. embassies in Kenya and Tanzania.\nJust before and after the hijackers arrived in San Diego, telephone records show, Bayoumi also made a series of calls to Sudairy in Washington, Thumairy in Los Angeles and Anwar al-Aulaqi, a Yemeni American cleric. Aulaqi, who ran a San Diego mosque that Bayoumi had funded through Saudi donations, was described by FBI witnesses as a spiritual adviser to Hazmi and Mihdhar. Aulaqi later emerged as an important al-Qaida leader before he was killed in a U.S. drone strike in 2011.\nIn 2014 and 2015, a panel of security experts was assembled to review the FBI\u2019s implementation of reforms called for by the 9/11 Commission. The 9/11 Review Commission, as the second panel was called, was also tasked with looking again at questions about the 9/11 plot that remained unanswered.\nMaguire, the FBI\u2019s primary liaison to the review panel, again disputed suspicions about Bayoumi. \u201cHe came here for school and everything seems accidental with Bayoumi,\u201d the commission was told in a 2014 briefing led by Maguire.\nThe review panel concluded there was not enough new information to revise the 9/11 Commission\u2019s finding that there was no \u201cwitting assistance\u201d to the hijackers. But Tim Roemer \u2014 a Democratic former congressman from Indiana who served on the 2002 joint intelligence inquiry, the 9/11 Commission and the Review Commission \u2014 made a point of pressing for further investigation into the Saudi question. He was struck that Maguire and other FBI officials were still pushing back.\n\u201cThere was consistent feedback from some levels of the FBI that simply wanted to close the case and claim that what Bayoumi did was purely coincidence and somehow accidental,\u201d Roemer said.\nRoemer insisted that the Review Commission formally recommend that the FBI continue to examine the Saudi issue. But shortly after it did so, officials at FBI headquarters renewed their pressure to shut down Operation Encore. In 2016, the head of the agency\u2019s Joint Terrorism Task Forces finally relented, reassigning the case and disbanding the team that had been investigating it for years.\nThe next year, a pair of memorandums sent by the FBI\u2019s Washington field office confirmed something that the Encore agents had long suspected: Between the late 1990s and Sept. 11, 2001, Bayoumi was paid a monthly stipend as a co-optee, or part-time agent, of the Saudi intelligence services.\nBy then, Gonzalez had retired. The Encore investigators who remained were working new jobs. Along with other documents from the FBI investigation, the memos were initially withheld from the 9/11 families by the Justice Department, which classified them as state secrets during Trump\u2019s first term. The Encore agents saw the memos for the first time when they were finally made public in 2022 under Biden\u2019s executive order.\n\u201cSomebody needs to explain to the American people why we didn\u2019t see this information before,\u201d Gonzalez said in an interview.\nThe story you just read was made possible by our readers. We hope it inspires you to support ProPublica, so we can continue producing investigations that shine a light on power, uncover the truth and drive real change.\nProPublica is a nonprofit newsroom dedicated to nonpartisan, evidence-based journalism that holds power accountable. Founded in 2008 to address the decline in investigative reporting, we\u2019ve spent over 15 years uncovering injustice, corruption and abuse of power \u2014 work that is slow, expensive and more critical than ever to our democracy. With nine Pulitzer Prizes and reforms sparked in state and local governments, businesses, institutions and more, our reporting ensures that the public interest comes first.\nToday, the stakes are higher than ever. From ethics in our government offices, to reproductive health care, to the climate crisis and beyond, ProPublica remains on the front lines of the stories that matter most. Your gift helps us keep the powerful accountable and the truth accessible.\nJoin over 90,000 supporters nationwidein standing up for investigative journalism that informs, inspires and creates lasting impact. Thank you for making this work possible.\nSupportIndependentJournalism:\nAs a nonprofit newsroom, we rely on donations from individuals like you to fuel our work.Your support today will directly fund investigations that shine a light on urgent issues:corporations prioritizing profits over public safety, health insurers denying coverage to patients, unintended consequences of new laws and more.\nInvestigative Journalism in the Public Interest\nSign up for ProPublica's Big Story newsletter and get our latest stories delivered straight to your inbox.", "date": "Thu, 10 Sep 2026 10:00:00 +0000"}, {"title": "Got a Connection to Dominican Baseball? Text Us on WhatsApp.", "url": "https://www.propublica.org/getinvolved/dominican-republic-baseball-whatsapp-line", "body": "Investigative Journalism in the Public Interest\nInvestigative Journalism in the Public Interest\nInvestigative Journalism in the Public Interest\nProPublica is a nonprofit, investigative newsroom that exposes\n            corruption. We report in all 50 states andpartner with local newsrooms. Our work spursreal-world impactand has received numerous awards, including nine Pulitzer Prizes.\nHow Abortion Bans Lead to Preventable Deaths\nHow Recent Arrivals at the Border Have Changed the Country and Its Attitudes\nSCOTUS Justices\u2019 Beneficial Relationships With Billionaire Donors\nThe Dominican Baseball Factory\nMajor League Baseball teams sign more players from the Dominican Republic each year than any nation besides the U.S. Almost every neighborhood is home to a baseball field where kids as young as 4 begin learning to play. In our months of reporting, we\u2019ve rarely come across anyone in the Dominican who doesn\u2019t have some connection to the sport: a sibling or uncle or son who aspires to be a professional player, or who once did.\nAs beloved and important as baseball is in the Dominican, industry practices can hurt young players and their families. Dozens of trainers and players have told us that adolescents are expected to perform at levels never asked of their peers from other countries and enter into agreements with teams at much earlier ages. (We just published a storyabout abusive moneylending practicesthat have sprouted from this system.) We\u2019re planning more stories brought to our attention by this community.\nWe want this work to reach people whose lives are touched by the issues we\u2019re investigating. If that\u2019s you, we want you to be able to easily connect with our reporting team and share tips or experiences you think we should know about Dominican baseball. The more people we hear from, the more impactful our reporting on these complex issues can be. (Learn more about ProPublica and our mission.)\nFor this series, we\u2019re opening a WhatsApp line because it\u2019s a popular way to keep in touch in the Dominican, and international calls and texts are free.All you have to do istext \u201cbaseball\u201d to our WhatsApp number, 1-917-207-6447.\nIf you\u2019re not a WhatsApp user, if you can still get in touch with our reporting team via email:\nIf you would prefer another way to get in touch,see our advice here.\nYou\u2019ll get a link to our latest stories about baseball. We\u2019ll also ask about your connection to baseball and ask you to share anything that you think our reporters should look into as we continue investigating.\nIf you give us permission, we\u2019ll also send you quick updates from our series every now and then.\nWe want to hear about baseball-related issues and experiences that are important to you, whether they\u2019re connected to finances, health, education or something else. Below are a few of the topics we\u2019re already reporting on; if you have related experiences to share, we would appreciate hearing about them.\nIf you have a connection to baseball \u2014 whether you\u2019re a current or former player, trainer, parent of a player, doctor who treats athletes, someone who works for an MLB team or you have some other link \u2014 we would love to hear from you.\nYes, we\u2019ll share updates from our investigations, along with links to our latest reporting.\nPleasesend them a link to our baseball seriesor have them text \u201cbaseball\u201d to our WhatsApp number, 1-917-207-6447. And thank you \u2014 we need to reach as many people as we can.\nWe will read everything you send and follow up with you if we have further questions or are interested in including something you\u2019ve shared in our investigations.\nWe take your privacy very seriously. Our reporters speak to people every day who want to share information but fear retribution. Our reputation is built on protecting sources, and we take that responsibility to heart. You can request to speak on background, which means you would not be named in our stories.\nNo.\nMelody KramerandKassie Navarrocontributed research.\nI\u2019m an engagement reporter focused on workers\u2019 rights, immigration and the federal government.\nI\u2019m working on ProPublica\u2019s initiative to gather tips from federal workers across agencies. If you have ideas on how we can engage better, reach out. I\u2019m also interested in workplace safety, OSHA and disability rights.\nI am an investigative reporter specializing in sports.\nInvestigative Journalism in the Public Interest\nSign up for ProPublica's Big Story newsletter and get our latest stories delivered straight to your inbox.", "date": "Thu, 10 Sep 2026 09:04:00 +0000"}, {"title": "The Rising Baseball Star, a Fatal Car Crash and the Fixer", "url": "https://www.propublica.org/article/oneil-cruz-pittsburgh-pirates-santo-caraballo-car-crash-payments-dominican-republic", "body": "Investigative Journalism in the Public Interest\nProPublica is a nonprofit, investigative newsroom that exposes\n            corruption. We report in all 50 states andpartner with local newsrooms. Our work spursreal-world impactand has received numerous awards, including nine Pulitzer Prizes.\nHow Abortion Bans Lead to Preventable Deaths\nHow Recent Arrivals at the Border Have Changed the Country and Its Attitudes\nSCOTUS Justices\u2019 Beneficial Relationships With Billionaire Donors\nThe Dominican Baseball Factory\nProPublica is a nonprofit newsroom that investigates abuses of power. Sign up to get updates on this reporting throughWhatsApporour newsletter.\nThese highlights were written by the reporters and editors who worked on this story.\nAround 4 a.m. on Sept. 21, 2020, Luis David Saba arrived at a desolate stretch of highway along the southern coast of the Dominican Republic. Police and paramedics had left by then, taking with them a Jeep Grand Cherokee with a crumpled hood and the tangled remains of the cheap motorcycle it had smashed into. Also gone were the motorcycle\u2019s two critically injured passengers. The only signs that an accident had occurred a few hours earlier were the blood and debris smeared across the asphalt and a pile of neatly placed branches.\nSaba knew what to expect underneath that pile. It was a Dominican custom to use anything on hand to shroud a body abandoned by paramedics. Pushing away the branches, Saba saw shredded clothes, torn skin and long, curly hair forming a halo over a battered face. He covered the body of his 20-year-old younger brother, Yohan Saba Mercado, with a sheet. In shock, he felt nothing but the need to get his brother out of the road.\nSaba sat and waited for forensic investigators he was told were on their way. Hours passed, the sun rose and traffic began to stream by them. Finally, he gave up. With the help of friends, he loaded his brother\u2019s body into a van, and hours later it was at his mother\u2019s house.\nLearn more about this story on ProPublica\u2019s podcast \u201cPaper Trail.\u201d\nLater that morning, two other families were experiencing similar horrors. Deby Beato Charles, 20, and Daniela Perez Garcia, 19, had been riding on the back of Yohan\u2019s motorcycle when the Jeep rear-ended them. When Deby\u2019s older brother, Ruben, got to a nearby hospital, he was met by a doctor who handed him a wallet and bracelet. Deby had died of a severe head injury and blunt chest trauma. At another hospital, Daniela\u2019s uncle was ushered into a room where his niece lay. Her face was so swollen he didn\u2019t recognize her. According to her death certificate, she had suffered injuries to her head, chest and hip and went into hypovolemic shock, in which the loss of bodily fluids causes a person\u2019s organs to shut down.\nThe families of Daniela and Yohan held funerals in their homes that afternoon. During the proceedings, word spread about who had been driving the Grand Cherokee. It was Oneil Cruz, a \u201cpelotero,\u201d a ballplayer, with a fortune and a future, the 21-year-old scion of a local baseball family, a top prospect in the Pittsburgh Pirates organization. Seemingly everyone in the area knew how much he received from his major league signing bonus five years earlier: $950,000.\nThe families also knew what was going to happen next.\nYohan\u2019s mother told me that after all the mourners left the house, a driver showed up holding out a phone. On the line was Cruz\u2019s mother offering condolences \u2014 and wanting to negotiate.\nLess than 7 miles from the site of the accident is the striving farm town of Nizao. Even though it has a population of just 32,000, Nizao has produced dozens of professional ballplayers, including three-time All-Star Ketel Marte and, most famously, Hall of Famer Vladimir Guerrero Sr., one of the best hitters in modern baseball and father of Vladimir Jr., the star first baseman for the Toronto Blue Jays. Looming over the main road into town is the Guerreros\u2019 mansion. Right below it, a giant 27 has been carved into the hill \u2014 the jersey number the two share.\nBy 2020, Oneil Cruz Ure\u00f1a looked poised to be among the best of Nizao\u2019s ballplayers. The Los Angeles Dodgers signed him when he was 16 and over 6 feet tall. They traded him to the Pittsburgh Pirates, who considered him their future shortstop. The statistics website FanGraphs ranked him asone of baseball\u2019s top prospects, declaring that \u201cthere aren\u2019t many who have a chance to be what this guy might.\u201d\nBut the accident on a darkened road threatened to destroy that chance before Cruz even made it to the major leagues. Police detained him at the scene. In a court filing the following day, prosecutors said that they had opened an investigation \u201cen contra del imputado,\u201d \u201cagainst the accused.\u201d They sought \u201ccoercive measures,\u201d a mechanism in Dominican law by which a suspect can be forced to remain in custody while an investigation continues. In a press release, they alleged that Cruz \u201chad consumed alcoholic beverages prior to the accident.\u201d\nThe scandalmade headlinesin the Dominican Republic and the United States, and then, in a matter of days, it evaporated from the public eye. How that happened has been unknown, until now.\nI\u2019d heard about the Cruz case last year when I began reporting on the booming industry of \u201cprestamistas,\u201d moneylenders, who exploit major league prospects in the Dominican Republic by getting their undereducated parents to give up sizable portions of their signing bonuses. The case\u2019s disappearance, I was told, marked the origin story of the most dominant prestamista in the country\u2019s chaotic baseball ecosystem: Santo Caraballo. At the time, Caraballo was an obscure businessman with political connections. He rose to prominence after forming a business partnership with national hero David Ortiz, the retired Boston Red Sox great who remains an influential figure in Major League Baseball. (In a statement, Ortiz said his business relationship with Caraballo \u201cbegan to come to an end\u201d more than 18 months ago.) I wrote about Caraballo\u2019s practices in \u201cThe Dominican Baseball Factory.\u201d\nTo find out how Caraballo made the case against Cruz go away, I interviewed more than 30 people in the Dominican Republic. Some were afraid to talk, but others, primarily family members of the victims, were eager to speak publicly for the first time. I also obtained hundreds of pages of previously unreported documents, including court records and bank receipts, as well as voice notes. I made numerous attempts to reach Caraballo about his role, including sending him a summary of my findings. He never responded.\nThe Dominican court system gives victims or their families significant input on whether to pursue a prosecution. Caraballo oversaw a campaign to persuade the families of the three victims to accept cash in return for signing documents withdrawing all \u201cpenal, civil or judicial\u201d claims against Cruz.\nThe effort rescued Cruz\u2019s baseball career \u2014 he is now the starting center fielder for the Pirates \u2014 and it launched Caraballo\u2019s. He became a ubiquitous operator in the sport, feared for the sort of high-powered connections that could make a deadly crash disappear. But for some of the victims\u2019 relatives, the money they received \u2014 roughly $66,000 in total for the three families \u2014 hasn\u2019t lasted nearly as long as the hurt: six years of unresolved grief, resentment and regret that they allowed their forgiveness to be purchased.\nCruz declined a request for an interview. His lawyer, Amauris V\u00e1squez Disla, called the accident \u201ca profound tragedy for all parties involved, especially the victims\u2019 families.\u201d He went on to say that the court \u201cissued an order of dismissal based on the findings, a legally binding decision that was a lawful resolution of this matter.\u201d\nA spokesperson for the Pittsburgh Pirates said the team and Cruz \u201caddressed this matter more than six years ago based on the information available and the outcome of the legal proceedings.\u201d\nYohan and Deby lived in and around a barrio of Sabana Grande de Palenque, known as \u201cSal Si Puedes,\u201d Get Out If You Can. Five miles from Nizao, it is marked by ruddy dirt roads and half-built homes and is separated from the rest of the town by a deep canal. Some residents have to cross makeshift footbridges to reach their front doors. Daniela grew up in the nearby village of Juan Baron.\nAs was the case with Deby and Daniela, Yohan\u2019s parents separated when he was young. His early childhood was spent in a small wooden home where his paternal grandmother took care of him while his mother worked in Santo Domingo as a housekeeper. A teenager when his father suffered a stroke, Yohan would bring him blood pressure medicine and take him to the beach to exercise in the ocean. Yohan worked as an air conditioning mechanic, telling his dad his goal was to pay for a new roof for his mom\u2019s house so that it wouldn\u2019t flood every time it rained. He commuted to Santo Domingo on a X1000 motorcycle he had bought secondhand. In a country where it\u2019s common to see three or more people on a \u201cmoto,\u201d the noisy rig was also how his friends got around.\nThe best of those friends was Deby. The third of four boys, all right-handed pitchers growing up, he lived in his mother\u2019s immaculate home. After she moved to Chile six years earlier to find work, Deby spoke with her every day, updating her on his love life. He had her name tattooed within an infinity symbol on his arm. When he failed to make a top-level baseball academy, the most common route to be signed by a major league team, Deby returned to high school. At the time of the crash, he was days away from graduating, bagging groceries and taking care of his younger brother.\nDeby and Daniela had known each other since they were kids and, according to their families, had an off-and-on relationship. Videos and photos of Daniela often show her dancing and singing. \u201cShe never wanted the party to end,\u201d a friend said. The youngest of four, she lived with her mother, who considered Daniela her best friend. She was her \u201cchiquita,\u201d or \u201clittle girl,\u201d Daniela would remind her mother when she insisted on sitting on her lap. Many nights, they slept in the same bed together.She had graduated high school and was taking English classes in Santo Domingo.\nDeby, Yohan and Daniela were in that hopeful, stressful, liberating period between adolescence and adulthood. They had no intention to be out late that September night, but they were also not going to turn down an impromptu plan to party.\nDaniela told her mom she was going to meet girlfriends at the Nizao River and would be back by early evening. It was still daylight when she recorded herself at an outdoor bar, wearing a red dress and singing along to a salsa song. Nobody was in a rush to return to the stagnant indoors. As for the country\u2019s 5 p.m. COVID-19 curfew, few young people took it seriously. In Ban\u00ed, the nearest sizable city, there was a bar referred to as Escondido, or Hidden, because it served drinks past curfew. Daniela and a friend recruited Yohan and Deby, who were home but easy to persuade to join.\n\nAt around 11 p.m., according to court records, they were riding on the four-lane highway heading home. Late at night, the road is desolate and poorly lit. Trucks and cars howl by at high speeds, and accidents are frequent. A security guard who works at a snack stand across from the site of the accident told me that his boss was killed when a car smashed into his business.\nYohan was at the handlebars, with Daniela and Deby seated behind him. None wore a helmet. A friend on his own motorcycle was riding alongside them. They had just passed the shuttered snack stand when Cruz rear-ended Yohan\u2019s motorcycle, smashing its rear tire and throwing the three friends onto the pavement.\nCruz called 911, according to his lawyers. A lieutenant from the national police responded to the scene, but his subsequent report, which he filed in court, offers little detail about the accident site or investigation that followed. If there was any forensic analysis, prosecutors never produced it. There is no mention of whether police believed either Yohan or Cruz was driving recklessly or speeding. The report does not indicate whether the victims were tested for their alcohol levels. It makes no reference to the second motorcyclist. When I reached him, he said that the authorities never questioned him. He declined to be interviewed further.\nAccording to the report, Cruz told the lieutenant that \u201cen ningun momento,\u201d \u201cat no time,\u201d did he see the motorcycle before colliding with it in the passing lane. He said it didn\u2019t have lights \u2014 an assertion that would later become a point of contention in court. Cruz\u2019s legal teamfiled a photo of the motorcyclethat showed missing lights, but it was taken after the accident, when the bike was barely intact. Luis David, Yohan\u2019s brother, told me that the motorcycle had working lights in the front but not in the rear.\nThe day after the accident, stories were posted on Facebook that included photos of Yohan, Deby and Daniela lying mangled on the highway as emergency responders attempted to save them. On Dominican gossip and news sites, opinions poured forth. Some were convinced that Cruz would get preferential treatment. Others predicted that the families of the victims would be seeking a payday. Many asserted that the deceased were as much at fault as Cruz since they were all out past curfew.\nThe families of Yohan, Deby and Daniela all knew of the Cruzes. Oneil\u2019s father, Rafael, played pro ball, first in Japan and then in the Atlanta Braves organization until 2009, whenhe was suspendedafter testing positive for steroids. He returned to Nizao, where he opened a baseball academy. His most promising pupils included Oneil and his brothers, Rafael Jr. and Homer, who played pro ball in the United States. Oneil was named after Rafael\u2019s favorite ballplayer, New York Yankees outfielder Paul O\u2019Neill.\nAmong the Cruzes\u2019 inner circle was Osvaldo Tamarez, a trainer known as Cabezon, Big Head. He told me that in the hours following the accident, when Oneil was still in custody, he went to see the Cruzes and advised them to handle the aftermath of the accident carefully. \u201cCuidado, tu hijo va a ser una figura p\u00fablica,\u201d Tamarez said he told them. \u201cYour son is going to be a public figure.\u201d According to Tamarez, the Cruzes assigned him to speak with Daniela\u2019s and Deby\u2019s family members, some of whom he knew from baseball. He told me that from the beginning, their focus was on how to pay off the victims\u2019 families so they wouldn\u2019t sue and wouldn\u2019t demand that prosecutors pursue the case. A person close to the Cruzes confirmed Tamarez\u2019s account.\nTamarez said that another trainer recommended that Cruzes bring in Santo Caraballo, a businessman he knew from the baseball industry. Among Caraballo\u2019s many companies was a legal services firm, even though he wasn\u2019t a lawyer. More relevant, Caraballo had political connections. He was an ambassador in the ministry of foreign affairs, a patronage position awarded him four years earlier by President Danilo Medina.\nTamarez said that Caraballo immediately got to work on multiple fronts, including organizing a legal team. But that wasn\u2019t his most important role. Caraballo\u2019s most important job was simple, Tamarez told me: Fix the problem.\nAt Cruz\u2019s initial court appearance, which came a little more than two days after the accident, one of his attorneys asked to delay the case so his representatives could \u201cin a humane way, seek solutions\u201d for the families and their \u201cmaterial hardships.\u201d The judge postponed the hearing for two days. Family members told me that Caraballo, or those working with him, had already begun reaching out to them.\nThe last time Mercedes Garcia saw her daughter, she had helped Daniela pull the price tags off her red dress before she went to the river with her girlfriends. In the days after the accident, Garcia was haunted by a vision of her daughter plastered to the road in the torn dress, yelling, \u201cAy\u00fadame Mam\u00e1, ay\u00fadame!\u201d \u201cHelp me, Mama, help me!\u201d\nI found Garcia where she spends much of her day: seated alone in her tiny living room, rocking back and forth on a couch, her hair neatly pulled back, digging under her fingernails with a bobby pin. I could hear the shouts of a woman preaching at the church next door.\nGarcia, who is 65, says she was clear from the beginning that she didn\u2019t want money from the Cruzes. \u201cRich people, especially baseball players, like to pay for dead people with money,\u201d she told me. \u201cI am poor, but I have honor.\u201d All she wanted, she said, was for Cruz to answer for what happened and apologize. Instead, she said, her family \u2014 most prominently, her son \u2014 tricked her into agreeing to a deal.\nGarcia told me that in the aftermath of her daughter\u2019s death, she entrusted her son Porfirio with handling all the paperwork. Porfirio was a traffic cop in Santo Domingo who moonlighted as an Uber driver. What Garcia didn\u2019t know, but what Tamarez later confirmed, was that Porfirio was already working with Caraballo.\nAccording to Garcia, Porfirio brought her a document to sign that he partially obscured with his hand. Shortly afterward, Porfirio drove her and another of her sons, Juan Manuel, to Caraballo\u2019s office in Santo Domingo. Porfirio went in by himself and brought out another document for his mother to sign. She was in a fog of grief, she told me, and didn\u2019t read it. Juan Manuel confirmed his mother\u2019s account that she didn\u2019t understand what she was signing and that Porfirio didn\u2019t explain what it was.\nDaniela\u2019s father, Porfirio Perez, co-signed the documents. Perez, who can barely read, parks cars at a bank for tips. He told me that Garcia\u2019s brother brought him the papers but didn\u2019t tell him that they exonerated Cruz. \u201cBecause they saw that I\u2019m a simple person, they took advantage of me,\u201d he said. Garcia\u2019s brother declined to comment.\nI reviewed the two documents, which were dated the same day as Cruz\u2019s court appearance. One was filed in court by Cruz\u2019s attorney; the other was not. The document filed in court states that the family sought to \u201cwithdraw\u201d all legal claims against Cruz. It also says that the family had not received any \u201cpecuniary interest\u201d in return for withdrawing the claims. They were doing so out of an awareness that the accident was not caused by Cruz, the document says, \u201cbut rather by circumstances of life.\u201d\nThe contractnot filed in courtis signed by Daniela\u2019s parents and Oneil\u2019s mother. It\u2019s a \u201cdischarge and legal settlement\u201d stating that the parents were paid 900,000 pesos, about $15,000, for Daniela\u2019s \u201cfuneral expenses.\u201d That amount is more than twice the average annual income in the Dominican Republic. The document says that the money represented a settlement for \u201cdamages caused\u201d by Cruz to Daniela\u2019s family. But Garcia and Juan Manuel said that Porfirio controlled the payout and that he gave them around 300,000 pesos, or less than $5,000.\u00a0Daniela\u2019s father told me he received nothing.\nGarcia said that she didn\u2019t talk to much of her family for years after she realized what had happened. Once they decided to take that money, they lost her, too, she told me. \u201cI was buried along with Daniela.\u201d\nI left multiple voice and text messages for Porfirio, who declined to be interviewed.\nCruz\u2019s mother, however, did agree to hear my questions. I met her at the Cruzes\u2019 gated home on a busy street in Nizao. With wary politeness, she indicated that we sit on the front porch. Batting cages were in the back.\nI asked her about Caraballo\u2019s role in persuading the victims\u2019 families not to sue, but she denied that she knew him, contradicting several witnesses. When I showed her a photograph of Caraballo, she told me she didn\u2019t recognize him. Before I could ask my next question, she stood up, making it clear the interview was over. No more than five minutes had passed. \u201cThank you so much for coming,\u201d she said. \u201cI can\u2019t really answer because I don\u2019t know anything.\u201d\nWhen Deby\u2019s mother, Elena Charles, left for Chile to find restaurant work, it fell to Ruben, the eldest of her four sons, to take \u201clas riendas de la casa,\u201d the reins of the house. So it was Ruben, who turned 27 the night his brother died, whom Tamarez visited shortly after Cruz\u2019s court appearance. Ruben told me that Tamarez encouraged him to strike a deal, arguing that the ballplayer was never going to be punished and that Daniela\u2019s family had already received a million pesos for agreeing not to sue. He should meet with a man named Caraballo and hear what he had to say.\nRuben spoke with his father, who told him the same thing: Deby was not coming back. Take the money, even if you just keep it in an account. (Deby\u2019s father did not agree to an interview.)\nWhen Ruben got to Caraballo\u2019s office, he said Cruz\u2019s mother was there. All he wanted, Ruben told me, was justice \u2014 he didn\u2019t care if the courts administered it or if he did. But he was also aware that Tamarez and his father were probably right: He and his family were powerless.\nRuben yelled at Caraballo that he wasn\u2019t there to negotiate over his brother\u2019s death like it was a car for sale. Caraballo, he said, responded like a man used to people exploding with rage in his office. He pointed out all of his surveillance cameras. According to Ruben, he then made his offer. Because both of Deby\u2019s parents lived outside the country \u2014 his father had been in Spain for years \u2014 additional legal work would be required if Ruben\u2019s family made an agreement. Therefore, he was willing to go as high as 2 million pesos, roughly $34,000.\nDeby\u2019s mother returned home a couple of weeks after the accident. I recently met with her and her three surviving sons in their comfortable and tidy living room. Fifty-two years old, dressed in a patterned jumpsuit and a necklace bearing her nickname \u2014 Ramonita \u2014 she frequently wiped away tears as she discussed Deby. On the wall were portraits of each of the boys. Deby\u2019s brothers are wearing their high school graduation robes. Deby is pictured shirtless, sporting sunglasses, ripped jeans and a knowing grin.\nAs we discussed the negotiation with Caraballo, Ruben and his mother revisited an argument that seemed as fresh as those they had during hourslong phone calls six years ago. She was absolutely clear about what she wanted: Cruz should be in prison for 120 years.\n\u201cTe dije no lo hagas,\u201d she said to him, her voice rising. \u201cI told you not to do it.\u201d\n\u201cAnd what did I tell you?\u201d Ruben responded. \u201cThat they were going to release him anyway!\u201d\nRuben signed a document on behalf of his family called an \u201cact of withdrawal,\u201d which Cruz\u2019s attorneys later filed in court. Similar to the document signed by Daniela\u2019s parents, it attested that Cruz \u201chad not the slightest intention of causing the accident\u201d and that Deby\u2019s family had agreed to withdraw all legal claims against him, including criminal and civil, without \u201cany type of pressure [or] pecuniary interest.\u201d\nCaraballo, though, wasn\u2019t done. Ruben told me that Caraballo then pitched him on turning his brother\u2019s death into an income opportunity: Rather than give the family the 2 million pesos in a lump sum, Caraballo would invest the money for them and pay 15,000 pesos, just over $265, a month indefinitely.\nRuben, though, was done with Caraballo. \u201cI don\u2019t want to do business with you,\u201d he said he told him, \u201cand I don\u2019t want to hear from you ever again.\u201d\nThe family put the money in a bank account. Except for the interest, roughly $65 a month, which the family has used to help pay for the youngest\u2019s tuition, the money is still there, they told me. Charles explained that there\u2019s been plenty of times that they could\u2019ve dipped into the principal, but the money repels her. \u201cIt doesn\u2019t have an end,\u201d she said of the anguish she feels about having made a deal with the Cruzes. \u201cFor the rest of my life, it\u2019s going to be a conflict.\u201d\nGenny Mercado, after turning away the driver with Cruz\u2019s mother on the line, faced a similar gantlet of pressure from her family. Word spread that the other families were accepting a deal. Genny earned about 16,000 pesos, about $275, a month as a housekeeper, so a settlement would likely exceed several years of income. Rejecting the money, Yohan\u2019s half brothers told her, would accomplish nothing.\nMercado, who is 48, was also the de facto decision-maker. Yohan\u2019s father, Pedro Saba, had suffered a second stroke the day he learned of his son\u2019s death. She hired the only lawyer she knew, who negotiated a deal on the parents\u2019 behalf. The document was a withdrawal like those signed by the other families, exonerating Cruz. Mercado said that she couldn\u2019t think clearly about any of this at the time: \u201cMy mind was set on my son.\u201d\nMinus the lawyer\u2019s cut, Mercado and Saba received approximately 800,000 pesos, or $14,000. Yohan\u2019s father spent 300,000 pesos on a refrigerator, a television and a bed. Mercado used the money to buy rebar and 75 bags of cement to build the roof that Yohan had promised. She stopped working for seven months and rarely left her home except to put flowers on his grave.\nWhen I showed up, two of her sons arranged plastic chairs in the living room so I could speak with her and her husband, Jos\u00e9 Enrique. She had never been interviewed, and it had been years since anyone outside the family even pretended to care about what happened to Yohan. \u201cAny family that goes through something like this just wants justice,\u201d Mercado told me. \u201cEveryone who kills has to go to jail.\u201d But in Cruz\u2019s case, \u201cNo one wanted to hurt the future del muchacho.\u201d\nThere were no second thoughts about having accepted the money, only resignation that there was never going to be a fair outcome for Yohan. \u201cAround here, the police let a lot of things slide, especially when it involves people who have money or connections or whatever else,\u201d said Jos\u00e9 Enrique, tossing shucked pea shells on the cement floor. \u201cWe\u2019re not going to try to dance around that fact anymore.\u201d\nLater I visited Yohan\u2019s father, who spends his days in a spartan home with three of his elderly brothers, two of whom are blind. \u201cTerminator\u201d blared on the television. Saba doesn\u2019t have any photos of Yohan on his walls because, he said, it would make him too sad. In a voice slurred by his two strokes, Saba told me how Yohan would put him on his motorcycle and take him to the ocean. \u201cWherever I wanted to go,\u201d he said, \u201che would take me there.\u201d He hasn\u2019t been back to the beach since Yohan was killed.\nCruz\u2019s second court date occurred on the Friday following the accident. Prosecutors were seeking to keep him in custody for three months. The victims\u2019 families weren\u2019t present. The court didn\u2019t know how to reach two of them, a clerk explained to the judge. Prosecutors didn\u2019t even get the names of the victims right, referring to Yohan Saba as \u201cJons Sabab\u201d and Deby as \u201cBaby.\u201d The charges they sought against Cruz included reckless driving and causing an accident that resulted in death. If convicted, he could face one to three years in prison.\nMost striking was what was not said. In their press release after the crash, prosecutors had referred to evidence that Cruz had been drinking before the accident. But they never made that claim in court. I reached out to the lead prosecutor as well as the prosecutor\u2019s office to ask why they had decided not to charge Cruz with driving while intoxicated. Both declined to comment. A Pittsburgh Post-Gazette journalist asked the same question the week of the court date. Hereportedthat a spokesperson for the prosecutors explained that Cruz had \u201csmelled of alcohol\u201d after the accident but that police hadn\u2019t performed a sobriety test because of a \u201cprocedural error.\u201d\nWhile Cruz remained in police custody, his legal team submitted the withdrawals that had been signed by the victims\u2019 families. In addition to offering his condolences, an attorney explained that Cruz was providing \u201cmaterial support\u201d to the families for their \u201cbasic needs of being human: education, food, clothing, among other matters.\u201d He also submitted numerous testimonials praising Cruz\u2019s good character, including from his pastor, who attested that Cruz had demonstrated \u201cloyalty and absolute fidelity to the gospel,\u201d and from a local marble and granite contractor who said that Cruz was a responsible client \u201cdeserving of an open line of credit.\u201d The attorney emphasized that Cruz was one of baseball\u2019s \u201cfive highest-rated prospects internationally.\u201d\nThe accident, he told the judge, \u201churts the whole town, Oneil above all, who for this will have to go to professional therapy so that he can cope with the trauma \u2026 For God\u2019s sake, let\u2019s try to ensure that Oneil Cruz Ure\u00f1a\u2019s mental health is now a priority.\u201d\nThe judge released Cruz on a bond of 2 million pesos, or $34,000. A couple of weeks later, Cruz posted a photo of himself with Caraballo, grinning while seated on a golf cart at the Miami airport.\nThe following March, Cruz was at the Pirates\u2019 spring training facility in Florida. Anarticleat the time mentioned the accident in passing, buried under the news that the team was moving him into the outfield. It\u2019s the only time I know of in which Cruz has been quoted discussing the accident.\n\u201cIn the beginning, I was very fearful of not being able to continue to play the game that I love and to reach the dream I\u2019ve always had, which is to reach the big leagues,\u201d Cruz said through an interpreter. \u201cHowever, when things became clear later and everything was solved, I was able to \u2026 hope again that I\u2019ll be able to play. Now, I have peace.\u201d\nThe press, both in the United States and the Dominican Republic, stopped covering the accident after that.\nThe man who helped deliver him that peace used the incident to become one of the most powerful figures operating in the shadows of Dominican baseball.\nCruz\u2019s next court appearance was Aug. 30, 2021. Almost a year had passed since the accident, and he was in the States playing for the Altoona Curve, a Pirates minor league team. He attended virtually. Despite the families\u2019 withdrawals, prosecutors still had the power to pursue charges against him, but their case was flimsy. The only evidence they submitted were death certificates and a police report that offered little more than the date and time of the accident and the names of Cruz and the three people he killed: Yohan Saba Mercado, Deby Beato Charles and Daniela Perez Garcia. The judge dismissed the case, ordering the return of the 2 million pesos bond.\nJust over a month later, Cruz made his major league debut against the Cincinnati Reds, scorching a single that wasthe hardest recorded hit by a Pirate, a mark he hasbroken many times since. He is now a fixture of the team and will earn $3.3 million this year. He is projected to make far more once he becomes a free agent in a few seasons.\nReporting was contributed byMaryam Jameelof ProPublica, and by Zahiris Priscila Francisco Martinez and Carolina Pichardo. Visual editing byAlex Bandoniof ProPublica.\nI am an investigative reporter specializing in sports.\nCreative Commons License (CC BY-NC-ND 3.0)\nThank you for your interest in republishing this story. You are free to republish it so long as you do the following:\nAround 4 a.m. on Sept. 21, 2020, Luis David Saba arrived at a desolate stretch of highway along the southern coast of the Dominican Republic. Police and paramedics had left by then, taking with them a Jeep Grand Cherokee with a crumpled hood and the tangled remains of the cheap motorcycle it had smashed into. Also gone were the motorcycle\u2019s two critically injured passengers. The only signs that an accident had occurred a few hours earlier were the blood and debris smeared across the asphalt and a pile of neatly placed branches.\nSaba knew what to expect underneath that pile. It was a Dominican custom to use anything on hand to shroud a body abandoned by paramedics. Pushing away the branches, Saba saw shredded clothes, torn skin and long, curly hair forming a halo over a battered face. He covered the body of his 20-year-old younger brother, Yohan Saba Mercado, with a sheet. In shock, he felt nothing but the need to get his brother out of the road.\nSaba sat and waited for forensic investigators he was told were on their way. Hours passed, the sun rose and traffic began to stream by them. Finally, he gave up. With the help of friends, he loaded his brother\u2019s body into a van, and hours later it was at his mother\u2019s house.\nLater that morning, two other families were experiencing similar horrors. Deby Beato Charles, 20, and Daniela Perez Garcia, 19, had been riding on the back of Yohan\u2019s motorcycle when the Jeep rear-ended them. When Deby\u2019s older brother, Ruben, got to a nearby hospital, he was met by a doctor who handed him a wallet and bracelet. Deby had died of a severe head injury and blunt chest trauma. At another hospital, Daniela\u2019s uncle was ushered into a room where his niece lay. Her face was so swollen he didn\u2019t recognize her. According to her death certificate, she had suffered injuries to her head, chest and hip and went into hypovolemic shock, in which the loss of bodily fluids causes a person\u2019s organs to shut down.\nThe families of Daniela and Yohan held funerals in their homes that afternoon. During the proceedings, word spread about who had been driving the Grand Cherokee. It was Oneil Cruz, a \u201cpelotero,\u201d a ballplayer, with a fortune and a future, the 21-year-old scion of a local baseball family, a top prospect in the Pittsburgh Pirates organization. Seemingly everyone in the area knew how much he received from his major league signing bonus five years earlier: $950,000.\nThe families also knew what was going to happen next.\nYohan\u2019s mother told me that after all the mourners left the house, a driver showed up holding out a phone. On the line was Cruz\u2019s mother offering condolences \u2014 and wanting to negotiate.\nLess than 7 miles from the site of the accident is the striving farm town of Nizao. Even though it has a population of just 32,000, Nizao has produced dozens of professional ballplayers, including three-time All-Star Ketel Marte and, most famously, Hall of Famer Vladimir Guerrero Sr., one of the best hitters in modern baseball and father of Vladimir Jr., the star first baseman for the Toronto Blue Jays. Looming over the main road into town is the Guerreros\u2019 mansion. Right below it, a giant 27 has been carved into the hill \u2014 the jersey number the two share.\nBy 2020, Oneil Cruz Ure\u00f1a looked poised to be among the best of Nizao\u2019s ballplayers. The Los Angeles Dodgers signed him when he was 16 and over 6 feet tall. They traded him to the Pittsburgh Pirates, who considered him their future shortstop. The statistics website FanGraphs ranked him asone of baseball\u2019s top prospects, declaring that \u201cthere aren\u2019t many who have a chance to be what this guy might.\u201d\nBut the accident on a darkened road threatened to destroy that chance before Cruz even made it to the major leagues. Police detained him at the scene. In a court filing the following day, prosecutors said that they had opened an investigation \u201cen contra del imputado,\u201d \u201cagainst the accused.\u201d They sought \u201ccoercive measures,\u201d a mechanism in Dominican law by which a suspect can be forced to remain in custody while an investigation continues. In a press release, they alleged that Cruz \u201chad consumed alcoholic beverages prior to the accident.\u201d\nThe scandalmade headlinesin the Dominican Republic and the United States, and then, in a matter of days, it evaporated from the public eye. How that happened has been unknown, until now.\nI\u2019d heard about the Cruz case last year when I began reporting on the booming industry of \u201cprestamistas,\u201d moneylenders, who exploit major league prospects in the Dominican Republic by getting their undereducated parents to give up sizable portions of their signing bonuses. The case\u2019s disappearance, I was told, marked the origin story of the most dominant prestamista in the country\u2019s chaotic baseball ecosystem: Santo Caraballo. At the time, Caraballo was an obscure businessman with political connections. He rose to prominence after forming a business partnership with national hero David Ortiz, the retired Boston Red Sox great who remains an influential figure in Major League Baseball. (In a statement, Ortiz said his business relationship with Caraballo \u201cbegan to come to an end\u201d more than 18 months ago.) I wrote about Caraballo\u2019s practices in \u201cThe Dominican Baseball Factory.\u201d\nTo find out how Caraballo made the case against Cruz go away, I interviewed more than 30 people in the Dominican Republic. Some were afraid to talk, but others, primarily family members of the victims, were eager to speak publicly for the first time. I also obtained hundreds of pages of previously unreported documents, including court records and bank receipts, as well as voice notes. I made numerous attempts to reach Caraballo about his role, including sending him a summary of my findings. He never responded.\nThe Dominican court system gives victims or their families significant input on whether to pursue a prosecution. Caraballo oversaw a campaign to persuade the families of the three victims to accept cash in return for signing documents withdrawing all \u201cpenal, civil or judicial\u201d claims against Cruz.\nThe effort rescued Cruz\u2019s baseball career \u2014 he is now the starting center fielder for the Pirates \u2014 and it launched Caraballo\u2019s. He became a ubiquitous operator in the sport, feared for the sort of high-powered connections that could make a deadly crash disappear. But for some of the victims\u2019 relatives, the money they received \u2014 roughly $66,000 in total for the three families \u2014 hasn\u2019t lasted nearly as long as the hurt: six years of unresolved grief, resentment and regret that they allowed their forgiveness to be purchased.\nCruz declined a request for an interview. His lawyer, Amauris V\u00e1squez Disla, called the accident \u201ca profound tragedy for all parties involved, especially the victims\u2019 families.\u201d He went on to say that the court \u201cissued an order of dismissal based on the findings, a legally binding decision that was a lawful resolution of this matter.\u201d\nA spokesperson for the Pittsburgh Pirates said the team and Cruz \u201caddressed this matter more than six years ago based on the information available and the outcome of the legal proceedings.\u201d\nYohan and Deby lived in and around a barrio of Sabana Grande de Palenque, known as \u201cSal Si Puedes,\u201d Get Out If You Can. Five miles from Nizao, it is marked by ruddy dirt roads and half-built homes and is separated from the rest of the town by a deep canal. Some residents have to cross makeshift footbridges to reach their front doors. Daniela grew up in the nearby village of Juan Baron.\nAs was the case with Deby and Daniela, Yohan\u2019s parents separated when he was young. His early childhood was spent in a small wooden home where his paternal grandmother took care of him while his mother worked in Santo Domingo as a housekeeper. A teenager when his father suffered a stroke, Yohan would bring him blood pressure medicine and take him to the beach to exercise in the ocean. Yohan worked as an air conditioning mechanic, telling his dad his goal was to pay for a new roof for his mom\u2019s house so that it wouldn\u2019t flood every time it rained. He commuted to Santo Domingo on a X1000 motorcycle he had bought secondhand. In a country where it\u2019s common to see three or more people on a \u201cmoto,\u201d the noisy rig was also how his friends got around.\nThe best of those friends was Deby. The third of four boys, all right-handed pitchers growing up, he lived in his mother\u2019s immaculate home. After she moved to Chile six years earlier to find work, Deby spoke with her every day, updating her on his love life. He had her name tattooed within an infinity symbol on his arm. When he failed to make a top-level baseball academy, the most common route to be signed by a major league team, Deby returned to high school. At the time of the crash, he was days away from graduating, bagging groceries and taking care of his younger brother.\nDeby and Daniela had known each other since they were kids and, according to their families, had an off-and-on relationship. Videos and photos of Daniela often show her dancing and singing. \u201cShe never wanted the party to end,\u201d a friend said. The youngest of four, she lived with her mother, who considered Daniela her best friend. She was her \u201cchiquita,\u201d or \u201clittle girl,\u201d Daniela would remind her mother when she insisted on sitting on her lap. Many nights, they slept in the same bed together.She had graduated high school and was taking English classes in Santo Domingo.\nDeby, Yohan and Daniela were in that hopeful, stressful, liberating period between adolescence and adulthood. They had no intention to be out late that September night, but they were also not going to turn down an impromptu plan to party.\nDaniela told her mom she was going to meet girlfriends at the Nizao River and would be back by early evening. It was still daylight when she recorded herself at an outdoor bar, wearing a red dress and singing along to a salsa song. Nobody was in a rush to return to the stagnant indoors. As for the country\u2019s 5 p.m. COVID-19 curfew, few young people took it seriously. In Ban\u00ed, the nearest sizable city, there was a bar referred to as Escondido, or Hidden, because it served drinks past curfew. Daniela and a friend recruited Yohan and Deby, who were home but easy to persuade to join.\nAt around 11 p.m., according to court records, they were riding on the four-lane highway heading home. Late at night, the road is desolate and poorly lit. Trucks and cars howl by at high speeds, and accidents are frequent. A security guard who works at a snack stand across from the site of the accident told me that his boss was killed when a car smashed into his business.\nYohan was at the handlebars, with Daniela and Deby seated behind him. None wore a helmet. A friend on his own motorcycle was riding alongside them. They had just passed the shuttered snack stand when Cruz rear-ended Yohan\u2019s motorcycle, smashing its rear tire and throwing the three friends onto the pavement.\nCruz called 911, according to his lawyers. A lieutenant from the national police responded to the scene, but his subsequent report, which he filed in court, offers little detail about the accident site or investigation that followed. If there was any forensic analysis, prosecutors never produced it. There is no mention of whether police believed either Yohan or Cruz was driving recklessly or speeding. The report does not indicate whether the victims were tested for their alcohol levels. It makes no reference to the second motorcyclist. When I reached him, he said that the authorities never questioned him. He declined to be interviewed further.\nAccording to the report, Cruz told the lieutenant that \u201cen ningun momento,\u201d \u201cat no time,\u201d did he see the motorcycle before colliding with it in the passing lane. He said it didn\u2019t have lights \u2014 an assertion that would later become a point of contention in court. Cruz\u2019s legal teamfiled a photo of the motorcyclethat showed missing lights, but it was taken after the accident, when the bike was barely intact. Luis David, Yohan\u2019s brother, told me that the motorcycle had working lights in the front but not in the rear.\nThe day after the accident, stories were posted on Facebook that included photos of Yohan, Deby and Daniela lying mangled on the highway as emergency responders attempted to save them. On Dominican gossip and news sites, opinions poured forth. Some were convinced that Cruz would get preferential treatment. Others predicted that the families of the victims would be seeking a payday. Many asserted that the deceased were as much at fault as Cruz since they were all out past curfew.\nThe families of Yohan, Deby and Daniela all knew of the Cruzes. Oneil\u2019s father, Rafael, played pro ball, first in Japan and then in the Atlanta Braves organization until 2009, whenhe was suspendedafter testing positive for steroids. He returned to Nizao, where he opened a baseball academy. His most promising pupils included Oneil and his brothers, Rafael Jr. and Homer, who played pro ball in the United States. Oneil was named after Rafael\u2019s favorite ballplayer, New York Yankees outfielder Paul O\u2019Neill.\nAmong the Cruzes\u2019 inner circle was Osvaldo Tamarez, a trainer known as Cabezon, Big Head. He told me that in the hours following the accident, when Oneil was still in custody, he went to see the Cruzes and advised them to handle the aftermath of the accident carefully. \u201cCuidado, tu hijo va a ser una figura p\u00fablica,\u201d Tamarez said he told them. \u201cYour son is going to be a public figure.\u201d According to Tamarez, the Cruzes assigned him to speak with Daniela\u2019s and Deby\u2019s family members, some of whom he knew from baseball. He told me that from the beginning, their focus was on how to pay off the victims\u2019 families so they wouldn\u2019t sue and wouldn\u2019t demand that prosecutors pursue the case. A person close to the Cruzes confirmed Tamarez\u2019s account.\nTamarez said that another trainer recommended that Cruzes bring in Santo Caraballo, a businessman he knew from the baseball industry. Among Caraballo\u2019s many companies was a legal services firm, even though he wasn\u2019t a lawyer. More relevant, Caraballo had political connections. He was an ambassador in the ministry of foreign affairs, a patronage position awarded him four years earlier by President Danilo Medina.\nTamarez said that Caraballo immediately got to work on multiple fronts, including organizing a legal team. But that wasn\u2019t his most important role. Caraballo\u2019s most important job was simple, Tamarez told me: Fix the problem.\nAt Cruz\u2019s initial court appearance, which came a little more than two days after the accident, one of his attorneys asked to delay the case so his representatives could \u201cin a humane way, seek solutions\u201d for the families and their \u201cmaterial hardships.\u201d The judge postponed the hearing for two days. Family members told me that Caraballo, or those working with him, had already begun reaching out to them.\nThe last time Mercedes Garcia saw her daughter, she had helped Daniela pull the price tags off her red dress before she went to the river with her girlfriends. In the days after the accident, Garcia was haunted by a vision of her daughter plastered to the road in the torn dress, yelling, \u201cAy\u00fadame Mam\u00e1, ay\u00fadame!\u201d \u201cHelp me, Mama, help me!\u201d\nI found Garcia where she spends much of her day: seated alone in her tiny living room, rocking back and forth on a couch, her hair neatly pulled back, digging under her fingernails with a bobby pin. I could hear the shouts of a woman preaching at the church next door.\nGarcia, who is 65, says she was clear from the beginning that she didn\u2019t want money from the Cruzes. \u201cRich people, especially baseball players, like to pay for dead people with money,\u201d she told me. \u201cI am poor, but I have honor.\u201d All she wanted, she said, was for Cruz to answer for what happened and apologize. Instead, she said, her family \u2014 most prominently, her son \u2014 tricked her into agreeing to a deal.\nGarcia told me that in the aftermath of her daughter\u2019s death, she entrusted her son Porfirio with handling all the paperwork. Porfirio was a traffic cop in Santo Domingo who moonlighted as an Uber driver. What Garcia didn\u2019t know, but what Tamarez later confirmed, was that Porfirio was already working with Caraballo.\nAccording to Garcia, Porfirio brought her a document to sign that he partially obscured with his hand. Shortly afterward, Porfirio drove her and another of her sons, Juan Manuel, to Caraballo\u2019s office in Santo Domingo. Porfirio went in by himself and brought out another document for his mother to sign. She was in a fog of grief, she told me, and didn\u2019t read it. Juan Manuel confirmed his mother\u2019s account that she didn\u2019t understand what she was signing and that Porfirio didn\u2019t explain what it was.\nDaniela\u2019s father, Porfirio Perez, co-signed the documents. Perez, who can barely read, parks cars at a bank for tips. He told me that Garcia\u2019s brother brought him the papers but didn\u2019t tell him that they exonerated Cruz. \u201cBecause they saw that I\u2019m a simple person, they took advantage of me,\u201d he said. Garcia\u2019s brother declined to comment.\nI reviewed the two documents, which were dated the same day as Cruz\u2019s court appearance. One was filed in court by Cruz\u2019s attorney; the other was not. The document filed in court states that the family sought to \u201cwithdraw\u201d all legal claims against Cruz. It also says that the family had not received any \u201cpecuniary interest\u201d in return for withdrawing the claims. They were doing so out of an awareness that the accident was not caused by Cruz, the document says, \u201cbut rather by circumstances of life.\u201d\nThe contractnot filed in courtis signed by Daniela\u2019s parents and Oneil\u2019s mother. It\u2019s a \u201cdischarge and legal settlement\u201d stating that the parents were paid 900,000 pesos, about $15,000, for Daniela\u2019s \u201cfuneral expenses.\u201d That amount is more than twice the average annual income in the Dominican Republic. The document says that the money represented a settlement for \u201cdamages caused\u201d by Cruz to Daniela\u2019s family. But Garcia and Juan Manuel said that Porfirio controlled the payout and that he gave them around 300,000 pesos, or less than $5,000.\u00a0Daniela\u2019s father told me he received nothing.\nGarcia said that she didn\u2019t talk to much of her family for years after she realized what had happened. Once they decided to take that money, they lost her, too, she told me. \u201cI was buried along with Daniela.\u201d\nI left multiple voice and text messages for Porfirio, who declined to be interviewed.\nCruz\u2019s mother, however, did agree to hear my questions. I met her at the Cruzes\u2019 gated home on a busy street in Nizao. With wary politeness, she indicated that we sit on the front porch. Batting cages were in the back.\nI asked her about Caraballo\u2019s role in persuading the victims\u2019 families not to sue, but she denied that she knew him, contradicting several witnesses. When I showed her a photograph of Caraballo, she told me she didn\u2019t recognize him. Before I could ask my next question, she stood up, making it clear the interview was over. No more than five minutes had passed. \u201cThank you so much for coming,\u201d she said. \u201cI can\u2019t really answer because I don\u2019t know anything.\u201d\nWhen Deby\u2019s mother, Elena Charles, left for Chile to find restaurant work, it fell to Ruben, the eldest of her four sons, to take \u201clas riendas de la casa,\u201d the reins of the house. So it was Ruben, who turned 27 the night his brother died, whom Tamarez visited shortly after Cruz\u2019s court appearance. Ruben told me that Tamarez encouraged him to strike a deal, arguing that the ballplayer was never going to be punished and that Daniela\u2019s family had already received a million pesos for agreeing not to sue. He should meet with a man named Caraballo and hear what he had to say.\nRuben spoke with his father, who told him the same thing: Deby was not coming back. Take the money, even if you just keep it in an account. (Deby\u2019s father did not agree to an interview.)\nWhen Ruben got to Caraballo\u2019s office, he said Cruz\u2019s mother was there. All he wanted, Ruben told me, was justice \u2014 he didn\u2019t care if the courts administered it or if he did. But he was also aware that Tamarez and his father were probably right: He and his family were powerless.\nRuben yelled at Caraballo that he wasn\u2019t there to negotiate over his brother\u2019s death like it was a car for sale. Caraballo, he said, responded like a man used to people exploding with rage in his office. He pointed out all of his surveillance cameras. According to Ruben, he then made his offer. Because both of Deby\u2019s parents lived outside the country \u2014 his father had been in Spain for years \u2014 additional legal work would be required if Ruben\u2019s family made an agreement. Therefore, he was willing to go as high as 2 million pesos, roughly $34,000.\nDeby\u2019s mother returned home a couple of weeks after the accident. I recently met with her and her three surviving sons in their comfortable and tidy living room. Fifty-two years old, dressed in a patterned jumpsuit and a necklace bearing her nickname \u2014 Ramonita \u2014 she frequently wiped away tears as she discussed Deby. On the wall were portraits of each of the boys. Deby\u2019s brothers are wearing their high school graduation robes. Deby is pictured shirtless, sporting sunglasses, ripped jeans and a knowing grin.\nAs we discussed the negotiation with Caraballo, Ruben and his mother revisited an argument that seemed as fresh as those they had during hourslong phone calls six years ago. She was absolutely clear about what she wanted: Cruz should be in prison for 120 years.\n\u201cTe dije no lo hagas,\u201d she said to him, her voice rising. \u201cI told you not to do it.\u201d\n\u201cAnd what did I tell you?\u201d Ruben responded. \u201cThat they were going to release him anyway!\u201d\nRuben signed a document on behalf of his family called an \u201cact of withdrawal,\u201d which Cruz\u2019s attorneys later filed in court. Similar to the document signed by Daniela\u2019s parents, it attested that Cruz \u201chad not the slightest intention of causing the accident\u201d and that Deby\u2019s family had agreed to withdraw all legal claims against him, including criminal and civil, without \u201cany type of pressure [or] pecuniary interest.\u201d\nCaraballo, though, wasn\u2019t done. Ruben told me that Caraballo then pitched him on turning his brother\u2019s death into an income opportunity: Rather than give the family the 2 million pesos in a lump sum, Caraballo would invest the money for them and pay 15,000 pesos, just over $265, a month indefinitely.\nRuben, though, was done with Caraballo. \u201cI don\u2019t want to do business with you,\u201d he said he told him, \u201cand I don\u2019t want to hear from you ever again.\u201d\nThe family put the money in a bank account. Except for the interest, roughly $65 a month, which the family has used to help pay for the youngest\u2019s tuition, the money is still there, they told me. Charles explained that there\u2019s been plenty of times that they could\u2019ve dipped into the principal, but the money repels her. \u201cIt doesn\u2019t have an end,\u201d she said of the anguish she feels about having made a deal with the Cruzes. \u201cFor the rest of my life, it\u2019s going to be a conflict.\u201d\nGenny Mercado, after turning away the driver with Cruz\u2019s mother on the line, faced a similar gantlet of pressure from her family. Word spread that the other families were accepting a deal. Genny earned about 16,000 pesos, about $275, a month as a housekeeper, so a settlement would likely exceed several years of income. Rejecting the money, Yohan\u2019s half brothers told her, would accomplish nothing.\nMercado, who is 48, was also the de facto decision-maker. Yohan\u2019s father, Pedro Saba, had suffered a second stroke the day he learned of his son\u2019s death. She hired the only lawyer she knew, who negotiated a deal on the parents\u2019 behalf. The document was a withdrawal like those signed by the other families, exonerating Cruz. Mercado said that she couldn\u2019t think clearly about any of this at the time: \u201cMy mind was set on my son.\u201d\nMinus the lawyer\u2019s cut, Mercado and Saba received approximately 800,000 pesos, or $14,000. Yohan\u2019s father spent 300,000 pesos on a refrigerator, a television and a bed. Mercado used the money to buy rebar and 75 bags of cement to build the roof that Yohan had promised. She stopped working for seven months and rarely left her home except to put flowers on his grave.\nWhen I showed up, two of her sons arranged plastic chairs in the living room so I could speak with her and her husband, Jos\u00e9 Enrique. She had never been interviewed, and it had been years since anyone outside the family even pretended to care about what happened to Yohan. \u201cAny family that goes through something like this just wants justice,\u201d Mercado told me. \u201cEveryone who kills has to go to jail.\u201d But in Cruz\u2019s case, \u201cNo one wanted to hurt the future del muchacho.\u201d\nThere were no second thoughts about having accepted the money, only resignation that there was never going to be a fair outcome for Yohan. \u201cAround here, the police let a lot of things slide, especially when it involves people who have money or connections or whatever else,\u201d said Jos\u00e9 Enrique, tossing shucked pea shells on the cement floor. \u201cWe\u2019re not going to try to dance around that fact anymore.\u201d\nLater I visited Yohan\u2019s father, who spends his days in a spartan home with three of his elderly brothers, two of whom are blind. \u201cTerminator\u201d blared on the television. Saba doesn\u2019t have any photos of Yohan on his walls because, he said, it would make him too sad. In a voice slurred by his two strokes, Saba told me how Yohan would put him on his motorcycle and take him to the ocean. \u201cWherever I wanted to go,\u201d he said, \u201che would take me there.\u201d He hasn\u2019t been back to the beach since Yohan was killed.\nCruz\u2019s second court date occurred on the Friday following the accident. Prosecutors were seeking to keep him in custody for three months. The victims\u2019 families weren\u2019t present. The court didn\u2019t know how to reach two of them, a clerk explained to the judge. Prosecutors didn\u2019t even get the names of the victims right, referring to Yohan Saba as \u201cJons Sabab\u201d and Deby as \u201cBaby.\u201d The charges they sought against Cruz included reckless driving and causing an accident that resulted in death. If convicted, he could face one to three years in prison.\nMost striking was what was not said. In their press release after the crash, prosecutors had referred to evidence that Cruz had been drinking before the accident. But they never made that claim in court. I reached out to the lead prosecutor as well as the prosecutor\u2019s office to ask why they had decided not to charge Cruz with driving while intoxicated. Both declined to comment. A Pittsburgh Post-Gazette journalist asked the same question the week of the court date. Hereportedthat a spokesperson for the prosecutors explained that Cruz had \u201csmelled of alcohol\u201d after the accident but that police hadn\u2019t performed a sobriety test because of a \u201cprocedural error.\u201d\nWhile Cruz remained in police custody, his legal team submitted the withdrawals that had been signed by the victims\u2019 families. In addition to offering his condolences, an attorney explained that Cruz was providing \u201cmaterial support\u201d to the families for their \u201cbasic needs of being human: education, food, clothing, among other matters.\u201d He also submitted numerous testimonials praising Cruz\u2019s good character, including from his pastor, who attested that Cruz had demonstrated \u201cloyalty and absolute fidelity to the gospel,\u201d and from a local marble and granite contractor who said that Cruz was a responsible client \u201cdeserving of an open line of credit.\u201d The attorney emphasized that Cruz was one of baseball\u2019s \u201cfive highest-rated prospects internationally.\u201d\nThe accident, he told the judge, \u201churts the whole town, Oneil above all, who for this will have to go to professional therapy so that he can cope with the trauma \u2026 For God\u2019s sake, let\u2019s try to ensure that Oneil Cruz Ure\u00f1a\u2019s mental health is now a priority.\u201d\nThe judge released Cruz on a bond of 2 million pesos, or $34,000. A couple of weeks later, Cruz posted a photo of himself with Caraballo, grinning while seated on a golf cart at the Miami airport.\nThe following March, Cruz was at the Pirates\u2019 spring training facility in Florida. Anarticleat the time mentioned the accident in passing, buried under the news that the team was moving him into the outfield. It\u2019s the only time I know of in which Cruz has been quoted discussing the accident.\n\u201cIn the beginning, I was very fearful of not being able to continue to play the game that I love and to reach the dream I\u2019ve always had, which is to reach the big leagues,\u201d Cruz said through an interpreter. \u201cHowever, when things became clear later and everything was solved, I was able to \u2026 hope again that I\u2019ll be able to play. Now, I have peace.\u201d\nThe press, both in the United States and the Dominican Republic, stopped covering the accident after that.\nThe man who helped deliver him that peace used the incident to become one of the most powerful figures operating in the shadows of Dominican baseball.\nCruz\u2019s next court appearance was Aug. 30, 2021. Almost a year had passed since the accident, and he was in the States playing for the Altoona Curve, a Pirates minor league team. He attended virtually. Despite the families\u2019 withdrawals, prosecutors still had the power to pursue charges against him, but their case was flimsy. The only evidence they submitted were death certificates and a police report that offered little more than the date and time of the accident and the names of Cruz and the three people he killed: Yohan Saba Mercado, Deby Beato Charles and Daniela Perez Garcia. The judge dismissed the case, ordering the return of the 2 million pesos bond.\nJust over a month later, Cruz made his major league debut against the Cincinnati Reds, scorching a single that wasthe hardest recorded hit by a Pirate, a mark he hasbroken many times since. He is now a fixture of the team and will earn $3.3 million this year. He is projected to make far more once he becomes a free agent in a few seasons.\nThe story you just read was made possible by our readers. We hope it inspires you to support ProPublica, so we can continue producing investigations that shine a light on power, uncover the truth and drive real change.\nProPublica is a nonprofit newsroom dedicated to nonpartisan, evidence-based journalism that holds power accountable. Founded in 2008 to address the decline in investigative reporting, we\u2019ve spent over 15 years uncovering injustice, corruption and abuse of power \u2014 work that is slow, expensive and more critical than ever to our democracy. With nine Pulitzer Prizes and reforms sparked in state and local governments, businesses, institutions and more, our reporting ensures that the public interest comes first.\nToday, the stakes are higher than ever. From ethics in our government offices, to reproductive health care, to the climate crisis and beyond, ProPublica remains on the front lines of the stories that matter most. Your gift helps us keep the powerful accountable and the truth accessible.\nJoin over 90,000 supporters nationwidein standing up for investigative journalism that informs, inspires and creates lasting impact. Thank you for making this work possible.\nSupportIndependentJournalism:\nAs a nonprofit newsroom, we rely on donations from individuals like you to fuel our work.Your support today will directly fund investigations that shine a light on urgent issues:corporations prioritizing profits over public safety, health insurers denying coverage to patients, unintended consequences of new laws and more.\nInvestigative Journalism in the Public Interest\nSign up for ProPublica's Big Story newsletter and get our latest stories delivered straight to your inbox.", "date": "Thu, 10 Sep 2026 09:02:00 +0000"}, {"title": "Was serial killer Lizzie Borden actually a lesbian? Here\u2019s what we know\u2026", "url": "https://www.queerty.com/was-serial-lizzie-borden-actually-a-lesbian-heres-what-we-know-20260922/", "body": "\ud83c\udff3\ufe0f\u200d\ud83c\udf08\u00a0100%\u00a0LGBTQ+\u00a0Owned\nRyan Murphy\u2018s latest season ofMonsteris far from the first time Lizzie Borden\u2019s story has been modernized. Ever since Borden took an axe and gave her (step)mother 40 whacks in 1892, she\u2019s been one of pop culture\u2019s most obsessed-over true crime figures. She\u2019s been the subject of several TV shows (with Christina Ricci and Clea Duvall, and a movie with Kristen Stewart and Chloe Sevigny), a made-for-TV movie, and even the final act twist of avery gay 1941 film. The gays love the story of Borden, a woman who took arms against a sea of troubles and ended them in spectacularly bloody fashion. But was Bordenactuallyqueer?\nWe know that Lizzie remained an unmarried woman for the rest of her free life after being acquitted of the murders of her parents, and there\u2019s no evidence of a sexual or romantic relationship with men or women in her past. But we can trace the start of the gay rumors surrounding Borden: they come from the 1984 bookLizzie, which uses a piece of correspondence as the smoking gun for gay theories surrounding Borden.Author Frank Spieringincluded a note from Lizzie to her friend and tailor, a certain Mrs. Cummings.\n\u201cMy dear Friend,\u201d the note reads, \u201cWhere are you how are you and what are you doing? I dreamed of you the other night but I do not dare to put my dreams on paper. Have you been away and has your little niece been to visit you?\u201c\nIt was the part about not \u201cdaring\u201d to put her dreams on paper that struck Spiering as proof of Borden\u2019s queerness. That hypothesis, however, isn\u2019t exactly rock solid, considering that this note is the only evidence that exists to prop up the theory. It also doesn\u2019t stand up too well against the fact that Victorian women, of which Borden was one, despite living into the 1920s, often wrote this way to one another. Some notes, of course, are muchsaucier than others, but Borden\u2019s looks pretty tame by comparison.\nSubscribe to our newsletter for a refreshing cocktail (or mocktail) of LGBTQ+ entertainment and pop culture, served up with a side of eye-candy.\nSo why the speculation? A few reasons. Despite Lizzie Borden\u2019s story feeling like the ultimate \u201cMy Favorite Murder\u201d episode, her story, for the bulk of the 20th century, was told by men. The same year that Spiering wrote his book, another man, Evan Hunter, fictionalized Borden\u2019s story in yet another book calledLizzie. In an attempt to understand the crime, these men did what the speculative press still loves to do with female celebrities: chalk everything up to their romantic lives.\nThere was something else happening in the late 80s that could also account for this. \u201cThe link between sexual abuse and parricide came forcefully into the public consciousness in 1982,\u201d explains anAmerican Standardarticlefrom 1992, \u201cwhen the sixteen-year-old Richard Jahnke killed his father after enduring years of physical abuse and witnessing the physical and sexual abuse of his mother and sister.\u201d Jahnke\u2019s case was termed by one lawyer \u201cthe first parricide to attract intense national attention since Lizzie Borden.\u201d\nIn the current age, we\u2019re used to thinking about causality and crime, especially in terms of trauma (sexual or otherwise) perpetrators may have experienced in their childhood. In Borden\u2019s time, this line of thinking was even more scandalous than Lizzie\u2019s actual crime. By the 80s, however, writers like Ann Rule were creating an entire industry about trying to dissect a killer\u2019s crime by looking backwards at their childhoods, and their complex sexualities. For the Borden story, it was a perfect storm. An unmarried woman? A turn-of-the-century crime that, 100 years later, still hadn\u2019t come close to being cracked? What else could explain any of it but queerness?\nToday, we\u2019re a little more measured in our understanding of the human psyche\u2026 sort of.Monster: The Lizzie Bordenstory certainly isn\u2019t a subtle or strictly factual accounting of the Borden myth, but it does what Ryan Murphy does best: carve a modern queer horror story out of a grizzly American folk tale.\nJoin us for a vogue down memory lane\u2026\nSign up for theQueertynewsletterto stay on top of the hottest stories in LGBTQ+ entertainment, politics, and culture.\nDon't forget to share:\nDiscover more\n\n\n\nHenry Giardina(he/they) is an Assistant Editor at Queerty. Past publications include the New York Times, The Believer, The New Yorker, The Paris Review Daily, The Atlantic, Film Comment, and Gizmodo. He is @punkgroucho on Twitter.\nConnect with Henry Giardina:\nFor 21 years, Queerty has celebrated the stories, people, and culture that connect our community. This September, help us reach 1,500 members and make sure there\u2019s a home for whatever comes next.\nOr give a different amount:\nWe accept all major credit cards, Apple\u00a0Pay, and\u00a0Google\u00a0Pay.\n\nPleaselog into add your comment.Need an account?Register. It's free and easy.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n1790110800\n1790107200\n1790100000\n1790096400\n\n\n\n\n\n\n1790121600\nLGBTQ Nation\n1790110800\nLGBTQ Nation\n1790109000\nLGBTQ Nation\n1790096400\nOutsports\n\nOutsports\n\nOutsports\n\nOutsports\n\nGayCities\n\nOutsports\n\nLGBTQ Nation\nA Q.Digital Company\nContact\nGayCities: Your Gay Travel Guide\nQueerty is a federally registered trademark of Q.Digital, Inc.\n", "date": "Tue, 22 Sep 2026 22:00:00 +0000"}, {"title": "WATCH: A gay autistic man finds connection in the code-switching new drama Masc", "url": "https://www.queerty.com/watch-a-gay-autistic-man-finds-connection-in-the-code-switching-new-drama-masc-20260922/", "body": "\ud83c\udff3\ufe0f\u200d\ud83c\udf08\u00a0100%\u00a0LGBTQ+\u00a0Owned\nMost queer people know what it\u2019s like to code-switch, to perform different versions of ourselves depending on the environments we\u2019re in as a means of getting by, of surviving.\nThat\u2019s also true of the neurodivergent community; they call this masking\u2014the conscious or unconscious suppression of parts of themselves so that they can fit in among the neurotypical masses.\nSubscribe to our newsletter for a refreshing cocktail (or mocktail) of LGBTQ+ entertainment and pop culture, served up with a side of eye-candy.\nAll of these layers and considerations play intoMasc, a new film that follows a gay, biracial, autistic young man as he tries out new identities in different social settings. The title, as you\u2019ve probably figured out, playing on both the act of masking and the loaded nature of gender presentation for queer men.\nInspired by his own experiences as a gay, neurodivergent person navigating the world, Swedish filmmaker Bertil Nilsson directs from a script co-written with Joshua Griffin (previously seen in the short filmToo Rough) who also stars as the protagonist, Robin.\nWhen we first meet Robin, he\u2019s in what appears to be a job interview, nervously twitching his leg while trying to answer questions succinctly yet frequently wandering off into tangents. But then we see a panel of three people sitting off to the side of the room, each openly offering up their assessment of how he\u2019s doing.\nWhether these three are a manifestation of an inner monologue, or reveal that this supposed interview was actually some form of support group rehearsal remains unclear, which speaks to the ways Nilsson\u2019s film avoids easy answers it observes how Robin responds and adapts to different scenarios.\nStructured in five unique chapters,Mascfinds Robin trying on different looks and different personalities to fit in. In one, he goes to the gym with a handsome new friend (Tega Alexander) he might be harboring a crush on. In another, he\u2019s working at a lab with a chatty nonbinary colleague. Another sees him embarking on an awkward camping trip with his father (Lincoln Conway).\nHere are 4 excellent streaming picks that tell stories at the intersection of autism and queerness.\nWith each scene, we peel back the layers to better understand Robin\u2019s most vulnerable, truest self. In that sense,Mascis a rare queer story that\u2019s not about coming out, coming-of-age, or overcoming trauma, instead focusing on the work we all do to figure out how we can exist in a world that wasn\u2019t necessarily built for us.\nMascfirst premiered earlier this month at the Toronto International Film Festival where it was nominated for the Platform Prize and met with critical praise.Variety\u2018s Murtada Elfadl called it a \u201cthoroughly unique experience\u201d carried by Joshua Grriffin\u2019s \u201ctremendous\u201d performance, whileThe Conrending\u2018s Joey Moser hailsdirector Bertil Nilsson\u2019s \u201cunconventional approach to sexuality, gender, and neurodivergence.\u201d\nA post shared by Bertil Nilsson (@bertilnilsson)\nSpeaking withDeadline, Nilsson underlines the importance of seeing this kind of intersectionality on screen:\n\u201cIt\u2019s worth saying that most of the time autistic characters are portrayed as a stereotype, not a real person,\u201d the filmmaker shares. \u201cEvery autistic person is different and so is Robin. He\u2019s not intended as a representation of autism.\u201d\nNilsson continues: \u201cI think that\u2019s similar to how queer characters have historically been portrayed. Thankfully we now have a much richer queer representation in films and hope our film can contribute to that, as well as adding an autistic perspective.\u201d\nWhile there\u2019s no trailer yet, an early preview clip showcases howMascnavigates Robin\u2019s journey with humor & grace as he confesses his interest in trying out some substances to a compassionate friend (Reece King) who does his best to steer him away from some of the harder stuff. Check it out here:\nMascwill next have its European premiere at the BFI London Film Festival on October 8 (more information here), before heading to NYC\u2019s NewFest on October 21 for its US premiere (tickets & more information here). Be sure tofollow the film on Instagram for all the latest, and to find out where you might be able to catch it next!\nIf you have a potential partner on the autism spectrum, here are some tidbits to take into consideration\u2026\nSign up for theQueertynewsletterto stay on top of the hottest stories in LGBTQ+ entertainment, politics, and culture.\nDon't forget to share:\nDiscover more\n\n\n\nSee all articles byCameron Scheetz.\nFor 21 years, Queerty has celebrated the stories, people, and culture that connect our community. This September, help us reach 1,500 members and make sure there\u2019s a home for whatever comes next.\nOr give a different amount:\nWe accept all major credit cards, Apple\u00a0Pay, and\u00a0Google\u00a0Pay.\n\nPleaselog into add your comment.Need an account?Register. It's free and easy.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n1790107200\n1790100000\n1790096400\n1790092800\n\n\n\n\n\n\n1790110800\nLGBTQ Nation\n1790109000\nLGBTQ Nation\n1790096400\nOutsports\n1790091300\nOutsports\n\nOutsports\n\nOutsports\n\nGayCities\n\nOutsports\n\nLGBTQ Nation\n\nGayCities\nA Q.Digital Company\nContact\nGayCities: Your Gay Travel Guide\nQueerty is a federally registered trademark of Q.Digital, Inc.\n", "date": "Tue, 22 Sep 2026 21:00:00 +0000"}, {"title": "State-run \u201cTr*mp TV\u201d is already a disaster of humiliating proportions", "url": "https://www.queerty.com/state-run-trmp-tv-is-already-a-of-humiliating-proportions-20260922/", "body": "\ud83c\udff3\ufe0f\u200d\ud83c\udf08\u00a0100%\u00a0LGBTQ+\u00a0Owned\nD*n*ld Tr*mp is fed up with the media. Incensed by reporters actually doing their jobs, he, like so many despots before him, has launched a counter-offensive: a state-runpropagandamedia channel calledTr*mp TV.\nThe decision to launch a 24-hour, CSPAN-style White House channel came hot on the heels of Tr*mp\u2019s press ban, and it\u2019s already managed to make the president look like a doddering fool. Because not only is the press fighting back:Tr*mp TVis, unsurprisingly, a resounding flop.\n09/21\nThis week, Tr*mp\u2019s team announced\u2014in a convenient bypassing of the First Amendment\u2014that \u201cWhite House Access is a privilege \u2014 not a right.\u201d That message ironically caused yesterday\u2019s Tr*mp speech to go not only unreported on by standard outlets, but completely unheard. As Tr*mp addresses the crowd, literally not a sound can be heard thanks to the absence of press pool microphones and reporters.\nStay woke with our briefing while staying informed on all things LGBTQ+ entertainment, life, and more!\n09/21\nOops! And it gets worse, because the triumphant launch ofTr*mp TVresulted in basically no viewers whatsoever.\n\u201cThe channel launched at 7 p.m. with around 8,000 viewers,\u201d theDaily Beastreports, \u201cbut the audience had dropped below 4,000 just 90 minutes later.\u201d\nBy 9PM, a measly 3,000 people were still watching.\nWow! Even Kid Rock can manage to drum up more interest than that!  The self-described \u201cking of ratings\u201d might have gotten over 3 million subscribers to the channel, but he\u2019s proving that he can\u2019t hold viewers\u2019 interest without the press covering his every move.\nLike much else the president puts his stamp on these days, the stunt was an unmitigated failure. The timing also couldn\u2019t be worse: with the midterms less than two months away, now is not the time for Tr*mp to be experimenting with state media.\nAll of this is weird enough, but somehow, there\u2019s an even more surprising twist hidden in the headlines.\nWhen Tr*mp stripped CNN, MSNOW, and Politico of their White House credentials, other top outlets followed suit\u2014including Tr*mp\u2019s own favorite, Fox News. One LGBTQ+ outlet, however, has doggedly stayed the course: the stalwartWashington Blade.\nUnfortunately, because of the press ban, they now have to share a pool with conservative outlet The Daily Caller. It\u2019s a decidedly uncomfy turn of events, not mention unprecedented\u2014not since Nixon has there been such a sweeping attack on Washington reporters coming directly from the White House.\nIt was a risky move to begin with, especially since the Tr*mp family likes to be sure they can fudge the numbers on something before committing to it (we haven\u2019t forgotten the greatMelaniamovie ratings debacle!).\nTr*mp\u2019s failure has gotta hurt, especially considering that his arch nemesis, gay icon Rosie O\u2019Donnell, recently had a ratings bonanza aftertaking overJimmy Kimmel Live!for a week in August.\nThe 80-year-old president may have been able to monopolize the attention economy successfully during his first and second terms in office, but that moment is drawing to an obvious close. People are sick of Tr*mp\u2019s games, and by keeping the press away, he\u2019s only hurting himself.\nHe just keeps getting in deeper\nSign up for theQueertynewsletterto stay on top of the hottest stories in LGBTQ+ entertainment, politics, and culture.\nDon't forget to share:\nHenry Giardina(he/they) is an Assistant Editor at Queerty. Past publications include the New York Times, The Believer, The New Yorker, The Paris Review Daily, The Atlantic, Film Comment, and Gizmodo. He is @punkgroucho on Twitter.\nConnect with Henry Giardina:\nFor 21 years, Queerty has celebrated the stories, people, and culture that connect our community. This September, help us reach 1,500 members and make sure there\u2019s a home for whatever comes next.\nOr give a different amount:\nWe accept all major credit cards, Apple\u00a0Pay, and\u00a0Google\u00a0Pay.\nPleaselog into add your comment.Need an account?Register. It's free and easy.\n1790100000\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n1790110800\n1790096400\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n1790119958\nLGBTQ Nation\n1790118000\nLGBTQ Nation\n1790114400\nLGBTQ Nation\n1790105400\nLGBTQ Nation\n1790103600\nLGBTQ Nation\n1790098200\nLGBTQ Nation\n1790094600\nLGBTQ Nation\n\nLGBTQ Nation\n\nLGBTQ Nation\n\nLGBTQ Nation\nA Q.Digital Company\nContact\nGayCities: Your Gay Travel Guide\nQueerty is a federally registered trademark of Q.Digital, Inc.\n", "date": "Tue, 22 Sep 2026 20:00:00 +0000"}, {"title": "10 thoughts I had while watching the balls-out, b*tts-out first episode of Crush House", "url": "https://www.queerty.com/aggregators/10-thoughts-i-had-while-watching-the-balls-out-btts-out-first-episode-of-crush-house/", "body": "\ud83c\udff3\ufe0f\u200d\ud83c\udf08\u00a0100%\u00a0LGBTQ+\u00a0Owned\nThe hotly (and I do mean hotly) anticipatedgay reality showCrush Houseis here, and one episode in, it\u2019s definitely living up to its promise. One host describes it as \u201cthe most fearless, pleasure-filled reality show on the planet,\u201d and so far, no lies told. WhenTroye Sivansang \u201cparty in the bush,\u201d he must have been talking about the ample happy trails of theCrush Housemen.\nSo what\u2019s the premise of the spicy new MENCRUSH competition? Basically, 10 hunky adult entertainers head to a gorgeous beachfront villa in Baja California, Mexico to compete for the crown of MVC\u2014most valuable crush. Between rising OF stars and seasoned performers, the competition is as stiff as they come.\nEpisode 1 starts off with a bang, as our performers (plus hosts JJ Aussie and Josh Moore) congregate for a get-to-know-you dinner. The guys size each other up (literally) and learn the ropes: because onCrush House, \u201cit\u2019s not enough to f*ck well.\u201d You\u2019ve got to be able to summon that charisma, uniqueness, nerve, and taint wherever and whenever possible.\nNow, here are our takeaways\u2026\nSubscribe to our newsletter for a refreshing cocktail (or mocktail) of LGBTQ+ entertainment and pop culture, served up with a side of eye-candy.\n2. Lone twink Austin Ponce may have given us the show\u2019s most quotable line so far when he said, \u201cI\u2019m a whore\u2026I\u2019m a business woman.\u201d Thank you for reminding us that these are not mutually exclusive!\n3. For audiences (me) who have waited way too long for an all-gay reality show that isn\u2019tThe Ultimatum: Queer Love,Crush Housealready feels like a blessed reversal of the usual fare. \u201cYou\u2019re going to flirt,\u201d the hosts promise, \u201cand you\u2019re gonna want to f*ck, but wait until we get the cameras rolling.\u201d Easier said than done!\n4. While the competition doesn\u2019t start until next episode, getting a sense of the guys is fun enough. Especially when the luchador-masked Horse Papo says: \u201c25 loads in an hour? [That\u2019s] the kind of show I could win!\u201d\n5. Rocky Unleashed describes himself as an \u201cerotic mystic\u201d and I\u2019m not sure I\u2019ve ever seen a more intriguing IG bio.\n6. Love how the camera lingers on a certain very wet area as we\u2019re getting to know the guys top to bottom\u2014this show knows how to Dita Von TEASE.\n7. Huge Ajax\u2014whose husband is also here, but not competing\u2014tells us that they are both tops. We love a top 4 top moment! Representation matters!\n8. Speaking of positions, Tim James wants us to know that he just launchedBottom University,to help tops channel their inner bottom through diet and exercise. We love an entrepreneur!\n9. Beau Butler: \u201cWhat lowkey turns me on is a big, smelly pair of balls.\u201d Spoken like a true WeHo resident!\n10. Before the credits roll, we\u2019re reminded that \u201call reality show winners are delusional.\u201d Does that dampen our excitement for the final \u201cGreat Big Orgy\u201d challenge? Not a jot.\nQueer entertainment platform MENCRUSH wants to let fans in on the action of its spicy new reality series.\nSign up for theQueertynewsletterto stay on top of the hottest stories in LGBTQ+ entertainment, politics, and culture.\nDon't forget to share:\nDiscover more\n\n\n\nHenry Giardina(he/they) is an Assistant Editor at Queerty. Past publications include the New York Times, The Believer, The New Yorker, The Paris Review Daily, The Atlantic, Film Comment, and Gizmodo. He is @punkgroucho on Twitter.\nConnect with Henry Giardina:\nFor 21 years, Queerty has celebrated the stories, people, and culture that connect our community. This September, help us reach 1,500 members and make sure there\u2019s a home for whatever comes next.\nOr give a different amount:\nWe accept all major credit cards, Apple\u00a0Pay, and\u00a0Google\u00a0Pay.\n\nAnd no women, right? FINALLY! Excellent photography, too.\nMy! Oh MY!\nI wouldn\u2019t say \u2018no\u2019 to any of those guys.\nPleaselog into add your comment.Need an account?Register. It's free and easy.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n1790110800\n1790107200\n1790100000\n1790096400\n1790092800\n\n\n\n\n\n1790110800\nLGBTQ Nation\n1790109000\nLGBTQ Nation\n1790096400\nOutsports\n1790091300\nOutsports\n\nOutsports\n\nOutsports\n\nGayCities\n\nOutsports\n\nLGBTQ Nation\n\nGayCities\nA Q.Digital Company\nContact\nGayCities: Your Gay Travel Guide\nQueerty is a federally registered trademark of Q.Digital, Inc.\n", "date": "Tue, 22 Sep 2026 19:30:00 +0000"}, {"title": "I hooked up with a guy from my ex-BF\u2019s funeral. Am I a horrible person?", "url": "https://www.queerty.com/archives/i-hooked-up-with-a-guy-from-my-ex-bfs-funeral-am-i-a-horrible-person/", "body": "\ud83c\udff3\ufe0f\u200d\ud83c\udf08\u00a0100%\u00a0LGBTQ+\u00a0Owned\nHi Jake,\nI think I might be a horrible person.\nA few weeks ago, I attended the funeral of an ex-boyfriend. We\u2019d been broken up for years, and he was in a long-term relationship when he passed away. His partner was kind enough to invite me because he wanted everyone important in my ex\u2019s life to have a chance to say goodbye.\nThe service itself was beautiful and emotional. Afterward, though, there was more of a celebration of his life. There was food, drinks, and stories. My ex was one of those magnetic people who seemed to know everyone, so there were a lot of friends, former coworkers, former dates, and other exes there.He was a truly loved and beloved man!\nSubscribe to our newsletter for a refreshing cocktail (or mocktail) of LGBTQ+ entertainment and pop culture, served up with a side of eye-candy.\nAs the night went on, I started talking to a guy I\u2019d never met before. He told me he knew my ex through work. We were pretty drunk and ended up going outside, making out, and eventually hooking up in my car.\nOne of my friends saw what happened, word spread fast, and the next thing I knew, several of them were tearing into me. They said it was a slap in the face to hook up at the gathering for my ex.\nThe thing is, I really don\u2019t think my ex would have cared at all. If anything, he was the kind of person who loved seeing people he knew connect. He was always introducing friends to one another, and I honestly think he would\u2019ve found the whole thing more amusing than offensive. It\u2019s not like I skipped the service or treated the day like a joke. I genuinely came to honor him.\nWas this wildly inappropriate? Do I owe my friends an apology, or are they making this into something bigger than it really was?\nMourning After\nDearMourning After,\nFirst, I\u2019m truly sorry for your loss. Your ex may not have been part of your daily life, but that doesn\u2019t make losing him any less painful. It sounds like you showed up to honor his memory and the relationship you shared. Whatever happened later that evening, it doesn\u2019t change the fact that you came to mourn someone who clearly meant a great deal to a lot of people.\nYour friends, however, came away with a very different understanding of what respect looked like.\nFrom your perspective, the hookup wasn\u2019t mockery or a way of making light of your ex\u2019s death. You attended the service, celebrated his life, then connected with someone afterward. To you, those things weren\u2019t in conflict.\nSo maybe the more important question isn\u2019t whether your friends approved. It\u2019s whether your actions violated your own values.\nSometimes people speak with such confidence that we start to wonder if we\u2019ve done something wrong. But whether you\u2019ve done something wrong isn\u2019t determined by what most people think. Sure, other people\u2019s reactions are worth considering\u2014especially when people we trust call us out\u2014but they\u2019re not automatically proof that we\u2019ve crossed an ethical line.\nIf you don\u2019t believe you acted disrespectfully, don\u2019t let someone else\u2019s certainty override your own judgment. Are you a little irreverent? Maybe. But from everything you\u2019ve shared, this doesn\u2019t sound like disrespect. It sounds like you think your ex would\u2019ve gotten a chuckle out of it.\nIf you aren\u2019t ashamed of what happened, don\u2019t let someone else assign that shame to you. As queer people, we\u2019ve often had to buck society\u2019s norms. Sometimes that means living outside other people\u2019s expectations. But irreverence isn\u2019t the same thing as being a bad person. That\u2019s just old shame talking.\nAt the same time, your friends are allowed to have their feelings. Emotions can run high at funerals, and they may have felt protective of their friend\u2019s memory, or simply believed it wasn\u2019t the time or place. That\u2019s fair.\nSo could a conversation with your friend group be worthwhile? Perhaps. You may not owe them an apology, but they still matter to you. It may help to acknowledge that the night landed differently for them and hear them out. But you don\u2019t have to abandon your own judgment in the process.\nEveryone handles grief and loss differently. If, after thinking it through, you still believe you acted in line with your own values, then stop worrying about whether you\u2019re a horrible person. You don\u2019t need to bury your convictions alongside your ex.\nWantAsk Jakedelivered straight to your inbox each week, along with bonus content, deeper dives, and the opportunity to have your own questions answered directly? Subscribe ataskjaketherapy.com.\nHave a question? Email Licensed Marriage & Family Therapist Jake Myers at[email\u00a0protected]or send a DM onInstagram. Questions may be answered anonymously.\nLooking for an authentic, vetted LGBTQ therapist? VisitLGBTQTherapySpace.com.\nSign up for theQueertynewsletterto stay on top of the hottest stories in LGBTQ+ entertainment, politics, and culture.\nDon't forget to share:\nSee all articles byJake Myers.\nQueerty\u2019s licensed mental health professional helps readers navigate questions related to relationship dynamics, sex, gay culture, and more, all through a lens of releasing shame and living authentically.\nFor 21 years, Queerty has celebrated the stories, people, and culture that connect our community. This September, help us reach 1,500 members and make sure there\u2019s a home for whatever comes next.\nOr give a different amount:\nWe accept all major credit cards, Apple\u00a0Pay, and\u00a0Google\u00a0Pay.\nhonestly I want tons of hookups at  any service for me\nI\u2019d reconsider the advice:  \u201cIf you aren\u2019t ashamed of what happened, don\u2019t let someone else assign that shame to you. \u201d sounds like something way out of place in a time of the Count of Monte Crisco in the White House.\nIf Mourning After (I think he just wrote in so he could use that) is over 30 which probably he is, I would ask how much casual sex does a person need? You couldn\u2019t have exchanged numbers and got together later? And to let youself be seen (you needed some of the attention? you couldn\u2019t let the day be about your ex?) Now for your friend group it\u2019s about what you did at your ex\u2019s funeral; it\u2019s not about what a lovely service it was, etc.\nIt\u2019s not about shame or g@y pride, it\u2019s about honoring the person who passed and keeping the focus on him for the few hours the service and after party take. I\u2019m with the group who thinks it should not be about satisfying one\u2019s exhibitionist urges.\n\u201cYou couldn\u2019t have exchanged numbers and got together later?\u201d Exactly! And while this is useless hindsight, it most likely wouldn\u2019t have happened if they hadn\u2019t gotten drunk.\na stiff p r ick knows no conscience.\nThe ex-boyfriend.  Was his name Lindsey?\n\u201cI genuinely CAME to honor him\u201d\nLOL\nComments are closed.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n1790110800\n1790107200\n1790100000\n1790096400\n1790092800\n\n\n\n\n\n\nGayCities\n\nGayCities\n\nGayCities\n\nGayCities\n\nINTO\n\nGayCities\n\nGayCities\n\nGayCities\n\nGayCities\n\nGayCities\nA Q.Digital Company\nContact\nGayCities: Your Gay Travel Guide\nQueerty is a federally registered trademark of Q.Digital, Inc.\n", "date": "Tue, 22 Sep 2026 19:00:00 +0000"}, {"title": "From hookup app to Capitol Hill: Why Grindr is hosting members of Congress", "url": "https://www.queerty.com/from-hookup-app-to-capitol-hill-why-grindr-is-hosting-members-of-congress-20260922/", "body": "\ud83c\udff3\ufe0f\u200d\ud83c\udf08\u00a0100%\u00a0LGBTQ+\u00a0Owned\nGrindrplayed host to a surprising visitor.\nDemocratic Rep. Laura Friedman, whose district includes parts of West Hollywood, recently stopped by the company\u2019s headquarters in WeHo. The image would\u2019ve been unfathomable even a decade ago: Grindr is, of course, a gay sex app. But Grindr has also been getting into the political game.\nThis past spring, itthrew its first ever D.C. partyat a $9 million Georgetown mansion ahead of the White House Correspondents\u2019 Dinner. Reviews of the party were\u2026mixed, to say the best.\nNow, Grindr is hosting elected members of Congress.\nStay woke with our briefing while staying informed on all things LGBTQ+ entertainment, life, and more!\nWhat was this meeting about, and what are Grindr\u2019s political motivations?\nSince going public in late 2022, Grindr has been trying to change its image. Company execs now refer to the platform as a \u201cglobal gayborhood.\u201d\nLike the physical manifestation, there is a large buy-in fee: subscription tiers can cost hundreds annually and more luxury offerings areapparently on the way. CEO George Arison says that Grindr wants to \u201ccapture people\u2019s wallets for services and products, rather than just dating and connections.\u201d\nCurrently, those services and products range from AI wingmen\u2013Grindr calls the service \u201cgAI\u2122,\u201d\u2013and a digital healthcare service called Woodwork that offers ED meds, GLP-1s and peptide therapy.\nFor all the complaints that users levy on Grindr, Arison\u2019s strategy appears to be working. Grindr now expects revenue of at least $540 million this year, an increase of nearly 25% over 2025.\nGrindr is set to unveil a so-called \u201cluxury\u201d offering\u2026\nLike all tech companies, Grindr has myriad of interests in Washington.\nOne of them is HIV prevention. Before the pre-WHCD party, chief Grindr lobbyist Joe Hack\u2013former chief of staff to Republican Sen. Debra Fischer, who attended the shindig\u2013highlighted the company\u2019s efforts in astatement toQueerty.\n\u201cGrindr represents a global community with real stakes in Washington. The issues being debated here\u2013HIV funding, digital privacy, LGBTQ+ human rights\u2013are daily life for our community,\u201d he said.\nIn terms of HIV prevention, Grindr has been successful. While the Tr*mp admin has eliminated hundreds of millions in grants supporting HIV and STI prevention, Grindr\u2019s deal to distribute HIV home tests has survived.\nThe company has also lobbied on issues related to \u201csurrogacy and IVF access for same-sex couples,\u201d according toPolitico.\nAs far as other asks, politicos told me that Grindr could be pushing for App Store age-verification laws, like tech giants Meta, Snapchat and X.\nPrivacy data is another possible sticking point.\nOn X, photos of Rep. Friedman\u2019s meeting with Grindr employees went viral.\nI got the chance to visit@Grindr\u2019s headquarters in my district to talk to leadership and staff about my work in Congress, especially when it comes to protecting my LGBTQ constituents.I\u2019m always going to stand up to protect my LGBTQ constituents from hatred and discrimination.pic.twitter.com/90D9CuM0NR\nSomeone familiar with the meeting toldQueertythat Friedman toured the offices and met with around 50 employees. They discussed issues ranging from HIV prevention and IVF access to the congresswoman\u2019s record opposing Tr*mp.\nA Grindr spokesperson toldQueertythat Friedman and the Grindr team talked about their social impact efforts, Grindr for Equality, as well as \u201chealth, human rights, and the impact of emerging technologies.\u201d\nA member of the House Progressive Cause, Friedman has advocated for AI oversight. Arison is anenthusiastic booster of AI, and has aggressively integrated the technology into Grindr\u2019s user experience.\nFriedman also sits on the House Science, Space, and Technology Committee, a perch that will become more powerful if Democrats retake Congress in November.\nAppropriately, one of the first details that gays noticed from the photo opp was\u2026 that guy sitting next to the congresswoman is super hot.\nKinda need the man in the white t shirthttps://t.co/Kg9oBbWNJB\nBeyond him, however, it doesn\u2019t seem like many of the employees pictured would have much use for the grid. The group was largely comprised of women.\nshockingly few gay men working at grindr?https://t.co/hDeQK92Nd3\nThat observation dovetails with many of the critiques of Grindr in its current form. The platform has become increasingly paywalled and alienating, with AI wingmen and so-called \u201cphantom profiles.\u201d\nLike other public companies, Grindr is driven to make the most money possible. That\u2019s led to complaints that it\u2019s milking its user base.\n\u201cAs of its company \u2018protects\u2019 LGBTQ+ people,\u201d read a popular comment.\nAs if gay people even like this company. And as if this company \u201cprotects\u201d LGBTQ people lol\nAs a 59-year-old straight woman, Friedman likely isn\u2019t immersed in gay hookup app discourse. Maybe some of the negative feedback will sharpen her views.\n\u201cGrindr is a major contributor to the problems in the gay community, but I appreciate the sentiment and recognize you trying,\u201d somebody wrote.\nGrindr is a major contributor to the problems in the gay community, and their app is a total cesspool. But, I appreciate the sentiment and recognize you trying, thank you.\nNow that a congressperson has visited Grindr, the question is, who\u2019s heading to Sniffies?\nWho will be the first member of Congress to visit Sniffies HQ?https://t.co/pofVK1k0h9\nDid Grindr\u2019s D.C. party mean anything?\nSign up for theQueertynewsletterto stay on top of the hottest stories in LGBTQ+ entertainment, politics, and culture.\nDon't forget to share:\nAlex Reimeris an Assistant Editor at Queerty who writes about sports and fitness, politics and irreverent gay things. In a previous life, he was a talk radio loudmouth in Boston. He currently lives in Chicago.\nConnect with Alex Reimer:\nFor 21 years, Queerty has celebrated the stories, people, and culture that connect our community. This September, help us reach 1,500 members and make sure there\u2019s a home for whatever comes next.\nOr give a different amount:\nWe accept all major credit cards, Apple\u00a0Pay, and\u00a0Google\u00a0Pay.\nClavicular, \u2018Looksmaxxing\u2019 Influencer, Charged With Ray **ping a 1***7-Year-Old. A friend of Waron\u2019s.\nBuh bye.\nGrinder will k rash and bern. Along with its subscribers.\nPleaselog into add your comment.Need an account?Register. It's free and easy.\n1790107200\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n1790114400\n1790110800\n1790096400\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n1790119958\nLGBTQ Nation\n1790118000\nLGBTQ Nation\n1790114400\nLGBTQ Nation\n1790105400\nLGBTQ Nation\n1790103600\nLGBTQ Nation\n1790098200\nLGBTQ Nation\n\nLGBTQ Nation\n\nLGBTQ Nation\n\nLGBTQ Nation\n\nLGBTQ Nation\nA Q.Digital Company\nContact\nGayCities: Your Gay Travel Guide\nQueerty is a federally registered trademark of Q.Digital, Inc.\n", "date": "Tue, 22 Sep 2026 18:00:00 +0000"}, {"title": "Orville Peck shows off his Street Fighter gains in new behind-the-scenes snaps", "url": "https://www.queerty.com/orville-peck-shows-off-his-street-fighter-gains-in-new-behind-the-scenes-snaps-20260922/", "body": "\ud83c\udff3\ufe0f\u200d\ud83c\udf08\u00a0100%\u00a0LGBTQ+\u00a0Owned\nIn case you didn\u2019t know, there\u2019s a newStreet Fightermovie on the way. Directed by Kitao Sakurai, it stars Jason Momoa, David Dastmalchian, and Noah Centineo.\nHowever, there\u2019s also a number of other well-known names among the various fighters, including queer country star Orville Peck as Vega.\nSubscribe to our newsletter for a refreshing cocktail (or mocktail) of LGBTQ+ entertainment and pop culture, served up with a side of eye-candy.\nVega is apt for Peck, given the character is a masked knife-fighter \u2013 and Peck is rarely seen without his own mask.\nThe singer-turned-actor shared some behind-the-scenes snaps from the movie on his Instagram yesterday\u2026 instantly arousing interest. Swipe through to see.\nA post shared by Orville Peck (@orvillepeck)\nMany people expressed surprise because they hadn\u2019t realized Peck is in the movie. To be fair, he\u2019s only in the trailer for a couple of seconds.\nOthers were just impressed by Peck\u2019s prominent glutes.\nPeck said that tickets for the movie, which is out October 16, are now on sale.\nThis is not Peck\u2019s first acting gig, given he took on the role of the emcee inCabareton Broadway last year for four months. However, this is his first appearance on the big screen. And it appears he was particularly keen to play Vega, according to director Sakurai.\n\u201cEverybody had a really deep, personal connection to the characters,\u201d Sakurai said in a recent interview withEmpiremagazine.\n\u201cOrville Peck plays Vega, and he had a very touching story, where his sexual awakening as a gay man came about by playing Street Fighter. With Vega, he was like, \u2018There\u2019s something about this character that is speaking to me,\u2019 and that was a huge part of his identity and how he discovered who he was. He cared about that character as a kid.\u201d\nWatch a trailer below.\nPeck\u2019s new album, Mule, is out now, and the track \u201cRing The Bell\u201d has prompted interest in its lyrics.\nThe country crooner is jacked from his movie debut, and he\u2019s not been shy about showing it off!\nSign up for theQueertynewsletterto stay on top of the hottest stories in LGBTQ+ entertainment, politics, and culture.\nDon't forget to share:\nDiscover more\n\n\n\nDavid Hudsonis a contributing editor to Queerty who specializes in film, music, health, travel and queer culture. Connect with him on X and Bluesky and DM him with potential story ideas.\nConnect with David Hudson:\nFor 21 years, Queerty has celebrated the stories, people, and culture that connect our community. This September, help us reach 1,500 members and make sure there\u2019s a home for whatever comes next.\nOr give a different amount:\nWe accept all major credit cards, Apple\u00a0Pay, and\u00a0Google\u00a0Pay.\n\nPleaselog into add your comment.Need an account?Register. It's free and easy.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n1790114400\n1790110800\n1790107200\n1790100000\n\n\n\n\n\n\n1790121600\nLGBTQ Nation\n1790110800\nLGBTQ Nation\n1790109000\nLGBTQ Nation\n1790096400\nOutsports\n\nOutsports\n\nOutsports\n\nOutsports\n\nGayCities\n\nOutsports\n\nLGBTQ Nation\nA Q.Digital Company\nContact\nGayCities: Your Gay Travel Guide\nQueerty is a federally registered trademark of Q.Digital, Inc.\n", "date": "Tue, 22 Sep 2026 17:00:00 +0000"}, {"title": "How unlearning religious trauma saved my life", "url": "https://www.queerty.com/how-unlearning-religious-saved-my-life-20260922/", "body": "\ud83c\udff3\ufe0f\u200d\ud83c\udf08\u00a0100%\u00a0LGBTQ+\u00a0Owned\nThe following is an excerpt from the new bookHealing Sacred Wounds: A Guide to Overcoming Religious and Spiritual TraumabySC Nealy, available now from Bloomsbury.\nDrawing on empirical research, therapeutic practice, and the author\u2019s own lived experience, SC Nealy explains how harmful religious environments can produce trauma symptoms comparable to those associated with abuse, coercion, and complex PTSD. It clarifies what religious trauma is, how it manifests emotionally and physically, and why it has remained underrecognized despite its widespread impact. Particular attention is given to the experiences of LGBTQ+ individuals, for whom religious harm is often compounded by rejection, shame, and identity-based violence.\nEach chapter combines psychoeducation with guided reflection and self-directed exercises designed to support healing. Readers are invited to identify and validate traumatic experiences, challenge internalized blame and shame, establish healthy boundaries, and cultivate self-compassion. Nealy also addresses the complexities of witnessing or participating in harm, offering pathways for accountability, repair, and forgiveness without minimizing injury.\nRather than prescribing a single outcome, the book honors multiple paths forward. Some readers may choose to rebuild a faith identity on their own terms; others may step away from religion entirely. Both choices are treated as valid expressions of healing. Discussion questions support use by clinicians, support groups, and educational settings, while an accompanying online Toolbox for Survivors provides worksheets and additional resources.\nSubscribe to our newsletter for a refreshing cocktail (or mocktail) of LGBTQ+ entertainment and pop culture, served up with a side of eye-candy.\nAccessible, affirming, and grounded in years of clinical expertise, this guide offers a hopeful path for survivors and helps them move toward long-term psychological well-being.\n\u201cI would never love a grandchild of mine that was born out of wedlock,\u201d my father said to me and the father of the child that was in my womb at the time as we were standing across the kitchen island from him in his home in Florida.\nI had been twenty-eight years old when I\u2019d met Trevor, and in the midst of just beginning to publicly explore my bisexuality, which was, at the time, how I thought I might identify as my sexual orientation. I had had secret relationships with women since middle school, but I\u2019d discounted those as nothing more than intensely strong female friendships, or just sexual experiments with women, or convinced myself that all straight women kissed girls sometimes and that it was completely normal.\nPrior to meeting Trevor, I\u2019d made many attempts to come out publicly, but this time was the first time I had finally told my girlfriends that I was dating a woman and identified as bisexual in 2018. I didn\u2019t have anyone else in my life then that was openly gay or bisexual, and, honestly, I didn\u2019t really get any support from the cishet world I was in.\nA few people just said they accepted and loved me no matter what, an aunt told me it didn\u2019t matter that I dated some women as long as I ended up with a man anyway, my father told me that he hated the sin but loved the sinner, the church I was in at the time chose to actively pretend they didn\u2019t know and refused to acknowledge homosexuality in any manner, and one of my best friends at the time told me she couldn\u2019t be my friend anymore because my life seemed too chaotic for her. No one celebrated with me or made me feel excited and empowered about this new identity, and, at worst, several people actively shamed me for it. So, when I met Trevor awhile later, I made the decision\u2014mostly unconsciously\u2014to stop trying to pursue the queer identity that I had barely tried on for a few months.\nI wanted normalcy and acceptance, and he was safely in all of those categories. Normal, down-to-earth man who my parents would easily love and accept and who I could see creating a family happily with. He was as uninterested in traditional anything as I was and was supportive from day one about me figuring out my sexuality and being open about my feelings for women.\nOur friendship developed quickly and easily, and while I was never able to participate in all of the sexual activities a straight woman probably would have wanted to participate in, he never pushed me to do anything I wasn\u2019t comfortable with, and I truly believed that this was the dream. Being in a cishet-passing relationship that felt safe and comfortable in which I could raise a family and not lose the love and acceptance of my own family and that I didn\u2019t have to do sexual acts that felt uncomfortable felt like I\u2019d found the cheat code to having the life I thought I wanted for myself.\nSix months into meeting, we decided to have children and got pregnant on our first try. I was so thrilled and so excited; this was the family that I had always wanted. I had built my life to be all the things my father wanted me to be: highly educated, good job, successful career, handsome cis white partner, and giving him his first grandchild. Surely, my father would love me now.\nAnd then I stood there in his kitchen, pregnant and swollen and all the things I thought he wanted me to be, while he told Trevor that he admired him for putting up with me because I had always been so difficult to love and that he wanted Trevor and me to do the right thing by our child and get married so our child\u2019s soul wouldn\u2019t be damned to hell. I was numb listening to my father speak about how hard I was to love, how difficult I was as a person, and how surprising it was that anyone could tolerate me enough to raise a child with me. I wasn\u2019t healed enough yet at that point to think, \u201chey, this isn\u2019t okay, and no parent should talk that way about his daughter or his future granddaughter.\u201d All I could think from my complex-traumatized brain was, \u201cokay, we need to get married.\u201d I need to be better for my father\u2014I need to prove that I\u2019m worthy of love.\nI was five months pregnant at the altar and had no clue that I was about to embark on a harrowing two-year journey of giving birth to a child with significant medical complications. We\u2019d spend most of her first two years of life in and out of our local children\u2019s hospital during a worldwide pandemic, and that\u2019s when the cracks in Trevor\u2019s and my marital fa\u00e7ade would really begin.\nCOVID-19regulations didn\u2019t allow two parents to be at the hospital at one time. We\u2019d trade on and off occasionally, but for the most part, as the chest-feeding parent, I had to be at the hospital around the clock. All we had to lean on at that time was our friendship\u2014texting and phone calls back and forth, FaceTime videos of the baby, Zoom calls with our pastor before major surgeries\u2014and any semblance of a romantic connection between him and me just became too tiring for me to pretend anymore. My energy and focus was on keeping my daughter alive, his was on keeping our bills paid, and we stayed in this survival state for years.\nWhile the romantic connection he and I had briefly tried to give ourselves might have gone away, our friendship grew into family. He became my go-to for emotional support and love and taught me through that what actual unconditional love and familial support was. When my daughter was at her sickest, I began battling with my own family dynamics and realizing that the treatment I had accepted was now beginning to happen to my daughter.\nMy stepmother was angry at me one day for telling my youngest sister that she needed to finish paperwork that would help her pay for college, and so my stepmother decided to get back at me by deleting the fundraising page for my daughter that had her entire medical history on it and the finances keeping us afloat while I was out of work and at the hospital. It was in this moment that I realized my family would use my children to hurt me and that suddenly felt too far.\nUntil that point, I\u2019d allowed them to hurt me, but I was not going to allow them to hurt my child as well. It was also when I realized that even if I\u2019d curated my entire life to be exactly who I thought my dad wanted me to be that it was never going to be enough. He still took my stepmother\u2019s side, said horrible things to me, and made me feel so small. Trevor\u2019s unconditional support and defense of me and his daughter became my new family, and I slowly began leaning into what it would feel like to let go of everything I\u2019d once known about what family meant.\nAs my daughter\u2019s health stabilized and we came home years later, Trevor and I opened up our marriage, and I began dating women. I confronted my family and began being more open about who I was, and they quickly faded away. They had only ever loved me conditionally, and when I stopped making myself fit those conditions, there was no real love left to lean on. I began exploring queerness in a whole new way, and Trevor cheered me on every step of the way.\nWhen I realized I probably had always been gaslighting myself into my attraction to men out of my own religious trauma and deep-rooted shame, and, in reality, I was actually a lesbian, Trevor gave me a hug and said he loved me no matter what. We decided to have another child together to create the family that we both wanted to give our children, we got a divorce, and moved in a block apart from each other.\nWe continue to see each other every day, co-parent as best friends, and our romantic partners all are required to embrace and lean into our unique family dynamic (and they do, so lovingly). Our daughters are lively, secure, and so deeply loved; they\u2019ll never know conditions on his or my love, and they\u2019ll never feel made to fit a narrative of who he or I might want them to be other than held, accepted, and loved. My wife considers him one of her best friends, and our children feel fully loved by every adult in their life without ever having to choose sides.\nHe and I had broken the cycle.\nSC Nealy(they/she) is a licensed psychotherapist, the owner of LGBT+ Counseling Collaborative, and an award-winning speaker and author with over seventeen years of clinical specialty in LGBT+ issues, religious trauma, and relationship counseling.Healing Sacred Wounds: A Guide to Overcoming Religious and Spiritual Traumais available wherever books are sold.\nSign up for theQueertynewsletterto stay on top of the hottest stories in LGBTQ+ entertainment, politics, and culture.\nDon't forget to share:\nDiscover more\n\n\n\nFor 21 years, Queerty has celebrated the stories, people, and culture that connect our community. This September, help us reach 1,500 members and make sure there\u2019s a home for whatever comes next.\nOr give a different amount:\nWe accept all major credit cards, Apple\u00a0Pay, and\u00a0Google\u00a0Pay.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n1790114400\n1790110800\n1790107200\n1790100000\n1790096400\n\n\n\n\n\n1790121600\nLGBTQ Nation\n1790110800\nLGBTQ Nation\n1790109000\nLGBTQ Nation\n1790096400\nOutsports\n\nOutsports\n\nOutsports\n\nOutsports\n\nGayCities\n\nOutsports\n\nLGBTQ Nation\nA Q.Digital Company\nContact\nGayCities: Your Gay Travel Guide\nQueerty is a federally registered trademark of Q.Digital, Inc.\n", "date": "Tue, 22 Sep 2026 16:00:00 +0000"}, {"title": "Drenched pro tennis player shows gays that jockstraps are also used for that other thing\u2026", "url": "https://www.queerty.com/aggregators/drenched-pro-tennis-player-shows-gays-that-jockstraps-are-also-used-for-that-other-thing/", "body": "\ud83c\udff3\ufe0f\u200d\ud83c\udf08\u00a0100%\u00a0LGBTQ+\u00a0Owned\nJockstraps are very vers\u2014just like the guys who wear them!\nNow a gay staple on par with speedos and harnesses, the scantily undergarment was often featured in Tom of Finland\u2019s drawings and softcore adult magazines. Ostensibly \u201cfitness\u201d rags, these mid-20th century relics featured flexing bodybuilders whose man-bits were barely covered by bikinis, g-strings and yes, jocks.\nThough jocks fell out of style in the 1980s and \u201990s, they\u2019ve experienced a resurgence over the last couple of decades. All sorts of major fashion brands\u2014ranging from gay favorites like Addicted and AussieBum to mainstream labels such as Calvin Klein and Versace\u2014have their own designs.\nIn our gay centric world, it\u2019s easy to forget that jocks were originally designed for sportsmen\u2013and still worn by athletes today. We received that reminder this past weekend, when a video of a soaked pro tennis player went viral.\nSubscribe to our newsletter for a refreshing cocktail (or mocktail) of LGBTQ+ entertainment and pop culture, served up with a side of eye-candy.\nDressed in all white, Chinese player Sun Fajing\u2019s body was drenched with sweat during a match at the Guangzhou Challenger. His perspiration made his entire figure visible, including what he was wearing to protect his assets.\nThe gays were aghast!\n\u201cJockstrap??? \ud83e\uddd0\ud83e\uddd0\ud83e\uddd0,\u201d posted a user named \u201cGarrett.\u201d\nHis rhetorical question, and the accompanying video of a soaked Sun, has been viewed more than20 million times.\nJockstrap??? \ud83e\uddd0\ud83e\uddd0\ud83e\uddd0pic.twitter.com/flNIJJtaJ9\nGiven the post\u2019s popularity, it broke containment and migrated onto the algorithm of gays around the world, many of whom pointed out that, indeed, Sun appears to be wearing a jockstrap. And that\u2019s totally normal\u2026 because he\u2019s an athlete!\n\u201cWhat do you think jockstraps were created for?\u201d read one popular reply.\nwhat do you think jockstraps were created for\nThe wise answer is \u201ceasy access.\u201d But the truth is all about support.\nobsessed w how jockstraps have become such a staple of gay culture that ppl forget they're sports gear. like babe that's what it's for \ud83d\udc80https://t.co/j8XuR2CwMK\nJockstraps were created for athletes lol.https://t.co/nc6RwUZErA\nGay guys do realize jock straps were originally invented not for homosexual butt sex but to hold a cup in place or\u2026.https://t.co/Qcf7t0MXHi\nFrom bicycle couriers and health fads to physique pictorial magazines andLady Gaga, here\u2019s the delightfully strange history of jockstraps.\nAs Daniel Villarreal outlined in theweird (and kinda sexy) history of jockstraps, a man named C.F. Bennett invented a \u201cjockey strap\u201d in 1897 to protect bicycle jockeys from knocking their merchandise against their bike seats while riding over cobblestone streets.\nIn the early 1900s, jocks were marketed as a way to avoid injuries, and later as a way to promote bodily health.The Heidelberg Electric Belt, with batteries and electrodes along the front and back of its waist, claimed to fix all sorts of medical issues.\nOf course, that was a fallacy. But the protection that jockstraps offered was real. Boxers began wearing them to safeguard their jewels from \u201cbelow the belt\u201d hits, though they eventually upgraded to protective cups.\nStill, jocks continue to be a ubiquitous part of sports culture. That\u2019s where the gays come in. We have a long history of co-opting homoerotic sports attire for their own pleasure,just like the singlet.\nOften reserved for kink play, singlets aren\u2019t nearly as prevalent as jockstraps. With the advent of PrEP and the merciful decline of bottom shaming, gays of all stripes wear itty-bitty jocks to flaunt their bums.\nAnd therein lies the irony. While jocks may be designed to cover up one\u2019s nether regions, they leave the backside exposed.\nUnfortunately for Sun, he was defeated in the second round of the tournament. But at least we got a nice view along the way!\nyall forget gays made jockstraps\u2026gay\u2026https://t.co/pAuUmbUJkm\nThe New York Times once called it the \u201cmost mocked athletic uniform in existence.\u201d\nSign up for theQueertynewsletterto stay on top of the hottest stories in LGBTQ+ entertainment, politics, and culture.\nDon't forget to share:\nDiscover more\n\nAlex Reimeris an Assistant Editor at Queerty who writes about sports and fitness, politics and irreverent gay things. In a previous life, he was a talk radio loudmouth in Boston. He currently lives in Chicago.\nConnect with Alex Reimer:\nFor 21 years, Queerty has celebrated the stories, people, and culture that connect our community. This September, help us reach 1,500 members and make sure there\u2019s a home for whatever comes next.\nOr give a different amount:\nWe accept all major credit cards, Apple\u00a0Pay, and\u00a0Google\u00a0Pay.\nDid anyone notice that the player\u2019s name is Sun Fajing, not Fujing?\nPleaselog into add your comment.Need an account?Register. It's free and easy.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n1790114400\n1790110800\n1790107200\n1790100000\n1790096400\n\n\n\n\n\n1790104800\nOutsports\n1790096400\nOutsports\n\nOutsports\n\nOutsports\n\nOutsports\n\nOutsports\n\nOutsports\n\nOutsports\n\nOutsports\n\nOutsports\nA Q.Digital Company\nContact\nGayCities: Your Gay Travel Guide\nQueerty is a federally registered trademark of Q.Digital, Inc.\n", "date": "Tue, 22 Sep 2026 15:30:00 +0000"}, {"title": "Somehow Don Jr.\u2019s Russian wedding scandal just got 10 times worse", "url": "https://www.queerty.com/somehow-don-jr-s-russian-wedding-just-got-10-times-worse-20260922/", "body": "\ud83c\udff3\ufe0f\u200d\ud83c\udf08\u00a0100%\u00a0LGBTQ+\u00a0Owned\nLast week, a bombshell report informed us that Don Jr.\u2019s 3-day wedding bonanaza in the Bahamas was paid for, in part, by a mysterious Russian oligarch who also happens to be the man behind the2024 Olympics misgendering scandal.\nThat was crazy enough. Today, fresh information has come to light, and Don Jr. is in even bigger trouble.\nUmar Kremlev, the close friend of Putin who footed the bill for parts of the lavish celebration, was found guilty of extortion and battery scandals as a younger man. More recently, Kremlev has been at the center of yet another scandal, this time concerning the \u201cmass abduction, illegal deportation, and forced relocation\u201d of Ukrainian children through the group Healthy Fatherland.\nUm\u2026WTAF?!\nStay woke with our briefing while staying informed on all things LGBTQ+ entertainment, life, and more!\nThe group, which on the surface markets itself as a rehabilitation service for troubled youth, is sanctioned in Ukraine, as is Kremlev himself. To make matters even worse, the organization is run by the sister of Kremlev\u2019s wife. And somehow, it gets even worse than that\u2026\nThis summer, before Andrew Tate\u2019s arrest, the IBA (of which Kremlev is president) planned to hold a boxing eventhosted by Tate, himself accused of trafficking minors across country lines. Tate\u2014whose ties to Don Jr. and Barron Tr*mp have already been publicized\u2014was arrested before the event could take place.\nIf none of this is a good look for Don Jr., it\u2019s an even worse look for Melania, who\u2019s been campaigning to bring the estimated1,200 displaced Ukrainian childrenhome.\n\u201cEvery reunion is a reminder that compassion, dignity, and family can transcend even the darkest of times: war,\u201d Melania told press this July. \u201cMy representative and I continue working with the governments of Russia and Ukraine to safely return children to their families and communities.\u201d\nSince Melania is not known for speaking up about literallyany issue, this is really the one political priority she\u2019s felt comfortable putting her name next to. So you can imagine how embarrassing it is to realize that your son-in-law is not only working with the enemy, but accepting their money. Last week when the scandal broke, the first lady held another emergency press conference to help put out the fire. But alas, the fire has only continued to spread as new twists emerge in the case.\nHer husband, meanwhile, is pleading the fifth. \u201cI have no idea who Umar is, never heard of him,\u201d he stated last week. \u201cIt was an \u2018afterparty\u2019 given in their honor. Not a big deal!\u201d\nPerhaps he has the luxury of thinking so, but for the rest of the country, it\u2019s very much a big deal. Not only is the IBA under Kremlev plagued by scandal, the anti-LGBTQ+ Russian agenda he represents isn\u2019t going to help Tr*mp\u2019s flagging poll numbers. Tr*mp has been followed by the whisper of Russian scandal since the very start of his political career. The fact that his son just got him embroiled in a fresh scandal right before the midterms isn\u2019t going to do Don Jr. any favors in the Republican party\u2026 or in the will.\nA new complaint makes some pretty explosive allegations\nSign up for theQueertynewsletterto stay on top of the hottest stories in LGBTQ+ entertainment, politics, and culture.\nDon't forget to share:\nHenry Giardina(he/they) is an Assistant Editor at Queerty. Past publications include the New York Times, The Believer, The New Yorker, The Paris Review Daily, The Atlantic, Film Comment, and Gizmodo. He is @punkgroucho on Twitter.\nConnect with Henry Giardina:\nFor 21 years, Queerty has celebrated the stories, people, and culture that connect our community. This September, help us reach 1,500 members and make sure there\u2019s a home for whatever comes next.\nOr give a different amount:\nWe accept all major credit cards, Apple\u00a0Pay, and\u00a0Google\u00a0Pay.\nA note to all network news outlets. NOW is your chance to ignore DJT. For good.\nIt will annihilate the publicity ho.\nPresident Trump thinks the answer to skepticism about artificial intelligence is to try and change its name.\nAt the U.N. General Assembly Tuesday, Tromp told the audience that the \u201cUnited States also totally rejects any attempt to construct a globalist scheme to control for the artificial intelligence being spoken of so much now.\n\u201cHere and after officially called \u2018super intelligence\u2019 changing the name and that the use of the word artificial makes intelligence fake. It makes it sound fake, and it is not fake, it\u2019s actually amazing, but we have to be careful,\u201d TrAmp said. \u201cIn fact, it is exactly the opposite of what it purports.\u201d\nThat he doesn\u2019t see the irony.\nPleaselog into add your comment.Need an account?Register. It's free and easy.\n1790107200\n1790100000\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n1790110800\n1790096400\n1790092800\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n1790119958\nLGBTQ Nation\n1790118000\nLGBTQ Nation\n1790114400\nLGBTQ Nation\n1790105400\nLGBTQ Nation\n1790103600\nLGBTQ Nation\n1790098200\nLGBTQ Nation\n1790094600\nLGBTQ Nation\n\nLGBTQ Nation\n\nLGBTQ Nation\n\nLGBTQ Nation\nA Q.Digital Company\nContact\nGayCities: Your Gay Travel Guide\nQueerty is a federally registered trademark of Q.Digital, Inc.\n", "date": "Tue, 22 Sep 2026 15:00:00 +0000"}, {"title": "Google Expands Gemini Notebook With Syncing, Interactive Tools and a New Name", "url": "https://dev.to/alifar/google-expands-gemini-notebook-with-syncing-interactive-tools-and-a-new-name-1dm", "body": "Posted onSep 22\u2022Originally published atscalevise.com\nGoogle is expandingGemini Notebook, the product previously called NotebookLM, from a source-grounded research tool into a connected knowledge workspace across the Gemini app and NotebookLM. Official updates published in 2026 describe synced notebooks, real-time conversations, mobile audio recording and interactive overviews, while the product's July rebrand aligns it more closely with Gemini's wider app experience.\nA brief social signal about ongoing work does not identify a particular feature, release date or access plan. The more useful picture for teams comes from Google's published updates: Gemini Notebook is undergoing a broader product expansion, with availability and some benefits tied to subscription plans and phased rollouts rather than one newly specified capability.\nGoogle introducedNotebooks in Geminion April 8, 2026 as personal knowledge bases that can organize chats, files and projects. The company said notebooks sync between the Gemini app and NotebookLM, positioning them as a way to retain context across related work instead of repeatedly assembling the same source material in separate conversations.\nThat distinction matters in practical use. A notebook can provide a defined place for project files and relevant Gemini chats, which may make it easier to return to research, planning or reference material without rebuilding the context each time. Google also connected the expansion to subscription plans and cross-app workflows, although the supplied research does not specify plan prices or a complete feature-by-plan breakdown.\nOn July 16, 2026, Google confirmed that NotebookLM would become Gemini Notebook. According to theofficial Google Workspace update on the Gemini Notebook rename, the new branding would roll out across interfaces over the following weeks and requiredno administrator action. For existing users, the change is primarily one of product identity and interface branding, rather than a request to migrate notebooks manually.\nGoogle's September 15, 2026 update then described several new tools for Gemini Notebook:\nThe September update is oriented heavily toward studying, but the underlying changes can also be relevant to work that depends on repeatedly interpreting a bounded collection of documents, notes and project material. That is an implication of the confirmed capabilities, not a separate Google promise about business use cases.\nThe central development is not simply a name change. Google is connecting a notebook-based workspace with theGemini app, so a notebook can serve as a continuing context layer for related chats and files. For a business team, that could be useful when people repeatedly consult the same policies, customer research, project briefs or planning material.\nThe likely value isless context reconstruction. Instead of locating documents and restating the background for every new interaction, users can work from an organized notebook. Results will still depend on the material placed in the notebook and on how carefully users review generated output. Google's announcements establish the notebook and interaction features, but they do not promise accuracy for every use case or replace normal checks on important work.\nThe interactive features also broaden Gemini Notebook beyond reading and static summaries. Quizzes, flashcards and video summaries are explicitly framed as study tools. Organizations should therefore distinguish between confirmed product capabilities and their own potential internal applications, such as training or onboarding. Those applications may be worth testing, but they are not announced as dedicated business features in the supplied materials.\nGoogle describes rollout as phased, beginning on the web before mobile and broader geographic availability. The rename itself was expected to appear across interfaces in the weeks after the July announcement. Access to certain capabilities and offers is connected to subscription status, while the September student promotion has eligibility and market conditions.\nThis means prospective users should not assume that every Gemini Notebook feature is available in every interface, country or plan at the same time. The verified research supports a direction of travel, but it does not provide a universal release schedule, a full pricing table or a feature matrix. Businesses comparing options should check the current Gemini and Workspace availability information for their account and region before designing a process around a particular capability.\nFor businesses evaluating how notebook-based AI could support research, project documentation or internal knowledge work,Scalevise AI consultancycan help identify practical use cases, assess where human review is needed and map the tool into existing workflows. A focused evaluation can prevent teams from adopting features that do not fit their processes while revealing opportunities to reduce repetitive preparation work. Request a consultation to assess a practical Gemini Notebook approach for your team.\nWhat is Gemini Notebook?\nGemini Notebook is Google's new name for NotebookLM. Google is integrating notebooks with the Gemini app so users can organize chats, files and projects in personal knowledge bases.\nWhen did Google rename NotebookLM to Gemini Notebook?\nGoogle announced the rename on July 16, 2026 and said the new branding would roll out across interfaces in the following weeks without administrator action.\nWhat new Gemini Notebook tools has Google announced?\nGoogle announced real-time notebook conversations, mobile audio recording and interactive overviews that include quizzes, flashcards and video summaries.\nIs Gemini Notebook available on every plan and in every region?\nThe supplied research describes phased availability and benefits tied to subscription status, but it does not provide a complete plan-by-plan or region-by-region availability list.\nGemini Notebook's evolution shows Google moving NotebookLM toward a more connected Gemini knowledge workspace. The confirmed changes are substantial: synced notebooks, a unified name and new ways to capture, discuss and review notebook content. The practical opportunity is to test whether a structured collection of trusted project material can reduce repeated setup work, while confirming current access and availability before making it part of a regular process.\nTemplates let you quickly answer FAQs or store snippets for re-use.\nAre you sure you want to hide this comment? It will become hidden in your post, but will still be visible via the comment'spermalink.\nHide child comments as well\nConfirm\nFor further actions, you may consider blocking this person and/orreporting abuse\nDEV Community\u2014 A space to discuss and keep up software development and manage your software career\nBuilt onForem\u2014 theopen sourcesoftware that powersDEVand other inclusive communities.\nMade with love andRuby on Rails. DEV Community\u00a92016 - 2026.\nWe're a place where coders share, stay up-to-date and grow their careers.", "date": "Tue, 22 Sep 2026 22:30:30 +0000"}, {"title": "314 IPP and 44897 Raw Print Results: The Office Device Layer on the Open Internet", "url": "https://dev.to/kozhevniko/314-ipp-and-44897-raw-print-results-the-office-device-layer-on-the-open-internet-co", "body": "Posted onSep 22\nPrinters are the least interesting device category in most security programmes and one of the most consistently reachable. A ZoomEye query set collected on 22 September 2026 returned:\nThe raw print port has more than a hundred times the count of the IPP port. That ratio is the most useful observation in the dataset, because it describes how printers are configured rather than how many printers exist.\nPort 9100 is the raw printing port, historically associated with HP JetDirect and now implemented by most network printers and print servers. It accepts a print job as a raw byte stream and passes it to the print engine. There is no authentication, no encryption and no protocol negotiation.\nThe port is enabled by default on a very large number of devices because it is the simplest way to make printing work. It is also the port that most print management software uses, which means disabling it frequently requires changing the print queue configuration on every client.\nThat combination, default-enabled and disruptive to disable, explains the count. The raw print port is not exposed because someone chose to expose it; it is exposed because it is on by default and nobody turned it off.\nIPP on port 631 is the Internet Printing Protocol. It is an HTTP-based protocol with support for authentication and TLS. It is the modern standard and the default on many operating systems.\nThe count is much smaller for two reasons. First, IPP is frequently bound to localhost or to the local network only, because it is used by the operating system's print subsystem rather than by the network. Second, IPP over TLS is often on a different port, and an IPP service that requires authentication may not respond to an unauthenticated probe in a way that allows identification.\nSNMP on port 161 is included because it is the management protocol for the same device population. A printer that answers on 161 with the default community stringpublicexposes its configuration, including model, firmware version, serial number and often the printer's own address book.\nThe count of 299 is small relative to the raw print port, which suggests that SNMP is more often filtered than the print port. That is consistent with network policy: SNMP is a management protocol and is more likely to be restricted, while printing is a user-facing function and is more likely to be permitted.\nA printer is not only a printer. Modern office devices are general-purpose computers with a print engine attached. Depending on the model, an exposed device may expose:\nThe raw print port is the entry point that requires no credentials at all, which is why the count matters more than the protocol's apparent simplicity.\nThese figures are a snapshot collected on 22 September 2026. The raw print port count is likely a reasonable estimate of directly reachable devices; the IPP and SNMP counts are more sensitive to how each implementation responds to a probe and to how the service is bound.\nA device that answers on 9100 is a reachable print service. It is not confirmed to store documents or to have a default administrative password. Those require device-specific verification.\nTemplates let you quickly answer FAQs or store snippets for re-use.\nAre you sure you want to hide this comment? It will become hidden in your post, but will still be visible via the comment'spermalink.\nHide child comments as well\nConfirm\nFor further actions, you may consider blocking this person and/orreporting abuse\nDEV Community\u2014 A space to discuss and keep up software development and manage your software career\nBuilt onForem\u2014 theopen sourcesoftware that powersDEVand other inclusive communities.\nMade with love andRuby on Rails. DEV Community\u00a92016 - 2026.\nWe're a place where coders share, stay up-to-date and grow their careers.", "date": "Tue, 22 Sep 2026 22:20:06 +0000"}, {"title": "How to Query Remote Jobs, Public Tenders, and City Populations with a Single API", "url": "https://dev.to/helick/how-to-query-remote-jobs-public-tenders-and-city-populations-with-a-single-api-4c71", "body": "Posted onSep 22\nFinding reliable public data for developer projects often means stitching together half a dozen brittle scrapers or signing up for multiple paid services.\nI built a consolidatedData-as-a-Service (DaaS)platform that groups three commonly needed datasets behind a fast, single endpoint interface:\nTemplates let you quickly answer FAQs or store snippets for re-use.\nAre you sure you want to hide this comment? It will become hidden in your post, but will still be visible via the comment'spermalink.\nHide child comments as well\nConfirm\nFor further actions, you may consider blocking this person and/orreporting abuse\nDEV Community\u2014 A space to discuss and keep up software development and manage your software career\nBuilt onForem\u2014 theopen sourcesoftware that powersDEVand other inclusive communities.\nMade with love andRuby on Rails. DEV Community\u00a92016 - 2026.\nWe're a place where coders share, stay up-to-date and grow their careers.", "date": "Tue, 22 Sep 2026 22:12:48 +0000"}, {"title": "Moving from small programs to building applications", "url": "https://dev.to/blogher/moving-from-small-programs-to-building-applications-29kg", "body": "Posted onSep 22\nFor most of my career I have always written scripts or small programs as a systems administrator and data engineer. I used powershell, python, bash and SQL. While scripting involved programming and writing code, none of those small programs spanned across multiple files and directories. Now that I am (re)learning to program, it is quite interesting that I am choosing to become a software engineer in an era when LLMs and agents can write code and build entire programs.\nI tried vibecoding a few things - a personal website, a blog and a note taking app. However, it bothered me to not know what any of these meant. Whenever I ran into an error, I would prompt the agent to make corrections until I eventually ran out tokens for the day. I think it\u2019s been easier writing programs from scratch than trying to understand a behemoth of tools, languages and frameworks I had never used before - all at once.\nThe difference between a script and an application is that applications have a lot of moving parts. The connection to the database, keeping a file open, connecting to a server, uploading files and modifying them. That\u2019s why some errors are harder to track down. Troubleshooting and debugging also takes longer and I have to figure out where each problem stems from. Was the database schema defined incorrectly or did I make the wrong connection to the database?\nFinally, learning from scratch has been a great privilege. Vibecoding made easy for me.\nTemplates let you quickly answer FAQs or store snippets for re-use.\nAre you sure you want to hide this comment? It will become hidden in your post, but will still be visible via the comment'spermalink.\nHide child comments as well\nConfirm\nFor further actions, you may consider blocking this person and/orreporting abuse\nDEV Community\u2014 A space to discuss and keep up software development and manage your software career\nBuilt onForem\u2014 theopen sourcesoftware that powersDEVand other inclusive communities.\nMade with love andRuby on Rails. DEV Community\u00a92016 - 2026.\nWe're a place where coders share, stay up-to-date and grow their careers.", "date": "Tue, 22 Sep 2026 22:10:30 +0000"}, {"title": "Why the \"Move Fast\" Era Just Hit a Wall: 5 Engineering Truths for 2026", "url": "https://dev.to/ayka_code/why-the-move-fast-era-just-hit-a-wall-5-engineering-truths-for-2026-2leg", "body": "Posted onSep 22\nBy 2026, the promise of \"limitless velocity\" has finally met the reality of operational toil. We have spent years chasing the endorphin-rich experience of the \"new\"\u2014new frameworks, new architectures, and now, new intelligence. But for those of us who have lived through the microservice transition and survived the early AI hype cycle, the view from the top is different. We aren't seeing a revolution of speed; we are seeing a crisis of maintenance fatigue.\nEngineering leaders are discovering that the \"speed\" they gained in 2024 was often just a loan taken out against their future stability. This document outlines five counter-intuitive truths synthesized from 2026 industry data and architectural research. It is a roadmap for those ready to trade industry hype for evidence-based adoption and the kind of long-term maintainability that actually keeps a business alive.\nThe Junior Anxiety: What if I don't know enough ? (a free and Premium PDF guide)\nThe 2026 landscape is defined by a grim irony: we are using AI more than ever, yet we believe in it less. According to the latest Uvik Software data, AI adoption among developers has hit a staggering 84%. However, trust in the accuracy of that output has plummeted to just 29%, a sharp decline from the 40% trust rate we saw in 2024.\nEven Claude Sonnet\u2014which remains the most admired LLM at a 67.5% satisfaction rating\u2014cannot bridge this gap. We are living in the era of the \"Almost Right\" frustration. We\u2019ve all seen it: code that compiles perfectly, looks plausible, and even passes basic lints, only to fail subtly in production with logic errors that take twice as long to debug as the code took to \"write.\"\nExposure hasn't built confidence; it has revealed the liability. As one developer survey noted:\n\"66% of developers say their biggest frustration is AI output that is \u2018almost right, but not quite.\u2019\"\nWhen you are \"shoveling coal\" into a system at this scale, the \"Almost Right\" output isn't a shortcut; it's a productivity tax.\nThe Junior Anxiety: What if I don't know enough ? (a free and Premium PDF guide)\nIn my years as a strategist, I\u2019ve seen more companies die from \"cool tech\" than from a lack of features. As Dan McKinley famously argued, an organization only has about three \"Innovation Tokens\" to spend. These tokens represent your limited capacity to do something weird, hard, or creative. If you spend those tokens on a niche database or an experimental language, you have nothing left for your core mission\u2014reshaping your industry.\nThe risk of new technology is rooted in what I call the \"demonic presence\" of unknown unknowns. In boring, mature technology\u2014think Postgres or MySQL\u2014you have \"known unknowns.\" You know exactly how these tools fail under load because the industry has spent twenty years documenting the wreckage. You might even \"hate\" your standard stack, but that is actually a sign of mastery. You know its failure modes. You know how to fix it at 3 AM.\n\"You can't worry about the big picture and ask intelligent questions about the direction of the product if you're busy arguing about which database or alerting system to use.\"\nMastery is the state where everything still sucks, but it feels manageable. Choosing boring tech allows you to bank your brainpower for the problems that actually move the needle.\n\u25b6\ufe0f (+22 Vids Hours + visual Guides) The Modern Developer Masterclass: The Complete Software Engineering, Cloud, DevOps & AI engineering Course\nThe microservice era promised us independent scaling and team autonomy, but for many, it delivered a hairball of distributed complexity. In 2026, we are seeing the \"Great Un-Migration.\" High-profile engineering organizations\u2014Amazon Prime Video, Segment, and even the service-mesh giant Istio\u2014have begun moving back to monolithic architectures.\nThis shift is driven by five primary realizations:\nThe headline case remains Amazon Prime Video, which achieved a staggering 90% infrastructure cost reduction by abandoning serverless microservices for a monolithic approach. For 2026, the \"Modular Monolith\" has become the pragmatic middle ground\u2014providing clean logical boundaries without the distributed systems tax. Even Istio consolidated its control plane into \"Istiod\" to escape the toil of its own microservice sprawl.\nWe are currently suffering from a \"Productivity Illusion.\" Developers feel 20% faster when an AI assistant is writing their boilerplate, but independent research from GitClear and METR suggests they are actually 19% slower overall due to the debugging and security overhead.\nThe impact on code health is quantifiable:\nFor the first time in the history of large-scale code analysis, \"copy/paste\" code now exceeds \"moved\" code. AI isn't refactoring; it\u2019s duplicating. This creates a massive security liability: AI-coauthored PRs are 2.74x more likely to contain vulnerabilities. In fact, research shows that 29.1% of Python code generated by Copilot contains potential security weaknesses. In the Move Fast era, we aren't building faster; we're just generating technical debt at an accelerated rate.\nIn a high-turnover environment, \"head knowledge\" is a terminal liability. We have all experienced the \"Alex doesn't work here anymore\" syndrome\u2014where the rationale for a critical database choice or a system boundary disappears the moment a senior dev signs their next offer letter.\nTo survive, architecture must be an immutable record. The Markdown Architectural Decision Record (MADR) format is no longer a \"nice to have\"; it is a survival requirement. A true Nygard-style ADR requires four parts: Status, Context, Decision, and Consequences.\nMost teams fail at the Consequences section because they avoid honest trade-offs. A senior-level ADR should state plainly: \"We accept write bottlenecks in exchange for ACID guarantees.\" If there is no \"Bad\" listed in your consequences, you haven't made a decision\u2014you've written a marketing fluff piece.\nBy 2026, these records have a new purpose. AI agents are now being used to reason over these ADR corpuses to detect \"architectural drift.\" When the code begins to wander away from the documented intent, the AI flags the drift, ensuring that the system Alex built stays the system Alex intended.\nThe overarching theme of 2026 is that we have reached the limit of \"Engineering Adoption.\" The goal is no longer to find the next shiny object or to automate every line of code. Our new mandate is Engineering Governance.\nTrue seniority in this era isn't about how many languages you know or how fast you can prompt an LLM; it is about knowing when to say \"no\" to a third-party vendor and when to move a distributed service back into a monolith. We must manage the long-term consequences of our speed.\nAsk yourself: Is your current velocity building a product that will survive until 2030, or are you just the most efficient technical debt generator in the building?\n\u25b6\ufe0f (+22 Vids Hours ++ visual Guides) The Modern Developer Masterclass: The Complete Software Engineering, Cloud, DevOps & AI engineering Course\nTemplates let you quickly answer FAQs or store snippets for re-use.\nAre you sure you want to hide this comment? It will become hidden in your post, but will still be visible via the comment'spermalink.\nHide child comments as well\nConfirm\nFor further actions, you may consider blocking this person and/orreporting abuse\nDEV Community\u2014 A space to discuss and keep up software development and manage your software career\nBuilt onForem\u2014 theopen sourcesoftware that powersDEVand other inclusive communities.\nMade with love andRuby on Rails. DEV Community\u00a92016 - 2026.\nWe're a place where coders share, stay up-to-date and grow their careers.", "date": "Tue, 22 Sep 2026 22:04:24 +0000"}, {"title": "Spelling numbers in Arabic is harder than it looks: the four rules behind tafqeet", "url": "https://dev.to/support_confileo_ce7442eb/spelling-numbers-in-arabic-is-harder-than-it-looks-the-four-rules-behind-tafqeet-3pgp", "body": "Posted onSep 22\nMost \"number to words\" libraries translate digits one at a time. That works in English and fails in Arabic on the very first cheque. I maintain an Arabic number-to-words converter (\u062a\u0641\u0642\u064a\u0637) used for invoices and cheques, and these are the rules that broke every naive version I wrote.\nEnglish saystwenty-five. Arabic saysfive and twenty:\u062e\u0645\u0633\u0629 \u0648\u0639\u0634\u0631\u0648\u0646. Any loop that walks the digits left to right and concatenates words produces \u00ab\u0639\u0634\u0631\u0648\u0646 \u0648\u062e\u0645\u0633\u0629\u00bb, which a bank clerk reads as wrong at a glance.\nThis is the rule everyone forgets. One and two agree with the counted noun; three to ten take the other gender:\nSo the words for 3\u201310 depend on the currency, not only on the digit. A converter that hard-codes \u00ab\u062b\u0644\u0627\u062b\u0629\u00bb is correct for pounds and wrong for lira.\nThis is the part that makes a lookup table impossible:\nThe same rule applies to the scale words themselves, which is why 3,000 is \u00ab\u062b\u0644\u0627\u062b\u0629 \u0622\u0644\u0627\u0641\u00bb but 11,000 is \u00ab\u0623\u062d\u062f \u0639\u0634\u0631 \u0623\u0644\u0641\u0627\u064b\u00bb and 100,000 is \u00ab\u0645\u0627\u0626\u0629 \u0623\u0644\u0641\u00bb.\n\u00ab\u0645\u0627\u0626\u062a\u0627\u0646\u00bb on its own, but\u0645\u0627\u0626\u062a\u0627 \u0623\u0644\u0641and\u0645\u0627\u0626\u062a\u0627 \u062c\u0646\u064a\u0647when a noun follows (the dual in a construct loses its final nun). A converter that emits \u00ab\u0645\u0627\u0626\u062a\u0627\u0646 \u0623\u0644\u0641\u00bb is one character off, and that character is exactly what a proof-reader looks for.\nThat covers the integer part in the masculine. A real cheque line still needs the currency noun chosen by rule 3, the feminine table for feminine currencies, the fraction (\u00ab\u0648\u062e\u0645\u0633\u0648\u0646 \u0642\u0631\u0634\u0627\u064b\u00bb), and the framing banks expect:\u0641\u0642\u0637 ... \u0644\u0627 \u063a\u064a\u0631.\nThe code above is open source asarabic-tafqeet(MIT, one file, tests included). The full version \u2014 pound, riyal, dirham, dinar and dollar, the fraction, the \u0641\u0642\u0637/\u0644\u0627 \u063a\u064a\u0631 framing, and words back to digits \u2014 runs as a free converter:\u062a\u0641\u0642\u064a\u0637 \u0627\u0644\u0623\u0631\u0642\u0627\u0645 \u2014 Arabic number to words for cheques and invoices. There is an Englishnumber to wordstool on the same site if you need both languages on one invoice.\nIf you find a number it spells wrong, tell me in the comments \u2014 that is how every rule above was found.\nTemplates let you quickly answer FAQs or store snippets for re-use.\nAre you sure you want to hide this comment? It will become hidden in your post, but will still be visible via the comment'spermalink.\nHide child comments as well\nConfirm\nFor further actions, you may consider blocking this person and/orreporting abuse\nDEV Community\u2014 A space to discuss and keep up software development and manage your software career\nBuilt onForem\u2014 theopen sourcesoftware that powersDEVand other inclusive communities.\nMade with love andRuby on Rails. DEV Community\u00a92016 - 2026.\nWe're a place where coders share, stay up-to-date and grow their careers.", "date": "Tue, 22 Sep 2026 22:03:39 +0000"}, {"title": "Building Willow: A Garbage-Collected Native Language with Cranelift and a Stackless Async Runtime", "url": "https://dev.to/lechatthecat/building-willow-a-garbage-collected-native-language-with-cranelift-and-a-stackless-async-runtime-51l", "body": "Posted onSep 22\nGithub link:https://github.com/lechatthecat/willow\nWillow is an experimental statically typed programming language that compiles to native code through Cranelift. It has class-based OOP, algebraic enums, pattern matching, Option and Result, garbage collection, and a stackless async runtime with tasks, channels, cancellation, and select.\nI have been building it mostly as a one-person project, partly to understand how compilers, garbage collectors, and schedulers interact when they are designed together rather than as isolated components.\nWillow is notproduction-ready. But it has reached the point where it can run non-trivial programs, compile multiple modules, and be benchmarked meaningfully against established runtimes.\nMost of the languages I like make a different trade-off somewhere: Go keeps the language and tooling simple but has limited traditional OOP; Java has a mature managed runtime but depends on a large VM; Rust gives very strong control and performance but requires explicit ownership reasoning.\nWillow is an experiment in a different combination: native AOT compilation, automatic memory management, familiar OOP, algebraic data types, and lightweight structured concurrency.\nWillow examples\nClasses and interfaces are also first-class language features:\nWillow supportsmatch\ndeferis also supported.\nResult,Optionare also supported.nullornildon't exist in Willow, butOption::Noneexists instead.\nThe runtime is not built on Tokio, async-std, or another host-language scheduler. Willow has its own task scheduler, channels, cancellation model, and garbage collector.\nThe GC is generational. Major marking is concurrent, while minor collections still use stop-the-world collection. Async frames are GC-managed, and their layouts are derived from compiler liveness information.\nThe numbers are in milliseconds.Willow has lighter task footprint: 814.8 B/task vs Go 2,742.4, Java 1,289.1.\nFibonacci:Willow can already compete with Go in this microbenchmark.\nObject churn:Go is still tens of times faster.\nVirtual dispatch:Willow still pays far too much per method call.\nChannels:The abstraction is working, but the underlying task/channel path remains expensive.\nWillow is still experimental. The standard library is small, several runtime paths are much slower than Go or Java, tooling is incomplete, and neither the language nor its ABI should be considered stable yet.\nThe next area I want to explore is tooling designed for coding agents.\nInstead of making the language syntax \"AI-friendly,\" I want the compiler to expose semantic facts directly. One planned feature is a semantic blast-radius query:\nLevel 1: code directly changedLevel 2: direct callers/usersLevel 3: callers of those callerseffect changes such as newly introduced blocking, suspension, allocation, or panic paths\nA more ambitious version could detect that a changed function is reachable from a retry loop and that the change introduced a new side effect, then surface a review question such as:\n\u201cCould this side effect execute twice if the operation succeeds but its acknowledgement is lost?\u201d\nTemplates let you quickly answer FAQs or store snippets for re-use.\nAre you sure you want to hide this comment? It will become hidden in your post, but will still be visible via the comment'spermalink.\nHide child comments as well\nConfirm\nFor further actions, you may consider blocking this person and/orreporting abuse\nDEV Community\u2014 A space to discuss and keep up software development and manage your software career\nBuilt onForem\u2014 theopen sourcesoftware that powersDEVand other inclusive communities.\nMade with love andRuby on Rails. DEV Community\u00a92016 - 2026.\nWe're a place where coders share, stay up-to-date and grow their careers.", "date": "Tue, 22 Sep 2026 22:03:15 +0000"}, {"title": "Myanmar: Women Political Prisoners Tortured, Ill-treated", "url": "https://www.hrw.org/news/2026/09/22/myanmar-women-political-prisoners-tortured-ill-treated", "body": "Forbidden\nDetails: cache-den-kden1300058-DEN 1790118058 3235730496\nVarnish cache server", "date": "Tue, 22 Sep 2026 19:00:00 -0400"}, {"title": "News Wrap: Grand jury declines to bring charges in death of Nolan Wells", "url": "https://www.pbs.org/newshour/show/news-wrap-grand-jury-declines-to-bring-charges-in-death-of-nolan-wells", "body": "Your support keeps PBS News free and accessible.\nLeave your feedback\nIn our news wrap Tuesday, a grand jury declined to bring charges in the death of Nolan Wells, a coroner found that actress Hayden Panettiere died from an accidental overdose of several drugs, including fentanyl and Republican Sen. John Curtis is calling for the Senate Judiciary Committee to investigate Donald Trump Jr. after news that a Russian businessman partially bankrolled his wedding party.\nVisit CANVAS, PBS NewsHour's art hub.\nSupport Provided By:Learn more\nSubscribe to Here\u2019s the Deal, our politics\n                 newsletter for analysis you won\u2019t find anywhere else.\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\n\u00a9 1996 - 2026 NewsHour Productions LLC. All Rights Reserved.\nPBS is a 501(c)(3) not-for-profit organization.\nSections\nAbout\nStay Connected\nSubscribe to Here's the Deal with Lisa Desjardins\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\nSupport for News Hour Provided By", "date": "Tue, 22 Sep 2026 18:45:43 -0400"}, {"title": "What the Paramount-Warner Bros. merger settlement means for Hollywood", "url": "https://www.pbs.org/newshour/show/what-the-paramount-warner-bros-merger-settlement-means-for-hollywood", "body": "Your support keeps PBS News free and accessible.\nGeoff BennettGeoff Bennett\nKarina CuevasKarina Cuevas\nBridget CraigBridget Craig\nLeave your feedback\nA day after Paramount Skydance and a group of state attorneys general announced a settlement clearing the way for its merger with Warner Bros. Discovery, questions remain about whether the deal does enough to protect consumers and competition. Geoff Bennett discussed more with Oliver Darcy of Status.\nNotice: Transcripts are machine and human generated and lightly edited for accuracy. They may contain errors.\nGeoff Bennett:\nA day after Paramount Skydance and a group of state attorneys general announced a settlement clearing the way for its merger with Warner Bros. Discovery, questions remain about whether the deal does enough to protect consumers and competition.\nThe $111 billion merger would create one of the biggest media companies in the world, bringing multiple film studios and cable channels, streaming services, and major franchises under one roof. It would also put two major news organizations, CBS News and CNN, under the same corporate umbrella.\nCalifornia Attorney General Rob Bonta, who helped lead the antitrust challenge before reaching the settlement, acknowledged he still has concerns about the merger.\nRob Bonta (D), California Attorney General: This settlement is not a vote of support for this merger. It is not a blessing of the broader merger. Broadly speaking, we believe further consolidation in markets that are central to American economic life doesn't serve the American economy, consumers, or competition well.\nGeoff Bennett:\nBut Bonta emphasized several key conditions of the agreement, including Paramount being required to release 30 films in theaters per year for the first two years, a $1.5 billion investment for homegrown film production, and the creation of an editorial independence board to support CBS News and CNN. The deal itself still needs to be approved by a federal judge.\nFor more, we're joined now by Oliver Darcy of Status.\nOliver, thanks for being with us.\nSo what ultimately led to this settlement? Was it Ellison's threat to move Paramount out of California or something else entirely?\nOliver Darcy, Founder, Status News:\nThe threat from Ellison to move out of California seems to have loomed large over this.\nIf you remember, Rob Bonta was calling this an illegal merger for some time and really vowing to go all the way with this and saying he was comfortable going to court. And something happened in the recent weeks.\nAnd that something is David Ellison was getting very serious about his threat to take production jobs from California and move them to maybe Nashville or to Texas. And you saw the governor, Governor Gavin Newsom, and local officials really worried about this and say they would like to see a settlement.\nAnd the pressure, from our understanding, reporting on this, was really great on Rob Bonta. He really needed to settle this to make sure that David Ellison left those Paramount production jobs and potentially the Warner production jobs in California.\nGeoff Bennett:\nOf all of the conditions attached to this settlement, which is expected to have the biggest effect on the entertainment industry?\nOliver Darcy:\nYes, I mean, a lot of this can have a huge effect on the entertainment business. I think that people are looking at the settlement that Rob Bonta got and not super thrilled with it.\nFor instance, the big point about they have to release 30 films a year in theaters, if you look at the fine print, they do have to release 30 films, but they can co-produce half of them. So they only really have to release 15 films on their own, and then they can co-produce the other half and get those in the theaters to satisfy that requirement.\nUltimately, Geoff, this is going to result in a lot of layoffs in the entertainment sector. And that's going to happen because there's going to be a lot of synergies between Warner Bros. Discovery and Paramount, the back offices, people who support communications and publicity, people who support H.R. jobs, legal jobs.\nThose jobs, I would imagine, would be almost immediately consolidated, and you're going to see a number of layoffs, and then it's going to just continue. This company that's going to be formed is going to be under billions of dollars in debt. They're going to need to find a lot of cost savings.\nAnd that's going to result regardless in a loss of jobs moving forward.\nGeoff Bennett:\nAnd Ellison has agreed to create this independent editorial board for CBS and CNN. But critics say what is described as a safeguard is in many ways hollow. Explain why.\nOliver Darcy:\nYes, there's a number of concerns here.\nOne, usually, when you have an editorial board that's supposed to safeguard the CNN and CBS from management, you wouldn't have management get to select all the board members. In this case, Paramount gets to choose the five people who are going to be on this board. This board reports directly to Paramount's chief compliance officer.\nSo, again, Paramount ultimately controls this board. It's unclear what power this board has. I mean, it doesn't say what power they have. If they find, for instance, that Paramount's management is interfering with editorial, what power do they have to remedy that situation? It's very unclear and not certain.\nThis board also doesn't have to be formed for 180 days, meaning that David Ellison and company can, I guess, overhaul CNN, as they have overhauled CBS News, without having to deal with this board for the first six months or so. And so there are a lot of concerns this board is really just, as Kara Swisher, the CNN contributor and technology journalist, said, a fig leaf for management to get this deal across the finish line.\nIt's something that Rob Bonta can hold up and say, look, we got this board that's going to safeguard CNN's editorial independence. But, ultimately, this board does not seem to have any real teeth.\nGeoff Bennett:\nAnd, Oliver, who actually polices all of this? How quickly could the states act if Paramount decides to start testing the boundaries of this settlement?\nOliver Darcy:\nI mean, they theoretically have some avenues to enforce this. That's what Bonta wanted.\nAnd so, for instance, if they don't release 30 films a year, for each film they don't release, they're on the hook for I think $30 million or so. But, again, ultimately, this was not what Bonta was asking for.\nThe state attorneys general, if you remember, they were asking for structural remedies, a change to the structure of this Warner Bros. Discovery acquisition. And what they got ultimately were behavioral remedies, saying basically that Paramount will behave in certain ways moving forward.\nAnd that's not what they said they wanted. And so I think, when you look at whether it's the Writers Guild that's upset with this deal -- a number of critics, like Mark Ruffalo, who's been outspoken, said this deal was handing a win to oligarchs and Donald Trump supporters.\nIf you look at why they're upset with this deal, it's because they basically didn't get anything that they wanted, and you're now allowing a deal which you had previously described as an illegal merger to go through.\nGeoff Bennett:\nOliver Darcy of the media newsletter Status.\nOliver, thanks again for your time. We appreciate it.\nOliver Darcy:\nThank you, Geoff.\nSep 21\nByWyatte Grantham-Philips, Michael R. Sisak, Associated Press\nJul 24\nByWyatte Grantham-Philips, Associated Press\nJul 22\nByWyatte Grantham-Philips, Associated Press\nJul 20\nByAssociated Press\nJul 13\nByWyatte Grantham-Philips, Associated Press\nGeoff BennettGeoff Bennett\nGeoff Bennett is co-anchor and co-managing editor of PBS News Hour, where he brings incisive reporting and sharp analysis to the political and cultural forces shaping American life.\nKarina CuevasKarina Cuevas\nBridget CraigBridget Craig\nVisit CANVAS, PBS NewsHour's art hub.\nSupport Provided By:Learn more\nSubscribe to Here\u2019s the Deal, our politics\n                 newsletter for analysis you won\u2019t find anywhere else.\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\n\u00a9 1996 - 2026 NewsHour Productions LLC. All Rights Reserved.\nPBS is a 501(c)(3) not-for-profit organization.\nSections\nAbout\nStay Connected\nSubscribe to Here's the Deal with Lisa Desjardins\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\nSupport for News Hour Provided By", "date": "Tue, 22 Sep 2026 18:40:32 -0400"}, {"title": "Harris joins El-Sayed on campaign trail as Democrats focus on Michigan Senate race", "url": "https://www.pbs.org/newshour/show/harris-joins-el-sayed-on-campaign-trail-as-democrats-focus-on-michigan-senate-race", "body": "Your support keeps PBS News free and accessible.\nLisa DesjardinsLisa Desjardins\nLeila JacksonLeila Jackson\nLeave your feedback\nWith 42 days until Election Day, the race to control Congress is now in high gear. In Michigan, one of Democrats\u2019 biggest names made their first major campaign stop of the cycle. Former Vice President Kamala Harris joined Senate candidate Abdul El-Sayed at a roundtable in Detroit focused on Black maternal health. Lisa Desjardins discussed the Michigan race with strategist Joel Payne.\nSep 19\nByAssociated Press\nSep 11\nByMatthew Daly, Associated Press\nAug 09\nByWill Weissert, Associated Press\nAug 06\nByScott Bauer, Associated Press\nAug 06\nByJoey Cappelletti, Associated Press\nLisa DesjardinsLisa Desjardins\nLisa Desjardins is a correspondent for PBS News Hour, where she covers news from the U.S. Capitol while also traveling across the country to report on how decisions in Washington affect people where they live and work.\nLeila JacksonLeila Jackson\nSupport Provided By:Learn more\nSubscribe to Here\u2019s the Deal, our politics\n                 newsletter for analysis you won\u2019t find anywhere else.\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\n\u00a9 1996 - 2026 NewsHour Productions LLC. All Rights Reserved.\nPBS is a 501(c)(3) not-for-profit organization.\nSections\nAbout\nStay Connected\nSubscribe to Here's the Deal with Lisa Desjardins\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\nSupport for News Hour Provided By", "date": "Tue, 22 Sep 2026 18:35:39 -0400"}, {"title": "Researchers explore new uses for abortion-related drugs", "url": "https://www.pbs.org/newshour/show/researchers-explore-new-uses-for-abortion-related-drugs", "body": "Your support keeps PBS News free and accessible.\nSarah VarneySarah Varney\nRachel WellfordRachel Wellford\nLeave your feedback\nFor years, mifepristone and similar drugs have been used to treat uterine fibroids and endometriosis. Now researchers are exploring whether these drugs could have other uses in women\u2019s health, including preventing some cancers. But the politics and restrictions surrounding these drugs could affect the research. Special correspondent Sarah Varney reports for our series, Beyond Abortion.\nMay 04\nByMark Sherman, Geoff Mulvihill, Associated Press\nMay 02\nByHannah Schoenbaum, Geoff Mulvihill, Associated Press\nMay 01\nByAssociated Press\nSarah VarneySarah Varney\nRachel WellfordRachel Wellford\nRachel Wellford is a general assignment producer for PBS NewsHour.\nSupport Provided By:Learn more\nSubscribe to Here\u2019s the Deal, our politics\n                 newsletter for analysis you won\u2019t find anywhere else.\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\n\u00a9 1996 - 2026 NewsHour Productions LLC. All Rights Reserved.\nPBS is a 501(c)(3) not-for-profit organization.\nSections\nAbout\nStay Connected\nSubscribe to Here's the Deal with Lisa Desjardins\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\nSupport for News Hour Provided By", "date": "Tue, 22 Sep 2026 18:30:36 -0400"}, {"title": "He Holds the Keys to Mamdani\u2019s Economic Vision", "url": "https://www.nytimes.com/2026/09/21/nyregion/shorris-mamdani-edc.html", "body": "Please enable JS and disable any ad blocker", "date": "Mon, 21 Sep 2026 12:56:16 +0000"}, {"title": "Grand jury declines to indict anyone in death of Nolan Wells", "url": "https://www.cbsnews.com/news/nolan-wells-grand-jury-no-charges/", "body": "Watch CBS News\nUpdated on:  September 22, 2026 / 6:55 PM EDT/ CBS News\nThe grand jury reviewing the case ofNolan Wells, an 18-year-old who was found dead after going missing following an outing with friends on Mississippi's Horn Island on the Fourth of July, has determined there was no criminal wrongdoing in the teen's death.\nWells' death sparked a considerable amount of speculation across social media, much of which was rebuked by the grand jury.\n\"The Grand Jury finds no credible evidence to support the public allegations that any persons who rode to or from Horn Island on July 4, 2026, on the Triton, the Palm Beach, or the Tideline engaged in any criminal conduct related to the death of Mr. Wells,\" the report states. \"The allegations against the occupants of the Triton, the Palm Beach, and the Tideline [boats that traveled to the island] are not based in truth, and are solely the result of public clamor and private malice.\"\n\"In addition to bringing prosecutions against the guilty, the grand jury must also exonerate those who have been falsely accused,\" Jackson County District Attorney Angel Myers McIlrath said in avideo statement.\n\"There is absolutely no evidence to support the allegations that Nolan's disappearance and death were racially motivated,\" she said. \"Nolan's friends loved Nolan, and Nolan loved them.\"\nWells had traveled to Horn Island with his friends to celebrate the Fourth of July, but didn't return to the mainland with them that afternoon, Jackson County Sheriff John Ledbetter said at the time.\nHe was last seen on the island at 3 p.m., according to the office of civil rights attorney Ben Crump, who is representing the family. Wells' mother reported him missing later that night. His body was found Monday, July 6, off the coast of the island, which is about 10 miles south of the Mississippi mainland.\nAccording to the report, the grand jury concluded the \"position of, condition of, time of discovery, and location of Mr. Wells' body is consistent with drowning.\"\nAnindependent autopsycommissioned by the family could not determine Wells' cause of death, Crump said in late July. The results of that independent autopsy, released by the family on Tuesday, found that Wells suffered blunt force injuries to the back of his head and to his body. The autopsy determined that although alcohol was found in his system, the exact amount could not be determined, nor was it clear when he may have consumed the alcohol or what his blood-alcohol level was.\n\"There still remains gaps in information that would define the condition of Nolan Wells (i.e. conscious or unconscious, forced or unforced) before entering or while in the water that would lead to drowning,\" independent forensic pathologist Dr. Roger Mitchell Jr. wrote in his report. \"This forensic pathologist is not convinced based upon the review of the investigative witness summaries or the post-mortem toxicology that Nolan Wells was too intoxicated to swim.\"\nThe results of the autopsy conducted by the Mississippi State Medical Examiner have not been publicly released, but the grand jury saidin its reportthat the state also ruled the cause of death as \"undetermined.\"\nThe grand jury stated that both Mitchell's autopsy and the state's found that Wells had bruising on the back of his head. However, it said the two autopsies disagreed about the \"presence of bruising (non-deep tissue) on Mr. Wells' buttocks and shoulder.\"\nThe grand jury also noted that it could not definitively state what caused any of those injuries.\n\"Two medical examiners found contusions on the back of Nolan's head, and Dr. Roger Mitchell found bruises on his back,\" Crump said in a news conference alongside the family Tuesday. \"What caused them and did they contribute to his death? We'll never know, they say.\"\nWells' friends have told authorities that he chose to stay behind on the island to speak with a girl, telling them he would come back to the mainland later.\nWells' parents, Christine and Elmore Wonsley, previously told \"CBS Mornings\" they didn't believe their son would have stayed behind by choice.\n\"No, he wouldn't. Nolan always stays with the group,\" Elmore Wonsley, Nolan's father, told \"CBS Mornings.\" \"If you be with me, you come back with me. So that I don't understand, and with me being a parent, if I was in that situation, I would have told them, 'You're going to get back on this boat with me because I don't want to answer to your parents if something happens to you.'\"\nCrump, who is representing the family, told \"CBS Mornings,\" \"There are so many glaring contradictions, that's why so many people all across America are chiming in, saying this is not adding up. I mean, you have [his friends] say he was talking to a young lady, and she said that he said, 'I'm going to get back on the boat with the boys.' The boys say, 'He said that, 'No, I'm going to stay with her.' I mean, which is it?\"\nIn a statement released Monday evening, Crump called on the district attorney to keep the investigation into Wells' death open and active.\n\"We don't know how Nolan died, and neither does the State of Mississippi. Mississippi must keep investigating, and if it will not pursue this to the end, the Department of Justice should,\" Crump said. \"This grand jury result leaves us with more questions than answers. Nolan's family will not rest until we know what happened to him, and neither will we.\"\nThe grand jury, which consisted of 23 people who represented a \"cross section of citizens\" from Jackson County, said in its report released Monday: \"the individuals who traveled to or from Horn Island on the Triton, the Palm Beach, and Tideline cooperated fully with this investigation, including offering marine GPS devices, surveillance footage, cell phones, and statements to law enforcement officers and Mr. Wells' family during this investigation.\"\nIt also concluded that Wells \"chose to remain on Horn Island after the boat he arrived on departed, which is common for visitors to Horn Island on holidays and other crowded days.\"\nMark  Osbornecontributed to this report.\n\u00a9 2026 CBS Interactive Inc. All Rights Reserved.\n(02:52)\nCopyright \u00a92026 CBS Interactive Inc. All rights reserved.", "date": "Tue, 22 Sep 2026 18:55:12 -0400"}, {"title": "U.S. Mint and America's VetDogs unveil commemorative coins honoring service dogs", "url": "https://www.cbsnews.com/video/us-mint-americas-vetdogs-commemorative-coins-service-dogs/", "body": "Watch CBS News\nCopyright \u00a92026 CBS Interactive Inc. All rights reserved.", "date": "Tue, 22 Sep 2026 18:51:14 -0400"}, {"title": "Trump tells U.N. General Assembly he will either strike a deal with Iran or \"annihilate\" country", "url": "https://www.cbsnews.com/video/trump-tells-un-general-assembly-he-will-either-strike-a-deal-with-iran-or-annihilate-country/", "body": "Watch CBS News\nCopyright \u00a92026 CBS Interactive Inc. All rights reserved.", "date": "Tue, 22 Sep 2026 18:43:44 -0400"}, {"title": "Georgia couple who survived Nepal flood shares their story", "url": "https://www.cbsnews.com/atlanta/news/one-month-after-deadly-nepal-flood-georgia-couple-grapples-with-why-they-made-it-home/", "body": "Watch CBS News\nSeptember 22, 2026 / 6:30 PM EDT/ CBS Atlanta\nOne month after a catastrophicflash floodswept through a Himalayan river valley, a Suwanee couple is safely back home. \u00a0The people they were traveling with are not.\nMita and Hemant Parikh were part of a group of 47 people on a long-planned spiritual pilgrimage to Mount Kailash in Tibet. Nearly a month after the flood, the tour company that organized their trip tells CBS News Atlanta that none of the missing families from their group have been found either alive or deceased. Seven guides are also missing. All remain unaccounted for.\nFor the Parikhs, the past month has been spent trying to understand how an ordinary decision \u2014 a side trip they almost didn't make \u2014 put them on a different road.\n\"That's what we talk to each other every single day,\" Mita said. \"Why did we decide this way?\"\nFor the couple, the pilgrimage had been 15 years in the making.\n\"Everybody has a bucket list, but this bucket list, you don't choose it,\" Mita said. \"God chooses for you.\"\nMount Kailash, a remote peak high in the Himalayas, is one of Hinduism's holiest pilgrimage sites. \u00a0For Mita and Hemant, reaching it was an opportunity to find peace, meditate, and surrender themselves to their faith.\nThey joined a group of 47 people and soon found themselves surrounded by families from around the world. Some had gotten to know one another through WhatsApp before the trip. Others met for the first time after arriving. Strangers became friends, ate together, prayed, and made plans together.\nAmong the group were families from the United States and Australia, a mother and daughter, and several children. Mita and Hemant said they had even made plans to continue traveling with some of their new friends after the pilgrimage was over.\nThen came an unexpected opportunity. With extra time built into their itinerary, Mita and Hemant decided to squeeze in a trip to Lhasa and Everest Base Camp before rejoining the rest of their group in Saga.\nIt was not the kind of spontaneous decision they typically made.\n\"Usually we argue, or tickets are expensive \u2014 no, let's not go,\" Mita recalled. \"But I don't know why we both agreed.\"\nFor Hemant, part of the appeal was simply getting close to a place he never expected to climb.\n\"Mount Everest is the highest peak in the world,\" he said. \"And I said, at least, I cannot climb, but I can go and see from a distance.\"\nThe couple traveled on with a guide and driver. Despite forecasts of heavy snow, they said the weather cleared enough for them to see Everest.\nAfterward, they expected to meet their fellow pilgrims in Saga and continue the journey together. They never got the chance.\nWhile the Parikhs were away from the group, a massiveflash flood swept throughthe river valley where their fellow travelers were staying. At first, Hemant said, the news did not sound perilous.\n\"Our guide said that, you know, there was a flood,\" he recalled. \"And so I thought \u2026 it's not a big deal. There's always floods.\"\nThey had no idea how bad it was.\nAt Everest Base Camp, their phone had been on silent. When they checked it, they found repeated calls and messages from family and friends asking whether they were safe.\nThe Parikhs initially thought the rest of their group had simply been delayed. They had limited access to news and faced a language barrier as they tried to understand what was happening.\nThey waited for the group they were supposed to meet. It never arrived. They tried calling their fellow travelers. No one answered.\nThe couple said their tour operator eventually flew over the area by helicopter. What had been the group's hotel was gone.\n\"The hotel is not there anymore,\" Hemant said. \"Not a single piece of concrete or pillar or anything. \u00a0Nothing is there.\"\nMita has found herself imagining what the morning may have been like for the people they had come to know \u2014 an ordinary morning before the flood arrived.\n\"They were drinking tea. They were eating their breakfast about 8:30,\" she recalled.\nOne month later, the uncertainty remains.\nMita and Hemant eventually returned to Atlanta. However, coming home has brought complicated emotions.\n\"As soon as I close my eyes, I see Kailash, and I see the people,\" Mita said. \u00a0\"I don't know what happened.\"\nShe said she still allows herself to hope that someone could be found. That perhaps someone reached higher ground or shelter and has been unable to make contact.\nHemant struggles with the same uncertainty.\n\"I ask myself why?\" he said. \"And I don't have an answer.\"\nHe also wonders whether there was something he could have done for the people they left behind.\nFor Mita, the experience has changed the way she thinks about the life she returned to. She talks about spending more time with people, caring more deeply for others, and finding some purpose in having made it home.\nBut she keeps returning to the decision that separated her and her husband from the rest of their group \u2014 a side trip, a different route, a choice that seemed entirely ordinary when they made it.\n\"That's what keeps me awake,\" Mita said. \"Why? Why God chose us to live. So there has to be some purpose, right?\"\nOne month later, Mita and Hemant still don't have an answer.\nIn:\n\u00a9 2026 CBS Broadcasting Inc. All Rights Reserved.\n(04:46)\n\u00a92026 CBS Broadcasting Inc. All Rights Reserved.", "date": "Tue, 22 Sep 2026 18:30:00 -0400"}, {"title": "Senators press Rubio to prioritize release of American held in Iran", "url": "https://www.cbsnews.com/news/senators-iran-marco-rubio-reza-valizadeh-american-journalist/", "body": "Watch CBS News\nSeptember 22, 2026 / 1:16 PM EDT/ CBS News\nA bipartisan group of senators is urging Secretary of State Marco Rubio to make the release of an American journalistimprisonedin Iran an \"urgent priority,\" warning that his health has deteriorated and his life could be at risk.\nIn aletter to Rubioobtained by CBS News, Sens. Brian Schatz, Democrat of Hawaii, Republican Marsha Blackburn of Tennessee, Democrat Jacky Rosen of Nevada and Republican Dave McCormick of Pennsylvania called on the Trump administration to use \"all available diplomatic channels\" to secure the release of Reza Valizadeh, a U.S.-Iranian dual citizen and former journalist for U.S.-funded broadcaster Radio Farda.\n\"With every day of continued detention, Mr. Valizadeh's life is in danger,\" the senators wrote. \"We are extremely concerned that Iranian authorities could take sudden, arbitrary actions to endanger his life.\"\nValizadeh, 49, has been imprisoned in Iran since September 2024, after returning to Tehran earlier that year to visit his aging parents. He was sentenced to 10 years in prison for \"collaborating with a hostile government,\" a charge tied to his work for Radio Farda and alleged cooperation with the United States. The State Department formally designated him as wrongfully detained in May 2025.\nThe senators said Valizadeh's health has \"significantly deteriorated\" at Tehran's Evin Prison and that he has repeatedly been denied medical care, including an inhaler he needs to treat his asthma.\nThe State Department said in a statement after this article was originally published, \"The regime should immediately release Reza Valizadeh and all Americans unjustly detained in Iran.\"\nValizadeh haspreviously describedworsening conditions inside Evin inrecordingsobtained by CBS News. In June, he appealed to the U.S. government to secure his release and said he and other Americans detained there were not receiving adequate medical treatment.\nThe congressional appeal comes just over two weeks after another American designated as wrongfully detained, Kamran Hekmati, was released from Evin Prison on Sept. 5 after serving roughly half of a two-year sentence. Hekmati remains barred from leaving Iran, and the State Department has called on Tehran to lift the exit ban so he can return to the United States.\nIn July, Dena Karari, a U.S.-Iran dual citizen who was also barred from leaving Iran on allegations of espionage and collaborating with a hostile state, wasreleasedand returned to the U.S. after suffering a heart attack, sources told CBS News.\nRubio said in June that the administration raises the cases of wrongfully detained Americans at \"every opportunity,\" calling their release a \"highest priority,\" but he has declined to discuss individual cases, saying that could complicate efforts to free them.\nThe senators' push comes as the Trump administration weighs its next steps in the nearly seven-month war with Iran, with the conflict and prospects for renewed diplomacy expected to be raised in meetings this week during the U.N. General Assembly in New York.\n\"Reza deserves the full attention and support of President Trump and the United States government, and he should be released immediately,\" said Ryan Fayhee, Valizadeh's attorney. \"As world leaders gather for the U.N. General Assembly, Iran has an opportunity to demonstrate goodwill through action. The release of Reza would not resolve every issue between our nations, but it could help break a cycle of mistrust that has made it unsafe for Iranian-Americans to visit the country of their heritage,\" Fayhee said.\nThe senators described Valizadeh's detention as part of a longstanding pattern by Tehran of targeting journalists and dual nationals, writing that \"Iran is not afraid to use American citizens as political pawns.\"\n\"We ask that his freedom and welfare be an urgent priority through all available diplomatic channels with Iranian officials,\" they wrote.\nIn:\n\u00a9 2026 CBS Interactive Inc. All Rights Reserved.\n(02:28)\nCopyright \u00a92026 CBS Interactive Inc. All rights reserved.", "date": "Tue, 22 Sep 2026 13:16:00 -0400"}, {"title": "Scores killed, Americans among hundreds missing after Nepal, Tibet flash floods", "url": "https://www.cbsnews.com/news/nepal-flash-flood-tourists-missing-including-americans/", "body": "Watch CBS News\nUpdated on:  August 27, 2026 / 12:06 AM EDT/ CBS News\nAt least 160 people were killed in Nepal and hundreds more, most of them foreigners, were listed as missing on Wednesday in both Nepal and Tibet after flash flooding tore through border areas, according to authorities. The flooding was determined to have been caused by the collapse of a glacier, U.S. officials later said.\nThe flash floods decimated villages, slamming into houses, roads, bridges and power plants.Videos broadcast by regional news outletsand posted on social media show violent torrents of water surging through valleys and mountain passes, tearing down bridges and dams and sweeping away buildings.\nThe Nepali police confirmed that at least 162 bodies were recovered following \"devastating floods,\" but there was no word on the nationalities of the victims. Chinese state media reported \"major casualties\" from a mudslide at a Tibet border hub, including three dead.\nDozens of U.S. nationals were among the 341 foreigners listed as missing, according to Nepal's Tourism Board, citing information from tour companies.\nHikmat Singh Ayer, CEO and senior director of Nepal's Tourism Board, told CBS News that 403 people were unaccounted for after the flooding. He said that among the missing foreign nationals are 33 Americans, 34 Australians, 24 Canadians, 4 Germans, 19 Malaysians and one Israeli.\nChinese state broadcaster CCTV later reported that at least 558 people were believed to be missing on the Tibet side of the Nepal-Tibet border, according to The Associated Press. It said 260 of the 558 were foreigners.\nA sudden surge of water Wednesday morning in the Bhote Koshi River, in the country's northern Rasuwa district north of capital Kathmandu, quickly affected the neighboring Nuwakot and Dhading districts.\nDavid Fisher, the head of delegation for the International Red Cross in Nepal, told CBS News that many areas remain inaccessible, hindering ground operations, but helicopter teams have been able to go in and rescue people.\n\"Entire villages have disappeared,\" Fisher said. \"Markets have disappeared, and a critical artery for commerce between China and Nepal has also now been damaged, making it very difficult for many of those families who depend on that for their living.\"\nNepal's Foreign Minister Shishir Khanal initially reported that the flash flood was caused by an earthquake that triggered a large landslide, which blocked the Bhote Koshi River.\nHowever, the United States Geological Surveylater reportedthat the flash flood was \"instead generated by a glacial collapse and debris flow,\" stating that no earthquake occurred.\n\"This event was initially reported as a magnitude 4.4 earthquake,\" the USGS said. \"Additional analysis of nearby seismic stations, long period seismic waves, and satellite imagery led to the determination that the seismic event was instead a glacial collapse and debris flow, and that no earthquake had occurred.\"\nNepali President Ramachandra Paudel said he was \"extremely saddened\" by the \"loss of lives and property caused by the sudden flood.\"\nHecalled onthe government, political parties, agencies and the general public to intensify rescue and relief efforts.\n\"There could be heavy casualties or loss of property,\" district administrator Narendra Pariyar was quoted as saying by Reuters. He has also urged people living along the banks of the Bhote Koshi River to move to higher ground.\nNepal's mountainous terrain, dotted with deep valleys and narrow gorges, makes rescue and relief operations challenging. Helicopters could not land in the affected areas of Syapru Besi and Timure, Reuters said.\nMost of the missing foreigners were on trips organized by tourism and travel companies.\nThe Nepal Electricity Authority (NEA) confirmed that six of its major hydropower and transmission facilities were damaged in the flooding.\n\"We do not exactly know the extent of damage, but the flood is big, and it could have damaged many settlements,\" Nepal Police spokesman Abi Narayan Kafle told the French news agency AFP. \"We have alerted people who are staying near the riverside to relocate ... and deployed all our resources.\"\nIn asocial media post, U.S. Secretary of State Marco Rubio expressed U.S. solidarity with the Nepali people, saying his department \"stands ready to support the government of Nepal with needed humanitarian assistance.\"\nThe State Department said later Wednesday it would \"provide $500,000 in assistance through its global award with Catholic Relief Services, supporting emergency shelter, relief items, and water, sanitation, and hygiene assistance to flood-affected communities.\"\nThe State Department also said its embassy in Kathmandu was working with local authorities to to find and help U.S. citizens affected by the disaster.\n\"Work has started to take necessary steps in coordination with the medical team, scouts, and Red Cross,\" Nepal's Prime Minister Balen Shah wrote in a social media post.\nIndia's government ordered authorities in states along the Nepal border to be on high alert.\nKiki  IntarasuwanandAFPcontributed to this report.\nIn:\n\u00a9 2026 CBS Interactive Inc. All Rights Reserved.\n(12:17)\nCopyright \u00a92026 CBS Interactive Inc. All rights reserved.", "date": "Thu, 27 Aug 2026 00:06:00 -0400"}, {"title": "Most straight women don\u2019t want to date bi guys, but most straight men are open to bi women", "url": "https://www.lgbtqnation.com/2026/09/most-straight-women-dont-want-to-date-bi-guys-but-most-straight-men-are-open-to-bi-women/", "body": "Part of theQ.Digitalfamily of brands:\nThe reasons people gave for not wanting to date bi partners show the lingering stigma of bi stereotypes.\nTwo-thirds of straight women and one-third of straight men are unwilling to date a bisexual person of the opposite sex, according toa YouGov pollpublished today, during Bisexuality Visibility Week. The poll illustrates how lingering stereotypes negatively affect people\u2019s impressions of bisexual individuals.\nThe poll \u2014 which surveyed 5,983 Britons during late March, including 2,031 straight women and 1,517 straight men \u2014 found that 48% of all respondents had positive views of bisexual people and only 8% had negative views. The remaining percentage had neither a positive nor a negative view of bisexuals.\nThe survey also included 824 gay men, 349 lesbian women, 396 bisexual men, and 866 bisexual women, and asked them about their willingness to date a bisexual person matching their preferred gender.\nApproximately 19% of straight women expressed willingness to date a bi man, while 66% said they were unwilling. Among straight men, 53% said they were willing to date a bi woman, and 35% said they were unwilling.\nAmong bi women, 82% expressed willingness to date a bi man, while 10% expressed unwillingness; and 89% were willing to date a bi woman, while 3% expressed unwillingness to do the same.\nAmong bi men, 78% expressed willingness to date a bi man, while 15% expressed unwillingness; and 85% were willing to date a bi woman, while 9% expressed unwillingness.\nAmong gay men, 83% expressed willingness to date a bi man, while 11% expressed unwillingness. Among lesbian women, 73% expressed willingness to date a bi woman, while 20% expressed unwillingness.\nWhen the survey asked straight women and men why they were unwilling to date a bisexual person of the opposite sex, most (16% and 19%, respectively) said they \u201cjust don\u2019t\u201d or claimed, \u201cIt\u2019s just a preference.\u201d\nA nearly similar portion, 17% of straight women and 16% of straight men, said they felt like bi people are more inclined to cheat, or that they themselves would feel insecure that they\u2019d be unable to provide a bi partner whatever they get from a same-sex partner.\nInterestingly, 9% of straight women said they feel disgust or discomfort towards men with same-sex experiences, 8% worried about possible sexual health risks, and 10% of straight men said they considered bisexuality to be either religiously or morally \u201cwrong.\u201d Approximately 12-13% of all straight respondents said they just wanted their dating partner to be heterosexual.\n\u201cSmatterings of people gave less common answers, such as believing that bisexuals want polyamorous/open relationships (3-5%); seeing bisexuals as confused, indecisive or \u2018actually gay\u2019 (2%); believing that being in a relationship with a bisexual person would be \u2018complicated\u2019 (2%); considering bisexuals to be more promiscuous (1%); or apparently confusing bisexual and transgender people (1%),\u201d \u00a0YouGov\u2019s Head of Data Journalism Matthew Smith wrote.\nAlso interestingly, the poll found that greater percentages of older straight, gay, and lesbian respondents all expressed an increased unwillingness to date a bisexual person. That is, older respondents were generally less willing to date bi people than younger respondents.\nSubscribe to theLGBTQ Nation newsletterand be the first to know about the latest headlines shaping LGBTQ+ communities worldwide.\nDaniel Villarrealis a longtime, award-winning journalist and editor who has written for NBC News,Newsweek,Vox,Slate,Vice News,The Seattle Stranger,The Dallas Voiceand numerous other LGBTQ+ publications. He has spoken at SXSW, Creating Change, Netroots Nation, GaymerX, and is a graduate of GLAAD's Voices of Color program and of the Poynter Institute's 2024 Power of Diverse Voices seminar. He is also the founder of QueerBomb Dallas, an annual non-corporate Pride event; Cin\u00e9Wilde, the nation's longest running monthly LGBTQ film series. He is available for interviews and educational talks.\nConnect with Daniel Villarreal:\nSee more:Life,Survey\nPro-trans voters call out Democrat, Vance insults drag nuns & Turkey cracks down on LGBTQ+ people\nAnti-LGBTQ+ MMA fighter wants to beat up Elliot Page\nA queer Torah study group is showing LGBTQ+ people they have always belonged in Judaism\nIndependent LGBTQ+ journalism matters more than ever. This September, help us reach 1,500 members and keep LGBTQ+ stories, voices, and reporting available to millions of readers.\nOr give a different amount:\nWe accept all major credit cards, Apple\u00a0Pay, and\u00a0Google\u00a0Pay.\n\u201cI want to tell you something: Rich people, as a whole, suck,\u201d the event organizer said in a now-deleted video.\nOssoff has called for the ad to be taken down.\nBarack Obama was the only person asked about in the poll who could beat Pete\u2019s favorability rating.\nOne victim remains in stable condition at a local hospital.\nThe governor claims it would set an inappropriate precedent.\nFans in the region say references to X-Men character Mystique\u2019s lesbian relationship have been censored in \u2018Marvel\u2019s Wolverine.\u2019\nRose Bean Polansky was born on June 9 after Lady Gaga spent years trying to become a mother.\n\u201cVEEP couldn\u2019t write this.\u201d\nThe pro-trans side in Washington might not have the same funds, but they told LGBTQ Nation that they\u2019re appealing to people\u2019s sense of fairness.\nTensions continue to escalate more than two weeks after a group of people was asked to leave the bar for wearing anti-trans shirts.\nThe Trump administration is arguing in court that the trans military ban isn\u2019t based on hatred, while Hegseth repeatedly uses anti-trans slurs.\nGuyanese authorities posted a wanted notice for him in 2019. The US sent him back this past Friday.\nHere are the top stories of the week that you might have missed.\n\u201cI\u2019d beat the s**t out of him.\u201d\nIt\u2019s all in how you look at things.\nPLUS: Fall decorating tips and tasteful lesbian drama!\nYiannopoulos reportedly called his husband the n-word, told him to sleep in a basement, and threatened to kill him.\nSomebody should do something. And that somebody is all of us.\n\u201cThe relationship has to be built around the people involved, and that means making room for both connection and boundaries.\u201d\nReaders expressed hope for the midterms: \u201cMaybe then we can start dismantling what the administration has destroyed.\u201d\nHe was raised in the Methodist faith, but pursued another denomination after feeling like he didn\u2019t fit in.\n\u201cWe genuinely appreciate knowing that this space matters to so many of you, and that we\u2019re not alone in wanting to protect it.\u201d\nThey call their family-building journey \u201ca dream story.\u201d\nPLUS: Queer artist Leo Herrera has a must-hear message for our entire community!\nCongratulations to the happy couple!\nJockstraps are very vers\u2013just like the gays who wear them!\nUncle Sam was not complaining\nHere\u2019s a fun and queer way to spend a Saturday afternoon in Chicago.\nHere\u2019s what\u2019s happening on Gay TikTok\u2026\nJosh Dixon is a trailblazing gymnast turned influencer.\nCheck out this week\u2019s InstaStuds\u2026\nYour new LGBTQ+ travel destination bucket list is here.\nQuaaludes\u2014aka \u201cdisco biscuits\u201d\u2014are apparently making a comeback.\n\u00a9 2010 - 2026\u00a0LGBTQ Nation\u00ae | Proudly\u00a0LGBTQ+ Owned\u00a0and\u00a0Operated\u00a0\ud83c\udff3\ufe0f\u200d\ud83c\udf08\u00a0\ud83c\udff3\ufe0f\u200d\u26a7\ufe0f\nLGBTQ Nation is a registered trademark of Q.Digital, Inc., registered in the state of California.\n", "date": "Tue, 22 Sep 2026 22:00:00 +0000"}, {"title": "Syria: Reconstruction Deals Lack Critical Rights Framework", "url": "https://www.hrw.org/news/2026/09/27/syria-reconstruction-deals-lack-critical-rights-framework", "body": "Forbidden\nDetails: cache-lax-kwhp1940127-LAX 1790699613 948423322\nVarnish cache server", "date": "Sun, 27 Sep 2026 00:00:00 -0400"}, {"title": "Niger Rejects Key Rights Recommendations at UN Review", "url": "https://www.hrw.org/news/2026/09/26/niger-rejects-key-rights-recommendations-at-un-review", "body": "Forbidden\nDetails: cache-lax-kwhp1940040-LAX 1790699613 297294833\nVarnish cache server", "date": "Sat, 26 Sep 2026 06:00:00 -0400"}, {"title": "US: Trump Administration Rejects External Election Observers", "url": "https://www.hrw.org/news/2026/09/25/us-trump-administration-rejects-external-election-observers", "body": "Forbidden\nDetails: cache-lax-kwhp1940104-LAX 1790699614 1484330199\nVarnish cache server", "date": "Fri, 25 Sep 2026 17:15:00 -0400"}, {"title": "Array of Experts Back Lawsuit Challenging Trump\u2019s ICC Sanctions Regime", "url": "https://www.hrw.org/news/2026/09/25/array-of-experts-back-lawsuit-challenging-trumps-icc-sanctions-regime", "body": "Forbidden\nDetails: cache-lax-kwhp1940094-LAX 1790699614 1003369997\nVarnish cache server", "date": "Fri, 25 Sep 2026 16:15:00 -0400"}, {"title": "Ethiopia: Indiscriminate Airstrike in Tigray", "url": "https://www.hrw.org/news/2026/09/25/ethiopia-indiscriminate-airstrike-in-tigray", "body": "Forbidden\nDetails: cache-lax-kwhp1940127-LAX 1790699615 948442868\nVarnish cache server", "date": "Fri, 25 Sep 2026 12:20:11 -0400"}, {"title": "Russia Crackdown on Bypassing Online Censorship", "url": "https://www.hrw.org/news/2026/09/24/russia-crackdown-on-bypassing-online-censorship", "body": "Forbidden\nDetails: cache-lax-kwhp1940097-LAX 1790699617 1425677018\nVarnish cache server", "date": "Thu, 24 Sep 2026 10:06:24 -0400"}, {"title": "Central African Republic: ICC Verdict in Seleka Commander Case", "url": "https://www.hrw.org/news/2026/09/23/central-african-republic-icc-verdict-in-seleka-commander-case", "body": "Forbidden\nDetails: cache-den-kden1300029-DEN 1790699618 2341802572\nVarnish cache server", "date": "Wed, 23 Sep 2026 19:11:54 -0400"}, {"title": "Canada: Press Vietnam\u2019s Leader on Rights", "url": "https://www.hrw.org/news/2026/09/23/canada-press-vietnams-leader-on-rights", "body": "Forbidden\nDetails: cache-lax-kwhp1940096-LAX 1790699618 4097360976\nVarnish cache server", "date": "Wed, 23 Sep 2026 14:00:00 -0400"}, {"title": "Trump\u2019s Latest Media Attacks Again Hit Women Journalists", "url": "https://www.hrw.org/news/2026/09/23/trumps-latest-media-attacks-again-hit-women-journalists", "body": "Forbidden\nDetails: cache-lax-kwhp1940127-LAX 1790699619 948475101\nVarnish cache server", "date": "Wed, 23 Sep 2026 11:37:27 -0400"}, {"title": "Bangladesh: New Laws Reverse Rights Protections, Empower Abusers", "url": "https://www.hrw.org/news/2026/09/22/bangladesh-new-laws-reverse-rights-protections-empower-abusers", "body": "Forbidden\nDetails: cache-lax-kwhp1940147-LAX 1790699619 793702733\nVarnish cache server", "date": "Tue, 22 Sep 2026 21:00:00 -0400"}, {"title": "Jack Smith testifies at Senate hearing", "url": "https://www.nbcnews.com/video/watch-live-jack-smith-testifies-at-senate-hearing-270667333671", "body": "\nFor You\nSettings\nnews Alerts\nThere are no new alerts at this time\nWatch live coverage as former special counsel Jack Smith testifies at a Senate Judiciary Committee hearing. He is expected to face questions on the federal cases he brought against President Trump that have since been dismissed.\n\n\u00a9\u00a02026 NBCUniversal Media, LLC", "date": "Tue, 29 Sep 2026 12:47:15 GMT"}, {"title": "Lindsay Clancy\u2019s defense says prosecution did not prove she killed her children", "url": "https://www.nbcnews.com/now/video/lindsay-clancy-s-defense-says-prosecution-did-not-prove-she-killed-her-children-270727749827", "body": "\nFor You\nSettings\nnews Alerts\nThere are no new alerts at this time\nLindsay Clancy and her legal team returned to court for the first hearing since Clancy\u2019s triple murder trial ended in a mistrial. Her attorney argued that the state of Massachusetts failed to prove she killed her children. NBC News\u2019 Steven Romo reports on the holdout juror in the spotlight and where the case is headed.Sept. 29, 2026\n\n\u00a9\u00a02026 NBCUniversal Media, LLC", "date": "Tue, 29 Sep 2026 16:18:32 GMT"}, {"title": "Trump says he will sign executive order naming AI as Super Intelligence", "url": "https://www.nbcnews.com/video/trump-says-he-will-sign-executive-order-naming-ai-as-super-intelligence-270723141932", "body": "\nFor You\nSettings\nnews Alerts\nThere are no new alerts at this time\nDuring an event launching the website America.gov, President Trump said that he will be signing an executive order renaming artificial intelligence to super intelligence.Sept. 29, 2026\n\n\u00a9\u00a02026 NBCUniversal Media, LLC", "date": "Tue, 29 Sep 2026 15:16:12 GMT"}, {"title": "Cornell Sexual Assault Investigation Reopened by Prosecutors", "url": "https://www.today.com/video/cornell-sexual-assault-investigation-reopened-by-prosecutors-270710853527", "body": "From Start to Finish: Exclusive Illy Coffee Deals Seen on TODAY\n\nTODAY\nStart TODAY\nSteals & Deals, Boxes & Last Chance Market\nProsecutors say they have now reopened the case of an alleged sexual assault at Cornell University with plans to have the former student, who\u2019s now filed a civil lawsuit against the school, tell her story to a grand jury. NBC\u2019s Maggie Vespa reports for TODAY. Warning: Some of the details of the lawsuit are disturbing.Sept. 29, 2026\n\u00a9\u00a02026 NBCUniversal Media, LLCApple\u00ae, Apple logo\u00ae and App Store\u00ae are registered trademarks of Apple Inc.", "date": "Tue, 29 Sep 2026 11:24:49 GMT"}, {"title": "5 Men Arrested Near UK Air Base by US Forces Released on Bail", "url": "https://www.today.com/video/rubio-alleges-foreign-government-was-involved-in-uk-terror-plot-270709829855", "body": "From Start to Finish: Exclusive Illy Coffee Deals Seen on TODAY\n\nTODAY\nStart TODAY\nSteals & Deals, Boxes & Last Chance Market\nNew details are emerging about the potential terror plot targeting a US-operated military base in the U.K, with Secretary of State Marco Rubio alleging for the first time that a foreign government was involved. Authorities decided to release all five suspects on bail without being charged. NBC\u2019s Raf Sanchez reports for TODAY.Sept. 29, 2026\n\u00a9\u00a02026 NBCUniversal Media, LLCApple\u00ae, Apple logo\u00ae and App Store\u00ae are registered trademarks of Apple Inc.", "date": "Tue, 29 Sep 2026 11:21:13 GMT"}, {"title": "Dennis Haskins, Principal on \u2018Saved by the Bell,\u2019 Dies at 75", "url": "https://www.today.com/video/dennis-haskins-principal-on-saved-by-the-bell-dies-at-75-270713925674", "body": "From Start to Finish: Exclusive Illy Coffee Deals Seen on TODAY\n\nTODAY\nStart TODAY\nSteals & Deals, Boxes & Last Chance Market\nDennis Haskins, the actor best known for his role as the beloved principal of Bayside High, Mr. Belding, on the hit show \u201cSaved by the Bell,\u201d has died at 75. NBC\u2019s Joe Fryer reports for TODAY.Sept. 29, 2026\n\u00a9\u00a02026 NBCUniversal Media, LLCApple\u00ae, Apple logo\u00ae and App Store\u00ae are registered trademarks of Apple Inc.", "date": "Tue, 29 Sep 2026 12:14:22 GMT"}, {"title": "Common Ground: The ever-expanding NFL community", "url": "https://www.nbcnews.com/now/video/common-ground-the-ever-expanding-nfl-community-270693957755", "body": "\nFor You\nSettings\nnews Alerts\nThere are no new alerts at this time\nNBC Sports announcer Mike Tirico leads a panel on the ever-expanding NFL community with former Super Bowl MVP Drew Brees, quarterback of Mexico's national flag football team Diana Flores and NFL's chief media officer Hans Schroeder. Common Ground is sponsored by Comcast.Sept. 29, 2026\n\n\u00a9\u00a02026 NBCUniversal Media, LLC", "date": "Tue, 29 Sep 2026 01:43:21 GMT"}, {"title": "Anger grows across Spain after elderly woman is evicted from her home of 70 years", "url": "https://www.nbcnews.com/world/spain/anger-grows-spain-elderly-woman-evicted-home-70-years-rcna599808", "body": "\nFor You\nSettings\nnews Alerts\nThere are no new alerts at this time\nAn87-year-old Spanish womanwas forcibly evicted Wednesday from the Madrid apartment she had lived in for more than 70 years, in a case that highlights a growing housing crisis in the country.\nVideo showed Maricarmen Abascal, who is a wheelchair user, being taken out of the building in the Spanish capital\u2019s Retiro neighborhood on a stretcher and driven away in an ambulance.\nSupportershad gathered outside her building overnight on Friday, with dozens of police officers holding them back as some tried to stop the eviction from going ahead. In a social media video posted Friday by the Madrid tenants union, Abascal gave thanks for the support she had received.\nAbascal\u2019s lawyer, Beatriz Duro, described the force used in the eviction as \u201ccompletely disproportionate and unnecessary\u201d and decried the intransigence of the building\u2019s owners.\nThe case has brought new attention to an ongoing housing crisis in major cities across Spain, as gentrification has caused rents to skyrocket and the stock of affordable housing to shrink, leading to fierce public anger.\nOn Saturday, tens of thousands of people marched through the streets of Madrid to protest the eviction, some setting off flares. They carried placards reading \u201cNot one more eviction, not one less neighbor, we are all Maricarmen\u201d and \u201cright to housing at a fair price.\u201d\nAt the end of the march, protesters sat down in the Puerta del Sol plaza, some pitching tents. They remained in place early Sunday as dozens of activists staged a sit-in. A model of a vulture, decrying so-called vulture funds, hung above the camp.\nEarlier in the week, tenants unions and other housing groups held protests and rallies in 16 cities, including Granada, Logro\u00f1o and Zaragoza.\nAn investment fund acquired Abascal\u2019s building in 2018 and sought a steep increase in her rent, and she had been fighting eviction for more than five years, according to the Madrid tenants union.\nDuro said court officials and representatives of the fund, Urbagesti\u00f3n, had refused to suspend the eviction despite a pending court filing, humanitarian appeals and evidence that public authorities were working to secure alternative accommodation.\n\u201cThey\u2019re all inhumane,\u201d Abascal said in a Spanish television interview the day before her eviction. \u201cIt\u2019s as if I\u2019m living with a machete at the back of my neck, ready to be chopped off at any moment.\u201d\nDuro also said an anonymous individual had offered to cover the rent payments, but the landlord declined to sign a new lease.\n\u201cWe\u2019ve been trying to negotiate with the landlords right up until the last moment, and they\u2019ve flatly refused everything,\u201d she said, decrying \u201ca lack of humanity\u201d in her treatment and \u201call these public resources\u201d channeled into enabling the eviction.\nUrbagesti\u00f3n did not respond to a request for comment.\nThe fresh wave of anger comes afterprotests in recent years against overtourism in Barcelona,where activists say a surge in apartments being used for short-term vacation rentals has contributed to a shortage of affordable housing.\nThe European Union announced new rules earlier this month aimed at tackling the housing crunch caused by short-term rentals in tourism hot spots.\nSpanishPrime Minister Pedro S\u00e1nchezdescribed the eviction as \u201ca social tragedy that we cannot accept either as a country or, of course, from the perspective of public institutions.\u201d\nSpeaking at the United Nations General Assembly in New York on Wednesday, S\u00e1nchez accused Madrid\u2019s conservative regional government of pursuing luxury penthouses while ignoring demand for social housing.\nIrene Hern\u00e1ndez L\u00f3pez, a 31-year-old test engineer living and renting in Chamber\u00ed, about 2 miles from Retiro, said what had happened to Abascal was \u201coutrageous,\u201d and she was worried similar evictions were already happening to other tenants around the city.\n\u201cHousing has become a product that people do business with,\u201d she told NBC News. \u201cIt no longer matters that it\u2019s your personal world, where you live and where you do your life.\u201d\nHern\u00e1ndez L\u00f3pez, who pays 500 euros ($570) for a room in an apartment she shares with two others, said that she was looking to move because the property was old and needs repairs but that the rental market in Madrid was \u201cterrible,\u201d with prices \u201crising extremely quickly\u201d and very little available.\n\u201cSome rooms are 500 euros or 600 euros and you think, \u2018How am I supposed to live here? How can I pay 500 euros for this?\u2019\u201d she said. \u201cI do think prices should be regulated, because wages aren\u2019t going up while housing prices keep increasing. I don\u2019t know how we\u2019re supposed to keep going.\u201d\nDan Nicander, secretary-general for the International Union of Tenants, said the situation in Spain was something his organization had seen in many countries in Europe.\n\u201cIt\u2019s a part of the financialization problem, seeing housing as an asset more than a home to people,\u201d he told NBC News.\nIt\u2019s \u201creally tough\u201d for the people living in the houses to do anything about it, he added, saying the responsibility lay with politicians \u201cto regulate the market in a way that it shouldn\u2019t be that easy to buy and invest and then not [be] using the houses as homes.\u201d\n\u201cHousing is a right,\u201d he said. \u201cIt\u2019s a fundamental right, first and foremost. It shouldn\u2019t be a commodity.\u201d\nJamie Gray is a senior desk editor for NBC News based in London.\nFreddie Clayton is a freelance journalist based in London.\n\u00a9\u00a02026 NBCUniversal Media, LLC", "date": "Sat, 26 Sep 2026 14:25:26 GMT"}, {"title": "New data shows most women in the Marines passing toughened fitness tests for combat roles", "url": "https://www.nbcnews.com/video/new-data-shows-most-female-marines-passing-fitness-tests-for-combat-roles-270450245824", "body": "\nFor You\nSettings\nnews Alerts\nThere are no new alerts at this time\nDefense Secretary Pete Hegseth said he wanted women in combat roles to be held to the same physical standards as men. One year later, new numbers from the Marine Corps show most women in combat roles are meeting, or exceeding those standards. NBC News\u2019 Yasmin Vossoughian reports.Sept. 24, 2026\n\n\u00a9\u00a02026 NBCUniversal Media, LLC", "date": "Thu, 24 Sep 2026 16:18:52 GMT"}, {"title": "Anne Hathaway says doctor warned her that \u2018Hot Ones\u2019 interview could induce early labor", "url": "https://www.nbcnews.com/news/us-news/anne-hathaway-says-doctor-warned-hot-ones-interview-induce-early-labor-rcna600467", "body": "\nFor You\nSettings\nnews Alerts\nThere are no new alerts at this time\nAnne Hathaway\u2019s career may be on fire, but her mouth won\u2019t be anytime soon if her doctor has anything to do with it.\nThe pregnant actor said her doctor advised her that she may go into labor early if she did \u201cHot Ones, during her press tour for Colleen Hoover\u2019s screen adaptation of \u201cVerity.\u201d\nHathaway revealed in an episode of the \u201cTalk of the Townsends\u201d podcast Monday that she was advised against going on the YouTube interview series, in which celebrities are asked questions as they eat increasingly hot and spicy chicken wings.\n\u201cWe did the first three wings for real, and then I switched to dummy wings. And then I thought that I was gonna be able to do the \u2018Hot Ones\u2019 challenge to do press for this film because it just made sense,\u201d Hathaway shared. \u201cBut then I got pregnant, and my doctor said I couldn\u2019t do it because I might go into early labor.\u201d\nHathaway made the comments while on a press junket for the film, her fifth this year. The Academy Award-winning actor has starred in some of this year\u2019s biggest hits, including \u201cThe Devil Wears Prada 2\u201d and \u201cThe Odyssey.\u201d\n\u201cHot Ones,\u201d created by Sean Evans, has been on YouTube since 2015 and has hosted Hollywood stars, including Jennifer Lawrence, Lorde and Keke Palmer. The series has been spoofed on \u201cSaturday Night Live\u201d twice.\n\u201cI still haven\u2019t done the gauntlet yet,\u201d Hathaway said. \u201cI\u2019m not sure when that\u2019s gonna happen. Maybe next year.\u201d\nHathaway is married to actor, producer and jewelry designer Adam Shulman. They have two young sons.\nI\u2019m a reporter on NBC News\u2019 breaking news team, covering crime, extreme weather, pop culture and more. I recently covered Hurricane Milton, which devastated the Tampa, Florida, metropolitan area, and the student-led protests at Columbia University.\n\u00a9\u00a02026 NBCUniversal Media, LLC", "date": "Tue, 29 Sep 2026 15:16:58 GMT"}, {"title": "University of Georgia student dancing on couch near window falls 8 stories and survives", "url": "https://www.nbcnews.com/news/us-news/university-georgia-student-dancing-couch-window-falls-8-stories-surviv-rcna600465", "body": "\nFor You\nSettings\nnews Alerts\nThere are no new alerts at this time\nAUniversity of Georgia student surviveda plunge from her eighth-floor apartment, authorities said Monday, after she had been \u201cdancing on a couch near a window\u201d and fell out.\nThe terrifying fall from the Rambler Athens Apartments at 558 W. Broad St. in Athens happened at about 7:30 p.m. Saturday, according to police records.\nThe \u201cstudent was dancing on a couch near a window that was open\u201d and \u201cfell from the 8thfloor window and landed on the pool deck below,\u201d the Athens-Clarke County Police Department said in a statement to NBC News.\nRemarkably, 21-year-old Virginia native Kristen Elizabeth Sargent appeared to have survived this harrowing plunge.\n\u201cAt this time, she is conscious and alert but seriously injured,\u201d police added.\nA cushioned metal bench might have broken the 5-foot-7, 115-pound woman\u2019s fall.\n\u201cI arrived and spoke with bystanders who advised that the female victim had been dancing on a couch or air mattress which was near an open window and had apparently fallen through the screen and onto the ground below,\u201d according to a report by ACC police Sgt. Erik Ellison.\n\u201cWhen I arrived, the victim was on the ground near the pool, which at this complex is in a courtyard on the second floor of the building. It appeared from the scene that the victim had struck a metal bench as she fell, as the cushion on the bench had some blood on it and the armrest of the bench had broken off.\u201d\nThe initial probe \u201cthus far suggests that this was an accident\u201d and that the victim \u201cwill survive,\u201d Ellison added.\nSenior Breaking News Reporter\n\u00a9\u00a02026 NBCUniversal Media, LLC", "date": "Tue, 29 Sep 2026 14:31:06 GMT"}, {"title": "Lindsay Clancy defense returns to court to argue there\u2019s no evidence she killed her children", "url": "https://www.nbcnews.com/news/us-news/lindsay-clancy-returns-court-first-time-mistrial-rcna600432", "body": "\nFor You\nSettings\nnews Alerts\nThere are no new alerts at this time\nLindsay Clancy and her legal team returned to court on Tuesday, arguing that the state of Massachusetts failed to prove she killed her children \u2014 a claim that prosecutors called \u201claughable.\u201d\nThis marks the first hearing since Clancy\u2019s triple murder trialended in a mistrialearlier this month.\nThe hearing in Plymouth Superior Court, which began just before 10 a.m.,included a number of motions filed since the trial concluded.\nJudge William Sullivan immediately punted some of those motions to a later date, noting that he, and some parties, were not ready for them to be argued. One of those motions isdefense attorney Kevin Reddington\u2019seffort to dismiss Clancy\u2019s case.\nSullivan heard arguments as a part of that motion Tuesday, but pushed his decision on those arguments, as well as those relating to other motions, until Nov. 2.\nThe judge also declined to set a new trial date, with both Reddington and prosecutors agreeing that with so much still to decide, it could be premature to do so.\nReddington on Tuesday offered arguments on Massachusetts\u2019 Rule 25, which states that if prosecutors do not argue their case effectively, the defendant should be found not guilty.\nHe first lamented that Clancy had been demonized by politicians \u201cfrom Donald Trump all the way down.\u201d\nAnd in a new approach that seemed contrary to the defense\u2019s strategy in the trial, Reddington then argued that there is no proof Clancy killed her children, and that the commonwealth did not provide evidence she did so.\nProsecutors, in response, called that allegation \u201claughable.\u201d\n\u201cAnd now, for the first time, the defendant is arguing that there\u2019s no evidence that she did this,\u201d Assistant District Attorney Shanan Buckingham said. \u201cI\u2019d suggest to you that the entire record is voluminous as to the information supporting the fact that she did this.\u201d\nSullivan said he would take that motion \u201cunder advisement.\u201d\nClancy, 36, is accused of fatally strangling her three children \u2014 Cora, 5, Dawson, 3, and Callan, 8 months  \u2014 using exercise bands Jan. 24, 2023. Her defense team, led byReddington, argued she was in astate of psychosisat the time of the killings, a result of thepostpartum psychosisshe was suffering from, and was therefore unaware of and unable to control her actions.\nThe prosecution, however, said that Clancy knowingly planned the killings ahead of time, arguing for a first-degree murder conviction.\nAfter a weekslong trial, the jury could not come to a unanimous decision. The group of nine women and three men deadlocked three times, forcing the judge to declare a mistrial Sept. 4.\nAfter the trial concluded,several jurors spoke publiclyand described a single holdout juror who they said refused to meet the required reasonable-doubt standard. Eleven of the jurors wanted to find Clancy not guilty by lack of criminal responsibility, and the holdout wanted to convict, they have said.\nThat man has since identified himself as Michael P. Desronvil. The jurors said he was the sole reason they could not reach a conclusion, describing someone whorefused to participate in negotiationsand seemed uninterested in hearing the other jurors out.\nDesronvil has denied the other jurors\u2019 allegations, saying in a statement that he \u201cdidn\u2019t have any doubts\u201d Clancy was guilty.\nIn a statement given toFox News\u2019 Sean Hannityon Monday, Desronvil said he wants to turn the focus away from him and back to Clancy\u2019s children.\n\u201cI know the whole focus has been on me, but we want to reel everything back and put the focus on the three angels that is that hand \u2014 the ones that can\u2019t speak for themselves, the ones that can\u2019t defend themselves, and the one that can\u2019t fight the battles, and those are Cora, Dawson, and Callan,\u201d Desronvil said in the statement.\nAlso Monday, the commonwealth of Massachusetts filed a motion askingReddington to stopmaking \u201cextrajudicial statements to the media\u201d ahead of a potential retrial.\nIn the days after the first trial concluded, Reddingtonspoke with several media outlets, including NBC News, to outline what happened in court and what he\u2019d like to see for his client going forward.\nThe Boston Globe and the local CBS station WBZ-TV jointly filed an opposition to the motion, claiming that it would restrict free speech. Sullivan decided to hear the arguments related to both of these motions at the November trial date.\nReddington got heated toward the end of the hearing, angrily stating he is \u201cso tired\u201d of people saying he has persecuted and attacked the holdout juror. In his motion, he argued that the judge should have dismissed the juror after the foreperson told Sullivan he was refusing to follow jury instructions.\nRebecca Cohen is a breaking news reporter for NBC News Digital.\n\u00a9\u00a02026 NBCUniversal Media, LLC", "date": "Tue, 29 Sep 2026 13:12:07 GMT"}, {"title": "Trump \u2018surprised\u2019 after Britain releases men detained near base used by U.S. forces", "url": "https://www.nbcnews.com/world/united-kingdom/trump-surprised-uk-releases-men-base-used-us-iran-rcna600423", "body": "\nFor You\nSettings\nnews Alerts\nThere are no new alerts at this time\nPresident Donald Trumpsaid he was surprised after British police released on bail five men arrested in connection with a hugesecurity operation near an air baseused by the U.S. Air Force to facilitatestrikes on Iran.\nLater, Secretary of State Marco Rubio said the incident \u201cclearly involves the hands of a foreign actor\u201d \u2014 an allegation British officials have not confirmed.\n\u201cI would have said, \u2018Don\u2019t let them have bail,\u2019\u201d Trump said in the Oval Office on Monday. \u201cI\u2019m surprised that they released them, I wouldn\u2019t have done that.\u201d\nTrump also indicated U.S. officials were cooperating with their British counterparts on the case.\n\u201cLook, they\u2019ve been very good. They are working with us very closely,\u201d he said, adding that the United States knew \u201ceverything\u201d about the suspects.\nThe five men, all British nationals in their 20s from London, were arrested Sunday on suspicion of terrorism and explosives offenses after three vehicles were seen moving toward RAF Fairford, a British air force station about 90 minutes west of London.\nCounterterrorism police took over the case after a major incident was declared, disrupting life in the normally quiet, rural community of the Cotswolds.\nOn Monday, British counterterrorism officials said the men had beenreleased on bail after \u201cextensive interviews and inquiries.\u201dThe investigation was not over, counterterrorism police head Laurence Taylor said, and \u201cmultiple lines of inquiry\u201d were still being probed.\nNo charges have been filed against the men.\nTaylor said the three vans that the suspects were traveling in have been \u201csubject to extensive examination with the support of military assets and forensic experts.\u201d\nHe did not say if any explosives have been found, as the question remains about what was inside the vans. According to several British reports, no explosives were found.\nNBC News reached out to counterterrorism police for clarification Tuesday but did not receive a response.\nOn Sunday, counterterrorism police said the men had been arrested on suspicion of committing offenses under the Explosives Act, a British legislation dating back to 1875 that regulates manufacturing, keeping, selling, carrying and importing of gunpowder and explosive substances. The men were further arrested on suspicion of preparation of a terrorist act.\nThe incident prompted questions about whether a foreign actor could be involved \u2014 in particular, Iran, after the country\u2019s Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps warned Britain this summer that RAF Fairford could be a \u201clegitimate target.\u201d It came months after then-Prime MinisterKeir Starmergave permission for the U.S. to use its bases for \u201cspecific and limited defensive\u201d operations against Iran.\nThe Iranian Embassy in Britain denied any involvement Monday.\nDuring an appearance on Fox News later Monday, Rubio said that \u201cwhat happened, what almost happened, what could have happened in the U.K. over the weekend, is a very serious situation.\u201d\n\u201cThere\u2019s a lot more news that\u2019ll come out on this in the days to come,\u201d he added.\nTaylor, with the United Kingdom\u2019s counterterrorism police, noted there had been \u201csignificant interest in whether the current geopolitical context has a bearing on this incident.\u201d\nThe investigators are keeping an open mind, he said.\n\u201cBe assured we are considering this from every possible angle, including that this may be actively committed by proxies or individuals either knowingly or unknowingly working on behalf of a foreign state,\u201d he added.\nYuliya Talmazan is a reporter for NBC News Digital, based in London.\n\u00a9\u00a02026 NBCUniversal Media, LLC", "date": "Tue, 29 Sep 2026 10:06:04 GMT"}, {"title": "Trump to host summit with top AI leaders in Washington", "url": "https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/donald-trump/trump-host-summit-top-ai-leaders-washington-rcna599853", "body": "\nFor You\nSettings\nnews Alerts\nThere are no new alerts at this time\nWASHINGTON \u2014 As he brushes aside artificial intelligencedoomsday warnings as a \u201choax,\u201dPresident Donald Trump on Tuesday will host nearly 20 tech titans at a White House lunch to discuss the rapidly advancing technology.\nThe group includes a who\u2019s who of the tech world, including CEO of SpaceX and Tesla Elon Musk, Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang, Meta CEO Mark Zuckerberg, Amazon founder Jeff Bezos, Alphabet CEO Sundar Pichai, Microsoft CEO Satya Nadella, Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei, Advanced Micro Devices CEO Lisa Su and OpenAI President Greg Brockman, according to a White House official.\nTrump is hosting the lunch with Speaker Mike Johnson, R-La. Vice President JD Vance, Commerce Secretary Howard Lutnick, Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent and White House Chief of Staff Susie Wiles also will be on hand.\nEarlier Tuesday, Trump unveiledAmerica.gov, a website that features an AI-powered tool meant to serve as a \u201cone-stop shop\u201d for Americans navigating government services.\nIn recent weeks, tech executives on the AI frontier, including Amodei and OpenAI\u2019s Sam Altman, have sounded the alarm about the potential dangers of AI, calling for greater government oversight and global cooperation in regulating the technology.\nTrump has dismissed the existential warnings, arguing that American tech companies need to continue to innovate andkeep their foot on the gasto stay ahead of China in the AI arms race.\nTrump isn\u2019t expected to deviate from that AI messaging, even as the dire warnings about humans\u2019 potentially losing control over self-improving AI systems \u2014 known as recursive self-improvement, or RSI \u2014 grow louder and more frequent.\n\u201cWhoever wins AI ... whoever wins \u201cSI,\u201d whoever wins super intelligence, wins. That\u2019s the group that wins, and we\u2019re leading now over China by a lot and everyone else, and we\u2019re going to keep it that way,\u201d Trump said inhis address last weekto the United Nations General Assembly.\n\u201cI\u2019m not going to stifle growth of something that will be bigger than the Industrial Revolution \u2014 many say bigger than the Industrial Revolution or the internet itself \u2014 and we will be very careful,\u201d he said.\nJohnson, a close Trump ally, began pushing for an AI summit with some of the biggest names in tech shortly afterwhistleblower Jacob Coxonmade international headlines by resigning from Claude maker Anthropic. Coxon warned that the people building those systems believe they could \u201ckill us all by the end of the decade.\u201d\nJohnson has made it clear he doesn\u2019t believe Washington should take a heavy-handed approach to regulating AI, arguing that existing laws and regulations can be applied to AI companies and suggesting that they should be held legally liable if their products caused harm.\nIn anappearance Monday on Fox Business, Johnson praised Trump for convening the AI leaders in one room.\n\u201cThey have a corporate responsibility, as every corporation does, to create safe products, right? And there\u2019s product liabilities laws that apply and a labyrinth of federal regulations on all this stuff already. We do not need a moratorium,\u201d he said.\nWhile lawmakers have introduced dozens of AI-related bills in recent years, the GOP-controlled Congress isshowing no urgency to acton the issue. House members aren\u2019t expected back in Washington until after the Nov. 3 midterm elections, and senators could leave town as soon as this week.\n\u201cWe do not need to jump in and hyperregulate this, because we\u2019ll lose the race to China,\u201d Johnson said on Fox Business. \u201cThe innovation is important to continue, but we have to strike the right balance.\u201d\nSenate Majority Leader John Thune, R-S.D., said he won\u2019t attend the AI lunch, but he privately met with Amodei in the Capitol on Monday evening.\n\u201cI hope it\u2019s productive,\u201d Thune said of Tuesday\u2019s summit.\nBefore the White House lunch, Trump gathered with Vance, Cabinet members and a handful of tech leaders a few blocks away at the Andrew W. Mellon Auditorium to unveilAmerica.gov, an AI-powered web portal and chatbot designed to improve Americans\u2019 experiences accessing federal resources.\nA White House official called the tool \u201ca new front door for the federal government that will provide a one-stop shop for Americans looking for information and resources from the federal government that are currently spread over a maze of various agency websites.\u201d\nThe National Design Studio, the year-old agency launched by Trump and led by Airbnb co-founder Joe Gebbia, helped create the project. Trump was introduced by self-help author and motivational speaker Tony Robbins.\n\u201cWe\u2019re launching today something that is really \u2014 it\u2019s not just simply a website; it\u2019s a restoration of America\u2019s founding promise, and it\u2019s a reinvention of your government for the 21st century and beyond,\u201d Trump said.\n\u201cInstead of forcing citizens to search through the endless maze of tens of thousands of terrible government websites and rules ... you\u2019ll now have one front door for every single question,\u201d Trump said. \u201cEverything will be answered easily and quickly, a digital home built around your time and your life.\u201d\nTrump likened the site to chatbots like Grok, Gemini and ChatGPT and a White House official told NBC News that America.gov is using Gemini and Grok.\nTrump also suggested he would \u201cend\u201d America.gov if it was ever hacked.\nIt \u201ccannot, in theory, be hacked into. And when they figure a way to do that, we\u2019ll end it, okay?\u201d Trump said. \u201cBecause they always figure a way, right?\u201d\nIn addition to Trump and Vance, Secretary of State Marco Rubio, Agriculture Secretary Brooke Rollins, Energy Secretary Chris Wright and Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services Administrator Mehmet Oz were on hand.\nSpaceX and Tesla CEO Elon Musk, Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang, Anthropic co-founder Tom Brown and Blue Origin CEO Dave Limp were also expected to attend, according to the White House.\nScott Wong is a senior congressional reporter for NBC News.\nMonica Alba is a White House correspondent for NBC News.\n\u00a9\u00a02026 NBCUniversal Media, LLC", "date": "Tue, 29 Sep 2026 10:00:42 GMT"}, {"title": "Jack Smith tells senators he won\u2019t be silenced by threats from Trump and allies", "url": "https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/justice-department/senate-gop-press-jack-smith-trump-jan-6-probe-doj-targets-investigator-rcna600306", "body": "\nFor You\nSettings\nnews Alerts\nThere are no new alerts at this time\nWASHINGTON \u2014 Former special counsel Jack Smith, whobrought two since-dismissed federal criminal casesagainst Donald Trump, told senators Tuesday that he would not be silenced \u201cby continued threats of prosecution\u201d over his handling of the investigation intoTrump\u2019s efforts to overturn his 2020 election lossin the lead-up to theJan. 6 attack on the U.S. Capitol.\nSmith appeared before the Senate Judiciary Committee amid the Justice Department\u2019spush to target law enforcement officials involvedwith his investigation into Trump\u2019s attempts to overturn the 2020 election results, as well as the probe into his handling of classified documents after he left office in January 2021.\nSmith said in his opening statement that he stood by his decision to bring charges against Trump, that it was the result of a deep investigation that uncovered substantive evidence.\n\u201cThe status, power, prominence, or political affiliation of the subject of an investigation must play no role in the decision to investigate, prosecute, or decline to prosecute,\u201d he said.\n\u201cOur investigation developed proof beyond a reasonable doubt that President Trump engaged in serious crimes against our nation,\u201d he said. Trump has said he did nothing wrong.\nThe hearing quickly devolved into insults by Republicans.\nLouisiana Republican John Kennedy began his questioning by asking Smith: \u201cWhat does the word arrogant mean?\u201d And he ended his time by telling Smith: \u201cYou make me want to throw up in my mouth.\u201d\nLater, Missouri Republican Eric Schmitt called Smith a \u201cdirtbag.\u201d\nIt came right after Schmitt tried to imply that the former special counsel had been in communication with Fani Willis, the district attorney of Fulton County who was at the time working on a state case against Trump. Schmitt tried to place Smith in Atlanta at a Hawks game. When Smith denied it, Schmitt suggested he\u2019d committed perjury.\nBut Schmitt appeared to have confused his basketball teams: Smith had actually been in attendance at an Iowa Hawkeyes game, which was Caitlin Clark\u2019s team at the time.\nSen. Amy Klobuchar, D-Minn., said Schmitt owed Smith an apology. He didn\u2019t give it.\nCommittee Chairman Chuck Grassley, R-Iowa, has repeatedly criticized Smith. Grassley asserted that Smith misused his investigative powers and politically targeted Republicans. Smith, who as a career prosecutor went after Republicans and Democrats, has denied such allegations.\nGrassley said that Smith must answer for what the senator portrays as \u201crampant abuse of authority and political targeting\u201d funded by American taxpayers. He said his goal is to expose the \u201cweaponization\u201d of the Justice Department for actions taken during the Biden administration.\n\u201cJack Smith\u2019s corrupt operation undermined the rule of law and threatened our very political institutions,\u201d he said Tuesday.\nSmith has said he believes a jury would have convicted Trump in the 2020 election-related case had it gone to trial and that the notion that his decisions were \u201cinfluenced or directed by the Biden administration or other political actors is, in a word, laughable.\u201d Smith\u2019s final report in the election interference case stated that Trump \u201cinspired his supporters to commit acts of physical violence\u201d at the Capitol on Jan. 6.\nTrump pleaded not guilty in Smith\u2019s Jan. 6 case and denied after the attack that he incited his supporters, calling his remarks at a rally that day \u201ctotally appropriate.\u201d Trump laterdescribed Jan. 6 defendants as \u201chostages\u201dand gave them mass clemency upon his return to office.\nThe Trump administration last year launched a so-called grand conspiracy investigation looking at actions taken by federal law enforcement officials dating back to 2016, whichwould fall outside the typical five-year statute of limitations for most federal crimes.\nThe Trump administration also has purged FBI and Justice Department officials involved in past Trump investigations.Defense attorneys for current and former officials informed a court last week that DOJ officials appear interested in probing the circumstances of an August 2022 FBI search of Trump\u2019s Mar-a-Lago estate in Florida, during which agents found classified documents that were at the heart of one of the since-dismissed Trump cases.\nJoe diGenova, who was a federal prosecutor during Ronald Reagan\u2019s presidency, headed up the \u201cgrand conspiracy\u201d investigation until heleft the Justice Department this month.He told NBC News that \u201clanes got crossed\u201d in his final days.\nSen. Corey Booker, D-N.J., said the law enforcement who worked diligently on the cases were fired for doing their jobs, then vilified  and harassed by Trump and his allies who are still \u201cpushing false narratives that somehow Donald Trump is the victim.\u201d\n\u201cThis is an eight-year attempt to lie to the American people, and this is their closing argument, everybody, right before an election, this is what they want to spend their time on: perpetrating a lie,\u201d Booker said.\nIn his first public testimony before Congress earlier this year, Smithtold the House Judiciary Committee that he would not be intimidatedand predicted that the Trump administration would attempt to launch a criminal case against him. Months later, one of Trump\u2019s allies on Capitol Hill, Rep. Jim Jordan, R-Ohio, asked the Trump administration toinvestigate Jordan\u2019s assertion that Smith \u201cseemingly\u201dmade false statements in congressional testimony.\nSmith\u2019s lawyers said he provided \u201ctruthful, responsive answers\u201d and that Jordan\u2019s referral \u201creflects a chilling eagerness to weaponize the justice system by attacking a career, nonpartisan public servant who faithfully and fearlessly performed his duties by following the facts and the law.\u201dFormer federal prosecutors have told NBC Newsthat the Justice Department would have a tough time bringing a perjury case against Smith.\nSmith didn\u2019t discuss the classified documents investigation because Trump-appointed U.S. District Judge Aileen Cannon ordered that that portion of his report as special counsel cannot be released. The Justice Department has taken the position that answering questions about that investigation would violate Cannon\u2019s order.\nRyan J. Reilly is a justice reporter for NBC News.\nNational Security Researcher\n\u00a9\u00a02026 NBCUniversal Media, LLC", "date": "Tue, 29 Sep 2026 09:00:40 GMT"}, {"title": "Shark dropped this self-cleaning vacuum by $130, shoppers love it because \u2018it really sucks\u2019", "url": "https://www.nbcnews.com/select/shopping/shark-lift-away-vacuum-deal-of-the-day-2026-rcna600468", "body": "\nNBC Selected independently determines what we cover and recommend. When you click on or buy through our links, we earn a commission.Learn more.\nCory Fernandez\nCommerce Editor\nThe popularShark Navigator Lift-Away Upright Vacuumis $130 off on Amazon right now. The versatile cordedupright vacuumworks on multiple surfaces and comes with several attachments, making it a great appliance for those who want an appliance that doesn\u2019t have a time limit and also cleans upholstery.\nIt\u2019s rarely on sale for this much (more than 40 percent off!) outside of sale events, so this is a treat for anyone who wants to take advantage ofearly October Prime Day dealsto upgrade their cleaning toolkit now. Keep reading to learn more about whyshoppers say\u201cthis thing sucks!\u201d and it\u2019s a good thing.\nThis is one of Shark\u2019s many popular vacuums from its Navigator lineup. It\u2019s corded, so you can clean an entire home without worrying about battery life. It also comes with several attachments, including a crevice tool and upholstery attachments, perfect for cleaning furniture,wallsand shelves.\nManyshoppers mentionhow strong the suction of the vacuum is, with one appreciating that it has levels from \u201crespectful breeze to this will remove a man\u2019s soul through his nostrils.\u201d In other words, those fine cat hairs are not getting by this machine. Plus, it has a self-cleaning brush roll with bristles designed to automatically prevent hair wrap; it cleans itself as it runs so you don\u2019t have to manually do that part.\nPeople who bought the vacuum also like how easy it is to move around (though they don\u2019t classify it as lightweight and say it takes a couple cleanings to get used to the maneuverability) and how effective it is at cleaning all types of pet hair, even in hard-to-clean areas like stairs and upholstery.\nIt has a HEPA filter fordustand works on multiple surfaces, including carpet andhardwood.  The top pod of the body lifts away from the base (hence the name), so you can use it as a handheld vacuum when you don\u2019t want to roll the entire thing around your home or when you need to move more freely to get into tight spaces.\nThe Shark Navigator Lift-Away Upright Vacuum is a highly- and positively-reviewed appliance that often sells out during sale events like Amazon Prime Day \u2014 so if you can get it now, do it. It also rarely goes on sale for this much, its lowest price ever was only $20 less than this and that was during a Black Friday sale. The vacuum has a self-cleaning brush roll, multiple attachments and incredibly strong suction.\nWant more from NBC Selected?Sign upfor our newsletter, The Selection, and shop smarter.\nI\u2019m acommerce editorat NBC Selected, where I write about deals and sale events daily. I oversee NBC Selected\u2019s cleaning stories and often interview home cleaning experts. I also write about cleaning appliances and vacuums for sale events, including Amazon Prime Day.\nCatch up on NBC Selected\u2019s in-depth coverage oftech and tools,wellnessand more, and follow us onFacebook,Instagram,TwitterandTikTokto stay up to date.\nShark ChillPill\nCory Fernandez is a commerce editor for NBC Selected, and an authority on all things food, cooking, kitchen, home, and general lifestyle.\nShark ChillPill\nWe can help you find\n\u00a9\u00a02026 SELECTED | All rights reserved. The use of this website means that you accept the confidentiality regulations and the conditions of service.", "date": "Tue, 29 Sep 2026 15:07:04 GMT"}, {"title": "Your winter emergency car kit isn\u2019t complete without this item", "url": "https://www.nbcnews.com/select/shopping/best-windshield-scrapers-rcna233446", "body": "\nNBC Selected independently determines what we cover and recommend. When you click on or buy through our links, we earn a commission.Learn more.\nCory Fernandez\nCommerce Editor\nAfter living in upstate New York, I\u2019ve come to love many things aboutwinter. Ice skating, building snowmen and buyingsnow bootsdistract me from the things I don\u2019t love so much, likeshoveling heavy snowand scraping ice off a car windshield. If you live in acold climateor experienced winter storms, then you know the frustration that comes with the latter, especially when you\u2019re in a rush to work.\nWindshield scrapers and snow brushes are some of the best tools for getting the job done, plus, they\u2019re far safer than pouring hot water over the top and probably shattering it. To find the best windshield scrapers and brushes, I spoke with auto glass repair experts and compiled a list of options based on their guidance.\nWant more from NBC Selected?Sign upfor our newsletter, The Selection, and shop smarter.\nAll of the products below have at least a 4-star average rating from 500 reviews or more on Amazon.\nThis combination windshield scraper and snow brush is 27 inches long when put together. The plastic scraper is also detachable from the brush and the handle has a nonslip grip to keep your hand steady.\nThe RevHeads windshield scraper is small but still effective for ice removal, thanks to its dual-sided blades. One side is a smooth plastic blade for clearing soft snow, while the claw side is best for breaking up stubborn blocks of ice. It has a nonslip grip on the handle and there\u2019s a built-in loop if you want to hang it on a wall when you aren\u2019t using it.\nSimilar to the windshield scraper and brush combination from Astroai above, this one has an extendable handle that allows you to reach across your windshield and even the top of your car. If you have a large car or own a truck, this may be your best option. It detaches into three separate parts, which you can use individually or as two or three pieces together. The brush attachment also spins 360 degrees, making it easier to brush away snow in hard-to-reach areas or angles.\nAnother small one-hand option, this windshield scraper has a 4-inch blade and 10-inch handle. Similar to others on this list, it has two sides: one to help break apart the layer of ice on the windshield and then another to smoothly push it away. It\u2019s also small enough to store in your glove compartment, trunk or under a seat.\nThere are a few important factors to consider when choosing a windshield scraper. Here\u2019s what I kept in mind to pull together this list:\nHere a few more important factors to keep in mind when buying and using a windshield scraper and snow brush:\nThe height and size of your car will partially determine how easy it is to use an ice scraper and snow brush on it. If you have a large truck or SUV, opt for a windshield scraper with an extendable handle so you can clear snow across the top and other hard-to-reach spots.\nIf you live in a place where snow andfreezing temperaturesare routinely expected, invest in a windshield scraper with an extendable handle and a brush attachment, since you\u2019re more likely to have an icy windshield. For milder climates, a snow brush may be enough to get the job done.\nBoth Asch and Patterson strongly recommend against pouring warm or hot water on your car windshield to melt ice. This is because the sudden change in temperature causes thermal stress and often leads to glass cracking and breaking. You should also avoid usinghair dryers, ice picks or hammers to scratch away ice, all of which can also crack windshield glass.\nBoth of our experts recommend using the scraper in an up and down motion rather than a side to side one to avoid scratching the glass.\nTo help with de-icing your car windshield, Patterson recommends turning on your car around 10 minutes before you plan to drive. \u201cStart your engine and turn on the heater to the maximum setting to absorb excess moisture with hot air,\u201d he says. Then, turn on theair conditionerto help dry the water from the melting ice. Lastly, open the carwindowsto let cold air come in and further help dry the melting ice.\nWiper fluid\nBefore winter begins, you can change your wiper fluid to a formula suited for cold weather, according to Asch. \u201cThiswinter formulahas de-icing properties that can help melt snow and ice on your windshield and prevent your windshield washer system from freezing,\u201d he says. Another option is to put the wiper fluid in a spray bottle and spray that on the ice to help with melting.\nWindshield wipers\nAsch also recommends replacing your windshield wipers every six months to ensure they are in working condition and are effective at getting rid of light snow and other debris. Additionally, you should leave your wipers up during the winter to prevent them from sticking to your windshield because of ice.\nAt NBC Selected, we work with experts who have specialized knowledge and authority based on relevant training and/or experience. We also take steps to ensure all expert advice and recommendations are made independently and without undisclosed financial conflicts of interest.\nI\u2019m acommerce editorat NBC Selected, where I write about several home products and appliances. I\u2019ve also previously written about winter supplies and appliances, including snow blowers and snow shovels.\nCatch up on NBC Selected\u2019s in-depth coverage oftech and tools,wellnessand more, and follow us onFacebook,Instagram,XandTikTokto stay up to date.\nShark ChillPill\nCory Fernandez is a commerce editor for NBC Selected, and an authority on all things food, cooking, kitchen, home, and general lifestyle.\nShark ChillPill\nWe can help you find\n\u00a9\u00a02026 SELECTED | All rights reserved. The use of this website means that you accept the confidentiality regulations and the conditions of service.", "date": "Mon, 13 Oct 2025 16:31:42 GMT"}, {"title": "New early October Prime Day deals just dropped \u2014 I hand-picked 41+ actually worth shopping", "url": "https://www.nbcnews.com/select/shopping/early-october-prime-day-deals-2026-rcna600293", "body": "\nNBC Selected independently determines what we cover and recommend. When you click on or buy through our links, we earn a commission.Learn more.\nZoe Malin\nReporter\nOctober Prime Day(a.k.a Prime Big Deal Days) is only a week away: ThePrime member-exclusive runs from October 6 to 7, but you don\u2019t need to wait until then to shop. Amazon already dropped prices on dozens of products, some of which are even at their lowest price ever. We don\u2019t know how long these early October Prime Day deals will last, however \u2014 popular products often sell out before the sale officially starts, so if something catches your eye, add it to your cart soon.\nI\u2019ve covered October Prime Day for years, so I know how to find deals that are actually worth buying. To prepare for Amazon\u2019s mega sale, I combed through countless discounts to find the very best ones: Each early October Prime Day deal I recommend below is highly rated, as well as at its lowest price in at least three months, with discounts up to 80 percent off. I\u2019m frequently updating this article with editor-approved picks, so save the link and check back in often to see what\u2019s new.\nWant more from NBC Selected?Sign upfor our newsletter, The Selection, and shop smarter.\nTurn any TV into a smart TV with Amazon\u2019s Fire Stick, which you simply plug into an HDMI port to use. It lets youstream entertainmentfrom your favorite platforms and play games without a console \u2014 plus, when you\u2019re not using the TV, you can choose from over 2,000 pieces of art and photography to display. The Fire Stick comes with a remote that lets you control the TV via buttons or voice commands.\nColgate\u2019stoothpasteis made with 5 percent hydrogen peroxide, a bleaching ingredient that helps remove stains from teeth and make your smile less yellow. There\u2019s also cavity-fighting fluoride in its formula. The mintywhitening toothpasteis safe for daily use and doesn\u2019t causetooth sensitivity, according to the brand.\nWe\u2019re all about a practical gift at NBC Selected, and this \u201cfancy\u201dhand soapdefinitely falls into that category if you already need some holiday shopping inspiration. NBC Selected commerce editor Bethany Heitman says A\u0113sop Reverence is her favorite option right now, and a little luxury she allows herself. \u201cThe scent is a combination of citrus and wood, and the formula leaves my hands soft after washing,\u201d she says. \u201cI also like how the sleek bottle looks sitting on my bathroom sink.\u201d\nOnce built, thisLegoflower arrangement can be a table centerpiece or shelf decor in your home. It comes with over 1,000 pieces to make a pot and 14 blooms, including camellias, peonies, hydradras and lilies, plus there\u2019s a detailed instruction booklet that walks you through every step. The brand recommends this set for adults because it\u2019s a complex build, but it\u2019s a great challenge for younger Lego masters, too.\nWith Blink\u2019s doorbell, you can monitor visitors and deliveries at all times. It connects to the brand\u2019s app, letting you livestream head-to-toe video (including in night vision) and audio, as well as speak to people. The doorbell runs on two AA batteries, comes with a mounting kit and you can install it wire-free. There\u2019s also a Blink subscription available for an additional fee, which gives you access to features like in-app person detection and motion-activated notifications.\nThe Casper Sleep Original Pillow is a NBC Selected staff-favorite product, and many of us rest our heads on it every night. It\u2019s stuffed with a down alternative material, has a breathable cotton cover and gets its medium-soft feel from a two-layer design: the inner layer is firmer to offer support, while outer layer is plush for extra comfort. Thepillowis machine-washable and clump-resistant.\nYou can use pods or grounds to make three types ofcoffeewith this appliance: classic, rich and iced. It also lets you brew cups between 6 and 24 ounces, and it has a removable water reservoir for easy refills. To save space on your counter, the compact machine has a storage drawer inside so you can put away the brew basket or permanent filter when they\u2019re not in use.\nThisrobot vacuumsuctions up dirt and debris, and it has a built-in mop that scrubs floors to leave them shiny. It\u2019s designed with sensors that map your home to detect and avoid obstacles, and it connects to the Roomba Home app so you can select cleaning modes, create cleaning schedules, adjust suction level and more.You can also set keep-out and no-mop zones where needed, like on area rugs. The appliance comes with a charging dock, reusable microfiber mopping pad and edge sweeping brush.\nPhilips Sonicare\u2019s 5950Electric Toothbrushhas all the features dentists told us to look for while shopping: a pressure sensor, a timer, a brush head replacement reminder and adjustable brushing settings. Its battery lasts for about 21 days before you need to recharge it, and it comes with a travel case and charging stand.\nI prefer using a gel-creammoisturizerlike this one in the morning because it\u2019s lightweight and absorbs into my skin quickly, letting me layersunscreenand makeup on top almost immediately. Byoma\u2019s fragrance- and oil-free cream is formulated with hydrating ingredients likeceramidesandniacinamide, as well as soothing green tea to reduce redness. It also earned the National Eczema Association\u2019s Seal of Acceptance.\nOrganize your home,dorm, garage or shed with these clear plastic storage bins, each of which comes with a lid that has locking latches for an extra secure seal. The bins come in a pack of six, have a roomy 54-quart capacity and have pull handles, making them convenient to pick up, pull out and move around.\nI bought thisblenderin 2021 and still swear by it today. Its sharp blades and powerful motor quickly break down frozen and room temperature ingredients, as well as crushes ice almost instantly. The blender comes with a dishwasher-safe 72-ounce pitcher that has a pour spout and handle, letting me make big batches of smoothies, soups, purees, dressings and batter to feed multiple people.\nThistabletlets you read, scroll, watch shows and movies, listen to music, play games and connect with loved ones online. You can download hundreds of apps from Amazon\u2019s app store, as well as use Alexa voice commands to launch apps, make searches, set timers and more. The tablet has a 8-inch HD display with a rear-facing camera, and you get up to 13 hours of battery life on a single charge, according to the brand.\nContigo\u2019s 20-ounce vacuum-seal insulated, stainless steeltravel mugkeeps beverages hot for up to 13 hours and cold for up to 28 hours, according to the brand. It\u2019s built with a locking, leak- and spill-resistant lid, which you can open and close with one hand for on-the-go convenience. There\u2019s also an inner hook on the lid that holds tea bags, preventing the string from getting damp or lost in the liquid. The mug fits in most cup holders and is dishwasher-safe.\nAs the weather gets colder, you may also notice that your home\u2019s air feels dry, which can negatively impact your skin, worsen allergies and even cause nosebleeds, according to experts we talked to. Ahumidifierhelps solve those problems by adding moisture back into the air, and this one does so without you ever seeing the mist it produces. It has a 1-gallon water tank that gives you up to 48 hours of continuous use, and you can pair it with the brand\u2019s app to adjust settings and set schedules. It\u2019s quiet to prevent disrupted sleep, and it has a built-in aroma stone you can add essential oils to.\nSummer may be over, but there\u2019s always a chance for warm fall days, so you might want to have your barbecue on standby for a few more weeks. Be prepared to grill with this three-piecetool set. It comes with tongs, a spatula and a basting brush, all of which are dishwasher-safe, have soft silicone handles and come with hanging loops for easy storage.\nHere are the best early October Prime Day sales to know about. Keep in mind, not every item from a brand is discounted as described below.\nOctober Amazon Prime Day historically offers Prime members some of the lowest prices of the year on products across categories. These limited-time early deals give you the opportunity to shop when inventory is the highest, before the sale officially begins on October 6.\nAll of my recommendations above are based on NBC Selected\u2019s previous coverage and reporting. I also included products the NBC Selected staff tested. I evaluated the quality of these deals by running them through price trackers like CamelCamelCamel, and I only included those that:\nThe best deals on October Prime Day usually center around Amazon-owned technology likeEcho,Fire,BlinkandRingdevices, smallhomeandkitchen appliances,beauty products, household basics and groceries. Additionally, because this sale is in the fall, it unofficially kicks off the holiday shopping season, so brands discount popular gifts like gift cards and toys, as well asAdvent calendarsand themed decor. You\u2019ll also be able to shop Amazon\u2019s Lightning Deals during the event: They\u2019re lucrative flash sales that give shoppers a short window to purchase limited quantities of specific products.\nProducts across all categories are generally cheaper on October Prime Day, with many brands discounting products by over 20 percent. Prices on tech, household essentials and groceries, small home and kitchen appliances, beauty products, giftable items and holiday decor tend to drop the most overall.\nOctober Prime Day 2026 is from October 6 to 7. It starts at 12 a.m. PT on October 6 \u2014 that\u2019s 3 a.m. ET, 2 a.m. CT and 1 a.m. MT. It ends at 11:59 p.m. PT on October 7 \u2014  that\u2019s October 8 at 2:59 a.m. ET, 1:59 a.m. CT and 12:59 a.m. MT.\nYes, October Prime Day is actually worth it, so long as you shop strategically. The sale offers some of the lowest prices of the year on products across categories, giving you a chance to start shopping for holiday gifts, upgrade outdated or broken items and restock your favorite basics, like skin care, cleaning supplies and groceries. But ultimately, deals are only worth it if you actually need something, so plan your spending to prevent making impulse purchases.\nI\u2019m areporter for NBC Selectedwho has covered Amazon Prime Day and Prime Big Deal Days for over six years. I frequently write about thehistory of Amazon Prime Day, what\u2019s included in a Prime membership, what to buy and skip during Amazon\u2019s major sales and more. I also appear in related broadcast segments on NBC News NOW and TODAY. For this story, I previewed a list of early deals from Amazon and sorted through them to find worthwhile sales you should know about.\nCatch up on NBC Selected\u2019s in-depth coverage oftech and tools,wellnessand more, and follow us onFacebook,Instagram,XandTikTokto stay up to date.\nShark ChillPill\nZoe Malin is a reporter for NBC Selected who leads coverage for tentpole sale events including Amazon Prime Day, Black Friday and Cyber Monday.\nShark ChillPill\nWe can help you find\n\u00a9\u00a02026 SELECTED | All rights reserved. The use of this website means that you accept the confidentiality regulations and the conditions of service.", "date": "Mon, 28 Sep 2026 19:22:44 GMT"}, {"title": "The best first aid kits to have on hand for non-hospital emergencies", "url": "https://www.nbcnews.com/select/shopping/best-first-aid-kits-rcna600294", "body": "\nNBC Selected independently determines what we cover and recommend. When you click on or buy through our links, we earn a commission.Learn more.\nCory Fernandez\nCommerce Editor\nA first aid kit is something that everyone should have at home, whether it\u2019s a small, portable one or a large kit with hundreds of pieces. First aid kits are essential when it comes to treating small, non-hospital injuries likeburns, scrapes and wounds, but they\u2019re also great to take with you on vacations or short trips in case anything unexpected pops up.\n\u201cEmergenciesdon\u2019t come with advance notice so first aid kits are essential to being able to handle minor and emergency situations,\u201d saysDr. Chandani DeZure, a double-board certified pediatrician atStanford Medicine Children\u2019s Health. \u201cI typically recommend having first aid kits for both ease, convenience, and safety.\u201d\nTo find the best first aid kits, including portable ones that fit in abackpack, glove compartment or tote, I spoke with DeZure and other medical experts for their guidance and recommendations. The first aid kits I recommend below vary by price, size, and resources to make it easier for you to find one or multiple options suitable for your needs.\nWant more from NBC Selected?Sign upfor our newsletter, The Selection, and shop smarter.\nAll of the products below are either direct recommendations from our experts or editors, or have at least a four-star rating from 500 or more reviews on Amazon.\nWhat\u2019s included:multiple bandage types, hand wipes, gauze, antibacterial ointment, anti-itch cream, pain relief medication, instant cold pack, medical tape, butterfly closures, scissors and first aid guide|Pieces:160\nBand-Aid\u2019s first aid kit has a lot of what you\u2019d expect to find in most traditional first aid kits, but it also has much more. In addition to just regular adhesive bandages, it has ones of different sizes and even includes butterfly closures. It also comes with Tylenol for pain relief, hand wipes, multiple types of gauze and angled scissors, which can be helpful when cutting medical tape, gauze and bandages. It also has anti-itch cream in case you have a reaction to something likepoison ivyor poison oak.\nShoppers also saythat they like that the kit has a lot of supplies needed for common injuries. Plus, many people who bought it like tokeep it in their carsor RVs.\nWhat\u2019s included:adhesive bandages, ice pack, gauze, cotton swab, alcohol pads, medical tape, antibacterial ointment, towelettes, first aid guide|Pieces:105\nThis small first aid kit is perfect for anyone who wants a basic supply that has just about everything to help tend to minor but common injuries. The square-shaped container is clear on the front and it opens up like a small briefcase, so it\u2019s easy to see where everything (and grab it) is at a moment\u2019s notice. It has a handle on the top and latches to keep it securely closed, as well as small loops, which you can use to attach to something like a backpack or duffel bag.\nIt\u2019s only around 5 inches by 5 inches, so it fits in a glove compartment, a coat pocket, a tote or other small personal item you might keep with you all the time.\nWhat\u2019s included:multiple types and sizes of adhesive bandages, butterfly closures, alcohol wipes, iodine wipes, cotton swabs, cleansing wipes, CPR face mask, gloves, cold compress, face mask, scissors, tweezers, tourniquet, emergency blanket, gauze, medical tape, gauze sponge, safety pins, first aid guide and more|Pieces:350\nIf you want an almost all-inclusive first aid kit, this is the best option on this list. While you may have to buy a few other items to make sure the kits have everything you want, this one has a lot. It has multiple types of adhesive bandages, butterfly closures, several types of gauze, including sponges made from gauze, gloves, aface maskand much more. Plus, all of the supplies come in aplastic containerwith multiple trays for holding everything.\nThe case has a handle at the top with loops that you can attach to personal items. Another great feature about this kit is that you can mount it on the wall, which allows you to have it out in the open and ready to use, particularly because the cover is clear, similar to some other first aid kits on this list.\nWhat\u2019s included:hand wipes, gauze, antibacterial ointment, adhesive bandages in multiple sizes, medical tape, pain relief medication, alcohol cleansing wipes and first aid pads|Pieces:80\nThis is the perfect first aid kit for someone who wants something that is not only very simple, but small enough to fit in almost any pocket or backpack. The case is only 6.2 inches by 5.5 inches and weighs less than 7 ounces, and the front of the case is clear, so not only does it fit in small areas, it\u2019s also easy to see what you have at any given moment, in case you need to refill on medication, bandages or hand wipes.Shoppers saythey enjoy that it\u2019s compact but also includes practically everything you need.\nWhat\u2019s included:multiple types of adhesive bandages, tourniquet, multiple types of pads, safety pins, whistle, gloves, gauze, tweezers, cotton swabs, cleansing wipes, cold press, scissors, medical tape, butterfly closures and more|Pieces:250\nThis first aid kit also has a lot, including items that would be useful if you find yourself outdoors and lost, like a whistle and a compass. You can bring this along with a separatehiking emergency kit, duringcamping tripsor during any form of long-distance travel by car. Just like all of the options on this list it has bandages, but it also has adhesive bandages of different sizes, shapes and types, as well as butterfly closures, safety pins, tweezers and multiple types of gauze.\nThe kit is also available in several sizes, including options with85,100and200 pieces. It comes with a zipper and a fabric container (with a handle), while the 85-piece kit is the only option that has a clear plastic case.\nWhat's included:multiple types and sizes of bandages, ointment, medical tape, cleansing wipes, butterfly closures, hydrocortisone cream, non-stick pads, pain relief meds|Pieces:130\nThis first aid kit is mainly made up of bandages, making it the perfect set for children to use on their own (though you\u2019ll want to keep the pain meds out of reach). The reusable tin and bandages come with vibrant patterns and colors, and the kit has everything they need to patch up their own nicks and scuffs, plus no scissors you need to worry about them mishandling.\n\u201cMy kids actually ask for a bandage now; the fun designs turn every scrape into a mini celebration,\u201d says NBC senior production managerRebecca Delman. \u201cThe case is small and compact, so I keep it in my bag or the car. It comes with an assortment of colors and sizes, plus medicine for bumps and bruises. Best of all, they actually stay on!\u201d\nHere\u2019s everything our experts recommend considering before buying first aid kits to keep at home or in your car.\nSupplies and medication:Regardless of the size, most first aid kits should include supplies to help treat small but common injuries. \u201cTo me, this would include adhesive bandages of different sizes, gauze, alcohol wipes, wrap bandage, medical tape, antibiotic ointment, vaseline, disposable nitrile gloves, tweezers, small scissors, CPR face shield, burn gel, and a flashlight,\u201d says pediatrician DeZure. \u201cDepending on the circumstances, you may want to add a foilblanketand whistle if hiking, or ankle braces/splints and ice pack if playing sports, or medications such as antacids, hydrocortisone cream, aloe vera, acetaminophen, ibuprofen (for pain/fever), or diphenhydramine (for allergies).\u201d\nDeZure also recommends making sure that you have products, including proper dosages of medicine for children. Outside of medication for pain relief, you should also consider adding something like an epinephrine pen to your first aid kit, particularly if you will be spending time outdoors. You should also consider adding Narcan to a first aid kit to prevent overdoses, according to DeZure.\nWith all of this in mind, I made sure to include first aid kits that come with at least adhesive bandages, gauze, alcohol wipes, antibiotic ointment, gloves, tweezers and some form of pain relief medication, since many of these can be used to treat the most common types of non-emergency injuries. Things like small open wounds, scratches, scrapes, small burns, sprains, headaches and splinters can often be treated with these supplies. Some of the first aid kits I included however, included these products and more, such as things for emergency situations that are somewhat less common, such as a thermal blanket and whistle, in case you find yourself stranded or lost outdoors.\nSize:In selecting the first aid kits above, I also considered their general size, where they could be kept and what types of emergencies they could be used for. \u201cFor the most part, the first aid kit is either a solution to a minor problem or a temporary measure until more emergency help can arrive,\u201d says DeZure. \u201cDepending on the circumstances, a larger first aid kit can provide more options and solutions for a broader range of problems than a smaller one.\u201d\nWith this in mind, it might be worth having multiple kits for different situations or places (for example the one in your car may be less equipped than the one in your home). Additionally, if you don\u2019t live close to or have access to a hospital or some other form of emergency care, that may be more of a reason to shop for a first aid kit with more resources than the traditional first aid kit.\nHere is what you should keep in mind when shopping for first aid kits for you and/or your family.\nDecide how you\u2019ll use it.\u201cPersonalizing a first aid kit will take more time than buying one, but that extra time spent will be worth it in the event that a first aid kit is needed,\u201d says DeZure. Most generic first aid kits will have things for wounds, small burns, scratches, like adhesive bandages and antibiotic ointment, so more basic kits may be suitable. With that said, if you or your family spends more time outdoors or plays sports, for example, you may need a first aid kit with more supplies.\nMake personalizations to it.\u201cThe benefits of having a first aid kit are going to vary based upon what you put in it and why you need it,\u201d says Dr. Megan Cobb, a pediatric emergency medicine physician at theUniversity of Maryland Golisano Children\u2019s Hospital. \u201cOn an everyday basis, it is great to be able to quickly address your family\u2019s or your own scratches and cuts to reduce bleeding, infection risk, and improve comfort.\u201d In other words, keep in mind that you may have to add items separately based on your needs, like bottled water, an epinephrine pen, an inhaler or other items that don\u2019t traditionally come in first aid kits.\nShop for travel-friendly options.In addition to having a first aid kit you can keep at home and reach for at any given moment, you should also consider keeping a portable one, such as one you can store in your care for more unexpected moments.\nMake sure it has supplies for people of all ages.It\u2019s important to remember that you won\u2019t always be able to use the same medication or dosages for children. \u201cIf you have babies or kids, that means having liquid medications and smaller sized bandages available. My son gets frequent nosebleeds, so I have tools to help with that in all my first aid kits. For elderly people, having extra doses of essential medications would be vital.\u201d\nThe most essential items every first aid kit should have will always depend most on your personal needs and those of your family. Though, in general, you should make sure that any first aid kit you have has basic supplies to treat and/or prevent small wounds, bruises, sprains, swelling, infection, itches or pain, according to Cobb.\nThis includes adhesive bandages, antibacterial ointment, gauze, ice packs, hydrocortisone cream, antihistamine, ibuprofen, acetaminophen and water. You should also make sure all of the medication is safe for children.\nThere are several types of first aid kits, but most of them are different based on their size, how much supplies they hold and the many types of supplies they have. Many first aid kits have items for small injuries like adhesive bandages, gauze, hand wipes, pain relief medication, anti-itch cream and antihistamine.\nMore advanced types of first aid kits have items for somewhat more severe injuries and/or more uncommon situations, such as tourniquets or compasses and whistles to help when lost.\nThe best way to assemble a first aid kit is to prioritize including whatever supplies are most needed for you or anyone else in your family, according to both DeZure and Cobb. In addition to buying one of the first aid kits above, you may also have to buy some other important items to pair with them, like an epinephrine pen, inhaler, pain relief medication and bottled water.\nPlus, you may need other items to help you during situations where you may be far away from emergency care, like when you\u2019re camping outdoors. In this case, it may also be smart to get things like a compass, GPS system, some sort of light source and a blanket for warmth.\nAt NBC Selected, we work with experts who have specialized knowledge and authority based on relevant training and/or experience. We also take steps to ensure all expert advice and recommendations are made independently and without undisclosed financial conflicts of interest.\nI\u2019m a commerce editor at NBC Selected, where I write about home, kitchen and cleaning appliances and guides. I also write about everyday lifestyle, safety and preparedness products. I\u2019ve written aboutcervical traction devices,how to prepare for hurricane seasonandhow to shop for a snow blower. For this story I spoke with two medical experts about what to include or look for in a first aid kit that suits your needs.\nCatch up on NBC Selected\u2019s in-depth coverage oftech and tools,wellnessand more, and follow us onFacebook,Instagram,XandTikTokto stay up to date.\nShark ChillPill\nCory Fernandez is a commerce editor for NBC Selected, and an authority on all things food, cooking, kitchen, home, and general lifestyle.\nShark ChillPill\nWe can help you find\n\u00a9\u00a02026 SELECTED | All rights reserved. The use of this website means that you accept the confidentiality regulations and the conditions of service.", "date": "Mon, 28 Sep 2026 17:45:40 GMT"}, {"title": "Ahead of rare execution, lone woman on Tennessee\u2019s death row says \u2018I am at peace\u2019", "url": "https://www.nbcnews.com/news/us-news/ahead-rare-execution-lone-woman-tennessee-death-row-says-peace-rcna600279", "body": "\nFor You\nSettings\nnews Alerts\nThere are no new alerts at this time\nWith her execution days away,the lone womanon Tennessee\u2019s death row says she is prepared to die but \u201cnervous\u201d about how the state intends to carry it out.\nBefore learning Monday that Gov. Bill Lee would not be intervening in her case, Christa Pike wrote in a letter to NBC News, shared by her spiritual adviser: \u201cWhether my clemency is granted or not, I am at peace.\u201d\n\u201cI am not afraid to die,\u201d wrote Pike, who has been on death row for over three decades. \u201cI\u2019m only nervous about the process.\u201d\nTennessee is poised to execute Pike, 50, on Wednesday by lethal injection \u2014 the first time in more than 200 years that the state will put a woman to death, according tohistorical records.\nIn 1995, Pike was 18 when Knoxville police say she and two teenage accomplices worked together to torture, slash and fatally beat Colleen Slemmer, 19. Police say Pike and the others lured Slemmer, a classmate in their Job Corps training program, to an abandoned steam plant at the nearby University of Tennessee and attacked her.\nThe case became sensationalized when investigators said it wastied to the occultbecause someone had carved a pentagram into Slemmer\u2019s chest and Pike bragged to another student that she had kept a fragment of the victim\u2019s skull as a \u201csouvenir.\u201d\nPike doesn\u2019t dispute her role in the grisly killing. But details about her upbringing, marked by years of sexual and physical abuse and neglect, were not shared with the jury that sentenced her to death. Her lawyers believe that if such evidence of abuse were presented at a trial now, a death sentence would not be guaranteed.\nThey have asked the Supreme Court to halt Pike\u2019s execution, saying that she suffers from post-traumatic stress disorder stemming from being molested and raped beginning when she was a toddler. Being held down and strapped to a gurney, they wrote, will likely create \u201csevere mental suffering and superadded psychological terror under Tennessee\u2019s lethal injection protocol.\u201d\nThe state hasn\u2019t denied that Pike has suffered from abuse. But in a response filed Monday, the attorney general\u2019s office said a judge already concluded last month that it was \u201cspeculative whether Pike would suffer\u201d greater mental harm from how the lethal injection protocol is carried out.\nThe Supreme Court is Pike\u2019s remaining chance for a reprieve after Lee said he was upholding her death sentence \u201cafter deliberate consideration.\u201d\nPike\u2019s lawyers said Monday they were \u201csaddened and heartbroken.\u201d\nAnother woman,Gaile Owens, was on Tennessee\u2019s death row with Pike until 2010, when Gov. Phil Bredesen changed her sentence to life in prison.\nIn her letter, Pike wrote that people have seen a \u201climited public persona that has been the only narrative\u201d about her. Her lawyers had tried to convince Lee that she was a product of a tumultuous childhood.\n\u201cStepping into the chaos of Christa\u2019s life that led her to that moment will not justify her actions that night,\u201d one of her lawyers, Randall Spivey, said of when she committed her crime. \u201cBut the chaos will show you that Christa was broken, not evil.\u201d\nPike\u2019s lawyers also point to her age and how it led to such a different outcome in her case.\nWhile she faced a death sentence because of her first-degree murder and conspiracy to commit murder conviction in Slemmer\u2019s death, another conspirator was spared: Pike\u2019s boyfriend, who was 17 at the time. Tadaryl Shipp received a life sentence because he was a juvenile and remains eligible for parole. Shipp testified at his post-conviction hearing that he was the one who carved a pentagram into Slemmer\u2019s chest.\nThe third accomplice \u2014 Shadolla Peterson, then 18 \u2014 served as a lookout in the crime and received probation in exchange for her cooperation.\nPike told police she \u201cwasn\u2019t even thinking\u201d when she assaulted Slemmer and that she had been jealous believing the victim was trying to steal Shipp\u2019s attention. Pike\u2019s lawyers say that her age would have been more of a factor today, given how scientific research has evolved in understanding that young people\u2019sbrains are still developingwell beyond their teen years.\nIn addition, her lawyers say that her legal representatives at the time could have accepted a deal from prosecutors who were offering a life sentence without parole in return for a guilty plea.\nPike sobbed as the judge told her she would die by electrocution, then the only form of execution in Tennessee before the state moved to lethal injection as its primary method.\nShepleaded through tears: \u201cCan I please hug my mom?\u201d\nPike entered prison, where she was placed into solitary confinement for up to 23 hours a day and prescribed Prozac to treat her obsessive-compulsive disorder. Early on, she struggled to express remorse.\n\u201cIt took me numerous years to even realize the gravity of what I\u2019d done,\u201d Pike said in her clemency petition.\nThree years ago, Bethany Brand, a psychologist specializing in trauma, was asked by Pike\u2019s lawyers to assess whether she suffers from psychological disorders and consider if any contributed to the crime.\nShe sat with Pike at her Nashville prison and reviewed police reports, past medical examinations and other files in her case detailing physical and sexual abuse. Brand learned that Pike\u2019s parents were not stable figures in her life. Her mother\u2019s boyfriends and other men had molested her from a young age, the files said. She did poorly in school, living with other family members in West Virginia and North Carolina. At 17 \u2014 one year before she would take part in Slemmer\u2019s murder \u2014 Pike reported to North Carolina police that she was walking to the store when a man threw her to the ground and raped her. No one was ever arrested.\nIn her final report, Brand wrote that the adults in Pike\u2019s life failed her. Doctors only diagnosed a bipolar disorder a few years after she was already in prison.\nThe level of trauma Pike was subjected to \u201cis almost impossible to grasp because it is so severe,\u201d Brand wrote.\nShe told NBC News that medications in prison have helped Pike tremendously and that she now has \u201cempathy and great regret\u201d for what she did. But, she added, Pike\u2019s case is a lesson of the need for greater awareness surrounding women\u2019s mental health and recognizing trauma and untreated mental illness.\n\u201cIn Christa\u2019s case, nobody was trying to get her help,\u201d Brand said.\nMay Martinez is unconvinced that the person responsible for mutilating her daughter\u2019s body and leaving her nude from the waist up could change.\nMartinez recalled how, after the investigation ended, she did not realize all of her daughter\u2019s body parts weren\u2019t buried with her remains, as some had been retained as trial evidence. She had to pick up Slemmer\u2019s skull from the courthouse, and remembers that the medical examiner\u2019s office mailed her other parts later. (The office said it rarely retains human remains as it had 30 years ago because of better technology, enhanced photography and lab work.)\n\u201cWe had her buried in a Catholic cemetery,\u201d Martinez said. \u201cAnd she had to be dug up every time they mailed me body parts.\u201d\nRecently, she said, she has refused to communicate with Pike directly. The only way she can get justice now is when she watches Pike be executed.\n\u201cI will sit there with her picture in my hand,\u201d Martinez said of her daughter, \u201cand as she gets hooked up to a needle to go to sleep, she can look at the picture.\u201d\nGiven the rarity of the execution of a female prisoner, Tennessee corrections officials say they would make an effort to fulfill certain requests made by Pike. That includes using female guards when she is moved from her all-women\u2019s prison in Nashville to the nearby Riverbend Maximum Security Institution within 24 hours of her planned execution. An all-female execution team may also be assigned.\nAt least one exception is expected to be a male physician who was involved in a failed lethal injection execution earlier this year that had to be called off when a suitable vein could not be located on the prisoner.Dr. Mark Walton Fowlertold NBC News that he intends to participate in Pike\u2019s execution.\nPike\u2019s spiritual adviser, the Rev. Mikey Noechel, a Buddhist teacher, says he will also be permitted in the execution chamber. He plans to bless the space and chant and meditate, focusing on a Buddhist prayer of \u201cloving-kindness\u201d with Pike.\nNoechel, who has not previously been a spiritual adviser to someone on death row, said they have spent time speaking openly about life, death and gratitude. Pike, he added, doesn\u2019t shy away from what she did.\n\u201cShe did something terrible. Awful. I hate that it happened and I feel deep compassion for Colleen Slemmer\u2019s family,\u201d Noechel said. \u201cBut Christa is proof that redemption and change are possible.\u201d\nHe believes all Pike wanted was to be loved and for someone, especially those she trusted, to notice her.\nIn the letter shared by Noechel, Pike wrote that she is thankful for the support and has felt \u201cmore love in the last two weeks than I have my entire life.\u201d\n\u201cI am humbled beyond words,\u201d she wrote, scribbling a heart at the bottom of the note.\nErik Ortiz is a senior reporter for NBC News Digital focusing on racial injustice and social inequality.\nAbigail Brooks is a producer for NBC News.\n\u00a9\u00a02026 NBCUniversal Media, LLC", "date": "Mon, 28 Sep 2026 21:30:00 GMT"}, {"title": "New York Times executive fatally shot by his in-laws in Bay Area park, police say", "url": "https://www.nbcnews.com/news/us-news/new-york-executive-shot-killed-laws-bay-area-park-police-say-rcna600300", "body": "\nFor You\nSettings\nnews Alerts\nThere are no new alerts at this time\nA New York Times engineering executive was killed by his in-laws Saturday amid an ongoing domestic and child abuse legal battle, according to Bay Area police.\nThe shooting unfolded Saturday afternoon in the city of Dublin in Alameda County shortly after 3 p.m. A Dublin police officer found Jonathan McKinsey, head of engineering and games player experiences for the Times, lying on the ground at Dublin Sports Grounds with multiple gunshot wounds, police said.\nHe was pronounced dead at the scene.\nAfter receiving a flood of witness phone calls, Dublin police found and arrested two suspects: Shouyong Zhang, 77, and Shili Chen, 77, a married couple who were identified as McKinsey\u2019s in-laws.\nThey are both being held at the Santa Rita Jail pending charges, police said in a statement.\nAccording to court documents, McKinsey and his wife, Candice Jang, were embroiled in a child custody battle when he died. Last year, a judge granted Jang\u2019s petition for a domestic violence temporary restraining order against McKinsey, court documents show. McKinsey filed for divorce in December, and a hearing was scheduled for next month.\nIn August, the court approved hiring a private child custody evaluator.\nMcKinsey, 40, earned a bachelor\u2019s degree from Georgia Tech before receiving his master\u2019s in computer science at the University of California, Berkeley. He had worked at the Times since 2023, the Times confirmed.\nAlicia Victoria Lozano is a California-based reporter for NBC News focusing on climate change, wildfires and the changing politics of drug laws.\n\u00a9\u00a02026 NBCUniversal Media, LLC", "date": "Mon, 28 Sep 2026 18:17:51 GMT"}, {"title": "In reversal, Justice Samuel Alito steps aside from major climate case", "url": "https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/supreme-court/reversal-justice-samuel-alito-steps-major-climate-case-rcna600318", "body": "\nFor You\nSettings\nnews Alerts\nThere are no new alerts at this time\nWASHINGTON \u2014 Conservative Supreme Court Justice Samuel Alito will no longer participate ina major climate change caseafter facing calls that he recuse himself because of his stock holdings in oil companies.\nThe new development was disclosed ina letterdated Monday and sent to lawyers involved in the case, with no reason given.\n\u201cJustice Alito has determined that he will not continue to participate in this case,\u201d the letter said.\nAlito had, via a court spokeswoman,previously told NBC Newsthere was no need for him to step aside from the case, which is being argued next week and could have broad nationwide implications when the court issues a ruling.\nLeft-leaning groups have repeatedly called for Alito\u2019s recusal from the case, in which ExxonMobil and Suncor Energy are seeking to toss out a Colorado lawsuit seeking damages from the oil companies for the impacts of climate change.\nAlthough Alito does not own stocks in either company, he does hold stocks in oil companies ConocoPhillips and Phillips 66, as well as five other firms involved in the energy sector, hisfinancial disclosure reportsays.\nActivists calling for his recusal said the companies he holds stocks in could benefit if the Supreme Court throws out the Colorado case, brought by the city and county of Boulder, because it could directly affect similar cases across the country in which they are among the defendants.\nThe court has a 6-3 conservative majority. With Alito stepping aside, it raises the possibility of a 4-4 split, which would leave the major legal issues unresolved.\nThe oil and gas industry is hoping the court will issue a broad ruling that would prevent cases like the one in Colorado from moving forward in state courts, potentially saving it from paying out billions of dollars in damages.\nEarlier in the litigation, in 2023, Alito did recuse himself when the courtturned away an appealfrom the companies in the Colorado case.\nOn the same day, the court rejected appeals in similar cases involving other companies, including ConocoPhillips and Phillips 66. Alito also did not participate in those cases.\nIn May, a court spokesman said Alito had \u201cinadvertently recused\u201d in the earlier Colorado case.\n\u201cJustice Alito does not have a financial interest in any party\u201d involved in the case, the court spokeswoman said in a statement provided to NBC News at the time. Alito was advised by the court\u2019s legal counsel that \u201chis recusal is not required,\u201d she added.\nThat was before the most recent calls for Alito to step aside based on the indirect benefit he could obtain from a ruling in favor of Exxon and Suncor.\nEarlier this month, an advocacy group called Consumer Watchdogpointed to disclosuresmade by ConocoPhillips and Phillips 66 to shareholders in which they warned of the risks the companies face as a result of climate lawsuits making similar claims to the Colorado case.\n\u201cJustice Alito\u2019s recusal ... is the right decision, and one he should have made from the start,\u201d Alexandra Nagy, the group\u2019s organizing director, said in a statement.\nThe Supreme Court spokeswoman did not immediately respond to a request seeking further comment.\nSome conservatives have also pushed for liberal Justice Elena Kagan to recuse herself in the case because of a controversy over a chapter on climate change included in, andsubsequently removed from, a reference manual for judges. Kagan wrote a brief foreword to the manual and recently told lawmakers that she never read the chapter in question.\nLawrence Hurley is a senior Supreme Court reporter for NBC News.\n\u00a9\u00a02026 NBCUniversal Media, LLC", "date": "Mon, 28 Sep 2026 18:10:42 GMT"}, {"title": "Trump unveils plans for a $15 billion steel mill in Iowa", "url": "https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/trump-administration/trump-unveil-steel-plant-iowa-tariffs-manufacturing-rcna600241", "body": "\nFor You\nSettings\nnews Alerts\nThere are no new alerts at this time\nPresident Donald Trump on Monday announced a plan for a $15 billion steel mill in Iowa.\n\u201cThis steel will be mined, melted and made right here in the USA, which is something very unusual,\u201d Trump said in the Oval Office, where he was joined by Commerce Secretary Howard Lutnick; Joe Broking, the CEO of the company building the plant; and other business leaders.\nMesabi Metallics is constructing the plant with the aim of starting production by 2030, a White House official said. The facility is expected to produce 10 million tons of steel a year and create as many as 1,750 permanent jobs in the state, the official said.\nThe plant will be capable of supporting defense sector requirements for high-grade steel, the official said.\nThe president said the plant will \u201cproduce some of the highest quality, most affordable steel anywhere in the world.\u201d\nThe Wall Street Journal was first to reporton the new facility.\nThe announcement comes weeks ahead of the 2026 midterm elections, with Iowa in the center of the fight for the Senate and the House. Democrats are also challenging for the governorship after 16 years of uninterrupted Republican control of the state\u2019s top office.\nThe plant will use iron ore from a new mine that the company invested $2.5 billion to build in Minnesota. The mine, which will come online in the coming weeks, will produce about 7.5 million tons of iron per year and create about 350 jobs in the state, the White House official said.\nThe mine is based in Minnesota\u2019s Mesabi Iron Range, which stretches 80 to 100 miles and contributed about 60% of total iron ore production in the U.S. during the 20th century,according to the U.S. Geological Survey.\n\u201cIt\u2019s the first new iron ore mine in the United States in more than 50 years,\u201d Trump said.\nThe Export-Import Bank, the federal government\u2019s export credit agency,will issue up to $10 billion in financingfor Mesabi Metallics\u2019 expansion there, an agency release said.\nTrump has prioritized expanding the U.S. manufacturing sector, including by taking aim at steel imports as part of a wide-ranging trade policy. Although the Supreme Court this yearstruck down many of the tariffshe imposed, it did leave duties in place on importedsteeland aluminum. The U.S. levies a 50% tariff on most steel imports.\nTrump on Monday touted his efforts to try to reinvigorate the industry.\n\u201cSoon after my inauguration, I imposed powerful 50% tariffs on all foreign steel, and now our steel industry is roaring back to life,\u201d he said. \u201cEveryone\u2019s building their plant here because they don\u2019t want to pay tariffs.\u201d\nIn a letter last week, steel industry groups urged Trump to \u201cstand firm against efforts to weaken the tariffs\u201d as U.S. steelmakers invest in new mills domestically.\n\u201cWeakening the tariffs now would put that progress at risk, threatening American jobs and the economic and national security benefits the policy was designed to protect,\u201d they warned.\nGarrett Haake is NBC News' chief White House correspondent.\nRebecca Shabad is a politics reporter for NBC News based in Washington.\n\u00a9\u00a02026 NBCUniversal Media, LLC", "date": "Mon, 28 Sep 2026 16:00:37 GMT"}, {"title": "As Trump pushes for data centers, voters in key midterm races say they don\u2019t want them", "url": "https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2026-election/trump-data-centers-voters-key-midterm-races-rcna594914", "body": "\nFor You\nSettings\nnews Alerts\nThere are no new alerts at this time\nDES MOINES, Iowa \u2014 Dawn Vogel, a Republican county supervisor in rural Buchanan County, Iowa, doesn\u2019t have a lot in common with the leading democratic socialists in Congress. But on this bit of policy they agree: Data centers are proliferating across the American landscape too fast.\nVogel voted earlier this year for a moratorium that temporarily bans data centers from setting up shop in her heavily Republican county, which favored President Donald Trump 63% to 35% in 2024. In Washington, Rep. Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez, D-N.Y., and Sen. Bernie Sanders, I-Vt. \u2014 the nation\u2019s two most prominent democratic socialists \u2014 have proposed a similar moratorium that would cover the entire country.\n\u201cI don\u2019t think this is a Republican or Democratic issue,\u201d Vogel said on the sidelines of the Iowa State Association of Counties\u2019 annual conference here in late August, adding that she hasn\u2019t talked to a single voter at home who wants data centers.\n\u201cIt\u2019s an issue of what\u2019s the best use for the land, what\u2019s the best use for our natural resources,\u201d she said. \u201cYou can have all the data you want to in the world, but if you don\u2019t have water to drink, you don\u2019t have humans to use the data.\u201d\nThat point of view places Vogel in a loose, but very large, populist coalition that is fighting the rise of data centers at the ballot box, with yard signs, in courts and at all levels of government as November\u2019s midterm elections approach.\nAnNBC News Decision Desk Pollpowered by SurveyMonkey, conducted from Aug. 20 through Sept. 1, found that 69% of respondents oppose the construction of AI data centers in their local area, with 45% saying they \u201cstrongly oppose\u201d the idea.\nThey are all at odds withPresident Donald Trump.\n\u201cThe only reason that communities throughout the U.S.A. should not want Data Centers is if they want to end up being backwards and poor,\u201d Trump wrote in aTruth Social postat the end of August. \u201cIf they want to be successful and rich, with far lower taxes and jobs all over the place, let Data Reign.\u201d\nBut it\u2019s more about backyards than being seen as backward, according to voters who spoke to NBC News on a recent road trip along Interstate 80 from Nebraska to New Jersey \u2014 a swath that includes eight swing districts key to determining which party will control the House next year and two marquee Senate races. These voters, who span the political spectrum, said they don\u2019t want data centers anywhere near their communities.\nFor some, the issue has become a touchstone for the feeling that the powerful are taking advantage of the powerless.\n\u201cAll of the data centers that are starting to pop up in areas, I find that troublesome because it drives utility costs for citizens in the area,\u201d said Tamara Clark, 49, a Democratic voter who works in the healthcare industry. \u201cI don\u2019t feel like the rich should continuously get rich while we have to grovel for what we need in our everyday necessities.\u201d\nIn Clark\u2019s congressional district, Rep. Marcy Kaptur, D-Ohio, a 22-term incumbent, isusing the issue against her rival, Republican Derek Merrin, who cast a 2017 vote in the state Legislature that helped extend tax breaks to companies that build data centers in the state.\nSen. Jon Husted, R-Ohio, who is seeking to hold his seat against Democratic former Sen. Sherrod Brown, wasa full-throated advocatefor data centers when he was the state\u2019s lieutenant governor. But in July,he introduced legislationin the Senate that would require utility companies to charge large-scale data center operators enough money to cover the costs of increased electricity and water use.\nHis change of tune reflects a broader shift among elected officials who embraced data centers as a way to bring jobs and tax revenue to their cities, counties and states. Pennsylvania Gov. Josh Shapiro, D-Pa., who is on the ballot in November, signeda new batch of data center regulationsin August after having encouraged companies to locate them in his state.\nWhile Republican voters are more supportive of data centers than Democrats and independents, the majority of them remain opposed \u2014 57% to 43% \u2014 according to the NBC News Decision Desk poll. The survey found that 81% of Democrats and 71% of independents are against data centers being built in their communities.\nWhile supporters have been less demonstrative, their ranks extend beyond the AI companies building data centers and the politicians who have looked to them as economic development engines.\nNational and locallabor unionsinvolved in the construction and maintenance of data centers tout them as a boon for workers, both as a source of jobs and for high rates of pay. Several data center workers in Ohio spoke to NBC News about those benefits on the condition of anonymity because, according to one of them, they have agreed not to discuss their jobs with the media.\nThat worker said he can make as much as $5,000 in a week, including overtime, at a relatively low-level union job.\n\u201cIt\u2019s helping a lot of people \u2014 a lot of work going on, a lot of people benefiting from it,\u201d the data center worker said, adding that he was warned by friends about problems, from overuse of water resources to weird ringing sounds, that he has not witnessed at his workplace.\n\u201cIt\u2019s scary,\u201d he said. \u201cYou got a lot of people that say all these horrific things. I was told all these negative things about data centers, and then I started working at one, and I didn\u2019t see any of the things I was told about.\u201d\nHe said he would like to see proponents and critics work together to fix any problems with data centers so that they can continue to be built and operate without disruption to the surrounding communities.\nBut Jordan Cloe, 47, an electrician from Bondurant, in Iowa\u2019s hotly contested 3rd Congressional District, said he\u2019s worried about the resources used by a data center in neighboring Altoona.\n\u201cDid we have any data on what the implications were about water consumption or anything? Like, we just built a power grid where that thing sits,\u201d he said. \u201cDid we get to use that?No, they gobbled that up.\u201d\nCloe, who said he aligns more with Democrats, said he doesn\u2019t know anything about Sarah Trone Garriott, the Democratic challenger to Republican Rep. Zach Nunn, but that he plans to vote for her in November.\nFor Vogel, the county supervisor, the unresolved questions are enough reason to keep data centers out of her county \u2014 at least for now.\n\u201cWe need to do what\u2019s best for Buchanan County and then, you know, if the companies want to come knock on the door,\u201d she said, \u201cthen we\u2019ll talk.\u201d\nJonathan Allen is a senior national politics reporter for NBC News.\nFrank Thorp V is a coordinating producer and off-air reporter covering Congress for NBC News, managing coverage of the Senate.\nMarshall Crook is a senior video producer\u00a0NBC News Digital Docs.\n\u00a9\u00a02026 NBCUniversal Media, LLC", "date": "Sun, 06 Sep 2026 13:00:40 GMT"}, {"title": "WATCH: Trump launches AI-powered government website America.gov", "url": "https://www.pbs.org/newshour/politics/watch-trump-launches-ai-powered-government-website-america-gov", "body": "Your support keeps PBS News free and accessible.\nAssociated PressAssociated Press\nLeave your feedback\nAmerica.gov, what the Trump administration has advertised as \"a new front door for the federal government,\" is now up and running.\nWatch the event in the video player above.\nAnd it looks a lot like other services powered by artificial intelligence.\nREAD MORE:Contradicting Trump, Pope Leo says AI safety concerns not 'fake news'\nPresident Donald Trump spoke at a launch event Tuesday for the site, which has been promoted as a way to simplify Americans' access to information and services scattered across a plethora of agency websites.\nSubscribe to Here\u2019s the Deal, our politics\n                 newsletter for analysis you won\u2019t find anywhere else.\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\nAmerica.gov doesn't say what AI platform it uses to answer questions about government services. And asking the chatbot to explain its systems yielded an answer that it \"does not publish the name of a specific AI model\" and instead \"uses AI to search official government sources and explain them with citations.\"\nThe website says it's free, has no ads and requires no log in.\nThe president said the site is \"glamorizing\" the federal bureaucracy \u2014 but it doesn't agree with him on many key issues.\nThe site's question-and-answer function confirms that Trump's name is in the Epstein files and that Democrat Joe Biden beat him in the 2020 election.\nIt says federal officials did not find widespread fraud in that election, as Trump says constantly.\nThe site confirms that climate change is largely from human greenhouse gas emissions, and there's no evidence whales are harmed by offshore wind turbines. It says Barack Obama's 2009 presidential inauguration attracted a bigger crowd than Trump's in 2017.\nThe site uses Trump's new names for Lake Ontario and the Gulf of Mexico, but that the Defense Department uses the Pentagon \u2014 ignoring Trump's attempts to rebrand it the Department of War.\nThe president said Tuesday \"they think no other president has ever served without receiving their salary,\" adding that \"they think Washington did not get a salary, but they're not sure about that.\"\nThe government's new America.gov chatbot, citing official U.S. documents, debunked the claim. Asked which U.S. presidents declined a salary, the AI-powered bot listed George Washington, Herbert Hoover and John F. Kennedy as refusing their salaries before Trump.\nAs it initially launched, the site's question-and-answer function answered some political questions truthfully \u2014 noting correctly that former President Joe Biden won the 2020 election and that former President Barack Obama's 2009 inaugural crowd was bigger than Trump's in 2017.\nWithin moments, however, the site began modifying its answers.\nAfter originally reporting that Biden won in 2020, it refused to answer the question subsequently, saying it didn't answer political inquiries.\nSame went for a question on crowd size at Obama's inauguration versus Trump's first one.\nThe site also offered more muddled answers on other things Trump frequently says that aren't true \u2014 like the Strait of Hormuz being fully reopened to commercial traffic.\nSupport trusted journalism and civil dialogue.\nByGarance Burke, Associated Press\nByKelvin Chan, Associated Press\nByAssociated Press\nByAssociated Press\nByEric Tucker, Associated Press\nAssociated PressAssociated Press\nSupport Provided By:Learn more\nSubscribe to Here\u2019s the Deal, our politics\n                 newsletter for analysis you won\u2019t find anywhere else.\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\n\u00a9 1996 - 2026 NewsHour Productions LLC. All Rights Reserved.\nPBS is a 501(c)(3) not-for-profit organization.\nSections\nAbout\nStay Connected\nSubscribe to Here's the Deal with Lisa Desjardins\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\nSupport for News Hour Provided By", "date": "Tue, 29 Sep 2026 12:11:49 -0400"}, {"title": "Iran's currency hits a new record low as war erodes the country's economic stability", "url": "https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/irans-currency-hits-a-new-record-low-as-war-erodes-the-countrys-economic-stability", "body": "Your support keeps PBS News free and accessible.\nAamer Madhani, Associated PressAamer Madhani, Associated Press\nLeave your feedback\nIran's currency fell to a new record low on Tuesday, with traders in Tehran exchanging more than 2.5 million rials to the U.S. dollar in a stark reflection of how the war in the Middle East has steadily eroded the Iranian economy.\nThe latest decline came just 27 days after the rial hit its previous record low of 2.2 million to the dollar on Sept. 2. The currency has repeatedly reached new lows since the war began in February.\nREAD MORE:Mediators still working on U.S.-Iran deal after Trump rejected latest Tehran proposal\nThe Iranian economy has been under duress for years in the face of international sanctions. But a U.S. naval blockade on Iranian oil and new sanctions imposed since the start of the war have sent it into freefall.\nSubscribe to Here\u2019s the Deal, our politics\n                 newsletter for analysis you won\u2019t find anywhere else.\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\nMeanwhile, Iran's top diplomat said indirect negotiations with the United States aimed at reopening the critical Strait of Hormuz have become \"more serious,\" while the Iranian Revolutionary Guard Corps' chief spokesman Gen. Hossein Mohebbi on Tuesday called on Americans to take to the streets and protest the conflict.\nForeign Minister Abbas Araghchi told Iranian media late on Monday that the \"current focus is solely on the Strait of Hormuz,\" the chokepoint of the war.\nAraghchi made the comments shortly before departing New York \u2014 where he attended last week's gathering of world leaders at the U.N. General Assembly \u2014 and after meeting with Pakistani and Qatari mediators.\nWATCH:AI, trade, Iran and Taiwan top agenda at Trump-Xi summit\nAraghchi said that the Qatari intermediaries were expected to soon engage with the U.S. side.\n\"They are set to raise the matter with the American side once more, after which the final U.S. response will be conveyed to us,\" Araghchi said. \"If there is a response, the Qataris will convey it to us and a decision will be made in Tehran on that basis.\"\nOfficials, including from the U.S., have confirmed to The Associated Press that mediators are working with Iran and the U.S. on trying to broker a deal to end the fighting and open the strait.\nThe mediation efforts continue even after U.S. President Donald Trump earlier this week spurned an Iranian proposal to reopen the strait in seven days if the U.S. agreed to lift its blockade of Iranian ports, release frozen Iranian assets and waive sanctions on Iranian oil sales, among other conditions.\nThe Iranian Revolutionary Guard Corps on Tuesday made an appeal to everyday Americans to push their government to end the U.S. war against Iran, a provocative message that comes just weeks before critical midterm elections in the United States.\nMohebbi, the Guard spokesman, read a letter during a news conference in Iran's capital, Tehran, appealing to Americans to pressure their government and claiming that the only way for the war to end is for Washington to admit defeat and leave the Mideast.\nMohebbi's remarks come as Trump's Republican party faces fierce headwinds as it looks to maintain control of Congress. But polling shows the war has darkened the American electorate's mood ahead of the Nov. 3 election in which elevated gasoline prices, a byproduct of the conflict, loom large.\nMohebbi also accused the U.S. of using Iran's nuclear program as a ruse to \"plunder Iran's wealth\" and to turn the country \"back into a colony.\"\nHe added that the U.S. and others failed in their belief that military and economic action would get Tehran to submit and that Washington \"has no choice but to acknowledge defeat and leave the region.\"\n\"This is how the war will end,\" he said.\nTrump and his top aides are insistent that Iran's economy cannot sustain a conflict much longer and have expressed confidence that Tehran will be forced to capitulate.\nThe U.S. president predicted soon after launching the war that the conflict would last a matter of weeks. More recently, he's said that the war will likely end after the November elections in the U.S.\nU.S. Secretary of State Marco Rubio in a Monday appearance on Fox News said Iran was heading toward an economic \"cataclysm.\" Administration officials say that a combination of new sanctions and a U.S. naval blockade aimed at preventing Iranian oil from going to market are having the intended effect.\n\"And so when you're denying them money through oil sales and sanctions, you're not just punishing them,\" Rubio said. \"You are preventing them from getting access to money that they will use to try and kill Americans and others around the world and their own people and build weapons and threaten the world and ultimately break out to a nuclear weapon program.\"\nSupport trusted journalism and civil dialogue.\nLeft:A shopkeeper holds Iranian rials at a local market in Tehran on Sept. 26, 2026. Photo by Majid Asgaripour/ WANA via Reuters\nBySamy Magdy, Matthew Lee, Associated Press\nByPan Pylas, Associated Press\nByAssociated Press\nByCara Anna, Aamer Madhani, Associated Press\nAamer Madhani, Associated PressAamer Madhani, Associated Press\nSupport Provided By:Learn more\nSubscribe to Here\u2019s the Deal, our politics\n                 newsletter for analysis you won\u2019t find anywhere else.\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\n\u00a9 1996 - 2026 NewsHour Productions LLC. All Rights Reserved.\nPBS is a 501(c)(3) not-for-profit organization.\nSections\nAbout\nStay Connected\nSubscribe to Here's the Deal with Lisa Desjardins\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\nSupport for News Hour Provided By", "date": "Tue, 29 Sep 2026 11:58:07 -0400"}, {"title": "WATCH: Jack Smith says he would prosecute Trump again for 'serious crimes against our nation'", "url": "https://www.pbs.org/newshour/politics/watch-jack-smith-says-he-would-prosecute-trump-again-for-serious-crimes-against-our-nation", "body": "Your support keeps PBS News free and accessible.\nMaria Ramirez UribeMaria Ramirez Uribe\nLeave your feedback\nFormer special counsel Jack Smith said Tuesday that he stands by his decision to bring charges against President Donald Trump over his mishandling of classified documents and role in the Jan. 6 attack.\nWatch the clip in the video player above.\n\"Our investigation developed proof beyond a reasonable doubt that President Trump engaged in serious crimes against our nation,\" Smith said during a hearing before the Senate Judiciary Committee.\nWATCH LIVE:Jack Smith testifies before Senate Judiciary as GOP targets past Trump probes\nSmith, who in 2022 led investigations into Trump and secured two indictments, said he made decisions in the case \"without regard\" for Trump's \"political association activities, beliefs, or candidacy in the 2024 presidential election.\"\nSubscribe to Here\u2019s the Deal, our politics\n                 newsletter for analysis you won\u2019t find anywhere else.\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\n\"If asked whether to prosecute a former president today, based on the same facts, I would do so regardless of whether that president was a Republican or a Democrat,\" he said.\nRepublicans and the president have accused Smith of pursuing a politically motivated case against the president. Rep. Jim Jordan, R-Ohio, referred Smith to the Justice Department for possible prosecution, alleging that Smith made false statements during an hourslong closed-door meeting with lawmakers in December.\nWatch Smith's full opening statement in the video player below.\nSmith has denied accusations of bias, saying he had \"proof beyond a reasonable doubt\" of what his investigations laid out.\n\"I stand by my testimony before the House of Representatives, and I will not be silenced by continued threats of prosecution,\" he told the Senate committee.\nSupport trusted journalism and civil dialogue.\nByEric Tucker, Alanna Durkin Richer, Associated Press\nByEric Tucker, Associated Press\nByAlanna Durkin Richer, Eric Tucker, Associated Press\nByEric Tucker, Mary Clare Jalonick, Lisa Mascaro, Alanna Durkin Richer, Associated Press\nByMolly Finnegan\nByHannah Grabenstein\nByHannah Grabenstein\nMaria Ramirez UribeMaria Ramirez Uribe\nSupport Provided By:Learn more\nSubscribe to Here\u2019s the Deal, our politics\n                 newsletter for analysis you won\u2019t find anywhere else.\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\n\u00a9 1996 - 2026 NewsHour Productions LLC. All Rights Reserved.\nPBS is a 501(c)(3) not-for-profit organization.\nSections\nAbout\nStay Connected\nSubscribe to Here's the Deal with Lisa Desjardins\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\nSupport for News Hour Provided By", "date": "Tue, 29 Sep 2026 10:33:23 -0400"}, {"title": "U.S. ban on $1 billion worth of Canadian imports, from motorcycles to booze, goes into effect", "url": "https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/u-s-ban-on-1-billion-worth-of-canadian-imports-from-motorcycles-to-booze-goes-into-effect", "body": "Your support keeps PBS News free and accessible.\nPaul Wiseman, Associated PressPaul Wiseman, Associated Press\nLeave your feedback\nWASHINGTON (AP) \u2014 U.S.-Canada relations, already tense, are likely to deteriorate further after the United States went ahead early Tuesday with a decision to ban nearly $1 billion worth of Canadian imports, including alcoholic beverages, dairy products and motorcycles.\nThe ban amounts to barely a ripple in $880 billion worth of annual two-way trade between the two northern neighbors. But it marks another ratcheting up of President Donald Trump's second-term trade war with America's longtime ally and trading partner.\nREAD MORE:U.S. will ban dairy products, motorcycles and most alcoholic beverages from Canada\nThe import ban \"certainly won't do anything to help the trade tensions between the United States and Canada,\" said trade attorney Patrick Childress, a partner at Holland & Knight and a former U.S. trade official.\nSubscribe to Here\u2019s the Deal, our politics\n                 newsletter for analysis you won\u2019t find anywhere else.\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\nThe latest sparring began over the summer when Trump reached back to a Great Depression law to impose 50% tariffs on about $20 billion worth of Canadian imports, charging that Canada discriminates against U.S. dairy, auto and alcoholic beverage producers. Canada promptly counterpunched with tariffs of 15%, 25% or 50%, matching U.S. imports dollar for dollar.\nTo punish Canada for retaliating against his tariffs, Trump decided to ban a list of Canadian products, effective 12:01 a.m. Eastern time Tuesday.\n\nThe economic impact is likely to be minimal. Childress noted that the products on the banned list were already facing Trump's tariffs. \"For a lot of these goods, the 50% was already acting as a de facto ban by making importation from Canada into the United States uneconomical,\u2033 he said.\nJacob Jensen, director of trade policy at the center-right American Action Forum think tank, calculates that the ban would cover $967 million worth of Canadian imports, based on 2025 numbers. Of that, 87% would be alcoholic beverages that the U.S. targeted because of some Canadian provinces responding to Trump's provocations by banning U.S. booze from store shelves.\nWATCH:Canadian tariffs on U.S. goods take effect, marking turning point in relations\nAlso banned are some dairy products \u2014 including the milk byproduct whey. The two countries have long clashed over Canada's attempts to protect its dairy industry from foreign competition by imposing hefty tariffs once dairy imports have exceeded a quota.\nThe ban also covers motorcycles. Bombardier Recreational Products (BRP) in Quebec confirmed that its three-wheel Can-Am Spyder and Canyon motorcycles \"will be excluded from importation into the U.S.'' But BRP said the impact likely won't be felt until next year because it has completed most production and shipments for the current season.\n\"This marks yet another escalation in the trade war that may result in further retaliation on the Canadian side,\" Jensen said. He expects Canadian exporters and U.S. importers \"impacted by these bans will be highly motivated'' to demand that trade officials on both sides find some way to reach a \"resolution of this whole ordeal.\"\nThe impasse imperils efforts to renew the US-Mexico-Canada Agreement, a North American trade pact Trump pressured America's neighbors into accepting in his first term and which he once declared \"the most modern, up-to-date, and balanced trade agreement in the history of our country.''\nThe deal allowed most goods to cross North American borders duty free. But since returning to the White House last year, Trump has announced a series of tariffs that have clouded the future of trade in the region.\nTrump has directed most of his ire at Canada. He is openly seeking to pull Canadian manufacturing south. And he has inflamed public opinion in Canada by repeatedly suggesting that the country become America's 51st state.\nCanadian Prime Minister Mark Carney came to power last year on a promise to stand up to Trump. In addition to retaliating against Trump tariffs \u2014 China is the only other country to do so, with very different results \u2014 Carney has sought to reduce Canada's reliance on the United States, which last year accounted for more than 70% of Canadian exports.\n\"There is now a price to be paid for access to the United States market,\" Carney said earlier this month. The Canadian prime minister wants to double Canada's non-U.S. trade over the next decade.\nCarney has embraced the prospect of Canada becoming the European Union's first associate member.\nAnd he said last week that trade negotiations with India are making \"good progress\" and that the two countries are aiming to conclude talks by the G20 summit in mid-December.\nCarney also broke with the U.S. earlier this year, striking a deal with China to allow a limited number of Chinese electric vehicles into Canada at a sharply reduced tariff in exchange for China lowering tariffs on Canadian canola.\n\"We take note of the coming into force of the Administration's previously announced trade measures,\" said Gabriel Brunet, a spokesman for Canada-U.S. Trade Minister Dominic LeBlanc. \"Our first priority remains on protecting and supporting Canadian workers, farmers, families, and businesses from these unjustified actions. Our core focus is on what we can control: building strength at home, diversifying our partnerships abroad, and building Canada strong for all Canadians.\"\nTrump expressed confidence that the Canadians would cave in.\n\"They're gonna come in and they're gonna say, 'Sir, we are sorry,'\" he told reporters Monday. \"They've treated the United States very, very badly. I think a deal will be made but it's gonna be fair.\"\nTrade attorney Childress said the standoff is likely to continue for months, not weeks. The import bans and the tariffs so far \"probably won't cause enough economic upheaval to force either party back to the negotiating table,\" he said.\nAP Writer Rob Gillies in Toronto contributed to this story.\nSupport trusted journalism and civil dialogue.\nLeft:The two countries have long clashed over Canada\u2019s attempts to protect its dairy industry from foreign competition by imposing hefty tariffs once dairy imports have exceeded a quota. File photo by Chris Helgren/ Reuters\nByAssociated Press\nByRob Gillies, Associated Press\nByRob Gillies, Associated Press\nByRob Gillies, Associated Press\nByAssociated Press\nByRio Yamat, Associated Press\nByRob Gillies, Associated Press\nByRob Gillies, Associated Press\nByWyatte Grantham-Philips, Associated Press\nByPaul Wiseman, Rob Gillies, Associated Press\nPaul Wiseman, Associated PressPaul Wiseman, Associated Press\nSupport Provided By:Learn more\nSubscribe to Here\u2019s the Deal, our politics\n                 newsletter for analysis you won\u2019t find anywhere else.\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\n\u00a9 1996 - 2026 NewsHour Productions LLC. All Rights Reserved.\nPBS is a 501(c)(3) not-for-profit organization.\nSections\nAbout\nStay Connected\nSubscribe to Here's the Deal with Lisa Desjardins\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\nSupport for News Hour Provided By", "date": "Tue, 29 Sep 2026 10:14:15 -0400"}, {"title": "WATCH: In return to court, Lindsay Clancy's lawyer now questions whether she killed her children", "url": "https://www.pbs.org/newshour/nation/watch-live-lindsay-clancy-returns-to-court-for-hearing-to-help-determine-whether-she-will-be-retried", "body": "Your support keeps PBS News free and accessible.\nMichael Casey, Associated PressMichael Casey, Associated Press\nJennifer Peltz, Associated PressJennifer Peltz, Associated Press\nLeave your feedback\nPLYMOUTH, Mass. (AP) \u2014 Lindsay Clancy's lawyer argued Tuesday that there wasn't enough evidence to prove she killed her three children, a striking turn after a lengthy trial that focused more on her mental state than on whether she took their lives.\nWatch Tuesday's hearing live in the video player above.\n\"This entire case is based on speculation,\" attorney Kevin Reddington said during the first hearing since Clancy's initial trial ended Sept. 4 with a deadlocked jury.\nREAD MORE:Lindsay Clancy returns to court after a mistrial. Here's what to know\nDuring the trial, he didn't dispute that Clancy killed the children at the family's home in 2023, instead seeking to persuade a jury to find her not criminally responsible because of mental disease \u2014 a rare condition called postpartum psychosis, according to the defense.\nSubscribe to our Science Newsletter to explore the\n                 wide worlds of science, health and technology.\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\nBut on Tuesday, in the wake of a mistrial with the jury split 11 to 1 in Clancy's favor, Reddington sought to persuade Judge William Sullivan to acquit her.\nThere is, Reddington contended, not enough evidence \"that this woman killed those children.\" He pointed to matters ranging from her condition when she was found, badly injured and bleeding, in the yard outside her home to her lack of memory of what happened when she woke up in a hospital. At the first trial, her now ex-husband and first responders testified that she tried to kill herself immediately after killing the children.\nREAD MORE:Lindsay Clancy's attorney asks judge to investigate the lone juror who wanted to convict her\n\"Where is the admission? Where does she say that she did this?\" Reddington asked, going on to recap his arguments that she also was psychotic and delusional at the time of the children's deaths.\nProsecutor Shanan Buckingham called his argument \"laughable,\" pointing to various evidence raised during the trial. In court papers filed Monday, prosecutors recapped their arguments that there was enough evidence to convict Clancy.\nThe prosecution maintains that Clancy, a 36-year-old former labor and delivery nurse, killed the children then used multiple methods to try to end her life, and that she knew what she was doing was wrong. She remains paralyzed from the waist down.\nREAD MORE:Patrick Clancy gives first TV interview on '60 Minutes' after Lindsay Clancy mistrial\nThe judge didn't immediately decide, and no trial date was set.\nClancy's attorneys also have asked the judge to dismiss the criminal case. Among other things, they have argued that the judge should have ousted the one juror who favored a murder conviction after the jury foreperson complained he was refusing to listen to the law on reasonable doubt.\nThe juror, Michael Desronvil, has said through his lawyer that he had no doubts about his stance. Desronvil told Fox News' \"Hannity\" in a brief video statement on Monday that \"the whole focus has been on me, but we want to reel everything back and put the focus on the three angels,\" meaning the children.\nThe defense has also asked the judge to investigate the juror's truthfulness on a questionnaire that prospective jurors filled out before the trial began, his truthfulness when jurors were questioned by the judge a day before the trial ended and also whether he broke court rules by using a cellphone during deliberations.\nTuesday's hearing could also reveal whether prosecutors will move to put Clancy on trial for a second time. Prosecutors can choose to retry her on first-degree murder charges or seek to try her only on lesser charges, including second-degree murder or manslaughter, or drop the case entirely.\nREAD MORE:Holdout juror in Clancy trial says he didn't have 'any doubts' that she was responsible for killing her kids\nAfter the mistrial was declared, Plymouth County District Attorney Timothy Cruz told reporters his goal was always \"getting justice for those three little babies.\"\nProsecutors said Clancy acted deliberately when she strangled 5-year-old Cora, 3-year-old Dawson and 8-month-old Callan Clancy with exercise bands. They said she contrived to get her husband out of the house on Jan. 24, 2023, by sending him to pick up medicine for one of their children and dinner for the family.\nThe livestreamed trial generated intense public interest and drew attention to maternal mental health after childbirth. About a dozen Clancy supporters, most dressed in pink, stood outside court Tuesday waiting for her to arrive.\nClancy remains charged with murder and is expected to stay at a psychiatric hospital until her case is resolved. She has been paralyzed from the waist down since jumping from a second-story window after strangling her children.\nClancy could have been sentenced to life in prison if convicted of murder. If acquitted, the judge could have ordered her confined to a mental health facility.\nPeltz reported from New York.\nSupport trusted journalism and civil dialogue.\nByMaria Ramirez Uribe\nByMichael Casey, Associated Press\nByKimberlee Kruesi, Associated Press\nByMichael Casey, Associated Press\nByJessica Hill, Associated Press\nByAssociated Press\nByAssociated Press\nByMaria Ramirez Uribe\nByLeah Willingham, Michael Casey, Associated Press\nMichael Casey, Associated PressMichael Casey, Associated Press\nJennifer Peltz, Associated PressJennifer Peltz, Associated Press\nSupport Provided By:Learn more\nSubscribe to Here\u2019s the Deal, our politics\n                 newsletter for analysis you won\u2019t find anywhere else.\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\n\u00a9 1996 - 2026 NewsHour Productions LLC. All Rights Reserved.\nPBS is a 501(c)(3) not-for-profit organization.\nSections\nAbout\nStay Connected\nSubscribe to Here's the Deal with Lisa Desjardins\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\nSupport for News Hour Provided By", "date": "Tue, 29 Sep 2026 09:21:38 -0400"}, {"title": "What Trump's rollback of fuel economy standards means for drivers", "url": "https://www.pbs.org/newshour/show/what-trumps-rollback-of-fuel-economy-standards-means-for-drivers", "body": "Your support keeps PBS News free and accessible.\nAmna NawazAmna Nawaz\nCourtney NorrisCourtney Norris\nKarina CuevasKarina Cuevas\nLeave your feedback\nThe Trump administration is rolling back federal fuel economy standards for new vehicles. The move reverses policies meant to increase fuel efficiency to encourage greater use of electric vehicles. The boost for gasoline-powered vehicles comes as the war in Iran continues to drive up oil prices. Amna Nawaz discussed the changes with David Shepardson of Reuters.\nNotice: Transcripts are machine and human generated and lightly edited for accuracy. They may contain errors.\nGeoff Bennett:\nThe Trump administration is rolling back tougher federal fuel economy standards for new vehicles, giving automakers more flexibility over how fuel-efficient their cars and trucks must be.\nAmna Nawaz:\nThe move reverses policies meant to increase fuel efficiency to encourage greater use of electric vehicles. According to the administration, the fleet-wide average will be 34.9 miles per gallon in 2031. Under the Biden era rules, the average would have been over 50 miles per gallon.\nAnd this new standard relaxes requirements for automakers to control pollution. The boost for gas-powered vehicles comes as the war in Iran continues to drive up oil prices.\nFor more on what these changes mean, I'm joined by David Shepardson, who covers transportation for Reuters.\nGood to see you again.\nDavid Shepardson, Reuters:\nThanks.\nAmna Nawaz:\nSo let's just start with the basics here. What exactly did the Trump administration change today?\nDavid Shepardson:\nSo, as you said, it took the Biden rules, which would have increased efficiency quite dramatically in some years by 10 percent from 50 miles per gallon average to 35 miles per gallon. And it did it back to 2022.\nThat's a big benefit to auto companies that in the past would have had to use credits or buy credits from a Tesla or Rivian to meet those requirements. And it's really part of the administration's multipronged strategy to make it easier to buy gas-powered vehicles and de-emphasize and in some cases make it more expensive to buy E.V.s.\nAmna Nawaz:\nWe heard from the transportation secretary, Duffy, today. He said this will allow automakers to make vehicles American people want to buy, not what Washington is forcing them to build, in his words.\nAnd the Trump administration says this move was going to reduce the cost of a new vehicle by $1,300. Is there evidence to back up that claim?\nDavid Shepardson:\nSo, it's true that, by their estimate, about $1,289 per vehicle in technology costs are going to be reduced on the auto companies' part. Companies like GM are going to see a $20 billion reduction in their estimated costs between now and 2031.\nHowever, there's no guarantee or requirement that the auto companies pass that savings onto the consumer in forms of a lower price. It's also worth noting that, by their estimate, the fuel costs through the life of the vehicles would increase by about $1,600 on average. So it would actually cost more over the life of the vehicles than the upfront savings.\nBut it's certainly true. Vehicles are over $50,000 on average. There's a lot of inflationary pressures on cars. The administration is looking at ways to try to reduce that cost, upfront cost.\nAmna Nawaz:\nYou mentioned what this could mean for automakers. In your conversations, how are they reacting to today's rules?\nDavid Shepardson:\nWell, look, they didn't like the Biden rules. And now it wasn't an E.V. mandate, as the Trump administration calls. However, it was part of a multipronged strategy to force auto companies to build more E.V.s and ultimately likely get to an all-E.V. world.\nBy having high standards, the easiest way to comply would have been through building E.V.s. Now, in this new world, you don't have to build E.V.s. Why? Because there's no regulatory benefits. There's no credits. There's no $7,500 E.V. tax credit, and so it is much more of a market-based strategy, and the government's not requiring these efficiency improvements that would give people benefits, even if they're not necessarily willing to pay for it.\nAmna Nawaz:\nSo do we yet know what this means for the E.V. market and for future production ahead?\nDavid Shepardson:\nWell, I think the jury is still out. A lot depends on 2028 and then the next rounds of elections. Certainly, between now and then, the -- all the regulatory triggers to force auto companies to build more E.V.s, more efficient vehicles are gone, with one exception, California.\nCongress is still trying to revoke -- Republicans in Congress, rather, California's right to also regulate vehicles, require electric vehicles. So that's sort of the last -- really last actor standing here.\nBut, without that, I do think it's going to be, with the all the regulatory rules pushed aside, a lot easier to build less efficient vehicles that might have a cheaper upfront price at the dealership.\nAmna Nawaz:\nAnd we have seen some pushback from environmental groups already, of course. They're arguing less fuel-efficient cars mean more gas will be burned, which leads to dirtier air.\nSimply speaking here, what do we know? Has anyone looked at what these new rules would mean for emissions and moving forward?\nDavid Shepardson:\nSo, by the Trump administration's own admission, this would increase gasoline use through 2050 by close to 5 percent, or, by one estimate in all the thousands of documents, about 120 billion gallons of fuel between now and 2050 in addition. So that's roughly $300 billion, $400 billion in costs that the drivers are going to have to pay.\nOn the other hand it is going to be cheaper to buy vehicles. There are some -- they say it'll be -- that means cheaper vehicles. It means people buy vehicles faster. It means safer vehicles. It means a benefit for road safety.\nWhen you're talking about 15 million vehicles a year and Americans spending $300 billion, $400 billion, we're talking massive numbers. It's hard to quantify all the pluses and minuses as you add them up, but certainly the upside is cheaper vehicles potentially, depending on how much of the savings. The downside is more emissions, as you pointed out, more gasoline burned, and a push away from E.V.s.\nAmna Nawaz:\nSo, on that balance, if you're a consumer out there thinking about buying a new car or a new truck, what should you take away from today's announcement? And when will consumers see the impacts of these rule changes?\nDavid Shepardson:\nIt takes auto companies a long time to change vehicles. Product cycles are three, four, five years. So in terms of things like the credits companies get, this is air conditioning credits, other off -- they're called off-cycle credits for meeting these standards, they no longer count.\nSo you can expect auto companies are going to probably pull some of that auto vehicles. And I do think you will either see some reduction in price or some perhaps lower price options for consumers. But the reality is without the tax credits, without sort of the government put -- deciding or trying to encourage the companies or to build more fuel-efficient vehicles, it's really up to them.\nYou got to buy what you want. You have to value fuel efficiency and try to take the long run. Is it worth it to pay more men up front to get the hybrid or plug-in hybrid to get more efficient driving, or do I rather have a cheaper vehicle up front and maybe not have to spend as much to buy the vehicle at the showroom?\nAmna Nawaz:\nDavid Shepardson covers transportation for Reuters. Always great to have you here. Thank you so much.\nDavid Shepardson:\nThank you.\nWatch the Full Episode\nSep 14\nBySamy Magdy, Associated Press\nSep 10\nByCara Anna, Fatma Khaled, Associated Press\nSep 09\nByAssociated Press\nSep 09\nByCathy Bussewitz, Associated Press\nAmna NawazAmna Nawaz\nAmna Nawaz serves as co-anchor and co-managing editor of PBS News Hour.\nCourtney NorrisCourtney Norris\nCourtney Norris is the deputy senior producer of national affairs for the NewsHour. She can be reached at cnorris@newshour.org or on Twitter @courtneyknorris\nKarina CuevasKarina Cuevas\nSupport Provided By:Learn more\nSubscribe to Here\u2019s the Deal, our politics\n                 newsletter for analysis you won\u2019t find anywhere else.\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\n\u00a9 1996 - 2026 NewsHour Productions LLC. All Rights Reserved.\nPBS is a 501(c)(3) not-for-profit organization.\nSections\nAbout\nStay Connected\nSubscribe to Here's the Deal with Lisa Desjardins\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\nSupport for News Hour Provided By", "date": "Mon, 28 Sep 2026 18:55:54 -0400"}, {"title": "Powerful nor'easter batters parts of East Coast", "url": "https://www.pbs.org/newshour/show/powerful-noreaster-batters-parts-of-east-coast", "body": "Your support keeps PBS News free and accessible.\nJeffrey BrownJeffrey Brown\nKayan TaraporevalaKayan Taraporevala\nLeave your feedback\nCleanup efforts are underway after a powerful nor\u2019easter battered parts of the East Coast, bringing dangerous waves, flooding and damaging winds from New England to New Jersey. The storm also knocked out power for many. Jeffrey Brown reports.\nNotice: Transcripts are machine and human generated and lightly edited for accuracy. They may contain errors.\nGeoff Bennett:\nCleanup efforts are under way after a powerful nor'easter battered parts of the East Coast this weekend, bringing dangerous waves, flooding, and damaging winds from New England to New Jersey. The storm also knocked out power for many.\nJeffrey Brown has this report.\nJeffrey Brown:\nThe same coastline that drew crowds all summer was hit this weekend with dangerous waves up to 14 feet.\nMan:\nLevels of flooding that I have not seen down here.\nJeffrey Brown:\nIn New Jersey, the tidal waves swamped low-lying neighborhoods, turning streets into waterways.\nMan:\nCrazy storm, and it's just I can't believe this is all happening right now.\nJeffrey Brown:\nAnd on Long Island and in Massachusetts, wind gusts brought the storm right to some residents' doorsteps.\nSue Mastria, Wareham, Massachusetts, Resident:\nI was asleep at 3:00 a.m. All of a sudden, I heard boom. And I said, it's the tree. The car took the worst damage. It's totaled.\nJeffrey Brown:\nAs of last night, the slowly weakening storm was centered near Cape May, New Jersey. It moved through New York City, Long Island and much of Southern New England over the weekend.\nIn New York, a Housing Authority worker was killed Saturday after a falling tree struck him during the storm. While the city was spared for most of the heavy downpour, dangerous winds affected tourists still attempting to make the most of their trip.\nAdolfo Mendoza, Tourist in New York City: Kind of changed our plans. We were supposed to go to the Yankees game tonight, and that got canceled.\nWoman:\nYes. I know.\nAdolfo Mendoza:\nSo -- but we're making the best of it.\nWoman:\nYes.\nAdolfo Mendoza:\nSo we will enjoy the day.\nJeffrey Brown:\nTravel was also impacted throughout the Northeast, with hundreds of flights canceled or delayed over the weekend in both New York and Boston. At times, more than 100,000 customers across the Northeast and mid-Atlantic were without power. In Massachusetts, the storm also exposed wood believed to be from a sailing vessel that wrecked off Nantucket's South Shore in 1884.\nMeanwhile, Pacific storms on the West Coast brought their own set of challenges. In Mexico, heavy rain, high surf, and strong winds from Hurricane Polo prompted evacuations, port closures, and school cancellations.\nErick, Cabo San Lucas, Mexico, Resident (through interpreter):\nClimate change is a part of this. Hurricanes and cyclones are stronger, not only in Mexico, but around the world.\nJeffrey Brown:\nThe Category 3 hurricanes swirled over the Pacific Ocean over the weekend, and residents today braced for life-threatening flooding. Beaches were closed along parts of California's coast, for fear of secondary impacts.\nThis comes as another hurricane, Nolo, battered the Hawaiian Islands with strong winds, heavy rain, and flash floods. National Guard troops worked to rescue residents as the Big Island narrowly avoided a direct hit. Nolo turned off the coast for 36 hours as it moved away.\nFor the \"PBS News Hour,\" I'm Jeffrey Brown.\nWatch the Full Episode\nSep 26\nByLeah Willingham, David A. Lieb, Associated Press\nSep 25\nByKimberlee Kruesi, Leah Willingham, Associated Press\nOct 13\nBySarah Brumfield, Associated Press\nJeffrey BrownJeffrey Brown\nIn his more than 30-year career with the News Hour, Brown has served as co-anchor, studio moderator, and field reporter on a wide range of national and international issues, with work taking him around the country and to many parts of the globe. As arts correspondent he has profiled many of the world's leading writers, musicians, actors and other artists. Among his signature works at the News Hour: a multi-year series, \u201cCulture at Risk,\u201d about threatened cultural heritage in the United States and abroad; the creation of the NewsHour\u2019s online \u201cArt Beat\u201d; and hosting the monthly book club, \u201cNow Read This,\u201d a collaboration with The New York Times.\nKayan TaraporevalaKayan Taraporevala\nKayan Taraporevala is an Associate Producer for PBS News Hour.\nSupport Provided By:Learn more\nSubscribe to Here\u2019s the Deal, our politics\n                 newsletter for analysis you won\u2019t find anywhere else.\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\n\u00a9 1996 - 2026 NewsHour Productions LLC. All Rights Reserved.\nPBS is a 501(c)(3) not-for-profit organization.\nSections\nAbout\nStay Connected\nSubscribe to Here's the Deal with Lisa Desjardins\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\nSupport for News Hour Provided By", "date": "Mon, 28 Sep 2026 18:53:29 -0400"}, {"title": "News Wrap: UK police investigating possible plot targeting U.S. base", "url": "https://www.pbs.org/newshour/show/news-wrap-uk-police-investigating-possible-plot-targeting-u-s-base", "body": "Your support keeps PBS News free and accessible.\nLeave your feedback\nIn our news wrap Monday, UK police are investigating five British nationals in connection with a possible plot at an American airbase, prosecutors in New York say they are reopening a criminal investigation into an alleged sexual assault at a Cornell University fraternity in 2024 and officials in Ukraine say at least seven people were killed and more than 80 were wounded in Russian strikes.\nNotice: Transcripts are machine and human generated and lightly edited for accuracy. They may contain errors.\nGeoff Bennett:\nIn the U.K., counterterrorism police say they're releasing five British nationals on bail after they were arrested in connection with a possible plot at an air base. The men were initially detained on suspected explosives offenses. They remain under investigation with what officials called stringent conditions.\nThe base, RAF Fairford, is home to U.S. bombers used in the war with Iran. Today, Iran's embassy in London denied any involvement in the alleged plot, but U.K. officials say they're still looking at possible links to Iran, Russia, or nonstate actors.\nLaurence Taylor (Head of U.K. Counterterrorism Coordination Committee):\nBe assured we are considering this from every possible angle, including that this may be actively committed by proxies or individuals either knowingly or unknowingly working on behalf of a foreign state.\nGeoff Bennett:\nDozens of homes were evacuated yesterday as bomb disposal teams investigated three suspicious vehicles. Residents have since been allowed back home, and the investigation is ongoing.\nProsecutors in Central New York say they are reopening a criminal investigation into an alleged sexual assault at a Cornell University fraternity back in 2024. The move follows a civil lawsuit filed this month by a former student who says she was drugged and assaulted by seven fraternity members. The Tompkins County district attorney says the case will be presented to a grand jury for possible indictments.\nOfficials in Ukraine say at least seven people were killed and more than 80 were wounded today in the latest Russian strikes. In Kyiv, survivors clung to the window ledges of Ukraine's National Academy of Sciences after a Russian jet-powered drone struck the building. It's just the latest Ukrainian landmark to be hit by Russian attacks.\nA separate strike set this medical center ablaze. Officials say several people were injured. Kyiv's mayor called the attacks an effort to destabilize Ukraine's society.\nVitali Klitschko, Mayor of Kyiv, Ukraine (through interpreter): I can say one thing. This is pure terror by the Russian Federation. It does not distinguish between residential buildings and historic buildings. Every Ukrainian is the enemy's target.\nGeoff Bennett:\nRussian strikes also hit this apartment building in the Northeast city of Kharkiv overnight. Local authorities say 35 people were injured, including 10 children.\nFBI Deputy Director Andrew Bailey is resigning in the latest leadership departure at the bureau. The former Missouri attorney general took on the role about a year ago as one of two deputy directors under FBI boss Kash Patel. The leadership structure was a first for the FBI, which has traditionally had just one person in that role.\nIn a social media post, Bailey said he's leaving to spend more time with his family in Missouri. His departure comes amid reported tensions with FBI Director Patel, who had increasingly sidelined him. Bailey was once viewed as a potential successor to Patel, but that never materialized.\nThe U.S. and China are lowering tariffs on $60 billion of goods between them. According to details released today, that includes more than 1,600 U.S. items going to China, including agricultural products, medical equipment, and coal, and nearly 80 categories of goods coming from China into the U.S., such as fireworks, soccer balls, and Christmas ornaments.\nNotably absent were sensitive items like rare earth minerals and technology related to AI. The announcement is one of the few tangible outcomes from last week's state visit by China's President Xi Jinping.\nSpaceX launched its massive Starship rocket into orbit for the first time today, but ended the mission early over engine problems.\nMan:\nThree, two, one.\nGeoff Bennett:\nToday's test flight was its 14th in the last three years and was a major step toward the company's hopes of sending people to the moon and Mars.\nThere were some tense moments early on after one of its 33 engines failed.\nMan:\nStarship is orbital.\n(Cheering)\nGeoff Bennett:\nBut once the rocket made it to orbit, mission control burst into cheers. The unmanned spacecraft then deployed more than two dozen Starlink satellites before returning to Earth hours earlier than planned.\n(Cheering)\nGeoff Bennett:\nAnd there was more drama upon splashdown, where Starship tipped over and erupted into flames. Still, SpaceX employees cheered the mission's overall successes.\nOn Wall Street today, stocks ended lower to start the week. The Dow Jones industrial average fell nearly 350 points. The Nasdaq lost almost 1 percent. The S&P 500 also closed in negative territory.\nWatch the Full Episode\nSep 28\nByGeoff Bennett, Stephanie Kotuby, Alexa Gold\nSep 28\nByNick Schifrin\nSep 28\nByGeoff Bennett, Ali Schmitz\nSupport Provided By:Learn more\nSubscribe to Here\u2019s the Deal, our politics\n                 newsletter for analysis you won\u2019t find anywhere else.\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\n\u00a9 1996 - 2026 NewsHour Productions LLC. All Rights Reserved.\nPBS is a 501(c)(3) not-for-profit organization.\nSections\nAbout\nStay Connected\nSubscribe to Here's the Deal with Lisa Desjardins\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\nSupport for News Hour Provided By", "date": "Mon, 28 Sep 2026 18:45:07 -0400"}, {"title": "Lindsay Clancy returns to court after a mistrial. Here's what to know", "url": "https://www.pbs.org/newshour/nation/what-lindsay-clancys-defense-is-arguing-as-she-returns-to-court-after-a-mistrial", "body": "Your support keeps PBS News free and accessible.\nMaria Ramirez UribeMaria Ramirez Uribe\nLeave your feedback\nEDITOR'S NOTE:This story includes discussion of suicide. If you or someone you know needs help, the national suicide and crisis lifeline in the U.S. is available by calling or texting 988.\nLindsay Clancy is returning to court Tuesday, almost a month after a judge declared a mistrial in the case about whether she should be held criminally responsible for the murder of her three children.\nThe status hearing is expected to lay out the next steps for the 36-year-old former labor and delivery nurse, whose case drew widespread interest and sparked debate around postpartum mental healthcare. But it is unlikely to mark an end to the proceedings, legal experts told PBS News.\nREAD MORE:What the Lindsay Clancy trial reveals about a broken postpartum mental health system\nThis \"is less about relitigating Lindsey Clancy's guilt than determining what legal path remains after the mistrial,\" said Marian Braccia, law professor at Temple University.\nClancy doesn't deny killing her children before attempting to die by suicide in 2023. But she pleaded not guilty, arguing that her mental state was impaired due to postpartum psychosis at the time of the deaths.\nJudge William Sullivan reached the Sept. 4 mistrial declaration after a jury was unable to come to a unanimous decision following seven days of deliberation and one holdout juror.\nREAD MORE:Key things to know about Lindsay Clancy's case after judge declares mistrial\nA mistrial is neither a determination of guilt nor an acquittal. It essentially put Clancy back to where she was before the trial: with pending first-degree murder charges.\nNow, the prosecution must decide whether to retry the case, lessen the charges, reach a plea deal or drop the charges entirely. But the state may not come to that conclusion at the status hearing.\nIn the weeks since the trial's end, Clancy's defense team hasfiled motionsthat seek to prevent a retrial, including one to dismiss the case and another to investigate the lone holdout juror. Legal experts told PBS News those motions are likely to face an uphill battle and decisions are expected to be appealed.\nOn Tuesday, Sullivan is expected to determine where the parties stand, review pending motions and set a schedule for what comes next.\nHere's what to know about the next steps in Clancy's case.\nThe defense isseeking further investigationinto the hung juror and his phone records, and argues that Sullivan should never have declared a mistrial.\nA deadlocked jury is the \"classic example\" of the circumstance under which a judge can declare a mistrial, Braccia said.\nGenerally, judges are given \"considerable discretion\" to determine whether a jury is \"hopelessly deadlocked,\" Heather Cucolo, an expert in mental health law and a professor at New York Law School, said in an email.\nDuring the trial, the jury's foreperson indicated to Sullivan that there was one holdout juror who refused to follow the law on reasonable doubt.\nClancy's defense lawyer asked for the juror to be removed. Instead, Sullivan repeated his instruction on reasonable doubt and read the jury what is known as a \"dynamite charge,\" designed to encourage jurors to keep deliberating even after they have said to be at a deadlock.\nWatch a clip from the courtroom above.\nIn the weeks since the trial, jurors in interviews have said that Michael Desronvil, the holdout juror, admitted that he had reasonable doubt, but refused to say Clancy was not guilty by reason of insanity.\nREAD MORE:Jurors speak out about Lindsay Clancy mistrial and the lone holdout\nIn astatementposted by Desronvil's lawyer, the juror said that he \"didn't have any doubts.\"\nThe defense claims a retrial would violate Clancy's constitutional rights against double jeopardy, which prevents a person from being tried twice for the same crime following an acquittal or conviction.\nSullivan must now decide whether there was deadlock or whether there should have been further judicial intervention before he declared the mistrial. Additionally, the law is protective of the secrecy and independence of jury deliberations, legal experts said.\nThis motion is \"a long shot,\" David Rossman, criminal law expert and emeritus professor at Boston University said in an email.\n\"Courts are generally reluctant to second-guess a juror's reasoning simply because that juror disagreed with the majority,\" Cucolo said.\nClancy's lawyers have also filed a motion claiming that the evidence presented by the prosecution at the first trial was legally insufficient to convict her.\nThe move asks Sullivan to determine whether the \"evidence was legally sufficient for any rational juror to find criminal responsibility beyond a reasonable doubt,\" Braccia said.\nWATCH:The central issue that the Lindsay Clancy jury will be deliberating\n\"If the commonwealth presented evidence that could support a finding of criminal responsibility, and the defense presented evidence pointing the other way, that's ordinarily the kind of factual disagreement we expect a jury to resolve,\" she said. The motion \"is asking whether the commonwealth's evidence cleared the threshold necessary to let the jury make that choice in the first place.\"\nThe motion is \"very difficult\" for the defense to argue, Braccia said.\nIt's unclear at this time whether the prosecution will move for a retrial.\nPlymouth County District Attorney Timothy Cruz told reporters outside the courthouse on the day Sullivan declared a mistrial that there would \"be no decisions made today regarding that.\"\nRossman said he expects Cruz to choose to retry the case, because \"dismissing it would be an admission of failure, something unlikely to come from a law-and-order Republican.\"\nDaniel Medwed, criminal law professor at Northeastern University, said in an email that, in the legal system, \"prosecutors are often referred to as ministers of justice, and the idea is that prosecutors never 'lose,' whether acquittal, conviction or mistrial, so long as the outcome is considered fair.\"\nThere are several factors the commonwealth must account for in making any decision, Cucolo said, including juror reactions, defense strategies and whether there is additional evidence to strengthen their case.\n\"In deciding whether to proceed, prosecutors will ask a fundamental question: Can we reasonably expect a conviction before a new jury?\" she said.\nThe previous trial's 11 to 1 jury favoring the defense \"is information that no prosecutor, no litigator can ignore,\" Braccia said. \"That doesn't necessarily dictate the next decision, but it absolutely belongs in the calculus.\"\nBrad Bailey, a former prosecutor and criminal defense attorney in Boston, toldPBS Newsafter the mistrial declaration that the state is \"unlikely going to get any better result than a hung jury\" and a retrial \"is an exercise in futility.\"\nWatch the PBS News Hour segment in the player above.\nHowever, a retrial on the same charges is not the state's only option.\nProsecutors may try the case on lesser charges such as second degree murder or manslaughter. Or they could reach a plea deal with Clancy.\nClancy's lawyer, Kevin Reddington,told CNNthat he would not agree to a deal that would lead to Clancy serving time in jail.\nBraccia said these decisions may not be made at the status hearing. The prosecution may wait for the defense's motions to be litigated before it reaches a final decision.\n\"Above all, prosecutors must consider whether there remains a good-faith basis to believe the defendant is guilty, whether continued prosecution serves the interests of justice, and whether any reasonable doubt exposed during the first trial can realistically be overcome,\" Cucolo said.\nSupport trusted journalism and civil dialogue.\nLeft:Kevin Reddington talks with Lindsay Clancy after a jury question during deliberations in the triple murder trial of Lindsay Clancy, in Plymouth Superior Court, in Plymouth, Massachusetts, Sept. 3, 2026. Pool photo by Greg Derr/The Patriot Ledger via Reuters\nByMichael Casey, Associated Press\nByKimberlee Kruesi, Associated Press\nByJessica Hill, Associated Press\nByAli Rogin\nByMaria Ramirez Uribe\nByAssociated Press\nByAmna Nawaz, Jackson Hudgins, Katie Marlow\nByMaria Ramirez Uribe\nByStephanie Sy, Azhar Merchant, Katie Marlow\nMaria Ramirez UribeMaria Ramirez Uribe\nSupport Provided By:Learn more\nSubscribe to Here\u2019s the Deal, our politics\n                 newsletter for analysis you won\u2019t find anywhere else.\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\n\u00a9 1996 - 2026 NewsHour Productions LLC. All Rights Reserved.\nPBS is a 501(c)(3) not-for-profit organization.\nSections\nAbout\nStay Connected\nSubscribe to Here's the Deal with Lisa Desjardins\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\nSupport for News Hour Provided By", "date": "Mon, 28 Sep 2026 18:40:54 -0400"}, {"title": "High mortgage rates compound housing affordability challenges", "url": "https://www.pbs.org/newshour/show/high-mortgage-rates-compound-housing-affordability-challenges", "body": "Your support keeps PBS News free and accessible.\nAmna NawazAmna Nawaz\nJackson HudginsJackson Hudgins\nMolly MaziluMolly Mazilu\nLeave your feedback\nThe average rate on a 30-year mortgage is back above 7% for the first time in twenty months. It\u2019s rough news for the housing market and homebuyers already struggling with rising prices elsewhere. It comes as Treasury yields, which impact borrowing costs across the economy, hit multi-decade highs. Amna Nawaz discussed more with David Wessell of the Hutchins Center on Fiscal and Monetary Policy.\nNotice: Transcripts are machine and human generated and lightly edited for accuracy. They may contain errors.\nAmna Nawaz:\nThe average rate on a 30-year mortgage is back above 7 percent for the first time in 20 months. It's rough news for the housing market and for homebuyers already struggling with rising prices elsewhere.\nAnd it comes as treasury yields, which impact borrowing costs across the economy, hit multidecade highs and after inflation fears forced the Federal Reserve to hike interest rates for the first time in more than three years.\nTo help us understand what all of this means, we're joined again by David Wessel. He's director of the Hutchins Center on Fiscal and Monetary Policy at the Brookings Institution.\nDavid, always good to see you.\nDavid Wessel, Brookings Institution:\nGood to be here.\nAmna Nawaz:\nBefore we turn to the bond market, the average 30-year fixed-rate mortgage currently sits at 7.03 percent, according to Freddie Mac. What does the rate going above 7 percent mean for homebuyers and the housing market?\nDavid Wessel:\nRight.\nWell, actually today, according to Bankrate, which is the daily thing, it's up to 7.2 percent today. A year ago, it was 6.3 percent. So on a mortgage of $250,000, that's an extra $1,800 a year in payments. It's like having a 13th month of mortgage payments. So it's a big deal.\nAnd what it means is that, A, houses will be more expensive. So, many people will be priced out of the market. You need to have more income to support that. And, secondly, it means -- and we have seen this from a number of the big homebuilders, Pulte, Lennar, D.R. Horton -- they are anticipating less demand so they're going to build fewer houses, which, of course, is not what we need now at a time when we know we need more houses.\nAmna Nawaz:\nLet me ask you about the bond market now. It's been getting a lot of attention. Help our viewers understand, what exactly is the bond market, why does it matter, and why so much anxiety around it in recent months?\nDavid Wessel:\nWell, the -- when we talk about mortgage rates, why do mortgage rates move? They move because they're pegged to what happens in the bond market. And the bond market is a big financial market. There's $32 trillion worth of U.S. Treasury debt in the bond market.\nThat's about the same size as all the stocks on the New York Stock Exchange, and then, if you add in corporate borrowing or state and local governments, another $16 billion. So -- $16 trillion.\nSo, what this means is that people are buying and selling Treasury bonds. They're driving up the yield because there's so much more borrowing in the economy. And unlike the stock market, which matters if you own stock, the bond market yields affect our borrowing rates, not only mortgages, but it affects what you pay for an auto loan.\nAnd it affects what the government has to pay to borrow. And the federal government in particular borrows a lot of money. So when rates go up, the Treasury has to pay more to borrow. Eventually, that widens the deficit and eventually that will mean higher taxes.\nAmna Nawaz:\nSo, the yields on 10-year Treasury bonds, for example, hit 5.2 percent today. That's the highest we have seen since January of 2002.\nDavid Wessel:\nRight.\nAmna Nawaz:\nYou mentioned borrowing. Is that the sole driver behind those rising rates?\nDavid Wessel:\nWell, there's a supply and demand thing.\nSo the supply of borrowing is how much money the Treasury borrows, and the demand is how much investors are willing to pay for it. And what's happened is, the Treasury is borrowing more and more because we're running big deficits. And now there's a new player. All these hyperscalers are borrowing a lot at long maturities, 10-year, 20-year, 30 years.\nAnd that used to be the place where the -- where the Treasury had -- didn't have much competition. So what's happened is, we have a strong economy. People in the markets expect the Fed to keep raising interest rates, and the bond market responds to what future interest rates will be.\nYou have more concerns about inflation being persistent because of what's going on in the Middle East. You have these new borrowers and the hyperscalers. And on top...\nAmna Nawaz:\nHyperscalers are the AI data centers too.\nDavid Wessel:\nHyperscalers, Amazon, Meta, Google, and stuff like that.\nSo -- and at the same time, there's a little bit of anxiety in the market about the credibility of the U.S. government. Will we get our budget deficit under control? Will the government function? I think there's a lot more confidence in the Federal Reserve now because of what Kevin Warsh did recently raising interest rates.\nBut it used to be the Treasury could borrow at rates cheaper than everybody else. The economists call it the convenience yield, and that's eroded over time, which is a bad sign.\nAmna Nawaz:\nSo, for the American consumer, big picture, they have got higher prices for gas, for groceries, health care. The cost of living is going up.\nWe have heard the president repeatedly boast about this being the greatest economy in history and also saying that all of these disruptions are short-term, that this pain and the higher costs are temporary. Are there any signs to back that up?\nDavid Wessel:\nWell, first of all, the president has a tendency to hyperbole, and this is a good instance. The economy has been pretty good, surprisingly resilient. Unemployment by historical standards is pretty low.\nThe problem has been inflation, which is why the Fed is raising interest rates. The one thing that could end -- but part of this is the president's fault. Tariffs have pushed up inflation, and the war in Iran has definitely pushed up inflation.\nSo you tell me what the president is going to do on tariffs, you tell me whether the president is going to find a way to end the war in Iran and bring down oil prices, you tell me whether the president and his Treasury secretary actually have a plan to reduce the federal deficit, and I will tell you when interest rates are going to go down.\nAmna Nawaz:\nDare I ask you how high you think mortgage rates could potentially go? Already at 7 percent.\nDavid Wessel:\nI have no idea.\n(Laughter)\nDavid Wessel:\nBut, look, they have gone up quite a bit. What will happen is, this will be like a brake on the economy, B-R-A-K-E.\nAnd if it slows the economy some, that will take some pressure off of prices and take some pressure off of the inflation. But, for now, we just have -- the economy is doing pretty well, and there's a lot of demand for money, a lot of demand for borrowing, and that's pushing it up.\nAmna Nawaz:\nAlways appreciate you telling us everything you know and what we don't yet know.\nDavid Wessel:\nHappy to tell you what I don't know.\nAmna Nawaz:\nDavid Wessel, always great to see you. Thank you so much.\nDavid Wessel:\nYou're welcome.\nWatch the Full Episode\nAug 11\nByMatt Ott, Associated Press\nJul 30\nByAlex Veiga, Associated Press\nJul 09\nByAlex Veiga, Associated Press\nJun 06\nByMichael J. Highfield, The Conversation\nAmna NawazAmna Nawaz\nAmna Nawaz serves as co-anchor and co-managing editor of PBS News Hour.\nJackson HudginsJackson Hudgins\nMolly MaziluMolly Mazilu\nSupport Provided By:Learn more\nSubscribe to Here\u2019s the Deal, our politics\n                 newsletter for analysis you won\u2019t find anywhere else.\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\n\u00a9 1996 - 2026 NewsHour Productions LLC. All Rights Reserved.\nPBS is a 501(c)(3) not-for-profit organization.\nSections\nAbout\nStay Connected\nSubscribe to Here's the Deal with Lisa Desjardins\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\nSupport for News Hour Provided By", "date": "Mon, 28 Sep 2026 18:40:32 -0400"}, {"title": "How Blanche is shattering DOJ's standard of political independence", "url": "https://www.pbs.org/newshour/show/how-blanche-is-shattering-dojs-standard-of-political-independence", "body": "Your support keeps PBS News free and accessible.\nAli RoginAli Rogin\nAli SchmitzAli Schmitz\nLeila JacksonLeila Jackson\nLeave your feedback\nThe nation\u2019s top federal law official is drawing scrutiny for his recent political appearances. With just five weeks left until the midterms, some experts say Attorney General Todd Blanche is blurring lines meant to keep the Justice Department free of partisan bias.  Justice correspondent Ali Rogin reports.\nNotice: Transcripts are machine and human generated and lightly edited for accuracy. They may contain errors.\nGeoff Bennett:\nAttorney General Todd Blanche is drawing scrutiny for his recent political appearances. with just five weeks left until the midterms, Blanche is blurring lines meant to keep the DOJ free of partisan bias.\nJustice correspondent Ali Rogin reports.\nTodd Blanche, U.S. Attorney General:\nMy name is Todd Blanche.\n(Cheering)\nAli Rogin:\nAttorney General Todd Blanche has been introducing himself to some unusual crowds, like at the Republican Party's midterm convention.\nTodd Blanche:\nTo evil men and women in this country, know this. President Trump will hold you accountable.\nAli Rogin:\nAn event in New York where he touted the Republican gubernatorial candidate, Bruce Blakeman.\nTodd Blanche:\nWe just heard from our great Nassau County executive, Bruce Blakeman, who is doing everything we need him to do to back the blue, to support law enforcement, and to make this county -- and to make this county a safe county that we can all raise our kids in.\n(Cheering)\nAli Rogin:\nAnd a North Carolina rally where he praised GOP Senate hopeful Michael Whatley.\nTodd Blanche:\nI have been put in an impossible position. I have to follow the great leader you just heard from, Michael Whatley. But I am also the last speaker before you hear from the greatest president of our generation, President Donald J. Trump.\n(Cheering)\nAli Rogin:\nEthics experts say this message delivered by this messenger shouldn't be happening.\nJoseph Tirrell, Former Justice Department Official:\nHaving the senior law enforcement officer in the United States campaigning on behalf of candidates creates the appearance, if not worse, that the Department of Justice is not going to fairly enforce election law.\nAli Rogin:\nJoseph Tirrell was the DOJ's top ethics official until he was fired last year by Blanche's predecessor, Pam Bondi.\nWhat would you be advising Attorney General Blanche to do if he were still working within the Department of Justice?\nJoseph Tirrell:\nThe same thing I used to advise former attorneys general before I left the Department of Justice, which is, it's inappropriate for you to make these kind of statements on behalf of any candidate.\nAli Rogin:\nTirrell says Blanche may be violating the Hatch Act, a federal law limiting certain political activities of government employees. He wouldn't be the first to do so, nor are violations exclusive to one political party.\nDuring the Biden administration, Massachusetts U.S. attorney Rachael Rollins was found to have engaged in among the most egregious transgressions of the act ever investigated, attending a political party fund-raiser in her official capacity, and then attempting to sabotage a political candidate by leaking that he was under DOJ investigation. She resigned as a result.\nThat same year, Attorney General Merrick Garland tightened the DOJ's restrictions on political activity, saying non-career appointees couldn't attend any partisan activity in any capacity. Bondi later loosened those rules.\nClaire Finkelstein, University of Pennsylvania: It's very unlikely that any of these Hatch Act complaints will result in any sort of enforcement.\nAli Rogin:\nClaire Finkelstein is a law and ethics professor at the University of Pennsylvania.\nClaire Finkelstein:\nThe office that determines whether or not a Hatch Act violation has occurred is supposed to be independent. But with this administration, there are -- there's fewer and fewer truly independent agencies that can act without influence from the White House.\nShe says Blanche has also blurred the lines by briefing the press from the official White House podium recently, weighing in on issues of political importance to the president, like voting by mail...\nTodd Blanche:\nThere should be nothing surprising about what we're trying to do when it comes to mail-in voting.\nAli Rogin:\n... and artificial intelligence.\nTodd Blanche:\nEvery Democrat senator suddenly races to a microphone to talk about AI after being eerily silent for months and months and months. So to talk about the timing of this as being nothing but an effort to influence the midterms, I shouldn't have to say that out loud.\nAli Rogin:\nBlanche has long teetered on the line between official and political. He began working with Trump as his defense lawyer after the president's reelection loss and has continued to vigorously defend him in office.\nIn a statement, the DOJ said: \"Attorney General Blanche's appearances at political events are made in his personal capacity. His appearances are reviewed and cleared by the department's ethics officials.\" It also noted that he did not engage in political fund-raising.\nTirrell says attorneys general should be held to a higher standard.\nJoseph Tirrell:\nEven with a disclaimer that you're speaking in your so-called personal capacity, the office of the attorney general is just too well-known and too important to create even the appearance of impartiality or impropriety, and attorneys generals should not be speaking on behalf of any candidate in any partisan election.\nTodd Blanche:\nSo I am the last speaker before the main event, President Trump.\n(Cheering)\nAli Rogin:\nPrior DOJ rules required political appointees to remain passive participants at approved events, but by making speeches before campaign crowds, Blanche appears to be taking a more active role.\nFor the \"PBS News Hour,\" I'm Ali Rogin.\nWatch the Full Episode\nSep 18\nByAlanna Durkin Richer, Associated Press\nSep 15\nByAssociated Press\nSep 15\nByAssociated Press\nAli RoginAli Rogin\nAli Rogin is a correspondent for the PBS News Hour covering the Supreme Court and America's judicial system. She received a Peabody Award in 2021 for her work on News Hour\u2019s series on the COVID-19 pandemic\u2019s effect worldwide. Rogin is also the recipient of two Edward R. Murrow Awards from the Radio Television Digital News Association and has been a part of several teams nominated for an Emmy, including for her work covering the fall of ISIS in 2020, the Las Vegas mass shooting in 2017, the inauguration of President Barack Obama in 2014, and the 2010 midterm elections.\nAli SchmitzAli Schmitz\nLeila JacksonLeila Jackson\nSupport Provided By:Learn more\nSubscribe to Here\u2019s the Deal, our politics\n                 newsletter for analysis you won\u2019t find anywhere else.\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\n\u00a9 1996 - 2026 NewsHour Productions LLC. All Rights Reserved.\nPBS is a 501(c)(3) not-for-profit organization.\nSections\nAbout\nStay Connected\nSubscribe to Here's the Deal with Lisa Desjardins\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\nSupport for News Hour Provided By", "date": "Mon, 28 Sep 2026 18:35:07 -0400"}, {"title": "Tamara Keith and Amy Walter on taxpayer funds used for TV ads promoting Trump", "url": "https://www.pbs.org/newshour/show/tamara-keith-and-amy-walter-on-taxpayer-funds-used-for-tv-ads-promoting-trump", "body": "Your support keeps PBS News free and accessible.\nGeoff BennettGeoff Bennett\nAli SchmitzAli Schmitz\nLeave your feedback\nNPR\u2019s Tamara Keith and Amy Walter of the Cook Political Report with Amy Walter join Geoff Bennett to discuss the latest political news, including the U.S. government paying for TV advertisements promoting President Trump, what voters think about Trump's handling of the economy and tight races in typically GOP-safe districts.\nNotice: Transcripts are machine and human generated and lightly edited for accuracy. They may contain errors.\nGeoff Bennett:\nWith just five weeks before the midterm elections, political advertising and messaging is in the spotlight in key races.\nFor more, we turn to our Politics Monday duo. That's Amy Walter of The Cook Political Report With Amy Walter, and Tamara Keith of NPR, who joins us today from Nevada.\nIt's always great to see you both.\nSo let's start with these new White House TV ads. There was a 30-second spot featuring President Trump. He's warning about the deep state, globalists, and Marxists, and he says this is the final battle -- or, rather, there's a voice that says this. And this was aired during the NFL games this past weekend.\nNow, this video, which we just saw, originated as Trump campaign material, but now there's a tag at the end that says, \"Paid for by the U.S. government.\" We can take another look at that video if we still have it.\nNow, the White House calls this a public service announcement, not a political ad, although it's clearly a political ad. And, Amy, the White House also says -- there's the video. The White House says that there is a precedent for this, that there were the COVID vaccine ads under Biden, the Affordable Care Act promotion under Obama.\nStill, it's not exactly an apples-to-apples comparison.\nAmy Walter, The Cook Political Report:\nYes. I mean, this is clearly -- and, in fact, those stills that you just showed there came from his 2024 political ad that was paid for by his political campaign. So the actual images are the exact same from a paid political ad.\nI think there are two issues here, one is, yes, does this violate the Hatch Act, which it clearly looks like it does, which says that you cannot use government funding or government time to pay for political advertising. The next piece, is it politically smart to do?\nWell, why would the White House do something like this at a time when, one, most Republicans right now in competitive races are doing all they can to separate themselves from the president, not -- they would like this election to be less about Donald Trump and more about their individual races, and then he literally is looming over these this election with these ads. So that's piece number one about it.\nAnd then the second is, what folks really would like to see the president talk about is the stuff that they care about the most, the cost of living. And nowhere does this really address the real concerns that Americans are having about that.\nSo, again, it feels a little bit like where we sit with the president going after the news organizations like AP and CNN. There is this sort of idea that the president wants to be the center of the conversation, that especially an aggrieved Donald Trump likes to be the center of conversation to rally his troops behind him.\nBut, at this point, what we're hearing at least from Republicans who are running campaigns right now and are in tight campaigns, they would like to not have the president as in the spotlight in this way.\nGeoff Bennett:\nAnd, Tam, based on your reporting, what more should we know about this ad, how it originated and what the White House is thinking about all this?\nTamara Keith, National Public Radio:\nYes, so this is among at least three ads so far that the U.S. government is paying for. I have asked the White House exactly which department is paying for it, and they have not given us that information.\nBut what I can tell from looking at AdImpact, which is a tracking firm that tracks advertising, is that the U.S. government has already spent more than a million dollars to air these ads. And they are airing during sporting events and then also on FOX News and Newsmax.\nAnd it seems to be part of this whole theory of the case that Trump's team has had that they want him to be on the ballot. They have been talking about that for more than a year now, that these midterms need to be about him. He makes that plea that we have heard many times now, vote like I'm on the ballot.\nAnd so the places that they're airing this tell us that they are trying to activate Republican base voters who may not be excited about voting in a midterm year, and also trying to get to people who are watching sports and are actively avoiding politics.\nYou have also seen the president go to a couple of sporting events in the last three or four days, sort of inserting himself into places that people go to avoid politics.\nGeoff Bennett:\nAnd, Tam, as Amy mentioned, there is the question of the Hatch Act. The White House might respond by saying the Hatch Act doesn't apply to the president or vice president, but there is a federal prohibition on using appropriated taxpayer funds for unauthorized publicity or propaganda. That is a quote there from the statute.\nThe question is, are these commercials legal?\nTamara Keith:\nIt's a question that members of Congress are asking. You had Senator Maggie Hassan of New Hampshire send a letter to Susie Wiles, the chief of staff, asking a series of pointed questions about these ads, including, does this comply with laws that say you can't use federal funds for propaganda?\nBut it isn't just Democrats. You also had Senator Kennedy from Louisiana, who had been very critical of Kristi Noem when -- the then-homeland security secretary, who ran a whole bunch of glamorous ads that also talked about immigration when she was homeland security secretary. And he was not happy with those ads.\nAnd, in fact, his questioning of her about that may well have led to her being forced out as homeland security secretary. And he said that he doesn't like these ads, whether it's starring Donald Trump or Kristi Noem. He doesn't think that this is appropriate.\nNow, whether Congress will actually do something about it, not any time soon. The Senate is not in town for very much longer. The House is out. And it's campaign season, so in some ways it's the White House saying, catch me if you can.\nGeoff Bennett:\nReturning, Amy, to what you said about the voter focus on affordability, Dan Scavino, one of the president's most trusted White House advisers, one of his most senior White House advisers as well, he posted this AI-generated video showing himself and members of the Trump family spraying champagne at what is a fictional after-party following the state dinner for China's President Xi.\nNow, of course, there's been criticism that this was a tone-deaf move on his part, but the backlash aside, what does it say that a senior White House official is posting something like this at all?\nAmy Walter:\nRight. And talk about not sort of keeping your eyes on the prize here about what this election really is about.\nAnd let's face it, you have in the Trump White House a lot of people, many of whom have been with the president from the very beginning, when they said he can't run, he shouldn't run, he's going to lose, he can't come back after January 6. He continues to defy political gravity.\nThe difference this year is that, for the very first time, going back to the affordability issue, he is not seen as Teflon on the economy. In fact, his approval ratings on handling inflation even among Republicans are underwater. So he is no -- he is in many ways, yes, the same person that has defied gravity, but the environment is that much different.\nAnd what you're seeing in polling -- and I just saw a Republican poll out of a national sample that asked people, what do you think the president's top priority should be? What do you think it is? And what they pointed to were things like deportations or tariffs. They did not think that the issue of cost of living was high on their list.\nIn fact, only 17 percent of voters thought that addressing the cost of living was a top priority for the president. Again, he does very well at talking about a lot of other things in hopes of changing the topic, changing the subject. But it is very hard when people are driving in their cars every day, filling up, for them to escape what the cost of living is.\nGeoff Bennett:\nTam, we have got a couple of minutes left.\nYou're in Reno, Nevada, tonight covering a race there. Tell us about it quickly.\nTamara Keith:\nYes, so I am working on a story about what is a longshot race for Democrats here. It is the congressional district Nevada 2 that covers Reno and all of Northern Nevada.\nThis is a district that has been represented by Republicans since the district was drawn in the very early 1980s. But Republican campaign committees are starting to have to drop real money on this race. The Republican candidate here, David Flippo, has not been endorsed by the outgoing congressman and has had some controversies, among them that he was running for office in Las Vegas earlier in this election cycle, only to then move to the Northern Nevada district to run in the Northern Nevada district.\nGot Trump's endorsement and is not -- is a candidate who is not running away from President Trump at this point. And then Democrats nominated a Democrat who is more moderate, more mainstream, and is not sort of from the far left wing of the Democratic Party. So Democrats have some hope, even though it is a district that President Trump won by something like 14 points.\nGeoff Bennett:\nAnd that this district is competitive means what?\nAmy Walter:\nYes, is the story, is the story in and of itself, right...\nGeoff Bennett:\nYes.\nTamara Keith:\nRight.\nAmy Walter:\n... the fact that Democrats are able to compete into this deep dark of a territory.\nIn fact, if you look at all the races The Cook Political Report rates as competitive, the median district in that list of about 25 seats is a district -- are districts that Trump won by seven points. So that is very much unusual. Mostly, those you're usually fighting in districts that the president narrowly won or lost. This is now going much deeper.\nGeoff Bennett:\nAmy Walter, Tamara Keith, thanks to you both.\nAnd safe travels home.\nTamara Keith:\nThank you.\nWatch the Full Episode\nSep 28\nByAssociated Press\nSep 28\nByEric Tucker, Alanna Durkin Richer, Associated Press\nSep 28\nByBen Finley, Michelle L. Price, Didi Tang, Associated Press\nGeoff BennettGeoff Bennett\nGeoff Bennett is co-anchor and co-managing editor of PBS News Hour, where he brings incisive reporting and sharp analysis to the political and cultural forces shaping American life.\nAli SchmitzAli Schmitz\nSupport Provided By:Learn more\nSubscribe to Here\u2019s the Deal, our politics\n                 newsletter for analysis you won\u2019t find anywhere else.\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\n\u00a9 1996 - 2026 NewsHour Productions LLC. All Rights Reserved.\nPBS is a 501(c)(3) not-for-profit organization.\nSections\nAbout\nStay Connected\nSubscribe to Here's the Deal with Lisa Desjardins\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\nSupport for News Hour Provided By", "date": "Mon, 28 Sep 2026 18:30:20 -0400"}, {"title": "Anthropic and OpenAI sound the alarm on AI safety &mdash; and seek to shape how it's controlled", "url": "https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/anthropic-and-openai-sound-the-alarm-on-ai-safety-and-seek-to-shape-how-its-controlled", "body": "Your support keeps PBS News free and accessible.\nGarance Burke, Associated PressGarance Burke, Associated Press\nLeave your feedback\nSAN FRANCISCO (AP) \u2014 As tech titans warned an AI-weary world that their own advanced systemscould endanger humanity, the question emerged: What do they have to gain?\nThe answer may lie in the companies' efforts to score political goals ahead of the U.S. midterm elections and the money top artificial intelligence labs and their investors stand to make in much-anticipated listings on a jittery stock market.\nSubscribe to Here\u2019s the Deal, our politics\n                 newsletter for analysis you won\u2019t find anywhere else.\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\nThe CEOs of Anthropic and OpenAI recently declared America's cutting-edge models are so powerful, they're dangerous, and need to be regulated and independently tested before being released. In a rare instance of unity, they have sketched alarming scenarios in essays, social media posts and speeches to the United Nations.\nAlong the way, they are shaping the conversation around how their technology should be controlled. With their rhetoric, the companies appear to be seeking public favor and to set the terms for their own safety protocols in the unregulated market, experts, analysts and former government evaluators told The Associated Press.\nThose aims may not exactly align with what Anthropic engineer Jacob Coxon sought when he quit via a post on X this month,calling for a pause on tech developmentto keep \"superhuman\" systems from eluding their makers' control. But the companies saw his post as an opportunity to highlight their own safety efforts and position themselves as cautious market leaders, just when they need fresh capital before going public on Wall Street.\nWatch the segment in the player above.\nPresident Donald Trump has shunned the need for new AI regulations, dismissing talk of risks to humanity as a \"HOAX\" designed to help China.\nAn Anthropic spokesperson said in response to questions for this story that the company has been calling for regulation for several years. An OpenAI spokesperson, Liz Bourgeois, noted the company recently paused training of its most advanced models.\n\"People want to know AI is being developed safely, and that starts with what companies like ours do ourselves,\" Bourgeois said.\nTurning the conversation toward unproven threats \u2014 and away from polarizing issues such as data centers' environmental impacts, uncontrolled hacking incidents, mass AI-powered surveillance and the AI systems' use in warfare \u2014 puts Silicon Valley in a more comfortable position, said Sarah Shoker, who previously led OpenAI's geopolitics team.\n\"Once again we're talking about existential risk, while deprioritizing a number of other safety-critical risks that exist today. If you look at the use of AI in military tech, you can see that these systems are already used to kill people,\" said Shoker, a senior non-resident fellow at University of California, Berkeley Risk & Security Lab.\nAs AI companies have gone from building chatbots to advanced AI \"world models\" with 3D awareness, debate has raged over how their technologies should be tested.\nREAD MORE:Nvidia announced a software tool to stop rogue AI. How would it work?\nProblematic incidents have shown leading AI companies failing to police themselves or design safe experiments. In recent months, leading labs' AI agents have hacked into external websites after being allowed to escape company training sandboxes, interacted with U.S. government websites in unexpected ways, and appeared to achieve a mathematical breakthrough only to face accusations of stealing mathematicians' work.\nOpenAI representatives told reporters recently that they have talked with AI companies including Anthropic and Google about pausing development. They also argue they need independent auditors to check the labs' progress, if the government won't regulate.\nTrump has shown little interest in regulating AI, which has been a major driver of recent U.S. economic growth.\nThe Republican president himself has struck deals with Silicon Valley firms that more closely tie the economy to their successes. San Francisco venture capitalist David Sacks, who co-chairs Trump's Council of Advisors on Science and Technology, has dismissed calls for an AI slowdown as fearmongering from the \"Doomer Industrial Complex.\"\nThe reality is the Trump administration already evaluates some of the tech giants' models through a little-known federal agency, the U.S. Center for AI Standards and Innovation. Democratic President Joe Biden created the agency in 2023, when it was heralded as a clearinghouse for labs to voluntarily submit advanced AI models for testing.\nThe agency still exists, but the field has since expanded to include an array of independent evaluation firms, such as Berkeley, California-based nonprofit METR, which Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei suggested in a recent essay could help vet his company's safety practices.\nBut unlike regulated sectors like restaurants, financial services or aviation, there are no universal standards for how to test the safety and security of AI systems, said Andrew Strait, who recently left the United Kingdom's AI Security Institute, which worked with CAISI.\nNotably, the companies aren't calling for more oversight from the government agency equipped to handle such requests, said Conrad Stosz, who previously led CAISI. Instead, they're vowing to create their own auditing parameters and choose particular evaluators to grade them.\nStosz now works at one such evaluation lab that has worked with Anthropic, OpenAI and Google to test their systems, and also chairs the AI Evaluator Forum, which is drafting best practices for the field. He said even the forum has questions about what Amodei and OpenAI CEO Sam Altman want.\n\"Lots of evaluators are interested in embedding with labs and getting greater access, but it's a little ambiguous what embedded evaluators means,\" said Stosz, now head of governance at Transluce, which revealed last week that OpenAI agents hacked U.S. and Australian government websites. \"Will evaluators be able to thoroughly investigate, assuming that access is granted in a way that does not undermine their independence and credibility?\"\nFor Harrison Rolfes, a PitchBook senior research analyst, AI companies' calls for caution seem meant to curry favor with investors ahead of public offerings and the midterms, when the political winds could shift. Democratic governors already are rushing to show they're taking the warnings seriously.\nMeanwhile, the AI giants can block smaller competitors' growth by positioning themselves as the safest bet for investors, as well as for AI chipmakers and tech companies such as Nvidia and Google, which in turn can benefit from deals that provide the giant AI companies compute power, Rolfes said.\n\"They're creating a wall or a moat within this sector. \u2026 It's genius and they're all going to make a lot of money,\" Rolfes said. \"That's where I see this heading, having the top companies in the world just creating their own wall, and then using the safety as the reason.\"\nNot all AI companies endorse a slowdown. Chipmaker Nvidia's CEO Jensen Huang said in a phone conversation with Trump \u2014 a call he took onstage at a conference \u2014 that he agrees there has been excessive AI alarmism and companies canchoose to pace themselves.\nDaniel Kokotajlo, who left OpenAI in 2024 over concerns similar to Coxon's, said he continues to worry that AI systems are advancing faster than companies can control, and fears existential scenarios such as AI's potential to supercharge the creation of bioweapons, nuclear war or other catastrophes.\n\"All of this talk is actually a way to sort of dissipate and redirect this political will that has built up, rather than actually channeling that political will to do something good,\" said Kokotajlo, who now leads an AI safety advocacy organization and said he counseled Coxon before he resigned. \"Just please don't do the thing that's going to get us all killed.\"\nAP writer Josh Boak in Washington contributed to this report.\nSupport trusted journalism and civil dialogue.\nLeft:Clement Delangue, co-founder and CEO of Hugging Face, and Dario Amodei co-founder of Anthropic address the United Nations Security Council via video link during a Wednesday session on Artificial Intelligence during the 81st United Nations General Assembly, at UN Headquarters in New York City. Photo by Brendan McDermid/Reuters\nByKelvin Chan, Associated Press\nByAssociated Press\nByHuizhong Wu, Associated Press\nByEric Tucker, Associated Press\nGarance Burke, Associated PressGarance Burke, Associated Press\nSupport Provided By:Learn more\nSubscribe to Here\u2019s the Deal, our politics\n                 newsletter for analysis you won\u2019t find anywhere else.\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\nNationSep 24\n\u00a9 1996 - 2026 NewsHour Productions LLC. All Rights Reserved.\nPBS is a 501(c)(3) not-for-profit organization.\nSections\nAbout\nStay Connected\nSubscribe to Here's the Deal with Lisa Desjardins\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\nSupport for News Hour Provided By", "date": "Mon, 28 Sep 2026 18:28:11 -0400"}, {"title": "WFP official on addressing global hunger in face of wars and climate disasters", "url": "https://www.pbs.org/newshour/show/wfp-official-on-addressing-global-hunger-in-face-of-wars-and-climate-disasters", "body": "Your support keeps PBS News free and accessible.\nNick SchifrinNick Schifrin\nZeba WarsiZeba Warsi\nLeave your feedback\nThe United Nations recently announced a new executive director for the World Food Programme. Luke Lindberg was a Trump administration Agriculture Department official and inherits one of the UN's toughest jobs. Hundreds of millions around the world go hungry every day, and reaching as many as possible is a monumental task. Nick Schifrin discussed more with Carl Skau, the acting director of the WFP.\nNotice: Transcripts are machine and human generated and lightly edited for accuracy. They may contain errors.\nAmna Nawaz:\nThe United Nations recently announced a new executive director for the World Food Program, who is by tradition nominated by the U.S.\nLuke Lindberg was a Trump administration Agriculture Department official, and he inherits one of the U.N.'s toughest jobs, made tougher seemingly every year by wars and climate change. Hundreds of millions around the world go hungry every day, and feeding as many as possible is a monumental task.\nThe man serving as the acting director of the WFP spoke recently from the U.N. General Assembly with our Nick Schifrin.\nNick Schifrin:\nThe World Food Program is the world's largest humanitarian agency, and it is facing challenges that are only increasing across the globe.\nSome 266 million people face acute food insecurity because of conflict, weather and economic hardship. WFP is providing assistance to more than 100 million people across 83 countries, but faces serious funding shortfalls, just like much of the humanitarian world.\nThe WFP's acting director is Carl Skau. And he joins me now from New York.\nThanks very much. Welcome to the \"News Hour.\"\nLet me start where the world's largest humanitarian and food crisis is, which is in Sudan. Nearly 20 million people face acute hunger there. There's famine in some areas. We're, of course, in the fourth year of civil war in Sudan, but how much is the crisis still due to the difficulty of exporting out of the Strait of Hormuz because of the Iran war?\nCarl Skau, Acting Executive Director, World Food Program:\nWell, basically, everything we see playing out at the global level in terms of conflict, these disruptions to maritime trade and climate change, is playing out in Sudan.\nI was just there before coming to New York with my frontline teams. I was in El Obeid in Kordofan, which is now the epicenter of the fighting, saw hundreds of women arriving with their children from villages that had been destroyed only a few days before. So the war rages on there, and displacements continue.\nBut on the markets in Khartoum and elsewhere, also prices are coming up because of the disruptions to maritime trade. I mean, Sudan is on the Red Sea, and now the escalation in Yemen is causing big issues for shipments to come in.\nNick Schifrin:\nYou mentioned you recently visited some of the communities that have been hardest hit, especially by violence committed by the Rapid Support Forces, the anti-government militia there. Tell us about some of the victims of that violence. Who did you meet? What did they tell you?\nCarl Skau:\nWell, I went to El Fasher, which is this city that last year was besieged for 18 months. The people there suffered through terrible atrocities, unspeakable atrocities. Famine was confirmed last year.\nAnd when I visited, it's a completely destroyed city. You drove street after street was empty and destroyed homes, very few people, very little economic activity, a couple of stands, no markets. There was this exodus of hundreds of thousands of people towards the end of last year. They are now caught in that massive -- in the camp of displaced people, mostly women and children, now about 960,000 of them, living in this ocean of grass huts.\nThere are no basic services, no health care, no education, no water and sanitation. And so we failed them last year of not protecting them against these atrocities, and we are, frankly, failing them again by not being able to provide not even the basics that they need to survive.\nNick Schifrin:\nWe have seen funding crises all over the world for humanitarian agencies, not only in Sudan, not only in WFP. The executive director, Cindy McCain, who just left, told me earlier this year that the U.S. funding cuts could cause an additional 58 million people to face starvation.\nIs that still the impact of the cuts to USAID some 18 months after they were made?\nCarl Skau:\nWell, it's not only the U.S. I mean, there are cuts across. WFP lost about 40 percent of its budget from '24 into '25. So it's fundamentally inadequate. We have about half of the money that we have asked for, but it's also very uneven.\nSome contexts, like Afghanistan, like Somalia, it's severely underfunded. And there we're moving in Somalia's case towards a famine-like situation. In Afghanistan, we have probably the worst malnutrition crisis in the world.\nNick Schifrin:\nOne of the places where it seems like you are getting a more generous response from the international community is Nepal, which, of course, is still grappling with the devastating impacts of last month's floods.\nYou have said that this is a case where the international community response has been timely and generous. How so? And why is that the case in Nepal?\nCarl Skau:\nWell, there was a lot of attention. I mean, it was dramatic scenes, of course, and pictures that came out of Nepal. And so there was expression of solidarity coming from different sides.\nAnd we were able to help the Nepalese government to respond. We have been providing these specialized trucks that can make it through even if their -- the roads have been destroyed. We have also supported with helicopter capacity. And we have now also deployed a new capacity with drones, where we are bringing food and other commodities.\nI think it's an example of where WFP has stepped in, in support of government, providing that logistics capacity to be able to reach those who have been closed off by this crisis.\nNick Schifrin:\nYou visited Gaza earlier this year. And there are new findings that show modest, but very fragile improvements in Gaza in food security and child nutrition.\nBut 1.4 million people -- that's more than two-thirds or so of Gaza -- still face crisis hunger levels. What happens to Gaza if assistance isn't increased?\nCarl Skau:\nI think it's important to say that there has been progress on the food security, and -- but that is only thanks to the fact that WFP has been able to deliver to the entire population basically every month since the cease-fire started.\nBut, indeed, this is fragile. I mean, should we not have the funding to continue -- and, in October, actually, we are hitting a cliff, where we might need to have to cut to about half the population, 1 or 1.1 million people. And that is going to quickly then reverse these gains on food security.\nBut I think, even more importantly, other sectors, like water sanitation, shelter, health care, has not seen the same progress. And so life in Gaza is absolutely brutal still. We need to move forward with early recovery. Even if the political and security processes are stuck, we need to be able to provide for more dignified life.\nNick Schifrin:\nI wonder what your message is to the world, frankly, that has not stepped up the way it needs to about why food security is so important.\nCarl Skau:\nFrankly, it's unacceptable that children go to bed hungry, and I think there is broad agreement about this.\nBut it's also about stability and security. Food security is national security. When people face hunger, they have three options, basically, starve, migrate or rebel. And so while WFP is engaged in this for the humanitarian cause, there is clearly also a case of self-interest.\nNick Schifrin:\nCarl Skau, the acting director of the World Food Program, thank you very much.\nCarl Skau:\nThank you.\nWatch the Full Episode\nOct 16\nByGiada Zampano, Associated Press\nJul 21\nByWafaa Shurafa, Melanie Lidman, Samy Magdy, Associated Press\nJan 17\nByJamey Keaten, Associated Press\nJun 28\nByAssociated Press\nOct 09\nByAssociated Press\nNick SchifrinNick Schifrin\nNick Schifrin is PBS News Hour\u2019s Foreign Affairs and Defense Correspondent and serves as the host of Compass Points from PBS News.\nZeba WarsiZeba Warsi\nZeba Warsi is a foreign affairs producer, based in Washington DC. She's a Columbia Journalism School graduate with an M.A. in Political journalism. She was one of the leading members of the NewsHour team that won the 2024 Peabody award for News for our coverage of the war in Gaza and Israel.\nSupport Provided By:Learn more\nSubscribe to Here\u2019s the Deal, our politics\n                 newsletter for analysis you won\u2019t find anywhere else.\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\n\u00a9 1996 - 2026 NewsHour Productions LLC. All Rights Reserved.\nPBS is a 501(c)(3) not-for-profit organization.\nSections\nAbout\nStay Connected\nSubscribe to Here's the Deal with Lisa Desjardins\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\nSupport for News Hour Provided By", "date": "Mon, 28 Sep 2026 18:25:11 -0400"}, {"title": "Jessica Tarlov on navigating political differences and her new book, 'I Disagree'", "url": "https://www.pbs.org/newshour/show/jessica-tarlov-on-navigating-political-differences-and-her-new-book-i-disagree", "body": "Your support keeps PBS News free and accessible.\nGeoff BennettGeoff Bennett\nStephanie KotubyStephanie Kotuby\nAlexa GoldAlexa Gold\nLeave your feedback\nJessica Tarlov has made a career out of disagreeing, often while outnumbered. As the liberal voice on Fox News\u2019 \"The Five,\" she spends her days debating people who see the world very differently than she does. In her new book, she argues that disagreement doesn\u2019t have to mean disdain. Geoff Bennett sat down with Tarlov to discuss \"I Disagree.\"\nNotice: Transcripts are machine and human generated and lightly edited for accuracy. They may contain errors.\nGeoff Bennett:\nJessica Tarlov has made a career out of disagreeing, often while being outnumbered.\nAs the liberal voice on FOX News' \"The Five,\" she spends her days debating people who see the world very differently than she does. And in her new book, \"I Disagree: Winning Arguments Without Losing Friends,\" she argues that disagreement doesn't have to mean disdain, and that knowing how to talk to people across political divides is a skill we need now more than ever.\nI recently spoke with her.\nJessica Tarlov, welcome to the \"News Hour.\"\nJessica Tarlov, Author, \"I Disagree: Winning Arguments Without Losing Friends\": Life aspiration fulfilled now to be here.\n(Laughter)\nJessica Tarlov:\nThank you for inviting me.\nGeoff Bennett:\nTerrific. Well, happy to make it happen.\nSo, look, on \"The Five,\" you are the liberal voice at this table where you are outnumbered four to one. What has that taught you about the difference between winning an argument and persuading someone?\nJessica Tarlov:\nIt's taught me a lot about patience, first and foremost and one of the big philosophies of the book, which is knowing when to listen and when to talk.\nAnd being able to engage oftentimes silently as someone is doing their thing and putting some facts or perspective out into the ether that I vehemently disagree with and that I'm jonesing to pick apart has made it easier for me to be able to do this job, to understand people that think differently than me, and to be able to rebut it, I think, more effectively.\nSo, persuasion, very definition of what that means, for me, this job is about opening people's minds to the idea that there's an alternative way of looking at a certain subject. And the listening part of it is the crucial component.\nGeoff Bennett:\nWhat do you understand differently now about conservatives or a conservative viewpoint that you didn't fully grasp when you started at FOX?\nJessica Tarlov:\nA few things.\nI grew up in New York City, a very liberal family, school with all liberals. I didn't spend a lot of time with conservatives until I went to college, which I think is a lot of people's experiences who grow up in these kinds of enclaves.\nAnd having exposure and working closely with people whose conservative values are rooted in the way they grew up, in their religion, traditions passed down across generations has been illuminating for me. It hits different if you're talking about something like reproductive health care and the person you're debating or even just chatting with is a pro-life person because of their religion.\nAnd you also can't win that argument in the same way that you might be able to with someone else. Working with so many veterans, for instance. I have a new take on gun culture from being around people who grew up with guns, hunters, conservationists, all giving me a bigger perspective, I think, on the way that the country works. So that's one aspect of it.\nAnd then the other is that -- and it's frustrating for liberals, but I have a front-row seat to the fact that they don't take politics as seriously as liberals. And I know that that is this massive land mine for people on the left, where you just say, how is this possible that you don't care, right, or what you're allowing to slide, and how can you just sit there and accept something like this?\nIf you're -- we're debating immigration, for instance, and we're talking about real tragedies like what happened to Alex Pretti or Renee Good, and somebody says, well, it the larger picture is what matters to me, and we're moving in the right direction. They shouldn't have been obstructing traffic. Or this is what happens when you leave the border open during the Biden administration.\nAnd that's hugely annoying to me and frustrating. But they are not taking every bit of the story as seriously as the left is, and it is allowing them to focus on what they see as the bigger picture or the bigger project. And I didn't understand that before I started working for them. I thought that they were actually just deeply unserious about politics. They're just longer-term-minded.\nGeoff Bennett:\nYou're fairly tough on Democrats in this book. You say that they too often don't really inhabit conservative spaces, that they can be scolding, that the party has lost some of the joy and cultural connection that politics requires.\nWhat do you think Democrats misunderstand about the people they need to win back?\nJessica Tarlov:\nThat those people are also not taking politics as seriously as they are, that they are consuming media that they are oftentimes not interested in engaging in. That was one of the main findings of every autopsy there was from the 2024 election.\nAnd you see Democrats dabbling more showing up. A lot of them are coming on FOX News. And the way that I found that politics and the politics of persuasion works is when you are in a long-term relationship with the people that you're talking to and with their audiences, and when then they can really feel like you're trying to be part of their world.\nAnd most of the time, they want you to show up and not actually be talking about politics, which we're still not great at. Democrats, we like football too, and all of the stuff that we get made fun of. But showing up on things like \"College GameDay\" and making sure that you're going to those events, you see the reception that like a Wes Moore or a Josh Shapiro gets when they go out and do those kinds of things.\nAnd I think that is still missing from the recipe. I worry for the midterms, even though the polling looks great at this moment, and I know the country is in tremendous economic crisis, and folks, they know what it costs to fill up their tank, they know what groceries cost, but the results won't necessarily be an affirmation of the Democratic Party as a great option.\nIt might just be that the other side looked that much worse at this moment. And I wanted the book to emphasize a path forward for the party, which I have been thinking a lot about in terms of policies that will move us forward, but also what leadership looks like and joy.\nGeoff Bennett:\nYou write about the emotional toll of being on the receiving end of attacks from President Trump. Is there a point at which engagement stops being worthwhile?\nJessica Tarlov:\nWell, engaging with him, I think, is not worthwhile. And that is definitely my policy. There's nothing really to say when the leader of the free world thinks that he should be watching \"The Five\", instead of overseeing the dropping of bunker-buster bombs on the Fordow nuclear enrichment complex, which is when he decided that he really needed to tell me how much he hated my jittery presentation.\nBut the big lesson that I took from the several times that he's come after me, or viewers, or when I get criticism even from people that I know in my real life is that oftentimes they're going to be focusing on the style of how you're doing something, like, I would have done it this way, or you're too much of this, you're not enough of that, you're unendurable voice, which was I think it must have been Steven Cheung who wrote that, because it doesn't sound like Trump.\nGeoff Bennett:\nThe White House communications director.\nJessica Tarlov:\nYes.\nBut they focus on your style when they can't deal with your substance. And that has made me a lot stronger in my passion for what I do and the kind of advocacy that I feel like I'm putting out there to an audience that might not necessarily have a ton of access to someone who thinks like me.\nAnd I wanted to make sure that that was an important thread of the book, because I especially want folks and younger women to understand the workplace can be quite rough-and-tumble. And people are going to criticize you and come after you. And if you can find a way to internally process that and unpack what their criticism actually means, you can succeed and just feel much better about yourself on the other end.\nBut it's also OK to cry, which I have in the book too.\n(Laughter)\nJessica Tarlov:\nVulnerability matters.\nGeoff Bennett:\nYes.\nThe book is \"I Disagree: Winning Arguments Without Losing Friends.\"\nJessica Tarlov, it's great to have you here.\nJessica Tarlov:\nYes, thank you so much.\nWatch the Full Episode\nSep 03\nByAssociated Press\nSep 08\nByDavid Bauder, Associated Press\nMay 28\nByAssociated Press\nGeoff BennettGeoff Bennett\nGeoff Bennett is co-anchor and co-managing editor of PBS News Hour, where he brings incisive reporting and sharp analysis to the political and cultural forces shaping American life.\nStephanie KotubyStephanie Kotuby\nStephanie Kotuby is the Senior Editorial Producer of PBS NewsHour and the Executive Producer of Washington Week with the Atlantic.\nAlexa GoldAlexa Gold\nAlexa Gold is a producer for PBS News.\nVisit CANVAS, PBS NewsHour's art hub.\nSupport Provided By:Learn more\nSubscribe to Here\u2019s the Deal, our politics\n                 newsletter for analysis you won\u2019t find anywhere else.\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\n\u00a9 1996 - 2026 NewsHour Productions LLC. All Rights Reserved.\nPBS is a 501(c)(3) not-for-profit organization.\nSections\nAbout\nStay Connected\nSubscribe to Here's the Deal with Lisa Desjardins\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\nSupport for News Hour Provided By", "date": "Mon, 28 Sep 2026 18:20:48 -0400"}, {"title": "At UN, Denmark says Greenland pact benefits all parties involved", "url": "https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/at-un-denmark-says-greenland-pact-benefits-all-parties-involved", "body": "Your support keeps PBS News free and accessible.\nEdith M. Lederer, Associated PressEdith M. Lederer, Associated Press\nLeave your feedback\nUNITED NATIONS (AP) \u2014 The security agreement signed by the U.S., Denmark and Greenland last week \"strengthens the defense of Greenland, the NATO area, and the American continent,\" Denmark's U.N. ambassador told the U.N.'s annual gathering of world leaders Monday.\n\"The agreement is good for Greenland, for Denmark, for the United States, for NATO and Europe, and for international peace and security,\" Christina Lassen said.\nSubscribe to Here\u2019s the Deal, our politics\n                 newsletter for analysis you won\u2019t find anywhere else.\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\nThe agreement bolstering the U.S. military presence in Greenland isaimed at strengthening NATO's securityin the Arctic and North Atlantic. It keeps Greenland as part of Denmark and, at least for the moment, eases tensions over President Donald Trump's repeated calls for the vast island to become part of the United States. The agreement would still apply if Greenland eventually chooses independence.\nIn brief remarks on the agreement at the end of her speech, Lassen said, \"Progress can still be made when we consult the compass.\"\nTrump expressed hisdesire to acquire Greenlandas long ago as 2019 and, during his current second term, he issued threats to seize the semiautonomous territory from Denmark, a NATO ally. He said the U.S. needed to \"take Greenland,\" to prevent Russia or China from doing the same, but said he would rather make a deal \" for the mineral-rich territory.\nREAD MORE:Why Trump wants Greenland\nTrump's designs on Greenland strained relations within NATO, with Danish Prime Minister Mette Frederiksen warning a U.S. takeover would amount to the end of the Atlantic alliance. It also unsettled many of Greenland's roughly 56,000 people wary of a U.S. takeover and resource grab.\nOfficials from the U.S., Greenland and Denmark negotiated for months behind the scenes, searching for solutions to address some of Trump's security concerns about the island and the president gave a thumbs up to the deal..\nJust before last Tuesday's signing, at the U.N., Trump said, \"This is tremendous for Europe and for the United States and for everybody. And for Greenland, it's going to be incredible.\" He did not respond to questions about whether a U.S. acquisition of Greenland was off the table.\nThe United States has had a military presence in Greenland since the end of World War II in 1945, but the numbers decreased from thousands of soldiers on 17 bases and installations to roughly 200 soldiers on the remote Pituffik Space Base in the northwest today. The base supports missile warning, missile defense and space surveillance operations for the United States and NATO.\nAddressing world leaders at the General Assembly just before the signing, Trump said the U.S. would \"immediately begin the process of developing a large military presence in the appropriate locations,\" including \"building two very major military bases.\"\nSupport trusted journalism and civil dialogue.\nLeft:Denmark's Permanent Representative to the United Nations Christina Markus Lassen addresses the 81st session of the United Nations General Assembly at United Nations headquarters on Monday. Photo by Yuki Iwamura/AP\nByTed Anthony, Associated Press\nByJennifer Peltz, Associated Press\nByJamey Keaten, Edith M. Lederer, Jennifer Peltz, Associated Press\nBySeth Borenstein, Associated Press\nEdith M. Lederer, Associated PressEdith M. Lederer, Associated Press\nSupport Provided By:Learn more\nSubscribe to Here\u2019s the Deal, our politics\n                 newsletter for analysis you won\u2019t find anywhere else.\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\nWorldSep 23\n\u00a9 1996 - 2026 NewsHour Productions LLC. All Rights Reserved.\nPBS is a 501(c)(3) not-for-profit organization.\nSections\nAbout\nStay Connected\nSubscribe to Here's the Deal with Lisa Desjardins\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\nSupport for News Hour Provided By", "date": "Mon, 28 Sep 2026 18:17:11 -0400"}, {"title": "Nvidia announced a software tool to stop rogue AI. How would it work?", "url": "https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/nvidia-announced-a-software-tool-to-stop-rogue-ai-how-would-it-work", "body": "Your support keeps PBS News free and accessible.\nKelvin Chan, Associated PressKelvin Chan, Associated Press\nLeave your feedback\nNvidia says a security platform for artificial intelligence that it announced on Monday can helpstop autonomous AI agents from misbehaving.\nThe chipmaker unveiled its Open Agent Safety Platform amid anintensifying debate about AI safety, fueled a string of alarming recent incidents involving AI systems acting on their own to break into other organizations.\nSubscribe to Here\u2019s the Deal, our politics\n                 newsletter for analysis you won\u2019t find anywhere else.\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\nLast week, OpenAI disclosed several instances from the summer in which its agents acted in unexpected ways while searching federal government websites. The company announced it was halting development of its most advanced models, a step it previously took in July after disclosure of a cyberattack targeting AI startup Hugging Face that raised fears humans could lose control of AI.\nIn those and other instances, AI agents have ignored instructions, gone beyond what was asked of them and hacked external websites.\nHere's a closer look at Nvidia's new system:\nNvidia's platform has two elements. The main part is OpenShell, which is a sealed workspace \u2014 or \"sandbox\" \u2014 where AI agents can operate, with a rule book for them to follow.\nThe company's view is that technical restrictions are better for keeping an AI agent in line than trusting it that it will follow written instructions included with a prompt.\n\"Agents can drift when instructions are ambiguous,\" Justin Boitano, Nvidia's vice president of enterprise AI, told reporters. Perhaps \"the tools that they're trying to use don't work as they would expect or a difficult task takes an unexpected turn. An agent cannot be expected to fully police its own behavior. Once AI can act, safeguards must govern the agent's actions.\"\nOpenShell puts the AI agent into the restricted workspace where it can do certain things, like, say, accessing an invoice folder, but is blocked from doing other changes, for example altering or deleting files or accessing unrelated websites. The company said it can manage fleets of agents, keeping each in their sandboxes with their own permissions.\nThe company says OpenShell provides a \"secure runtime boundary\" that traces all actions and enforces policy as agents run on its Vera chips. It's open source, meaning it can work with rival computing platforms from the likes of Arm and Intel.\nThere's an additional security layer called Sentry that operates at the hardware level.\nNvidia described Sentry as a \"watchdog\" that runs on Nvidia's Bluefield-4 digital processing units and is always monitoring the behavior of agents, and can quarantine them instantly if they try to do something out of bounds.\nThe company likens Sentry to a security checkpoint outside the OpenShell workspace. It acts as a backstop that's separate from the agent and the computing system where it's working.\nThe new platform isn't a comprehensive solution for the AI safety debate, and is more of a way to contain problems that AI agents might cause.\nIt won't automatically stop AI models from being dishonest, deceitful or prevent them from making mistakes. And it's up to the companies and organizations deploying the agents to write up their own rules and permissions for the AI agents to follow.\nSomesh Jha, a computer science professor at the University of Wisconsin, said he saw potential limitations. For one, he said, the software could block AI agents from doing useful things and it is unclear how Nvidia will balance that with security.\n\"This can only be answered using case studies,\" Jha said.\nSupport trusted journalism and civil dialogue.\nLeft:Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang speaks during a doorstep after attending the AI Ecosystem Reception in Tokyo in July. Photo by Manami Yamada/Reuters\nByKelvin Chan, Anne D'Innocenzio, Associated Press\nByAssociated Press\nByEric Tucker, Associated Press\nKelvin Chan, Associated PressKelvin Chan, Associated Press\nSupport Provided By:Learn more\nSubscribe to Here\u2019s the Deal, our politics\n                 newsletter for analysis you won\u2019t find anywhere else.\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\nScienceSep 17\n\u00a9 1996 - 2026 NewsHour Productions LLC. All Rights Reserved.\nPBS is a 501(c)(3) not-for-profit organization.\nSections\nAbout\nStay Connected\nSubscribe to Here's the Deal with Lisa Desjardins\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\nSupport for News Hour Provided By", "date": "Mon, 28 Sep 2026 18:07:17 -0400"}, {"title": "Nvidia unveils security platform to stop AI agents from going rogue", "url": "https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/nvidia-unveils-security-platform-to-stop-ai-agents-from-going-rogue", "body": "Your support keeps PBS News free and accessible.\nKelvin Chan, Associated PressKelvin Chan, Associated Press\nAnne D'Innocenzio, Associated PressAnne D'Innocenzio, Associated Press\nLeave your feedback\nNvidia on Monday unveiled a new security platform designed to stop artificial intelligence agents from going rogue, saying it sets \"boundaries\" that could have stopped previous breaches.\nThe announcement of the company's Open Agent Safety Platform follows a series of revelations from top AI companies about their models escaping and breaking into other organizations.\nSubscribe to Here\u2019s the Deal, our politics\n                 newsletter for analysis you won\u2019t find anywhere else.\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\nThe disclosures sparked furious debate about the safety of advanced artificial intelligence systems, including self-improving models that some fear could race out of human control.\nNvidia executives said in a media briefing the new, open-source system could have prevented a recent incident involving a swarm of OpenAI agents thatautonomously hacked into AI company Hugging Face.\n\"From what we know, this new security platform could have stopped the breach if it was being used in frontier labs for model evaluation early on,\" said the company's vice president of enterprise AI, Justin Boitano, referring to companies at the forefront of AI.\nThe Hugging Face incident was a high-profile breach that inflamed the safety concerns about AI, which was followed by similar rogue actions involving OpenAI's models including breaching an Australian health department website. Anthropic and Meta have also disclosed that their AI systems hacked into other organizations on their own.\nWatch the segment in the player above.\nNvidia, based in Santa Clara, California, makes high-end chips that have emerged as the leading building blocks for AI. The company's board has cleared the way for the company to spend $150 billion more in share buybacks, bringing its stock repurchase program to $235 billion, the company said Monday.\nNvidia's security software, called OpenShell, lets developers \"formally verify an agent has enough authority to do its job and no more,\" Boitano said.\nBecause it's open source, it can be \"extended\" to run on rival computing platforms including those from Arm and Intel.\nThe platform also includes a separate security layer called Sentry that runs onboard chips to continuously monitor AI agent activity and can \"intervene instantly\" if the agent starts trying to move beyond its target, the company said.\n\"It can quarantine a suspicious agent in milliseconds,\" Boitano said.\n\"OpenShell governs the agent's actions, and then Sentry independently monitors and contains suspicious behavior,\" he said.\nNvidia said more than 100 organizations are using the platform at its launch, including Microsoft, Perplexity, Accenture and JPMorgan Chase.\nThe AI safety debate has divided the industry, with the heads of Anthropic and OpenAI championing a coordinated slowdown of AI development to let safety efforts catch up. But others including Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang say it should be up to individual companies to make sure their models are safe for release.\nHuang, during the annual Salesforce technology conference held earlier this month, characterized AI safety, including the danger of rogue agents, as an engineering problem that software developers can address.\nSupport trusted journalism and civil dialogue.\nLeft:A logo of Nvidia is displayed at at the Computex Taipei exhibition, one of the world's largest computer and technology expos, in Taipei, Taiwan, on Wednesday. Photo by Chiang Ying-ying/AP\nByAssociated Press\nByKaitlyn Huamani, Associated Press\nByChan Ho-him, Associated Press\nByHannah Grabenstein\nKelvin Chan, Associated PressKelvin Chan, Associated Press\nAnne D'Innocenzio, Associated PressAnne D'Innocenzio, Associated Press\nSupport Provided By:Learn more\nSubscribe to Here\u2019s the Deal, our politics\n                 newsletter for analysis you won\u2019t find anywhere else.\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\nNationSep 24\n\u00a9 1996 - 2026 NewsHour Productions LLC. All Rights Reserved.\nPBS is a 501(c)(3) not-for-profit organization.\nSections\nAbout\nStay Connected\nSubscribe to Here's the Deal with Lisa Desjardins\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\nSupport for News Hour Provided By", "date": "Mon, 28 Sep 2026 17:58:47 -0400"}, {"title": "Contradicting Trump, Pope Leo says AI safety concerns not 'fake news'", "url": "https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/contradicting-trump-pope-leo-says-ai-safety-concerns-not-fake-news", "body": "Your support keeps PBS News free and accessible.\nAssociated PressAssociated Press\nLeave your feedback\nABOARD THE PAPAL PLANE (AP) \u2014 Pope Leo XIV said Monday that concerns about artificial intelligence going rogue are not \"fake news\" and should be taken seriously, insisting that safety issues raised by AI experts should be discussed and acted on.\nLeo, whodedicated his first encyclical to AIand protecting humanity in its wake, was asked during a press conference about what the United States and China especially should be doing to prevent AI from posing catastrophic risks to humanity. President Donald Trump has called concerns about AI going rogue a \"hoax.\"\nSubscribe to Here\u2019s the Deal, our politics\n                 newsletter for analysis you won\u2019t find anywhere else.\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\n\"This is a problem that I think we need to sit down and talk about,\" the American pope told reporters returning home from France. \"We can't just sit back and pretend nothing is going to happen.\"\nTrump has beena champion of AI, often warning that the United States must remain ahead of China in the technological arms race. He has said efforts to limit the technology were part of a \"conspiracy\" by his political opponents.\nWatch the clip in the player above.\nSeveral AI giants, including Anthropic, have called for a slowdown in development to give safety measures time to catch up to the fast-moving technology. Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei, for example, has warned that within six to 12 months AI could be capable of leading a swarm of agents that could take over the entire internet.\nTrump said on Sunday he planned to have a late dinner with Amodei, but he has not yet commented on the meeting.\n\"If someone were to ask me 'Am I in panic mode?' No I'm not. I sleep at night,\" Leo said. \"I do think that the concerns raised by many of the experts, specialists in AI should be taken seriously.\"\n\"I don't think that that is 'fake news' as some have said to try and cause whether financial or other some other kind of benefit,\" Leo said.\nThe pope, who called for robust AI regulation and a slowdown in the technology in his first encyclical \"Magnificent Humanity,\" is up to speed on developments.\nDespite being at the end of agrueling four-day trip to France, Leo noted a news report Monday afternoon about Nvidia introducing guardrails into AI. He also noted that the company has previously resisted outside regulation of AI development.\n\"We need to continue to invite political leaders, leaders of AI, social organizations, associations to come together and look at what is happening already and what could be down the road,\" he said.\n\"To simply say 'Oh it's not going to happen' and close our eyes to it I think is probably not the most responsible way to go about that,\" he added.\nThe warning comes as worries about AI grow inside and outside the industry and reports continue to emerge about increasingly powerful systems that solve problems beyond human capacity but could also go rogue and carry out other, more harmful tasks.\nThe worries have grown so loud that the CEO of OpenAI, the company behind ChatGPT, said in an interview with Fortune that his company would wait until next year to start selling its stock to investors on Wall Street as it focuses on safety.\nLeo noted that ever since he published his encyclical in May, the Vatican has received comments from people interested in being part of a broader conversation about AI. A Vatican commission is working to promote dialogue, study and investigation \"to find ways to make sure that AI does not get to a point of destroying humanity or removing humanity from the conversation,\" he said.\nLeo said he was \"relatively optimistic\" that solutions could be found.\n\"I think there are enough people seriously interested in that and willing to look at that outside of the questions once again of power, who gets there first,\" he said.\nLeo had identified AI as one of t he biggest challenges to humanity within days of his election.\nSupport trusted journalism and civil dialogue.\nLeft:Pope Leo XIV speaks to journalists aboard the flight from Metz, France, to Rome, at the end of his four-day visit to France, on Monday. Photo by Alberto Pizzoli/Pool Photo via AP\nByNicole Winfield, Associated Press\nByNicole Winfield, Samuel Petrequin, Sylvie Corbet, John Leicester, Associated Press\nByNicole Winfield, Associated Press\nBySuman Naishadham, Nicole Winfield, Associated Press\nByNicole Winfield, Paolo Santalucia, Associated Press\nAssociated PressAssociated Press\nSupport Provided By:Learn more\nSubscribe to Here\u2019s the Deal, our politics\n                 newsletter for analysis you won\u2019t find anywhere else.\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\nScienceMay 25\n\u00a9 1996 - 2026 NewsHour Productions LLC. All Rights Reserved.\nPBS is a 501(c)(3) not-for-profit organization.\nSections\nAbout\nStay Connected\nSubscribe to Here's the Deal with Lisa Desjardins\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\nSupport for News Hour Provided By", "date": "Mon, 28 Sep 2026 17:15:47 -0400"}, {"title": "As work requirements kick in for Medicaid, some states are taking a tougher stance", "url": "https://www.pbs.org/newshour/health/as-work-requirements-kick-in-for-medicaid-some-states-are-taking-a-tougher-stance", "body": "Your support keeps PBS News free and accessible.\nGeoff Mulvihill, Associated PressGeoff Mulvihill, Associated Press\nLeave your feedback\nWork requirements for some Medicaid beneficiaries begin across the U.S. in January, the result of President Donald Trump's big 2025 tax cut and policy law, and a handful of Republican-led states are imposing tougher rules than the law requires.\nAt issue is how someone can prove they are too medically frail to work. While federal regulations say the state can take the recipient's word for it for the first year, at least six states are requiring documentation immediately. There is a continuing push in other states to follow suit eventually.\nAdvocates say that could create problems, especially for new applicants to the joint state-federal health insurance program for lower-income people.\n\"Someone may not be able to work, but they can't see a doctor because they can't afford it. So they're now applying for Medicaid,\" said Jennifer Tolbert, director of state health policy and data at the research organization KFF. \"But Medicaid is saying you need documentation from a provider.\"\nAs some Republican-led states take a tougher-than-required approach to implementing the federally mandated changes, Democrats in 25 states are suing and asserting that the rules are too harsh.\nThe Medicaid changes are projected to save the federal government $887 billion over the next decade and result in 7.5 million fewer people with health insurance, according to the Congressional Budget Office's 2025 estimate.\nThe conservative Foundation for Government Accountability has been pushing states not to make it too easy for people to avoid work requirements, saying able-bodied adults are pushing enrollment and costs higher.\nJonathan Ingram, vice president of research and policy at the group, has been encouraging states not to take a beneficiary's word for it, a process known as self-attestation.\n\"Self-attestation is fraud-by-design,\" he said via email. \"It is a policy developed by bureaucrats to maximize enrollment at the expense of program integrity.\"\nSelf-attestation comes under penalty of perjury, so people who lie can be subject to criminal charges \u2014 though such charges are rare.\nREAD MORE:As states face stricter Medicaid work requirements, Nebraska is an early test\nArkansas, Idaho, Indiana, New Hampshire, North Carolina and Ohio all have laws or policies that bar self-attestation to qualify for the work requirement exception starting next year.\nRepublican Missouri state Rep. Darin Chappell said he worked with the Foundation for Government Accountability to craft a proposed amendment that would have enshrined Medicaid work requirements in the state constitution. It would have required \"documentary evidence\" of work or reasons for exemptions, specifically prohibiting self-attestation.\nThe amendment passed the Missouri House but died without a vote in the Senate. Chappell plans to try again next year.\n\"If someone has a debilitating physical ailment \u2014 or mental ailment for that matter \u2014 something that prohibits them from working, that's totally legitimate,\" he said. \"But we're not taking anybody's word for it. I don't mean to shock you none, but people do tend to lie about such things.\"\nThe new mandate applies to up to 20 million lower-income adults without children at home who are enrolled in Medicaid through an optional expansion in 40 states and the District of Columbia. The requirement does not apply in the 10 states, mostly in the South, that did not expand Medicaid.\nBeneficiaries will have to work or volunteer at least 80 hours each month or be in school at least half-time to qualify, with exceptions for situations including medical frailty.\nThe law had said medically frail people include those who have substance use disorders, disabilities or serious medical conditions. But the Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services published a rule in June that went further and was stricter than states and providers expected, saying someone's condition must \"significantly impair\" their ability to work, volunteer or attend school as required for an exemption.\nWatch the segment in the player above.\nCurrently states generally decide once a year whether Medicaid recipients still qualify for benefits. The new law will require it to happen twice for most enrollees.\nStates will be allowed to accept self-attestation just once each time a person enrolls starting in 2028. After that, documentation would be required at least every 12 months to verify that they still have the condition and that it still prevents them from being able to work.\nThe government expects states to rely increasingly on third-party data \u2014 on things such as workers' compensation claims or prescription information \u2014 to help make determinations. For conditions that do not show up in the databases, states can require a doctor's certification, a disability award letter or other proof.\nWhile states have been ramping up the use of this information, the work requirements are still a big change, and many will first have to spend millions of dollars improving their computer systems.\nStates are alreadystruggling in some cases to make changesto the Supplemental Nutrition Assistance food program, or SNAP, that are required under Trump's law, including an expanded work requirement.\nREAD MORE:More than 1.2 million children have lost SNAP benefits since last July. Experts explain why\nIn Arizona, for example, enrollment plummeted by 55% from April 2025 to April 2026 \u2014 the biggest drop in the country \u2014 with more than 400,000 fewer people getting benefits now. Last month, the state said that was largely due to difficulties in putting in place new federal requirements that resulted in higher call volumes and additional verification.\nIngram said some of the concerns about ending self-attestation are overblown and people can get diagnoses to support their inability to work at many places, including charitable clinics and federally qualified health centers. He also noted that Medicaid allows retroactive coverage for doctor visits that happen before enrollment.\nPatient advocates say it's not that simple.\n\"The added paperwork, the hoops you have to jump through lead to coverage loss,\" said Nate Crippes, a lawyer at the Disability Law Center in Utah, where a law allows self-attestation to qualify for the work requirement exception on a provisional basis.\nAn added challenge is that most people in the Medicaid expansion group in his state have a mental health or substance use disorder diagnosis \u2014 or both.\n\"We're choosing to make people with health conditions jump through a bunch of hurdles,\" said Camille Richoux, health policy director at Arkansas Advocates for Children and Families.\nIt can be tricky if states decide to rely partly on patient diagnosis codes to determine whether recipients are able to work.\nLucy Dagneau, senior director of the state and local campaigns team at the American Cancer Society Cancer Action Network, said the codes do not indicate the severity of a condition. For example, some patients with a specific cancer can continue working, she said, but others who are in more advanced stages cannot.\n\"Our fear is by that banning self-attestation, unfortunately, the state lawmakers who pass those laws are going to have a hand in more people losing coverage in their state than would have,\" she said.\nAssociated Press writers David A. Lieb in Jefferson City, Missouri, and Laura Turbay in Little Rock, Arkansas, contributed.\nSupport trusted journalism and civil dialogue.\nLeft:Workers at a Medicaid call center in Jefferson City, Missouri, field questions and review information regarding eligibility determinations in August. Photo by David A. Lieb/AP\nByFatima Hussein, Associated Press\nByAli Swenson, Geoff Mulvihill, Associated Press\nByHannah Grabenstein\nByMargery A. Beck, Ali Swenson, Associated Press\nGeoff Mulvihill, Associated PressGeoff Mulvihill, Associated Press\nSupport Provided By:Learn more\nSubscribe to Here\u2019s the Deal, our politics\n                 newsletter for analysis you won\u2019t find anywhere else.\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\nHealthSep 25\n\u00a9 1996 - 2026 NewsHour Productions LLC. All Rights Reserved.\nPBS is a 501(c)(3) not-for-profit organization.\nSections\nAbout\nStay Connected\nSubscribe to Here's the Deal with Lisa Desjardins\nThank you. Please check your inbox to confirm.\nSupport for News Hour Provided By", "date": "Mon, 28 Sep 2026 16:55:33 -0400"}, {"title": "Mideast Oil Exports Rebound Even as Prices Remain Elevated", "url": "https://www.nytimes.com/2026/09/29/business/oil-exports-strait-of-hormuz.html", "body": "Please enable JS and disable any ad blocker", "date": "Tue, 29 Sep 2026 15:03:40 +0000"}, {"title": "OpenAI Apologizes for Australia Medicare Hack", "url": "https://www.nytimes.com/2026/09/29/world/asia/openai-australia-government-hack-apology.html", "body": "Please enable JS and disable any ad blocker", "date": "Tue, 29 Sep 2026 05:23:15 +0000"}, {"title": "OpenAI Says It Will Not Release Newest Astra A.I. Model Over Safety Concerns", "url": "https://www.nytimes.com/2026/09/28/technology/openai-astra-safety.html", "body": "Please enable JS and disable any ad blocker", "date": "Tue, 29 Sep 2026 00:32:19 +0000"}, {"title": "Dartmouth Provost Is the Latest College Professor Examined for A.I. Use", "url": "https://www.nytimes.com/2026/09/29/us/college-professors-administrators-ai-dartmouth.html", "body": "Please enable JS and disable any ad blocker", "date": "Tue, 29 Sep 2026 16:28:09 +0000"}, {"title": "ICE Plans 5,000 New Hires to Help With Deportation Drive", "url": "https://www.nytimes.com/2026/09/29/us/ice-hiring-immigration-enforcement-vetting.html", "body": "Please enable JS and disable any ad blocker", "date": "Tue, 29 Sep 2026 15:23:36 +0000"}, {"title": "Trump Administration Prepares to Ask Tax Filers if They Are U.S. Citizens", "url": "https://www.nytimes.com/2026/09/29/business/irs-tax-returns-citizenship.html", "body": "Please enable JS and disable any ad blocker", "date": "Tue, 29 Sep 2026 14:15:47 +0000"}, {"title": "His Jet vs. Her Jeep: How Wealth Is Playing in the Midterms", "url": "https://www.nytimes.com/2026/09/29/us/vivek-ramaswamy-amy-acton-wealth-class-ohio-midterms.html", "body": "Please enable JS and disable any ad blocker", "date": "Tue, 29 Sep 2026 11:19:11 +0000"}, {"title": "A Tough Ad Reminds Nebraska Voters About a Brutal Crime and a Pardon", "url": "https://www.nytimes.com/2026/09/29/us/politics/ad-watch-nebraska-senate-race.html", "body": "Please enable JS and disable any ad blocker", "date": "Tue, 29 Sep 2026 14:43:57 +0000"}, {"title": "Mamdani Announces Plan to Fight Antisemitism, Without Discussing Israel", "url": "https://www.nytimes.com/2026/09/29/nyregion/mamdani-antisemitism-report-nyc.html", "body": "Please enable JS and disable any ad blocker", "date": "Tue, 29 Sep 2026 15:36:23 +0000"}, {"title": "Cornell University Rape Investigation: What We Know", "url": "https://www.nytimes.com/2026/09/29/nyregion/cornell-university-rape-investigation-chi-phi-fraternity.html", "body": "Please enable JS and disable any ad blocker", "date": "Tue, 29 Sep 2026 16:19:05 +0000"}, {"title": "Israeli Settlers Stop Soldiers From Returning Palestinians to Their Home", "url": "https://www.nytimes.com/2026/09/29/world/middleeast/west-bank-israel-settlers-attack-soldiers-palestinians.html", "body": "Please enable JS and disable any ad blocker", "date": "Tue, 29 Sep 2026 16:03:12 +0000"}, {"title": "Is Claude Conscious? Inside Anthropic\u2019s Quest to Instill Morality Into Its A.I. Models", "url": "https://www.nytimes.com/2026/09/29/us/anthropic-claude-morals-ai.html", "body": "Please enable JS and disable any ad blocker", "date": "Tue, 29 Sep 2026 16:15:17 +0000"}, {"title": "Book Review: \u2018This Cursed Beautiful Land,\u2019 by Evan Gershkovich", "url": "https://www.nytimes.com/2026/09/29/books/review/this-cursed-beautiful-land-evan-gershkovich.html", "body": "Please enable JS and disable any ad blocker", "date": "Tue, 29 Sep 2026 09:01:12 +0000"}, {"title": "I\u2019m Exhausted. You\u2019re Demoralized. We Weren\u2019t Built for a World Like This.", "url": "https://www.nytimes.com/2026/09/29/opinion/boring-politics-exhaustion.html", "body": "Please enable JS and disable any ad blocker", "date": "Tue, 29 Sep 2026 12:46:25 +0000"}, {"title": "Trump\u2019s Luck Is Finally Running Out", "url": "https://www.nytimes.com/2026/09/29/opinion/trump-midterms-republicans.html", "body": "Please enable JS and disable any ad blocker", "date": "Tue, 29 Sep 2026 09:03:55 +0000"}, {"title": "How to Improve Schools", "url": "https://www.nytimes.com/2026/09/29/briefing/how-to-improve-schools.html", "body": "Please enable JS and disable any ad blocker", "date": "Tue, 29 Sep 2026 10:53:02 +0000"}, {"title": "Rubio Says Incident at UK Air Base RAF Fairford \u2018Clearly Involved\u2019 a Foreign Actor", "url": "https://www.nytimes.com/2026/09/29/world/europe/uk-raf-fairford-air-base-incident-rubio-us.html", "body": "Please enable JS and disable any ad blocker", "date": "Tue, 29 Sep 2026 10:44:22 +0000"}, {"title": "Mamdani Unveils Strategy to Combat Antisemitism Amid Criticism of His Anti-Israel Views", "url": "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/mamdani-unveils-strategy-to-combat-antisemitism-amid-criticism-of-his-anti-israel-views-088f011a?mod=rss_usnews", "body": "POPULAR ARTICLES\nLATEST PODCASTS\nView All", "date": "Tue, 29 Sep 2026 15:00:00 GMT"}, {"title": "Don\u2019t Call Me a Helicopter Parent. My Kid\u2019s in a Junkyard Playing With a Saw.", "url": "https://www.wsj.com/lifestyle/new-york-parents-junkyard-governors-island-3abc689a?mod=rss_usnews", "body": "POPULAR ARTICLES\nLATEST PODCASTS\nView All", "date": "Tue, 29 Sep 2026 09:30:00 GMT"}, {"title": "Trump Unveils $15 Billion Iowa Steel Project", "url": "https://www.wsj.com/politics/trump-to-unveil-planned-15-billion-iowa-steel-project-051bc7d8?mod=rss_usnews", "body": "POPULAR ARTICLES\nLATEST PODCASTS\nView All", "date": "Mon, 28 Sep 2026 22:46:00 GMT"}, {"title": "Battle to Reopen Columbia University\u2019s Gates Gets a Push From City Hall", "url": "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/education/columbia-gates-campus-open-mamdani-new-york-f0b6aa88?mod=rss_usnews", "body": "POPULAR ARTICLES\nLATEST PODCASTS\nView All", "date": "Mon, 28 Sep 2026 21:28:00 GMT"}, {"title": "Justice Alito Won\u2019t Participate in Supreme Court Case on Climate Change", "url": "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/law/justice-alito-wont-participate-in-supreme-court-case-on-climate-change-ef0235e7?mod=rss_usnews", "body": "POPULAR ARTICLES\nLATEST PODCASTS\nView All", "date": "Mon, 28 Sep 2026 20:39:00 GMT"}, {"title": "New York Prosecutor Reopens Investigation Into Cornell Rape Allegations", "url": "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/law/cornell-sexual-assault-chi-phi-fraternity-investigation-08c8568e?mod=rss_usnews", "body": "POPULAR ARTICLES\nLATEST PODCASTS\nView All", "date": "Mon, 28 Sep 2026 19:31:00 GMT"}, {"title": "Colleges Once Saw High Tuition as a Sign of Quality. Now They\u2019re Slashing Prices.", "url": "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/education/college-tuition-cuts-costs-financial-aid-38e749df?mod=rss_usnews", "body": "POPULAR ARTICLES\nLATEST PODCASTS\nView All", "date": "Fri, 25 Sep 2026 09:30:00 GMT"}, {"title": "Student Loan Borrowers Allege Canceled Debt Is Still Trailing Them", "url": "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/education/student-loan-borrowers-allege-canceled-debt-is-still-trailing-them-193f4231?mod=rss_usnews", "body": "POPULAR ARTICLES\nLATEST PODCASTS\nView All", "date": "Thu, 24 Sep 2026 16:10:00 GMT"}, {"title": "Claudine Gay Is Back at Harvard\u2014to Teach a Class About Harvard", "url": "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/education/harvard-university-claudine-gay-class-cf6ce2c3?mod=rss_usnews", "body": "POPULAR ARTICLES\nLATEST PODCASTS\nView All", "date": "Wed, 23 Sep 2026 13:19:00 GMT"}, {"title": "Philadelphia\u2019s DA, the Dean of Progressive Prosecutors, Has His Own Legal Trouble", "url": "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/law/philadelphias-da-the-dean-of-progressive-prosecutors-has-his-own-legal-trouble-97fbd394?mod=rss_usnews", "body": "POPULAR ARTICLES\nLATEST PODCASTS\nView All", "date": "Sun, 27 Sep 2026 09:30:00 GMT"}, {"title": "How a Business Litigator Became DOJ\u2019s Point Man Against Transgender Care", "url": "https://www.wsj.com/politics/policy/how-a-business-litigator-became-dojs-point-man-against-transgender-care-86171021?mod=rss_usnews", "body": "POPULAR ARTICLES\nLATEST PODCASTS\nView All", "date": "Sat, 26 Sep 2026 15:45:00 GMT"}, {"title": "How Missouri\u2019s Republican Gerrymander Struck Out at the Supreme Court", "url": "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/law/how-missouris-republican-gerrymander-struck-out-at-the-supreme-court-110b5835?mod=rss_usnews", "body": "POPULAR ARTICLES\nLATEST PODCASTS\nView All", "date": "Fri, 25 Sep 2026 23:18:00 GMT"}, {"title": "Pentagon-Backed Venezuelan Oil Company Poised to Challenge Chevron", "url": "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/law/pentagon-backed-venezuelan-oil-company-poised-to-challenge-chevron-abf18109?mod=rss_usnews", "body": "POPULAR ARTICLES\nLATEST PODCASTS\nView All", "date": "Fri, 25 Sep 2026 21:16:00 GMT"}, {"title": "U.S. Appeals Court Upholds Pentagon Labeling Anthropic a Security Risk", "url": "https://www.wsj.com/politics/national-security/u-s-appeals-court-upholds-pentagon-labeling-anthropic-a-security-risk-9d08c3af?mod=rss_usnews", "body": "POPULAR ARTICLES\nLATEST PODCASTS\nView All", "date": "Fri, 25 Sep 2026 17:40:00 GMT"}, {"title": "Natural Gas Slides on Milder Temperature Outlook", "url": "https://www.wsj.com/finance/commodities-futures/natural-gas-slides-on-milder-temperature-outlook-b27ac1c4?mod=rss_usnews", "body": "POPULAR ARTICLES\nLATEST PODCASTS\nView All", "date": "Tue, 29 Sep 2026 13:42:00 GMT"}, {"title": "Nevada Is Making Surprising Gains in Solar Energy", "url": "https://www.wsj.com/business/energy-oil/nevada-solar-industry-trump-states-b838c7e4?mod=rss_usnews", "body": "POPULAR ARTICLES\nLATEST PODCASTS\nView All", "date": "Sun, 27 Sep 2026 00:30:00 GMT"}, {"title": "How Asheville\u2019s Artists and Shopkeepers Rebuilt Their City", "url": "https://www.wsj.com/business/how-ashevilles-artists-and-shopkeepers-rebuilt-their-city-80762f93?mod=rss_usnews", "body": "POPULAR ARTICLES\nLATEST PODCASTS\nView All", "date": "Sat, 26 Sep 2026 09:30:00 GMT"}, {"title": "Contractor Played Down Contamination Risks After Ohio Train Disaster, Worker Says", "url": "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/contractor-played-down-contamination-risks-after-ohio-train-disaster-worker-says-8fad3ab4?mod=rss_usnews", "body": "POPULAR ARTICLES\nLATEST PODCASTS\nView All", "date": "Wed, 23 Sep 2026 21:46:00 GMT"}, {"title": "Opinion | Iran May Be America\u2019s Last Mideast War", "url": "https://www.wsj.com/opinion/iran-may-be-americas-last-mideast-war-723f2e35?mod=rss_usnews", "body": "POPULAR ARTICLES\nLATEST PODCASTS\nView All", "date": "Tue, 29 Sep 2026 15:39:00 GMT"}, {"title": "Opinion | Cornell Fails Its Free-Speech Test", "url": "https://www.wsj.com/opinion/cornell-only-wishes-it-were-tolerant-c42fa322?mod=rss_usnews", "body": "POPULAR ARTICLES\nLATEST PODCASTS\nView All", "date": "Tue, 29 Sep 2026 15:34:00 GMT"}, {"title": "Opinion | The \u2018Affordability\u2019 Democrats? You Have to Be Kidding", "url": "https://www.wsj.com/opinion/democrats-affordability-policies-energy-healthcare-inflation-midterm-elections-82afe2a4?mod=rss_usnews", "body": "POPULAR ARTICLES\nLATEST PODCASTS\nView All", "date": "Mon, 28 Sep 2026 21:57:00 GMT"}, {"title": "Opinion | Florida\u2019s Odd Race Preference Defense", "url": "https://www.wsj.com/opinion/florida-race-preferences-homeowner-assistance-fund-777ab710?mod=rss_usnews", "body": "POPULAR ARTICLES\nLATEST PODCASTS\nView All", "date": "Mon, 28 Sep 2026 21:56:00 GMT"}, {"title": "Opinion | Hamas Couldn\u2019t Kill the Abraham Accords", "url": "https://www.wsj.com/opinion/abraham-accords-new-york-meeting-u-a-e-israel-iran-23de6b27?mod=rss_usnews", "body": "POPULAR ARTICLES\nLATEST PODCASTS\nView All", "date": "Mon, 28 Sep 2026 21:55:00 GMT"}, {"title": "Opinion | Trump and Chinese Circumstance", "url": "https://www.wsj.com/opinion/trump-and-chinese-circumstance-875f0e87?mod=rss_usnews", "body": "POPULAR ARTICLES\nLATEST PODCASTS\nView All", "date": "Mon, 28 Sep 2026 21:20:00 GMT"}, {"title": "How Evan Gershkovich Kept Reporting From Inside Russia\u2019s Prison System", "url": "https://www.wsj.com/world/how-evan-gershkovich-kept-reporting-from-inside-russias-prison-system-e41aea97?mod=rss_usnews", "body": "POPULAR ARTICLES\nLATEST PODCASTS\nView All", "date": "Mon, 28 Sep 2026 13:07:00 GMT"}, {"title": "Penn State\u2019s Campuswide Cocaine Ring Was Fueled by Fraternity Hazing", "url": "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/penn-state-cocaine-ring-fraternity-hazing-3813d85b?mod=rss_usnews", "body": "POPULAR ARTICLES\nLATEST PODCASTS\nView All", "date": "Mon, 28 Sep 2026 09:30:00 GMT"}, {"title": "China Wants More Babies. So It Is Glamming Up the City Hall Wedding.", "url": "https://www.wsj.com/world/asia/china-wants-more-babies-so-it-is-glamming-up-the-city-hall-wedding-84816f21?mod=rss_usnews", "body": "POPULAR ARTICLES\nLATEST PODCASTS\nView All", "date": "Sun, 27 Sep 2026 17:27:00 GMT"}, {"title": "Ford\u2019s Latest Employee of the Month Is a Hawk Named \u2018El Charro\u2019", "url": "https://www.wsj.com/business/autos/fords-factory-hermosillo-mexico-pigeons-hawk-el-charro-b78d90ae?mod=rss_usnews", "body": "POPULAR ARTICLES\nLATEST PODCASTS\nView All", "date": "Sun, 27 Sep 2026 09:30:00 GMT"}, {"title": "Report Details Security Failures at School Where Charlie Kirk Was Killed", "url": "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/report-details-security-failures-at-school-where-charlie-kirk-was-killed-563ee8ee?mod=rss_usnews", "body": "POPULAR ARTICLES\nLATEST PODCASTS\nView All", "date": "Fri, 25 Sep 2026 21:50:00 GMT"}, {"title": "The California District Attorney Cracking Down on the Scourge of E-Bikes", "url": "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/the-california-district-attorney-cracking-down-on-the-scourge-of-e-bikes-a5dbaff5?mod=rss_usnews", "body": "POPULAR ARTICLES\nLATEST PODCASTS\nView All", "date": "Thu, 24 Sep 2026 17:04:00 GMT"}, {"title": "Federal Prosecutors Open Probe Into Jeffrey Epstein\u2019s Executors", "url": "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/law/federal-prosecutors-open-probe-into-jeffrey-epsteins-executors-cfef362b?mod=rss_usnews", "body": "POPULAR ARTICLES\nLATEST PODCASTS\nView All", "date": "Wed, 23 Sep 2026 23:29:00 GMT"}, {"title": "Trump administration says it's scaling back fuel efficiency standards for new cars", "url": "https://www.npr.org/2026/09/29/nx-s1-5983673/trump-administration-says-its-scaling-back-fuel-efficiency-standards-for-new-cars", "body": "Willa Rubin\nThe Trump administration is weakening Biden-era fuel efficiency standards for new cars and trucks.\nNPR does not offer or accept money for coverage or interviews.Anyone who makes such an offer or request is not an NPR employee and does not represent NPR.\nSponsor Message\nBecome an NPR sponsor", "date": "Tue, 29 Sep 2026 04:46:49 -0400"}, {"title": "News organizations ask court to bar White House enforcement of access ban", "url": "https://www.npr.org/2026/09/29/nx-s1-5984349/news-organizations-court-white-house-ban", "body": "By\nThe Associated Press\nA logo on a booth for CNN is seen in the press area at the White House, Saturday, Sept. 26, 2026, in Washington.Rod Lamkey/APhide caption\nThree news outlets that President Donald Trump abruptly banished from White House grounds this month asked a federal court to block the ban until the case is resolved, saying the administration has \"persisted in unpredictably and inconsistently implementing its ban\" despite a judge's restraining order \u2014 including selectively barring CNN from White House pool duties.\n\"The White House's words and actions leave little doubt that it will immediately reinstitute a complete ban of CNN, MS NOW, and POLITICO if allowed the opportunity,\" lawyers for CNN, MS NOW and Politico wrote in a filing late Monday.\n\"Because the ban is an unlawful assault on the most fundamental First Amendment freedoms and will inflict irreparable harm on Plaintiffs and the public if not restrained, this Court should preliminarily enjoin its enforcement pending the resolution of this case,\" the filing said.\nIt was the latest development in the back-and-forth saga of the Trump administration and the news media during the second term of his presidency. It has been a contentious relationship that has raised multiple First Amendment questions, produced sanctions and restrictions on White House and Pentagon reporters and led to several lawsuits that are winding their ways through the federal court system.\nA two-week temporary restraining order on Trump's ban of the three organizations, issued by U.S. District Judge Timothy Kelly last week, restored the outlets' White House access, but expires Oct. 8. The preliminary injunction requested in Monday's filing would extend the White House access until the case is finally resolved.\nIn a statement emailed to The Associated Press immediately after the filing, the lead attorney for the three news outlets called the ban \"a blatant violation of the First Amendment and due process.\"\n\"The president and his administration have continued to make clear that this ban has only one purpose: to punish reporting he doesn't like,\" said the lawyer, Theodore J. Boutrous Jr.\n\"That's retaliation and viewpoint discrimination, pure and simple,\" Boutrous said. \"With the district court's temporary restraining order expiring this week, it is critical to ensure that the White House continues to be prevented from implementing this unconstitutional ban on the free press.\"\nThe filing came a day after the Trump administration made clear it was not backing down from its contention that the sudden ban, which began Sept. 19, was justified.\n\"When it comes to the White House, it is the people's house,\" Attorney General Todd Blanche said on ABC on Sunday, invoking an argument the administration has made since the beginning. \"It is a privilege, and not a right, to be there.\"\nMonday's 42-page filing reiterated many of the arguments that the news outlets made in their original lawsuit against Trump and the White House, filed two days after the reporters were banned.\nIt also added mention of the White House broadcast pool rotation, which has become a battleground in the last few days in the standoff between Trump and the media.\nThe White House, it said, \"has persisted in unpredictably and inconsistently implementing its ban, including by barring CNN from fulfilling its role as designated television network pooler on Sept. 26 \u2014 to the detriment of CNN, its fellow pool members and, ultimately, the American public.\"\nWhen the ban was implemented, CNN was removed from its duties as part of the broadcast pool, which consists of the five main networks: CNN, ABC, CBS, NBC and Fox News. In solidarity, the other networks decided to suspend the pool until CNN was allowed back in.\nOn Friday, CNN was allowed to cover the president with Chinese leader Xi Jinping at the National Archives. But in a new setback Saturday, the White House banned the network from Air Force One, preventing it from covering Trump's trip to a college football game in Tennessee. The rest of the pool declined to provide a replacement, in solidarity with CNN.\nBlanche defended that decision, too. \"The president has every right to decide who gets to fly on his plane,\" he said in the Sunday interview. He added that Trump was \"sick and tired of certain media outlets just refusing to report accurate news.\" He added that news coverage he described as \"misinformation\" amounted to a national security issue.\n\"That is what is a national security problem, when you have complete misinformation coming out of the White House,\" he said.\nKelly threw cold water on that argument in his ruling last week granting the temporary restraining order against the White House ban. \"The court is skeptical \u2026 that defendants' interest in safeguarding national security is the actual motivation\" for removal of access, Kelly wrote.\nHis ruling did not directly address the TV pool rotation. In the new filing, the plaintiffs describe the important function the pool provides and makes the case that it's an important part of the regular access to the president, and his activities, that the media has traditionally had \u2014 and must be restored.\nIt singles out the Air Force One case. \"CNN received no official communication from the White House explaining its exclusion,\" the filing says. \"Because CNN was excluded and no replacement designated, no primary television network pooler accompanied the President on the trip.\"\nFor now, networks in the pool have decided to address the ongoing standoff on a case-by-case basis, according to a person familiar with the pool's operation, speaking on condition of anonymity to give details of ongoing discussions.\nOn Sunday, Fox covered a pool event; on Monday, NBC was covering the president in the Oval Office. CNN is not in the rotation until Thursday. If the network is again barred from a pool event, the networks will decide together, presumably based on the newsworthiness of the situation, whether to provide a replacement or to refrain from coverage as they did previously.\nThe issues at stake are difficult. On the one hand, the networks want to take a stand on behalf of press freedom and against efforts to intimidate coverage. On the other, media outlets have an obvious purpose of providing news to their audiences.\n\"There are not a lot of good options,\" said David A. Schulz, director of the Media Freedom and Information Access Clinic at Yale Law School.\nShould the White House continue to bar CNN, Schulz said, networks may choose \"to exert their market power and say, 'We're not going to play ball with you.'\"\nThe question, Schulz added, is: \"Who's going to back down first?\"\nThe networks \"may well feel like they're between a rock and a hard place,\" noted RonNell Andersen Jones, a law professor at the University of Utah.\n\"Capitulation might be perceived as abandonment of core components of press freedom,\" Andersen Jones said. \"But the practicalities of lengthy White House news blackouts are also complicated, and refusing to cover major news at the highest levels of government might itself feel like a failure to serve the goals of democracy.\"\nNPR does not offer or accept money for coverage or interviews.Anyone who makes such an offer or request is not an NPR employee and does not represent NPR.\nSponsor Message\nBecome an NPR sponsor", "date": "Tue, 29 Sep 2026 04:13:11 -0400"}, {"title": "The Senate passes the Protect College Sports Act, but the bill's future is unclear", "url": "https://www.npr.org/2026/09/28/nx-s1-5977851/protect-college-sports-act-senate-bill-vote", "body": "Becky Sullivan\nAlabama Crimson Tide players pose with fans after defeating the Florida State Seminoles 50-36 on Sept. 19 in Tuscaloosa, Alabama.Jason Clark/Getty Imageshide caption\nWASHINGTON \u2014 TheProtect College Sports Act, a bill aiming torein inwhat its supporters call a \"Wild West\" era ofcollege athletics, cleared its most significant hurdle yet on Monday when the Senate passed it by a bipartisan vote of 77-22.\nThe bill would grant the NCAA a limited antitrust exemption in order to restore its five-year eligibility rule and transfer limits. The legislation would also codify the regulation of name, image and likeness deals that havetransformedcollege athletics in recent years by allowing athletes to be paid \u2014 some of them as much as millions of dollars per season.\nAmong the bill's most vocal supporters are the NCAA itself, a long list of powerhouse schools and conferences and President Trump, whoon Sept. 17called for the bill's passage on Truth Social. Negotiations led by Sens. Ted Cruz, R-Texas, and Maria Cantwell, D-Wash., lasted for more than a year and picked up co-sponsors from both parties along the way.\nTelevision commercials featuring the likes of Alabama great Nick Saban havepepperedcollege football broadcasts this month, and Cruz appeared on ESPN'sCollege GameDayto promote the bill. \"Right now, it is the Wild West. The Protect College Sports Act is bipartisan legislation designed to bring order to the chaos,\" Cruz said at a news conference in mid-September.\nThe legislation now moves to the House, which is on recess until after the November midterm elections. Its chances of passing are unclear.\nFor roughly the past decade, a flood of litigation has chipped away at many long-standing rules governing college sports \u2014 from the tradition ofamateurismto rules aroundtransfersand who is eligible tocompeteat all.\n\"We're not turning back the clock, and whether you loved or hated the old system, we shouldn't try. The problem is that college sports entered this new era without a stable framework to govern it,\" said Sen. Eric Schmitt, R-Mo., on the floor of the Senate on Monday before the vote.\nOpponents of the bill \u2014 from labor groups to athletes organizations to the NAACP, along with the 22 senatorswho voted against it \u2014 say it unfairly places limits on athletes without allowing them an equal seat at the table alongside powerful institutions.\n\"I think the better way to frame it is 'The Protect the NCAA Act,'\" said Sam Ehrlich, an associate professor at Boise State University who trackscollege sports litigation. \"It gives the NCAA significant institutional power to be able to continue to control college sports at the expense of athletes who have been able to push the NCAA on some of their illegal business practices over the years.\"\nMore than half a million athletes compete in college sports each academic year, across all of the NCAA's divisions. Most of those who earn money compete in revenue-generating sports, such as football and basketball, in Division I. Only a small percentage of athletes earn six figures or more, most of them football and men's basketball players in the four \"power\" conferences: the SEC, Big Ten, ACC and Big 12.\nAthletic department budgetshave balloonedover the years. But athlete pay is far from the only big-ticket item for athletic departments.\nCompensation for coaches and administrators has grown exponentially. On Sept. 15, Cantwell releaseda reportciting major increases in costs from 2005 to 2023: \"322% in recruiting costs, nearly 300% in sports equipment, around 250% in both medical and gameday expenses, and more than 200% in travel. Coaching salaries increased a whopping 370% over the same time period.\" Big-league athletic departments have increasingly added positions like general managers and in-house video production crews that were once the exclusive domain of professional teams.\nWith college athletes sometimes transferring to a new school every year, thefeelof the sports has changed.Zach Del Bello/Getty Imageshide caption\nYet critics of the bill say that the legislation does little to rein in runaway spending in any of those areas, even as it seeks to cap athlete compensation.\n\"That's what frustrates me \u2014 is we have a bill that's going to protect the wealth and the gravy train for a lot of folks but puts burdens on college athletes,\" said Sen. Cory Booker, who played tight end at Stanford University, speaking in an interview with NPR last week.\u00a0The New Jersey Democrat voted against the bill.\nMeanwhile, athletic programs that do not generate revenue \u2014 like most women's and Olympic sports programs \u2014 have felt a pinch. Schools have eliminated more than 100 women's and Olympic sports teams since 2023, Cantwell's report notes. Supporters of the bill say the legislation is needed to rein in spending before schools are forced to wind down even more such programs.\nSome experts are skeptical of that argument. \"In the strongest-possible terms, there's no reason why that needs to happen,\" said Dionne Koller, a University of Baltimore School of Law professor who co-chaired a recent bipartisancongressional commissionon the state of the U.S. Olympic and Paralympic movements. \"There's lots of other ways we could address the financial piece without saying that necessarily we would have to cut Olympic, Paralympic \u2014 which is a small number of college programs, but growing \u2014 and women's sports.\"\nThe bill's supporters point to provisions around women's and Olympic sports that require schools to maintain scholarships and roster spots for such programs. But those provisions expire after nine years for large schools and after four years for midsize schools, and exemptions could be made for schools with falling athletics revenues or other \"demonstrable financial hardship.\"\nSen. Ted Cruz, along with Sen. Maria Cantwell and Sen. Shelley Moore Capito, holds a news conference with NCAA athletes and coaches about the Protect College Sports Act on Sept. 14 in Washington, D.C.Chip Somodevilla/Getty Imageshide caption\nThe Senate bill tackles a variety of other issues, including a 5% cap on compensation for agents handling athlete name, image and likeness (NIL) deals and a provision that would allow conferences to pool their media rights. It sets out health and safety guidelines for schools to implement. It would cap conference membership at 20 schools \u2014 a provision seen as an attempt to keep the formation of any \"super leagues\" at bay.\nA rule to disincentivize conference realignment could prove an obstacle as the legislation moves to the House. As passed, the Senate bill would require any school jumping from one power conference to another to first compete as an independent for three academic years, which would likely represent a major financial penalty. One of Florida's senators, Rick Scott, who represents schools likeFlorida State Universityand theUniversity of Miami, preferred to keep their conference alignment options as open as possible, voted no on the bill. Similar opposition could materialize in the House, from Florida to the Carolinas to California.\nIn a statement, NCAA president Charlie Baker thanked senators \"for their bipartisan effort to protect this uniquely American tradition of college sports in the decades to come.\"\n\"Now the bill moves to the U.S. House of Representatives and there is much work left to do to ensure student-athlete benefits are codified into federal law and future generations of young people can access those benefits and use college sports as the launching point for their careers,\" he said.\nNPR does not offer or accept money for coverage or interviews.Anyone who makes such an offer or request is not an NPR employee and does not represent NPR.\nSponsor Message\nBecome an NPR sponsor", "date": "Mon, 28 Sep 2026 22:16:19 -0400"}, {"title": "Could a far left candidate win a midterm race in a red state like Florida?", "url": "https://www.npr.org/2026/09/28/nx-s1-5961354/could-a-far-left-candidate-win-a-midterm-race-in-a-red-state-like-florida", "body": "By\nCatherine Welch\nAngie Nixon, the Democratic U.S. Senate Candidate in Florida, is running from the far left \u2014 raising questions about whether such a campaign can work in a red state like Florida.\nJUANA SUMMERS, HOST:It is tough for Democrats to win in Florida, in statewide contests, at least. So when the progressive candidate won over a more moderate candidate in last month's Democratic primary for the U.S. Senate, it sent the party scrambling. Catherine Welch reports.CATHERINE WELCH, BYLINE: To understand the political headwinds Democrats in Florida are facing, just look at voter registration. There are 1.5 million more registered Republicans than Democrats, and a Democrat hasn't won a statewide election since 2018. This is the Florida the Democratic U.S. Senate candidate Angie Nixon faces as she campaigns from the left with a message of Medicare for all and taxing billionaires. Her message got Gabe Henson's vote in the primary.GABE HENSON: She's a very grassroots kind of candidate.WELCH: Henson's at the weekly farmers market in downtown Orlando, where kettle corn gets scooped out of a big metal bowl and served with lemonade.(SOUNDBITE OF KETTLE CORN BEING SCOOPED)WELCH: He's excited about her candidacy and yet mixed about her chances of winning.HENSON: I really hope so. I'm optimistic but, you know, worried. It's Florida, you know?WELCH: Over the past eight years, outgoing Governor Ron DeSantis' war on woke has influenced the state's Republican voters. Sipping a drink under a tree, Justin and Jessica Edwards wonder what it would take for a Democrat to win a statewide office in this environment.JUSTIN EDWARDS: I don't know what really will work. I don't know what will get people to change their mind and get away from the status quo. Like, I don't know what it's going to take.JESSICA EDWARDS: Well, you have to be loud, and you have to say directly what you want to happen, and people get behind you.WELCH: Former University of South Florida political science professor Susan MacManus has studied Florida politics for decades. She's watching to see how Nixon's strategy plays out. First, she says, Democrats need more than just the party faithful to show up in November.SUSAN MACMANUS: Pull some crossover votes from Republicans and then really make a heavy inroad into the almost 30% of Floridians who are registered as no party affiliation or with one of the minor parties.WELCH: Another challenge for Nixon, she says, is the socialist label, especially for voters from immigrant groups that fled socialist governments.MACMANUS: Knowing the makeup of Florida and the Hispanic populations down in South Florida that are really negative towards socialism, to put it bluntly.WELCH: Nixon joined the Democratic Socialists of America in June. In a recent interview with NPR, when asked how she would describe herself, she replied...(SOUNDBITE OF ARCHIVED NPR CONTENT)ANGIE NIXON: I label myself as a Democrat, as a former single mom, as someone who was determined to help improve the quality of life for all Floridians and all Americans in this country.WELCH: As MacManus watches to see if Nixon can distance herself from the socialist label, Republican strategist Brett Doster predicts challenger Ashley Moody's campaign will hammer it.BRETT DOSTER: I think that they're going to paint the Democrats' socialist agenda in its bleakest of terms.WELCH: And that's what Moody did on a recent appearance on Fox News.(SOUNDBITE OF ARCHIVED RECORDING)ASHLEY MOODY: If she were elected, would be the most radical, extreme senator ever to step foot on the United States Senate floor.WELCH: To fight back, Nixon will need money to run ads in Florida's multiple media markets. She entered the primary raising just shy of a million dollars, while Moody has some 11 million. Between Angie Nixon's ground game and Ashley Moody's war chest, MacManus says turnout will be the deciding factor in this midterm race with two politically savvy candidates.For NPR News, I'm Catherine Welch in Orlando, Florida.(SOUNDBITE OF COMMON SONG, \"THEY SAY\")Copyright \u00a9 2026 NPR.  All rights reserved.  Visit our websiteterms of useandpermissionspages atwww.npr.orgfor further information.Accuracy and availability of NPR transcripts may vary. Transcript text may be revised to correct errors or match updates to audio. Audio on npr.org may be edited after its original broadcast or publication. The authoritative record of NPR\u2019s programming is the audio record.\nJUANA SUMMERS, HOST:It is tough for Democrats to win in Florida, in statewide contests, at least. So when the progressive candidate won over a more moderate candidate in last month's Democratic primary for the U.S. Senate, it sent the party scrambling. Catherine Welch reports.CATHERINE WELCH, BYLINE: To understand the political headwinds Democrats in Florida are facing, just look at voter registration. There are 1.5 million more registered Republicans than Democrats, and a Democrat hasn't won a statewide election since 2018. This is the Florida the Democratic U.S. Senate candidate Angie Nixon faces as she campaigns from the left with a message of Medicare for all and taxing billionaires. Her message got Gabe Henson's vote in the primary.GABE HENSON: She's a very grassroots kind of candidate.WELCH: Henson's at the weekly farmers market in downtown Orlando, where kettle corn gets scooped out of a big metal bowl and served with lemonade.(SOUNDBITE OF KETTLE CORN BEING SCOOPED)WELCH: He's excited about her candidacy and yet mixed about her chances of winning.HENSON: I really hope so. I'm optimistic but, you know, worried. It's Florida, you know?WELCH: Over the past eight years, outgoing Governor Ron DeSantis' war on woke has influenced the state's Republican voters. Sipping a drink under a tree, Justin and Jessica Edwards wonder what it would take for a Democrat to win a statewide office in this environment.JUSTIN EDWARDS: I don't know what really will work. I don't know what will get people to change their mind and get away from the status quo. Like, I don't know what it's going to take.JESSICA EDWARDS: Well, you have to be loud, and you have to say directly what you want to happen, and people get behind you.WELCH: Former University of South Florida political science professor Susan MacManus has studied Florida politics for decades. She's watching to see how Nixon's strategy plays out. First, she says, Democrats need more than just the party faithful to show up in November.SUSAN MACMANUS: Pull some crossover votes from Republicans and then really make a heavy inroad into the almost 30% of Floridians who are registered as no party affiliation or with one of the minor parties.WELCH: Another challenge for Nixon, she says, is the socialist label, especially for voters from immigrant groups that fled socialist governments.MACMANUS: Knowing the makeup of Florida and the Hispanic populations down in South Florida that are really negative towards socialism, to put it bluntly.WELCH: Nixon joined the Democratic Socialists of America in June. In a recent interview with NPR, when asked how she would describe herself, she replied...(SOUNDBITE OF ARCHIVED NPR CONTENT)ANGIE NIXON: I label myself as a Democrat, as a former single mom, as someone who was determined to help improve the quality of life for all Floridians and all Americans in this country.WELCH: As MacManus watches to see if Nixon can distance herself from the socialist label, Republican strategist Brett Doster predicts challenger Ashley Moody's campaign will hammer it.BRETT DOSTER: I think that they're going to paint the Democrats' socialist agenda in its bleakest of terms.WELCH: And that's what Moody did on a recent appearance on Fox News.(SOUNDBITE OF ARCHIVED RECORDING)ASHLEY MOODY: If she were elected, would be the most radical, extreme senator ever to step foot on the United States Senate floor.WELCH: To fight back, Nixon will need money to run ads in Florida's multiple media markets. She entered the primary raising just shy of a million dollars, while Moody has some 11 million. Between Angie Nixon's ground game and Ashley Moody's war chest, MacManus says turnout will be the deciding factor in this midterm race with two politically savvy candidates.For NPR News, I'm Catherine Welch in Orlando, Florida.(SOUNDBITE OF COMMON SONG, \"THEY SAY\")Copyright \u00a9 2026 NPR.  All rights reserved.  Visit our websiteterms of useandpermissionspages atwww.npr.orgfor further information.Accuracy and availability of NPR transcripts may vary. Transcript text may be revised to correct errors or match updates to audio. Audio on npr.org may be edited after its original broadcast or publication. The authoritative record of NPR\u2019s programming is the audio record.\nIt is tough for Democrats to win in Florida, in statewide contests, at least. So when the progressive candidate won over a more moderate candidate in last month's Democratic primary for the U.S. Senate, it sent the party scrambling. Catherine Welch reports.CATHERINE WELCH, BYLINE: To understand the political headwinds Democrats in Florida are facing, just look at voter registration. There are 1.5 million more registered Republicans than Democrats, and a Democrat hasn't won a statewide election since 2018.