oesn't raise the same concerns as these overt political ads targeting the midterms.FADEL: If these ads do violate federal law, what can be done to enforce it?EISEN: Well, as is so often the case, when Donald Trump oversteps, there's a role for the courts to play, and also the American people. They hate this. He's taking money from them to run these ads. So court of law, court of public opinion.FADEL: Norm Eisen served as White House ethics czar in the Obama administration. He is also a co-founder of the Democracy Defenders Fund. Thank you for your time.EISEN: Thank you, Leila.(SOUNDBITE OF MUSIC)Copyright \u00a9 2026 NPR.  All rights reserved.  Visit our websiteterms of useandpermissionspages atwww.npr.orgfor further information.Accuracy and availability of NPR transcripts may vary. Transcript text may be revised to correct errors or match updates to audio. Audio on npr.org may be edited after its original broadcast or publication. The authoritative record of NPR\u2019s programming is the audio record.\nINSKEEP: As of Sunday, AdImpact said the government paid more than $918,000 to air this commercial across the country. Two additional ads have also come out in the last week, including this.(SOUNDBITE OF ARCHIVED RECORDING)TRUMP: We will cast out the communists, Marxists and fascists. We will throw off the sick political class that hates our country.INSKEEP: That's essentially a 2024 Trump campaign ad with an added disclosure that the new version was paid for by taxpayers.FADEL: Democratic appropriators in the House and Senate sent a letter to the White House calling for the first ad to be pulled. They argue it violates a federal ethics law and an appropriations law, which prevents taxpayer funds from being used to produce government publicity or propaganda.INSKEEP: Now, on Friday, the White House told NPR, quote, \"the announcements are very clearly not campaign ads.\" We followed up and asked about the repurposing of that 2024 campaign ad. In a new statement, they 