ltiple laws. You cannot run political ads. You cannot run propaganda ads. So these two advertisements are so obviously political, coming in campaign season. One of them is actually a recycle of a political ad. There's no question here. The law is violated.FADEL: What do you make of the Trump administration's defense that this is allowed because it's not a presidential election, he's not on the ballot and they're not explicitly asking viewers to do anything?EISEN: Well, it's so ridiculous. Obviously, he's out stumping for candidates. Obviously, his failures to control costs in the United States because of the, in part, corruption scandals where he's taking money out of the pockets of Americans, not to mention abroad, the disastrous Iran war driving gas and diesel prices to the roof - his conduct is central. He's out stumping in the midterms. The timing, the content, the fact that it's an old political ad that's being played again for one of these two is so obviously against the law.FADEL: Are you considering a lawsuit? Is your group considering a lawsuit?EISEN: Well, at Democracy Defenders Fund and Democracy Defenders Action, we are not shy, with over 300 legal matters, including some of the landmark wins stopping Donald Trump's illegality. All options are on the table. We worked actively on this through the weekend. Stay tuned.FADEL: Now, the Trump administration says that other administrations have promoted their own priorities in public service announcements, including in the Obama administration in which you served. How are these ads different?EISEN: Well (laughter), they're different because those were public service announcements. Sometimes they went a little too far. The Bush administration did an ad touting the advantages for the elderly of some of its healthcare policies, but nothing like this. No one has ever dreamed, because of these laws of doing overt political ads, so that's apples and oranges. I grew up on the Smokey the Bear ads, but Smokey the Bear was never running for office.FADEL: Now, there are other commercials that have raised concerns. There was an ad from the Federal National Mortgage Association, or Fannie Mae, that came out in January. I just want to listen to it.(SOUNDBITE OF ARCHIVED RECORDING)UNIDENTIFIED NARRATOR: President Donald J. Trump has built a strong economy and is revitalizing the American dream. He will go down as the greatest housing president in history.TRUMP: America was...FADEL: Do you see legal or ethical issues with this ad?EISEN: Well, I never would have approved it when I was the ethics czar in the Obama White House. The same is true with my colleagues from other administrations - Richard Painter - I talked to him about it - in the Bush White House. But the content and the timing of it are different than these ads that are coming now in September as we round to the midterm elections. So it's not a good one, but doesn't raise the same concerns as these overt political ads targeting the midterms.FADEL: If these ads do violate federal law, what can be done to enforce it?EISEN: Well, as is so often the case, when Donald Trump oversteps, there's a role for the courts to play, and also the American people. They hate this. He's taking money from them to run these ads. So court of law, court of public opinion.FADEL: Norm Eisen served as White House ethics czar in the Obama administration. He is also a co-founder of the Democracy Defenders Fund. Thank you for your time.EISEN: Thank you, Leila.(SOUNDBITE OF MUSIC)Copyright \u00a9 2026 NPR.  All rights reserved.  Visit our websiteterms of useandpermissionspages atwww.npr.orgfor further information.Accuracy and availability of NPR transcripts may vary. Transcript text may be revised to correct errors or match updates to audio. Audio on npr.org may be edited after its original broadcast or publication. The authoritative record of NPR\u2019s programming is the audio record.\nFADEL: For more on this, we're joined by Norm Eisen. He served as the