www.wsj.com/us-news/trading-cards-worth-1-million-stolen-from-fedex-truck-in-pokemon-heist-c524d19c?mod=rss_usnews", "body": "POPULAR ARTICLES\nLATEST PODCASTS\nView All", "date": "Tue, 20 Oct 2026 21:23:00 GMT"}, {"title": "Former OpenAI Worker Accused of Bugging Discussed Jobs With Saudi Royal", "url": "https://www.wsj.com/tech/ai/former-openai-worker-accused-of-bugging-discussed-jobs-with-saudi-royal-277c1c45?mod=rss_usnews", "body": "POPULAR ARTICLES\nLATEST PODCASTS\nView All", "date": "Mon, 19 Oct 2026 21:52:00 GMT"}, {"title": "The Deadly 1.5-Mile Corridor That Shows the Fatal Flaw in America\u2019s Road Design", "url": "https://www.wsj.com/us-news/the-deadly-1-5-mile-corridor-that-shows-the-fatal-flaw-in-americas-road-design-8f424579?mod=rss_usnews", "body": "POPULAR ARTICLES\nLATEST PODCASTS\nView All", "date": "Sun, 18 Oct 2026 23:00:00 GMT"}, {"title": "The Financial\u2014and Psychological\u2014Cost of Being the Friend Who Picks Up the Tab", "url": "https://www.wsj.com/personal-finance/splitting-bills-reimbursement-young-adults-a8a33405?mod=rss_usnews", "body": "POPULAR ARTICLES\nLATEST PODCASTS\nView All", "date": "Wed, 21 Oct 2026 23:04:00 GMT"}, {"title": "The Pentagon's guidelines for \"responsible AI\" aren't always followed, report shows", "url": "https://www.npr.org/2026/10/23/nx-s1-6012296/ai-department-of-defense-war", "body": "Chiara Eisner\nThree of the Pentagon projects surveyed by GAO included weapons for counter-camouflage (upper image) and target recognition (lower images).Government Accountability Office/GAOhide caption\nThe Pentagon is building weapons and projects with artificial intelligence capabilities that officials say don't always closely adhere to the agency's own guidelines for AI, according to a congressional watchdogreportpublished in October.\nAI researchers and international organizations have longadvocatedfor strict regulation of AI when used in weapons that could become unpredictable or function with little human control, arguing that those tools could endanger the lives of soldiers and civilians and risk quickly escalating wars. But Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth has repeatedly dismissed previous caution of AI tools and encouraged rapid autonomous innovation at the Pentagon.\nThe Pentagon issued amemoin January saying the department would become \"an 'AI-first' warfighting force across all components, from front to back.\" Last month, Hegsethannounceda new warfare command focused on autonomous weapon development, including drones.\nSecretary of Defense Pete Hegseth arrives on stage for a discussion during an annual Army meeting on October 14, 2026 in Washington, DC.Kevin Dietsch/Getty Images North Americahide caption\nThe watchdog report, published by the U.S. Government Accountability Office on Oct. 14, grants a rare look inside how those kinds of projects are being developed.\nThe GAO surveyed 10 officials involved with building 10 weapons and tools for the Pentagon that use AI to automatically identify targets on the battlefield, process surveillance and improve tactical decision making, and other tasks. The projects are among more than300being built with AI in the Pentagon.\nWhen responding to the GAO survey, six of the 10 officials said their teams had \"identified risk(s)\" with using the projects \"in an operational environment.\"\nTo address some of those sorts of risks, in 2021, the Pentagon sent a memo outlining six basic tenets to ensure that weapons built with AI are created responsibly. Those six guidelines mainly stress the importance of Pentagon-wide oversight, verification and \"real-time monitoring\" of the technology, and cooperation with allies \"to advance global norms grounded in shared values.\"\nAlissa Czyz, who directed the GAO review, said the tenets evolved from earlier ethical principles, and while they weren't rules, they were understood to be important by people involved with building AI tools at the Pentagon.\n\"It's a memo, right, at the end of the day, so it's not a department instruction,\" said Czyz. \"It's something that the department has signaled that should be implemented.\"\nMost of the people involved with making the 10 weapons and projects reviewed by GAO had taken \"at least initial steps\" to follow the six tenets outlined in the memo, according to the report, and some of the projects were in early stages, Czyz clarified.\nBut some of the responses indicated officials weren't always closely following the guidelines.\nFour officials told the GAO that requirements for the weapons and projects they were working on weren't \"aligned with Responsible AI tenets.\" And three of the 10 officials told the GAO they \"don't know\" if the projects will be continuously monitored after they're deployed, though the six tenets encourage monitoring.\nOfficials told the GAO that more access to expertise about the agency's guidelines or staff who know about them would increase their ability to incorporate AI responsibly. But the group that helps manage education on the Pentagon's guidelines, the Department of the Responsible AI Division, is understaffed. Although the group was authorized to hire 10 people in 2023, as of February, only three of those positions were filled and the Pentagon \"is unlikely to fill vacant positions or increase staffing,\" the report states.\n\"That's a very small number of folks tasked with a large responsibility of responsible AI throughout the department,\" said Czyz.\nThe GAO issued two recommendations: The Pentagon should more clearly outline the responsibilities and meeting schedules of the office charged with overseeing AI regulation, and address understaffing. Although the Pentagon agreed with those recommendations, as of Oct. 22, the agency had not implemented those changes, a GAO representative confirmed to NPR.\nNPR asked the Pentagon when it planned to make those changes, but did not receive a response.\nWhen delivering a speech at SpaceX in January, Hegseth indicated he believed AI development at the Pentagon should not be slowed by those sorts of steps.\n\"Our enterprise currently operates on staffing and committee cycles measured in months and years, and that's unacceptable,\" said Hegseth. \"Military AI is going to be a race for the foreseeable future where the risks to U.S. national security of moving too slowly outweigh the impacts of imperfect alignment.\"\nNPR would like to hear from more people who work with federal agencies or AI companies and have information to share about how agencies are using AI. You can email the reporter of this article, Chiara Eisner, at ceisner@npr.org or contact her on Signalhere, username ceis.78.\nNPR does not offer or accept money for coverage or interviews.Anyone who makes such an offer or request is not an NPR employee and does not represent NPR.\nSponsor Message\nBecome an NPR sponsor", "date": "Fri, 23 Oct 2026 15:31:15 -0400"}, {"title": "Ohio could decide control of the Senate. Its rural voters might decide the winner", "url": "https://www.npr.org/2026/10/23/nx-s1-6012809/ohio-senate-governor-election-rural-voters", "body": "Saige Miller\nChris Gibbs, a cattle farmer in rural Ohio, says he's been in a deficit for almost three years.Keren Carri\u00f3n/NPRhide caption\nAt the weekday farmers market in Athens, Ohio, colorful vegetables are on display for patrons to purchase, community members interact with each other and vendors and one another. It feels like most people in attendance know one another on some personal level.\nFor a small town like Athens, the farmers market is a gathering place.\n\"Our Saturday markets have been fully at capacity,\" said Paige Walters, the Athens farmers market promotions manager.\nAthens, Ohio is home to Ohio University. The city is, like many college towns, pretty liberal. But that's not the case in most of the Appalachian communities in the region.\nWalters noticed that this year, for the first time in a while, the Athens County Republican Party has set up a table at the market.\n\"I think that does speak to the political climate right now \u2013 that it's pretty competitive,\" she said.\nDespite its past as a swing state, Ohio is now considered to be reliably Republican. This election is a little different. With toss-up races for governor and U.S. Senate, Democrats have a chance to win statewide.\nBut if Democrats want to win, they have to capture voters who voted for Republicans in the past. That includes the rural Ohio voter base, which historically leans conservative.\nWhile rural voters nationwide have voted red in the past (they backed Trump in 2024 by 40 points), recent polling has shown his approval rating in rural America dropping dramatically amid high inflation, tariffs and the war in Iran.\nDemocrats are trying to gain ground in the unexpected areas. That's especially true in the competitive Senate match up between Sen. Jon Husted, the Republican nominee, and former Democratic Sen. Sherrod Brown as both candidates invest in outreach to rural parts of the state.\n\"There's a lot of people in rural Ohio that are not happy, whether it's gas prices, diesel prices, the tariffs, the war,\" Matthew White of Athens told NPR at the farmers market.\nMulticolored bell peppers, onions and squash line the table at the Cowdery Farms booth. While the weekday market usually has slower foot traffic, farmer Larry Cowdery sold a good amount of produce.\nHe owns a small vegetable farm along the Ohio River in Long Bottom. There are two big concerns at the top of Cowdrey's mind. The first is the cost of healthcare.\n\"The big, big issue that we run into is since we're self-employed, we have to find health insurance somewhere,\" he told NPR. \"It's going to be much more expensive next year.\"\nHealthcare premiums on the Affordable Care Act marketplace are expected to increaseby roughly 15% in Ohio, in part because of the expiration of federal subsidies. Republicans, including Husted, voted to eliminate the subsidies under the Trump-backed \"One Big Beautiful Bill.\" As a result of sticker shock, Ohio saw the biggest drop \u2014 tied with Oklahoma for the most in the nation \u2014 in the number of people enrolled in the marketplace last year, with a nearly 33% decline in enrollment.\nCowdery's second concern is the survival of his multigenerational family farm. It's becoming too expensive to run. He said the cost of electricity and gasoline is a big factor. Plus, he relies on fertilizer to grow his vegetables.\n\"I'm not convinced we're even going to be able to get fertilizer next year,\" Cowdery said.\nThat's because of the war in Iran. Most of the nitrogen used in fertilizer travels through the Strait of Hormuz, a vital shipping channel in the Middle East controlled by Iran. Travel through the Strait has been volatile.\nAs a result of the healthcare and business hardships, Cowdery isn't keen on voting Republican this year. He added that he hasn't been happy with the Republican-led Ohio legislature for the last few years and he doesn't like Husted, either.\n\"I don't trust Jon Husted at all. I think he's a crook,\" Cowdery said.\nDean Ferrell is at the farmers market chatting with people and milling around the market. The 62-year-old man, a lifelong resident of Athens, said the cost of gas, utilities and groceries have become burdensome. He has had to cut back on buying certain foods because he can't afford it.\n\"I mean, who would ever thought you'd be paying ten bucks a pound for ground beef? That used to be filet mignon prices, not ground beef,\" he said.\nFerrell tried to look on the upside of his financial reality. Since he's older, Ferrell said he doesn't eat as much as he used to, so he can save some money that way. He added that he wishes politicians could spend \"a week or two living like I do.\"\n\"Their attitude would be a lot different. I mean, I gotta save money wherever I can, buy reduced and on sale items,\" Ferrell said. \"There's very few things that I pay full price for. You got to, to survive.\"\nFerrell considers himself to be an independent. While he hasn't decided who to vote for the Senate, Ferrell said he is leaning toward Husted. He doesn't like the direction the Democratic party is headed.\n\"The far left has basically hijacked the Democrat Party,\" he said.\nOn a crisp fall day in rural central Ohio, farmer Chris Gibbs climbs into his John Deere utility terrain vehicle, setting out to survey his land.\n\"It started in 1983 on our own with $2,500, a pickup truck and a two-year-old daughter,\" Gibbs said with a chuckle. \"So we built that up to 560 acres.\"\nGibbs, a first generation farmer in Shelby County, grows corn, soybeans, alfalfa hay and has a few dozen cows.\nFarming is a tough industry at the best of times. Farms are at the behest of market fluctuations, domestic policy, international relations and mother nature. Tack on high fuel and fertilizer prices, along with Trump's tariffs and the cost of production exceeds the profit.\nSitting in his huge combine that used to harvest corn and soybeans, Gibbs said the diesel fuel to run the machinery has increased 50% under the Trump administration and the war in Iran. He fills up the combine about seven times during the harvest season.\n\"I think about 150 gallons at $7 is $1,000, and that'll run a day and a half,\" Gibbs said. \"That'd be $7,000 worth of diesel fuel.\"\nPolitically, farmers tend to vote Republican. Gibbs was one of those Republicans at one point. In a past life, he was the chair of the Shelby County Republicans. He voted for Trump in 2016.\n\"I'm looking at certainly, buyer's remorse,\" Gibbs said about voting for Trump.\nGibbs couldn't stand behind Trump's character, his persona on the global stage or the punitive tariffs he said hurt \"United States of America's soft underbelly,\" referring to agriculture. He moved away from Trump in 2018.\nNow, in 2026, Gibbs is the chair of the Ohio Democratic Party Rural Caucus and he wants to send Democrat Sherrod Brown back to the Senate.\nBrown, Gibbs argued, has the experience necessary to represent rural Ohioans at the federal level.\n\"We're going to get Sherrod Brown across the line. I have complete confidence in that,\" Gibbs said.\nAt a Republican Party event in Guysville, Melissa Jant said she doesn't want a career politician representing her in Congress and that's exactly how she views Brown.\n\"I absolutely do not want Sherrod Brown back in there. He was there for 30 years. We saw the policies that he put in place. He ran the country down. He ran Ohio down. We just need new blood in there,\" she told NPR.\nVANDALIA, OHIO - OCTOBER 03: U.S. President Donald Trump (R) is joined by Sen Jon Husted, R-Ohio, on stage with President\u00a0Trump in Vandalia, Ohio on Oct. 3, 2026.Roberto Schmidt/Getty Imageshide caption\nVANDALIA, OHIO - OCTOBER 03: U.S. President Donald Trump (R) is joined by Sen Jon Husted, R-Ohio, on stage with President\u00a0Trump in Vandalia, Ohio on Oct. 3, 2026.Roberto Schmidt/Getty Imageshide caption\nPresident Trump spoke near Dayton recently, throwing his support behind Republican Sen. Jon Husted. His stop is part of a series of rallies to support Republicans heading into the midterm elections.\nHer displeasure toward Brown isn't just because he is a Democrat. Some voters view him as the establishment, despite not being a lawmaker anymore.\nJimmy Williams of Monroe County feels similarly. He said Brown was voted out and shouldn't try and head back to his old stomping grounds. Instead, Williams views Husted as the anti-establishment candidate, despite the fact that he is a sitting senator.\n\"He's a first-term senator there, and he hasn't even been in there long enough to do everything that they're saying on television,\" Williams said about ads aimed at Husted. \"And people need to be smart enough to look through that.\"\nBob Rouch, of Washington County told NPR he is optimistic that Ohio will stay Republican. He believes Trump has done a lot of good for the county and \"it's Ohio's responsibility to keep up with that.\" If Democrats win control, Rouch thinks they won't doing anything aside from target Trump.\n\"We're going to have these damn investigations, these attempted impeachments and all the baloney that we went through,\" he said, referring to the last time Democrats controlled Congress while Trump was president.\nWilliams argued preserving Republican control of Congress is the goal right now.\n\"America is at risk,\" Williams said.\nNPR does not offer or accept money for coverage or interviews.Anyone who makes such an offer or request is not an NPR employee and does not represent NPR.\nSponsor Message\nBecome an NPR sponsor", "date": "Fri, 23 Oct 2026 14:30:00 -0400"}, {"title": "A D.C. park was made 'for the pleasure of the people.' Trump's plan would change that", "url": "https://www.npr.org/2026/10/23/nx-s1-6010488/east-potomac-trump-golf-hains-point", "body": "By\nRachel Treisman\nLocals rally against Trump's potential redevelopment of East Potomac Park at a protest in early October. Runners, cyclists, birders and more worry they will lose access to the park under Trump's plan to overhaul the municipal golf course.Saul Loeb/AFP via Getty Imageshide caption\nWASHINGTON, D.C. \u2014 President Trump's express wish totake over the East Potomac Golf Linkshas thrust a municipal D.C. golf course into the national spotlight. But locals rely on that area for much more than golfing \u2014 and worry the district could lose an invaluable recreational oasis.\nThe southern tip of East Potomac Park, known as Hains Point, is beloved by cyclists, runners, picnickers and birders for its waterfront views, cherry trees and Ohio Drive, the scenic three-mile loop around its perimeter. Runners use the wide, flat road to train; it's even a fixture of several major race courses, including theMarine Corps Marathonon Sunday.\n\"It's such an important part of the fabric of this region, even though it's not very well known [beyond it],\" said Elizabeth Kiker, the executive director of the Washington Area Bicyclists Association. She herself took her now-husband on a bike ride on their second date 20 years ago.\nLocals describe Hains Point as a rare public resource in D.C. It's easily accessible from the National Mall yet removed from the crowds, full of people but free of traffic. Congresscreated it in 1897, \"to be forever held and used as a park for the recreation and pleasure of the people.\"\nAnd yet, under the president's proposed golf course redesign, Hains Point as people know and love it may cease to exist.\nEast Potomac Golf Links and Hains Point as seen from the top of the Washington Monument this week.Saul Loeb/AFP via Getty Imageshide caption\nTrumpsaid on Truth Sociallast weekend that renovations would keep the entire park closed for 15 months starting in November. But it's not clear what would be left when it reopens: The rendering he posted shows an 18-hole course spanning the entire peninsula \u2014 including the current playground and picnic area on its southern tip \u2014 with no road visible around the perimeter.\nThe White House referred NPR to the Department of the Interior, which did not respond to NPR's questions about whether those features would be removed as part of the redesign. The proposed renovations have so far been the subject of mixed messages from the administration: Trump said last weekend that work would begin in mid-November, then \"immediately.\" But government lawyers said in court Monday that they werenot aware of a final plan.\nEven so, the government abruptly downedhundreds of treeson the course with little warning earlier this month, which critics worry was a pretext for beginning renovations. (The government says it was following up on recommendations from a 2019 landscaping report.) Judge Ana Reyes held a hearing on Monday to learn more about the tree-clearing and decide whether to block further work on the golf course.\nBut it's not just the golf course that's at stake, as a group called Hains Point Runners told Reyes in the courtroom and a written brief. In its 35-page brief, the group says federal law requires the National Park Service to identify and consider the \"longstanding recreational uses of the Hains Point corridor\" and ways to avoid harming those uses, before embarking on any work that would change it.\n\"From the perspective of the running community, that did not occur here,\" lawyers wrote.\nAshley Hawkins of Prince George's County, Md., runs at Hains Point every day, even in the snow. She especially loves the river view and people watching: the families with fishing poles, binocular-wielding birders, people taking a leisurely drive.\n\"Running is so tied to my mental health,\" Hawkins said on a recent sunny morning, stretching before she hit the road. \"And no place in this area has given me the peace that Hains Point has.\"\nHawkins has been following the headlines about her beloved running route. While she says she's still in denial about its potential closure, she sees the prospect as part of a pattern of Trump \"bypassing\" courts toadvance his construction projects.\n\"But to me, this feels the most egregious, because he's taking away public space and trying to make it private,\" she says. \"It feels like one of the more king-like things that he's done so far.\"\nTrump's proposal would combine the park's three historic \u2014 and historically affordable \u2014 courses into one 18-hole \"championship-style\" course, which has already raised concerns about who will be able to play on it. Trumphas saidon Truth Social the golf course will \"be open to the Public\" while also able to host \"world class tournaments\" such as the U.S. Open and PGA Championship. He's called the current course \"dilapidated, and of no further use or effect,\" dubbing part of it \"Trash Island.\"\nEach spring, when D.C.'s iconic cherry blossoms bloom, Hains Point is a fan-favorite viewing spot for both locals and tourists. The annual Credit Union Cherry Blossom 10-mile run takes roughly 20,000 participantsaround the peninsulafor that very reason.\nBut race organizers are looking into backup options for next year given the rumored renovations, event director Rob Wolfe told NPR.\nIn an email to prospective runners earlier this year, the organization said both the proposed golf course renovations andTrump's planned archon nearby Arlington Memorial Bridge could significantly alter the course. It urged people to share their concerns with lawmakers and the National Park Service.\nHains Point is part of the course of several major road races, including the Credit Union Cherry Blossom ten-mile run each April and the Marine Corps Marathon every October.Andrew Harnik/APhide caption\nWolfe said the best race experience includes as many cherry blossoms as possible \u2014 ideally the ones that frame the park. But, he says, the park's future is \"more important than our Sunday in April or Marine Corps Marathon's Sunday in October.\"\n\"It's really important that people understand that if this roadway goes away, hundreds, thousands of people on a daily basis would lose out,\" he said.\nLuke Cancilla, a runner and physical therapist who works primarily with endurance runners and cyclists, said Hains Point is a particularly safe environment because there are fewer obstacles such as traffic, stoplights and sidewalks. He often recommends it for people who are starting out or returning after an injury.\n\"It's open to all of us, which makes it so unique,\" he said. \"You don't have to have an experience level \u2026 or be intimated to go out there.\"\nThe park is a popular spot for fishing in the Potomac River.Andrew Harnik/APhide caption\nSummer Delehanty, an avid birder, found out about Hains Point when she moved to the area for work about a year ago. She said it's played a huge role in building her D.C. community.\n\"I've only been here a year, but I still have so many fond memories of going there,\" she said. \"I was there January 1st looking for thesnowy owl\u2026 I've been there on super hot days, I've picnicked there.\"\nDelehanty has spotted all kinds of birds at that park, including rarities such as the fork-tailed flycatcher. She said Hains Point is a uniquely great birdwatching spot in the district because of its position right along the Potomac River: People have a 360-degree view of the migrating birds that fly overhead or take shelter in the trees.\n\"There's nothing else in D.C. like it,\" she said, adding that she's not sure where regular birders would go instead. They could perhaps get similar views from the Virginia side of the river, she said, but those wouldn't count as D.C. bird sightings.\nThe Trump administrationcut down 430of the park's trees earlier this month, with less than a day's notice. That means fewer places for tired birds to rest, Delehanty says, and potentially fewer birds stopping by altogether. And, if the park is closed to humans, there won't be anyone there to track the species that do, which she said is important for conservation purposes.\nMar\u00eda-Elena Montero, president of the D.C. Bird Alliance, says the potential closure of Hains Point would mean a \"devastating loss of habitat\" for birds and humans alike.\n\"It belongs to the people,\" she said. \"And to lose that aspect of being able to experience such a gorgeous space right in the middle of the city, right in the shadow of an airport and monuments \u2026 is a big loss for everybody.\"\nLast October, the Trump administration started dumping debris from another controversial construction project \u2014 the demolition of theWhite House East Wing\u2014 on the grounds of the golf course. In February, the D.C. Preservation League and two local golfers sued the government in an effort to stop what they describe as an unlawful takeover of a public course.\nFear and confusion grew this summer, when Trump was photographed touring East Potomac with a group including golf course architect Tom Fazio and staffers holding rolled-up renderings. Trump later announced that Fazio would design a new championship-level course, with work set to begin on September 1.\nWhen Delehanty saw that news, she realized \"this involves me directly,\" she said. She decided to put her design skills and sizablesocial media followingto use, designing \"Hands off Hains\" T-shirts to raise awareness and money for the D.C. Preservation League. She says she's sold around 200 so far, raising about $1,500.\nPresident Trump and officials toured the East Potomac golf course in June, fueling locals' concerns that construction would soon begin. Trump said this week that work would close the park starting in mid-November, though a plan has not been finalized.Mark Schiefelbein/APhide caption\n\"In the grand scheme of things, against an administration, is that going to do anything in court? Not really,\" she said. \"But for me, it's about [the fact] these are 200 people that care so deeply about this they're willing to \u2026 wear a shirt, show up to these events and say 'This is not something I'm happy about.'\"\nThe push to protect the park is grassroots \u2013 and growing.\nThere's Save East Po, a nonprofit started by a resident named Alex Rosen who works as a consultant and described himself to NPR as \"just a guy who really likes a park and doesn't want to see its fundamental characters change significantly, especially without any public input.\"\nThe D.C. Bird Alliance is leading a series of daily \"Hold the Point!\" bird walks next week. And the Washington Area Bicyclists Association has held several installments of a combined bike-ride rally called \"Hains Point Hoopla.\"\nEast Potomac Park is a fan-favorite spot for locals and tourists alike to enjoy the cherry blossoms each spring.Alex Wong/Getty Imageshide caption\nThe most recent Hoopla, held earlier this month, featured speakers includingDavid Hearn, the former Olympian falsely accused of damaging the Lincoln Memorial Reflecting Pool \u2014 and an avid cyclist.\nCritics see a throughline in Trump's D.C. construction projects.\n\"It goes to this overall theme of potentially taking away from the local communities, making things a little bit more challenging and having us modify things in order for something that no one really asked for in the first place,\" said Cancilla, the running coach.\nHe's one of many who would like to see more input from the community about the changes to East Potomac. Critics of Trump's plan say there are steps the government can take to improve the park \u2014 such as adding benches and fixing the crumbling seawall \u2014 without risking access to public resources.\nAmidst the court battle and confusion, locals are at least heartened to see their beloved park getting more national attention. At a time when its future feels out of their hands, they're encouraging people to speak up and show up.\nThat's no problem for Hawkins, who says she plans to keep running at Hains Point every day \"until they physically tell me that I can't come anymore.\" Where would she go then?\n\"I'm not ready to answer that question,\" she said. \"I feel really tied to this place.\"\nNPR does not offer or accept money for coverage or interviews.Anyone who makes such an offer or request is not an NPR employee and does not represent NPR.\nSponsor Message\nBecome an NPR sponsor", "date": "Fri, 23 Oct 2026 13:35:14 -0400"}, {"title": "Ex-FBI employees charged with lying to Congress over Hunter Biden inquiries", "url": "https://www.npr.org/2026/10/23/g-s1-149534/justice-fbi-charged-hunter-biden-laptop", "body": "Ryan Lucas\nHunter Biden, son of former President Joe Biden, seen in July 2024 in Washington, DC.Anna Moneymaker/Getty Imageshide caption\nThe Justice Department has charged two former FBI employees with making false statements to Congress in connection with the bureau's investigations into Hunter Biden and his laptop ahead of the 2020 presidential election.\nBradley Benavides and Nikki Floris each face a single criminal count stemming from testimony before lawmakers in 2023. The pair, who held senior positions on the FBI's now defunct Foreign Influence Task Force, both turned themselves in and made their initial court appearance Friday morning in Washington, D.C.\nThe new prosecution is the latestpolitically fraughtcase to be brought by the Justice Department since President Trump returned to the White House. It dredges back up the bizarre saga surrounding former President Joe Biden's son, Hunter Biden, and his various legal troubles that featured throughout the 2020 presidential campaign.\nHunter Biden's laptop was the subject of intense scrutiny afterThe New York Postreported weeks before the 2020 presidential election that the device was abandoned at a Delaware repair shop in 2019, and then obtained by President Trump's former personal lawyer Rudy Giuliani.\nRepublicans have long accused the FBI of mishandling the laptop and the investigations into the younger Biden. Hunter Biden was convicted offederal gun chargesand later pleaded guilty to separate tax offenses. (Joe Biden signed afull and unconditional pardonfor his son in December 2024.)\nIn astatement on X, the U.S. attorney for Washington, D.C., Jeanine Pirro, said Floris and Benavides \"allegedly lied to Congress about their roles in the FBI's Hunter Biden investigations, with one claiming they only learned about it from Wikipedia \u2014 despite evidence showing their involvement in helping shape the public narrative about Hunter Biden's laptop right before the election.\"\nProsecutors charged Benavides and Floris by criminal complaint, which means prosecutors have yet to secure an indictment from a grand jury. Under the second Trump administration, grand juries in the nation's capital and other citieshave refused on several occasionsto return indictments in high-profile, politically charged cases.\nAt a court hearing Friday morning, Magistrate Judge Zia Faruqui noted that both of the defendants have \"sparkling backgrounds\" in federal law enforcement and were longtime dedicated public servants. He also noted that charging them by criminal complaint for conduct that is \"quite old\" is unusual.\nBenavides, 56, and Floris, 46, are due back in court for a preliminary hearing on Tuesday.\nIn a statement, Floris' attorneys called her a \"patriot\" who served as \"dedicated FBI intelligence officer for nearly 20 years.\"\n\"Yet today, the DOJ has accused her falsely of a crime on the say-so of politicians, not on the facts. It's no coincidence the government is filing these nonsense charges now, years later, on the eve of an election,\" they added.\nThePostreported Hunter Biden's laptop showed wrongdoing by then-Democratic nominee Joe Biden, who subsequently won the election. Some of the data on the laptop was later ascertained to belong to Hunter Biden. House Republicans subsequently launched animpeachment inquiryinto President Biden, focusing on Hunter Biden's business dealings at the time his father was vice president. But no evidence has ever been presented that Joe Biden financially gained from his son's business activities.\nThe complaint alleges that Benavides and Floris both had real-time knowledge of the Hunter Biden investigations, including into his laptop, in their top posts on the Foreign Influence Task Force.\nBut in interviews with the House Judiciary Committee in 2023, both of them allegedly lied about their knowledge and involvement in those investigations.\nThose purported false statements were material to the committee's investigation, the complaint says, because they allowed both Benavides and Floris to \"escape\" further questioning about Hunter Biden, which hindered the panel's investigation.\nNPR does not offer or accept money for coverage or interviews.Anyone who makes such an offer or request is not an NPR employee and does not represent NPR.\nSponsor Message\nBecome an NPR sponsor", "date": "Fri, 23 Oct 2026 11:51:04 -0400"}, {"title": "Military reinforcements move to Mideast. And, millions of Americans vote early", "url": "https://www.npr.org/2026/10/23/g-s1-149514/up-first-newsletter-iran-war-midterm-voting-trump-bbc", "body": "By\nBrittney Melton\nGood morning. You're reading the Up First newsletter.Subscribehere to get it delivered to your inbox, andlistento the Up First podcast for all the news you need to start your day.\nHeavier fighting in Iran could resume in the coming weeksas military reinforcements arrive and some U.S. personnel leave the region. This shift comes as a person briefed on the U.S. and Israel's war planning told NPR that the countries were preparing plans for a possible attack on Iran after the U.S. midterms. Iran saidit is prepared.\nTraffic in Tehran on Wednesday moves past an installation depicting butterflies forming the Iranian national flag.Atta Kenare/AFP via Getty Imageshide caption\nAlthough Election Day is more than a week away, almost 21 million Americans have already voted, either by mail or early in-person voting, according to the University of Florida's Election Lab.\nTrump must sit for a deposition in his $10 billion defamation caseagainst the BBC, a federal magistrate judge ruled. Trump is required to comply with court rules or face sanctions, the judge said yesterday. The case centers on aPanoramabroadcast from late October 2024, which allegedly edited the president's Jan. 6 speech to make it appear he incited a violent siege of Congress. Trump has described the broadcast as false, misleading and defamatory under Florida law.\nTrump promised to lower grocery prices in his second term. While some individual items have gotten cheaper since he returned to office, overall grocery prices have risen, driven by factors such as increased labor costs, tariffs and higher energy prices due to the conflict with Iran. Specific issues facing a single crop, animal species or geographic region can cause the price of one item to rise well above inflation, while lowering the price of another. NPRanalyzed five everyday food items\u2014 ground beef, eggs, coffee, butter and chocolate chip cookies \u2014 to better understand the math behind grocery store prices.\nRead more aboutwhy these prices have fluctuated here.\nPetter Mattei portrays stars in the Metropolitan Opera production ofLincoln in the Bardo, adapted from George Saunders' surreal novel about the president grieving the loss of his son in the midst of the Civil War.Karen Almond/Metropolitan Operahide caption\nCheck out whatNPRis watching, reading and listening to this weekend:\n\ud83c\udf7f Movies:A new film version of Jane Austen'sSense and Sensibilityfocuses on the Dashwood family's two oldest sisters, Elinor and Marianne, who face financial insecurity due to laws that disadvantage widows and daughters.\n\ud83d\udcfa TV:Actor Sharon Horgan stars as Alex, a 50-year-old divorc\u00e9e who finds herself at multiple personal and professional crossroads in HBO's new showYouth.\n\ud83d\udcda Books:Barbara Trapido'sBrother of the More Famous Jackfollows Katherine Browne, who is determined to escape her stiflingly conventional upbringing and create a life with fewer constraints.\n\ud83c\udfb5 Music:NPR'sNew Music Fridayhost Stephen Thompson breaks down the best albums released today, including Anderson .Paak and Cordae'sHeavy Is the Crownand Fenne Lily'sWin Win. Listen to a playlist of thebest songs from the new albums.\n\ud83c\udfad Theater:Missy Mazzoli's music brings George Saunders' Booker Prize-winning novel,Lincoln in the Bardo, to life in a stage adaptation at the Metropolitan Opera House in New York. Mazzoli is the first woman composer to have a world premiere on the Met stage.\nKarl Bushby, pictured here in Turkey in 2025, started his Goliath Expedition 28 years ago.Karl Bushbyhide caption\nThis newsletter was edited bySuzanne Nuyen.\nNPR does not offer or accept money for coverage or interviews.Anyone who makes such an offer or request is not an NPR employee and does not represent NPR.\nSponsor Message\nBecome an NPR sponsor", "date": "Fri, 23 Oct 2026 07:22:25 -0400"}, {"title": "Trump's midterm campaign tour stops in Iowa", "url": "https://www.npr.org/2026/10/23/nx-s1-6009838/trumps-midterm-campaign-tour-stops-in-iowa", "body": "From\nBy\nSheila Brummer\nRepublicans are facing a tough fight in Iowa. President Trump's campaign tour stopped in the state in hopes of rallying voters.\nA MART\u00cdNEZ, HOST:President Trump is on a whirlwind tour to rally support for Republicans in competitive midterm races. Iowa Public Radio's Sheila Brummer reports from Sioux City, where he campaigned last night.SHEILA BRUMMER, BYLINE: A circus-like atmosphere outside a hangar at the Sioux Gateway Airport, where vendors sell all things Trump. Among them was Ricardo Boyles (ph) with his colorful hats.RICARDO BOYLES: I have them in black, camo, pink, blue, red, green.BRUMMER: Boyles left a Trump rally in Greenville, South Carolina, Tuesday night to make it to Iowa by Thursday.BOYLES: One thousand three hundred and thirty-one miles, 21 hours and 40 minutes. It was fun. I love the cornfields.BRUMMER: Local supporters included Robert Byrne (ph), who says he bleeds red when it comes to politics.ROBERT BYRNE: My vote will be for Republicans up and down the ballot, every bit of it, because, for some reason, the choice this year is between sanity and insanity.BRUMMER: This is friendly territory for the president.(SOUNDBITE OF ARCHIVED RECORDING)PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: Well, thank you very much. I love this place.BRUMMER: Here in Iowa's 4th Congressional District, registered Republicans outnumber Democrats by more than 2 to 1. But republicans face a tougher fight statewide. Congresswoman Ashley Hinson is running for Senate against Democrat Josh Turek. A new poll conducted for the Cook Political Report puts Turek 2 percentage points ahead within the poll's margin of error. Trump urged people to get behind Hinson.(SOUNDBITE OF ARCHIVED RECORDING)TRUMP: She will never let far-left maniacs push the radical left insanity on Iowa's children. She's...BRUMMER: Trump also called on voters to elect Republican Zach Lahn as governor, who faces Democrat Rob Sand, Iowa's state auditor. Polls gives Sand an 11-point lead. While speaking for more than 90 minutes, Trump mainly talked about himself, touting tax cuts, his international policies and support for farmers. He riled people up by bringing up transgender rights and another favorite topic, fake news, multiple times. He also returned to his false claims that he won in 2020, and that he should be able to run again because...(SOUNDBITE OF ARCHIVED RECORDING)TRUMP: We won three times, but that's right. You know, I want the three wins, but I don't...BRUMMER: Trump plans to return to Iowa November 1, just two days before the midterms.For NPR News, I'm Sheila Brummer, Sioux City, Iowa.Copyright \u00a9 2026 NPR.  All rights reserved.  Visit our websiteterms of useandpermissionspages atwww.npr.orgfor further information.Accuracy and availability of NPR transcripts may vary. Transcript text may be revised to correct errors or match updates to audio. Audio on npr.org may be edited after its original broadcast or publication. The authoritative record of NPR\u2019s programming is the audio record.\nA MART\u00cdNEZ, HOST:President Trump is on a whirlwind tour to rally support for Republicans in competitive midterm races. Iowa Public Radio's Sheila Brummer reports from Sioux City, where he campaigned last night.SHEILA BRUMMER, BYLINE: A circus-like atmosphere outside a hangar at the Sioux Gateway Airport, where vendors sell all things Trump. Among them was Ricardo Boyles (ph) with his colorful hats.RICARDO BOYLES: I have them in black, camo, pink, blue, red, green.BRUMMER: Boyles left a Trump rally in Greenville, South Carolina, Tuesday night to make it to Iowa by Thursday.BOYLES: One thousand three hundred and thirty-one miles, 21 hours and 40 minutes. It was fun. I love the cornfields.BRUMMER: Local supporters included Robert Byrne (ph), who says he bleeds red when it comes to politics.ROBERT BYRNE: My vote will be for Republicans up and down the ballot, every bit of it, because, for some reason, the choice this year is between sanity and insanity.BRUMMER: This is friendly territory for the president.(SOUNDBITE OF ARCHIVED RECORDING)PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: Well, thank you very much. I love this place.BRUMMER: Here in Iowa's 4th Congressional District, registered Republicans outnumber Democrats by more than 2 to 1. But republicans face a tougher fight statewide. Congresswoman Ashley Hinson is running for Senate against Democrat Josh Turek. A new poll conducted for the Cook Political Report puts Turek 2 percentage points ahead within the poll's margin of error. Trump urged people to get behind Hinson.(SOUNDBITE OF ARCHIVED RECORDING)TRUMP: She will never let far-left maniacs push the radical left insanity on Iowa's children. She's...BRUMMER: Trump also called on voters to elect Republican Zach Lahn as governor, who faces Democrat Rob Sand, Iowa's state auditor. Polls gives Sand an 11-point lead. While speaking for more than 90 minutes, Trump mainly talked about himself, touting tax cuts, his international policies and support for farmers. He riled people up by bringing up transgender rights and another favorite topic, fake news, multiple times. He also returned to his false claims that he won in 2020, and that he should be able to run again because...(SOUNDBITE OF ARCHIVED RECORDING)TRUMP: We won three times, but that's right. You know, I want the three wins, but I don't...BRUMMER: Trump plans to return to Iowa November 1, just two days before the midterms.For NPR News, I'm Sheila Brummer, Sioux City, Iowa.Copyright \u00a9 2026 NPR.  All rights reserved.  Visit our websiteterms of useandpermissionspages atwww.npr.orgfor further information.Accuracy and availability of NPR transcripts may vary. Transcript text may be revised to