formation.Accuracy and availability of NPR transcripts may vary. Transcript text may be revised to correct errors or match updates to audio. Audio on npr.org may be edited after its original broadcast or publication. The authoritative record of NPR\u2019s programming is the audio record.\nTRUMP: We won three times, but that's right. You know, I want the three wins, but I don't...BRUMMER: Trump plans to return to Iowa November 1, just two days before the midterms.For NPR News, I'm Sheila Brummer, Sioux City, Iowa.Copyright \u00a9 2026 NPR.  All rights reserved.  Visit our websiteterms of useandpermissionspages atwww.npr.orgfor further information.Accuracy and availability of NPR transcripts may vary. Transcript text may be revised to correct errors or match updates to audio. Audio on npr.org may be edited after its original broadcast or publication. The authoritative record of NPR\u2019s programming is the audio record.\nBRUMMER: Trump plans to return to Iowa November 1, just two days before the midterms.For NPR News, I'm Sheila Brummer, Sioux City, Iowa.Copyright \u00a9 2026 NPR.  All rights reserved.  Visit our websiteterms of useandpermissionspages atwww.npr.orgfor further information.Accuracy and availability of NPR transcripts may vary. Transcript text may be revised to correct errors or match updates to audio. Audio on npr.org may be edited after its original broadcast or publication. The authoritative record of NPR\u2019s programming is the audio record.\nBRUMMER: Trump plans to return to Iowa November 1, just two days before the midterms.For NPR News, I'm Sheila Brummer, Sioux City, Iowa.Copyright \u00a9 2026 NPR.  All rights reserved.  Visit our websiteterms of useandpermissionspages atwww.npr.orgfor further information.Accuracy and availability of NPR transcripts may vary. Transcript text may be revised to correct errors or match updates to audio. Audio on npr.org may be edited after its original broadcast or publication. The authoritative record of NPR\u2019s programming is the audio record.\nFor NPR News, I'm Sheila Brummer, Sioux City, Iowa.Copyright \u00a9 2026 NPR.  All rights reserved.  Visit our websiteterms of useandpermissionspages atwww.npr.orgfor further information.Accuracy and availability of NPR transcripts may vary. Transcript text may be revised to correct errors or match updates to audio. Audio on npr.org may be edited after its original broadcast or publication. The authoritative record of NPR\u2019s programming is the audio record.\nFor NPR News, I'm Sheila Brummer, Sioux City, Iowa.Copyright \u00a9 2026 NPR.  All rights reserved.  Visit our websiteterms of useandpermissionspages atwww.npr.orgfor further information.Accuracy and availability of NPR transcripts may vary. Transcript text may be revised to correct errors or match updates to audio. Audio on npr.org may be edited after its original broadcast or publication. The authoritative record of NPR\u2019s programming is the audio record.\n\nCopyright \u00a9 2026 NPR.  All rights reserved.  Visit our websiteterms of useandpermissionspages atwww.npr.orgfor further information.\nAccuracy and availability of NPR transcripts may vary. Transcript text may be revised to correct errors or match updates to audio. Audio on npr.org may be edited after its original broadcast or publication. The authoritative record of NPR\u2019s programming is the audio record.\nNPR does not offer or accept money for coverage or interviews.Anyone who makes such an offer or request is not an NPR employee and does not represent NPR.\nSponsor Message\nBecome an NPR sponsor", "date": "Fri, 23 Oct 2026 06:36:09 -0400"}, {"title": "Gen Z is 'in the thick' of high housing costs. These candidates aim to solve it", "url": "https://www.npr.org/2026/10/23/nx-s1-5983813/gen-z-candidates-midterms-housing-affordability", "body": "Jennifer Ludden\nLeft: Democrat Olaleye Onikuyide, 28, is running for state representative in Connecticut. Right: Republican Lukas Schubert, 21, is campaigning for reelection as a state representative in Montana. Both candidates are focusing on a lack of affordable housing in their states.Raquel C. Zald\u00edvar and Sarah Mosquera for NPRhide caption\nWEST HARTFORD, Conn. \u2014 \"My bus is running slightly behind,\" Olaleye Onikuyide texted ahead of our meeting at a coffee shop on West Hartford's main street. The 28-year-old Democrat running for Connecticut state representative still made it on time and said he loved riding public transit.\nOnikuyide also lives with his parents, though he said that's by choice to save money, unlike themany young adultswho can't afford to live on their own.\nStill, as part of a generation that facesrecord-highhousingunaffordability, he started obsessing years ago over how to make it cheaper. Now that the cripplingly high cost of both rent and homes has become an urgent national conversation, he likens it to being a fan of a niche music group before everyone else caught on.\n\"All of a sudden, it's blown up and everybody's at the concerts and you're like, 'OK, this is a bit of a crowd here now,'\" he said with a broad smile.\nOnikuyide and his campaign manager, Karo Shakhnazarov, canvass in West Hartford, Connecticut.Raquel C. Zald\u00edvar for NPRhide caption\nNationwide, housing costs are a top issue for voters in a midterms season laser focused on affordability. A chronic housing shortage has pushed up prices, and the fallout cuts across generations.\nYounger people are renting with roommatesinto their 30s. They're struggling tosave for a down paymentand wondering whether it even makes sense to buy, given today's sky-high prices. Empty nesters looking to downsize can't find an affordable place to go, and longtime homeowners are reluctant to move at all, given current high mortgage rates.\nGeneration Z candidates from both major parties said their age group has a lot at stake and is uniquely positioned to help create solutions for everyone.\n\"We're in the thick of it,\" Onikuyide said, citing not only high housing and gas prices but also student loan debt and a tough labor market. \"We might be the generation that is more educated and yet worse off than our parents.\"\nOnikuyide leaves information about his campaign at a voter's home in West Hartford. He appreciates the national debate on high housing costs but says the action to address it is happening at the state level.Raquel C. Zald\u00edvar for NPRhide caption\nHis policy goals include creating more mixed-income housing and walkable cities. Adding density to urban areas can becontroversial, as longtime residents worry about more traffic or strained infrastructure. But Onikuyide and others also see an opportunity for bipartisanship. And if elected, he would feel well-placed to make life less expensive for his constituents.\n\"I keep one eye on the national conversation about it,\" he said, \"but the action is happening at the state level.\"\nAbout an hour away in New Haven, Conn., 26-year-old Eli Sabin found a quiet room for talking inside the ornate City Hall. He's also a Democrat running for state representative, and he has worked in this building.\nWhen he was a sophomore in college, he started serving on the Board of Alders. That's \"our old-fashioned term for the city council,\" he explained. \"We changed some of the zoning rules to allow for denser development and more affordable units.\"\nDemocrat Eli Sabin, 26, is running for state representative in Connecticut. He feels his experience as a renter is important, since elected officials at all levels in the U.S. are overwhelmingly homeowners.Raquel C. Zald\u00edvar for NPRhide caption\nElected officials at all levels in the U.S. areoverwhelmingly homeowners. Sabin said it makes a difference that he is a renter, like about70% of residentsin this city.\n\"I think it's the experience of being a renter and just the insecurity and instability that you feel,\" he said. His lease is up in May, and he has no idea whether the rent will go up or whether the landlord will even allow him to stay.\nHe would like more protections against eviction and would also like to make it easier to buy a home.\nOlder people have told him how they bought 30 years ago on a teacher's salary but could never do that today, while younger people feel \"pushed out of the community.\" Some have moved to states such as Texas, Tennessee or Florida, \"not because that's where they have the best job opportunities or where they want to be, but because simply that's the place they can afford,\" he said.\nNew Haven resident James Fripp Jr. (left) speaks with Sabin.Raquel C. Zald\u00edvar for NPRhide caption\nSabin won a three-way primary, beating both a democratic socialist and a longtime incumbent first elected more than a decade before he was born. \"I think people feel like we need fight right now,\" he said. \"We need energy, we need new ideas and we can't accept that the status quo is all right.\"\nLike Onikuyide, he credited New York Mayor Zohran Mamdani for igniting political energy around tackling the everyday cost of living. But Sabin also pointed to Republican-led states such asTexasandMontanaas role models for bipartisan legislation on housing affordability.\nIn Kalispell, Mont., Republican Lukas Schubert is only 21 and campaigning for reelection as a state representative. \"I'm not even recently the most youngest,\" he laughed. The state allows people to run for office at age 18.\nOn Schubert'scampaign website, housing is at the top of the issues page, along with Christianity and taxes. \"It is a top priority among Republicans as well,\" he said \"It's not just, you know, a Democrat issue.\"\nMontana state Rep. Lukas Schubert, a Republican, hangs a campaign sign in Kalispell. He says homeownership is critical to building wealth, and he wants to create more starter homes that people can afford.Sarah Mosquera for NPRhide caption\nHome prices in Montana jumped sharply during the COVID-19 pandemic as more people moved there.Gen Z and millenniallawmakers were part of a push to overhaul zoning and red tape. A 2023 package of laws dubbed the \"Montana Miracle\" aims tomake it easierand cheaper to build housing.\nThose changes are rolling out, but the housing challenge remains vast. Schubert would like to rein in large foreign corporations that buy up homes and noncitizens who own large parcels of land and property but don't live in the state. He also sees a big gap in the market.\n\"Seemingly all of the new development is apartment buildings or big, you know, 5, 10 million-dollar mansions,\" he said. His main focus is on starter homes, either single family or duplexes. \"That's what we need to be really getting rid of government barriers to producing.\"\nSchubert (left) speaks with voter Mark Shropshire in Kalispell.Sarah Mosquera for NPRhide caption\nThis past session, Schubertproposeda major tax break for developers that sell a newly built starter home for less than 85% of the local median value. It did not pass, but he plans to try again to boost homeownership.\n\"That has been one of the biggest factors in wealth retention and development for the middle class in this country,\" he said.\nLike the Democratic candidates in Connecticut, Schubert wants to keep the American dream alive for new generations.\nNPR does not offer or accept money for coverage or interviews.Anyone who makes such an offer or request is not an NPR employee and does not represent NPR.\nSponsor Message\nBecome an NPR sponsor", "date": "Fri, 23 Oct 2026 05:00:00 -0400"}, {"title": "What's behind the early voting surge? It's more than voter enthusiasm, expert says", "url": "https://www.npr.org/2026/10/23/nx-s1-6012308/midterm-elections-early-voting-data", "body": "By\nA Mart\u00ednez\n,\nMajd Al-Waheidi\nElection workers remove ballots from a drop box outside the polling center at the Silver Spring Civic Building on Oct. 22 in Silver Spring, Md.Finn Gomez/Getty Imageshide caption\nAs of Friday morning, nearly 21 million Americanshave already cast ballotsin the midterm elections, according to the University of Florida's Election Lab,which tracks votingby mail and in person.\nBut early voting numbers can be misleading. A surge in ballots cast before Election Day doesn't necessarily mean more Americans will vote overall.\nThat's because many of those voting early would have voted on Election Day anyway, according to Charles Stewart, a political science professor at MIT and founding director of the MIT Election Data and Science Lab.\nStewart toldMorning Editionthat the increase reflects both voter enthusiasm and the money campaigns are spending to encourage supporters to vote early.\n\"It also kind of reflects the large amount of money that's in this campaign that's going into retail politics, going door to door, and just making sure that supporters are energized,\" he said.\nStewart also sees changes in how Democrats and Republicans are voting. Democrats still have an advantage in voting by mail, but that gap is smaller than in 2022 as more Democrats shift to early in-person voting. Republicans' slight advantage in early in-person voting could also narrow.\nWhat stood out to Stewart was the early voting activity in Maine and Iowa, two states that haven't been major battlegrounds in recent elections. He said the numbers reflect voter enthusiasm and campaign efforts to mobilize supporters in places that haven't seen as much political activity in recent years.\nWhile speaking with NPR's A Martinez, Stewart also discussed whether concerns about the U.S. Postal Service (USPS) and Trump's opposition to mail voting are changing how Americans vote \u2014 and why polls still matter, even if they can't predict exactly what will happen on Election Day.\nListen to the full interview by clicking the blue button above.\nThe digital version was edited by Treye Green.\nNPR does not offer or accept money for coverage or interviews.Anyone who makes such an offer or request is not an NPR employee and does not represent NPR.\nSponsor Message\nBecome an NPR sponsor", "date": "Fri, 23 Oct 2026 04:42:03 -0400"}, {"title": "Trump must sit for a deposition in his BBC defamation lawsuit, judge rules", "url": "https://www.npr.org/2026/10/23/nx-s1-6012695/trump-must-sit-for-a-deposition-in-his-bbc-defamation-lawsuit-judge-rules", "body": "By\nDavid Folkenflik\n,\nA Mart\u00ednez\nA magistrate judge orders President Trump to sit for a deposition in a case he's brought against the BBC for defamation.\nA MART\u00cdNEZ, HOST:President Trump will have to sit for a deposition in a $10 billion defamation case he brought against the BBC.LEILA FADEL, HOST:Yeah. A federal magistrate judge ruled Thursday that it was Trump's choice to sue the British broadcaster, and he has to play by the rules of the court or face sanctions.MART\u00cdNEZ: NPR media correspondent David Folkenflik attended the hearing in Miami. David, why was there even a question about whether the president would sit for a deposition?DAVID FOLKENFLIK, BYLINE: Well, I mean, he is the president. And it's this president - right? - A. You know, this was a - in some ways, a hearing about logistics in front of a magistrate judge. It sounds like it's a pretty pro forma kind of thing. But actually, you know, really fundamentally at its core is a question of whether the sitting president of the United States will submit to the will of a federal court. He had agreed to sit for a deposition, which is to say questions under oath, for last Friday, October 16, but it was canceled. President's legal team said it was due to matters of national importance and said he could do it sometime in January. There's a deadline in mid-December for the completion of what's called discovery. It's where the two sides get evidence for one another for this case.Trump's private attorney invoked the idea yesterday the president does a lot more things than you could possibly know. The BBC's lead attorney said, hey. That's fake news. He noted that last Friday, Trump campaigned with a Republican Senate candidate in Dalton, Georgia, then went to Mar-a-Lago for an event for the Republican National Committee. The magistrate judge said, no. Let's do this. You're going to have to do this by December 11. There's a possibility that could shift if the judge presiding over the case changes the deadline.And the judge also said, look. You got to hand over evidence. All the material that the president's legal team gave to the BBC was stuff that the BBC had either handed over to the president's team or that - stuff that the president's legal team, according to the BBC's lawyers, had pulled down from the internet. Not any private records at all. The judge said that strained credulity.MART\u00cdNEZ: All right. So tell us about the case.FOLKENFLIK: So back in October of 2024, just a few weeks before the presidential elections, the BBC show \"Panorama\" broadcast a documentary in which it spliced together parts of the January 6 speech, 2021, that made it seem as though Trump, at that, you know, pivotal day, when there was a siege of Congress - made it seem like he was bluntly calling for violence to prevent the certification of Joe Biden as the winner of that race. Trump says this was false, deceptive and defamatory under Florida law - is seeking $10 billion.He's tweaked a little bit the grounds on which he's doing it, putting aside the claim that he made initially that this damaged his business and future financial prospects. Now he's talking mostly about his reputational injury. But as the BBC argues, his reputation is bound up in business and politics. I want to point out to listeners, \"Panorama\" was not aired in Florida or on U.S. channels or on the BBC's, you know, streaming partners here in the U.S. And the other thing the BBC points out is Trump not only won the 2024 race. He won Florida, handily, too.MART\u00cdNEZ: What's the BBC hope to learn from Trump's testimony?FOLKENFLIK: The BBC did apologize for the splicing. It did - in fact, there was a fallout for some of its officials. But it's now trying to determine what his mindset was. Maybe they can show that he did want to encourage violence. They noted that a lot of defendants said Trump's speech had urged them on.MART\u00cdNEZ: Last thing, David. I mean, President Trump has sued lots of news organizations. What's the significance of this one?FOLKENFLIK: Well, you saw at the outset of his term that media outlets were settling - CBS and ABC. And he's sued, again as a private citizen, all kinds of news outlets, including The New York Times, The Wall Street Journal, others. The BBC has been fighting back, going after details of January 6, but also going after potential vulnerability to the president. He doesn't really want his financial details public. And they are securing information about both the level of interest in people joining Mar-a-Lago, his resort, as members and what costs he has charged them over the years, in recent years since - has been able to charge them.Trump right now in this lawsuit has to decide how willing he is to play by the rules. Is he willing to submit to the rule of law in federal court in a lawsuit that he voluntarily brought? And if not, it looks very likely that this case will be dismissed.MART\u00cdNEZ: That's NPR media correspondent David Folkenflik. David, thanks.FOLKENFLIK: You bet.Copyright \u00a9 2026 NPR.  All rights reserved.  Visit our websiteterms of useandpermissionspages atwww.npr.orgfor further information.Accuracy and availability of NPR transcripts may vary. Transcript text may be revised to correct errors or match updates to audio. Audio on npr.org may be edited after its original broadcast or publication. The authoritative record of NPR\u2019s programming is the audio record.\nA MART\u00cdNEZ, HOST:President Trump will have to sit for a deposition in a $10 billion defamation case he brought against the BBC.LEILA FADEL, HOST:Yeah. A federal magistrate judge ruled Thursday that it was Trump's choice to sue the British broadcaster, and he has to play by the rules of the court or face sanctions.MART\u00cdNEZ: NPR media correspondent David Folkenflik attended the hearing in Miami. David, why was there even a question about whether the president would sit for a deposition?DAVID FOLKENFLIK, BYLINE: Well, I mean, he is the president. And it's this president - right? - A. You know, this was a - in some ways, a hearing about logistics in front of a magistrate judge. It sounds like it's a pretty pro forma kind of thing. But actually, you know, really fundamentally at its core is a question of whether the sitting president of the United States will submit to the will of a federal court. He had agreed to sit for a deposition, which is to say questions under oath, for last Friday, October 16, but it was canceled. President's legal team said it was due to matters of national importance and said he could do it sometime in January. There's a deadline in mid-December for the completion of what's called discovery. It's where the two sides get evidence for one another for this case.Trump's private attorney invoked the idea yesterday the president does a lot more things than you could possibly know. The BBC's lead attorney said, hey. That's fake news. He noted that last Friday, Trump campaigned with a Republican Senate candidate in Dalton, Georgia, then went to Mar-a-Lago for an event for the Republican National Committee. The magistrate judge said, no. Let's do this. You're going to have to do this by December 11. There's a possibility that could shift if the judge presiding over the case changes the deadline.And the judge also said, look. You got to hand over evidence. All the material that the president's legal team gave to the BBC was stuff that the BBC had either handed over to the president's team or that - stuff that the president's legal team, according to the BBC's lawyers, had pulled down from the internet. Not any private records at all. The judge said that strained credulity.MART\u00cdNEZ: All right. So tell us about the case.FOLKENFLIK: So back in October of 2024, just a few weeks before the presidential elections, the BBC show \"Panorama\" broadcast a documentary in which it spliced together parts of the January 6 speech, 2021, that made it seem as though Trump, at that, you know, pivotal day, when there was a siege of Congress - made it seem like he was bluntly calling for violence to prevent the certification of Joe Biden as the winner of that race. Trump says this was false, deceptive and defamatory under Florida law - is seeking $10 billion.He's tweaked a little bit the grounds on which he's doing it, putting aside the claim that he made initially that this damaged his business and future financial prospects. Now he's talking mostly about his reputational injury. But as the BBC argues, his reputation is bound up in business and politics. I want to point out to listeners, \"Panorama\" was not aired in Florida or on U.S. channels or on the BBC's, you know, streaming partners here in the U.S. And the other thing the BBC points out is Trump not only won the 2024 race. He won Florida, handily, too.MART\u00cdNEZ: What's the BBC hope to learn from Trump's testimony?FOLKENFLIK: The BBC did apologize for the splicing. It did - in fact, there was a fallout for some of its officials. But it's now trying to determine what his mindset was. Maybe they can show that he did want to encourage violence. They noted that a lot of defendants said Trump's speech had urged them on.MART\u00cdNEZ: Last thing, David. I mean, President Trump has sued lots of news organizations. What's the significance of this one?FOLKENFLIK: Well, you saw at the outset of his term that media outlets were settling - CBS and ABC. And he's sued, again as a private citizen, all kinds of news outlets, including The New York Times, The Wall Street Journal, others. The BBC has been fighting back, going after details of January 6, but also going after potential vulnerability to the president. He doesn't really want his financial details public. And they are securing information about both the level of interest in people joining Mar-a-Lago, his resort, as members and what costs he has charged them over the years, in recent years since - has been able to charge them.Trump right now in this lawsuit has to decide how willing he is to play by the rules. Is he willing to submit to the rule of law in federal court in a lawsuit that he voluntarily brought? And if not, it looks very likely that this case will be dismissed.MART\u00cdNEZ: That's NPR media correspondent David Folkenflik. David, thanks.FOLKENFLIK: You bet.Copyright \u00a9 2026 NPR.  All rights reserved.  Visit our websiteterms of useandpermissionspages atwww.npr.orgfor further information.Accuracy and availability of NPR transcripts may vary. Transcript text may be revised to correct errors or match updates to audio. Audio on npr.org may be edited after its original broadcast or publication. The authoritative record of NPR\u2019s programming is the audio record.\nPresident Trump will have to sit for a deposition in a $10 billion defamation case he brought against the BBC.LEILA FADEL, HOST:Yeah. A federal magistrate judge ruled Thursday that it was Trump's choice to sue the British broadcaster, and he has to play by the rules of the court or face sanctions.MART\u00cdNEZ: NPR media correspondent David Folkenflik attended the hearing in Miami. David, why was there even a question about whether the president would sit for a deposition?DAVID FOLKENFLIK, BYLINE: Well, I mean, he is the president. And it's this president - right? - A. You know, this was a - in some ways, a hearing about logistics in front of a magistrate judge. It sounds like it's a pretty pro forma kind of thing. But actually, you know, really fundamentally at its core is a question of whether the sitting president of the United States will submit to the will of a federal court. He had agreed to sit for a deposition, which is to say questions under oath, for last Friday, October 16, but it was canceled. President's legal team said it was due to matters of national importance and said he could do it sometime in January. There's a deadline in mid-December for the completion of what's called discovery. It's where the two sides get evidence for one another for this case.Trump's private attorney invoked the idea yesterday the president does a lot more things than you could possibly know. The BBC's lead attorney said, hey. That's fake news. He noted that last Friday, Trump campaigned with a Republican Senate candidate in Dalton, Georgia, then went to Mar-a-Lago for an event for the Republican National Committee. The magistrate judge said, no. Let's do this. You're going to have to do this by December 11. There's a possibility that could shift if the judge presiding over the case changes the deadline.And the judge also said, look. You got to hand over evidence. All the material that the president's legal team gave to the BBC was stuff that the BBC had either handed over to the president's team or that - stuff that the president's legal team, according to the BBC's lawyers, had pulled down from the internet. Not any private records at all. The judge said that strained credulity.MART\u00cdNEZ: All right. So tell us about the case.FOLKENFLIK: So back in October of 2024, just a few weeks before the presidential elections, the BBC show \"Panorama\" broadcast a documentary in which it spliced together parts of the January 6 speech, 2021, that made it seem as though Trump, at that, you know, pivotal day, when there was a siege of Congress - made it seem like he was bluntly calling for violence to prevent the certification of Joe Biden as the winner of that race. Trump says this was false, deceptive and defamatory under Florida law - is seeking $10 billion.He's tweaked a little bit the grounds on which he's doing it, putting aside the claim that he made initially that this damaged his business and future financial prospects. Now he's talking mostly about his reputational injury. But as the BBC argues, his reputation is bound up in business and politics. I want to point out to listeners, \"Panorama\" was not aired in Florida or on U.S. channels or on the BBC's, you know, streaming partners here in the U.S. And the other thing the BBC points out is Trump not only won the 2024 race. He won Florida, handily, too.MART\u00cdNEZ: What's the BBC hope to learn from Trump's testimony?FOLKENFLIK: The BBC did apologize for the splicing. It did - in fact, there was a fallout for some of its officials. But it's now trying to determine what his mindset was. Maybe they can show that he did want to encourage violence. They noted that a lot of defendants said Trump's speech had urged them on.MART\u00cdNEZ: Last thing, David. I mean, President Trump has sued lots of news organizations. What's the significance of this one?FOLKENFLIK: Well, you saw at the outset of his term that media outlets were settling - CBS and ABC. And he's sued, again as a private citizen, all kinds of news outlets, including The New York Times, The Wall Street Journal, others. The BBC has been fighting back, going after details of January 6, but also going after potential vulnerability to the president. He doesn't really want his financial details public. And they are securing information about both the level of interest in people joining Mar-a-Lago, his resort, as members and what costs he has charged them over the years, in recent years since - has been able to charge them.Trump right now in this lawsuit has to decide how willing he is to play by the rules. Is he willing to submit to the rule of law in federal court in a lawsuit that he voluntarily brought? And if not, it looks very likely that this case will be dismissed.MART\u00cdNEZ: That's NPR media correspondent David Folkenflik. David, thanks.FOLKENFLIK: You bet.Copyright \u00a9 2026 NPR.  All rights reserved.  Visit our websiteterms of u