afety net.Celinda Lake and Oren Cass each think their party has the best chance to find the new sweet spot in American politics. More liberal on economics than Republicans are now, more conservative on social issues than Democrats. And the political prize could be a very big one. Instead of one super close election after another that produces only gridlock, the party that figures out the new economic populism could create a functioning, durable majority. In the meantime, says Celinda Lake, populism will keep shaking up American politics.LAKE: You're going to see populist candidates emerge on the left and the right, and you're going to also see more volatility. And you're going to see more outsiders be elected because of the power of this message and the emotion behind it.LIASSON: Mara Liasson, NPR News.(SOUNDBITE OF SURPRISE CHEF'S \"ALL NEWS IS GOOD NEWS\")Copyright \u00a9 2026 NPR.  All rights reserved.  Visit our websiteterms of useandpermissionspages atwww.npr.orgfor further information.Accuracy and availability of NPR transcripts may vary. Transcript text may be revised to correct errors or match updates to audio. Audio on npr.org may be edited after its original broadcast or publication. The authoritative record of NPR\u2019s programming is the audio record.\nLIASSON: This year's populism is bipartisan. It's why you see Steve Bannon and Bernie Sanders appear back-to-back at the same anti-AI conference, and it's reflected in poll after poll. Democratic pollster Celinda Lake surveyed 500 working-class Americans without college degrees and with incomes under $150,000. The poll was commissioned by the Friedrich Ebert Foundation, which is affiliated with the German Social Democratic Party.CELINDA LAKE: What really surprises us is the number of people - about 40% - who are cynical about the American dream don't think it's possible anymore and are sarcastic about it, that it's gone. It's a lie. It's a false promise.LIASSON: A false promise that if you worked hard and