nd availability of NPR transcripts may vary. Transcript text may be revised to correct errors or match updates to audio. Audio on npr.org may be edited after its original broadcast or publication. The authoritative record of NPR\u2019s programming is the audio record.\nSIMON: Ron, I understand the Brewers were going to call you in to pinch hit last night but won despite that. What's it feel like in Milwaukee?ELVING: (Laughter) Oh, my goodness. You and I both grew up in Chicago, Scott. We're Cub fans for life, but it turns out that grandsons have a way of scrambling even one's baseball loyalties.SIMON: Well, NPR's Ron Elving. Thanks so much for being with us.ELVING: Thank you, Scott.Copyright \u00a9 2026 NPR.  All rights reserved.  Visit our websiteterms of useandpermissionspages atwww.npr.orgfor further information.Accuracy and availability of NPR transcripts may vary. Transcript text may be revised to correct errors or match updates to audio. Audio on npr.org may be edited after its original broadcast or publication. The authoritative record of NPR\u2019s programming is the audio record.\nELVING: (Laughter) Oh, my goodness. You and I both grew up in Chicago, Scott. We're Cub fans for life, but it turns out that grandsons have a way of scrambling even one's baseball loyalties.SIMON: Well, NPR's Ron Elving. Thanks so much for being with us.ELVING: Thank you, Scott.Copyright \u00a9 2026 NPR.  All rights reserved.  Visit our websiteterms of useandpermissionspages atwww.npr.orgfor further information.Accuracy and availability of NPR transcripts may vary. Transcript text may be revised to correct errors or match updates to audio. Audio on npr.org may be edited after its original broadcast or publication. The authoritative record of NPR\u2019s programming is the audio record.\nELVING: (Laughter) Oh, my goodness. You and I both grew up in Chicago, Scott. We're Cub fans for life, but it turns out that grandsons have a way of scrambling even one's baseball loyalties.SIMON: Well, NPR's Ron Elving. Thanks so much for being with us.ELVING: Thank you, Scott.Copyright \u00a9 2026 NPR.  All rights reserved.  Visit our websiteterms of useandpermissionspages atwww.npr.orgfor further information.Accuracy and availability of NPR transcripts may vary. Transcript text may be revised to correct errors or match updates to audio. Audio on npr.org may be edited after its original broadcast or publication. The authoritative record of NPR\u2019s programming is the audio record.\nSIMON: Well, NPR's Ron Elving. Thanks so much for being with us.ELVING: Thank you, Scott.Copyright \u00a9 2026 NPR.  All rights reserved.  Visit our websiteterms of useandpermissionspages atwww.npr.orgfor further information.Accuracy and availability of NPR transcripts may vary. Transcript text may be revised to correct errors or match updates to audio. Audio on npr.org may be edited after its original broadcast or publication. The authoritative record of NPR\u2019s programming is the audio record.\nSIMON: Well, NPR's Ron Elving. Thanks so much for being with us.ELVING: Thank you, Scott.Copyright \u00a9 2026 NPR.  All rights reserved.  Visit our websiteterms of useandpermissionspages atwww.npr.orgfor further information.Accuracy and availability of NPR transcripts may vary. Transcript text may be revised to correct errors or match updates to audio. Audio on npr.org may be edited after its original broadcast or publication. The authoritative record of NPR\u2019s programming is the audio record.\nELVING: Thank you, Scott.Copyright \u00a9 2026 NPR.  All rights reserved.  Visit our websiteterms of useandpermissionspages atwww.npr.orgfor further information.Accuracy and availability of NPR transcripts may vary. Transcript text may be revised to correct errors or match updates to audio. Audio on npr.org may be edited after its original broadcast or publication. The authoritative record of NPR\u2019s programming is the audio record.\nELVING: Thank you, Scott.Copyright \u00a9 2026 NPR.  All rights reserved.  Visit our websiteterms of useandpermissionspages atwww.npr.orgfor further information.Accuracy and availability of NPR transcripts may vary. Transcript text may be revised to correct errors or match updates to audio. Audio on npr.org may be edited after its original broadcast or publication. The authoritative record of NPR\u2019s programming is the audio record.\n\nCopyright \u00a9 2026 NPR.  All rights reserved.  Visit our websiteterms of useandpermissionspages atwww.npr.orgfor further information.\nAccuracy and availability of NPR transcripts may vary. Transcript text may be revised to correct errors or match updates to audio. Audio on npr.org may be edited after its original broadcast or publication. The authoritative record of NPR\u2019s programming is the audio record.\nNPR does not offer or accept money for coverage or interviews.Anyone who makes such an offer or request is not an NPR employee and does not represent NPR.\nSponsor Message\nBecome an NPR sponsor", "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 07:42:06 -0400"}, {"title": "This decade has been missing its big-impact midterm", "url": "https://www.npr.org/2026/10/24/nx-s1-6014811/midterm-election-history-house-senate", "body": "Ron Elving\nNew speaker of the U.S. House of Representatives Nancy Pelosi, D-Calif., holds the gavel during the opening session of the 116th Congress at the Capitol in Washington, D.C., on Jan. 3, 2019.Saul Loeb/AFP via Getty Imageshide caption\nGiven all that's happened in American politics since 2019, it may be hard to believe there's been anything missing.\nBut the decade of the 2020s has yet to see a midterm that mattered much \u2014 or that ranked among the impact midterms of past decades.\nThat sets the 2020s apart, as every other decade over the past hundred years has produced at least one midterm that made a big difference in Congress and in national politics more broadly.\nThese big-impact midterms have also shifted power in the statehouses, often signaling changes in state and national political landscapes that have mattered and lasted.\nMidterms do not attract the same level of voter participation or voter interest as presidential elections. When the White House is not at stake, \"low propensity voters\" tend to tune out. Overall vote totals slide by about one-third.\nBut those voters who show up for midterms elect just as many members of Congress and fill just as many state legislative seats as voters in presidential years. Moreover, midterm voters determine about two-thirds of the nation's governorships.\nThe big midterms have tended to come in presidents' second terms, when their popularity and political energy may be waning. And just because a president is not on the ballot does not mean he's irrelevant. Historically, each president's detractors have seen midterms as an opportunity, while his supporters have often been complacent \u2014 or at least less motivated.\nAll this contributes to the general expectation that the midterms arriving Nov. 3 will be important, and perhaps momentous.\nBecause this is a decade with three presidential cycles (2020, 2024 and 2028), it is also a decade with just two midterms. One went by in 2022. Some expected at the time that a \"big red wave\" would sweep then-President Biden's majorities out of Congress.\nIt did not happen. Democrats held on in the Senate, and the House shifted from barely Democratic to barely Republican. That did bring an end to House Speaker Nancy Pelosi's second (and last) turn with the Big Gavel. But the GOP would spend much of the next two yearsstruggling to unite behind a speakerof their own.\nThat's a far cry from the big midterms that have at times rivaled presidential elections for responding to the mood of the times and rearranging the landscape.\nIf we take the last hundred years as our sample, we can start with the midterms that followed the Wall Street \"Crash of 1929.\" That financial panic set off a sequence of events that would be called the Great Depression, and the collateral damage included the closing of an era of Republican dominance in Washington.\nThat era ended with the midterms of 1930, which cost the GOP eight Senate seats and 52 seats in the House in November 1930. Although the GOP initially retained small majorities in both chambers, they lost it in the House with the results of a few special elections early in 1931.\nThe \"Democratic New Year's Eve Cheer\" cartoon from Dec. 31, 1931, reflects the Democrats' win of the House that year after special elections.National Archiveshide caption\nThe Senate remained nominally Republican but featured a working majority of Democrats and progressive Republicans. A page had been turned, and Democrats would take control outright in 1932 and hold that majority through World War II.\nAfter that war had ended, the period of Democratic hegemony under President Franklin Roosevelt also ended, and abruptly, with the results of the midterm of 1946.\nRepublican numbers in Congress had fallen so far in the FDR years (below 100 at one point in the 435-seat House) that it seemed impossible for them to return to power. But in 1946 they stormed back to majority control of both House and Senate in a single midterm cycle.\nMidterms have continued to serve as political regulators in the generations since. Dwight Eisenhower won two landslide elections as president in the 1950s, but he lost the House in 1954 and needed the vice presidentto break tiesin the Senate.\nIn his second term, the re-elected \"Ike\" did not control Capitol Hill, and in the 1958 midterms saw his party drop tojust 35 seats in the Senate.\nThe Senate Democrats first elected in 1958 established a critical mass that would later drive the bipartisan super-majorities needed to break the filibuster and pass the Civil Rights Act of 1964 and the Voting Rights Act of 1965.\nIn the same era, Democrats in Congress enacted a raft of social programs that would become part of the language, such as Medicare and Medicaid. It was all part of President Lyndon Johnson's \"Great Society\" program.\nDemonstrators carrying signs against the war in Vietnam march in front of the White House in April 1965 in Washington, D.C.AFP via Getty Images/AFPhide caption\nBut by the midterm year of 1966, much of the energy from LBJ's landslide election in 1964 had been expended \u2014 and much more had been siphoned off by his escalation of the war in Vietnam.\nRight on time, the midterm arrived, and while the Democrats only took a haircut in the Senate elections they lost a painful 47 seats to the GOP in the House. It gave the party a new lease on life and encouraged itsnew emphasis on winning in the South. The redirection would guide the party's 1968 presidential candidate, Richard Nixon, and remain a focus for the onetime \"Party of Lincoln\" for decades to come.\nAlso in the midterm of 1966, out on the West Coast, a first-time candidate named Ronald Reagan won the governorship of California. People were starting to use the term \"Sunbelt.\"\nBut what the midterms of 1966 gave the GOP, the midterms of 1974 would largely take away. Nixon had been elected president in 1968, pursuing what was called a Southern Strategy, and was reelected in a resounding 49-state landslide in 1972.\nBut by 1974 it had all gone the other way for Nixon. The \"Watergate\" scandals emerged from his 1972 campaign's use of burglary and electronic surveillance among other unsavory tactics. An attempt to cover it all up blew back on the White House and forced Nixon to resign on the brink of impeachment in August of 1974 \u2013 just three months before the midterm for his second term.\nThe result was a crushing defeat for the GOP, the loss of 48 seats in the House and several more in the Senate (where the Democrats already had more than 60 votes).\nWhile it is tempting to attribute these losses to Nixon's downfall, the country had \u2014 to some extent \u2014 rallied behind his vice president and successor, Gerald R. Ford. The underlying vulnerability of the GOP was the state of the economy, which was suffering from inflation and the hangover fromthe oil embargo of 1973by Arab members of OPEC, the Organization of Petroleum Exporting Countries.\nThat embargo, a side effect of the Arab-Israeli \"Yom Kippur War\" of 1973, temporarilyraised the price of crude oilfrom $3 to $12 and introduced Americans to the phrases \"oil shock\" and \"energy crisis.\"\nPresident Ronald Reagan waves beside his wife, Nancy Reagan, after being sworn in as 40th president of the United States by Chief Justice Warren Burger on Jan. 20, 1981, at the Capitol in Washington, D.C.Consolidated News Pictures/AFP via Getty Imageshide caption\nReagan became the dominant political personality of the 1980s, defeating incumbent President Jimmy Carter in 1980 and winning a 49-state landslide reelection in 1984. He was able to carry in a majority for Republicans in the Senate and establish a kind of working majority in the House with the aid of conservative Democrats in that chamber.\nOn the strength of those elections, restrained only marginally by the midterm elections of 1982, Reagan began his second term on top of the world. But he soon learned how hard that height can be to hold. As he neared his second midterm, Democrats were on the march nationally and restoring their strength in the more populous Southern states.\nIn 1986, Democrats defeated six GOP senators who had been elected with Reagan in 1980. The net gain of eight Senate seats gave them a 55-45 majority. In the session of Congress that followed, the Senate would override Reagan's veto of a highway bill and reject his nomination of Robert Bork to the Supreme Court. Reagan was a spent force.\nReagan was able to see his vice president, George H.W. Bush, take the White House in 1988, but the party's hopes for momentum into the 1990 midterms were largely unfulfilled. The presidency was also lost in 1992, when Democrat Bill Clinton won a three-way race with just 43% of the popular vote, defeating Bush and independent candidate H. Ross Perot.\nClinton's lack of a majority and list of frustrations in his first term set the stage for a bruising first midterm election in 1994. But what came was beyond bruising. Republicans stormed to their first majority in the House (54 seats net) in 40 years and recaptured the Senate with eight new seats there as well.\nThe new GOP speaker, Newt Gingrich of Georgia, was a hard-driving partisan who had portrayed the chamber as aHouse of Ill Repute,the title of a book he and other House Republicans had published.\nThe use of a \"Contract with America\" theme promised lower taxes, less corruption and a healthier economy in a compact agenda that had voter appeal as well.\nGingrich explicitly rejected the go-along-get-along attitude of previous GOP leaders in the House and advocated confrontation. The strategy proved less fruitful once he had the Big Gavel, however, and Gingrich spent much of his time as speaker making deals with Clinton. Internal dissatisfaction with him forced him out late in 1998.\nIn the new century that followed, parties have continued to prosper when their presidential candidates win and give ground in the midterms. George W. Bush was an exception as the president after the terror attacks of September 2001. In his first midterm the following year, the GOP made gains across the board.\nBut the larger dynamic caught up to him in 2006, when he and his party took what he called \"a thumpin'\" in elections at all levels. Bush had initially gained popularity with his \"War on Terror\" and won a second term. But thereafter, his invasion of Iraq had become an albatross. And in 2006 he paid the historic price, losing both his party majorities in the House and Senate. The new majority in the House made Pelosi the first woman speaker in history.\nThat created an atmosphere of optimism for Democrats, who elected Barack Obama president in 2008 and gave him majorities in the House and Senate (the latter briefly reaching 60 seats). But Obama felt the sting of the midterms early \u2014 much as Clinton had. Republicans in 2010 capitalized on an economy still experiencing the recession that had aided Obama's win in 2008. The GOP also capitalized on fears and confusion surrounding the enactment of the Affordable Care Act, or \"Obamacare,\" in 2010.\nEchoing his predecessor, Obama called this setback \"a shellacking,\" and settled in to work with a much diminished Senate majority and a Republican-dominated House under Speaker John Boehner.\nPresident Barack Obama is greeted by House Speaker John Boehner and Vice President Joe Biden prior to delivering his State of the Union address in January 2011 at the U.S. Capitol in Washington, D.C.Jim Watson/AFP via Getty Imageshide caption\nObama managed to thread the needle well enough to win a second term for himself in 2012, but the House remained Republican and the Senate followed suit in Obama's second midterm election in 2014.\nThat second midterm revealed continuing GOP strength across the South and Midwest and to some degree augured the Electoral College majorities that would make Donald J. Trump president in 2016 and 2024.\nIf the 2020s are to have a midterm with the kind of impact others have had, roughly once a decade for a century, the last opportunity for that to happen is Nov. 3.\nIf the once-a-decade rhythm has any rationale, in fact, the pressure for a meaningful midterm ought to be building substantially. And in fact most pollsters, pundits and other prognosticators now predict Democrats could flip control of the House and even the Senate as well.\nJust as in the previous 10 cycles, the current cycle combines economic concerns (including energy prices) and negative stories about incumbents in various offices. It is the party perceived as being in power that is exposed in a midterm, and it pays a price when voters perceive that party to have lost its mandate \u2014 or at least its momentum.\nNPR does not offer or accept money for coverage or interviews.Anyone who makes such an offer or request is not an NPR employee and does not represent NPR.\nSponsor Message\nBecome an NPR sponsor", "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 05:00:00 -0400"}, {"title": "At a Texas high school football game, fans offer a glimpse into evolving politics", "url": "https://www.npr.org/2026/10/23/nx-s1-6003856/at-a-texas-high-school-football-game-fans-offer-a-glimpse-into-evolving-politics", "body": "From\nBy\nBianca Seward\nWe talk with voters at a Texas high school football game at the tailgate, in line at the concession booth and in the stands to hear about the issues that matter to Texans this cycle.\nSCOTT DETROW, HOST:Texas and football - a classic combination no matter who you root for.(CHEERING)DETROW: Root for or vote for. Texas has been a safe Republican state for three decades, but polling suggests next month's midterms could be tight. So Houston Public Media's Bianca Seward takes us to the bleachers at Deer Park High School's homecoming game, where fans in this Houston suburb offer a glimpse into the state's changing politics.UNIDENTIFIED PERSON #1: Go go go.(CHEERING)BIANCA SEWARD, BYLINE: In Texas, the high school football field is sacred ground.ERIC SCHUTLZ: And it's a culture that, no matter if you're in big city or rural Texas, where I grew up, it's a time for everybody, just the same principle as going to church, right?SEWARD: That's Eric Schutlz. He and his wife and kids come every Friday night that they can.SCHUTLZ: It's kind of a function of the community and for families to get together - community - and support everybody - band, football players, cheerleaders.SEWARD: That's what brought Emily Taylor out to the game.EMILY TAYLOR: Sometimes I forget that there's a football game going on 'cause I'm only here for her. She's next after this.SEWARD: Taylor comes to watch her niece Sydney cheer on the sidelines.UNIDENTIFIED PERSON #2: Junior co-captain, two time All American - Sydney Wilber.TAYLOR: Let's go, Sydney.SEWARD: Taylor is a Democrat living in Deer Park, a conservative suburb of Houston. But she says something feels different this cycle.TAYLOR: The other day, I took some back roads, and I saw two houses that had Talarico and Gina Hinojosa signs outside. And it made me so hopeful because I do not remember seeing that during Kamala Harris' run.SEWARD: Recent polling supports Taylor's hunch. Democrats Gina Hinojosa and James Talarico have forced Governor Greg Abbott and Attorney General Ken Paxton into competitive contests. Both races are effectively tied.TAYLOR: If we can do it, this is the year.UNIDENTIFIED PERSON #2: Deer Park, first down.SCHUTLZ: I figure it'll still stay red.SEWARD: When Eric Schutlz heads to the poll this year, he says he'll vote Republican with two specific groups of people on his mind.SCHUTLZ: You know, and at the end of the day, you know, kids and elderly are important, right? Elderly paved the way, kids are the future. So those are important topics that need to be taken care of.SEWARD: And despite all the talk of a tight race, he's not convinced the state will change parties this year.SCHUTLZ: The dividing line comes in bigger cities, but when you get in smaller communities, they're more like-minded. And, you know, I think it'll stay the same.SEWARD: Nate Esser, sitting a couple dozen bleachers down, is hoping Schutlz is wrong.NATE ESSER: I'm hoping people will wake up and realize that current administration is not doing anything for them right now.SEWARD: And Esser has a long list of concerns.ESSER: Inflation, the economy - I mean, my 401K is doing great, but, you know, that doesn't benefit most people. Yeah, gas prices, fairness to people, like, treating everyone fairly, equal rights for trans people, gay people, people of color. Like, I'm sick of this ICE crap.SEWARD: Immigration enforcement has taken center stage in political debates across Texas after a series of incidents involving ICE agents and allegations of excessive force. But still, Esser isn't sure many others in the Deer Park community agree with him.ESSER: You've interviewed a lot of people around here. And my guess is I'm one of the few that has spoken like this to you.SEWARD: I tell him it's actually been a mixed bag.ESSER: Really?SEWARD: Fifty/fifty.ESSER: Really? That's - that makes me feel good then. That surprises me, yeah.UNIDENTIFIED PERSON #3: What color (inaudible)?UNIDENTIFIED PERSON #4: Blue.UNIDENTIFIED PERSON #3: Blue. OK.STEPHANIE PETERSON: I need two chili cheese hot dogs.SEWARD: Stephanie Peterson manages the concession booth.PETERSON: Plain nachos.SEWARD: She makes sure the shelves stay stocked and keeps an eye on rising prices, both for the booth and for her own family's pantry.PETERSON: Just the prices going up, it's killing us. Basically, now you have to have two income family just to survive, and we don't have that. So it's like you don't know how you're going to survive anymore.SEWARD: In the fourth quarter of the election, for either party to win, they'll need all voters ready to jump off the bench. But Peterson, who voted for Trump in 2024, says she might stay home on Election Day.PETERSON: I don't know. It depends. I'm undecided. I'll just say that.SEWARD: From the economy to immigration, folks in the crowd have their own priorities, and they want very different outcomes on election day.UNIDENTIFIED PERSON #2: Touchdown Deer.(CHEERING)SEWARD: But as the game comes to a close, their voices blend into one, cheering together for the same team under the Friday night lights.For NPR News, I'm Bianca Seward in Deer Park, Texas.(SOUNDBITE OF HI-TEK SONG, \"ALL I NEED IS YOU\")Copyright \u00a9 2026 NPR.  All rights reserved.  Visit our websiteterms of useandpermissionspages atwww.npr.orgfor further information.Accuracy and availability of NPR transcripts may vary. Transcript text may be revised to correct errors or match updates to audio. Audio on npr.org may be edited after its original broadcast or publication. The authoritative record of NPR\u2019s programming is the audio record.\nSCOTT DETROW, HOST:Texas and football - a classic combination no matter who you root for.(CHEERING)DETROW: Root for or vote for. Texas has been a safe Republican state for three decades, but polling suggests next month's midterms could be tight. So Houston Public Media's Bianca Seward takes us to the bleachers at Deer Park High School's homecoming game, where fans in this Houston suburb offer a glimpse into the state's changing politics.UNIDENTIFIED PERSON #1: Go go go.(CHEERING)BIANCA SEWARD, BYLINE: In Texas, the high school football field is sacred ground.ERIC SCHUTLZ: And it's a culture that, no matter if you're in big city or rural Texas, where I grew up, it's a time for everybody, just the same principle as going to church, right?SEWARD: That's Eric Schutlz. He and his wife and kids come every Friday night that they can.SCHUTLZ: It's kind of a function of the community and for families to get together - community - and support everybody - band, football players, cheerleaders.SEWARD: That's what brought Emily Taylor out to the game.EMILY TAYLOR: Sometimes I forget that there's a football game going on 'cause I'm only here for her. She's next after this.SEWARD: Taylor comes to watch her niece Sydney cheer on the sidelines.UNIDENTIFIED PERSON #2: Junior co-captain, two time All American - Sydney Wilber.TAYLOR: Let's go, Sydney.SEWARD: Taylor is a Democrat living in Deer Park, a conservative suburb of Houston. But she says something feels different this cycle.TAYLOR: The other day, I took some back roads, and I saw two houses that had Talarico and Gina Hinojosa signs outside. And it made me so hopeful because I do not remember seeing that during Kamala Harris' run.SEWARD: Recent polling supports Taylor's hunch. Democrats Gina Hinojosa and James Talarico have forced Governor Greg Abbott and Attorney General Ken Paxton into competitive contests. Both races are effectively tied.TAYLOR: If we can do it, this is the year.UNIDENTIFIED PERSON #2: Deer Park, first down.SCHUTLZ: I figure it'll still stay red.SEWARD: When Eric Schutlz heads to the poll this year, he says he'll vote Republican with two specific groups of people on his mind.SCHUTLZ: You know, and at the end of the day, you know, kids and elderly are important, right? Elderly paved the way, kids are the future. So those are important topics that need to be taken care of.SEWARD: And despite all the talk of a tight race, he's not convinced the state will change parties this year.SCHUTLZ: The dividing line comes in bigger cities, but when you get in smaller communities, they're more like-minded. And, you know, I think it'll stay the same.SEWARD: Nate Esser, sitting a couple dozen bleachers down, is hoping Schutlz is wrong.NATE ESSER: I'm hoping people will wake up and realize that current administration is not doing anything for them right now.SEWARD: And Esser has a long list of concerns.ESSER: Inflation, the economy - I mean, my 401K is doing great, but, you know, that doesn't benefit most people. Yeah, gas prices, fairness to people, like, treating everyone fairly, equal rights for trans people, gay people, people of color. Like, I'm sick of this ICE crap.SEWARD: Immigration enforcement has taken center stage in political debates across Texas after a series of incidents involving ICE agents and allegations of excessive force. But still, Esser isn't sure many others in the Deer Park community agree with him.ESSER: You've interviewed a lot of people around here. And my guess is I'm one of the few that has spoken like this to you.SEWARD: I tell him it's actually been a mixed bag.ESSER: Really?SEWARD: Fifty/fifty.ESSER: Really? That's - that makes me feel good then. That surprises me, yeah.UNIDENTIFIED PERSON #3: What color (inaudible)?UNIDENTIFIED PERSON #4: Blue.UNIDENTIFIED PERSON #3: Blue. OK.STEPHANIE PETERSON: I need two chili cheese hot dogs.SEWARD: Stephanie Peterson manages the concession booth.PETERSON: Plain nachos.SEWARD: She makes sure the shelves stay stocked and keeps an eye on rising prices, both for the booth and for her own family's pantry.PETERSON: Just the prices going up, it's killing us. Basically, now you have to have two income family just to survive, and we don't have that. So it's like you don't know how you're going to survive anymore.SEWARD: In the fourth quarter of the election, for either party to win, they'll need all voters ready to jump off the bench. But Peterson, who voted for Trump in 2024, says she might stay home on Election Day.PETERSON: I don't know. It depends. I'm undecided. I'll just say that.SEWARD: From the economy to immigration, folks in the crowd have their own priorities, and they want very different outcomes on election day.UNIDENTIFIED PERSON #2: Touchdown Deer.(CHEERING)SEWARD: But as the game comes to a close, their voices blend into one, cheering together for the same team under the Friday night lights.For NPR News, I'm Bianca Seward in Deer Park, Texas.(SOUNDBITE OF HI-TEK SONG, \"ALL I NEED IS YOU\")Copyright \u00a9 2026 NPR.  All rights reserved.  Visit our websiteterms of useandpermissionspages atwww.npr.orgfor further information.Accuracy and availability of NPR transcripts may vary. Transcript text may be revised to correct errors or match updates to audio. Audio on npr.org may be edited after its original broadcast or publication. The authoritative record of NPR\u2019s programming is the audio record.\nTexas and football - a classic combination no matter who you root for.(CHEERING)DETROW: Root for or vote for. Texas has been a safe Republican state for three decades, but polling suggests next month's midterms could be tight. So Houston Public Media's Bianca Seward takes us to the bleachers at Deer Park High School's homecoming game, where fans in this Houston suburb offer a glimpse into the state's changing politics.UNIDENTIFIED PERSON #1: Go go go.(CHEERING)BIANCA SEWARD, BYLINE: In Texas, the high school football field is sacred ground.ERIC SCHUTLZ: And it's a culture that, no matter if you're in big city or rural Texas, where I grew up, it's a time for everybody, just the same principle as going to church, right?SEWARD: That's Eric Schutlz. He and his wife and kids come every Friday night that they can.SCHUTLZ: It's kind of a function of the community and for families to get together - community - and support everybody - band, football players, cheerleaders.SEWARD: That's what brought Emily Taylor out to the game.EMILY TAYLOR: Sometimes I forget that there's a football game going on 'cause I'm only here for her. She's next after this.SEWARD: Taylor comes to watch her niece Sydney cheer on the sidelines.UNIDENTIFIED PERSON #2: Junior co-captain, two time All American - Sydney Wilber.TAYLOR: Let's go, Sydney.SEWARD: Taylor is a Democrat living in Deer Park, a conservative suburb of Houston. But she says something feels different this cycle.TAYLOR: The other day, I took some back roads, and I saw two houses that had Talarico and Gina Hinojosa signs outside. And it made me so hopeful because I do not remember seeing that during Kamala Harris' run.SEWARD: Recent polling supports Taylor's hunch. Democrats Gina Hinojosa and James Talarico have forced Governor Greg Abbott and Attorney General Ken Paxton into competitive contests. Both races are effectively tied.TAYLOR: If we can do it, this is the year.UNIDENTIFIED PERSON #2: Deer Park, first down.SCHUTLZ: I figure it'll still stay red.SEWARD: When Eric Schutlz heads to the poll this year, he says he'll vote Republican with two specific groups of people on his mind.SCHUTLZ: You know, and at the end of the day, you know, kids and elderly are important, right? Elderly paved the way, kids are the future. So those are important topics that need to be taken care of.SEWARD: And despite all the talk of a tight race, he's not convinced the state will change parties this year.SCHUTLZ: The dividing line comes in bigger cities, but when you get in smaller communities, they're more like-minded. And, you know, I think it'll stay the same.SEWARD: Nate Esser, sitting a couple dozen bleachers down, is hoping Schutlz is wrong.NATE ESSER: I'm hoping people will wake up and realize that current administration is not doing anything for them right now.SEWARD: And Esser has a long list of concerns.ESSER: Inflation, the economy - I mean, my 401K is doing great, but, you know, that doesn't benefit most people. Yeah, gas prices, fairness to people, like, treating everyone fairly, equal rights for trans people, gay people, people of color. Like, I'm sick of this ICE crap.SEWARD: Immigration enforcement has taken center stage in political debates across Texas after a series of incidents involving ICE agents and allegations of excessive force. But still, Esser isn't sure many others in the Deer Park community agree with him.ESSER: You've interviewed a lot of people around here. And my guess is I'm one of the few that has spoken like this to you.SEWARD: I tell him it's actually been a mixed bag.ESSER: Really?SEWARD: Fifty/fifty.ESSER: Really? That's - that makes me feel good then. That surprises me, yeah.UNIDENTIFIED PERSON #3: What color (inaudible)?UNIDENTIFIED PERSON #4: Blue.UNIDENTIFIED PERSON #3: Blue. OK.STEPHANIE PETERSON: I need two chili cheese hot dogs.SEWARD: Stephanie Peterson manages the concession booth.PETERSON: Plain nachos.SEWARD: She makes sure the shelves stay stocked and keeps an eye on rising prices, both for the booth and for her own family's pantry.PETERSON: Just the prices going up, it's killing us. Basically, now you have to have two income family just to survive, and we don't have that. So it's like you don't know how you're going to survive anymore.SEWARD: In the fourth quarter of the election, for either party to win, they'll need all voters ready to jump off the bench. But Peterson, who voted for Trump in 2024, says she might stay home on Election Day.PETERSON: I don't know. It depends. I'm undecided. I'll just say that.SEWARD: From the economy to immigration, folks in the crowd have their own priorities, and they want very different outcomes on election day.UNIDENTIFIED PERSON #2: Touchdown Deer.(CHEERING)SEWARD: But as the game comes to a close, their voices blend into one, cheering together for the same team under the Friday night lights.For NPR News, I'm Bianca Seward in Deer Park, Texas.(SOUNDBITE OF HI-TEK SONG, \"ALL I NEED IS YOU\")Copyright \u00a9 2026 NPR.  All rights reserved.  Visit our websiteterms of useandpermissionspages atwww.npr.orgfor further information.Accuracy and availability of NPR transcripts may vary. Transcript text may be revised to correct errors or match updates to audio. Audio on npr.org may be edited after its original broadcast or publication. The authoritative record of NPR\u2019s programming is the audio record.\nTexas and football - a classic combination no matter who you root for.(CHEERING)DETROW: Root for or vote for. Texas has been a safe Republican state for three decades, but polling suggests next month's midterms could be tight. So Houston Public Media's Bianca Seward takes us to the bleachers at Deer Park High School's homecoming game, where fans in this Houston suburb offer a glimpse into the state's changing politics.UNIDENTIFIED PERSON #1: Go go go.(CHEERING)BIANCA SEWARD, BYLINE: In Texas, the high school football field is sacred ground.ERIC SCHUTLZ: And it's a culture that, no matter if you're in big city or rural Texas, where I grew up, it's a time for everybody, just the same principle as going to church, right?SEWARD: That's Eric Schutlz. He and his wife and kids come every Friday night that they can.SCHUTLZ: It's kind of a function of the community and for families to get together - community - and support everybody - band, football players, cheerleaders.SEWARD: That's what brought Emily Taylor out to the game.EMILY TAYLOR: Sometimes I forget that there's a football game goi