iteterms of useandpermissionspages atwww.npr.orgfor further information.Accuracy and availability of NPR transcripts may vary. Transcript text may be revised to correct errors or match updates to audio. Audio on npr.org may be edited after its original broadcast or publication. The authoritative record of NPR\u2019s programming is the audio record.\nBIANCA SEWARD, BYLINE: In Texas, the high school football field is sacred ground.ERIC SCHUTLZ: And it's a culture that, no matter if you're in big city or rural Texas, where I grew up, it's a time for everybody, just the same principle as going to church, right?SEWARD: That's Eric Schutlz. He and his wife and kids come every Friday night that they can.SCHUTLZ: It's kind of a function of the community and for families to get together - community - and support everybody - band, football players, cheerleaders.SEWARD: That's what brought Emily Taylor out to the game.EMILY TAYLOR: Sometimes I forget that there's a football game going on 'cause I'm only here for her. She's next after this.SEWARD: Taylor comes to watch her niece Sydney cheer on the sidelines.UNIDENTIFIED PERSON #2: Junior co-captain, two time All American - Sydney Wilber.TAYLOR: Let's go, Sydney.SEWARD: Taylor is a Democrat living in Deer Park, a conservative suburb of Houston. But she says something feels different this cycle.TAYLOR: The other day, I took some back roads, and I saw two houses that had Talarico and Gina Hinojosa signs outside. And it made me so hopeful because I do not remember seeing that during Kamala Harris' run.SEWARD: Recent polling supports Taylor's hunch. Democrats Gina Hinojosa and James Talarico have forced Governor Greg Abbott and Attorney General Ken Paxton into competitive contests. Both races are effectively tied.TAYLOR: If we can do it, this is the year.UNIDENTIFIED PERSON #2: Deer Park, first down.SCHUTLZ: I figure it'll still stay red.SEWARD: When Eric Schutlz heads to the poll this year, he says he'll vote Republican with two s