g us. Basically, now you have to have two income family just to survive, and we don't have that. So it's like you don't know how you're going to survive anymore.SEWARD: In the fourth quarter of the election, for either party to win, they'll need all voters ready to jump off the bench. But Peterson, who voted for Trump in 2024, says she might stay home on Election Day.PETERSON: I don't know. It depends. I'm undecided. I'll just say that.SEWARD: From the economy to immigration, folks in the crowd have their own priorities, and they want very different outcomes on election day.UNIDENTIFIED PERSON #2: Touchdown Deer.(CHEERING)SEWARD: But as the game comes to a close, their voices blend into one, cheering together for the same team under the Friday night lights.For NPR News, I'm Bianca Seward in Deer Park, Texas.(SOUNDBITE OF HI-TEK SONG, \"ALL I NEED IS YOU\")Copyright \u00a9 2026 NPR.  All rights reserved.  Visit our websiteterms of useandpermissionspages atwww.npr.orgfor further information.Accuracy and availability of NPR transcripts may vary. Transcript text may be revised to correct errors or match updates to audio. Audio on npr.org may be edited after its original broadcast or publication. The authoritative record of NPR\u2019s programming is the audio record.\nERIC SCHUTLZ: And it's a culture that, no matter if you're in big city or rural Texas, where I grew up, it's a time for everybody, just the same principle as going to church, right?SEWARD: That's Eric Schutlz. He and his wife and kids come every Friday night that they can.SCHUTLZ: It's kind of a function of the community and for families to get together - community - and support everybody - band, football players, cheerleaders.SEWARD: That's what brought Emily Taylor out to the game.EMILY TAYLOR: Sometimes I forget that there's a football game going on 'cause I'm only here for her. She's next after this.SEWARD: Taylor comes to watch her niece Sydney cheer on the sidelines.UNIDENTIFIED PERSON #2: Junior co-c