I don't know. It depends. I'm undecided. I'll just say that.SEWARD: From the economy to immigration, folks in the crowd have their own priorities, and they want very different outcomes on election day.UNIDENTIFIED PERSON #2: Touchdown Deer.(CHEERING)SEWARD: But as the game comes to a close, their voices blend into one, cheering together for the same team under the Friday night lights.For NPR News, I'm Bianca Seward in Deer Park, Texas.(SOUNDBITE OF HI-TEK SONG, \"ALL I NEED IS YOU\")Copyright \u00a9 2026 NPR.  All rights reserved.  Visit our websiteterms of useandpermissionspages atwww.npr.orgfor further information.Accuracy and availability of NPR transcripts may vary. Transcript text may be revised to correct errors or match updates to audio. Audio on npr.org may be edited after its original broadcast or publication. The authoritative record of NPR\u2019s programming is the audio record.\nSCHUTLZ: It's kind of a function of the community and for families to get together - community - and support everybody - band, football players, cheerleaders.SEWARD: That's what brought Emily Taylor out to the game.EMILY TAYLOR: Sometimes I forget that there's a football game going on 'cause I'm only here for her. She's next after this.SEWARD: Taylor comes to watch her niece Sydney cheer on the sidelines.UNIDENTIFIED PERSON #2: Junior co-captain, two time All American - Sydney Wilber.TAYLOR: Let's go, Sydney.SEWARD: Taylor is a Democrat living in Deer Park, a conservative suburb of Houston. But she says something feels different this cycle.TAYLOR: The other day, I took some back roads, and I saw two houses that had Talarico and Gina Hinojosa signs outside. And it made me so hopeful because I do not remember seeing that during Kamala Harris' run.SEWARD: Recent polling supports Taylor's hunch. Democrats Gina Hinojosa and James Talarico have forced Governor Greg Abbott and Attorney General Ken Paxton into competitive contests. Both races are effectively tied.TAYLOR: If we can do it,