r information.Accuracy and availability of NPR transcripts may vary. Transcript text may be revised to correct errors or match updates to audio. Audio on npr.org may be edited after its original broadcast or publication. The authoritative record of NPR\u2019s programming is the audio record.\n(CHEERING)SEWARD: But as the game comes to a close, their voices blend into one, cheering together for the same team under the Friday night lights.For NPR News, I'm Bianca Seward in Deer Park, Texas.(SOUNDBITE OF HI-TEK SONG, \"ALL I NEED IS YOU\")Copyright \u00a9 2026 NPR.  All rights reserved.  Visit our websiteterms of useandpermissionspages atwww.npr.orgfor further information.Accuracy and availability of NPR transcripts may vary. Transcript text may be revised to correct errors or match updates to audio. Audio on npr.org may be edited after its original broadcast or publication. The authoritative record of NPR\u2019s programming is the audio record.\nSEWARD: But as the game comes to a close, their voices blend into one, cheering together for the same team under the Friday night lights.For NPR News, I'm Bianca Seward in Deer Park, Texas.(SOUNDBITE OF HI-TEK SONG, \"ALL I NEED IS YOU\")Copyright \u00a9 2026 NPR.  All rights reserved.  Visit our websiteterms of useandpermissionspages atwww.npr.orgfor further information.Accuracy and availability of NPR transcripts may vary. Transcript text may be revised to correct errors or match updates to audio. Audio on npr.org may be edited after its original broadcast or publication. The authoritative record of NPR\u2019s programming is the audio record.\nSEWARD: But as the game comes to a close, their voices blend into one, cheering together for the same team under the Friday night lights.For NPR News, I'm Bianca Seward in Deer Park, Texas.(SOUNDBITE OF HI-TEK SONG, \"ALL I NEED IS YOU\")Copyright \u00a9 2026 NPR.  All rights reserved.  Visit our websiteterms of useandpermissionspages atwww.npr.orgfor further information.Accuracy and availability of NPR transcripts may vary. Transcript text may be revised to correct errors or match updates to audio. Audio on npr.org may be edited after its original broadcast or publication. The authoritative record of NPR\u2019s programming is the audio record.\nFor NPR News, I'm Bianca Seward in Deer Park, Texas.(SOUNDBITE OF HI-TEK SONG, \"ALL I NEED IS YOU\")Copyright \u00a9 2026 NPR.  All rights reserved.  Visit our websiteterms of useandpermissionspages atwww.npr.orgfor further information.Accuracy and availability of NPR transcripts may vary. Transcript text may be revised to correct errors or match updates to audio. Audio on npr.org may be edited after its original broadcast or publication. The authoritative record of NPR\u2019s programming is the audio record.\nFor NPR News, I'm Bianca Seward in Deer Park, Texas.(SOUNDBITE OF HI-TEK SONG, \"ALL I NEED IS YOU\")Copyright \u00a9 2026 NPR.  All rights reserved.  Visit our websiteterms of useandpermissionspages atwww.npr.orgfor further information.Accuracy and availability of NPR transcripts may vary. Transcript text may be revised to correct errors or match updates to audio. Audio on npr.org may be edited after its original broadcast or publication. The authoritative record of NPR\u2019s programming is the audio record.\n(SOUNDBITE OF HI-TEK SONG, \"ALL I NEED IS YOU\")Copyright \u00a9 2026 NPR.  All rights reserved.  Visit our websiteterms of useandpermissionspages atwww.npr.orgfor further information.Accuracy and availability of NPR transcripts may vary. Transcript text may be revised to correct errors or match updates to audio. Audio on npr.org may be edited after its original broadcast or publication. The authoritative record of NPR\u2019s programming is the audio record.\n(SOUNDBITE OF HI-TEK SONG, \"ALL I NEED IS YOU\")Copyright \u00a9 2026 NPR.  All rights reserved.  Visit our websiteterms of useandpermissionspages atwww.npr.orgfor further information.Accuracy and availability of NPR transcripts may vary. Transcript text may be revised to correct errors or match updates to audio. Audio on npr.org may be edited after its original broadcast or publication. The authoritative record of NPR\u2019s programming is the audio record.\n\nCopyright \u00a9 2026 NPR.  All rights reserved.  Visit our websiteterms of useandpermissionspages atwww.npr.orgfor further information.\nAccuracy and availability of NPR transcripts may vary. Transcript text may be revised to correct errors or match updates to audio. Audio on npr.org may be edited after its original broadcast or publication. The authoritative record of NPR\u2019s programming is the audio record.\nNPR does not offer or accept money for coverage or interviews.Anyone who makes such an offer or request is not an NPR employee and does not represent NPR.\nSponsor Message\nBecome an NPR sponsor", "date": "Fri, 23 Oct 2026 18:35:40 -0400"}, {"title": "Russia steps up election influence efforts in final stretch of midterms", "url": "https://www.npr.org/2026/10/23/nx-s1-5995508/russia-midterms-election-influence", "body": "Shannon Bond\nA pedestrian looks at his smartphone on an embankment with the Kremlin in the background in central Moscow on Oct. 8, 2026. Researchers who track online influence operations say Kremlin-backed groups are renewing tactics they've used to sow distrust and discord in previous elections in the U.S. and around the world.Igor Ivanko/AFP via Getty Imageshide caption\nRussian operatives are pumping out fabricated claims about Democratic candidates, AI-manipulated videos of celebrities and fake posts impersonating real news outlets in an effort to influence American voters in the final run-up to Election Day.\nResearchers who track online influence operations and lawmakers who've been briefed by the intelligence community say Kremlin-backed groups are renewing familiar tactics they've used to sow distrust and discord in previous elections in the U.S. and around the world.\n\"It's a carnival out there,\" Rep.Jim Himes of Connecticut, the ranking member on the House intelligence committee, told reporters this week. \"They're throwing everything out there to generate a sense of chaos and anarchy.\"\nThe goal is to exacerbate divisions, erode confidence in democracy and, crucially, undermine U.S. support for Ukraine.\n\"At the end of the day, the main issue for Russia is and has to be Ukraine,\" said Darren Linvill, co-director of Clemson University's Media Forensics Hub, who has tracked foreign propaganda campaigns for years.\nLinvill says the Russian playbook is consistent. A false claim or divisive narrative is seeded on social media or on a fake website posing as a news organization or think tank. It's spread by state media, automated fake accounts or, increasingly, paid influencers, in the hopes that it gets seen and repeated by real people.\n\"In the same way that marketing companies will pay an influencer to disseminate, you know, 'Nike's a great shoe,' the Russians do it with false information and made-up lies,\" he said. \"They use influencers that know their audience, that are part of that audience, and they know how to give that audience a message that they want to hear,\" Linvill said.\nA Russian operation put that playbook to work this week to spread a baseless accusation against a Democratic U.S. Senate candidate.\nLinvill and several other research groups attributed them to Russian operators the research community has dubbedStorm-1516. It's known for staging fake videos with actors and laundering their claims through influencer accounts with large followings.\nTwo fraudulent videos targeting the Senate candidate were posted on Tuesday on X by an account created last month that had few followers. The account's X profile said it was based in the U.S. but the social media platform publicly flagged it as possibly using a proxy such as a virtual private network, which could disguise its location.\nThe videos were likely created using artificial intelligence, according to Graphika, a research company that analyzes online communities. Linvill said a hallmark of Storm-1516 videos is the use of real actors whose faces are swapped using AI to look like someone else.\nThe post was immediately shared by a network of 15 accounts Linvill flagged as bots. About 12 hours later, one of the videos was shared in new posts by three X accounts with large followings, which researchers said have previously amplified other narratives attributed to Storm-1516. One of them has solicited paid partnerships on X. Soon other X accounts began resharing the large accounts' posts in replies to politicians and journalists.\nOne of the two fake videos racked up roughly 857,000 views within 24 hours, according to an analysis by Alethea, a threat intelligence company. By late on Wednesday, the account that first posted the videos and one of the large accounts that reshared the claim had been suspended by X, and the two other large accounts that amplified it had taken down their posts.\nIn the run-up to the 2024 presidential election, Storm-1516 posted fraudulent videos making similar unfounded accusationsagainst Democratic vice presidential nominee Tim Walzand purporting to showballots for President Trump being torn up.\nLinvill says the operation tries to glom on to narratives that are already circulating among Americans. \"It is hard to persuade anybody of anything, ever,\" he said. \"But what you can do is reinforce beliefs.\"\nThis election cycle, Russian operations have also spread messages about Democrats turning away from supporting Israel and fabricated news reports accusing Democratic candidates of corruption.\n\"I increasingly have come to think of Russia as essentially a dark money PAC,\" said Lisa Kaplan, who founded Alethea. \"You know they're going to be running advertisements. You want to understand what's out there. You want to understand if they're actually effective in moving people in one direction or another.\"\nIntelligence officials have long said Russia has favored Trump and targeted Democrats in its efforts to erode support for Ukraine in the U.S.\nOn Wednesday, Himes and Sen. Mark Warner, D-Va., vice chairman of the Senate intelligence committee, told reporters they'd seen a recent classified intelligence report on foreign election meddling showing Russia opposes candidates of either party who have backed Ukraine.\nHimes stressed that while Russia and other foreign countries have sought to influence voters, there has been no indication that they have been able to interfere with the actual mechanics of voting.\n\"Nothing out there suggests that there is either the capability or the intent to do interference, whereas there is a pretty robust influence environment,\" Himes said.\nThe same Russian operations now active in the U.S. midterms have also targeted elections this year in France, Germany and elsewhere. They've used AI-generated audio and impersonated politicians, celebrities and media outlets, Graphika said in a new report on Friday.\n\"They have run this playbook in half a dozen other countries in the past six months throughout Europe. We're just the next in line,\" Linvill said. \"They attack with these same themes. Anything that's going to make a candidate seem degenerate, seem corrupt, seem incapable.\"\nKaplan of Alethea says this focus on polarizing issues in the final stretch of the election is less about shaping voter's views and more about inflaming anger or fear to affect turnout.\n\"In these final posts in the lead up to the election, it's really more about evoking that emotion,\" she said.\nResearchers tracking Storm-1516 and other groups say they expect more false claims and fake videos to come, and for these operations to focus on voting and ballot processing in the period immediately surrounding election day.\nNPR's Eric McDaniel contributed to this report.\nNPR does not offer or accept money for coverage or interviews.Anyone who makes such an offer or request is not an NPR employee and does not represent NPR.\nSponsor Message\nBecome an NPR sponsor", "date": "Fri, 23 Oct 2026 17:40:02 -0400"}, {"title": "Company says these smart insoles could help avid runners avoid injury", "url": "https://www.cbsnews.com/video/striv-smart-insoles-runners-injury/", "body": "Watch CBS News\n48 Hours\nLatest Baltimore News & Headlines\nLatest NorCal News & Headlines\nWBZ News Weekend 6pm\nCBS News Chicago Saturday 5pm\nLatest Colorado News & Headlines\nCBS News Detroit Weekend 6pm\nLatest Los Angeles News & Headlines\nLatest Miami News & Headlines\nWCCO News at 5pm\nLatest New York News & Headlines\nJersey Pulse\nKDKA News Saturday 6pm\nYour Stories From Your Community Reporters\nCBS News Texas Saturday 5pm\nCopyright \u00a92026 CBS Interactive Inc. All rights reserved.", "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 18:01:15 -0400"}, {"title": "Breast cancer impacting more young women every year, studies show", "url": "https://www.cbsnews.com/video/breast-cancer-impacting-more-young-women-every-year-studies-show/", "body": "Watch CBS News\n48 Hours\nLatest Baltimore News & Headlines\nLatest NorCal News & Headlines\nWBZ News Weekend 6pm\nCBS News Chicago Saturday 5pm\nLatest Colorado News & Headlines\nCBS News Detroit Weekend 6pm\nLatest Los Angeles News & Headlines\nLatest Miami News & Headlines\nWCCO News at 5pm\nLatest New York News & Headlines\nJersey Pulse\nKDKA News Saturday 6pm\nYour Stories From Your Community Reporters\nCBS News Texas Saturday 5pm\nCopyright \u00a92026 CBS Interactive Inc. All rights reserved.", "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 17:42:10 -0400"}, {"title": "From the archives: Paul Stankard's dazzling paperweights", "url": "https://www.cbsnews.com/video/from-the-archives-paul-stankards-dazzling-paperweights/", "body": "Watch CBS News\n48 Hours\nLatest Baltimore News & Headlines\nLatest NorCal News & Headlines\nWBZ News Weekend 6pm\nCBS News Chicago Saturday 5pm\nLatest Colorado News & Headlines\nCBS News Detroit Weekend 6pm\nLatest Los Angeles News & Headlines\nLatest Miami News & Headlines\nWCCO News at 5pm\nLatest New York News & Headlines\nJersey Pulse\nKDKA News Saturday 6pm\nYour Stories From Your Community Reporters\nCBS News Texas Saturday 5pm\nCopyright \u00a92026 CBS Interactive Inc. All rights reserved.", "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 17:27:58 -0400"}, {"title": "Foldable iPhone Duo hits market amid debate about price, features", "url": "https://www.cbsnews.com/video/iphone-duo-price-features/", "body": "Watch CBS News\n48 Hours\nLatest Baltimore News & Headlines\nLatest NorCal News & Headlines\nWBZ News Weekend 6pm\nCBS News Chicago Saturday 5pm\nLatest Colorado News & Headlines\nCBS News Detroit Weekend 6pm\nLatest Los Angeles News & Headlines\nLatest Miami News & Headlines\nWCCO News at 5pm\nLatest New York News & Headlines\nJersey Pulse\nKDKA News Saturday 6pm\nYour Stories From Your Community Reporters\nCBS News Texas Saturday 5pm\nCopyright \u00a92026 CBS Interactive Inc. All rights reserved.", "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 17:25:04 -0400"}, {"title": "The controversy surrounding convicted former election official Tina Peters", "url": "https://www.cbsnews.com/video/the-controversy-surrounding-convicted-former-election-official-tina-peters/", "body": "Watch CBS News\n48 Hours\nLatest Baltimore News & Headlines\nLatest NorCal News & Headlines\nWBZ News Weekend 6pm\nCBS News Chicago Saturday 5pm\nLatest Colorado News & Headlines\nCBS News Detroit Weekend 6pm\nLatest Los Angeles News & Headlines\nLatest Miami News & Headlines\nWCCO News at 5pm\nLatest New York News & Headlines\nJersey Pulse\nKDKA News Saturday 6pm\nYour Stories From Your Community Reporters\nCBS News Texas Saturday 5pm\nCopyright \u00a92026 CBS Interactive Inc. All rights reserved.", "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 17:23:40 -0400"}, {"title": "Elon Musk says SpaceX throttled Ukraine Starlink speeds over drone strikes", "url": "https://www.cbsnews.com/news/ukraine-starlink-drone-spacex-musk-speed-restriction/", "body": "Watch CBS News\nUpdated on:  October 24, 2026 / 5:23 PM EDT/ CBS/AP\nElon Musk said on Saturday that his company SpaceX had throttled the Starlink satellite internet service in Ukraine, disrupting operations that the country relies on to defend itself against Russia.\nMusk's admission on social media came after Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyysaidthat Ukraine's access to Starlink and Starshield satellite links has been restored to the speeds it had before restrictions that disrupted front-line operations for more than a week.\nQuestions have persisted since last week about who ordered the interruptions to the Musk-made satellite internet system that Kyiv has relied on for years tocounter Moscow's attacks.\n\"The speeds are now where they need to be,\" Zelenskyy wrote on X, adding that restoration began overnight and were sufficient by Saturday morning, improving front-line logistics and defense against Russian jet-powered Shahed drones.\nBefore Musk's confirmation, questions persisted last week about who ordered the interruptions to the Musk-made satellite internet system that Ukraine has relied on for years to counter Moscow's attacks.\nPresident Trump had said he didn't make the call, while also saying Musk should not be blamed. On Tuesday, CBS News reported thatMusk was behindthe disruption.\nIn a post Saturday on X, Musk acknowledged that his company SpaceX throttled the Starlink satellite internet service.\n\"Using Starlink, a civilian commercial service, for offensive drone strikes to kill people has never been allowed, nor should it be,\" Musk wrote. \"Ukraine military violated Starlink terms of service and used it for offensive drone strikes to kill people. That loophole was closed last week.\"\nBut he maintained that the U.S. government controlled whatever changes happened with Starshield, SpaceX's military-grade version of the Starlink satellite internet service and thebackboneof Ukraine's drone operations and communication. He also defended the role of SpaceX in bolstering Ukraine's defenses since Russia's full-scale invasion of Ukraine in February 2022.\nRestrictions imposed last weekon Starshield alarmed the country's military and government officials, with commanders reporting lost drones and disrupted missions.\nThe restoration follows a new U.S. diplomatic push to end the war. Russia agreed Wednesday to resume three-way negotiations with Washington and Kyiv next month. On Friday, Kyiv and Moscow also agreed to a localized ceasefire brokered by first lady Melania Trump around Oleshky, a war-ravaged, Russian-occupied town in Ukraine's southern Kherson region.\nThe episode added to tensions between Kyiv and Washington, a relationship already strained by Mr. Trump's reported deal with Moscow on Russian diesel imports, which Ukraine viewed as a betrayal.\nKhmara credited \"direct dialogue\" and Zelenskyy's diplomacy with Washington, adding that the outcome mattered most \"for our front, for the military, the work of drones, logistics and communications.\"\nZelenskyy on Saturday thanked Americans and \"all our partners, who took all this seriously and are really helping us.\"\nIn:\n\u00a9 2026 CBS Interactive Inc. All Rights Reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. The Associated Press contributed to this report.\n(06:35)\nCopyright \u00a92026 CBS Interactive Inc. All rights reserved.", "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 17:23:29 -0400"}, {"title": "President Trump, first lady vote early in person in Florida", "url": "https://www.cbsnews.com/news/trump-melania-vote-early-in-person-florida-saturday/", "body": "Watch CBS News\nUpdated on:  October 24, 2026 / 5:01 PM EDT/ CBS News\nPresident Trump and first lady Melania Trump cast their votes for the midterms in person in Palm Beach County, Florida, on Saturday.\nMr. Trump, who has been on the campaign trail this week, voted at the Palm Beach County Supervisor of Elections building before jetting off to Georgia for a rally in support of Republican Reps. Mike Collins and Rick Jackson.\nEarlier this week, the president said he prefers to vote on Election Day. However, he is registered to vote in Palm Beach County.\nIn recent elections, Mr. Trump hasvoted by mail. The president, however, is critical of widespread mail-in voting.\nSaturday is the first day of early voting in Florida, which runs from Oct. 24 through Halloween.\nMr. Trump's Election Day plans are not yet clear. He didn't answer when reporters asked this week what he'll be doing that day.\nThe president is traveling most days to hold rallies for Republicans. He was in South Carolina Wednesday night and in Iowa Thursday night. On Friday night, he delivered remarks in Fort Myers, Florida.\nAhead of Saturday's rally in Perry, Georgia, Mr. Trump said Collins and Jackson are \"two incredible people.\"\n\"Their opponents are Radical Left Lowlifes and Lunatics who want to Take Away Your Guns, Open Your Borders, Allow Men To Play In Women's Sports, and have Transgender nonsense for everyone. I Won Georgia in a Record Vote. See you later!!!,\" he wrote onTruth Social.\nLucia I Suarez Sangcontributed to this report.\nIn:\n\u00a9 2026 CBS Interactive Inc. All Rights Reserved.\n(02:46)\nCopyright \u00a92026 CBS Interactive Inc. All rights reserved.", "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 17:01:28 -0400"}, {"title": "This week on \"Sunday Morning\" (Oct. 25)", "url": "https://www.cbsnews.com/news/this-week-on-sunday-morning-oct-25-2026/", "body": "Watch CBS News\nUpdated on:  October 24, 2026 / 3:56 PM EDT/ CBS News\nThe Emmy Award-winning \"CBS News Sunday Morning\" is broadcast on CBS Sundays beginning at 9:00 a.m. ET.\u00a0 \"Sunday Morning\" alsostreams on the CBS News appbeginning at 11:00 a.m. ET. (Download it here.)\nHosted by Jane Pauley\nCOVER STORY: The mystery of Houdini's deathHarry Houdini, the greatest escape artist who ever lived, died 100 years ago on Halloween \u2013 a fact that has only burnished his image as a daredevil who often cheated death. Correspondent David Pogue examines the life, and after-life, of a man who seemingly performed the impossible while also debunking seances and fake mystics; and looks into the mystery surrounding Houdini's death at age 52.\nFor more info:\nALMANAC: October 25\"Sunday Morning\" looks back at historical events on this date.\nWORLD: Israeli electionsMatt Gutman reports from Tel Aviv.\nENVIRONMENTS: Ghost signs: Fading reminders of the pastThey're painted advertisements from long ago, for businesses that may have since died. But remnants of these \"ghost signs\" still remain, haunting our urban spaces. Correspondent Conor Knighton talks with marketing expert Ronald Ladouceur about the origins of these outdoor ads; and with artist Craig Winslow, who is bringing ghost signs back to life.\nFor more info:\n\nMOVIES: \"The Mosquito Bowl\" and the human cost of warThe new Netflix film \"The Mosquito Bowl\" recounts the true story of All-American football stars who volunteered for the Marines following Pearl Harbor \u2013 and fought in the Battle of Okinawa, the last and costliest World War II battle in the Pacific. CBS News national security correspondent David Martin talks with director Peter Berg (\"Friday Night Lights\") about translating the story of young men trained for war to the screen.\nTo watch a trailer for \"The Mosquito Bowl,\" click on the video player below:\nFor more info:\nPASSAGE: In memoriam\"Sunday Morning\" remembers some of the notable figures who left us this week.\nWORLD: Meet the Tower of London's RavenmasterLegend warns that if the ravens leave London's Tower of London, the monument would crumble - as would the British monarchy. And so, they are kept, a well-fed draw for tourists. Correspondent Seth Doane meets the man charged with caring for these feathered protectors of the realm.\nFor more info:\nHARTMAN: TBD\nSUNDAY PROFILE: Parker PoseyIn the 1990s actress Parker Posey became a breakout star in independent films like \"Party Girl,\" and was dubbed \"The Queen of the Indies.\" Recently she earned an Emmy nomination for \"The White Lotus,\" and is featured in the new Martin McDonagh film \"Wild Horse Nine.\" She talks with correspondent (and fellow summer theater school classmate) Mo Rocca about her Southern upbringing, and how her parents inspired her acting.\nFor more info:\nCOMMENTARY: David Sedaris\nFor more info:\nMUSIC: How punk rock and MTV inspired opera composer Missy MazzoliComposer Missy Mazzoli is the first woman ever to have an opera premiered by the Metropolitan Opera. Based on the award-winning novel by George Saunders, Mazzoli and librettist Royce Vavrek's \"Lincoln in the Bardo\" depicts Abraham Lincoln mourning his 11-year-old son, Willie, whose passing has led the young boy to a threshold place between the living and the dead. Correspondent Faith Salie talks with Mazzoli about the opera and the influence that punk rock and music videos had on her work.\nFor more info:\n\nNATURE: Spiders in Florida\n\nMARATHON:Prison stories (YouTube)This collection of \"CBS Sunday Morning\" stories offers a glimpse inside the American prison system, showcasing the lives of those incarcerated within.\nThe Emmy Award-winning \"CBS News Sunday Morning\" is broadcast on CBS Sundays beginning at 9:00 a.m. ET. Executive producer is Rand Morrison.\n\"Sunday Morning\": About us\nDVR Alert! Find out when \"Sunday Morning\" airs in your city\n\"Sunday Morning\" alsostreams on the CBS News appbeginning at 11:00 a.m. ET. (Download it here.)\nFull episodes of \"Sunday Morning\" are now available to watch on demand on CBSNews.com, CBS.com andParamount+, including via Apple TV, Android TV, Roku, Chromecast, Amazon FireTV/FireTV stick and Xbox.\nFollow us onTwitter/X;Facebook;Instagram;YouTube;TikTok;Bluesky; and atcbssundaymorning.com.\nYou can also download the free\"Sunday Morning\" audio podcastatiTunesand atPlay.it. Now you'll never miss the trumpet!\nDo you have sun art you wish to share with us? Email your suns to SundayMorningSuns@cbsnews.com.\n\u00a9 2026 CBS Interactive Inc. All Rights Reserved.\n\nCopyright \u00a92026 CBS Interactive Inc. All rights reserved.", "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 15:56:23 -0400"}, {"title": "More than 1,000 arrested in France as protestors and police clash", "url": "https://www.cbsnews.com/news/paris-france-new-protests-riots-museums-shops/", "body": "Watch CBS News\nUpdated on:  October 24, 2026 / 3:48 PM EDT/ CBS/AP\nNearly 1,000 people were arrested in nationwide protests in France, Interior Minister Laurent Nu\u00f1ez said Saturday, after demonstrations over the high cost of living and the country's education system earlier in the day remained largely peaceful.\nNu\u00f1ez said the number of arrests is expected to rise significantly. He said marches \"passed off well\" in cities, but police officers found themselves confronted with \"an extremely elevated level of violence\" from \"ultra-left\" groups later in the day. Nu\u00f1ez said some shops and businesses had been looted, but \"the scale was far smaller\" than a week earlier, when some 3,000 businesses were damaged or looted.\nHe said police acted more rapidly and conducted more pre-demonstration checks. Businesses and shopping centers had also been better protected. Earlier Saturday, the interior minister said police had confiscated weapons including metal balls, lead pellets, fireworks, slingshots, steel balls, improvised weapons, a katana and airsoft weapons, he said.\nThe French Interior Ministry said about 410,000 people participated in about 298protests around the country. Union organizers from the General Confederation of Labor put the number at around 800,000.\u00a0 An explanation for the discrepancy was not immediately available.\nSome confrontations were reported between authorities and demonstrators in several cities, but as of Saturday evening there had been no repeat of the major street violence and rioting that erupted amid similar protests last week.\nNu\u00f1ez previously said that French authorities werepreparing to maintain orderthis weekend by maintaining up to 70,000 officers nationwide along with assets including helicopters, armored vehicles and water cannons.\nNu\u00f1ez said authorities will remain \"on high alert\" overnight and into Saturday.\nParis had prepared for unrest, with shops boarded up and events including those related to theNFL's first French football gamecanceled, but authorities described the atmosphere in the city as \"calm.\"\nThousands gathered at the Porte d'Italie on the city's southeastern rim for an authorized demonstration. The march had originally been set for the Bastille, near the city's center, and was changed to the more remote Porte d'Italie at the last minute. Union representative Murielle Guilbert told reporters that it was \"scandalous\" that Macron had forbidden protesters from demonstrating \"where we wish to demonstrate,\" but participants complied with the change.\nThe front of the march ended without incident. Some chanted \"We are not happy\" as they peeled away from the Porte de Vincennes. Police said 110,000 people participated in the event, while union members put the number at 250,000.\nAs the evening went on, clashes were reported between police and demonstrators, the Agence France-Presse reported. Nu\u00f1ez said that \"several thousand radical activists\" had \"gathered at the rear of the march, including around 500 individuals identified as rioters.\"\nClashes between protesters and police lasted about two hours, he said.\nThe Paris prosecutor's office said 64 people, including six minors, were arrested and 40 were placed in police custody. Most arrests concerned suspected weapons offenses or participation in a group preparing violence or property damage, the office said. More than 3,000 were stopped and checked by officers, police said.\nIn Orleans, thousands of people marched against a congress held by Marine Le Pen's far-right National Rally party. Le Pen is launching her campaign to succeed Emmanuel Macron as France's president. Elections will be held in six months, and surveys show Le Pen is best-placed to win. Demonstrators in Orleans protested both Macron and Le Pen.\n\"Macron resign. Le Pen in prison,\" the crowd yelled, according to the AFP. Police put their numbers at 11,000, while organizers said 26,000 took part. About 50 organizations, including labor unions, left-wing parties and student groups, had called for a protest against Le Pen's nationalist, anti-immigration party.\nSome placards seen at the march referenceda shooting in Trappes, a Paris suburb, that left one person dead and several others injured across several locations. Macron called the rampage a \"racist terror attack.\" Prosecutors are investigating the shooting as a terrorist incident.\nFrench media reported that the protest against the congress ended peacefully early Saturday afternoon. The gathering is expected to continue through Sunday.\nDemonstrations had been banned in the southeast city of Lyon, but about 150 protesters near the city's center briefly tried to block a road. Police fired tear gas to disperse the group. Tear gas was also used to break up a group of around 1,000 people gathered in the south of the city. Street fires were also reported.\nA police helicopter was seen flying over the city, and security forces, armored vehicles and riot police vans were patrolling. A 21-year-old student, who spoke to the Agence France-Presse on the condition of anonymity, described authorities as \"really mobile.\"\nIn the western city of Nantes, thousands of protesters broke through police lines to enter the city center, the AFP reported. Officers used tear gas and a water cannon to try and disperse them. Some on the fringes of the demonstration threw projectiles and aimed fireworks at officers.\nPolice said about 17,000 attended an event in Marseille. Officers fired tear gas during clashes with several dozen people near the march's endpoint. Street fires were seen in the city.\nTear gas was also deployed in Clermont-Ferrand, Bordeaux and Strasbourg, police said. Nu\u00f1ez said one person was seriously injured in Clermont-Ferrand, but did not provide any details.\nIn the city of Toulouse, a 20-year-old man reportedly had fingers blown off, Nu\u00f1ez said. He did not provide more details about the person, their condition, or how they came to be injured.\nNu\u00f1ez said that in the southern cities of Cannes and Montpellier, authorities saw far-right activists who claimed they were there to protect businesses or sought confrontations with \"ultra-left militants.\" He said it was the first time authorities witnessed this phenomenon.\nIn Nice, where demonstrations had also been banned, 11 people were arrested after far-right extremists fought with demonstrators downtown, regional authorities said. Water cannons were also used in the city.\nOne group of protesters, the Yellow Vests, said they would hold another round of protests on Oct. 31. The Yellow Vests emerged about eight years ago as a loosely organized group that staged disruptive protests against Macron.\nOrganizers called for rallies and a sit-in outside parliament and city halls nationwide, condemning the \"authoritarian drift\" of Macron's government and urging citizens to stand up for \"public freedoms.\"\nThe demonstrations have been ongoing since September, when high school students in the southeast of Paris protested for better conditions. The unrestquickly spread across the countryas people took to the streets to demand relief from rising living costs.\nOther protests took place across Europe on Saturday, encompassing a broad range of issues including pensions, housing costs, anti-war movements, education and corruption.\nItalian police said in a statement that they carried out nine arrests and briefly detained 371 people during protests in eight cities on Saturday. Of the people who were detained and taken to police stations, 110 were minors, the statement said.\nSoldiers were deployed to Milan, the nation's fashion and finance capital, ahead of fresh demonstrations one week after looting and police clashes. Many stores were boarded up with wooden planks on Saturday. Premier Giorgia Meloni announced a raft of measures, including sending an additional 500 soldiers to Milan and tougher penalties for minors, who police said made up a large part of the violent protesters.\nIn Rome, authorities locked down luxury shopping streets and stepped up patrols around key sites. Several hundred students demonstrated peacefully in the historic city. Organizers of peaceful events sought to distance themselves from potential unrest, urging participants to keep their faces uncovered and their hands visible.\nIn Albania's capital of Tirana, riot police used water cannons to subdue large groups of protesters. Some of the demonstrators threw projectiles at police, the AP reported. A separate protest organized by the opposition outside the office of Prime Minister Edi Rama turned violent earlier Saturday, with some activists lighting fires on the street. The demonstrators are mainly high school and university students, demanding lower tuition fees, greater state investment in education and protesting corruption in the education system. A broader anti-government movement is calling for Rama's resignation.\nIn Spain, thousands of retirees and others marched through central Madrid to demand that public pensions aren't meddled with.\nProtesters in Lisbon, Portugal, are expected to march across the city Saturday to demand affordable housing.\nAFPcontributed to this report.\nIn:\n\u00a9 2026 CBS Interactive Inc. All Rights Reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. The Associated Press contributed to this report.\n(02:58)\nCopyright \u00a92026 CBS Interactive Inc. All rights reserved.", "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 15:48:12 -0400"}, {"title": "Malik Beasley negotiating plea deal over NBA betting charges", "url": "https://www.cbsnews.com/news/malik-beasley-sports-betting-plea-deal-negotiation/", "body": "Watch CBS News\nOctober 24, 2026 / 3:06 PM EDT/ CBS/AP\nFormer NBA player Malik Beasley is negotiating a plea agreement with the Department of Justice after initially pleading not guilty to charges that he altered his play in certain games in 2024 to enrich sports bettors and ease his own debts, his attorney said Saturday.\nIn July, Beasley entered a not guilty plea in New York federal court. He was among the latest big names caught up in asweeping federal gambling investigation.\nBeasley's attorney, Steve Haney, told The Associated Press in a text message that they were negotiating an agreement with the government \"with an expected direction in the coming days.\"\nBeasley, 29, and sports agent Paolo Zamorano, who has also pleaded not guilty, were among six people charged in anindictment unsealedthis summer detailing a gambling scandal that has involved more than three dozen arrests. All six of them were charged with wire fraud conspiracy, bribery in sporting contests, honest services wire fraud conspiracy, and money laundering conspiracy for their alleged roles in the gambling scheme, according to prosecutors.\nIn April, former NBA player Damon Jones, 49, became thefirst person to plead guiltyin the gambling sweep.\nZamorano, 39, formerly represented another co-defendant, ex-NBA player Ed Davis, who had lent money to Beasley and is accused of acting as his \"gatekeeper\" in the alleged scheme.\nBeasley is accused of fixing or trying to fix his performance in at least four games while playing for the Milwaukee Bucks in 2024 by under- or overperforming bookmakers' expectations. In exchange, the indictment said, the bettors bribed Beasley \u2014 and his debts to Davis were reduced or eliminated.\n\"Only way you can beat Vegas is sports betting,\" Davis told Beasley in a Jan. 26, 2024, text message, according to the indictment. \"Everything else they got the edge.\"\nBeasley borrowed money from Davis, a former teammate, after racking up millions of dollars in gambling losses. Hiswidely reportedfinancial problems include disputes with a Detroit landlord, a Milwaukee barber and a Minnesota dentist. A 2025 lawsuit from a sports marketing agency resulted in a $1 million default judgment against him.\nBeasley has been out of the NBA since playing with the Detroit Pistons in 2024-25 on a one-year, $6 million contract. He averaged 16 points per game and scored 20 in his last game, a playoff loss to the New York Knicks. He is one of five players in NBA history with more than 300 three-pointers in a season.\nDavis was a journeyman who was primarily a backup in his 12-year career that saw him play for eight franchises and earn roughly $48 million in gross salary. He averaged 5.9 points and 6.4 rebounds per game and last appeared in the NBA in 2022.\nBeasley and Davis were briefly teammates in Minnesota in the 2020-21 season.\nIn:\n\u00a9 2026 CBS Interactive Inc. All Rights Reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. The Associated Press contributed to this report.\n(00:50)\nCopyright \u00a92026 CBS Interactive Inc. All rights reserved.", "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 15:06:00 -0400"}, {"title": "Trump, Vance defend controversial Ramaswamy attack ad; Obama campaigns with Amy Acton", "url": "https://www.cbsnews.com/video/trump-vance-defend-controversial-ramaswamy-attack-ad-obama-campaigns-with-amy-acton-1/", "body": "Watch CBS News\n48 Hours\nLatest Baltimore News & Headlines\nLatest NorCal News & Headlines\nWBZ News Weekend 6pm\nCBS News Chicago Saturday 5pm\nLatest Colorado News & Headlines\nCBS News Detroit Weekend 6pm\nLatest Los Angeles News & Headlines\nLatest Miami News & Headlines\nWCCO News at 5pm\nLatest New York News & Headlines\nJersey Pulse\nKDKA News Saturday 6pm\nYour Stories From Your Community Reporters\nCBS News Texas Saturday 5pm\nCopyright \u00a92026 CBS Interactive Inc. All rights reserved.", "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 14:29:35 -0400"}, {"title": "Deadly shooting in Paris suburb investigated as terrorism, officials say", "url": "https://www.cbsnews.com/news/gunman-terrorist-attack-paris-suburb/", "body": "Watch CBS News\nUpdated on:  October 24, 2026 / 2:14 PM EDT/ CBS/AFP\nA gunman shooting from his car killed one person and injured at least 5 others, including a 12-year-old girl, in the Paris suburb of Trappes on Friday, in what authorities described as a racially motivated act of terrorism.\nPresident Emmanuel Macron called it \"a racist terror attack\" and said the suspect's statements suggested it was premeditated.\n\"These people were targeted because of their skin color or perceived religion, and for no other reason,\" Macron said following a meeting with officials and residents. \"There is no place for this in France.\"\nProsecutors said they were investigating whether the acts targeted the \"real or supposed race, ethnicity, nationality or religion\" of the victims. They said they have opened an investigation for \"murder in connection with a terrorist enterprise.\"\nThe alleged shooter was \"firing at random, wanting to cause a bloodbath\" as he drove through Trappes and neighboring La Verriere, Trappes Mayor Ali Rabeh told broadcaster BFMTV.\n\"This is probably one of the first attempted mass killings motivated by Islamophobia and racism,\" Rabeh told reporters, the Associated Press reported. \"This was a terrorist attack with racist, likely Islamophobic, overtones.\"\nInterior Minister Laurent Nu\u00f1ez said the suspect fired \"what appears to have been a military-grade weapon, or at least an automatic weapon.\" Rabeh called it a \"miracle\" that there were not more casualties. Nu\u00f1ez said the gunman \"clearly\" committed \"a terrorist crime\" that may have been racially motivated.\nRabeh said the gunman targeted passersby at three separate locations, including a playground. Forensic investigators were also seen examining evidence at a gas station.\nA police source told the Agence France-Presse news agency that the gunman had fired in bursts from his vehicle around 10:30 a.m. local time. Samy, a resident of Trappes, about 13 miles west of Paris, told AFP he saw a body on the ground from his window. His mother, he said, saw a gray Renault Clio car speeding by and heard \"loud bangs.\"\nThe alleged shooter was arrested in the nearby town of Coignieres. Nu\u00f1ez said the attack lasted just under 15 minutes and was stopped by five officers from the anti-crime brigade.\nPrime Minister Sebastien Lecornu said on X that the case was \"very serious\" and that \"all resources of the security forces and the judiciary are fully mobilised.\" Macron said \"the nation is standing with\" the victims of this \"terrible ordeal.\"\nThe suspect has been partly identified as Kevin T.He is 39 years old.The National Anti-Terrorism Prosecutor's Office said Saturday that his detention has been extended.\nInvestigators said the suspect had booby-trapped his apartment with gas bottles and electrical wires connected to a timer. He also had tear gas grenades in his car, officials said.\nA source close to the investigation told the AFP that a copy of Adolf Hitler's \"Mein Kampf\" was found in the suspect's apartment. A Bible and Quran were also found, the source said.\nThe prosecutor's office said Saturday that \"three additional individuals\" from the suspect's \"family and circle of friends\" were placed in police custody on Friday evening and overnight. The office did not say why those individuals were placed in custody.\nThe AFP reported later Saturday that four members of the suspect's family and circle of friends were arrested and were being questioned by investigators. A source close to the case told AFP that the suspect's parents were among those under arrest.\nThe office also said the suspect's criminal record includes four prior convictions, all of which had been legally expunged. The office did not say what those convictions were for or when they occurred.\nThe deceased victim was identified as Pengo Bossio Wangi, a 26-year-old man of African origin. He was an up-and-coming mixed martial arts fighter.\nHundreds of people rallied Saturday at the spot where he was killed, as other protests swept across France. The demonstrators held placards that called for \"justice\" and led a moment of silence honoring Wangi.\n\"It could have been anyone. He opened fire on Blacks, Arabs. He knew the majority of the population here in Trappes is Muslim,\" a local shopkeeper of North African origin told AFP.\nWangi's family said the shooter was known to authorities, and demanded to know whether he had been properly monitored before the incident. In a statement released by the family's lawyer, they called for an investigation into potential failures on the part of the authorities and \"to exhaustively establish the path that led to (the shooter's) radicalisation.\"\nOfficials initially said four people had been wounded, but Rabeh told reporters there were five, including three who were very seriously injured, with their lives in danger.\nA 12-year-old girl was seriously wounded after being shot eight times, but is in stable condition, the mayor of La Verriere, Nicolas Dainville, told the AFP.\nThe injured victims have not been identified. Rabeh said that one of the wounded, \"in critical condition, is of North African origin.\"\nThe shooting came with France on edge followingriots that eruptedamid mass protests over the cost of living, which saw some of theworst street violencein recent years. Demonstrators, including students, labor union members and activists from France's \"Yellow Vest\" movement,took to the streets again Saturdayacross the country.\nFrench police have maintained a large presence to maintain order. Dozens of arrests were reported, and some groups were tear-gassed. Resources including elite police units, helicopters, armored vehicles and water cannons have been deployed.\nIn the city of Orleans, thousands of people marched against a congress held by Marine Le Pen's far-right National Rally party. About 50 organizations, including labor unions, left-wing parties and student groups, had called for a protest against Le Pen's nationalist, anti-immigration group. Some placards seen at the march referenced the shooting in Trappes.\n\"Racism kills. The far right kills,\" a lawmaker from the far-left France Unbowed (LFI) party, Mathilde Panot, said at the rally near the spot of the shooting. Demonstrators also protested against Macron's policies and called on him to resign.\nLe Pen called for a \"relentless\" investigation and said it was \"despicable\" if racism was the shooter's motive.\nThe Associated  Presscontributed to this report.\nIn:\n\u00a9 2026 CBS Interactive Inc. All Rights Reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. Agence France-Presse contributed to this report.\n(02:40)\nCopyright \u00a92026 CBS Interactive Inc. All rights reserved.", "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 14:14:41 -0400"}, {"title": "Iran says U.S. military not allowed in Gulf \"under any circumstance\"", "url": "https://www.cbsnews.com/live-updates/iran-war-trump-us-oil-gas-prices-strait-of-hormuz-midterms/", "body": "Watch CBS News\nUpdated on:  October 24, 2026 / 1:40 PM EDT/ CBS News\nAyatollah Mojtaba Khamenei, who has still not been seen in public since the beginning of the war, met with Hujjat al-Islam Mohseni Ejei, the head of Iran's judiciary, to discuss a range of issues on Saturday.\nMost interestingly, in connection to the war,a readout of the meeting on Xsaid Iran was committed to \"resolutely pursuing compensation for material and moral damages arising from the crimes committed by\" the U.S. and Israel.\nOne of Iran's demands in the past as part of a peace agreement was the U.S. would have to pay to rebuild much of the damage in the country. The demand was dismissed by the U.S.\nWhile not specific, the statement also said they discussed \"making the fullest possible use of modern technologies.\"\nKhamenei was injured in a strike on the palace that killed his father, the Ayatollah Ali Khamenei. The strike on the first day of the war also killed the leader's daughter, granddaughter, son-in-law and daughter-in-law. Mojtaba Khamenei survived though he's been in hiding ever since the strike.\nIran remains open to talks with the U.S. under a Pakistan-brokered interim deal reached in June to end the conflict, Tehran's ambassador to Islamabad said Saturday, accusing Washington of undermining diplomacy and prolonging the standoff over the Strait of Hormuz.\nAmbassador Reza Amiri Moghadam said Iran recently conveyed through Pakistan it was prepared to reopen the Strait of Hormuz if Washington lifted its naval blockade, but accused President Trump of rejecting the proposal by insisting the blockade of Iranian ports would continue.\nPakistan and Qatar helped broker the June agreement, which sought to restore shipping through the strait, lift the U.S. naval blockade and begin negotiations toward a broader deal addressing Iran's nuclear program.\nMoghadam, speaking at a seminar in Islamabad, said Iranian officials had conveyed their willingness to reopen the strait during visits to Tehran by Pakistan's army chief, Field Marshal Asim Munir, provided Washington ended its blockade.\nMoghadam also said Tehran would study the Mecca Joint Defense Agreement that includes Pakistan, Saudi Arabia and Turkey before deciding whether to support it, describing it as a potential instrument for unity. \"We will study the Mecca Agreement, and if it is good, we will support it,\" he said.\nIranian President Masoud Pezeshkian said Saturday many Iranians were struggling to put food on the table, as economic pressure from the war with the United States mounts.\nThe U.S. has in recent weeks dialed back military operations in favor of economic pressure, while Iran's leaders have said peace negotiations will only succeed without pressure and threats from Washington as the conflict remains locked in a stalemate.\n\"There are many people in this society who are struggling to put food on the table because of the war we have found ourselves in, because of the wartime conditions we are living under,\" Pezeshkian said in a speech shared on his website.\nPezeshkian called for an end to what he described as a \"state of neither war nor peace\", saying Tehran \"must also get out of this situation,\" without specifying how.\nHis remarks came as fears of renewed fighting grew. On Friday, U.S. Ambassador to Turkey Tom Barrack said U.S. strikes on Iran could resume as soon as this weekend.\n\"Could there be a strike this weekend? Absolutely,\" Barrack told podcast host Mario Nawfal. Barrack, who also serves as Washington's envoy on Syria, does not have a direct role on Iran.\nAFP/CBS\nYemen's Iran-backed Houthis claimed an attack Saturday on a naval base and a military camp in Aden, the interim seat of the internationally recognized government, after blasts were heard there.\n\"Our armed forces targeted Saudi enemy concentrations at the so-called Al-Nasr camp and the naval base in Aden,\" Houthi military spokesman Yahya Saree said, adding the sites were overseen by Saudi forces.\nThe Houthis fighting the Saudi-backed government have targeted the southern city several times in recent weeks, including the presidential palace.\nThe Houthis said they attacked a Saudi position in Aden on Wednesday, with smoke seen rising near the city's airport.\nLast week, two missiles also landed within the perimeter of the airport, with the Houthis claiming the attack.\nThe U.S. State Departmenthas updated travel advisoriesfor 14 countries in the Middle East \"to reflect the threat of Iranian-supported Houthi regional aggression.\"\nThe advisories also followthe public disclosure of a memofrom Director of National Intelligence Jay Clayton to Secretary of State Marco Rubio warning of Iranian plots to kill Americans reaching \"advanced stages\" in the Middle East and elsewhere.\nThe warnings come as the Trump administration weighs whether to restart military strikes against Iran, and whether to provide air support to Saudi forces confronting the Houthis.\nAn official with Iran's Revolutionary Guard took a swipe at the U.S. and President Trump's repeated threats, calling them \"largely for media consumption\" and saying the country is ready if there are renewed hostilities.\n\"The enemy itself knows better than anyone that it cannot carry out effective operations under current conditions and will certainly fail to achieve its objectives,\" IRGC spokesperson Brig. Gen. Hossein Mohebbi told semi-official Fars news agency on Saturday.\nMr. Trump has said any possible strikes on Iran will wait until after the U.S. midterm elections on Nov. 3, but he has repeatedly said he could end the war with Iran any time he wants through renewed military action.\nMohebbi said Iran will be ready if the U.S. does return to military strikes.\n\"Even if the enemy undertakes limited action, Iran's response will be several times greater, given the existing state of readiness and the operational plans of the armed forces,\" he said.\nThe United Arab Emirates has closed beaches and warned fishermen to avoid parts of its Gulf shores after an oil spill from a tanker.\nThe spill impacted the beaches of Umm Al Quwain, one of the seven emirates making up the federation, and cleanup efforts were underway, the Climate Change and Environment Ministry said. Images obtained by AP showed oil washing up along the beach in Umm Al Quwain.\nThe ministry said the spill poses no health risks but did not provide details about the scale of the leak.\nThe spill came two weeks after Iran's state-run broadcaster reported an oil tanker had struck a naval mine and caught fire while attempting to transit through the Strait of Hormuz without coordinating with Iran.\nTwo days of strikes on Yemen have killed nine people including women and children, according to the Iranian-backed Houthi rebels.\nTwo separate strikes killed five people, including a woman and child, in the Yemeni capital of Sanaa on Saturday, according to the Houthi-run Health Ministry.\nAlso Saturday, the Houthi media center said a wave of strikes struck the coastal city of Mokha in Taiz province, which the Houthis captured in early September. The center didn't provide details on what was struck and The Associated Press couldn't independently verify the Houthi claims.\nOn Friday, an airstrike killed four people \u2014 two women and two children \u2014 from the same family when it struck a house in the town of Hiba near the city of Taiz, a hotbed of violence in the country's revived civil war.\nThe Houthis blamed Saudi Arabia for the strikes, which came as fighting between the kingdom and the Iranian-backed rebels has escalated. Saudi Arabia did not respond to a request for comment.\nThe Saudi-led coalition fighting in Yemen announced on Saturday it had intercepted two missiles fired by the Houthis toward Taif and Yanbu, in the kingdom's west.\nIt came shortly after Riyadh's civil defence agency broadcast warnings of \"potential danger\" for four western regions, including Taif and Yanbu as well Mecca, home to Islam's holiest city, and Yanbu.\nThe alerts were all rescinded minutes later.\nResidents were instructed to \"proceed to the nearest safe place\" by the agency. The agency also repeated a legal warning against sharing images or information related to \"countering missiles and drones.\"\nThe Gulf kingdom has come under repeated attack from Yemen's Iran-backed Houthis in recent weeks as part of the latter country's revived civil war.\nYanbu is a major oil export hub on the Red Sea coast that has allowed Riyadh to bypass Iran's blockade of the Strait of Hormuz for much of its crude exports.\nIran said on Saturday that U.S. ships would never be allowed to enter the Gulf \"under any circumstances\" and President Trump will \"take that dream to his grave\".\nThe remarks came amid mounting US and Israeli threats of renewed attacks on Iran, as U.S. ambassador to Turkey Tom Barrack warned that Washington could \"absolutely\" strike Iran as soon as this weekend.\n\"We will not allow U.S. military ships in the Persian Gulf under any circumstances and at any time,\" said Iran's armed forces chief of staff Ali Abdollahi, according to Iranian state television.\n\"The US president must dream of seeing the armed forces enter the Persian Gulf. He will take that dream to his grave.\"\nCBS/AFP\nDirector of National Intelligence Jay Clayton warned Friday that Iran has \"substantially increased efforts to target U.S. personnel\" abroad, with plots to kill Americans reaching \"advanced stages\" in the Middle East and other countries.\nThe warning, contained in an unclassified memo that was sent to Secretary of State Marco Rubio and obtained by CBS News, comes as the State Departmentadjusts staffing at diplomatic posts across the Middle East, and the U.S. military pares back its footprint in the region, amid concerns about direct threats from Iran and threats from the Iranian-backed Houthi movement in Yemen.\n\"The lethal plotting efforts by the Iranians against U.S. citizens have reached advanced stages in both the Middle East and certain other countries,\" Clayton wrote.\nThe memo, which was issued as the Trump administration weighs resuming military strikes against Iran, does not identify specific targets or countries where the alleged plots are underway, or indicate whether attacks may be imminent.\nClayton said the U.S. intelligence community is working to downgrade sensitive intelligence \u2014 received through various channels \u2014 so it can be shared more broadly with State Department personnel. CIA Director John Ratcliffe was copied on the memo.\nRead more here.\nThe U.S. State Department says it will reduce staffing at four U.S. embassies in the Middle East, not including Saudi Arabia, where nonessential diplomats and family memberswere ordered to leaveearlier this week.\nThe staffing changes \u2014 driven by concerns that Yemen's Iran-backed Houthi militants may escalate rocket, missile and drone attacks around the region \u2014 will affect the embassies in Jordan, Lebanon, Qatar and the United Arab Emirates, but do not entail an order to leave, the department said.\nThe embassies involved are all under what is known as \"restricted operations\" status that has already reduced the size of their personnel footprints. The embassies will remain open.\nOn Wednesday, a U.S. official and a senior U.S. military official told CBS News that the U.S. military had been quietly reducing its footprint at posts in the Middle East due to ongoing regional threats from the Houthis and direct threats from Iran.\nCBS/AP\nIranian President Masoud Pezeshkian will travel to Moscow next week for talks with Russian President Vladimir Putin, Iranian Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi said, according to a report by Reuters.\nIt hasn't been long since Pezeshkian spoke to Putin in person, but this will be the first meeting between the two in Russia during the ongoing war.\nThe two leaders met with each other on the sidelines of the Caspian Environmental Summit in Turkmenistan on Oct. 8. The two also met at a summit in Kyrgyzstan in September.\nRussian Foreign Minister Sergey Lavrov visited Tehran earlier this week as the two sides continue to strengthen relations. Araghchi said Russia and Iran were closer than ever, despite President Trump saying Russia was doing little to help Iran a day prior.\nTime is running out to get aid into Taiz, besieged by pro-Iran Houthi fighters who are preventing supplies from reaching Yemen's third-largest city, the United Nations said Friday.\nHouthi fighters have encircled Taiz since early October, blocking the road that links it to Aden, interim capital of the Saudi-backed, internationally recognized government.\n\"Safe, sustained and unimpeded humanitarian access is urgently needed so that essential supplies and life-saving assistance can reach people in need,\" said the U.N. humanitarian agency OCHA.\n\"It's 20 days and counting since Taiz has been cut off,\" said OCHA spokesman Jens Laerke.\n\"We are seeing increasingly that the fighting makes it impossible for us to get in and impossible for people to get out,\" he told a press conference in Geneva.\nLaerke said the dwindling water supply was a growing concern, as most residents depended on water brought in by trucks.\n\"With the roads closed and the diesel to run the trucks very scarce, water production in the city has fallen by about half,\" he said.\nA U.S. official left open the door for the possibility of a strike on Iran this weekend, despite President Trump previously saying there would be none before the midterm elections in less than two weeks.\n\"Could there be a strike this weekend? Absolutely,\" Tom Barrack, the U.S. ambassador to Turkey and point man on Syria, who does not have a direct role on Iran policy, told Israeli army radio in response to a question.\n\"(President Trump) is very clear. He's open and available and accessible to a deal and we've been at the table for months and months and months,\" Barrack said.\nIsraeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu, who faces elections Tuesday, in recent days has repeatedly vowed to topple the clerical state in Iran, following two major bombing campaigns against Iran since last year.\nMr. Trump has also signaled an openness to resuming strikes on Iran after a long deadlock in diplomacy although he earlier said it would not happen before midterm elections on Nov. 3.\nEarlier Friday, Mr. Trump repeated a common refrain that the U.S. could choose to end the war anytime it wants through a renewed military campaign, but could also let the country \"wither\" under sanctions.\nAFP/CBS\nForeign ministers from eight European and Middle Eastern countries met in Istanbul on Friday in an unprecedented format, urging diplomacy and peaceful solutions to prevail across the Middle East as well as the reopening of the Strait of Hormuz.\nMinisters from Britain, France, Germany and Italy met counterparts from Turkey, Saudi Arabia, Pakistan and Egypt to discuss Hormuz \u2014 a crucial trade route blocked in the Iran war \u2014 as well as Yemen and ways to end the Russia-Ukraine conflict.\nAt the heart of the talks was the situation in the Straits of Hormuz and Bab el-Mandeb, which is paralyzing global trade and driving up energy prices as well as the attacks on Saudi Arabia by Houthis from Yemen, allies of Tehran.\nFrench Foreign Minister Jean-Noel Barrot said a new escalation between Iran and the United States \"would create nothing but losers and would undoubtedly have very serious consequences for hydrocarbon prices.\"\n\"The first thing this group of eight countries ... will do is call on both sides to exercise the utmost restraint, because we cannot afford another escalation in the region,\" he told journalists.\nItaly's Antonio Tajani said the regional problem cannot be solved \"from a purely military standpoint.\"\nStocks slipped Friday on Wall Street under increasing pressure from rising oil prices.\nThe S&P 500 fell 0.2%, adding to its decline from a day before. The index is on track for a second-straight week of losses. The Dow Jones Industrial Average fell 550 points, or just over 1%, as of 11:50 a.m. Eastern time.\nThe Nasdaq composite rose 0.2%, bolstered by gains for several big tech stocks that also helped ease losses elsewhere.\nRising oil prices weighed heavily on the broader market, where three out of every four stocks within the benchmark S&P 500 index fell. The price for a barrel of Brent crude oil rose 3.5% to over $117. It has surged from less than $97 at the start of October and is now at its highest level since March.\nPresident Trump delivered a familiar message on Iran as he left the White House for a trip to rally voters in Florida, but also added the U.S. could take all of Iran's oil if we wanted to.\n\"Iran is not able to do much now. And we could, if we wanted to, just take all of the oil,\" Mr. Trump said. \"We could take the whole country.\"\n\"And as you know, the Hormuz Strait is ours, and that's the way we'll keep it,\" the president added.\nMr. Trump was also asked about U.S. Ambassador to the United Nations Mike Waltz saying the war with Irancould drag on for another year, while the president has said for months the war will end \"very soon.\"\n\"We could end it very quickly,\" Mr. Trump said. \"But Iran is a failing nation. We're taking all the oil out that we want.\"\n\"The question is, 'Do we want to finish it off or do we just want to let it wither?' Because we're getting everything we wanted,\" he said.\nThe tanker involved in an oil spill off the coast of the United Arab Emirates has been identified, according to analysts who spoke to the Reuters new agency.\nEmirati authorities have not named the vessel, but Martin Kelly, CEO of Orbital Risk Intelligence, told Reuters the oil slick was coming from a vessel called the Gem No. 2.\nAnother analyst told the news agency that satellite imagery appears to show a large spill coming from the tanker.\n\"The slick is considerably larger, covering approximately 160 square km (60 square miles), and looks to be coming from the ship,\" Leon Moreland, a researcher at \u2060the Conflict and Environment Observatory, said.\n\"Furthermore, a slick this close to shore increases the chance it could impact nearby beaches and ecologically sensitive habitats,\" he said.\nThe Gem No. 2 was hit by an unidentified projectile 13 nautical miles west of Al Jazeera in the UAE on Oct. 9, shipping sources told Reuters.\nNo group claimed responsibility for the incident at the time, but maritime security sources told the news agency that it took place outside of the Strait of Hormuz.\nAny further escalation between Iran and the U.S. \"would leave no winners,\" France's foreign minister said Friday.\nSpeaking to reporters in Istanbul after a meeting with foreign ministers from the U.K., Germany, Turkey, Egypt, Pakistan and Saudi Arabia, Jean-Noel Barrot said that \"a further escalation between Iran and the United States would \u2060leave no winners and would likely have very \u200cserious consequences for hydrocarbon prices,\" according to remarks reported by the Reuters news agency.\nResidents in the north of the United Arab Emirates have described smelling gas after an oil spill off the country's coastline.\nMany Emiratis have reported chemical smells on social media in recent days, including residents in northern districts of Dubai, the largest city in the UAE.\n\"For the past two days it smelled like 10 gas stations spilled their gas everywhere,\" a Sharjah resident told the AFP news agency, asking to remain anonymous.\n\"I was on my balcony and panicked thinking gas was leaking in my house and I will blow up everyone.\"\nEmirati authorities \"detected a tanker oil spill affecting Umm Al Quwain's beaches,\" the Ministry of Climate Change and Environment said in a statement Friday, referring to an emirate north of Dubai.\nNews of the spill came a day after Iran claimed to have struck four commercial vessels in Persian Gulf waters and said three others had hit sea mines in the region, though President Trump has accused Tehran of making up the incidents to drive up oil prices.\nCBS/AFP\nIranian state TV claimed Friday, citing an unnamed source close to the Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps (IRGC) Navy, that all movement of \"non-compliant\" vessels through the Strait of Hormuz had been completely halted following a series of Iranian attacks on ships and navigational support systems.\nThe report cited the source as saying that if U.S. military escorts and ship-to-ship transfers - all of which the Iranian regime refers to as \"oil smuggling\" - resumes, action against the vessels and technical systems supporting them will be \"much more forceful.\"\nIran has repeatedly warned commercial ships not to use the long-established shipping lanes through the strait, through which about 20% of global oil supplies passed before the war. Tehran demands that all vessels seek authorization and use a northern route, close to its coast, and it has attacked ships for months that refuse.\nThe Trump administration, however, has encouraged shippers to use an alternative southern route, close to Oman, to avoid Iranian tolls.\nIran claimed Thursday to have struck four ships that used that route, and said three others hit sea mines in the U.S.-designated shipping lanes.\nPresident Trump on Thursday dismissed Iranian claims about striking vessels as lies aimed at increasing global oil prices ahead of the looming U.S. midterm elections.\nBoth Iran and the U.S. have claimed at various times during the war to have completely halted traffic that violates their respective blockades of the waterway.\nIran's foreign minister has called Israel's relationship with the United States \"parasitic,\" citing alleged American taxpayer funding for missile defense in Israel.\n\"The U.S. has over the past three years spent $11 BILLION in taxpayer money on missile defense for Israel,\" Abbas Araghchi wrote Friday in apost on X, citing calculations by independent policy analyst Stephen Semler, who said he arrived at the number using Pentagon budget documents, media reports, and open-source intelligence.\nCBS News has not been able to independently verify the figures.\nSemler is the founder of the Security Policy Reform Institute, a think tank that, according to its website, is \"driven by empiricism and guided by progressive values\" to promote \"the meaningful reform of U.S. foreign policy to break the cycle of endless war and serve the most pressing economic, social, and political needs of working-class Americans.\"\nAraghchi also said the U.S. had \"fired more interceptors defending Israel than Israel has,\" a claim supported by aWashington Poststory from May that cited U.S. Defense Department data and U.S. officials.\n\"That is not a symbiotic relationship,\" Araghchi said. \"It is a parasitic one.\"\nThe United Arab Emirates reported an oil spill from a tanker off its coast Friday, a day after Iran claimed to have struck four commercial vessels in Persian Gulf waters and said three others had hit sea mines in the region.\nEmirati authorities \"detected a tanker oil spill affecting Umm Al Quwain's beaches,\" the Ministry of Climate Change and Environment said in a statement, referring to an emirate north of Dubai.\nIt said \"teams immediately began containment, cordoning, clean-up and remediation ... to limit environmental impacts,\" adding that Umm Al Quwain beaches had been closed.\nMany residents in the north of the UAE have reported oil smells on social media in recent days, including residents in northern districts of Dubai.\nFrequent attacks on ships have been reported in recent weeks near the Strait of Hormuz, the vital waterway at the heart of the conflict between Iran and the United States.\nThe British maritime security UKMTO reported on Oct. 9 that a vessel was hit by a projectile off the UAE coast, causing a fire that was later brought under control. According to the maritime security company Vanguard, the vessel in question was the Panama-flagged oil tanker GEM NO.2.\nIran's Revolutionary Guard Corps claimed the seven additional incidents on Thursday, but none have been corroborated thus far, and President Trump accused Tehran of inventing strikes on ships to drive up oil prices.\nCBS/AFP\nSaudi Arabia has postponed the sixth U.N. World Data Forum and the second Saudi Statistical Forum, which had been scheduled to take place in Riyadh next month, until 2027.\nThe kingdom'sGeneral Authority for Statisticssaid the delay was intended to ensure broad international participation.\nIt comes after the recent rescheduling of Saudi Arabia's Future Investment Initiative, known as \"Davos in the Desert,\" which was also pushed back to 2027 amid escalating regional tension anddeadly Houthi attacks on the kingdom.\nU.S. Ambassador to the United Nations Mike Waltz has said the war with Iran could drag on for another year.\nIn an interview with theFinancial Timespublished Friday, Waltz said the U.S. could still be fighting the war President Trump initially said would last \"four to five weeks\" 12 months from now.\nWaltz told the FT the U.S. will not have achieved its key goals in Iran until the country disposes of its highly enriched uranium and accepts a \"meaningful agreement on their domestic enrichment.\"\n\"I'm not going to put out hypotheticals. One would hope that they'll come to the table in a meaningful way before that,\" Waltz said, referring to the possibility of another year of war.\nHe added that Mr. Trump \"and the U.S. Navy can maintain this blockade [on Iranian ports] for the foreseeable future,\" and said the administration was still \"tightening\" its economic pressure campaign against Iran.\nMr. Trump and Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu have renewed their threats to launch new strikes on Iran after the U.S. midterm elections on Nov. 3.\nForeign ministers from seven nations in Europe, the Middle East and elsewhere will hold talks in Istanbul Friday focused on the Strait of Hormuz and the interlinked crises in the Middle East, as well as the Ukraine war.\nMinisters from the so-called E3 group \u2013 Britain, France and Germany \u2013 will meet counterparts from the newly created R4 group, composed of Turkey, Saudi Arabia, Pakistan and Egypt, to discuss the Israel-Palestinian crisis, developments in Yemen and the Strait of Hormuz, and ways to end the Russia-Ukraine war, a Turkish foreign ministry source told AFP.\nBritish Foreign Minister Ed Miliband's office said the talks would revolve around \"the Strait of Hormuz and the situation in Saudi Arabia,\" as theSaudis back Yemen's beleaguered governmentin its decade-long civil war, which has flared up amid the U.S. war on Iran.\n\"Despite positive progress from the U.S. and regional partners, passage through the Strait remains under threat, with traffic through the waterway sharply down on pre-war levels,\" a U.K. foreign ministry statement said.\nIt said Miliband would \"advocate for a diplomatic solution to the crisis that restores full navigational rights and freedoms\" and stress \"how Iranian tactics are being replicated across the region, with attacks on Saudi Arabia further destabilizing the region and increasing pressure on vital waterways and global chokepoints, such as Bab el-Mandeb and the Suez Canal.\"\n\"The U.K. is stepping up to help stabilize the region, havingthis week committed RAF Typhoons to defend Saudi Arabia from Houthi attacksin their airspace,\" it added.\nCBS/AFP\nThe president of Saudi state-owned oil company Saudi Aramco, whose facilities have been targeted by Yemen's Iran-backed Houthi militants repeatedly for weeks, says the amount of oil moving through a pipeline that bypasses the Strait of Hormuz has more than tripled.\nThe East-West pipeline's capacity has surged from 2 million to 7 million barrels each day, President and CEO Amin Nasser said in an interview with Saudi-owned television Al Arabiya Business.\nBuilt in the early 1980s, the pipeline has played a crucial role in Saudi Arabia's ability to export oil amid disruption to shipping via the Strait of Hormuz during the Iran war. It carries crude from Saudi oil fields and production facilities in the east, on the Persian Gulf, to the Yanbu oil terminal on the Saudi Red Sea coast on the other side of the vast kingdom, from which it can be shipped to global markets.\nThat route, which, unlike the Gulf, has two available exits, has also been threatened and attacked by the Houthis, who have vowed to stop Saudi shipments via the Red Sea's southern gateway, the Bab el-Mandeb Strait.\nNasser said Saudi Aramco has used drones to assess damage caused to infrastructure, including sites key to the East-West pipeline, by Houthi attacks, and to help with firefighting.\nCBS/AP\nAs crude oil prices hit their highest levels since May, President Trump accused Iran on Thursday of lying about attacks on tankers in the Strait of Hormuz to drive up global energy prices.\n\"Iran is doing everything they can to drive up the price of Oil prior to the U.S. Midterm Election,\" Mr. Trumpwrote on Truth Social. \"They are even making up fake hits, mines, and everything else, because more Oil is coming through the Hormuz Strait now than it did even prior to the beginning of the Conflict.\"\nBrent crude, the international standard for oil, hit $112 a barrel Thursday morning, its highest price since May 18.\nIran claimed to have struck four vessels in the Persian Gulf Thursday after they transited the Strait of Hormuz using a U.S.-endorsed route that Tehran rejects, and it said three others hit sea mines in the region after using the same shipping lanes. All of the ships were damaged along or after using southern shipping lanes in the strait near the Omani coast, which the Trump administration has insisted for weeks is open to traffic.\nThe U.S. military has escorted tankers out of the Persian Gulf using that route, boosting the quantity of oil leaving the region, but it is a costly process. Overall instability in the Middle East has kept energy prices high despite the recently increased supplies.\nIn:\n(03:38)\nCopyright \u00a92026 CBS Interactive Inc. All rights reserved.", "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 13:40:20 -0400"}, {"title": "German man arrested for allegedly driving U.S. teen to die by suicide", "url": "https://www.cbsnews.com/news/german-man-arrested-teen-suicide-764/", "body": "Watch CBS News\nOctober 24, 2026 / 1:26 PM EDT/ CBS/AFP\nGerman police, acting on an FBI tip-off, arrested an alleged online sadist accused of driving a 14-year-old U.S. girl to hang herself, prosecutors said Friday.\nThe 18-year-old German was arrested in Hamburg on Thursday, accused of being the indirect perpetrator of murder and grievous bodily harm.\nFBI Indianapolis Special Agent in Charge Timothy J. O'Malleysaidin a statement that their office worked with the Fort Wayne Police Department to provide information to the German police to help advance the arrest. The Fort Wayne Police Departmentsaidin a statement that its Homicide Unit identified a suspect who contributed to the juvenile's death.\n\"Anyone who targets or exploits a child should understand this: There is no safe haven from justice. It doesn't matter where you live or where you try to hide. The FBI will work with our partners around the world to find you and hold you accountable,\" O'Malley said.\nThe suspect was linked to an online group called \"Nefarious Maniac Kult\" or \"NMK,\" part of a global network known by the labels\"764\"or \"COM,\" the Hamburg public prosecutor's office said.\nFrom September 2024 to September 2025, the FBI has seen a 300% increase in nihilistic violent extremism cases \u2014 many involving the 764 group, saidFBI Director Kash Patel. The group 764 lacks a specific ideology, but members openly encourage self-harm and violence and encourage victims to produce videos documenting acts of self-harm or child sexual abuse material.\nBetween September and October, the suspect, then aged 17, allegedly \"exerted influence\" via the social network Discord over the girl from Indiana state whom prosecutors described as \"psychologically fragile\".\nThe suspect allegedly pressured the girl to cut herself and write the letters \"NMK\" on a wall in her own blood, and had her send him video recordings of these acts. A few days later, she hanged herself in her bedroom, prosecutors said.\nThe suspect allegedly sought a video recording of her death, hoping to gain status within the group or to secure entry into it.\nIn:\n\u00a9 2026 CBS Interactive Inc. All Rights Reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. Agence France-Presse contributed to this report.\n(07:20)\nCopyright \u00a92026 CBS Interactive Inc. All rights reserved.", "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 13:26:19 -0400"}, {"title": "Emotional moments as hero dad describes fear and action in face of school shooting", "url": "https://www.cbsnews.com/video/emotional-moments-as-hero-dad-describes-fear-and-action-in-face-of-school-shooting/", "body": "Watch CBS News\n48 Hours\nLatest Baltimore News & Headlines\nLatest NorCal News & Headlines\nWBZ News Weekend 6pm\nCBS News Chicago Saturday 5pm\nLatest Colorado News & Headlines\nCBS News Detroit Weekend 6pm\nLatest Los Angeles News & Headlines\nLatest Miami News & Headlines\nWCCO News at 5pm\nLatest New York News & Headlines\nJersey Pulse\nKDKA News Saturday 6pm\nYour Stories From Your Community Reporters\nCBS News Texas Saturday 5pm\nCopyright \u00a92026 CBS Interactive Inc. All rights reserved.", "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 13:24:43 -0400"}, {"title": "U.K. police confirm it received letter with claims against Meghan's father", "url": "https://www.cbsnews.com/news/uk-police-letter-claims-meghan-duchess-sussex-thomas-markle-allegations/", "body": "Watch CBS News\nUpdated on:  October 24, 2026 / 12:56 PM EDT/ CBS News\nA letter making a \"number of claims\" against the father of Meghan, the Duchess of Sussex, was received by the Metropolitan Police in 2019, a Scotland Yard spokesperson confirmed to CBS News on Saturday.\nPolice said they passed the letter to the office of the Duke and Duchess \"for their awareness,\" however, the details of the allegations made in the letter were not shared with other law enforcement agencies.\nOn Friday, it was reported that Meghan'sestrangedfather, Thomas Markle, isunder investigationby authorities in the Philippines for alleged possession of child sexual abuse materials.\nA spokesperson for the Philippine National Police told CBS News in a statement Saturday that they launched an investigation after Markle's personal items were turned over to law enforcement by his son, Thomas Markle Jr.\n\"At this point, investigators are looking into the nature and origin of the materials and whether these may be connected to any violation of law,\" Col. Allen Rae Co said in a statement. \"U.S. authorities have also offered assistance should Philippine authorities determine that their support is needed as the investigation progresses.\"\nThe British tabloid The Sun was the first toreportthe allegations against Markle Sr. The report said the younger Markle allegedly discovered child abuse materials in storage boxes at his father's home in the Philippines.\nThe Daily Mailreported Fridaythat Markle had denied the allegations against him.\nMeghan, who recentlymoved back to Britainwith her husband Prince Harry and their children, said in a statement sent to CBS News on Friday that she was \"deeply distressed\" by reports about her father. She said she had been aware of the allegations against him since 2018 and that the allegations were shared with U.K. authorities at the time.\n\"In 2018, a letter was sent to the Metropolitan Police and Royalty and Specialist Protection. That letter was subsequently shared with me. It made troubling and disturbing allegations against my father and I was given assurances that these were being investigated,\" her Friday statement said.\nMeghan said she had distanced herself from her father in an effort to protect her children.\n\"I hope it can now be understood that, despite certain media publications' distortion over the years, any distance from my father and my choice to not allow him to have contact with our children, was for one reason: to protect them,\" she said.\nResponding to Saturday's developments dating the letter to 2019, a spokesman for the duchess told CBS News partnerBBC Newsthat \"any discrepancy of a few months\" was \"an understandable consequence of that passage of time.\"\nScotland Yard said Saturday that Met Police will be reviewing the handling of this matter in 2019 and deciding whether to refer itself to the police watchdog.\nIn:\n\u00a9 2026 CBS Interactive Inc. All Rights Reserved.\n(01:27)\nCopyright \u00a92026 CBS Interactive Inc. All rights reserved.", "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 12:56:33 -0400"}, {"title": "Saturday Sessions: Vacations plays \"I See Myself In You\"", "url": "https://www.cbsnews.com/video/saturday-sessions-vacations-plays-i-see-myself-in-you/", "body": "Watch CBS News\n48 Hours\nLatest Baltimore News & Headlines\nLatest NorCal News & Headlines\nWBZ News Weekend 6pm\nCBS News Chicago Saturday 5pm\nLatest Colorado News & Headlines\nCBS News Detroit Weekend 6pm\nLatest Los Angeles News & Headlines\nLatest Miami News & Headlines\nWCCO News at 5pm\nLatest New York News & Headlines\nJersey Pulse\nKDKA News Saturday 6pm\nYour Stories From Your Community Reporters\nCBS News Texas Saturday 5pm\nCopyright \u00a92026 CBS Interactive Inc. All rights reserved.", "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 12:15:29 -0400"}, {"title": "Saturday Sessions: Vacations plays \"Holy Grail\"", "url": "https://www.cbsnews.com/video/saturday-sessions-vacations-plays-holy-grail/", "body": "Watch CBS News\n48 Hours\nLatest Baltimore News & Headlines\nLatest NorCal News & Headlines\nWBZ News Weekend 6pm\nCBS News Chicago Saturday 5pm\nLatest Colorado News & Headlines\nCBS News Detroit Weekend 6pm\nLatest Los Angeles News & Headlines\nLatest Miami News & Headlines\nWCCO News at 5pm\nLatest New York News & Headlines\nJersey Pulse\nKDKA News Saturday 6pm\nYour Stories From Your Community Reporters\nCBS News Texas Saturday 5pm\nCopyright \u00a92026 CBS Interactive Inc. All rights reserved.", "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 12:13:51 -0400"}, {"title": "Saturday Sessions: Vacations plays \"Ready or Not\"", "url": "https://www.cbsnews.com/video/saturday-sessions-vacations-plays-ready-or-not/", "body": "Watch CBS News\n48 Hours\nLatest Baltimore News & Headlines\nLatest NorCal News & Headlines\nWBZ News Weekend 6pm\nCBS News Chicago Saturday 5pm\nLatest Colorado News & Headlines\nCBS News Detroit Weekend 6pm\nLatest Los Angeles News & Headlines\nLatest Miami News & Headlines\nWCCO News at 5pm\nLatest New York News & Headlines\nJersey Pulse\nKDKA News Saturday 6pm\nYour Stories From Your Community Reporters\nCBS News Texas Saturday 5pm\nCopyright \u00a92026 CBS Interactive Inc. All rights reserved.", "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 12:12:25 -0400"}, {"title": "2000s comedy movies marked a golden age, Hollywood critic argues", "url": "https://www.cbsnews.com/video/2000s-comedy-movies-marked-a-golden-age-hollywood-critic-argues/", "body": "Watch CBS News\n48 Hours\nLatest Baltimore News & Headlines\nLatest NorCal News & Headlines\nWBZ News Weekend 6pm\nCBS News Chicago Saturday 5pm\nLatest Colorado News & Headlines\nCBS News Detroit Weekend 6pm\nLatest Los Angeles News & Headlines\nLatest Miami News & Headlines\nWCCO News at 5pm\nLatest New York News & Headlines\nJersey Pulse\nKDKA News Saturday 6pm\nYour Stories From Your Community Reporters\nCBS News Texas Saturday 5pm\nCopyright \u00a92026 CBS Interactive Inc. All rights reserved.", "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 11:53:10 -0400"}, {"title": "Rubio calls on Cubans seeking \"positive change\" to work with CIA", "url": "https://www.cbsnews.com/news/rubio-cuban-work-with-cia/", "body": "Watch CBS News\nOctober 24, 2026 / 11:49 AM EDT/ CBS News\nU.S. Secretary of State Marco Rubio called on Cubans seeking \"positive change\" to work with the CIA, the latest ploy in the growing efforts from the Trump administration to removeCuba's communist regimefrom power.\n\"To those who seek to create positive change in Cuba - now is the time,\" RubiowroteFriday on X. \"The disappointment of failed promises, corruption, and the privileged lives of only a few; seize this opportunity to create the change you desire through a discrete partnership with the CIA.\"\nShortly afterward, the CIArepostedRubio's message and a similar call in Spanish for Cubans to work with the agency. A video was posted alongside the message showing the steps needed to contact the intelligence agency.\nA strict U.S. embargo and sanctions seeking to put pressure onCuba'scommunist government have led to prices of basic goods skyrocketing and decreased access to medical care for its citizens. Cubansreportedwaiting in bread lines for hours and others reported lining up to see a doctor.\nLast week, the U.S. governmentannouncedadditional economic sanctions on Cuba's state-run bank, saying the institution had ties to Iran.\nPresident Trump said on Friday during a campaign rally in South Florida that \"we'll take care of Cuba on our way back from Iran.\" He was referring to the U.S. Navy warships deployed near the Strait of Hormuz.\nMr. Trump added: \"Those beautiful ships will pass Cuba. We'll have one of them stop about 50 yards off the coast. They'll raise up their hands, and then we're going to send Marco Rubio in to finish up the deal.\"\nCuba's government has pushed back, with Foreign Minister Bruno Rodr\u00edguez Parrillaresponding Friday on X, saying: \"We reject any threats stemming from U.S. ambitions toward Cuba.\"\nRodriguez Parrilla added: \"Our shores are lined with beautiful beaches, but they are also rugged and inhospitable to those who arrive with hostile intentions and a desire for domination.\"\nOlivia  Gaziscontributed to this report.\nIn:\n\u00a9 2026 CBS Interactive Inc. All Rights Reserved.\n(03:21)\nCopyright \u00a92026 CBS Interactive Inc. All rights reserved.", "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 11:49:32 -0400"}, {"title": "Chef Ayesha Nurdjaja shares passion for mouthwatering family-style Mediterranean dishes", "url": "https://www.cbsnews.com/video/chef-ayesha-nurdjaja-shares-passion-for-mouthwatering-family-style-mediterranean-dishes/", "body": "Watch CBS News\n48 Hours\nLatest Baltimore News & Headlines\nLatest NorCal News & Headlines\nWBZ News Weekend 6pm\nCBS News Chicago Saturday 5pm\nLatest Colorado News & Headlines\nCBS News Detroit Weekend 6pm\nLatest Los Angeles News & Headlines\nLatest Miami News & Headlines\nWCCO News at 5pm\nLatest New York News & Headlines\nJersey Pulse\nKDKA News Saturday 6pm\nYour Stories From Your Community Reporters\nCBS News Texas Saturday 5pm\nCopyright \u00a92026 CBS Interactive Inc. All rights reserved.", "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 11:42:04 -0400"}, {"title": "France braces for another weekend of protests", "url": "https://www.cbsnews.com/video/france-braces-for-another-weekend-of-protests/", "body": "Watch CBS News\n48 Hours\nLatest Baltimore News & Headlines\nLatest NorCal News & Headlines\nWBZ News Weekend 6pm\nCBS News Chicago Saturday 5pm\nLatest Colorado News & Headlines\nCBS News Detroit Weekend 6pm\nLatest Los Angeles News & Headlines\nLatest Miami News & Headlines\nWCCO News at 5pm\nLatest New York News & Headlines\nJersey Pulse\nKDKA News Saturday 6pm\nYour Stories From Your Community Reporters\nCBS News Texas Saturday 5pm\nCopyright \u00a92026 CBS Interactive Inc. All rights reserved.", "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 11:14:00 -0400"}, {"title": "Eye Opener: Typhoon sweeps through East Asia", "url": "https://www.cbsnews.com/video/eye-opener-typhoon-sweeps-through-east-asia/", "body": "Watch CBS News\n48 Hours\nLatest Baltimore News & Headlines\nLatest NorCal News & Headlines\nWBZ News Weekend 6pm\nCBS News Chicago Saturday 5pm\nLatest Colorado News & Headlines\nCBS News Detroit Weekend 6pm\nLatest Los Angeles News & Headlines\nLatest Miami News & Headlines\nWCCO News at 5pm\nLatest New York News & Headlines\nJersey Pulse\nKDKA News Saturday 6pm\nYour Stories From Your Community Reporters\nCBS News Texas Saturday 5pm\nCopyright \u00a92026 CBS Interactive Inc. All rights reserved.", "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 10:04:00 -0400"}, {"title": "1 arrested after fire at Hitler's Eagle's Nest mountain retreat", "url": "https://www.cbsnews.com/news/hitler-eagles-nest-mountain-retreat-fire-nazi/", "body": "Watch CBS News\nUpdated on:  October 24, 2026 / 10:03 AM EDT/ CBS News\nA suspect has been arrested in Germany following a fire at Adolf Hitler's mountain retreat, known as Eagle's Nest, German police said.\nAuthorities were alerted to a fire at the Nazi leader's mountain residence on the peak of Obersalzberg at approximately 9:15 p.m. local time on Friday, Upper Bavaria South police said in a statement.\nThe building, also known as Kehlsteinhaus, was \"completely engulfed in flames\" when firefighters arrived, police said, but no one was found inside and no injuries were reported.\nFire trucks had some difficulty accessing the site \"due to the local conditions,\" authorities added.\n\"Shortly after the fire broke out, a suspect came into the focus of investigators,\" the statement said, adding that the cause of the fire is now under criminal investigation and one person was taken into custody.\nThe 33-year-old suspect, a German citizen who has ties to the Berchtesgaden area but doesn't currently live there, prosecutors said, has been questioned, The Associated Press reported. They said he showed signs of mental illness and he was being sent to a psychiatric facility, but they also would look into the possibility of a political motive in view of the building's history.\nThere were no current indications that anyone else was involved, police said.\nAt an elevation of 6,017 feet, Hitler's Eagle's Nest was completed in 1938 and occasionally used by the Nazi leader as a temporary seat of government. The site was spared from Allied bombing during World War II, largely preserving its original state.\nSince then, it has become a popular tourist attraction, which also operates as a restaurant and beer garden. According to its website, the Eagle's Nest is \"one of the few undamaged monuments of the Hitler era\" and it \"stands for the insanity of the regime.\"\nBavaria's interior minister, Joachim Herrmann, indicated to reporters in Berchtesgaden that the Eagle's Nest would be restored. But what the site will look like in the future will be a matter for discussions with local officials, historians, preservationists and others.\n\"We're not going to leave a ruin there,\" Herrmann said, according to the AP, adding, \"I think we will be able to find a sensible solution.\"\nThe minister stressed \"it is important that we continue to do the work of remembrance.\"\nIn:\n\u00a9 2026 CBS Interactive Inc. All Rights Reserved.\n\nCopyright \u00a92026 CBS Interactive Inc. All rights reserved.", "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 10:03:39 -0400"}, {"title": "10/24: CBS Saturday Morning", "url": "https://www.cbsnews.com/video/102426-cbs-saturday-morning/", "body": "Watch CBS News\n48 Hours\nLatest Baltimore News & Headlines\nLatest NorCal News & Headlines\nWBZ News Weekend 6pm\nCBS News Chicago Saturday 5pm\nLatest Colorado News & Headlines\nCBS News Detroit Weekend 6pm\nLatest Los Angeles News & Headlines\nLatest Miami News & Headlines\nWCCO News at 5pm\nLatest New York News & Headlines\nJersey Pulse\nKDKA News Saturday 6pm\nYour Stories From Your Community Reporters\nCBS News Texas Saturday 5pm\nCopyright \u00a92026 CBS Interactive Inc. All rights reserved.", "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 09:00:00 -0400"}, {"title": "NASA outlines revised 2028 Artemis moon landing campaign", "url": "https://www.cbsnews.com/news/nasa-artemis-2028-moon-landing-program-adjustments/", "body": "Watch CBS News\nUpdated on:  October 23, 2026 / 8:05 PM EDT/ CBS News\nNASA is pressing ahead withArtemis moon programrevisions to give the agency its best shot at a lunar landing in 2028 using a lander built by either Elon Musk's SpaceX or Jeff Bezos' Blue Origin, depending on which one testing indicates is the right choice, officials said Friday.\nThe strategy is intended to provide an alternative path to an Artemis IV landing near the moon's south pole if either company runs into supply chain problems, significant technical trouble or other issues that might trigger major delays.\nTo helppave the wayto that long-awaited moon landing, Artemis Program Manager Jeremy Parsons said the agency and its contractors are working flat out to launch the Artemis III mission next year, sending four astronauts into Earth orbit to rendezvous and dock with a Blue Origin test lander and then a SpaceX Starship upper stage.\nThe goal is to test critical procedures close to home before launching unpiloted lunar landers on test flights leading to astronaut boots on the moon in 2028.\nThe Artemis III flight will require coordinated launches of three of the world's most powerful rockets in quick succession \u2014Blue Origin's New Glenn,SpaceX's Super Heavy-Starshipand NASA's Space Launch System. It will also require management of separate rendezvous maneuvers enablingNASA's Orion crew capsuleto dock with both test landers.\nThe astronauts first will rendezvous and dock with Blue Origin's Blue Moon Mark 1 spacecraft, open hatches and float inside to test life support, communications and other critical systems.\nAfter two days, the crew will undock and fly the Orion to the SpaceX Starship stage, docking at the top of the big rocket using a passive docking mechanism. The otherwise \"off-the-shelf\" Starship will not be equipped with a crew cabin and the astronauts will not be able to enter the spacecraft.\nBut Parsons said SpaceX already has extensive experience with life support systems, communications and navigation procedures using its Crew and Cargo Dragon spacecraft, and is making solid progress getting the Starship moon lander variant ready for flight.\nEven with an off-the-shelf Starship for Artemis III, he said NASA will still gain valuable experience bringing the SpaceX and Orion spacecraft together and learning how the much smaller Orion capsule interacts with the much larger Starship.\nDepending on how those flights go, NASA will have a better idea of the path forward toward the Artemis IV moon landing mission.\n\"We intend to wait as long as possible to make a decision about which provider is ultimately going to provide (a lander to carry) the first American astronauts back to the moon in 2028,\" Parsons said. \"We have dedicated teams working hand in hand with both providers, marching along the way, doing everything we can to (assist).\"\n\"Regardless of who goes first,\" he added, \"both providers are planning for lunar landing missions in 2028.\"\nNASA is in a self-declared space race with China, which plans to land \"taikonauts\" on the moon by 2030. NASA wants to get there first, to explore the lunar south pole region where ice deposits might be accessible to provide rocket fuel, air and water, and toestablish a moon basein the 2030s for long-duration astronaut stays.\nRegardless of which lander is used for the Artemis IV mission, Parsons said NASA will launch its astronauts to space using the SLS rocket and Orion crew capsule. How the astronauts will get to the moon depends on which lander is being used.\nFor a SpaceX Starship landing mission, the company will launch three versions of the upper stage used with the Super Heavy booster: a propellant depot, a tanker and the lander itself. All three will use almost all of their supercold methane and liquid oxygen propellants just getting to low-Earth orbit.\nFor a moon mission, SpaceX will have to launch multiple tanker flights to fill up the depot's tanks. The depot, in turn, will provide the propellants needed by the lander to break out of Earth orbit and head for the moon.\nWhen the tanker flights are almost complete, an Orion capsule, launched by an SLS rocket, will dock with the waiting Starship lander. The lander then will boost both spacecraft to the moon.\nOnce docked in lunar orbit, two astronauts will descend to the surface in the lander and carry out several moonwalks before blasting off to rendezvous with the Orion, which will carry the crew back to Earth.\nFor a Blue Origin moon landing, the company will launch three \"transfer stages\" using its New Glenn rocket, followed by the launch of the Blue Moon Mark 2 lander. The transfer stages will dock with the lander and boost it to orbit around the moon. An SLS rocket will launch an Orion crew capsule directly to the moon for a rendezvous and docking.\nTwo astronauts then will move into the lander and a transfer stage will drop it into a lower lunar orbit. The lander will descend to touchdown and, after surface operations are complete, blast off to rendezvous with the Orion, which will carry the astronauts back to Earth.\nThe 2028 target landing date assumes SpaceX and/or Blue Origin can demonstrate reliable, rapid-fire launches of heavy-lift rockets, large-scale in-space refueling in Earth orbit and the ability to safely land on an uncertain surface and in poor lighting.\nBoth companies are required to launch successful unpiloted moon landings before a flight carrying astronauts will be allowed.\nFor its part, SpaceX has launched 13 suborbital Super Heavy-Starship test flights since 2023 and just one, the most recent, putting a Starship into orbit. But Musk told investors earlier this week the company will be launching multiple Super Heavy-Starships per week by the end of 2027 using launch pads in Texas and Florida.\n\"We expect Starship to fly weekly at some point in the first half next year, and then (in the) second half of next year twice weekly, or maybe even more frequently than that,\" he said. SpaceX President Gwynne Shotwell said the first large-scale in-orbit refueling demonstration is planned for \"early next year.\"\nBlue Origin is still recovering from a catastrophic launch pad explosion of its New Glenn rocket in May that destroyed the booster and heavily damaged its launch pad. A new pad is being built and Blue Origin expects to resume flights late this year or in early 2027 and to send two unpiloted Blue Moon Mark 1 landers to the lunar surface by the end of next year.\n\"What we are undertaking is ambitious,\" said NASA Administrator Jared Isaacman. \"It's extraordinarily difficult, and years of decisions, both within and beyond the walls of this agency, have cost us time and resources that we never had to spare. But the agency we are reinvigorating, and the extraordinary vehicles our industry partners are building, are worth every bit of that effort. This will not be a repeat of the flags and footprints (of the Apollo program).\n\"These vehicles are being designed to dramatically reduce the cost of delivering mass to orbit and to the moon to support longer surface operations, enable more frequent astronaut missions, and transform what we see as a small scientific outpost into something that may one day resemble a city on the lunar surface.\"\nHe concluded his remarks by saying, \"When the history books look back on this time, the politics over which rocket and landers carried us to the moon, how long it took, and at what cost will be a footnote compared to all that followed. That when the time came, the Artemis generation chose to go. And we went.\"\nIn:\n\u00a9 2026 CBS Interactive Inc. All Rights Reserved.\n(04:58)\nCopyright \u00a92026 CBS Interactive Inc. All rights reserved.", "date": "Fri, 23 Oct 2026 20:05:00 -0400"}, {"title": "UFO files detail strange, silent triangular craft: \"Acoustic silence\"", "url": "https://www.cbsnews.com/news/ufo-files-pentagon-triangular-craft/", "body": "Watch CBS News\nOctober 23, 2026 / 5:02 PM EDT/ CBS News\nWashington\u2014 Federal agents in the western U.S. this year encountered a large, triangular aircraft that zoomed through the sky from a standstill while making no noise or giving off any exhaust, according to new documents released by the Pentagon.\nThe agents' recollections were included in the Defense Department's latest release of files related to UFOs, or UAP, unidentified anomalous phenomena. Friday's disclosure was theseventhunder an executive order that President Trump signed earlier this year mandating the release of sensitive UAP files. Thelatest batchincluded 28 PDF files.\nThe triangular craft is detailed in two documents: amemofrom the Pentagon's All-domain Anomaly Resolution Office describing investigators' debrief with the agents, and asigned statementthe agents gave to the FBI.\nThe encounter occurred in June near an unspecified \"national security site\" in the western U.S.\nThe agents told investigators that they were alerted to the object by a third agent stationed nearby. They quickly repositioned themselves to get a better look, and spotted the craft above a ridgeline a few miles to the west, against the glow of the sky after sunset.\n\"We initiated a visual survey of the western horizon and almost immediately observed a substantial, nonreflective, dark black triangular object most resembling a delta wing drone,\" they said in their FBI statement. \"We estimated the object's wingspan at approximately nine to fifteen feet, its depth at six to nine feet, and its thickness at one to two feet.\"\nThe FBI document included illustrations showing the scene and a representation of the craft.\nThe AARO report notes that the \"unidentified craft was executing a slow, controlled, perfectly vertical ascent directly over the ridge line. The craft climbed approximately two to three hundred feet while maintaining a completely static orientation without tilting, swaying, or wobbling. The non-reflective, matte black exterior presented a continuous, featureless profile.\"\nInvestigators wrote that the \"ascent occurred in absolute acoustic silence.\"\nThe agents told the FBI that when viewed through an infrared scope, the object \"appeared hotter than ambient temperature with a completely consistent thermal signature across the entire airframe and no visible exhaust gases.\"\nWhen the craft was 200 to 300 feet above the ridgeline, it \"instantaneously\" stopped moving and began hovering, staying perfectly still for about 20 seconds, the AARO debrief said.\nThe craft then sped off, reaching a velocity \"compared to that [of] a military fighter jet.\" The agents lost sight of it within a few seconds.\n\"Absolute acoustic silence persisted throughout the high-velocity phase,\" the memo said. \"No impact signature, smoke, dust, or physical disturbance was observed. A ground survey of the canyon drainage conducted the following morning located no wreckage, ground scarring, or vegetation damage.\"\nThe incident is similar to other encounters reported by federal agents in the western U.S., detailed in previous releases of UAP files.\nIn 2023, federal law enforcement agents at an undisclosed location reported seeing orange orbs thatreleased smaller red orbsover a two-day period, according to files released in May. Alater batch included the agents' own accounts, with one describing red lights that \"accelerated instantly\" and another comparing the orbs to \"grapes being expelled from a basketball.\"\nAnother instance,detailed in August, involved an agent who recorded infrared video of two \"black-hot\" objects moving together at the same slow speed.\nRegarding the June incident, the Pentagon noted that AARO said it \"warrant[s] further investigation due to the close alignment of the phenomena's reported morphological features and kinematic profiles with those of other reported incidents in the western United States region.\"\nIn:\n\u00a9 2026 CBS Interactive Inc. All Rights Reserved.\n(05:12)\nCopyright \u00a92026 CBS Interactive Inc. All rights reserved.", "date": "Fri, 23 Oct 2026 17:02:00 -0400"}, {"title": "ICE crackdown prompts some Texas Latinos to turn away from Trump", "url": "https://www.cbsnews.com/news/ice-crackdown-texas-latinos-trump/", "body": "Watch CBS News\nOctober 23, 2026 / 5:00 PM EDT/ CBS News\nMcAllen, Texas\u2014 Benny Melendez, the owner of a small construction company along this stretch of the U.S.-Mexico border, says he's been forced to take drastic measures to protect his immigrant workers.\n\"If I don't install fences, ICE is going to come into my property and take my workers away,\" Melendez said as he showed CBS News the fencing he's set up at one of the several construction sites he manages in South Texas.\nMelendez says the fencing is designed to prevent U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement agents from arresting his workers, and to require them to have judicial warrants before entering his job sites.\nLocal officials sayconstructionis one of the industries hardest hit by President Trump's sweeping crackdown on illegal immigration. Construction company owners like Melendez say they're struggling to find workers, with some having been detained by ICE and many scared of showing up to work and getting arrested. Building a home, he said, is now taking his team a year due to manpower issues \u2014 up from an average of four months before the Trump administration's crackdown began.\n\"We're blessed that we have two workers today,\" Melendez said.\nThe South Texas resident, who voted for Mr. Trump in 2024 after supporting President Biden in 2020, said ICE operations have also made him rethink his vote.\n\"I regret my vote for Biden. Now I regret my vote for Trump,\" Melendez said. \"What other options do we got? They're not working for us, neither party.\"\nMelendez's disillusionment is emblematic of broader concerns among some Latino voters \u2014 including some who voted for Mr. Trump in 2024 \u2014 who believe his mass deportation crackdown has gone too far. A recentCBS News pollfound nearly 60% of Latinos believe the Trump administration has deported more people than they expected.\nDemocrats are hoping those concerns will help them in key races in the midterm elections in November, as they try to retake control of Congress. That bet will be put to the test in Texas, where Mr. Trump and Republicans saw huge gains among Latinos in 2024, especially in communities along the southern border directly affected by the migration crisis there under the Biden administration.\nDemocratic state representative James Talarico and Ken Paxton, Texas' Republican attorney general, are in a hotly contested race for an open Senate seat. In Texas' 15th congressional district, Tejano singer Bobby Pulido, a Democrat, is hoping to unseat Republican Congresswoman Monica De La Cruz.\nJavier Villalobos, the Republican mayor of McAllen, the largest city in that congressional district, warned that his party is now at risk of losing some of the gains it made in Latino border communities. He called concerns about ICE's crackdown the No. 1 issue among his constituents.\n\"Inflation, the economy, everything is important. But what hits you immediately, what hits personally is the crackdown, when you see the ICE agents, masked, and pulling people out of vehicles,\" Villalobos told CBS News. \"It is not a good picture.\"\nVillalobos noted that local residents supported efforts to bolster border security and deport violent criminals in the U.S. illegally. But he said many now see ICE'sdeportation campaignas \"a crackdown on the Hispanic community,\" one he believes has instilled fear in his city, including among American citizens.\nCBS News has reached out to the White House for a response. Trump administration officials have routinely defended ICE's enforcement efforts, noting the president ran on expanding deportations and cracking down on illegal immigration.\n\"I particularly don't care about midterms,\" White House border czar Tom Homantold CBS Newsearlier this month when asked about criticism of ICE by some Republicans. \"My job as a border czar is to keep that border secure and enforce immigration law.\"\nJessica Vuittonet, a coffee shop owner in San Benito, roughly a 45-minute drive from McAllen, said she voted for Mr. Trump two years ago, mainly because of her religious views. But she says she now regrets that decision, citing cases of local residents getting detained by ICE.\n\"I know he was going to start deporting people but not innocent people \u2014 not hardworking people,\" she said.\nIn:\n\u00a9 2026 CBS Interactive Inc. All Rights Reserved.\n(02:49)\nCopyright \u00a92026 CBS Interactive Inc. All rights reserved.", "date": "Fri, 23 Oct 2026 17:00:00 -0400"}, {"title": "An auto clicker should not treat a delay as proof. Wait for a visible cue, act once, then verify the next screen. If it never appears, stop instead of clicking blindly. That\u2019s the visual workflow idea behind macro8n. What would you automate first?", "url": "https://dev.to/macro8n/an-auto-clicker-should-not-treat-a-delay-as-proof-wait-for-a-visible-cue-act-once-then-verify-3ig8", "body": "Posted onOct 24\nTemplates let you quickly answer FAQs or store snippets for re-use.\nAre you sure you want to hide this comment? It will become hidden in your post, but will still be visible via the comment'spermalink.\nHide child comments as well\nConfirm\nFor further actions, you may consider blocking this person and/orreporting abuse\nDEV Community\u2014 A space to discuss and keep up software development and manage your software career\nBuilt onForem\u2014 theopen sourcesoftware that powersDEVand other inclusive communities.\nMade with love andRuby on Rails. DEV Community\u00a92016 - 2026.\nWe're a place where coders share, stay up-to-date and grow their careers.", "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 22:12:47 +0000"}, {"title": "Shipment Fan-Out \u2014 Checking Delayed Webhook Payload Formatting Issues", "url": "https://dev.to/viggoknight2318/shipment-fan-out-checking-delayed-webhook-payload-formatting-issues-2kk7", "body": "Posted onOct 24\nTL;DR: validate and normalize a shipment event before it enters a delayed queue, then log the exact schema version and delivery attempt at the fan-out boundary. In a fintech system, that small ordering choice protects latency-sensitive subscribers from malformed payloads without making every consumer rediscover the same rules.\nThe useful mental model is a two-stage pipeline. Before: accept an arbitrary webhook-shaped object, wait 30 seconds, and discover at delivery time thattrackingNumberis nested under a different key. After: turn the inbound event into one canonical envelope, delay that envelope, and send a separately validated delivery payload to each subscriber. The queue schedules work. It should not be the place where meaning is invented.\nA delayed webhook has at least three representations: the request received from a carrier, the message stored for later work, and the HTTP body sent to a subscriber. Treating those as the same object is the trap. A carrier may send a timestamp as a string, the queue serializer may preserve it as JSON text, and a subscriber may require an RFC 3339 timestamp plus a stable event name. None of those transformations is mysterious. They are just easy to leave implicit.\nFor a shipment-update fan-out, I keep the queue message deliberately boring. It contains an event ID, an immutable shipment reference, the status, the event time, and a schema version. Subscriber-specific headers, signatures, and retry metadata belong at delivery time. This separation matters because a delayed job can be replayed, while a signature is normally tied to the exact body and delivery moment.\nHere is a compact TypeScript boundary. It rejects missing values and refuses to silently coerce an invalid date. That is a better failure than publishing a payload that looks plausible.\nThe important detail is not the helper itself. It is where the helper runs: before enqueueing. A malformed event can still be recorded for investigation, but it does not become a delayed delivery job by accident.\nThat boundary is the whole trick.\nUse a stable envelope rather than passing the original request through unchanged. The queue payload can look like this:\nA consumer then has a narrow contract: readkind, inspectevent.schemaVersion, and deliver the event to the subscribers selected for that shipment. It should not guess whetherstatuscame fromstate, whetheroccurredAtis local time, or whether the body was already stringified. One JSON encoding is enough. Double encoding is a classic symptom: the receiver parses the body once and gets a string containing JSON instead of an object.\nThis is the formatting check I want visible in a trace: raw input enters the normalizer, the canonical object is logged by ID and version, serialization happens once, and the delivery renderer produces the final bytes. If the receiver reports a shape error, the trace points to one boundary instead of asking an on-call engineer to compare three opaque blobs. The extra fields are small, but the debugging path is much shorter.\nI also record the payload shape without recording sensitive shipment details. A useful log line includeseventId,schemaVersion,subscriberId,attempt,queueDelayMs, and a validation result. It should exclude authentication material and avoid dumping the full body. Logs answer \u201cwhich shape failed?\u201d without becoming a second copy of the customer data.\nFor metrics, separate queue delay from delivery duration. Queue delay is the time betweenoccurredAtor enqueue time and the worker starting. Delivery duration is the time spent calling one subscriber. If those values share one histogram, a slow endpoint can look like a scheduling problem and lead to the wrong fix.\nYes, if the job is idempotent and the retry decision is explicit. A timeout is not proof that the subscriber did not receive the event. Include the stableeventIdin the request and let the receiver deduplicate it. Keep the same event body across retries; changing the timestamp or generating a new ID turns one delivery into several logical events.\nA practical delivery record tracks the event ID, subscriber ID, attempt number, response class, and next eligible time. A 2xx response completes that subscriber delivery. A temporary transport failure or a 5xx response can be retried with bounded backoff. A 4xx response usually needs classification: malformed authentication and unsupported schema are not helped by repeating the same request forever.\nThe fan-out decision is where latency and cost meet. Sending to every subscriber in parallel reduces wall-clock latency, but it increases concurrent connections and the chance that a burst exhausts a worker pool. A bounded concurrency limit gives you a visible control knob. For example, process 50 subscriber deliveries per worker, measure the resulting queue delay, and adjust from observed latency objectives rather than from a convenient round number. The number is a starting configuration, not a universal target.\nThe event should be accepted once, then each subscriber should have an independent delivery state. If one bank's risk system is slow, the notification for another subscriber should not be held hostage by a shared \u201call delivered\u201d flag. That is the difference between fan-out as a workflow and fan-out as a single fragile HTTP call.\nKeep the failure local.\nTest the boundaries in the order they run. First, feed the normalizer representative carrier input and assert the exact canonical object. Include a missing field, an unknown status, an invalid timestamp, and an already-stringified body. Second, serialize and deserialize the delayed job. Third, render the subscriber request and assert its content type, signature input, and body bytes. Finally, replay the sameeventIdand verify that the delivery state does not create a duplicate side effect.\nA small contract fixture catches more than a large collection of happy-path snapshots. Keep one fixture for each schema version. When version 2 arrives, make the compatibility rule visible: either migrate at enqueue time or let the worker handle both versions for a defined period. Do not silently accept every unknown field and call that forward compatibility; permissive parsing can hide a producer typo.\nThe queue itself adds another operational constraint. Ordered delivery and deduplication semantics are useful tools, but they do not replace an application-level idempotency key. Queue documentation describes the behavior and limits of FIFO queues, including message groups and deduplication windows; those guarantees still need to be matched to the shipment event's business identity.\nChoose the simplest path that meets the latency objective while keeping payload ownership clear. If a subscriber needs near-real-time updates, route it through the same canonical event but use a short delay or immediate work. If a downstream system is sensitive to bursts, keep the delayed queue and bound concurrency. If schema errors are frequent, move validation earlier and add a metric for rejected events instead of increasing retries.\nThe before/after check is straightforward: before, a worker learns about formatting trouble after waiting; after, ingestion rejects or quarantines the bad shape, the queue stores a versioned event, and delivery logs explain each attempt. That sequence makes a malformed webhook observable, replayable, and isolated from unrelated subscribers. It also gives the team a clean trade-off: spend concurrency to reduce latency, or spend queue time to protect downstream capacity.\nTemplates let you quickly answer FAQs or store snippets for re-use.\nAre you sure you want to hide this comment? It will become hidden in your post, but will still be visible via the comment'spermalink.\nHide child comments as well\nConfirm\nFor further actions, you may consider blocking this person and/orreporting abuse\nDEV Community\u2014 A space to discuss and keep up software development and manage your software career\nBuilt onForem\u2014 theopen sourcesoftware that powersDEVand other inclusive communities.\nMade with love andRuby on Rails. DEV Community\u00a92016 - 2026.\nWe're a place where coders share, stay up-to-date and grow their careers.", "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 22:12:19 +0000"}, {"title": "Llama 3 8B vs GPT-4o-mini: Node.js Text Moderation JSON Schema, Latency 2026", "url": "https://dev.to/jaggerblack5781/llama-3-8b-vs-gpt-4o-mini-nodejs-text-moderation-json-schema-latency-2026-28pd", "body": "Posted onOct 24\nTL;DR: For a junior developer moderating messy logistics catalog descriptions, start with GPT-4o-mini through chat completions and a strictjson_schema. It is the quickest path to a predictable result without taking on GPU operations. Choose self-hosted Llama 3 8B when infrastructure control is a product requirement. Choose Amazon Bedrock when the team already wants a managed AWS model layer.\nThe decision is about quality per unit of waiting time, not a spreadsheet of token prices. A catalog record that arrives quickly but gets the category wrong still needs human review. A very careful classifier that blocks an import queue for minutes has a different cost: delayed inventory and a support ticket. I would ship the smallest contract that lets me change the model behind it next week.\nFor a one-person SaaS, that makes GPT-4o-mini the practical first choice. The important boundary is the interface: your application should receive the same small moderation object no matter which model is behind it.\nThat is the trade-off.\nThe limitation is easy to miss when a model comparison is reduced to a latency number. Suppose an importer receives a description such as \u201cindustrial cleaner, 20 L drum, for workshop floors,\u201d followed by a second record with a vague claim about safety. The first record can move through the allowed lane. The second should land in review if the schema and prompt make uncertainty explicit. A faster response does not make that judgment better by itself, and a stronger response does not help if the worker cannot keep up. This is why I would keep the raw description, the decision, and the reason together, then compare the same records across the hosted model and the self-hosted candidate. The useful output is a decision boundary the catalog team can inspect, not a vendor slogan or a single average.\nMessy descriptions are where a loose prompt becomes an expensive queue of edge cases. Define the categories before choosing a model. For a logistics catalog, I would separateallowed,review, andblocked, then require a short reason and the extracted product text. The schema should reject a free-form answer before that answer reaches the catalog database.\nKeep the contract boring. A boolean such asis_allowedis easy to consume, but it hides uncertainty. A three-way decision gives the importer a useful middle lane: hold questionable copy for review instead of silently publishing it. This is a small design choice with a real latency effect because only the review lane needs a slower human workflow.\nI would also keep normalization separate from the safety decision. \u201cPallet 1200 x 800 mm, used\u201d can be cleaned for search later. The moderation call should answer its narrow question first. That keeps the prompt shorter, makes responses easier to compare, and gives the runtime fewer ways to drift.\nSelf-hosting Llama 3 8B looks attractive if the requirement is control. The hidden work is the service around the model: GPU capacity, deployment, updates, and the path from a request to a stable response. For simple moderation, that is a lot of machinery to own before the catalog importer has proved its value. Managed inference can make latency for an 8B model comparable, but comparable request time does not remove that operational burden.\nGPT-4o-mini is the easier starting point because the fallback can use chat completions with native structured output throughjson_schema. That matters more than a model leaderboard for this workflow. The junior developer can wire one response contract, log the result, and move on to the importer. Ship weekly. Outsource the undifferentiated infrastructure.\nAmazon Bedrock is a fair alternative when the rest of the application already depends on AWS operations and permissions. Its official service page documents the managed model platform, so it belongs in the shortlist. It is not automatically the best fit for a small standalone SaaS: the surrounding cloud boundary can be useful or can become another thing to configure, depending on where the product already runs.\nA unified OpenAI-compatible gateway such as Infrai can also keep the application contract stable while the provider behind that contract changes. That is useful when the catalog service may try different backends later. It does not remove the need to define categories, inspect failures, or test quality on the descriptions that actually arrive.\nAnthropic Claude and Google Gemini are also real managed alternatives for teams that prefer a direct vendor relationship. They sit in the same broad hosted category as GPT-4o-mini, while Llama 3 8B is the self-hosting option in this comparison. I am not assigning them a latency or price winner here because those measurements are not established by this article's evidence. That is the honest boundary.\nIt is not the right fit if the requirement is a dedicated moderation endpoint: the supported fallback is a chat model withjson_schema. A direct model provider is the cleaner choice when that extra gateway boundary brings no value to the team.\nThe example below is intentionally small. It calls the OpenAI-compatible chat surface, reads the key from the environment, states every moderation category in the schema, and retries a rate limit with exponential backoff. The application can swap its configured base URL without changing the moderation contract.\nThere are two practical details here. First, an empty response is an error, not analloweddecision. Second, a rate limit needs backoff; a tight retry loop turns a temporary limit into a longer outage. For a catalog batch, persist the input and the returned decision so a worker retry does not create a second review item.\nStart with 20 representative descriptions. Small samples expose category ambiguity before a larger import hides it in aggregate counts.\nPick Llama 3 8B when self-hosting is part of the requirement rather than a reflex. A team that needs its own GPU environment, accepts the deployment work, and wants control over the runtime has a coherent reason to do it. The model can be the right answer inside that boundary. The mistake is treating \u201c8B\u201d as a free latency optimization while ignoring the service that must keep it available.\nPick Bedrock when AWS is already the operational center and its managed model boundary reduces work elsewhere. Pick GPT-4o-mini when a small team needs the first reliable moderation path with the least infrastructure to manage. Those are different constraints, so there is no honest universal winner.\nMy decision rule is simple: collect a small, representative set of catalog descriptions, run each candidate through the same three-way schema, and record both decision quality and request delay. Do not use a price table as a proxy for either. If the hosted path meets the review threshold, keep it and spend the saved engineering time on the importer, not on a GPU control plane.\nTemplates let you quickly answer FAQs or store snippets for re-use.\nAre you sure you want to hide this comment? It will become hidden in your post, but will still be visible via the comment'spermalink.\nHide child comments as well\nConfirm\nFor further actions, you may consider blocking this person and/orreporting abuse\nDEV Community\u2014 A space to discuss and keep up software development and manage your software career\nBuilt onForem\u2014 theopen sourcesoftware that powersDEVand other inclusive communities.\nMade with love andRuby on Rails. DEV Community\u00a92016 - 2026.\nWe're a place where coders share, stay up-to-date and grow their careers.", "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 22:11:50 +0000"}, {"title": "The judge said the camera held still. It had not: measuring viewpoint drift in AI room renders", "url": "https://dev.to/aifliproom/the-judge-said-the-camera-held-still-it-had-not-measuring-viewpoint-drift-in-ai-room-renders-4ac3", "body": "Posted onOct 24\u2022Fully Autonomous\nWe build an AI virtual staging tool: a user uploads a photo of a room, an image model restyles it, and automatic checks compare the result with the original before anyone sees it. We described the three gates in anearlier post. This one is about the second gate alone, the one we could not get from a language model.\nThe image model sometimes moves the camera. Not the furniture: the camera. The render comes back from a step back and to the left, or an angled bedroom shot is straightened to face the bed, or the frame has crept in toward the fireplace. The room in the picture is not the room in the photograph, which is exactly the complaint buyers have abouta house that does not look like its listing photos.\nSome MLS rule books require an unaltered imagewith the same viewbeside an altered one (the Texas rules, for example); a render shot from elsewhere cannot satisfy that.\nOur vision judge, a model that receives both images and answers a fixed list of yes/no questions, did not see it. Three rounds of hand-verified cases passed every check; one retry passed with every feature on the right wall and the room visibly shot from somewhere else. We gave the camera its own boolean; still blind. We blended the two images 50/50, double-exposure style, and asked whether the architecture doubled; not reliable either. Months later a stronger judge had the opposite problem, calling 13 of 43 in-place renders moved; its camera answers no longer count indoors.\nSo: can a few hundred lines of plain image maths answer \"did the camera move?\" better than a model that describes rooms in paragraphs?\nSix approaches, A to F in the research scripts, on a hand-labelled set that grew to 24 pairs across two rooms: five with the camera marked as moved, nineteen without.\nA, edge correlation in the top strip.Furniture rarely reaches the ceiling, so compare only the top 28% of the frame. Seven iterations later: 3 of 5 shifts caught with no false alarms; the two misses lived mid-frame (an arched window where the photo had three) and never reached the strip.\nB, the ceiling line.Trace where wall meets ceiling as a curve, one y per column, by dynamic programming over the vertical brightness gradient, and compare the original's curve with the render's. Clean on the first room. On the second, 8 false flags out of 11 good renders: art over a mantel and dark flush beams pulled the tracked line off the ceiling. Paint fools it.\nC and F, block matching on edge maps and a vote-based re-tracker, never separated the set cleanly.Ewas the double-exposure judge above.\nD, a depth profile.Run a monocular depth model on both images. In the upper band of the frame take, per column, how far the far wall reads, and compare the two profiles. Furniture changes the depth model's own normalisation between images, so the comparison is a robust affine fit, a rank correlation and a search for a sideways slide. Glass fools it: windows and mirrors are re-estimated differently each time.\nThe two failure modes do not overlap: paint is flat in depth, glass does not move the ceiling line. So the score is the smaller of the two normalised scores, and a render is flagged only when both channels fire:\nOn the 24 pairs: 0 false flags, 5 of 5 shifts caught. The margin was thin: the worst good render scored 0.920, the weakest real shift 1.082. Hence a grey zone, logged and never failed.\nThe referee runs beside the vision judge on every indoor attempt, retries included; the depth call (about half a cent, three to seven seconds) hides behind the judge's call. \"Shifted\" counts as a geometry defect, the same class as a missing door, not a styling miss: the attempt is rejected, and when the cheap image model breaks geometry the pipeline escalates to a pricier one instead of rerolling. Everything fails open: if the referee errors or runs out of time, the camera is simply left unchecked and the other gates decide. A flaky referee must never block a paying user.\nFail-open was where the bugs were. The check's 15-second wall used to fail open by resolving past a slow depth call, leaving the prediction running and billing us for a verdict we had abandoned; now it aborts the call. The original's reference had no wall at all, and the depth model is a shared one that scales to zero: a cold start rejected the whole QA stage, and a finished, already-paid render was thrown away as \"Generation failed\" after 55 seconds. In September, 8 of 26 live renders lost the camera check because the cold model did not start within 15 seconds (the 215 calls that did start waited at most 2.1 s). The render window has no slack for a sleeping model; the upload does, since a customer spends at least 30 seconds, typically about 60, choosing a style. So the reference is now computed at upload and stored as 1,536 numbers (ceiling curve, glare mask, depth profile); the referee never looks at the original again.\nWas the referee too strict? Over 14 days in September, 78 customer renders went out as drafts. The referee had flagged the camera on 29 of them, but the camera was theonlyfailed check on 6, and those six scored 1.33 to 5.40 against a line at 1.08. All seven borderline flags (1.08 to 1.20) sat on renders that had also failed something else. Raising the line to 1.25 would have flipped 7 of 67 flags and un-drafted nothing.\nIn the lab that month, four full-pipeline runs (345 attempts) produced 25 flags. Checked against the originals, 25 of 25 had really moved, 24 obviously, and none alone: every one also failed a door, window or layout check. The pricier model drifted at the same rate as the cheap one (8 of 104 versus 15 of 192). Camera drift is a symptom, not a class of defect: the model rebuilds the room as a catalogue photo. A wardrobe cut by the frame edge becomes a wall and the camera steps back behind it; an angled bedroom shot is straightened to face the bed; the frame zooms in toward the archway.\nTwo fixes aimed at the model, both measured, both discarded. A prompt anchor saying the frame is fixed cut camera flags from 10 to 6, but clean renders fell from 15 to 12 and missing doors rose from 11 to 14; reverted. A second premium retry when only the camera had failed: the condition fired in 1 of 39 cells, and the retry gave 0 clean images in 12 throws, the camera returning five times, each with a new defect; not merged.\nThen live pairs. Of 36 live drafts with a camera flag, 12 renders still existed (free-plan images expire) and one original; the rest were reconstructed from the stored reference curve and the door and window boxes. By eye: the model had moved the camera in 5 (plus one probable), the referee was wrong in 3, and 3 were arguable.\nAll three errors were the same error. They scored 1.12 to 1.17, barely over the line, and each room had paint and glass together: a towel rail and a glass shower, a picture shelf and a full-height window. The ceiling-line channel locked onto the decor, the depth channel edged over on the glass, and the minimum rule, built on the assumption that the two deceivers do not meet, met them in one room. The one real soft shift in the set scored 1.10. No line separates 1.10 from 1.12; the calibration rooms simply had neither towel rails nor glass showers.\nOne blind spot is by construction. A pan in a room with a flat ceiling barely changes the ceiling line's height, so the RGB channel stays quiet and the minimum discards the depth channel's alarm. Of 12 reviewed cases where the judge said moved and the referee said no, 7 were real, five of them vertical bedroom photos straightened to face the bed. One such render in 192 lab cells reached a user clean.\nA second turned up in October, on 20 renders of hard amateur photos (close-ups, cluttered rooms). The referee caught 2 of 5 framing failures and missed 3, and in all three the camera had pulled straight back while the model drew in more room. The referee compares where the ceiling line and the far wall sit across the frame, not how large they appear; a pure pull-back changes scale, not position. Of the 15 renders that looked fine it flagged one, and a second look found a real shift there too. A cheap frame-scale comparison caught 2 of the 3 misses in the same set, with false alarms of its own, so it needs its own calibration, in a separate module, before it ships.\nThe file is frozen; any edit means re-running the calibration. The proposed fix for the near-line errors, cross-checking the door and window boxes before a near-line \"shifted\" verdict, is written down and not yet done. Until then the referee is right far more often than the model it watches, and wrong in ways we can name.\nWe build this atAI Flip Room.\nTemplates let you quickly answer FAQs or store snippets for re-use.\nAre you sure you want to hide this comment? It will become hidden in your post, but will still be visible via the comment'spermalink.\nHide child comments as well\nConfirm\nFor further actions, you may consider blocking this person and/orreporting abuse\nDEV Community\u2014 A space to discuss and keep up software development and manage your software career\nBuilt onForem\u2014 theopen sourcesoftware that powersDEVand other inclusive communities.\nMade with love andRuby on Rails. DEV Community\u00a92016 - 2026.\nWe're a place where coders share, stay up-to-date and grow their careers.", "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 22:11:10 +0000"}, {"title": "Overnight Brain: A Lazy AI Assistant That Works the Night Shift", "url": "https://dev.to/creed725/overnight-brain-a-lazy-ai-assistant-that-works-the-night-shift-oj2", "body": "Posted onOct 24\u2022AI-assisted\nHacktoberfest Open-Source AI Challenge Week 3: Slow and Steady Submission \ud83e\udda5\nThis is a submission for theHacktoberfest Open-Source AI Challenge Week 3: Slow and Steady\nWhat does it do?\nOvernight Brain is a lazy person's AI assistant. During the day, I drop things into a queue: questions I've been meaning to look into, long articles to summarize, big goals to break into steps, and messages I keep putting off. At night, Google's open-weight Gemma model runs right on my laptop and works through the list one task at a time while I sleep. In the morning, I wake up to a digest with everything done.\nWhat effort does it save?\nIt saves me from sitting in front of a chatbot waiting for answers, and from the mental load of carrying around a to-do list of \"I'll look that up later.\" I hand it off once and stop thinking about it.\nWho gets to be a little lazier?\nAnyone with a full day and a tired brain by evening. I start work at 6 a.m., so my evenings are filled with exercising, dinner, and other tasks. Now I can queue things up before bed and read the answers with breakfast.\nI queued three tasks at 10 p.m., went to bed, and at 1 a.m. my laptop started working through them on its own.\nThere\u2019s no deployed link because Overnight Brain is designed to run privately on your own computer. Here\u2019s what a real overnight run looked like on my MacBook.\n\nThe worker rested until 1 a.m., finished all three tasks, and saved the digest at 1:04 a.m.\n\nWhat I woke up to: a plan for this post, a way to explain open-weight models, and a LinkedIn draft. (Click to enlarge.)\nHand it off tonight. Read it with your coffee.\nOvernight Brain is a lazy person's AI assistant. During the day you drop questions, long articles, big goals, and messages you keep putting off into a queue. At night, a small open-weight model running on your own laptop works through them one at a time, slowly, while you sleep. In the morning you get a digest.\nBuilt for the Hacktoberfest 2026 Open-Source AI Challenge, Week 3:Slow and Steady.\nMost AI tools race to answer in seconds, which means a giant model in someone else's data center. Overnight Brain flips that. If the answer can wait until morning, a small open-weight model like Gemma on an ordinary laptop is plenty. It can take its time, run with fewer threads, rest between tasks, and nobody has to watch a spinner.\nI built Overnight Brain with help from an AI assistant. I brought the idea, and it wrote most of the code. My job was setting it up on my own Mac, testing it, troubleshooting, and publishing it.\nOpen-source AI used:Gemma 4 E2B, an open-weight model from Google, running locally through Ollama, an open-source tool for local inference. No cloud AI services are involved.\nThe pieces:\nAt 1 a.m., the worker sends each task to Gemma through Ollama on my own laptop, saves the answer, and then has Gemma write the short good-morning note at the top of the digest.\nThe 8 GB plot twist:My first model download was 9.5 GB. Then I checked my MacBook and found it only has 8 GB of memory, so the model would never fit. I switched to a smaller, compressed version of Gemma that's built for lighter machines, and it ran fine at about a minute per task.\nGentle mode:Since nobody's waiting on the answers, the worker uses fewer CPU threads and rests between tasks, so my laptop stays cool and quiet overnight.\nI work in federal payroll, where protecting sensitive information is part of my everyday job. That shapes how I think about AI. Most AI tools send whatever you type to someone else's servers. With an open-weight model like Gemma running on my own laptop, nothing leaves my computer. I can hand off personal notes, half-formed ideas, and drafts without wondering where they end up.\nOpen models also make this kind of project possible for everyday people:\nSlow and steady only works because the model is mine. A closed AI service is built to answer fast. An open model on my own laptop can take its time while I sleep.\nI used it this week. On Wednesday night, I queued three tasks around 10 p.m.: a plan for writing this post, a question about explaining open-weight models, and a LinkedIn announcement. Then I went to bed. At 1 a.m., my laptop started on its own and finished all three in about four minutes. When I woke up at 6, the digest was waiting.\nThe effort it saves isn't really the minutes. It's that I didn't sit in front of a screen waiting, and I didn't carry those to-dos around in my head all evening.\nHonest lessons from a small model:\nOvernight Brain is a great head start, not a replacement for my own judgment.\nI'd love for you to try Overnight Brain on your own laptop. Setup takes about 15 minutes, plus download time. Just follow the README in the GitHub repo linked in the Code section above.\nYou'll need Python, the free Ollama app, and about 5 GB of free space for the Gemma model. I built and tested it on a Mac, so if you try it on Windows or Linux, let me know how it goes.\nIf you do try it, tell me in the comments:\nFeedback, ideas, and pull requests are all welcome!\nGemma\nTemplates let you quickly answer FAQs or store snippets for re-use.\nAre you sure you want to hide this comment? It will become hidden in your post, but will still be visible via the comment'spermalink.\nHide child comments as well\nConfirm\nFor further actions, you may consider blocking this person and/orreporting abuse\nDEV Community\u2014 A space to discuss and keep up software development and manage your software career\nBuilt onForem\u2014 theopen sourcesoftware that powersDEVand other inclusive communities.\nMade with love andRuby on Rails. DEV Community\u00a92016 - 2026.\nWe're a place where coders share, stay up-to-date and grow their careers.", "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 22:09:27 +0000"}, {"title": "FastAPI Latency Spikes Under Load: How One Blocking Call in an async Endpoint Stalls Every Request", "url": "https://dev.to/libme/fastapi-latency-spikes-under-load-how-one-blocking-call-in-an-async-endpoint-stalls-every-request-29e1", "body": "Posted onOct 24\nTL;DR:Anasync defendpoint runs directly on the event loop. If it calls anything that blocks (time.sleep,requests.get, a sync database driver, heavy CPU work), the loop cannot serve any other request until that call returns. The fix is to either declare the endpoint with plaindefso the framework runs it in a thread pool, push the blocking call into a thread withasyncio.to_thread, or switch to a genuinely async client. The symptom that sends people searching is odd: health checks time out and p99 explodes while CPU looks fine.\nThe pattern I see over and over: an endpoint is fast in isolation, and a load test or a traffic burst makesunrelatedendpoints slow, including/health. Uptime probes fail, the load balancer marks the instance unhealthy, and the instance restarts under load, which makes things worse.\nHere is a minimal reproduction. Python 3.11+, withfastapi,uvicornandhttpxinstalled:\nRun it withuvicorn app:appand fire ten concurrent requests at each slow route. Against/slow-async, the ten requests are served one after another, so the last one waits roughly ten times 0.5 seconds. That is arithmetic, not a benchmark: a single-threaded loop can only do one blocking call at a time. During that window/healthalso waits, because it is queued behind them on the same loop.\nRunpython hammer.py /slow-asyncand thenpython hammer.py /slow-sync. The first shows/healthstuck for seconds; the second answers it almost immediately.\nTakeaway:async defis a promise that you never block, and the framework cannot enforce it for you.\nFastAPI (via Starlette and AnyIO) treats the two kinds differently. A plaindefendpoint is dispatched to a worker thread pool, so a blocking call occupies a thread, not the loop. Anasync defendpoint is awaited on the loop itself. As of late 2026 the AnyIO thread limiter defaults to 40 concurrent threads, so adefendpoint is not free of limits either: the 41st concurrent blocking request queues.\nThe common mistake is the well-meaning \"everything should be async\" refactor. Convertingdeftoasync defwithout converting the calls inside it makes the endpointworse, because you moved it off the thread pool onto the loop.\nTakeaway: convertingdeftoasync defis a performance regression unless every call inside it was converted too.\nDo not guess. Two tools find it quickly.\n1. asyncio debug mode.Start the server withPYTHONASYNCIODEBUG=1 uvicorn app:app. The loop logs any callback that holds it longer thanslow_callback_duration(0.1 s by default), with the task and the coroutine involved:\nThat line names the endpoint. Debug mode adds overhead, so use it in staging or a local repro, not on a hot production box.\n2. A sampling profiler.If you cannot reproduce locally,py-spy dump --pid <pid>shows where each thread currently is. When the loop thread sits intime.sleep,socket.recv, or a driver'sexecute, that is your culprit. The honest limitation ofpy-spy: it needs permission to attach to the process (ptrace on Linux, root on macOS), which is often locked down in containers.\nThe usual suspects, in the order I find them:requestsinside an async handler, a sync ORM session orpsycopg2call,open().read()on large files,json.loadson multi-megabyte payloads, password hashing (bcrypt/argon2 are deliberately slow), and image or PDF processing.\nTakeaway: the slow-callback log turns \"something is blocking\" into a file and line number in one run.\nFor the one-call case:\nFor outbound HTTP, create the client once at startup rather than per request:\nIf you want an HTTP client that works in both sync and async code with the same API,httpxis the one I reach for; its drawback is that it is a bit slower than lower-level clients such asaiohttpon very high request rates, and the default timeouts differ fromrequests, so set them explicitly.\nCPU-bound work is the case threads do not rescue: threads share the GIL, so heavy pure-Python computation into_threadstill starves other Python threads (C extensions that release the GIL, such as bcrypt, are fine). UseProcessPoolExecutorvialoop.run_in_executoror hand it to a worker queue.\nTakeaway: match the fix to the kind of blocking: threads for I/O and GIL-releasing libraries, processes or queues for pure-Python CPU work.\nYes, and that is why it survives so long.uvicorn --workers 4(or Gunicorn with Uvicorn workers) gives you four independent loops, so one stuck loop no longer stalls everything. But four workers each blocked by one slow request is just a smaller version of the same outage. More workers are good capacity planning and bad bug-hiding; fix the block first, then size the workers.\nIf you want the guard rail rather than the fix, Uvicorn's--timeout-keep-aliveand your proxy's upstream timeout will at least turn a silent stall into a visible 504, which is easier to alert on.\nTakeaway: extra workers raise the number of requests a blocking call can hurt at once; they do not remove the block.\nWhy is my FastAPI app slow when I use async def?Almost always because something inside the handler blocks the event loop, such asrequests, a sync database driver, ortime.sleep. Run withPYTHONASYNCIODEBUG=1to see which coroutine holds the loop.\nShould I use def or async def in FastAPI?Useasync defonly when every I/O call inside it is awaited. Use plaindefwhen you depend on sync libraries; FastAPI runs it in a thread pool.\nDoes asyncio.to_thread make blocking code non-blocking?It moves the call off the event loop onto a worker thread, so other requests keep being served. It does not speed up the call, and pure-Python CPU work still contends for the GIL.\nIf you use sync libraries, write plaindefendpoints and stop worrying. If you are committed toasync def, treat every library call as guilty until proven awaitable, and keep asyncio debug mode in your staging setup so a new blocking dependency gets caught in review rather than at 2 a.m. For pure CPU work, move it out of the web process entirely.\nTemplates let you quickly answer FAQs or store snippets for re-use.\nAre you sure you want to hide this comment? It will become hidden in your post, but will still be visible via the comment'spermalink.\nHide child comments as well\nConfirm\nFor further actions, you may consider blocking this person and/orreporting abuse\nDEV Community\u2014 A space to discuss and keep up software development and manage your software career\nBuilt onForem\u2014 theopen sourcesoftware that powersDEVand other inclusive communities.\nMade with love andRuby on Rails. DEV Community\u00a92016 - 2026.\nWe're a place where coders share, stay up-to-date and grow their careers.", "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 22:08:17 +0000"}, {"title": "Whisper Model Availability Before Transcription API Calls: Structured Support Output", "url": "https://dev.to/solacew31/whisper-model-availability-before-transcription-api-calls-structured-support-output-5boh", "body": "Posted onOct 24\nTL;DR: Do not discover a speech model by sending it a customer recording. Keep a small capability registry, check that registry immediately before transcription, and still treat the transcription call as fallible. The check decides whether to proceed, queue, or use a declared fallback; it does not prove that the next request will succeed.\nFor a support system answering from a private knowledge base, the real contract is structured output correctness. A transcript that looks readable but loses a negation, speaker turn, or ticket identifier can produce a confident answer from the wrong evidence. The availability gate is therefore one input to a larger decision, not a green light to skip validation.\nCount one recording as four operational units: an upload, a transcription attempt, a validation pass, and a retention decision. A retry adds another transcription attempt and often another validation pass. A catalog lookup adds almost no work by comparison, so the number worth measuring is the retry count,n, not the number of availability checks. If a service retries twice, one user action has become three attempts before downstream work is considered.\nThat changes the design question. The useful check is not \u201cdoes this model exist?\u201d It is \u201ccan this model serve this request under the constraints that affect the output?\u201d Those constraints include region, account permissions, input format, maximum duration, and the response schema your support workflow expects. The exact fields belong in your own capability record because a model name alone cannot express those conditions.\nFor example,v3can name the expected output schema, while a 30-second freshness window can bound the registry cache. Neither value is universal. The team should choose them from its rollout and incident policy, then keep them visible in configuration rather than burying them in retry code.\nRetention is the deliberate cost. Keep the original audio only for the period required by support policy, investigation, or an explicit customer request. Keep the normalized transcript and validation result separately, with an identifier that lets an operator trace the decision without making every future answer depend on raw audio. The trade-off is uncomfortable: deleting audio makes a disputed transcript harder to reconstruct. Keeping it indefinitely expands the data surface and the consequences of an access mistake.\nThat check is cheap.\nUse a queue.\nA good gate returns a typed decision, not a boolean.availablecan mean that the registry has a matching capability record;proceedshould mean that the request passes local policy and the record is fresh enough to use. Keep those states separate.\nThe registry can be backed by configuration, a control-plane query, or a health-aware service owned by the team. The transcription adapter should hide that choice and expose the same decision shape to the job worker. Here is a deliberately generic Python boundary; it has no vendor-specific route or model catalog assumptions.\nThe important failure mode is stale state. A successful check at 10:01:00 does not reserve capacity for a request at 10:01:01. Treat the transcription operation as the authority for the final result, classify its response, and make retries bounded and idempotent. An availability cache should have an explicit freshness window; an undocumented, permanent \u201cavailable\u201d value is an outage waiting to be encoded.\nThe race deserves its own test. Have the registry reportenabled=True, then make the adapter return a temporary capacity response. The worker should preserve the idempotency key, record the failed attempt, and queue the job with a next-attempt time; it should not overwrite the capability record based on one request or send the same recording through an unbounded loop. This small scenario catches the dangerous assumption that discovery and execution are one atomic operation.\nThe transcript is input to the support workflow, not its final answer. Define the fields needed by the private knowledge-base lookup before calling the model: for example,customer_id,issue_summary,product_area,quoted_error, andneeds_human_review. Then validate presence, type, length, and allowed values. An emptyquoted_errormay be valid; a missingcustomer_idmay require a queue. Those are different decisions.\nA practical pipeline has four checkpoints:\nDo not silently turn a malformed result into a support answer. Send it to a review queue with the reason attached. This is also where the security boundary matters: a private knowledge base should not receive unvalidated text merely because a speech request returned a successful HTTP status. OWASP treats risks around untrusted model inputs and outputs as application concerns, so validation and authorization stay in the application layer rather than being delegated to a model.\nThe concrete pitfall is treating format acceptance as semantic quality. A file can be accepted and still produce a transcript that cannot safely populate the support schema. Keep those tests distinct: capability tests answer \u201ccan this request be attempted?\u201d; contract tests answer \u201ccan its result be consumed?\u201d\nQueue when capability state is missing or stale, when the schema version is incompatible, or when the failure suggests temporary capacity pressure. Reject when the audio violates a known local constraint, such as an unsupported format or a duration limit. Retry only when the error classification supports it, and stop after a bounded number of attempts. HTTP 429 means the client has sent too many requests in a given period; the response may includeRetry-After, which makes it useful input to a backoff policy.\nThis separation keeps the operator signal clean. \u201cModel unavailable\u201d is not the same incident as \u201cbad audio,\u201d and neither is the same as \u201ctranscript failed schema validation.\u201d Log the capability snapshot used for the decision, the request correlation ID, the attempt number, and the final retention action. Do not log private audio or full customer content by default. Metrics should expose counts and latency, while traces should let an authorized operator follow one request across the gate, transcription, validation, and queue.\nA 2026 implementation should also test the negative paths deliberately: stale registry data, permission changes after the check, a transient 429, a valid transcript missing one required field, and a duplicate worker delivery. The test suite does not need a live vendor call for each case. An adapter contract plus recorded, sanitized responses is enough to exercise the decision logic without making production dependencies part of every build.\nCheck availability before uploading, but never confuse that check with a guarantee. Use a capability record to make policy visible, a bounded worker to handle uncertainty, and schema validation to protect the private knowledge-base lookup. Retain only what the support and compliance process can justify.\nThe system is correct when it fails specifically: it queues a temporary capacity problem, rejects an invalid recording, and routes an unusable transcript for review. That specificity is worth more than a permanently green availability badge.\nTemplates let you quickly answer FAQs or store snippets for re-use.\nAre you sure you want to hide this comment? It will become hidden in your post, but will still be visible via the comment'spermalink.\nHide child comments as well\nConfirm\nFor further actions, you may consider blocking this person and/orreporting abuse\nDEV Community\u2014 A space to discuss and keep up software development and manage your software career\nBuilt onForem\u2014 theopen sourcesoftware that powersDEVand other inclusive communities.\nMade with love andRuby on Rails. DEV Community\u00a92016 - 2026.\nWe're a place where coders share, stay up-to-date and grow their careers.", "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 22:07:33 +0000"}, {"title": "How I kept two docs sites apart in one WordPress install (and almost gave up)", "url": "https://dev.to/antonioprosperi2svg/how-i-kept-two-docs-sites-apart-in-one-wordpress-install-and-almost-gave-up-md7", "body": "Posted onOct 24\nI already had the BeeEngine docs on beeenginejs.com as static WordPress pages. When BeeLadybug grew big enough to need its own docs, about 33 pages, I hit a wall. Everything I made ended up mixed in with the BeeEngine pages: the same page list, the same hierarchy, the same mess. Using posts wasn't an option either, because docs aren't blog articles.\nI'll be honest: I was close to dropping it. I'm not a WordPress plugin expert, and every solution I found was either too complicated or broke something else.\nThe answer turned out to be simple. I gave BeeLadybug its own content type:\nNow every BeeLadybug page lives under/ladybug/...with its own URL, and it's completely separate from the BeeEngine pages in the admin.\nEach page is a Custom HTML block, and all the CSS is scoped under a.bld-docswrapper, so it never leaks into the theme or the BeeEngine docs. Links to pages I haven't written yet point to#and get dimmed:\nThe problem was never WordPress, it was not knowing which tool to use. If you're stuck on something similar, keep going. The fix might be one plugin and one checkbox away.\ud83e\udec8\ud83e\udec8\ud83e\udec8\n\ud83d\udc49BeeLadybug docs\nTemplates let you quickly answer FAQs or store snippets for re-use.\nAre you sure you want to hide this comment? It will become hidden in your post, but will still be visible via the comment'spermalink.\nHide child comments as well\nConfirm\nFor further actions, you may consider blocking this person and/orreporting abuse\nDEV Community\u2014 A space to discuss and keep up software development and manage your software career\nBuilt onForem\u2014 theopen sourcesoftware that powersDEVand other inclusive communities.\nMade with love andRuby on Rails. DEV Community\u00a92016 - 2026.\nWe're a place where coders share, stay up-to-date and grow their careers.", "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 22:07:28 +0000"}, {"title": "Structured Code Review Chatbots: Semantic Search Without Losing Finding Contracts", "url": "https://dev.to/xaviorcross6845/structured-code-review-chatbots-semantic-search-without-losing-finding-contracts-1k3d", "body": "Posted onOct 24\nShort answer:For an in-app chatbot API with semantic search integration, use chat completions for the final contract and embeddings for scoped search; choose the retrieval backend by ownership, not by how fluent the demo sounds.\nAn e-commerce code-review chatbot has a stricter job than answering questions. It must find the relevant rule, explain the risk, and return findings that a pipeline can parse every time. For that constraint, the practical design in 2026 is an OpenAI-compatible/v1/chat/completionscall paired with/v1/embeddings, with semantic retrieval happening before generation.\nThe retrieval layer supplies evidence. The chat layer turns that evidence into a contract-shaped review. Keep those responsibilities separate and the chatbot can handle a question such as \u201ccould this coupon change bypass the refund rule?\u201d without pretending that a plausible paragraph is a valid review result.\nStart with the output, not the model. A useful finding has a stable severity, a file and line reference when available, a short explanation, and a suggested action. I would also return a review status and an explicit evidence list. Missing evidence should produce \u201cneeds review,\u201d not a confident approval.\nHere is the small contract I would put in front of the model:\nThe important detail is that retrieval does not decide severity. It finds policy documents, prior fixes, and the relevant repository passages. The application validates the final JSON against the contract and records which chunks were supplied. That gives a reviewer something auditable when a semantic match is close but wrong.\nA user question should first be embedded with the same embedding model used for the indexed review corpus. Search can then return a compact set of policy and code excerpts. The chat request receives the question, those excerpts, and an instruction to emit only the agreed schema.\nEmbeddings help with reinterpretation and intent extraction. \u201cCoupon refund edge case\u201d can retrieve a document titled \u201cpromotion reversal after fulfillment,\u201d which lexical matching may miss. They do not prove that the retrieved document applies to the current branch. That is why every result needs identifiers and why the prompt should tell the model to abstain when the evidence is insufficient.\nFor an in-app chatbot, the flow is short:\nThe same split leaves room for text-aligned multimodal extensions later, but it does not make every modality available. Audio transcription may be represented in a catalog without being serviceable, and real-time voice session availability can be region- and key-dependent. Those are separate readiness checks, not reasons to weaken the text review contract.\nThe following example shows the two calls and the failure behavior that matters in production. It usesrequestsso the HTTP method is visible, keeps the key in the environment, and honorsRetry-Afteron 429 responses. The vector search itself is represented by a local function because its storage engine is an application choice.\nThe placeholder model value is deliberate: model identifiers are account and catalog decisions, not safe constants to invent in an article. In a real service,search_indexmust perform vector search and enforce tenant and branch filters. The example also validates transport errors before reading a response body as a successful completion. That matters during rate-limit bursts, when a retry without a bound can turn one user action into a request storm.\nThe core trade-off is ownership of the index and the response contract. OpenAI gives a direct chat-and-embedding path, which is attractive when the application already has its own vector store and needs to control retrieval filters. Anthropic is a reasonable generation alternative when the team prefers its model and prompt stack, but it does not remove the need to select and operate a retrieval layer. Gemini is useful for teams already standardizing on Google's model and cloud ecosystem; again, search scope and output validation stay application concerns. OpenRouter can simplify access to multiple model providers, with an extra routing and provider-policy boundary to evaluate. Pinecone is a focused managed vector database: it reduces the operational work around indexing and similarity search, while leaving the chat orchestration and output validation to the application. Elasticsearch is a stronger fit when semantic search must live beside lexical queries, metadata filters, and existing catalog or log search; it brings a broader search surface and more tuning responsibility.\nA fourth pattern is to use a platform that exposes several backend capabilities through one plain REST API, without an SDK installation. Infrai fits that boundary when a team wants chat and embeddings alongside other production modules under one key and one consistent interface, so adding a capability is another endpoint rather than another integration. The public discovery surface is self-describing and exposes schemas, billing, readiness, and runnable examples without requiring a key; the documented surface covers 295 routes across 20 modules, with examples in 10 languages. That can reduce the code-review service's integration friction when a queue, storage, or notification capability joins the workflow. It does not remove the need to own evidence scoping, schema validation, or retrieval quality.\nNo row wins universally. If product search already depends on Elasticsearch, adding a second retrieval system may create more failure modes than it removes. If the team has a small policy corpus and strong platform skills, a managed vector index can be the cleaner boundary. If the code-review service needs dynamic, real-time queries and a consistent multi-capability surface, the unified REST approach is reasonable, provided the evidence contract stays in your application.\nI would ship this in three passes. First, index a narrow corpus of review policies and code snapshots, then build fixtures for ambiguous terms such as \u201crefund,\u201d \u201creversal,\u201d and \u201cauthorization.\u201d Second, log retrieved evidence IDs and validate every generated response against the schema. Third, release the chatbot behind a feature flag and compare its findings with human review on a fixed sample.\nDo not use a moderation endpoint assumption as part of this design: when no dedicated moderation route is available, text or image checks need a chat-model JSON-schema fallback, with its own policy and review path. That is another reason to keep the finding contract explicit instead of mixing safety decisions into retrieval.\nThe decision rule is simple: choose the chat-plus-embeddings pattern, then choose the retrieval owner that matches your existing search boundary. The model can draft the finding. Only scoped evidence and validation should make it visible as a finding.\nTemplates let you quickly answer FAQs or store snippets for re-use.\nAre you sure you want to hide this comment? It will become hidden in your post, but will still be visible via the comment'spermalink.\nHide child comments as well\nConfirm\nFor further actions, you may consider blocking this person and/orreporting abuse\nDEV Community\u2014 A space to discuss and keep up software development and manage your software career\nBuilt onForem\u2014 theopen sourcesoftware that powersDEVand other inclusive communities.\nMade with love andRuby on Rails. DEV Community\u00a92016 - 2026.\nWe're a place where coders share, stay up-to-date and grow their careers.", "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 22:07:01 +0000"}, {"title": "React View Transitions in Next.js 16.4: ViewTransition, addTransitionType and Tested Examples", "url": "https://dev.to/fabwaseem/react-view-transitions-in-nextjs-164-viewtransition-addtransitiontype-and-tested-examples-41ln", "body": "Posted onOct 24\u2022Originally published atfabwaseem.com\nCross-posted from my blog. The original, with any updates, lives onfabwaseem.com.\nReact View Transitions animate UI changes with the browser's View Transition API, declaratively. You wrap elements in<ViewTransition>, and React animates them when they enter, exit, update or move between pages. They became stable inReact 19.3on September 9, 2026, and Next.js 16.4 ships React 19.3, so they work in the App Router with no configuration.\nI built a small Next.js 16.4.2 app with the four main patterns: a shared-element morph, a Suspense reveal, directional slides and a tab animation withaddTransitionType. Then I recorded every transition in headless Chrome 155 to see what actually fires. Most of it behaves as documented. Browser back and forward do not, so that gets its own section below.\nReact View Transitions are the<ViewTransition>component and theaddTransitionTypefunction, stable since React 19.3. They hand animations to the browser'sdocument.startViewTransition()from theView Transition API: the browser snapshots the old and new UI and animates between them, while React decides which elements take part and which CSS class each animation uses.\nReact picks the animation from how the tree changed. From the React 19.3 announcement:\nEach prop takes\"auto\"(the browser's crossfade),\"none\", a CSS class name you style, or an object that maps transition types to classes.\n\nOnly Transitions do. React animates updates insidestartTransition, a<Suspense>boundary revealing its content, and updates fromuseDeferredValue. A plainsetStatedoes not animate, because React treats it as urgent. In Next.js App Router, every<Link>androuter.push()navigation is a Transition, so<ViewTransition>animates route changes automatically.\nThis is the mistake behind most \"my view transition doesn't run\" reports. If you callsetIndex(2)directly in a click handler, nothing animates. Wrap it instartTransition(() => setIndex(2))and it does.\nNo. TheNext.js view transitions guidesays they \"work in the App Router with no configuration.\" Theexperimental.viewTransitionoption that older versions needed is gone: I searched Next.js 16.4.2's config schema and it has noviewTransitionkey. You need React 19.3, whichnext@16.4installs.\nRequirements and support on October 17, 2026:\nNext.js notes that \"some animations may behave differently in Safari.\" Test there before you ship anything elaborate.\nImportViewTransitionfromreact, wrap the element you want to animate, and add CSS for the class names you pass. For route animations, give matching elements the samenameon both pages, tag links withtransitionTypes, and wrap eachpage.tsx(not the layout) in a<ViewTransition>that maps those types to animations.\n\nMy demo is a grid of four cards. Each card opens a detail page with a large hero, details that stream in from the server, and a tabs page. It runs Next.js 16.4.2, React 19.3.1 andcacheComponents: true(the default for new 16.4 apps), so each detail page is a partial prerender: a static shell plus a dynamic hole. The patterns follow the officialNext.js view transitions demo, trimmed down so each one is easy to see.\nGive the thumbnail and the hero the samename. React pairs them across the navigation and the browser animates size and position:\ndefault=\"none\"stops the named element from crossfading during unrelated transitions. Next.js warns that withdefault=\"none\"you must keep the explicitshare, or \"the pair silently stops morphing.\" Names must be unique on a page, so build them from an ID.\nThe morph needs both elements in the same commit. Next.js says this happens with prefetched pages; if the destination suspends first, \"no pair forms.\" In my app the hero sits in the static shell, which<Link>prefetches, so it morphed on every click.\nWrap the fallback in an exit animation and the content in an enter animation:\nDetailscallsawait connection(), so it renders at request time. When it streamed in, React started a second, separate view transition about 800 ms after the navigation one, which matches the delay I put in the data function.\nTag links withtransitionTypes, then map the types to classes in a wrapper you put in every page:\nUse it in eachpage.tsx. Layouts persist across navigations, so enter and exit never fire on a wrapper placed there.useRouter().push()andreplace()also accepttransitionTypes.\nOutside navigation, calladdTransitionTypeinsidestartTransitionto tell React why the update happened. The samesetIndexslides left or right depending on direction:\nkey={index}makes React treat each panel as a new element, so it runs exit on the old one and enter on the new one. React also adds each type to the browser's view transition, so CSS can match it with:active-view-transition-type(tab-next).\nThe class names become selectors on the browser's view transition pseudo-elements:\nThepointer-events: nonerule matters: while a transition runs, the::view-transitionoverlay otherwise swallows clicks.\nEvery in-app pattern worked as documented. A forward click produced one transition typednav-forwardwith thecard-2pair morphing, then a second untyped transition when the streamed details arrived. Tabs gottab-nextandtab-prev. Reduced motion kept the transitions but finished them in under 50 ms. Browser back and forward produced no view transition.\nI wrappeddocument.startViewTransitionin a Puppeteer script and logged each call's types, the pseudo-elements it animated, and how long it took to finish:\nBoth navigation transitions included::view-transition-old(card-2)and::view-transition-new(card-2), which is the shared pair.\nNot in my test. Using the browser's back or forward button in Next.js 16.4.2 started no view transition at all, even for the named morph. It was a client-side navigation (same document, URL and grid restored), and zero calls reachedstartViewTransition. Use in-app back links withtransitionTypes={[\"nav-back\"]}for animated returns.\nThe Next.js guide says browser back navigations \"do not carry a transition type, so the directional slide does not play,\" and that \"the shared element morph from Step 1 still applies.\" In headless Chrome 155 against a production build (next start), I could not reproduce the morph on back or forward. Treat back-button animation as unreliable until you have tested it in your own app and browsers.\nThe usual problems are updates that are not Transitions, wrappers placed in layouts, duplicate names on one page, a destination that suspends before the morph can pair, and expecting the back button to animate. Each fails silently: the UI changes instantly with no error.\nUse View Transitions for route changes, shared elements across pages and loading reveals. The browser animates snapshots, so you add no animation library to your bundle. Keep Motion (formerly Framer Motion) for gestures, springs, drag, scroll-linked effects and animations that must respond while the user interacts.\nFor the agent route, Next.js links avercel-react-view-transitionsskill you can install withnpx skills add vercel-labs/agent-skills --skill vercel-react-view-transitions. MyAgent Skills guideexplains what that command installs. For the partial prerendering setup used in this demo, see myNext.js use cache guide.\nRelated:Next.js 16.4 also ships the experimental Rust React Compiler. I measured it against the Babel compiler and no compiler inReact Compiler in Next.js 16.4: 20 counter clicks caused 0 row re-renders instead of 20,000.\nReact View Transitions are the ViewTransition component and the addTransitionType function, stable since React 19.3 (September 9, 2026). They use the browser View Transition API to animate elements when they enter, exit, update or move between pages, while React chooses which elements take part and which CSS class each animation uses.\nYes. Next.js 16.4 ships React 19.3, and the Next.js docs say view transitions work in the App Router with no configuration. The old experimental.viewTransition flag is gone. Import ViewTransition from react, and every Link or router.push navigation animates because it is a Transition.\nMost often the update is not a Transition. A plain setState never animates; wrap it in startTransition. Other causes: the wrapper sits in a layout instead of page.tsx, two elements share a name on one page, the browser lacks support, or the navigation came from the browser back button.\nWrap the element on both pages in a ViewTransition with the same unique name, such as card-2, and add share=\"morph\" with default=\"none\". React pairs them across the navigation and the browser animates size and position. The destination element must render in the same commit, so keep it in the prefetched shell.\naddTransitionType tags the current Transition with a reason, such as next or previous. Call it inside startTransition next to the state update, then map types to CSS classes in the enter, exit or update props. React also passes each type to the browser, so :active-view-transition-type() works in CSS.\nNot reliably. In my test with Next.js 16.4.2 and Chrome 155, the browser back and forward buttons started no view transition at all, even for named shared elements. Animated returns worked through in-app back links using transitionTypes={[\"nav-back\"]}.\nNext.js says the integration needs Chromium 125 or newer and recent Safari and Firefox versions, and some animations may behave differently in Safari. In browsers without support the app works normally and updates happen instantly, without animation.\nUse View Transitions for route changes, cross-page shared elements and Suspense reveals; they add no animation library because the browser animates snapshots. Keep Motion (formerly Framer Motion) for gestures, springs, drag, scroll-linked effects and animations that must stay interactive while they run.\nOriginally published onfabwaseem.com.\nTemplates let you quickly answer FAQs or store snippets for re-use.\nAre you sure you want to hide this comment? It will become hidden in your post, but will still be visible via the comment'spermalink.\nHide child comments as well\nConfirm\nFor further actions, you may consider blocking this person and/orreporting abuse\nDEV Community\u2014 A space to discuss and keep up software development and manage your software career\nBuilt onForem\u2014 theopen sourcesoftware that powersDEVand other inclusive communities.\nMade with love andRuby on Rails. DEV Community\u00a92016 - 2026.\nWe're a place where coders share, stay up-to-date and grow their careers.", "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 22:05:26 +0000"}, {"title": "Shopify para pequeno neg\u00f3cio no Brasil: quanto custa de verdade em reais (out/2026)", "url": "https://dev.to/sinalaberto/shopify-para-pequeno-negocio-no-brasil-quanto-custa-de-verdade-em-reais-out2026-lld", "body": "Posted onOct 24\nResposta r\u00e1pida:para come\u00e7ar sozinho(a), o planoBasicda Shopify custaUS$ 29/m\u00eas no pagamento anual(cerca deR$ 151/m\u00easno cart\u00e3o brasileiro) ou US$ 39/m\u00eas no mensal (cerca deR$ 204). Mas a mensalidade n\u00e3o \u00e9 o custo inteiro: tem taxa por venda, IOF e, dependendo do gateway, uma taxa extra. Abaixo a conta em reais, com data.\nTranspar\u00eancia: este artigo tem links de afiliado. Se voc\u00ea assinar por eles, o Sinal Aberto pode receber uma comiss\u00e3o, sem custo extra para voc\u00ea.\n\n*Taxas do Shopify Payments, conforme a p\u00e1gina oficial. Confirme na sua conta se o Shopify Payments est\u00e1 dispon\u00edvel para o seu caso.\nComo calculei: US$ \u00d7 5,04 (c\u00e2mbio de 24/10/2026) \u00d7 1,035 (IOF de 3,5% em compra internacional no cart\u00e3o). O banco pode aplicar spread diferente, ent\u00e3o trate como estimativa.\nA p\u00e1gina de pre\u00e7os da Shopify Brasil, consultada em 24/10/2026, anuncia3 dias gr\u00e1tis e depois US$ 1/m\u00eas por 3 meses(a maioria dos planos). Se voc\u00ea pagar mensal no Basic, o primeiro ano ficaria em cerca de US$ 354 (3 \u00d7 US$ 1 + 9 \u00d7 US$ 39), ou~R$ 1.848. N\u00e3o consegui confirmar se a oferta vale tamb\u00e9m para o plano anual, ent\u00e3o confira no checkout.\nSimula\u00e7\u00e3o ilustrativa (n\u00e3o \u00e9 teste real): numa loja que vende R$ 5.000 por m\u00eas via gateway de terceiros, s\u00f3 a taxa da Shopify de 2% j\u00e1 seria cerca de R$ 100, fora a taxa do gateway.\n\nQuer testar? A Shopify tem avalia\u00e7\u00e3o gratuita pelolink do Sinal Aberto. O que o plano gr\u00e1tis n\u00e3o resolve: se voc\u00ea s\u00f3 quer vender um produto digital ou um item por vez, vale comparar com alternativas mais simples antes.\nFonte dos pre\u00e7os:shopify.com/br/precos, consultada em 24/10/2026. C\u00e2mbio: open.er-api.com, 24/10/2026. Os pre\u00e7os podem mudar. N\u00e3o testamos a plataforma neste artigo; os n\u00fameros v\u00eam da p\u00e1gina oficial.\nTexto produzido com assist\u00eancia de IA.\nTemplates let you quickly answer FAQs or store snippets for re-use.\nAre you sure you want to hide this comment? It will become hidden in your post, but will still be visible via the comment'spermalink.\nHide child comments as well\nConfirm\nFor further actions, you may consider blocking this person and/orreporting abuse\nDEV Community\u2014 A space to discuss and keep up software development and manage your software career\nBuilt onForem\u2014 theopen sourcesoftware that powersDEVand other inclusive communities.\nMade with love andRuby on Rails. DEV Community\u00a92016 - 2026.\nWe're a place where coders share, stay up-to-date and grow their careers.", "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 22:04:05 +0000"}, {"title": "How WhereToGo works, and what it is for", "url": "https://dev.to/spdev39/how-wheretogo-works-and-what-it-is-for-akd", "body": "Posted onOct 24\u2022Originally published atgetwheretogo.com\nWhereToGo is a live map of the places people are at right now. This is the long version of what that means: what the app does, what it is good for, and the few things it refuses to do.\nEvery time I moved to a new city the same thing happened. The map showed me ten thousand places. The review sites showed me what strangers thought three years ago. And the only information I actually wanted, where people like me go on a Thursday night and whether it is any good right now, lived in the heads of friends I did not have yet.\nReview scores do not answer that question. A place can be rated 4.6 and empty, or rated 3.9 and the best room in the city tonight. What answers it is two things: people whose taste you trust, and a sense of where the crowd actually is. WhereToGo is built around exactly those two things, and nothing else.\nPopular: the map when nobody is out yet. Every place with its own photograph, spread so a city reads like a city.\nThe map has two modes, and the switch between them is the heart of the app.\nPopularis the map when nobody has told it anything yet. Tens of millions of real places, each with its own photograph, laid out so you can read a neighbourhood at a glance instead of scrolling a list. Filter it to caf\u00e9s, bars, restaurants, parks or museums, whatever you are in the mood for. This is what a new user sees in a city nobody has posted in, and it is already useful on its own.\nCrowdedis the map lit up by people. A pin that reads 17 means seventeen people are at that place this minute. Not a rating, not a review from last year, a count. It is the fastest way I know to tell whether a bar is worth the walk before you walk.\nCrowded: the same map, showing where people are right now.\nA post in WhereToGo is a place, a photo, and a real pin. You stand somewhere you love, or remember somewhere you love, and you post it with its actual location. The app shrinks the photo and strips the camera's metadata before it leaves the phone, so the picture travels but your camera's own GPS stamp and serial number do not.\nThat is deliberately the whole post. There are no stories, no filters, no ranking of your taste against anyone else's. The point of a post is that a friend can open the map in that city and see the place where you would have sent them.\nYou follow people whose taste you trust. Their places appear in your feed and on your map, and that is the only curation there is. Nothing is recommended to you by an engine, nothing is promoted, and there are no adverts anywhere in the app. If you follow nobody, you see the Popular map and the Crowded counts. If you follow ten people who know a city, you have the city.\nAccounts can be private, so only approved followers see what you post. And every field of your profile, your photo, your age, your bio, your location and your presence, has its own visibility setting, so you decide what each group of people gets to see.\nEach place has its own thread. Not a feed of what people posted, but a place to ask the people who are there: is the terrace open, are there tables left, is it worth queuing. It is the question you would ask a friend who happened to be inside, asked of whoever is.\nA place page: how many people are here now, and the thread for asking them things.\nThis is the part people care about most, so here it is precisely.\nCounted, not named.Being counted at a place is anonymous by default. Nobody sees your name or picture at a place until you choose, in your privacy settings, whether followers or everyone may see you there. Until then you are a number in a count.\nDelete your account and all of it goes: posts, photos, comments, follows, the stored position, the notification tokens. Permanently, from inside the app or from the website.\nNew in a city.Follow a few locals and the map fills with their places before you have made a single friend.Travelling.One map that follows you from Paris to Istanbul, in your language, with the same two modes everywhere.A group of friends.Post the places you would send each other to, and let the count tell you where everyone drifted tonight.Anyone tired of review scores.A live count of real people beats a star rating every time.\nWhereToGo went live on Google Play this week, which means honesty is cheap: your city is probably quiet. The Popular map works everywhere from the first day, because it is built from tens of millions of real places with photographs. The Crowded map and the feed fill up as people in a city post and follow. The first place you post is the one that starts your city.\nIt is free, it has no adverts and no plans for any, and it runs on Android 8 and up in English, French, Spanish, Portuguese, Arabic and Hindi. An iPhone version is next.\nGet WhereToGo on Google Play\nQuestions, or something that looks wrong?contact@getwheretogo.com.The privacy policy is atgetwheretogo.com/privacy.\nTemplates let you quickly answer FAQs or store snippets for re-use.\nAre you sure you want to hide this comment? It will become hidden in your post, but will still be visible via the comment'spermalink.\nHide child comments as well\nConfirm\nFor further actions, you may consider blocking this person and/orreporting abuse\nDEV Community\u2014 A space to discuss and keep up software development and manage your software career\nBuilt onForem\u2014 theopen sourcesoftware that powersDEVand other inclusive communities.\nMade with love andRuby on Rails. DEV Community\u00a92016 - 2026.\nWe're a place where coders share, stay up-to-date and grow their careers.", "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 22:03:54 +0000"}, {"title": "BlackX UEFI bootkit exploits Baton Drop flaw to bypass Secure Boot", "url": "https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/blackx-uefi-bootkit-exploits-baton-drop-flaw-to-bypass-secure-boot/", "body": "Content not found", "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 10:17:32 -0400"}, {"title": "Abandoned Popcorn Time app exposed millions of devices to RCE attacks", "url": "https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/abandoned-popcorn-time-app-exposed-millions-of-device-to-rce-attacks/", "body": "Content not found", "date": "Fri, 23 Oct 2026 16:55:05 -0400"}, {"title": "Brotli Decoder Flaw Behind Node.js RCE Also Affects Bun, Python and Other Widely Used Software", "url": "https://thehackernews.com/2026/10/brotli-decoder-flaw-behind-nodejs-rce.html", "body": "A memory-corruption flaw in Google's Brotli decoder sits in many programs that unpack Brotli-compressed data, among them Bun, the Python\u00a0Brotli\u00a0and\u00a0brotlicffi\u00a0packages, and Envoy proxies set up to decompress Brotli.\nTracked asCVE-2026-111497, the flaw allows crafted compressed data to overwrite past the end of a buffer and corrupt a program's memory, potentially causing a crash, a memory leak, or the execution of attacker-controlled code.\nImperva researchers have shown thatcode can run on one unpatched Node.js build. The same has not been demonstrated on the other software, and no real-world attacks have been reported.\nThe flaw wasfirst disclosed on October 21, when Node.js and Google shipped fixes, and more have followed product by product since. Because the bug is in Google's decoder and each program ships its own copy of it, every one has to update that copy on its own.\nBrotli is a compression format from Google, used across the web to shrink pages and other traffic. The bug is in the C code that unpacks a Brotli stream, so it travels into anything that reuses that code, from language runtimes to proxies to Python packages.\nA program is exposed only if it decodes Brotli data that someone else controls. For Node.js and Bun, that happens when the built-in\u00a0fetch()\u00a0reads a response sent with\u00a0Content-Encoding: br, or when request-body code such as Express 5 and body-parser 2 unpacks a Brotli upload. A plain Node.js HTTP server does not decode request bodies on its own.\nEnvoy and Istio are affected only when Brotli decompression is enabled, for example, through an Envoy filter. Where it is off, the flaw cannot be reached.\nIn Python, the risk comes from the\u00a0Brotli\u00a0or\u00a0brotlicffi\u00a0package being installed at all. Once either is present, HTTP clients such as requests, urllib3, httpx, and aiohttp decode Brotli responses on their own, so an application can be exposed without ever importing Brotli in its own code.\nA web application firewall helps only so far. Vercel, whichadded firewall ruleson the day of disclosure, said those rules do not cover \"responses your functions fetch from other servers.\" For that path, only updating the runtime or library closes the hole.\nHow far the flaw can be pushed depends on the software.\u00a0Imperva's Red Team, which calls its work Node2Shell, ran attacker-supplied code on an unmodified Node.js 24.22.0 build for Linux on ARM64, delivering it both as a fetched response and as an Express request body. The exploit took several tries, has not been released, and was not tested on other builds.\nOpenAI, whose Codex Security team reported the flaw, describes it as enabling unauthenticated remote code execution in Brotli and lists Node.js, Bun, and Envoy as affected software. In its account, the flaw works both ways:\n\n\nNo in-the-wild exploitation has been reported. CISA's October 23 assessment recorded exploitation as \"none\" and automation as \"no,\" andRed Hatsaid there is no reliable public proof-of-concept for running code.\nSeverity ratings differ by who is scoring. Google, which assigned the CVE, rates it 9.2, or critical, on the CVSS 4.0 scale. Node.js calls it high, and Red Hat rates it 8.1, or important.\nThe fix has an unusual history. The change that closes the flaw was added to Brotli's source code on February 27, but no new Brotli release carried it until October 21. OpenAI said the root cause \"was actually fixed in the Brotli source code in February, but it wasn't known at the time that it gave rise to a security vulnerability and there was no more recent release of Brotli.\"\nNode.js credits Khai Tran and Sylvie Mayer of OpenAI's Codex Security for reporting the flaw, and maintainers Matteo Collina and Antoine du Hamel for the fix.\nUpdate to a fixed version from the table above, then restart the service and rebuild any container image, because a running process keeps the old decoder until it restarts.\nWhere you cannot update yet, stop decoding untrusted Brotli.OpenAI's disclosure pageand the security firmAikidoboth recommend:\nDroppingbrfrom the\u00a0Accept-Encoding\u00a0header does not help on its own, because a server can send Brotli whatever the client asks for. For Node.js, no workaround has been published, so updating the runtime is the only fix.\nSystem packages are being fixed at different times. Debianfixed its brotli packagefor Debian 13 (trixie) on October 23 in version 1.1.0-2+deb13u1. Amazon Linux 2023 still listed its brotli and Node.js 24 packages as pending a fix as of October 22.\nDependency scanners may not flag the Python packages yet. The CVE record names Google's Brotli repository, not the PyPI packages, and GitHub's advisory still lists no affected package, which is what Dependabot reads. Advisory entries for the two Python packages, PYSEC-2026-4322 and PYSEC-2026-4323, were added on October 23, so check the installed versions manually as well.\nLearn how to close critical identity visibility gaps in your SOC, detect hidden threats, and accelerate incident response across human, machine, and AI identities.\nLearn how to assess AI threat readiness, strengthen attack surface visibility, and prepare security operations for emerging threats in this practical webinar.\nGet the latest news, expert insights, exclusive resources, and strategies from industry leaders, all for free.", "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 16:39:29 +0530"}, {"title": "Leaked LiteLLM Master Key Let Attackers Steal GitHub Tokens and Hijack Release Pipeline", "url": "https://thehackernews.com/2026/10/leaked-litellm-master-key-let-attackers.html", "body": "LiteLLM on Friday revealed that an Agent Platform master key accidentally published to a public GitHub repository in late May 2026 ultimately allowed the threat actor topush a malicious versionof the popular Python package.\n\"On May 27, 2026, a configuration file wascommittedto the public LiteLLM-Labs/lite-harness repository which inadvertently contained the master key to a deployment of the LiteLLM Agent Platform, an open source tool we use for orchestrating agents,\" LiteLLM's Oliver Jensen and Mateo Wangsaid.\n\"The error was quickly identified, the commit force-pushed over, and we identified the exposed keys as requiring revocation. However, key revocation did not occur for the master key.\"\nLiteLLM said the unknown attacker (operating from \"69.48.229[.]143\") used this unrevoked key on October 10, 2026, to gain access to the Agent Platform and extracted a GitHub CLI OAuth token the service was configured with to allow self-hosted cloud agents to file pull requests to GitHub repositories.\nThe token, per the developers of the open-source large language model (LLM) gateway, was \"not scoped down\" and associated with its GitHub organization owner's account.\nGaining access to the token allowed the attacker to possess owner-level access to all repositories across the organization and perform additional credential exfiltration. The sequence of actions performed by the threat actor following this step is as follows -\n\"In typical operation, building and publishing a release requires being kicked off by an authorized engineer and being approved by a special account locked to a physical device in our office,\" LiteLLM said.\n\"This restriction was relaxed earlier this week to accommodate the temporary push for more frequent releases and bugfix backports, allowing any two engineers to trigger and approve a release. As such, the threat actor used one of the tokens to trigger the release (after having repointed the deployer to their malicious repository), and another to approve it.\"\nIn addition, the project maintainers noted that the threat actor could have very well loosened this restriction (or removed it entirely) themselves given that they were already in possession of a token belonging to an organization owner. It's suspected that this was not done to evade detection.\nLiteLLM also said the workflows are designed to identify and exfiltrate additional keys and tokens, specifically focusing on upstream inference API tokens. In response to the incident, the company said it's working to revoke each and every token associated with every internal system and vendor platform.\nSocket, whichpublishedits own investigation into the malicious workflows, said it recovered two malicious commits that each added a workflow at \".github/workflows/cache.yml.\" The first commit was made on October 10, 2026, at 10:11:45 p.m. UTC. It was followed by a second commit on October 12.\n\"Neither commit was pushed to a branch that still exists,\" Socket security researcher Karlo Zanki said in a report shared with The Hacker News. \"Both were pushed to short-lived branches named ci-tmp-<hex> that were created, used, and deleted within minutes, which is why the activity leaves almost nothing behind in the repository's ordinary history.\"\nAccording to the application security firm's analysis of the repository's activity log records, both commits rely on a legitimate commit as the branch base to make the push look seemingly harmless -\nBoth workflows, as highlighted by LiteLLM, are equipped to harvest GitHub tokens and API keys for the organization's Linear workspace, LiteLLM deployment, and Together AI, along with the identifier and signing key of a GitHub App.\nThe October 12 run extends the collection to more secrets, including API keys for Anthropic, OpenAI, AWS Bedrock, Tavily, and GitGuardian's secret-scanning tenant. It also captures Playwright storage state, PostgreSQL database information (connection string, account name, password), Redis endpoint address, port, and authentication password.\nThe data gathered from the two workflows is exfiltrated to two different webhook[.]site endpoints -\n\"A GitHub token and a GitHub App private key are credentials for the platform hosting the repository, not for a third-party service,\" Zanki said. \"GITGUARDIAN_API_KEY is a credential for the secret-scanning platform itself, which gives an attacker visibility into, and potentially control over, the alerts that would surface this activity.\n\"E2E_LINEAR_STORAGE_STATE_B64 is serialized browser session state used by Playwright for end-to-end tests; a valid storage state permits authenticated access to the associated Linear account without a password and without satisfying multi-factor authentication.\"\nThe findings rule out LiteLLM's initial hypothesis that the GitHub CLI OAuth tokens were stolen from the logs of an internal LiteLLM deployment. \"The investigation was a process in which we continually learned additional information, meaning some of our assumptions and beliefs were later proven to be inaccurate or incomplete,\" Jensenacknowledged.\nMultiple reports haverevealedthat the files bundled with LiteLLM version 1.107.0 are engineered to execute automatically when Python starts, even if an application did not import LiteLLM.\nThe malware featurestwo execution paths: a credential harvesting module that targets authentication material across developer tools, AI applications, cloud environments, and web browsers, and a Unix-oriented backdoor that's launched as a background process to fetch and execute arbitrary code supplied by the attacker every one hour.\nThe stealer also collects system information, SSH files, Git and GitLab credentials and tokens, environment variables, and tokens associated with npm, PyPI, pip, Yarn, Maven, Gradle, Composer, RubyGems, and Cargo. The data is then compressed and sent to \"litellm[.]dev/healthcheck\" using an HTTP POST request. The domain was registered on October 13, 2026.\n\"Together, these mechanisms extended potential exposure beyond active LiteLLM deployments: simply starting Python in an affected environment could activate the malicious code, even when LiteLLM itself was never imported,\" the Microsoft Security Research teamsaid.\n\"Because the startup files resided outside the LiteLLM application directory, removing only the application files could leave the malicious startup components behind.\"\nLearn how to close critical identity visibility gaps in your SOC, detect hidden threats, and accelerate incident response across human, machine, and AI identities.\nLearn how to assess AI threat readiness, strengthen attack surface visibility, and prepare security operations for emerging threats in this practical webinar.\nGet the latest news, expert insights, exclusive resources, and strategies from industry leaders, all for free.", "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 16:33:33 +0530"}, {"title": "Google Says Gmail and Photos Data Shared With AI Search Could Be Used for Targeted Ads", "url": "https://thehackernews.com/2026/10/google-says-gmail-and-photos-data.html", "body": "Google hasupdated its privacy policyto spell out that using personalized artificial intelligence (AI) features in Search or Gemini could allow the company to serve targeted ads to users based on their activity on other connected Google services.\n\"You may choose to allow certain Google services, including services like Search or Gemini Apps, to access data from other Google services you use, such as Google Photos and Workspace apps like Gmail and Drive, in order to provide you with a more personalized experience, subject to the policies and settings of the accessing service,\" reads apolicy updateeffective October 16, 2026.\n\"For example, Search may process some of the data that you allow Search to access to personalize content, including ads.\"\nGoogle also notes in its revised policy that \"We don't show you personalized ads based on your content from Drive, Gmail, or Google Photos, unless you choose to make data from these services available to other Google services that support personalized ads.\"\nNews of the policy update wasfirst reportedby Android Authority.\nPersonal Intelligence,unveiledby Google in Gemini in January 2026, allows users to connect other services like Gmail, Google Photos, and YouTube as a way to provide tailored answers in combination with their chat history preferences.\nThe feature iscurrently rolling outas a beta to eligible users over the age of 18 globally, except in the European Economic Area, Switzerland, the U.K., and Nigeria.\nIt's worth noting that the policy states it doesn't use content from Gmail, Drive, or Photos to personalize ads by default. However, that changes should users opt to share photos or emails with AI Mode and other Search tools via the Personal Intelligence feature.\nIn other words, if users interact with AI Mode or Gemini while Personal Intelligence is enabled, the data that informs those answers (i.e., content from Gmail or Photos) could be used as a signal to serve targeted ads. The privacy policy aims to make it clear that feeding personal data into its AI tools could contribute to ad personalization.\nUsers should be mindful of the privacy trade-off when connecting their apps to enable AI features. Those who prefer to opt out can do so by selecting the apps they want to disconnect from Personal Intelligence or disable Personalized Recommendations entirely via theSearch Services Personalizationsettings (\"www.google[.]com/search-personalization\").\nIn January 2026, OpenAIbeganshowing ads to logged-in adult U.S. users in both the free and ChatGPT Go tiers, while emphasizing that users' data and conversations are protected and never sold to advertisers. It has also said the ads do not influence responses from the chatbot, and that users are in control of the experience.\nEarlier this week, the AI company said it's expanded advertising on ChatGPT to 26 more countries, \u200bbringing the total to 89. This includes 14 more \u200bLatin American countries and several markets across Europe, Africa, and Asia.\n\"We keep conversations private from advertisers and do not sell customer data,\" OpenAIstressed. \"Ads in ChatGPT are clearly labeled and separate from ChatGPT's answers, and advertising does not influence the answers ChatGPT provides.\"\nLearn how to close critical identity visibility gaps in your SOC, detect hidden threats, and accelerate incident response across human, machine, and AI identities.\nLearn how to assess AI threat readiness, strengthen attack surface visibility, and prepare security operations for emerging threats in this practical webinar.\nGet the latest news, expert insights, exclusive resources, and strategies from industry leaders, all for free.", "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 16:06:33 +0530"}, {"title": "Researchers Push Over 2 Million Updates to Remotely Disable Popcorn Time Updater That Could Fuel a Botnet", "url": "https://thehackernews.com/2026/10/researchers-push-over-2-million-updates.html", "body": "Between November 2025 and March 2026, the Dutch security firmEye Securitypushed more than 2 million updates to computers it did not own to switch off a background updater that it said could have allowed an outsider to take over the machine.\nThe updater belongs toTime4Popcorn, an abandoned copy of Popcorn Time, a free app that streamed pirated films and TV over BitTorrent.\nPopcorn Time was popular about a decade ago, and the original shut down in 2014, but several copies kept running under the same name. A separate, legal project that took the Popcorn Time name in early 2025 is unrelated to it.\nEye Security, which monitors client networks for threats, found the updater during a routine threat hunt: a program called Updater.exe on one client's laptop kept trying to reach a web address that was no longer registered.\nThe firm registered the address itself to see what would connect, and about 50 requests a second poured in. It later laid out the operation, which it named Operation IXIM, in awrite-upand atalk at Hack.luon October 22.\nOver six months, those requests carried more than 3.8 million different hardware IDs. The IDs proved an unreliable way to count machines, because some Windows PCs shared one and some Macs changed theirs, so Eye revised its estimate to 2.5 to 3 million Windows computers and 250,000 to 350,000 Macs.\nThe updater ran in the background on Windows and Mac from the moment the computer started. It checked for new versions over an unencrypted connection and installed whatever it was sent, at the highest privilege level, called SYSTEM on Windows and root on Mac, without checking who sent it.\nBecause the web addresses it called had been abandoned, Eye said, anyone who registered the right one could have served their own program to every machine still calling it.\nOn a machine with that access, an attacker could install ransomware, steal saved passwords, or pull the computer into a botnet, Eye said. Mapping a sample of the connecting addresses, the firm said it saw home users around the world, as well as machines on government, military, and corporate networks, though it added that it is \"hard to draw conclusions\" from that.\nEye haddisclosed the flaw, now tracked asCVE-2026-30612, in June. The U.S. Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency rates it 9.8 out of 10. It affects three versions of the Time4Popcorn software:\nWith no company left to fix the software and no patch coming, Eye decided to use the updater against itself. After taking legal advice, it served the machines an update that does one thing: it stops and disables the updater, deleting no files and collecting nothing.\nEye compared the step toOperation Ladybirdin 2021, when Dutch police pushed an update to computers infected with the Emotet botnet to disable the malware on them.\nThe difference, Eye said, is that Emotet's victims never chose to be infected, while Popcorn Time's users installed the app themselves. Still, the firm said, \"none of them chose to have a forgotten updater running in the background for years.\"\nSoon after the updates began, someone uploaded Eye's update to VirusTotal, and antivirus engines started flagging it as malware, a fair description of a program that disables a Windows service. Because every product that blocked it left the vulnerable updater running, Eye reworked the update until only one scanner continued to flag it.\nBy December 2025, Eye had updated more than 1 million systems and cut the traffic to its web addresses by about 90 percent. By March 1, it was sending about 5,000 updates a day, a 98 percent reduction. Judging the remaining numbers too small to interest a botnet operator, Eye stopped, having sent more than 2 million update downloads in all.\nIn late March, Eye handed the 15 web addresses it controlled to theShadowserver Foundation, a non-profit that takes abandoned and malicious domains out of circulation, so a trusted party would hold them rather than Eye.\nPublic registration records for one of them, popcorntimeupd[.]xyz, now point to Shadowserver's sinkhole servers, with a transfer dated March 20, 2026. Other addresses the updater calls were never Eye's to take and remain with outside owners, the firm said, so machines that reach those are still exposed.\nEye found no sign that anyone else was serving code through the addresses: the ones already registered by other people were parked or up for sale, not pushing updates to the machines calling them. The flaw is not listed in CISA's catalog of known exploited vulnerabilities as of October 23, 2026.\nSwitching off the updater does not make the app safe. Eye said Popcorn Time runs on a 2014 browser engine with its security sandbox turned off, loads content over unencrypted connections, and pulls in code from other abandoned web addresses.\nEye registered those abandoned addresses too, which it said would have let it run its own code on every machine that opened the app. It did not, but it built a working proof of concept, so the risk is real. The update only changed the updater, so the rest of the app remains dangerous for anyone who keeps using Popcorn Time.\nEye's advice is to uninstall Popcorn Time. The updater can also be turned off by hand: on Windows with two commands that stop and disable its \"Update service,\" and on a Mac with one that unloads the PT.updd helper. Removing the app is the safer step, because disabling the updater leaves the rest of it in place.\nOn Android, the updater is built into the app and cannot be turned off on its own, and it installs only if the user taps to accept, so Eye did not send updates to Android devices. Removing the app is the only fix there.\nTo check a network, defenders can look for the two updater files, Updater.exe on Windows and PT.updd on Mac, and for traffic to the web addresses in Eye's write-up, which lists all 20 domains and the file hashes.\nLearn how to close critical identity visibility gaps in your SOC, detect hidden threats, and accelerate incident response across human, machine, and AI identities.\nLearn how to assess AI threat readiness, strengthen attack surface visibility, and prepare security operations for emerging threats in this practical webinar.\nGet the latest news, expert insights, exclusive resources, and strategies from industry leaders, all for free.", "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 14:34:24 +0530"}, {"title": "CISA Flags Two Actively Exploited Critical Flaws in Collabora Online and Arista VeloCloud", "url": "https://thehackernews.com/2026/10/cisa-flags-two-actively-exploited.html", "body": "The U.S. Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency (CISA) on Friday added two vulnerabilities impactingCollabora Online Built-in CODE ServerandArista VeloCloud Orchestrator(VCO) to its Known Exploited Vulnerabilities (KEV), citing reports of active exploitation in the wild.\nThe critical-severity vulnerabilities are listed below -\nAsreportedby The Hacker News, threat actors have been observed exploiting CVE-2026-110447 to backdoor Nextcloud servers with a web shell so as to conduct reconnaissance and search for credential stores, password manager artifacts, and cryptocurrency wallets.\nAs for CVE-2026-114625, successful exploitationrequiresan attacker to have network access to the VCO web interface and a valid authenticated session on the VCO portal. The minimum user role required is Enterprise Read Only.\nAlthough Arista has acknowledged that the vulnerability is \"known to be actively exploited,\" it has not attributed the activity to a particular threat actor or provided technical details on how the vulnerability is being exploited and what actions the attackers took after privilege escalation. The company also has not disclosed the number of affected organizations.\nHowever, it has shared two IP addresses geolocated to Japan as responsible for driving the activity -\nCVE-2026-114625 is the third vulnerability in the same product to have been weaponized in the wild since July 2026, followingCVE-2026-16812(CVSS score: 10.0) andCVE-2026-93952(CVSS score: 10.0).\nIn light of active exploitation, Federal Civilian Executive Branch (FCEB) agencies are recommended to apply the fixes by October 26, 2026, to secure their networks.\nLearn how to close critical identity visibility gaps in your SOC, detect hidden threats, and accelerate incident response across human, machine, and AI identities.\nLearn how to assess AI threat readiness, strengthen attack surface visibility, and prepare security operations for emerging threats in this practical webinar.\nGet the latest news, expert insights, exclusive resources, and strategies from industry leaders, all for free.", "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 11:55:57 +0530"}]